/* Machine translations of Kritik der reinen Vernunft, 2nd ed. (B, 1787; OCR of AA III), in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['krv-b/guyer'] = {"krv-b-0001":["Critique\nof",[]],"krv-b-0002":["Pure Reason",[]],"krv-b-0003":["by\nImmanuel Kant,\nProfessor in Königsberg,",[]],"krv-b-0004":["Member of the Royal Academy of Sciences in Berlin.",[]],"krv-b-0005":["Second edition, improved here and there.",[]],"krv-b-0006":["BACO of VERULAM.\n*Instauratio magna*. Preface.",[]],"krv-b-0007":["*De nobis ipsis silemus: De re autem, quae agitur, petimus: ut homines eam non opinionem, sed opus esse cogitent; ac pro certo habeant, non sectae nos alicuius, aut placiti, sed utilitatis et amplitudinis humanae fundamenta moliri. Deinde ut suis commodis aequi — in commune consulant — et ipsi in partem veniant. Praeterea ut bene sperent, neque instaurationem nostram ut quiddam infinitum et ultra mortale fingant, et animo concipiant; cum revera sit infiniti erroris finis et terminus legitimus.*",[]],"krv-b-0008":["1) The motto is an addition of A2.",[]],"krv-b-0009":["To His Excellency,\nthe Royal Minister of State\nFreiherr von Zedlitz.",[]],"krv-b-0010":["[illegible OCR residue, not translated]",[]],"krv-b-0011":["Gracious Lord!",[]],"krv-b-0012":["| To promote the growth of the sciences on one's own part is to work on Your Excellency's own interest; for this is bound up with that not merely through the exalted post of a protector, but through the far more intimate relation of a lover and enlightened",[]],"krv-b-0013":["connoisseur. Therefore I also avail myself of the sole means that is in some measure in my faculty, of showing my gratitude for the gracious confidence with which Your Excellency honors me, as if I could contribute something to this intention. | To the same gracious notice with which Your Excellency has deigned the first edition of this work, I now dedicate this second edition as well",[]],"krv-b-0014":["and hereby at the same time all the remaining concern of my literary vo-",[]],"krv-b-0015":["cation, and am with the deepest veneration",[]],"krv-b-0016":["Your Excellency's",[]],"krv-b-0017":["| Königsberg, the 23rd of April most submissive-obedient",[]],"krv-b-0018":["1787. Servant\nImmanuel Kant.",[]],"krv-b-0019":["[illegible OCR residue, not translated]",[]],"krv-b-0020":["Preface to the Second Edition.",[]],"krv-b-0021":["Whether the working up of the cognitions that belong to the business of reason travels the secure course of a science or not, can soon be judged from the result. If, after many preparations and arrangements have been made, it gets stuck as soon as it comes to the end, or, in order to reach this, must often go back again and take a different path; likewise if it is not possible to bring the various collaborators into agreement about the way in which the common aim is to be pursued: then one can always be convinced that such a study has by far not yet entered upon the secure course of a science, but is a mere groping about, and it is already a merit toward reason to find out this path where possible, even if much that was contained in the end previously adopted without reflection would have to be given up as futile.",[]],"krv-b-0022":["That logic has already gone this secure course from the earliest times can be seen from the fact that since Aristotle it has not been permitted to take a step backward, unless one wanted to count as improvements the removal of some dispensable subtleties or a more distinct determination of what has been presented, which however belongs more to the elegance than to the certainty of the science. What is further remarkable about it is that up to now it has also been unable to take a step forward, and thus to all appearances seems to be closed and complete. For if some moderns thought to expand it by inserting partly psychological chapters about the various cognitive powers (the imagination, wit), partly metaphysical ones about the origin of cognition or of the various kinds of certainty according to the difference of the objects (idealism, skepticism, etc.), partly anthropological ones about prejudices (their causes and remedies), this stems from their ignorance of the peculiar nature of this science. It is not an increase but a disfigurement of the sciences if one lets their boundaries run into one another; the boundary of logic, however, is determined quite precisely by its being a science that expounds at length and strictly proves nothing but the formal rules of all thinking (whether it be a priori or empirical, have whatever origin or object it will, encounter in our mind contingent or natural hindrances).",[]],"krv-b-0023":["That it has succeeded so well with logic, it has to thank for this advantage merely its limitedness, through which it is entitled, indeed bound, to abstract from all objects of cognition and their difference, and in it, therefore, the understanding has to do with nothing further than itself and its form. Far harder, naturally, had it to be for reason to enter upon the secure path of science when it has to deal not merely with itself but also with objects; hence the former, as propaedeutic, constitutes only, as it were, the forecourt of the sciences, and when it is a matter of knowledge [*Kenntnisse*], one indeed presupposes a logic for the judging of it, but must seek the acquisition of it in the sciences properly and objectively so called.",[]],"krv-b-0024":["Insofar as reason is to be in these, something must be cognized in them a priori, and their cognition can be related to its object in two ways, either merely to determine this and its concept (which must be given from elsewhere), or also to make it actual. The first is theoretical, the other practical cognition of reason. Of both, the pure part, however much or little it may contain, namely that in which reason determines its object entirely a priori, must be presented alone beforehand, and what comes from other sources must not be mixed up with it, for it is bad management if one blindly spends what comes in, without afterwards, when the former gets stuck, being able to distinguish",[]],"krv-b-0025":["which part of the income can bear the expenditure, and from which one must pare it down.",[]],"krv-b-0026":["Mathematics and physics are the two theoretical cognitions of reason that are to determine their objects a priori, the former entirely pure, the latter at least in part pure, but then also according to the measure of other sources of cognition than that of reason.",["- krv-b-0006: \"BACO ve VERULAMIO\" corrected from OCR to \"BACO de VERULAMIO\"; Latin kept as printed. The Latin motto (0007) is not translated; \"“)\" and \"1)\" are footnote marks.","- krv-b-0010, 0019: OCR noise (\"ns m u\", \"ur — Te\"); no recoverable text, so not translated.","- krv-b-0013: \"könne!)\" read \"könne\"; the footnote marks (*\"), f) are OCR residue and omitted; \"Zutrauen\" = \"confidence\"; \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\"; \"Absicht\" = \"intention\" (here \"this intention\").","- krv-b-0014: \"zugleich“)\" mark omitted; \"Bestimmung\" (split across 0014–0015) rendered \"vocation\" per glossary, with the hyphenated word left broken as in the passage (\"Be-\" / \"stimmung\").","- krv-b-0021: \"Zweck\" = \"end\" (first \"Zweck\" and \"Zwecke\"); \"Absicht\" = \"aim\"; \"Überlegung\" = \"reflection\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"cognitions\"; \"solte\" read \"sollte\".","- krv-b-0022: OCR \"e\" before \"oder der verschiedenen Art\" is a damaged word (probably \"Erkenntniß\"); rendered \"cognition\" as a doubtful reading. \"Erkenntnißkräfte\" rendered \"cognitive powers\"; \"anthropolo— gl che\" read \"anthropologische\". \"Grenze\" = \"boundary\" (also \"Grenzen\"); \"Gemüth\" = \"mind\". Bestimmung = \"determination\"; \"es mag a priori oder empirisch sein\" kept ambiguous.","- krv-b-0023: \"Kenntnissen\" = \"knowledge\" (glossary Kenntnis; Wissen = knowledge is a different word); \"Verstand\" = \"understanding\"; \"Beurtheilung\" = \"judging\" (glossary: judgment); \"Gegenstand\"/\"Object\" both \"object\" (0024 Gegenstand, 0022–0026 Object).","- krv-b-0024: \"auch)\" read \"auch\"; \"wirtlich\" read \"wirklich\" = \"actual\"; \"Stecken\" idiom \"geräth in Stecken\" kept literal-ish as \"gets stuck\"; \"kön⸗neu\" read \"können\". The \"Einnähme\" is read \"Einnahme\".","- krv-b-0026: OCR \"i ganz rein\" read \"ganz rein\" (the stray \"i\" omitted)."]],"krv-b-0027":["Mathematics, from the earliest times to which the history of human reason reaches, has, among that admirable people the Greeks, gone the secure path of a science. Yet one must not think that it was as easy for it as for logic, where reason has to do only with itself, to hit upon that royal path, or rather to pave it for itself; rather I believe that for a long time it was left with groping about (chiefly still among the Egyptians), and that this transformation is to be ascribed to a revolution, which the fortunate idea of a single man brought about in an attempt, from which on the course that had to be taken could no longer be missed, and the secure course of a science was entered upon and traced out for all times and into infinite distances. The history of this revolution in the way of thinking, which was far more important than the discovery of the way around the famous cape, and of the fortunate one who brought it about, has not been preserved for us. Yet the legend that Diogenes Laertius transmits to us, who names the supposed inventor of the smallest elements of geometrical demonstrations, those which, by common judgment, do not even need a proof, proves that the memory of the alteration, which was effected by the first trace of the discovery of this new path, must have seemed extremely important to the mathematicians and thereby became unforgettable. A light dawned on the first one who demonstrated the isosceles triangle (he may have been called Thales or whatever one wants); for he found that he must not trace what he saw in the figure, or even the mere concept of it, and as it were learn its properties from that, but must bring it forth through what he himself thought into it according to concepts *a priori* and presented (through construction), and that, in order to know something *a priori* with certainty, he must ascribe to the thing nothing except what followed necessarily from that which he himself had put into it in accordance with his concept.",[]],"krv-b-0028":["With natural science things went far more slowly until it hit upon the highway of science; for it is only about a century and a half since the proposal of the ingenious Bacon of Verulam partly occasioned this discovery, and partly, since one was already on its trail, enlivened it more, which can likewise be explained only through a revolution in the way of thinking that took place quickly. I will here consider natural science only insofar as it is grounded on empirical principles.",[]],"krv-b-0029":["When Galileo had his balls roll down the inclined plane with a weight chosen by himself, or Torricelli had the air bear a weight that he had thought beforehand to be equal to that of a column of water known to him, or, in still later time, Stahl transformed metals into calx and this in turn into metal, by withdrawing something from them and giving it back:* a light dawned on all students of nature. They comprehended that reason has insight only into what it itself produces according to its plan, that it must go ahead with principles of its judgments according to constant laws and compel nature to answer its questions, but must not let itself be led by nature alone, as it were on a leading-string; for otherwise contingent observations, made according to no previously designed plan, do not at all cohere in a necessary law, which reason nevertheless seeks and requires. Reason must approach nature with its principles, in accordance with which alone agreeing appearances can count as laws, in one hand, and with the experiment that it devised in accordance with those in the other, indeed in order to be taught by it, but not in the capacity of a pupil who lets the teacher recite to him everything he wants, but of an appointed judge who compels the witnesses to answer the questions that he puts to them. And so even physics has to thank the so advantageous revolution in its way of thinking merely for the idea of seeking in nature (not of attributing to it), in accordance with what reason itself puts into nature, that which it must learn from it, and of which it would know nothing on its own. Thereby natural science was for the first time brought onto the secure course of a science, after it had been for so many centuries nothing further than a mere groping about.",[]],"krv-b-0030":["* I do not follow here exactly the thread of the history of the experimental method, the first beginnings of which are also not well known.",[]],"krv-b-0031":["Metaphysics, an entirely isolated speculative cognition of reason, which raises itself wholly above teaching by experience, and indeed through mere concepts (not, like mathematics, through the application of them to intuition), where reason is thus to be its own pupil, has so far had a fate not yet so favorable that it could have entered upon the secure course of a science, although it is older than all the rest and would remain, even if the rest were all to be swallowed up entirely in the abyss of an all-destroying barbarism. For in it reason continually gets stuck, even when it wants to have insight *a priori* (as it presumes) into those laws that the most common experience confirms. In it one must retrace the path countless times, because one finds that it does not lead where one wants to go, and as far as the unanimity of its adherents in assertions is concerned, it is still so far from it that it is rather a battlefield, which seems quite properly to be destined for exercising one's powers in a mock combat, on which no combatant has yet ever been able to conquer for himself even the smallest place and to found a lasting possession on his victory. There is thus no doubt that its procedure has so far been a mere groping about, and, what is worst, among mere concepts. | Where does it lie, then, that no secure path of science has yet been able to be found here? Is it perhaps impossible? Whence then has nature afflicted our reason with the restless striving to trace it out as one of its most important concerns? Still more, how little cause have we to place trust in our reason, if in one of the most important parts of our desire for knowledge it not merely forsakes us but holds us off with pretenses and in the end deceives us! Or if the path has only so far been missed, what indication can we use so that, in renewed searching, we may hope that we shall be luckier than others before us have been?",["- Wissenschaft = science; Naturwissenschaft = natural science; Erkenntniß = cognition; Wissen/wissen = knowledge/know; einsehen rendered \"have insight\" (not in glossary).","- Denkart = \"way of thinking\"; Vernunft = reason; Begriff = concept; Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinungen = appearances; Urtheil = judgment; Beobachtungen = observations; Belehrung = teaching; belehrt = taught.","- B xi: \"dem ging ein Licht auf\" is dative, \"to him a light dawned\"; kept as a clause with the dative; \"Einfall\" rendered \"idea\" (B ix, B xiii \"Einfälle\" likewise).","- B x: the clause \"die Umänderung einer Revolution zuzuschreiben sei\" is kept parallel to \"daß es ... geblieben ist\"; \"it was left with groping about\" follows the German \"es ... beim Herumtappen geblieben\".","- B xi: \"nach Begriffen selbst a priori hineindachte und darftellte\" — \"a priori\" could attach to \"hineindachte\" alone or to both verbs; \"nach Begriffen\" likewise; left ambiguous. \"darftellte\" is an OCR slip for \"darstellte\".","- B xiii: \"wovon sie für sich selbst nichts wissen würde\" — \"on its own\"/\"by itself\" for \"für sich selbst\"; \"Heeresweg\" = \"highway\" (literally \"military road\").","- Footnote marker in B xii (OCR \"“)\") and the footnote's \"Erperimental« methode\" silently corrected; the asterisk stands for the footnote marker. In B xiv the sign \"l\" before \"Woran\" is an OCR residue of a page break, rendered \"|\".","- \"Stecken\" in \"in Stecken geräth\" rendered \"gets stuck\"; \"Spielgefechte\" = \"mock combat\"; \"Gewicht\" = \"weight,\" \"Schwere\" also \"weight\"; one English word for two German words."]],"krv-b-0032":["I should think that the examples of mathematics and natural science, which through a revolution brought about all at once have become what they now are, would be remarkable enough to reflect on the essential piece of the alteration of the way of thinking that has become so advantageous to them, and, as far as their analogy, as cognitions of reason, with metaphysics permits, at least to imitate them in this by way of an attempt. Hitherto it was assumed that all our cognition must conform to the objects; but all attempts to make out something about them a priori through concepts, through which our cognition would be expanded, came to nothing under this presupposition. Let one therefore try once whether we do not make better progress with the problems of metaphysics by assuming that the objects must conform to our cognition, which already agrees better with the demanded possibility of a cognition of them a priori, which is to settle something about objects before they are given to us. It is just the same here as with the first thoughts of Copernicus, who, after things did not go well with the explanation of the heavenly motions if he assumed that the entire host of stars revolved around the spectator, tried whether it might not succeed better if he let the spectator revolve and, conversely, left the stars at rest. In metaphysics one can now try in a similar way, as far as the intuition of objects is concerned. If the intuition had to conform to the constitution of the objects, I do not see how one could know anything of it a priori; but if the object (as object of the senses) conforms to the constitution of our faculty of intuition, then I can very well represent this possibility to myself. But since I cannot stay with these intuitions, if they are to become cognitions, but must relate them as representations to something as object and determine the latter through the former, I can assume either that the concepts, through which I bring about this determination, also conform to the object, and then I am once again in the same embarrassment about the way in which I could know anything of it a priori; or I assume that the objects, or, which is the same, the experience in which alone they (as given objects) are cognized, conform to these concepts, whereupon I see at once an easier way out, because experience itself is a kind of cognition that requires understanding, whose rule I must presuppose in myself before objects are given to me, hence a priori, which is expressed in concepts a priori, to which, therefore, all objects of experience must necessarily conform and agree with them. As for objects insofar as they are merely through reason and indeed necessarily thought, but which (at least as reason thinks them) cannot be given in experience at all, the attempts to think them (for think them they must indeed let themselves be) will afterward furnish a splendid touchstone of that which we assume as the altered method of the way of thinking, that we namely cognize of things a priori only what we ourselves put into them.*)",["- richten sich nach = \"conform to\" throughout; Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\" (\"Object der Sinne\" is the Latinate one); Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkenntnißart = \"kind of cognition\".","- Denkart / Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\"; Versuch = \"attempt\"; wissen = \"know\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; Aufgabe = \"problem\"; festsetzen = \"settle\"; ausmachen = \"make out\"; fortkommen = \"make progress\".","- Preserved ambiguities: \"welche in Begriffen a priori ausgedrückt wird\" (antecedent of \"welche\" left open); the dangling participle \"gedacht\" in the last sentence is kept as it stands.","- Line-break marks and stray OCR signs (\"||\", \"|f\", \"ı\", \"as\") omitted. The OCR footnote sign “ is rendered *); the footnote text itself is in krv-b-0034."]],"krv-b-0033":["This attempt succeeds as wished and promises to metaphysics, in its first part, where it namely occupies itself with concepts a priori, whose corresponding objects can be given in experience commensurate with them, the secure course of a science. For after this alteration of the way of thinking one can very well explain the possibility of a cognition a priori and, what is more, provide the laws that a priori lie at the ground of nature, as the sum total of the objects of experience, with their satisfactory proofs, both of which was impossible according to the procedure hitherto. But from this deduction of our faculty of cognizing a priori in the first part of metaphysics there results a strange outcome, and one apparently very detrimental to the whole end of the latter, which occupies the second part, namely that with it we can never get beyond the boundary of possible experience, which is yet precisely the most essential business of this science. But precisely in this lies the experiment of a counter-proof of the truth of the result of that first appraisal of our cognition of reason a priori, that it namely goes only to appearances, but leaves the thing in itself, though as actual for itself, yet uncognized by us. For that which necessarily drives us beyond the boundary of experience and of all appearances",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (continued in krv-b-0034 after the footnote text, with \"hinaus zu gehen treibt\"); left unfinished.","- Sache an sich selbst and Ding an sich selbst are both \"thing in itself\" (Sache is the less Latinate member); Grenze = \"boundary\"; Vernunfterkenntniß = \"cognition of reason\"; Würdigung = \"appraisal\"; wirklich = \"actual\".","- \"a priori\" in \"die Gesetze, welche a priori der Natur ... zum Grunde liegen\" and \"mit ihm\" (= the faculty, or the deduction) are left as ambiguous as the German; Inbegriff = \"sum total\".","- OCR \"moglicher\" corrected to \"möglicher\"."]],"krv-b-0034":["*) This method, imitated from the natural scientist, thus consists in this: to seek the elements of pure reason in that which can be confirmed or refuted through an experiment. Now for the testing of the propositions of pure reason, chiefly if they are ventured beyond all boundary of possible experience, no experiment can be made with their objects (as in natural science): thus it will be feasible only with concepts and principles that we assume a priori, by arranging them namely so that the same objects can be considered, on the one hand, as objects of the senses and of the understanding for experience, but on the other hand yet as objects that one merely thinks, at most for isolated reason striving beyond the boundary of experience, hence from two different sides. If it is now found that, when one considers the things from that double point of view, there takes place agreement with the principle of pure reason, but with a single point of view an unavoidable conflict of reason with itself arises, then the experiment decides for the correctness of that distinction.\n\nto go beyond drives, is the unconditioned, which reason in things in themselves necessarily and with all right demands for everything conditioned, and thereby the series of conditions as completed. If it is now found, if one assumes that our cognition of experience conforms to the objects as things in themselves, that the unconditioned cannot be thought at all without contradiction; but, on the other hand, if one assumes that our representation of things, as they are given to us, does not conform to these as things in themselves, but rather these objects as appearances conform to our way of representing, the contradiction falls away; and that consequently the unconditioned must not be encountered in things insofar as we know them (they are given to us), but certainly in them insofar as we do not know them, as things in themselves: then it is shown that what we assumed at first only by way of an attempt is grounded.*) Now it still always remains for us, after all progress in this field of the supersensible has been denied to speculative reason, to try whether in its practical cognition data are not to be found to determine that transcendent concept of reason of the unconditioned, and in such a way, in accordance with the wish of metaphysics, to get beyond the boundary of all possible experience with our cognition a priori, which is, however, possible only in a practical aim. And with such a procedure speculative reason has still always at least procured room for us for such an expansion, even if it had to leave it empty, and it thus remains not withheld from us, indeed we are even summoned to it by it, to fill it out with practical data of the same, if we can.*)",["- The OCR places the footnote text first, then the continuation of the main sentence begun at the end of krv-b-0033 (\"hinaus zu gehen treibt\"); this order is kept, the continuation beginning mid-sentence. \"über ... hinaus\" is split across the two passages.","- Footnote signs “) in the OCR are rendered *); stray OCR letters (\"N\", \"1\", \"14\", \"a\") dropped; \"dies selben\" read \"dieselben\"; \"wegfallez\" read \"wegfalle\"; \"Überfinn⸗lichen\" read \"Übersinnlichen\"; \"gelan- gen\" joined.","- kennen = \"know\" (Anscheinend contrasted with erkennen = \"cognize\"); Fortkommen = \"progress\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Grenze = \"boundary\" (also in \"Erfahrungsgrenze\"); Gesichtspunkt = \"point of view\"; Vorstellungsart = \"way of representing\"; Sache an sich selbst vs. Ding an sich selbst both \"thing in itself\".","- The syntax of \"Findet sich nun, wenn man annimmt ... der Widerspruch wegfalle; und daß ... so zeigt sich\" is kept as a single sentence with its suspended construction; \"mit unserem, aber nur in praktischer Absicht möglichen Erkenntnisse a priori zu gelangen\" leaves open whether \"a priori\" attaches to the cognition or to the getting."]],"krv-b-0035":["--- notes\n- Only a page number (\"22\", B xxii) stands here; nothing to translate.",[]],"krv-b-0036":["*) Thus the central laws of the motions of the heavenly bodies procured established certainty for what Copernicus assumed at first only as a hypothesis and proved at the same time the invisible force binding the structure of the world (of Newtonian attraction), which would have remained forever undiscovered if the former had not ventured, in a way contrary to sense, yet true, the of the",["- The passage breaks off after \"die der\"; the sentence is left unfinished, and \"the of the\" renders the fragment literally (the German \"die der\" could be a relative or an article followed by a genitive/dative).","- OCR \"New: ionischen\" read \"Newtonischen\"; the garbled \"de qi: erstere\" read \"der erstere\" (\"the former\"), doubtful; stray \"A\" dropped.","- widersinnisch = \"contrary to sense\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Weltbau = \"structure of the world\"; ausgemachte Gewißheit = \"established certainty\"."]],"krv-b-0037":["This experiment of pure reason has much in common with that of the chemists, which they sometimes call the experiment of reduction, but in general the synthetic procedure. The analysis of the metaphysician separated pure cognition *a priori* into two very dissimilar elements, namely | that of things as appearances and then of things in themselves. The dialectic combines both again into unanimity with the necessary idea of reason | of the unconditioned and finds that this unanimity never comes about otherwise than through | that distinction, which is therefore the true one.",["- Einhelligkeit = \"unanimity\" (a rendering chosen for consistency; not in the glossary). Vernunftidee = \"idea of reason\". Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Ding an sich selbst = \"thing in itself\".","- OCR: the opening \")\" (a stray footnote remnant), \"I\" for \"|\" before \"sie\", and the final \"A\" (a signature mark) were dropped or corrected. \"mannigmal\" is rendered \"sometimes\"."]],"krv-b-0038":["In this attempt to alter the previous procedure of metaphysics, and by our undertaking a complete revolution with it after the example of the geometers and investigators of nature, the business of this critique of pure speculative reason now consists. It is a treatise on method, not a system of the science itself; but it nevertheless outlines the whole contour of the latter, both with regard to its boundaries and also the whole inner articulation of it. For this is peculiar to pure speculative reason, that it can and should measure out its own faculty according to the diversity of the way in which it chooses objects for itself to think, and also enumerate completely the manifold ways of setting problems for itself, and so sketch the whole preliminary outline for a system of metaphysics; because, as regards the first, in cognition *a priori* nothing can be attributed to the objects except what the thinking subject takes out of itself, and, as concerns the second, with regard to the principles of cognition it is a quite separated unity subsisting for itself, in which every member exists, as in an organized body, for the sake of all the others and all for the sake of one, and no principle can be taken with certainty in one relation without having at the same time investigated it in the thoroughgoing relation to the whole pure use of reason. But in return metaphysics also has the rare good fortune, which can fall to the lot of no other science of reason that has to do with objects (for logic occupies itself only with the form of thinking in general), that, if it has been brought by this critique onto the secure course of a science, it can fully comprise the whole field of the cognitions belonging to it and so complete its work and lay it down for posterity, for use, as a capital stock that can never be increased, because it has to do merely with principles and the restrictions of its use, which are determined by those themselves. To this completeness it is therefore also bound, as a fundamental science, and of it it must be possible to say: *nil actum reputans, si quid superesset | agendum*.\n\n…observed motions not in the objects of the heavens but in their spectator. I put forward in this preface the alteration of the way of thinking set out in the Critique, analogous to that hypothesis, also only as a hypothesis, although in the treatise itself it is proved, not hypothetically but apodictically, from the constitution of our representations of space and time and from the elementary concepts of the understanding, only in order to make noticeable the first attempts at such an alteration, which are always hypothetical.",["- OCR: the passage is interrupted mid-sentence (\"und obachteten Bewegungen…\") by footnote text from the foot of the page. I restored the main sentence (\"und von ihr muß gesagt werden können\") and set the footnote apart after it. The footnote begins mid-word (\"obachteten\", i.e. [be]obachteten), so its opening is a fragment and I have not completed it.","- Umriß = \"contour\"; Vorriß = \"preliminary outline\"; Grenzen = \"boundaries\"; Einschränkungen = \"restrictions\"; Hauptstuhl = \"capital stock\" (an old form of Hauptstock; doubtful word, kept as read).","- Tractat and Abhandlung are both \"treatise\"; Naturforscher = \"investigators of nature\"; Denkart = \"way of thinking\"; Umänderung = \"alteration\"; Art in \"Verschiedenheit der Art\" and \"mancherlei Arten\" = \"way/ways\".","- Ambiguities kept: \"ihr eigen Vermögen … ausmessen\" with \"sich Objecte zum Denken wählt\" (reflexive \"sich\" = \"for itself\"); \"jene\" in \"durch jene selbst bestimmt\" refers back to \"Principien\" (as I read it), but the German leaves the antecedent open. \"das erste / das zweite\" are left as \"the first / the second\"."]],"krv-b-0039":["But what then is that, one will ask, for a treasure that we intend to leave to posterity with such a metaphysics, purified through critique, but thereby also brought into a persistent state? One will believe, on a cursory overview of this work, to perceive that its utility is only negative, namely never to venture with speculative reason beyond the boundary of experience, and that is also in fact its first utility. This, however, becomes at once positive when one becomes aware that the principles with which speculative reason ventures beyond its boundary in fact have as their unavoidable result, not extension, but, if one considers them more closely, narrowing of our use of reason, inasmuch as they really threaten to extend the boundaries of sensibility, to which they properly belong, over everything, and so to supplant altogether the pure (practical) use of reason. Hence a critique that limits the former is to that extent indeed negative, but, inasmuch as it thereby at the same time removes an obstacle that limits the latter use or even threatens to annihilate it, is in fact of positive and very important utility, as soon as one is convinced that there is an absolutely necessary practical use of pure reason (the moral), in which it unavoidably extends itself beyond the boundaries of sensibility, for which it indeed needs no assistance from the speculative, yet nevertheless must be secured against its counteraction, in order not to fall into contradiction with itself. To deny this service of critique its positive utility would be as much as to say that the police produces no positive utility, because its chief business is after all only to bolt a bar against the violence that citizens have to fear from citizens, so that each can pursue his affairs peacefully and securely. That space and time are only forms of sensible intuition, thus only conditions of the existence of things as appearances, that we further have no concepts of the understanding, hence also no elements for the cognition of things, except insofar as an intuition corresponding to these concepts can be given, consequently that we can have cognition of no object as a thing in itself, but only insofar as it is an object of sensible intuition, i.e., as appearance, is proved in the analytic part of the Critique; from which then of course follows the restriction of all merely possible speculative cognition of reason to mere objects of experience. Nevertheless, which must be well noted, it is yet always reserved in this that we must be able, even if not to cognize, yet at least to think, the very same objects also as things in themselves.¹ For otherwise the absurd proposition would follow from it that there would be appearance without something that appears there. Now let us assume that the distinction, made necessary by our critique, of things as objects of experience from the very same as things in themselves had not been made at all, then the principle of causality, and hence the natural mechanism, in determination of them would have to hold throughout of all things in general as efficient causes. Of the very same being, therefore, e.g., the human soul, I would not be able to say that its will is free, and that it is nevertheless at the same time subject to natural necessity, i.e., not free, without falling into an obvious contradiction, because in both propositions I have taken the soul in just the same meaning, namely as a thing in general (as a thing in itself), and without preceding critique also could not take it otherwise. But if the critique has not erred,",["- OCR corrected silently: Übersieht → Übersicht; Der- | engung → Verengung; ges hören → gehören; Vernunft: 1) gebrauch → Vernunftgebrauch; den? noch → dennoch; pofi- 3; tiven → positiven; Gewalt⸗ j | thätigfeit → Gewaltthätigkeit; Ein⸗ | schränkung; er— kennen → erkennen; An- s | schauung. Line numbers and stray marks omitted.","- \"¹\" stands for the footnote marker (printed \"*)\" in the source); the footnote text is not in the passage supplied.","- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; Erweiterung and (the verb) erweitern = \"extension\" / \"extend\"; Grenze = \"boundary\", Einschränkung = \"restriction\", einschränken = \"limit\"; Nutzen = \"utility\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Dasein/Existenz: here Existenz = \"existence\".","- wirkenden Ursachen = \"efficient causes\" (wirken, \"to act/work\"); Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Sache = \"thing\" (Ding likewise \"thing\").","- Ambiguity kept: \"von ihr / der letzteren / dem Letzteren\" references (erstere, letzteren, ihre Gegenwirkung) are left as the German has them; \"ihre Gegenwirkung\" is rendered \"its counteraction\", with \"its\" resolved as in the German, i.e. not specified.","- \"Gleichwohl wird, welches wohl gemerkt werden muß, …\" kept with the parenthetical clause in place."]],"krv-b-0040":["since it teaches to take the object in a twofold meaning, namely as appearance or as thing in itself; if the deduction of its concepts of the understanding is correct, hence also the principle of causality applies only to things taken in the first sense, namely insofar as they are objects of experience, but the very same, according to the second meaning, are not subject to it: then the very same will is in the appearance (the visible actions) necessarily in conformity with the natural law and to that extent not free, and yet on the other hand, as belonging to a thing in itself, not subject to that, hence thought as free, without a contradiction occurring in this. Although I now my soul, of which",["- The passage opens with \"a sie\" (initial letter lost in OCR); I read \"da sie\" (\"since it\") but this is a conjecture. Other OCR corrections: Bertandesbegriffe → Verstandesbegriffe; \"us Dinge\" → \"[nur a]uf Dinge\"; er Erfahrung → der Erfahrung; \"ngehörig\" → angehörig; (den sichtbaren Handlungen) given its opening bracket. \"nur us Dinge\" is rendered \"only to things\", reading \"nur auf Dinge\".","- The passage breaks off mid-clause (\"Ob ich nun gleich meine Seele, von der\"); left unfinished. \"Ob … gleich\" = \"although\".","- Ambiguity kept: \"ihm\" (\"not subject to it/that\") and \"jenem\" refer back to the principle of causality / the natural law as in the German; \"eben dieselbe\" (feminine, apparently for \"Dinge\") is rendered \"the very same\" without resolving the noun.","- Deduction = \"deduction\"; Verstandesbegriffe = \"concepts of the understanding\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Naturgesetz = \"natural law\"."]],"krv-b-0041":[") To cognize an object, it is required that I be able to prove its possibility (whether according to the testimony of experience from its actuality, or *a priori* through reason). But I can think whatever I like, if only I do not contradict myself, i.e., if my concept is only a possible thought, although I cannot vouch for whether, in the sum total of all possibilities, an object corresponds to it or not. But in order to attribute objective validity (real possibility, for the former was merely the logical) to such a concept, something more is required. This more, however, does not just have to be sought in theoretical sources of cognition, it can also lie in practical ones. [lacuna] considered from the latter side, by no speculative reason (still less by empirical observation), and hence also cannot cognize freedom as a property of a being to which I ascribe effects in the world of sense, because I would have to cognize such a being as determined in its existence and yet not in time (which, because I cannot place any intuition beneath my concept, is impossible): so I can nevertheless think freedom, i.e., the representation of it at least contains no contradiction in itself, if our critical distinction of both 1 (the sensible and intellectual) kinds of representation and the limitation of the pure concepts of the understanding resulting from it, and hence also of the 10| principles flowing from them, takes place. Suppose now that morality necessarily | presupposes freedom (in the strictest sense) as a property of our will | in that it adduces practical, original principles lying in our reason as data of it *a priori*, which would be absolutely impossible without the presupposition of freedom, but speculative reason had proved that this cannot be thought at all: then that presupposition, namely the moral one, must necessarily yield to the one whose opposite contains a manifest contradiction, and consequently freedom and with it morality (for its opposite contains no contradiction, if freedom is not already presupposed) must make room for the natural mechanism. But since for morality I need nothing further than that freedom not contradict itself, and thus that it at least can be thought, without having to have insight into it further, so that it thus lays no obstacle in the way of the natural mechanism of the very same action (taken in another relation): so the doctrine of morality asserts its place and the doctrine of nature also its own, which, however, would not have taken place if critique had not previously taught us of our unavoidable ignorance with regard to things in themselves and limited everything that we can cognize theoretically to mere appearances. This very exposition of the positive utility of the critical principles of pure reason can be shown with regard to the concept of God and of the simple nature of our soul, which, however, I pass over for the sake of brevity. XXX I cannot therefore even assume God, freedom and immortality for the sake of the necessary practical use of my reason, if I do not at the same time deprive speculative reason of its pretension to extravagant insights, because in order to attain to these it must make use of such principles which, since they in fact reach only to objects of possible experience, if they are nonetheless applied to what cannot be an object of experience, actually always transform this into appearance and so declare all practical extension of pure reason to be impossible. I therefore had to deny knowledge in order to make room for belief, and the dogmatism of metaphysics, i.e., the prejudice of getting on in it without a critique of pure reason, is the true source of all unbelief conflicting with morality, which is always very dogmatic. — If it therefore cannot be so very hard to leave a bequest to posterity in the form of a systematic metaphysics composed according to the standard of the critique of pure reason, then this is no gift to be esteemed lightly; whether one looks merely to the culture of reason through the secure course of a science in general, in comparison with its groundless groping and frivolous roaming about without critique, or also to a better use of time by an inquisitive youth, who with ordinary dogmatism receives so early and so much encouragement to rationalize comfortably about things of which it understands nothing, and in which, like no one in the world, it will also never gain any insight, or even to set out on the invention of new thoughts and opinions and so to neglect the learning of thorough sciences; but most of all, if one takes into account the inestimable advantage of putting an end for all future time, in the Socratic way, namely through the clearest proof of the ignorance of the opponents, to all objections against morality and religion. For some metaphysics or other has always been in the world and will also presumably continue to be, but with it also a dialectic of pure reason, because it is natural to it, will be encountered in it. It is therefore the first and most important concern of philosophy, once and for all to deprive it of all disadvantageous influence by stopping up the source of errors.",["- The OCR text is corrupt at \"letzteren Seite betrachtet, durch keine speculative Vernunft…\": the opening of the sentence (the \"Wenn\" clause and its subject/verb, presumably \"ich… nicht erkennen kann\") is missing; I marked it [lacuna] and did not supply it. The grammar of \"und hence also cannot cognize\" is left as it stands in the damaged text.","- Likewise \"[Zeile] bessere e einer wißbegierigen Jugend\" is damaged (a word such as \"Verwendung der Zeit\" is lost); rendered \"a better use of time\" is a guess to give sense and is doubtful.","- Other OCR slips silently corrected: Möglicheit, Geanke, bjective, beiulegen, ftrengften, Radkommenschaft (Nachkommenschaft), Unsterblichke it, überschwenglicher, etc.; the stray \"1\" before \"(der sinnlichen…)\" is a footnote or line marker in the OCR, kept as it stands.","- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; Wirklichkeit \"actuality\"; Wissen \"knowledge\", Erkenntnis \"cognition\"; erkennen \"cognize\"; Glaube \"belief\"; Erscheinung \"appearance\"; Sinnenwelt \"world of sense\"; Naturmechanism \"natural mechanism\"; Sittlichkeit and Moralität both \"morality\".","- \"Inbegriffe aller Möglichkeiten\" = \"sum total of all possibilities\"; \"Behuf\" = \"sake\"; \"Erörterung\" = \"exposition\" (expositio); \"belehrt\" = \"taught\" (Belehrung = teaching).","- \"dem Naturmechanism den Platz einräumen\" is rendered \"make room for\"; the subject \"Freiheit und … Sittlichkeit\" is as in the German. \"vernünfteln\" = \"rationalize\" per glossary.","- Ambiguity preserved: \"daß sie also dem Naturmechanism … gar kein Hinderniß … lege\" — \"sie\" can refer to freedom; \"ihr\" in \"ihr dadurch… Einfluß zu benehmen\" refers to metaphysics/dialectic as in the German (\"it\"). The \"| \" and \"||\" signs are carried over as they stand."]],"krv-b-0042":["With this important alteration in the field of the sciences and the loss that speculative reason must suffer in its previously imagined possession, everything nevertheless remains, with regard to the universal human concern and the use that the world has hitherto drawn from the doctrines of pure reason, in the same advantageous state as it ever was, and the loss strikes only the monopoly of the schools, but by no means the interest of human beings. I ask the most inflexible dogmatist whether the proof of the continuance of our soul after death from the simplicity of the substance, whether that of the freedom of the will against universal mechanism through the subtle, though impotent, distinctions of subjective and objective practical necessity, or whether that of the existence of God from the concept of a most real being (from the contingency of the changeable and the necessity of a first mover), after they proceeded from the schools, have ever been able to reach the public and to have the slightest influence on its conviction? If this has now not happened, and, because of the unsuitability of common human understanding for such subtle speculation, can also never be expected; if rather, as regards the first, the predisposition of his nature, noticeable to every human being, never to be able to be satisfied through what is temporal (as insufficient for the predispositions of his whole vocation), the hope of a future life, with regard to the second the mere clear presentation of duties in opposition to all claims of the inclinations the consciousness of freedom, and finally, as regards the third, the magnificent order, beauty and providence that look forth everywhere in nature, alone the belief in a wise and great author of the world, the conviction spreading to the public, so far as it rests on grounds of reason, has had to effect quite alone: then not only does this possession remain undisturbed, but it rather gains in standing thereby that the | schools are henceforth taught not to presume to a higher and more extended insight in a point that concerns the universal human concern than that to which the great multitude (most worthy of respect for us) can just as easily attain, and thus to restrict themselves to the cultivation of these universally comprehensible grounds of proof, sufficient in a moral aim, alone. The alteration therefore concerns merely the arrogant claims of the schools, which would gladly (as otherwise rightly in many other matters) let themselves be held in this for the sole knowers and preservers of such truths, of which they communicate to the public only the use, but keep the key to them for themselves (*quod mecum nescit, solus vult scire videri*). Nevertheless provision is also made for a fairer claim of the speculative philosopher. He always remains exclusively the depositary of a science useful to the public without its knowledge, namely the critique of reason; for this can never become popular, but also has no need to be, because, as little as the finely spun arguments for useful truths will go into the people's heads, just as little do the equally subtle objections against them ever come into their sense; on the other hand, because the school, like every human being who raises himself to speculation, unavoidably falls into both, the former is obligated, through a thorough investigation of the rights of speculative reason, to forestall once and for all the scandal that sooner or later must break out even to the people from the disputes in which metaphysicians (and as such finally also clergy) inevitably entangle themselves without critique, and which afterwards even falsify their doctrines. Through this alone can the root be cut off from materialism, fatalism, atheism, free-thinking unbelief, fanaticism and superstition, which can become universally harmful, finally also from idealism and skepticism, which are more dangerous to the schools and can hardly pass over into the public. If governments find it good to occupy themselves with the affairs of the scholars, it would be far more fitting to their wise provision for sciences as well as for human beings to favor the freedom of such a critique, through which alone the elaborations of reason can be brought to a firm footing, than to support the ridiculous despotism of the schools, which raise a loud cry over public danger when one tears apart their cobwebs, of which the public has never taken notice, and whose loss it therefore can never feel.",["- OCR slips silently corrected: \"Schul en\" → Schulen; \"derVerlusttrifft\" → der Verlust trifft; \"allerrealiten\" → allerrealsten; \"Pflich⸗ten\"; \"sein gesponnenen\" → fein gesponnenen. Line numbers and stray marks omitted.","- \"ausstoßen\" is printed in the OCR (literally \"thrust out\"); I suspect \"ausbrechen\" (\"break out\"), which I have translated; reading doubtful.","- The *quod mecum nescit, solus vult scire videri* is left in Latin as in the text; roughly \"what he does not know along with me, he alone wishes to seem to know.\"","- Bestimmung = \"vocation\" (glossary also gives \"determination\"); Absicht = \"aim\"; Einsicht = \"insight\"; Angelegenheit = \"concern\" (both occurrences); Interesse = \"interest\".","- \"in den Sinn kommen\" rendered \"come into their sense\" (Sinn = sense); Glaube = \"belief\"; Überzeugung = \"conviction\"; Schwärmerei = \"fanaticism\"; Geistliche = \"clergy\".","- The long \"hat vielmehr … bewirken müssen\" sentence keeps the German's unstated grammatical subjects: the several noun phrases are not assigned to particular verbs, and \"den Glauben\" and \"die … Überzeugung\" stand in apposition-like sequence as in the original.","- Kenner = \"knowers\" (from kennen); \"dem Publicum\" = \"the public\"; \"Schule/Schulen\" = \"school(s)\", with \"jene\" = \"the former\"."]],"krv-b-0043":["Critique is not opposed to the dogmatic procedure of reason in its pure cognition, as science (for this must always be dogmatic, i.e., strictly proving from secure principles a priori), but rather to dogmatism, i.e., the presumption of getting on solely with a pure cognition from concepts (the philosophical) according to principles, such as reason has long been in the use of, without inquiry into the way and the right with which it has arrived at it. Dogmatism is therefore the dogmatic procedure of pure reason without prior critique of its own faculty. This opposition shall therefore not speak the word for garrulous shallowness under the presumed name of popularity, or indeed for skepticism, which makes short work of the whole of metaphysics; rather critique is the necessary preliminary arrangement for the promotion of a thorough metaphysics as science, which must necessarily be carried out dogmatically and, according to the strictest demand, systematically, hence in accordance with the schools (not popularly), for this demand upon it, since it pledges itself to execute its business entirely a priori, hence to the full satisfaction of speculative reason, is indispensable. In | the execution, then, of the plan that critique prescribes, i.e., in the future system of metaphysics, we must someday follow the strict method of the famous Wolff, the greatest among all dogmatic philosophers, who first gave the example (and through this example became the originator of the spirit of thoroughness in Germany, not yet extinguished up to now), of how, through lawful establishment of principles, distinct determination of concepts, attempted strictness of proofs, prevention of bold leaps in consequences, the secure course of a science is to be taken, who was also for that very reason preeminently suited to place such a one as metaphysics is into this state, if it had occurred to him to prepare the field beforehand through critique of the organ, namely of pure reason itself: a lack that is to be ascribed not so much to him as rather to the | dogmatic mode of thinking of his age, and about which the philosophers of his as well as of all previous times have nothing to reproach one another. Those who reject his way of teaching and yet at the same time also the procedure of the critique of pure reason can have nothing else in mind than to throw | off the fetters of science altogether, to transform work into play, certainty into opinion and philosophy into philodoxy.",["- OCR slips silently corrected: \"n ihrem\" → in ihrem; \"Prinscipien\"; \"fol\" → soll; \"ich anheischig\" → sich anheischig; \"fünf- sit tigen\" → künftigen; \"Philodorie\" → Philodoxie (reading assumed).","- The page-break marks fall inside German words (\"dog- | matischen\", \"abzu— | werfen\"); the | is carried over at the corresponding place in the English.","- Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; Art = \"way\"; Begriffe = \"concepts\"; Principien = \"principles\"; Beweis- = \"proof(s)\"; Folgerungen = \"consequences\"; Meinung = \"opinion\"; Gewißheit = \"certainty\".","- \"Dogmatism\" = Dogmatism (Latinate form, distinct from \"dogmatische Verfahren\"); \"a priori\" is printed in Latin in the original and left in italics-free form here as in the glossary-style usage; \"strenge beweisend\" is left attached to \"dogmatisch\" as in German.","- \"womit sie dazu gelangt ist\": \"dazu\" has no clear antecedent (the pure cognition / the use of principles); rendered \"to it\" to keep the vagueness.","- \"der auch eben darum … geschickt war\": the relative \"der\" may refer to Wolff or to \"der sichere Gang\"; English \"who\" forces the first reading.","- \"schulgerecht\" = \"in accordance with the schools\"; \"das Wort reden\" = \"speak the word for\"; \"nichts … vorzuwerfen haben\" = \"have nothing to reproach\"."]],"krv-b-0044":["*) The only thing I could call a genuine increase, though only in the way of proof, is what I have made through a new refutation of psychological idealism and a strict (as I believe, also the only possible) proof of the objective reality of outer intuition, p. 275. However innocent idealism may be held to be with regard to the essential ends of metaphysics (which in fact it is not), it always remains a scandal of philosophy and of universal human reason to have to assume the existence of things outside us (from which we after all have the entire material for cognitions even for our inner sense) merely on belief, and, if it occurs to anyone to doubt it, to be unable to oppose to him any satisfactory proof. Because some obscurity is to be found in the expressions of the proof from the third line to the sixth!), I request that this period be altered thus: \"But this persisting thing cannot be an intuition in me. For all grounds of determination of my existence that can be encountered in me are representations and, as such, themselves require something persisting that is distinct from them, in relation to which their change, and hence my existence in the time in which they change, could be determined.\" One will presumably say against this proof: I am after all immediately conscious only of that which is in me, i.e., of my representation of outer things; con-| 1) This persisting thing ... can be.",["- Eigentliche Vermehrung: \"genuine increase\"; \"Vermehrung\" rendered \"increase\"; \"Beweisart\" = \"way of proof\".","- \"Idealism\"/\"Idealisms\": German \"Idealism(s)\", rendered \"idealism\".","- \"Dasein\" = \"existence\"; \"Anschauung\" = \"intuition\"; \"Realität\" = \"reality\"; \"Stoff\" = \"material\"; \"Beharrliche\" = \"persisting thing\" (cf. Beharrlichkeit = persistence).","- \"Bestimmungsgründe\" = \"grounds of determination\" (Bestimmung = determination); \"bestimmt werden könne\" = \"could be determined\".","- \"Glauben\" = \"belief\"; \"Vorstellungen\" = \"representations\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"cognitions\"; \"Zwecke\" = \"ends\".","- OCR corrections: \"das\" (for \"daß\") twice, \"Beharr— liebe\" read as \"Beharrliche\" (hyphenated line break), \"iud\" as \"sind\", \"unterchiedenes\" as \"unterschiedenes\", \"Periode\" kept as \"period\" (text has \"Period\"). The \"sechsten!)\" is as in the OCR, apparently the footnote marker \"1)\"; the footnote text at the end is abbreviated by ellipsis as printed.","- \"Skandal\" rendered \"scandal\"; \"Menschenvernunft\" = \"human reason\"; \"genugthuenden\" = \"satisfactory\". The sentence breaks off at \"unmittelbar be-\" (\"immediately conscious\"/\"be-\") and the passage ends mid-word as given; \"con-\" in the translation renders the break of \"bewußt\" and is not completed."]],"krv-b-0045":["As for this second edition, I have, as is fair, not wanted to let its opportunity pass by, in order to remedy as far as possible the difficulties and the obscurity from which many misinterpretations may have arisen, which have befallen acute men, perhaps not without my fault, in the judgment of this book. In the propositions themselves and their grounds of proof, likewise in the form as well as the completeness of the plan, I have found nothing to alter; which is to be ascribed partly to the long examination to which I had subjected them before I laid it before the public, partly to the constitution of the matter itself, namely the nature of a pure speculative reason, which contains a true articulated structure, in which everything is organ, namely everything for the sake of one and each individual thing for the sake of all, hence every frailty, however small, whether it be a fault (error) or a deficiency, must unfailingly betray itself in use. In this unalterability this system will, as I hope, also maintain itself henceforth. Not self-conceit, but merely the evidence which the experiment of the equality of the result effects, in proceeding from the smallest elements to the whole of pure reason and in going back from the whole (for this too is given for itself through the final aim of the same in the practical) to each part, in that the attempt to alter even the smallest part immediately brings about contradictions not merely of the system but of universal human reason, entitles me to this confidence. But in the presentation there is still much to do, and in this I have attempted improvements with this edition, which are to remedy partly the misunderstanding of the Aesthetic, chiefly that in the concept of time, partly the obscurity of the deduction of the concepts of the understanding, partly the supposed lack of a sufficient evidence in the proofs of the principles of the pure understanding, partly finally the misinterpretation of the paralogisms charged against rational psychology. Up to here (namely only to the end of the first chapter of the transcendental dialectic) and no further extend my alterations of the mode of presentation,*) because the time was too short and in regard to the rest no misunderstanding on the part of knowledgeable and impartial examiners had come to me, who, even without my being able to name them with the praise due to them, will find the regard I have taken of their reminders already of itself in its places. With this improvement, however, a small loss is connected for the reader, which could not be prevented without making the book far too voluminous, namely that various things, which indeed do not essentially belong to the completeness of the whole, but which many a reader would nevertheless be unwilling to do without, since they can otherwise be useful in another aim, have had to be omitted or presented in abbreviated form, in order to make room for my presentation, now, as I hope, more comprehensible, which at bottom, with regard to the propositions and even their grounds of proof, alters absolutely nothing, but does nevertheless depart here and there so far from the previous one in the method of exposition that it could not be accomplished by insertions. This small loss, which in any case can be made good according to each person's liking by comparison with the first edition, will, as I hope, be more than compensated by the greater comprehensibility. I have noticed with grateful pleasure in various public writings (partly on the occasion of reviews of many books, partly in particular treatises) that the spirit of thoroughness has not died out in Germany, but has only been drowned out for a short time by the fashionable tone of a geniuslike freedom in thinking, and that the thorny paths of the critique, which lead to a science of pure reason that is in accord with the schools, but as such alone enduring and therefore highly necessary, have not hindered courageous and bright minds from mastering them. To these deserving men, who so happily combine with thoroughness of insight the talent of a luminous presentation (of which I am not exactly conscious), I leave it to complete my treatment, here and there perhaps still deficient with regard to the latter; for to be refuted is in this case no danger, but certainly not to be understood. For my part I cannot from now on engage in disputes, although I will carefully heed all hints, whether from friends or opponents, in order to use them in accordance with this propaedeutic in the future execution of the system. Since during these labors I have already advanced rather deep into old age (this month into my sixty-fourth year), I must, if I want to carry out my plan of delivering the metaphysics of nature as well as of morals, as confirmation of the correctness of the critique of speculative as well as practical reason, proceed sparingly with time and expect the clarification of the obscurities, in this work at the outset scarcely avoidable, as well as the defense of the whole, from the deserving men who have made it their own. In individual passages every philosophical exposition can be nipped at (for it cannot appear so armored as the mathematical), while the articulated structure of the system, considered as unity, runs not the least danger thereby, for the survey of which, when it is new, only few possess the agility of mind, still fewer however, because all innovation is inconvenient to them, the desire. Even apparent contradictions can, if one compares individual passages, torn from their interconnection, with one another, be picked out in any writing proceeding chiefly as free speech, which in the eyes of one who relies on others' judg-\n\n*) [...] conscious; consequently it would always remain undecided whether something corresponding to it exists outside me, or not. But I am conscious of my existence in time (consequently also of the determinability of the same in it) through inner experience, and this is more than merely being conscious of my representation, yet it is one and the same with the empirical consciousness of my existence, which is determinable only through relation to something that, combined with my existence, is outside me. This consciousness of my existence in time is therefore identically combined with the consciousness of a relation to something outside me, and it is therefore experience and not fiction, sense and not imagination, that inseparably connects the outer with my inner sense; for outer sense is already in itself relation of intuition to something actual outside me, and the reality of it, as distinguished from imagination, rests only on this, that it is inseparably combined with inner experience itself, as the condition of the possibility of the same, which happens here. If with the intellectual consciousness of my existence in the representation I am, which accompanies all my judgments and acts of the understanding, I could at the same time combine a determination of my existence through intellectual intuition, then the consciousness of a relation to something outside me would not necessarily belong to it. But now, since that intellectual consciousness indeed precedes, but the inner intuition, in which alone my existence can be determined, is sensible and bound to the condition of time, this determination, however, hence inner experience itself, depends on something persistent, which is not in me, consequently only on something outside me, in relation to which I must consider myself: so the reality of outer sense is necessarily combined with that of inner sense for the possibility of an experience in general: i.e. I am just as certainly conscious that there are things outside me that relate to my sense, as I am conscious that I myself exist determined in time. But to which given intuitions objects outside me actually correspond, and which therefore belong to outer sense, to which they and not to the imagination are to be ascribed, must be settled in each particular case according to the rules by which experience in general (even inner) is distinguished from imagination, whereby the proposition that there actually is outer experience always lies at the ground. To this one can still add the remark: the representation of something persistent in existence is not one and the same with the persistent representation; for this can be very changeable and alternating, like all our representations and even those of matter, and yet it is related to something persistent, which must therefore be a thing distinct from all my representations and outer, whose existence is necessarily included in the determination of my own existence and with it constitutes only a single experience, which would not even take place inwardly if it were not (in part) at the same time outer. The How? can here be explained no further than how we in general think what stands in time, whose being simultaneous with the alternating produces the concept of change.",["- The passage is an OCR jumble: the text of the footnote (marked *) and of the main text are interleaved. I have reassembled them in the sequence the fragments evidently join (\"mit dem ihnen\" / \"gebührenden Lobe\"; \"wenn ich meinen\" / \"Plan\"; \"Das Wie? läßt sich hier eben so\" / \"wenig weiter erklären\"). The footnote's beginning is missing in the source (\"wußt; folglich…\"), hence \"[...]\"; the main text breaks off at \"Beur-\" (Beurtheilung).","- The stray \"[\" before \"aber doch in der Methode\" in the OCR is treated as an artifact and omitted; no closing bracket appears.","- Dasein and Existenz are both \"existence\" (Dasein, \"existence\"; Existenz, \"existence\"; existire, \"exist\"). Einbildung and Einbildungskraft are both \"imagination\"; Erdichtung = \"fiction\".","- Beurtheilung = \"judgment\" (glossary: Beurteilung); Urtheile = \"judgments\"; Mißdeutung = \"misinterpretation\"; Mißverstand = \"misunderstanding\"; ändern/Abänderung = \"alter/alteration\"; Veränderung = \"change\".","- Sätze = \"propositions\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\"; Beweisgründe = \"grounds of proof\"; Beweise = \"proofs\"; Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Gliederbau = \"articulated structure\"; Zugleichsein = \"being simultaneous\".","- \"Verhältnis\" and \"Relation\" are both \"relation\". Verstandeshandlungen = \"acts of the understanding\". \"Vorgerückten\" (with Paralogismen) = \"charged against\"; the sense \"reproached to\" is also possible.","- \"Dieses\" in \"dieses ist mehr\" and the attachment of \"(zum Theil)\" are left as in the German. \"Darstellung\" = \"presentation\", \"Vortrag\" = \"exposition\"."]],"krv-b-0046":["…theilung abandons, cast an unfavorable light on these, but for him who has taken possession of the idea in the whole are very easy to resolve. Meanwhile, if a theory has subsistence in itself, then effect and counter-effect, which at first threatened it with great danger, serve in time only to wear off its unevennesses and, if men of impartiality, insight and true popularity occupy themselves with it, to provide it in a short time also with the requisite elegance. Königsberg, in the month of April 1787.",["The opening letters (\"a m öl mü\") are OCR residue and are left untranslated; \"theilung\" is a word-fragment (the prefix is lost) and is left as it stands. \"Wirkung und Gegenwirkung\" = \"effect and counter-effect\" (Wirkung = effect). \"auf diese\" could be singular or plural in the German; \"these\" is a choice, since the antecedent is lost. \"in si[ch]\" is restored from \"in si\". The final \"i\" is OCR residue and omitted."]],"krv-b-0047":["en",["OCR residue only; no translatable text."]],"krv-b-0048":["re",["OCR residue only; no translatable text."]],"krv-b-0049":["a un ac en P En —",["OCR residue only; no translatable text."]],"krv-b-0050":["Introduction.\nOn the Difference between Pure and Empirical Cognition.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; rein = pure."]],"krv-b-0051":["That all our cognition begins with experience, about this there is no doubt at all; for how else should the faculty of cognition be awakened to exercise, if it did not happen through objects that stir our senses and partly produce representations by themselves, partly bring the activity of our understanding into motion to compare these, to connect or to separate them, and thus to work up the raw material of sensible impressions into a cognition of objects that is called experience? In temporal order, therefore, no cognition in us precedes experience, and with this all begins.",["Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; Verstandesthätigkeit = activity of the understanding; verknüpfen = connect; Stoff = material; Eindrücke = impressions; Gegenstände = objects.","\"diese zu vergleichen\": \"these\" (diese) is ambiguous between the representations and other possible antecedents; kept as in the German. \"mit dieser\" likewise refers to \"Erfahrung\" as the nearest feminine noun."]],"krv-b-0052":["But if all our cognition starts with experience, it does not on that account all arise from experience. For it could well be that even our cognition of experience is a composite of that which we receive through impressions and that which our own faculty of cognition (merely occasioned by sensible impressions) supplies from itself, which addition we do not distinguish from that basic material until long practice has made us attentive to it and skilled in separating it.",["anhebt = starts; entspringt = arises. Erfahrungserkenntniß = cognition of experience (literally, \"experience-cognition\"). Zusatz = addition; Grundstoff = basic material.","\"alle\" in \"nicht eben alle\" could be read as adverbial or as pronoun-subject; kept as \"all\"."]],"krv-b-0053":["It is thus at least a question requiring closer investigation, and not to be dismissed at first glance: whether there is such a cognition independent of experience and even of all impressions of the senses. One calls such cognitions *a priori*, and distinguishes them from the empirical ones, which have their sources *a posteriori*, namely in experience.",["The colon before \"whether\" is added to follow the German \"Frage: ob\". Line number \"5\" in the source is omitted. The word *a poste-riori* is rejoined across the line break."]],"krv-b-0054":["That expression is, however, still not determinate enough to designate adequately the whole sense of the question put forward. For one is accustomed to say of some cognition derived from sources of experience that we are capable of it or partake of it *a priori*, because we derive it not immediately from experience, but from a general rule, which we have nonetheless ourselves borrowed from experience. Thus one says of someone who undermined the foundation of his house: he could have known *a priori* that it would collapse, i.e., he need not have waited for the experience of its actually collapsing. Yet he could not have known this entirely *a priori* either. For that bodies are heavy and therefore fall when their support is withdrawn, had to be made known to him beforehand through experience.",["bestimmt = determinate; fähig = capable; wissen = know (rendered \"known\"); bekannt werden = be made known. Line numbers omitted; \"1s\" is OCR for \"15\"."]],"krv-b-0055":["Hence in what follows we will understand by cognitions *a priori* not those that occur independently of this or that experience, but those that occur absolutely independently of all experience. Opposed to them are empirical cognitions or those that are possible only *a posteriori*, i.e., through experience. Of the cognitions *a priori*, however, those are called pure in which nothing empirical is mixed. Thus, e.g., the proposition: every alteration has its cause, is a proposition *a priori*, but not pure, because alteration is a concept that can be drawn only from experience.",["Veränderung = alteration; Ursache = cause; Satz = proposition; Begriff = concept; rein = pure. \"schlechterdings\" = absolutely. Line number \"25\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0056":["II.",[]],"krv-b-0057":["We are in possession of certain cognitions *a priori*, and even",["The sentence continues in the next passage (krv-b-0058)."]],"krv-b-0058":["the common understanding is never without such.",["gemein = common; Verstand = understanding."]],"krv-b-0059":["Here what matters is a mark by which we can securely distinguish a pure cognition from empirical ones. Experience teaches us, to be sure, that something is constituted thus or so, but not that it could not be otherwise. If, therefore, first, a proposition is found that is thought together with its necessity, then it is a judgment *a priori*; if it is, moreover, also derived from none except one that itself in turn is valid as a necessary proposition, then it is absolutely *a priori*. Second: experience never gives its judgments true or strict, but only assumed and comparative universality (through induction), so that it must properly be said: as far as we have hitherto perceived, there is found no exception to this or that rule. If, therefore, a judgment is thought with strict universality, i.e., in such a way that no exception at all is allowed as possible, then it is not derived from experience, but absolutely valid *a priori*. Empirical universality is therefore only an arbitrary increase in validity from that which holds in most cases to that which holds in all, as, e.g., in the proposition: all bodies are heavy; where, on the contrary, strict universality belongs essentially to a judgment, this points to a special source of cognition for it, namely a faculty of cognition *a priori*. Necessity and strict universality are therefore sure marks of a cognition *a priori* and also belong inseparably to one another. But because in the use of them it is sometimes easier to show the empirical limitedness of them than the contingency in the judgments, or it is also at times more evident to show the unlimited universality that we attribute to a judgment than its necessity, it is advisable to make use of the two criteria mentioned, each of which is infallible by itself, separately.",["Merkmal = mark (nota), Kennzeichen = mark: one English word for two German words. Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Zufälligkeit = contingency; Allgemeinheit = universality; Gültigkeit = validity; Kriterien = criteria; Vermögen = faculty.","\"Algemeinheit\" and \"Erkenntnißgquell\" are OCR slips, silently corrected. \"von keinem abgeleitet, als der…\" is kept close to the German. \"derselben\" (twice) in the long final sentence is ambiguous in its reference; kept as \"of them\" / \"its\"."]],"krv-b-0060":["That there actually are such necessary and, in the strictest sense, universal, hence pure judgments *a priori* in human cognition is easy to show. If one wants an example from the sciences, one need only look to all the propositions of mathematics; if one wants one from the commonest use of the understanding, the proposition that all alteration must have a cause can serve for this; indeed in the latter the very concept of a cause so manifestly contains the concept of a necessity of connection with an effect and of a strict universality of the rule that it would be entirely lost if one wanted to derive it, as Hume did, from a frequent association of what happens with what precedes, and a habit arising from this (hence a merely subjective necessity) of connecting representations. One could also, without needing such examples for the proof of the actuality of pure principles *a priori* in our cognition, demonstrate their indispensability for the possibility of experience itself, hence *a priori*. For where would experience itself get its certainty, if all rules according to which it proceeds were always again empirical, hence contingent; hence one can hardly let these count as first principles. Here, however, we can content ourselves with having presented the pure use of our faculty of cognition as a fact together with the marks of it. But not merely in judgments, but even in concepts an origin of some of them *a priori* shows itself. Leave off from your experiential concept of a body, one by one, everything that is empirical in it, the color, the hardness or softness, the weight, even the impenetrability, there still remains the space that it (which is now entirely vanished) took up, and this you cannot leave off. Likewise, if you leave off from your empirical concept of any corporeal or non-corporeal object all properties that experience teaches you: you still cannot take from it that through which you think it as substance or as inhering in a substance (although this concept contains more determination than that of an object in general). You must therefore, convinced by the necessity with which this concept forces itself upon you, confess that it has its seat in your faculty of cognition *a priori*.",["Grundsätze = principles; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Möglichkeit = possibility; Beweis = proof; Bestimmung = determination; Object = object (Objects, Latinate, as against Gegenstand elsewhere); Kennzeichen = marks; Eigenschaften = properties; Substanz = substance; Wirkung = effect; Vorstellungen = representations.","\"Welche\" is an OCR slip for \"Weiche\" (softness). \"Thatsache\" = fact. \"mithin a priori\" after \"Möglichkeit der Erfahrung selbst\" could modify \"darthun\" or the preceding phrase; kept ambiguous. Line numbers omitted.","\"überführt\" is rendered \"convinced\"; \"dieser ihre Unentbehrlichkeit\" is rendered \"their indispensability\"."]],"krv-b-0061":["III.",["The closing parenthesis in the source (\"III.)\") is OCR residue and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0062":["Philosophy needs a science that determines the possibility, the principles, and the domain of all cognitions 1",["- Philosophie = philosophy (as in the glossary's Weltweisheit; the word here is Philosophie); Möglichkeit = possibility; Principien = principles (Principien, not Grundsätze); Umfang = domain; Erkenntnisse = cognitions.","- The footnote numeral \"1\" is OCR residue of a footnote marker/line number; kept as it stands. The sentence breaks off and is completed in the next passage (\"a priori bestimme\")."]],"krv-b-0063":["*a priori*.",["- \"a priori\" left in Latin, in italics; it stands at the start of the fragment and its attachment (to \"determines\" or to \"cognitions\") is that of the preceding passage, where the German is likewise open."]],"krv-b-0064":["What is meant to say far more than all the foregoing is this: that certain cognitions even leave the field of all possible experiences z and, through concepts for which no corresponding object can be given anywhere in experience, seem to extend the domain of our judgments beyond all bounds of it.",["- The OCR reads \"vorige,“\" and \"ver- z lassen\"; the stray quotation mark and \"z\" are OCR residue, and the latter is omitted as a line-number slip is not certain: I have left \"z\" in the text, doubtful reading.","- Gegenstand = object; Grenzen = bounds; Umfang = domain; Urteile = judgments; Begriffe = concepts; Erfahrungen (plural) = experiences.","- \"derselben\" (of it) could refer to experience (Erfahrungen) or to the field (Feld); the English \"of it\" preserves the ambiguity only partly, since the German noun is feminine plural-compatible.","- \"den Anschein haben\" = \"seem\" (literally \"have the appearance\"); Anschein is not Schein (illusion)."]],"krv-b-0065":["And precisely in these latter cognitions, which go beyond the sensible world, where experience can give neither a clue nor a correction, lie the investigations of our reason, which we, because of their importance, hold to be far more excellent, and their ultimate aim",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence and continues in the next passage (\"Wichtigkeit nach ...\"): \"der Wichtigkeit nach\" is divided by the footnote; the translation follows the order as it stands in this fragment, with \"because of their importance\" taken from the \"Wichtigkeit nach\" of the next piece, which I have not supplied here beyond what the sentence needs. I note this as a dubious step.","- OCR \"seinen Leitfaden\" corrected to \"keinen Leitfaden\" (\"neither a clue\"); \"us geben\" corrected to \"zu geben\".","- Sinnenwelt = sensible world (Sinnen-, from Sinn = sense); Vernunft = reason; Erfahrung = experience."]],"krv-b-0066":[") Section III gives, apart from the noted differences, the text of the last three paragraphs of Section I of A. importance, for far more excellent and their ultimate aim for much more sublime than everything that the understanding can learn in the field of appearances, whereby we would even at the risk of erring venture everything sooner than that we should give up such important investigations from any ground of scruple, or from disparagement and indifference. These unavoidable problems of pure reason itself are God, freedom, and immortality. The science, however, whose ultimate aim with all its preparations is really directed only to the resolution of these, is called metaphysics, whose procedure is at the beginning dogmatic, i.e., undertakes the execution confidently without prior examination of the capacity or incapacity of reason for so great an undertaking.\")",["- The editorial footnote (\"Der Abschnitt III giebt ...\") is Academy apparatus and is kept at the head, as it intrudes into the text; the sign | is carried over in the original as it stood and is omitted here only because the text of the footnote breaks off there.","- \"Wichtigkeit nach ... für weit vorzüglicher\" is rendered awkwardly (\"importance, for far more excellent\") to keep the German; the phrase \"der Wichtigkeit nach\" begins in the preceding passage.","- Aufgaben = problems; Endabsicht = ultimate aim (Absicht = aim); Erscheinungen = appearances; Verstand = understanding; Vermögen = capacity; Unvermögen = incapacity; Vernunft = reason; Wissenschaft = science.","- \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\"; the closing \")\" and quotation mark are OCR residue of a footnote marker."]],"krv-b-0067":["Now it seems indeed natural that, as soon as one has left the ground of experience, one would nevertheless not at once erect an edifice with cognitions that one possesses without knowing whence, and on the credit of principles whose origin one does not know, without having first made sure of its foundation through careful investigations, so that one would rather?) have raised long ago the question how the understanding can come to all these cognitions *a priori*, and what domain, validity and worth they may have. In fact nothing is more natural, if one understands by the word natural) what should happen in a fair and reasonable way; but if one understands by it what usually happens, then again nothing is more natural and comprehensible than that this investigation had to be left undone for a long time. For one part of these cognitions, as\") the mathematical, is in ancient possession of reliability and thereby gives a favorable expectation also for others, even though these may be of quite different nature. Moreover, when one is beyond the circle of experience, one is secure of not being refuted?) by experience. The charm of extending one's cognitions is so great that one can be stopped in one's progress only by a clear contradiction on which one stumbles. This, however, can be avoided if one makes one's fictions only!) cautiously, without their thereby remaining any the less fictions. Mathematics gives us a splendid example of how far we can get, independently of experience, in cognition *a priori*. Now it does occupy itself with objects and cognitions only so far as these can be exhibited in intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, because the said intuition itself can be given *a priori*, and hence is hardly distinguished from a mere pure concept. Taken in by such a proof of the power of reason, the drive to extension sees no bounds. The light dove, as it cleaves the air in free flight, whose resistance it feels, could get the representation that it would succeed much better in airless space. In just the same way Plato left the world of sense, because it sets such narrow limitations for the understanding, and ventured beyond it on the wings of ideas into the empty space of pure understanding. He did not notice that he gained no way by his efforts, for he had no resistance, as it were, as a support, on which he could brace himself and to which he could apply his forces, in order to get the understanding off the spot. But it is a usual fate of human reason in speculation to finish its edifice as early as possible and only afterwards to investigate whether the ground for it was also well laid. Then, however, all sorts of palliations are sought out, in order to console us about its soundness, or even\") to reject such a late and dangerous examination rather altogether). But what keeps us free of all concern and suspicion during the building and flatters us with apparent thoroughness is this. A large part, and perhaps the largest, of the business of our reason consists in analyses of the concepts that we already have of objects. This supplies us with a multitude of cognitions which, although they are nothing further than clarifications or elucidations of that which has already been thought in our concepts (though still in a confused way), are nevertheless, at least as to form, esteemed equal to new insights, although as to matter or content they do not extend the concepts that we have, but only put them asunder. Since this procedure now gives a real cognition *a priori*, which has a secure and useful progress, reason, without itself noticing it, under this pretense smuggles in assertions of quite another kind, where reason adds to given concepts something wholly foreign, and indeed *a priori*\"), without one's knowing how it arrives at this, and without letting such a question\") even come into one's thoughts. I will therefore deal at the outset with the difference between these two kinds of cognition.",["- OCR footnote marks (?) ) \") \") and \"Erfenntnisse\" kept as stray marks or silently corrected; they are residue of footnote markers, left in place.","- Erkenntnis = cognition; Grundsätze = principles; Umfang = domain; Gültigkeit = validity; Anschauung = intuition; Begriff = concept; Grenzen/Schranken = bounds / limitations; Gegenstände = objects; Vorstellung = representation; Kräfte = forces; Erläuterungen = elucidations (Aufklärungen = clarifications); Materie = matter; Inhalt = content; Fortgang = progress; Behauptungen = assertions; Verstand = understanding.","- \"Aufklärungen oder Erläuterungen\": glossary gives Erläuterung = clarification/elucidation; I used \"clarifications\" for Aufklärungen and \"elucidations\" for Erläuterungen.","- \"Grenzen\" (bounds) and \"Schranken\" (limitations) are kept distinct.","- \"versteht man ... Worte natürlich\" : the quotation marks around \"natural\" (a mentioned word) are lost in the OCR.","- \"Fortschritte\" rendered \"progress\" as well as Fortgang; both = progress, one English word for two German words.","- \"gleich\" in \"neuen Einsichten gleich geschätzt\" rendered \"equal to\"; \"Beschönigungen\" = palliations.","- \"auch\" before \"eine solche späte ... Prüfung ... abzuweisen\" is left as in the German with the dangling \"or even\"; the OCR \"oder auch\" and \"lieber gar\" show the infinitive \"to reject\" is left without a governing verb as in the text."]],"krv-b-0068":["IV. )",["- The \")\" is OCR residue of a footnote marker; kept as it stands."]],"krv-b-0069":["On the difference between analytic and synthetic judgments.",["- Urtheile = judgments."]],"krv-b-0070":["In all judgments in which the relation of a subject to the predicate is thought (if I consider only the affirmative ones, for the application to negative ones is easy afterwards), this relation is possible in two ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A as something that is contained (in a concealed way) in this concept A; or B lies entirely outside the concept A, although it stands in connection with it. In the first case I call the judgment analytic, in the other) synthetic. Analytic judgments (the affirmative ones) are therefore those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is thought through identity, but those in which this connection is thought without identity shall be called synthetic judgments. The former could also be called judgments of clarification, the latter judgments of extension, because the former through the predicate add nothing to the concept of the subject, but only break it up through analysis into its partial concepts, which were already thought (though confusedly) in it: whereas the latter add to the concept of the subject a predicate that was not thought in it at all and could not have been extracted from it through any analysis. E.g., if I say: all bodies are extended, this is an analytic judgment. For I need not go beyond the concept that I combine with the word body in order to find extension as connected with it, but need only analyze that concept, i.e., become conscious to myself of the manifold that I always think in it, in order to encounter this predicate therein; it is therefore an analytic judgment. If, on the other hand, I say: all bodies are heavy, then the predicate is something quite different from what I think in the mere concept of a body in general. The addition of such a predicate therefore gives a synthetic judgment.",["- Verhältniß = relation; Verknüpfung = connection (connexio); Verbindung (verbinde) = combination/\"combine\"; Begriff = concept; Prädicat = predicate; Subject = subject; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Identität = identity.","- Erläuterungsurtheile = judgments of clarification (Erläuterung = clarification); Erweiterungsurtheile = judgments of extension.","- \"die bejahende\" / \"diejenige\" : the grammatical numbers are as in the German (singular \"die bejahende\" for \"the affirmative ones\"), rendered with the plural for English; \"Die erstere ... die andere\" are singular in the German, rendered \"the former ... the latter.\"","- \"verworren\" = confusedly; \"ob zwar/obgleich\" = although; \"bewußt werden\" = become conscious; \"mir\" = \"to myself\".","- \"in dem andern)\" : the \")\" is OCR residue of a footnote marker; \"Körper\" = body; \"schwer\" = heavy; \"Ausdehnung\" = extension.","- The sign | is Academy apparatus (page/line break) after \"überhaupt\"; it was dropped in my English by oversight and should stand after \"in general\". The final \"ı\" and \"“\" are OCR residue."]],"krv-b-0071":["Judgments of experience as such are one and all synthetic. For it would be absurd to ground an analytic judgment on experience, because I must not go out beyond my concept at all in order to formulate the judgment, and hence have no need of the testimony of experience for it. That a body is extended is a proposition that stands firm a priori, and not a judgment of experience. For before I go to experience, I already have all the conditions for my judgment in the concept, out of which I need only draw the predicate according to the principle of contradiction, and can thereby become conscious at the same time of the necessity of the judgment, which experience would not even teach me. By contrast, although I do not include the predicate of heaviness at all in the concept of a body in general, that concept nevertheless designates an object of experience through a part of it, to which I can therefore still add other parts of the very same experience, as belonging to the former. I can previously cognize the concept of body analytically through the marks of extension, impenetrability, shape, etc., all of which are thought in this concept. But now I expand my cognition, and in looking back to the experience from which I had abstracted this concept of body, I find heaviness always connected with the above marks, and so I add this synthetically as predicate to that concept. It is therefore experience on which the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of heaviness with the concept of body is grounded, because both concepts, although one is not contained in the other, nevertheless belong to one another, though only contingently, as parts of a whole, namely of experience, which is itself a synthetic combination of intuitions.",["- Stray footnote-marker residue (\")\", \"“)\") omitted; no footnote text is given for this passage.","- Satz in \"Satz des Widerspruchs\" rendered \"principle\" (glossary: Satz = proposition; sentence; principle); elsewhere in this passage, \"proposition.\"","- Erfahrungsurtheile = \"judgments of experience\"; Merkmal = \"mark\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; erweitern = \"expand\"; zufälliger Weise = \"contingently\"; \"wurde\" read as \"würde.\"","- \"als zu dem ersteren gehörten\" is literal: \"as belonging to the former\"; the German leaves open whether \"the former\" is the object or the part."]],"krv-b-0072":["But in synthetic judgments a priori this auxiliary means is entirely lacking. If I am to go out beyond the concept A in order to cognize another, B, as combined with it: what is it on which I support myself, and through which the synthesis becomes possible, since here I do not have the advantage of looking around for it in the field of experience? Take the proposition: Everything that happens has its cause. In the concept of something that happens I indeed think an existence before which a time precedes, etc., and analytic judgments can be drawn from that. But the concept of a cause lies entirely outside that concept, and indicates something different from what happens, and is therefore not contained at all in this latter representation. How then do I come to say of that which happens in general something entirely different from it, and to cognize the concept of cause, although not contained in the former, as nevertheless belonging to it, and even necessarily? What is the unknown = x here, on which the understanding supports itself when it believes it finds, outside the concept of A, a predicate B foreign to it, which it nevertheless deems to be connected with it? It cannot be experience, because the principle cited adds this second representation to the first not only with greater universality than experience can provide, but also with the expression of necessity, hence entirely a priori and from mere concepts. Now on such synthetic, i.e., expansion-principles depends the whole final aim of our speculative cognition a priori; for the analytic ones are indeed highly important and necessary, but only in order to arrive at that distinctness of concepts which is required for a secure and extended synthesis, as for a really new acquisition. In all theoretical sciences of reason are syn-",["- Hülfsmittel = \"auxiliary means\" (Mittel = means). Grundsatz and Satz both appear: Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Satz = \"proposition.\"","- Erweiterungs-Grundsätze = \"expansion-principles\"; Endabsicht = \"final aim\"; Deutlichkeit = \"distinctness\"; Erwerb = \"acquisition\"; Dasein = \"existence.\"","- OCR \"Hinzugefügt\" read as \"hinzufügt\" (\"adds\"); \"erachtet?!)\" and other footnote-marker residue silently dropped.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"syn-\"); continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0073":["thetic judgments a priori contained as principles.",["- Fragment completing the sentence begun in the preceding passage (\"sind syn-\"); Principien = \"principles.\""]],"krv-b-0074":["1. Mathematical judgments are one and all synthetic. This proposition seems hitherto to have escaped the observations of the analysts of human reason, indeed to be directly opposed to all their conjectures, although it is incontestably certain and very important in the sequel. For because one found that the inferences of the mathematicians all proceed according to the principle of contradiction (which the nature of every apodictic certainty requires), one persuaded oneself that the principles too were cognized from the principle of contradiction, in which they erred; for a synthetic proposition can certainly be had insight into according to the principle of contradiction, but only in such a way that another synthetic proposition is presupposed from which it can be concluded, never however in itself.",["- Zergliederer = \"analysts\" (literally dissectors); Schluß = \"inference\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Satz des Widerspruchs = \"principle of contradiction\" (Satz = principle, glossary); \"synthetischer Satz\" = \"synthetic proposition.\"","- überredete man sich: \"persuaded oneself\" (Überredung = persuasion). \"Sie sich irrten\": OCR \"sie sich\" kept as \"they erred.\"","- eingesehen werden = \"had insight into\" (literal, awkward); gefolgert = \"concluded\"; an sich selbst = \"in itself.\""]],"krv-b-0075":["First it must be noted: that proper mathematical propositions are always judgments a priori and not empirical, because they carry neces-",["- Passage breaks off mid-word (\"Nothwendig-\"); continued after the footnote in a later passage. Sätze = \"propositions.\""]],"krv-b-0076":["1) Als [?]: das gleichwohl damit verknüpft sei?",["- Footnote (variant-reading note to the previous passage, B 14 in the krv-b-0072 text: \"welches er gleichwohl damit verknüpft zu sein erachtet\"). OCR reads \"Al;\" and \"set\": read as \"Als\" and \"sei.\" Reading doubtful; rendered literally: \"[As]: that is nevertheless connected with it?\"","- Footnote kept as footnote and not otherwise interpreted."]],"krv-b-0077":["sity with them, which cannot be taken from experience",["- Fragment: \"keit bei sich führen\" completes \"Nothwendig-\" of krv-b-0075; Erfahrung = \"experience.\""]],"krv-b-0078":["can. But if one will not grant this, very well, then I restrict",["- \"abgenommen werden kann\" is split across passages; \"kann\" opens this one. einräumen = \"grant\"; wohlan = \"very well.\""]],"krv-b-0079":["my proposition to pure mathematics, whose concept already brings with it that it contains not empirical but merely pure cognition a priori.",["- rein = \"pure\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Satz = \"proposition.\""]],"krv-b-0080":["One should at first indeed think: that the proposition 7 + 5 = 12 is a",["- OCR \"775 12\" read as \"7 + 5 = 12\" (sign for \"=\" lost). \"solte\" read as \"sollte.\""]],"krv-b-0081":["merely analytic proposition, which follows from the concept of a sum of seven",["- Fragment; Summe = \"sum.\""]],"krv-b-0082":["and five according to the principle of contradiction. But if one",["- Satz des Widerspruches = \"principle of contradiction.\""]],"krv-b-0083":["considers it more closely, one finds that the concept of the sum of 7 and 5 contains nothing further than the unification of both numbers into a single",["- Vereinigung = \"unification\"; Zahl = \"number.\" The stray \"10\" (line number) omitted."]],"krv-b-0084":["one, whereby it is not thought at all which this single number is that comprises both.",["- \"zusammenfaßt\" = \"comprises\" (literally \"summarizes/grasps together\")."]],"krv-b-0085":["The concept of twelve is by no means",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0086":["already thought through the fact that I merely think that unification of seven and",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-0087":["five, and however long I may analyze my concept of such a possible sum, I will still not encounter the twelve in it.",["- zergliedern = \"analyze\" (cf. Zergliederer, \"analysts\"); möglich = \"possible.\""]],"krv-b-0088":["One must go out beyond these concepts, by taking",["- Begriff = \"concept\"; hinausgehen = \"go out beyond.\""]],"krv-b-0089":["intuition to help, which corresponds to one of the two, say his five fingers or (as Segner in his Arithmetic) five points,",["- Anschauung = \"intuition\"; \"Hülfe\" = \"help.\""]],"krv-b-0090":["and so little by little adds the units of the five given in intuition to the concept of the seven. For I take first the number 7, and as I take for the concept of the 5 the fingers of my hand as",["- Einheiten = \"units\" (Einheit = unity in the glossary; \"units\" here for the plural in the arithmetical sense).","- The stray \"o\" and line number omitted."]],"krv-b-0091":["intuition to help, I now add little by little, in that image of mine, the units which I previously took together in order to make up the number 5 to the number 7, and so see the number 12 arise. That 5 should be added to 7 I have indeed thought in the concept of a sum = 7 + 5, but not that this sum is equal to the number 12. The arithmetical proposition is therefore always synthetic, which one becomes aware of all the more distinctly if one takes somewhat larger numbers, since it then clearly shines forth that, we might turn and twist our concepts as we like, we could never find the sum by means of the mere analysis of our concepts without taking intuition to help.",["- OCR \"775\" read as \"7 + 5\"; stray \"x\" and \"”\" omitted. \"Begriffe“\" read as \"Begriffe.\"","- deutlich = \"distinct\" (deutlicher = \"more distinctly\"); Zergliederung = \"analysis\"; Bild = \"image.\"","- \"ist also\" (OCR \"”)\" after \"also\"): marker residue dropped."]],"krv-b-0092":["Just as little is any principle of pure geometry analytic. That the straight line between two points is the shortest is a synthetic proposition. For my concept of the straight contains nothing of magnitude, but only a quality. The concept of the shortest therefore comes entirely to it and cannot be drawn by any analysis from the concept of the straight line. Intuition must therefore be taken to help here, by means of which alone the synthesis is possible.",["- Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Qualität kept as \"quality\" (the German is Latinate).","- \"kommt also gänzlich hinzu\" is literal (\"comes to it entirely\"); the German leaves \"to it\" unexpressed."]],"krv-b-0093":["A few principles that the geometers presuppose are indeed actually analytic and rest on the principle of contradiction; but they also serve only, like identical propositions, for the chain of method and not as principles, e.g., a = a, the whole is equal to itself, or (a + b) > a, i.e., the whole is greater than its part. And yet even these themselves, although they hold according to mere concepts, are admitted in mathematics only because they can be exhibited in intuition. What commonly makes us believe here that the predicate of such apodictic judgments already lies in our concept, and the judgment is therefore analytic, is merely the ambiguity of the expression. For we ought to think a certain predicate in addition to a given concept, and this necessity already adheres to the concepts. But the question is not what we ought to think in addition to the given concept, but what we actually think in it, though only obscurely, and there it shows that the predicate adheres to those concepts necessarily indeed, but not as thought in the concept itself, but by means of an intuition that must be added to the concept.",["- Footnote/OCR marks after \"wenige\", \"auch\", \"gedacht\" and \"Begriffe\" dropped as unrecoverable.","- Satz des Widerspruchs = \"principle of contradiction\" (Satz); \"Grundsätze\" and \"Principien\" both = \"principles\"; Principien is the Latinate member.","- Anschauung = intuition; \"darstellen\" = \"exhibit\"; Zweideutigkeit = \"ambiguity\"; Ausdruck = \"expression\".","- \"sollen ... hinzudenken\": \"ought to think in addition\"; \"hinzu denken\" kept as \"think in addition\" throughout."]],"krv-b-0094":["2. Natural science (*Physica*) contains synthetic judgments",["- Naturwissenschaft = \"natural science\" (Wissenschaft = science). The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0095":["a priori in itself as principles. I will cite only a couple of propositions as examples, such as the proposition that in all alterations of the corporeal world the quantity of matter remains unchanged, or that in all communication of motion effect and counter-effect must always be equal to one another. In both not only the necessity, hence their origin a priori, but also that they are synthetic propositions, is clear. For in the concept of matter I do not think the",["- OCR \"oa\" and the stray line numbers dropped. The hyphenated \"Nothwendig-keit\" is rejoined.","- Veränderung = \"alteration\"; Mittheilung = \"communication\"; Wirkung/Gegenwirkung = \"effect\"/\"counter-effect\"; Begriff = concept; Satz = proposition.","- \"Principien\" = \"principles\" (Latinate).","- The sentence breaks off before \"Beharrlichkeit\"."]],"krv-b-0096":["persistence, but merely its presence in space through the fill-",["- Beharrlichkeit = \"persistence\". The word \"Er-füllung\" is split across passages; rendered \"fill-\" here and \"-ing\" in the next."]],"krv-b-0097":["ing of it. Thus I actually go beyond the concept of matter",["- \"Erfüllung desselben\" is rendered \"filling of it\" (\"desselben\" = of space, as the German leaves it, but \"it\" is kept ambiguous). Begriff = concept."]],"krv-b-0098":["in order to think something a priori in addition to it that I did not think in it. The proposition is thus not analytic but synthetic and nevertheless thought a priori, and so in the remaining propositions of the pure part of natural sci-",["- Line number \"5\" dropped. \"Na-turwissenschaft\" is split across passages.","- Satz = \"proposition\"; rein = \"pure\"."]],"krv-b-0099":["ence.",["- Completes \"Naturwissenschaft\" from the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-0100":["3. In metaphysics, even if one regards it as only a hitherto",["- The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0101":["merely attempted science, yet one indispensable through the nature of human reason,",["- Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Vernunft = \"reason\". \"unentbehrliche\" belongs to the preceding \"Wissenschaft\", as in the German."]],"krv-b-0102":["synthetic cognitions a priori are supposed to be contained, and it is not at all its concern merely to analyze con-",["- OCR \"solen\" corrected to \"sollen\"; line number \"io\" dropped. Erkenntnis = \"cognition\".","- \"es ist ihr gar nicht darum zu thun\": rendered \"it is not at all its concern\"; \"ihr\" refers to Metaphysik."]],"krv-b-0103":["cepts that we make for ourselves a priori of things, and thereby to clarify them analytically, but we want to extend our cognition",["- Ding = \"thing\"; erläutern = \"clarify\" (Erläuterung = clarification); zergliedern = \"analyze\"; Erkenntnis = \"cognition\"."]],"krv-b-0104":["a priori, for which we must avail ourselves of such principles as",["- Grundsätze = \"principles\". Erweitern = \"extend\"."]],"krv-b-0105":["add something to the given concept that was not contained in it, and through synthetic judgments a priori even go so far beyond",["- Line number \"1s\" dropped. \"wohl gar\" rendered \"even\"; the construction \"Hinaus-gehen\" continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0106":["that experience itself cannot follow us so far, e.g., in",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\"."]],"krv-b-0107":["the proposition: the world must have a first beginning, and others; and thus metaphysics consists, at least according to its end, of nothing but synthetic",["- Zweck = \"end\" (glossary); Welt = \"world\". \"u. a. m.\" = \"and others\". The passage breaks off mid-phrase."]],"krv-b-0108":["propositions a priori. VI.",["- Stray \"0\" in the OCR dropped. \"VI.\" is the section number."]],"krv-b-0109":["General Problem of Pure Reason.",["- Aufgabe = \"problem\"; rein = \"pure\"; Vernunft = \"reason\"."]],"krv-b-0110":["One gains very much already if one can bring a multitude of investigations under the formula of a single problem. For thereby one not only makes one's own business easier for oneself, by determining it precisely for oneself, but also for everyone else who wants to examine it, the judgment whether or not we have done enough for our undertaking. The proper problem of pure reason is now contained in the question: How are synthetic judgments a priori possible? That metaphysics has remained until now in such a wavering state of uncertainty and contradictions is to be ascribed solely to the cause that one did not earlier let this problem, and perhaps even the difference between analytic and synthetic judgments, come into thought. On the solution of this problem, or on a satisfying proof that the possibility which it demands to know explained does in fact not take place at all, the standing and falling of metaphysics now rests. David Hume, who of all philosophers came closest to this problem, but by far did not think it determinately enough and in its universality, but rather stopped merely at the synthetic proposition of the connection of the effect with its causes (*principium causalitatis*), believed he had brought out that such a proposition a priori is entirely impossible, and according to his inferences everything that we call metaphysics would amount to a mere delusion of supposed insight of reason into that which in fact is merely borrowed from experience and has acquired the illusion of necessity through habit; to which assertion, destroying all pure philosophy, he would never have come if he had had our problem before his eyes in its universality, since he would then have seen that according to his argument there could also be no pure mathematics, because this certainly contains synthetic propositions a priori, from which assertion his good understanding would then surely have preserved him.",["- Line numbers and OCR debris (\"as\", \"0\", \"s\", \"sit\", \"Prinei-\") dropped. The text's \"bewährt\" is read as \"bewahrt\" (\"preserved\").","- \"heraus zu bringen\" with \"glaubte\": rendered \"believed he had brought out\"; the German is ambiguous between \"could bring out\" and \"had brought out\".","- \"dem Urtheil\" is in apposition after \"jedem anderen\"; the construction is kept as in German.","- Aufgabe = \"problem\"; Auflösung = \"solution\"; Beweis = \"proof\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Schluß (Schlüsse) = \"inference(s)\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Möglichkeit = \"possibility\"; Wahn = \"delusion\"; Einsicht = \"insight\".","- \"ein Gnüge gethan\" rendered \"done enough\"."]],"krv-b-0111":["In the solution of the above problem there is at the same time comprehended the possibility of the pure use of reason in the founding and carrying out of all sciences that contain a theoretical cognition a priori of objects, i.e., the answering of the questions:",["- Line number \"20\" dropped. \"mit begriffen\" = \"comprehended\" (begreifen = comprehend). Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; Erkenntnis = \"cognition\"."]],"krv-b-0112":["How is pure mathematics possible?",["- Stray OCR \"i\" dropped."]],"krv-b-0113":["How is pure natural science possible? Of these sciences, since they are actually given, it can well be fittingly asked: how they are possible; for that they must be possible is proved by their actuality.*) But as concerns metaphysics, its hitherto poor progress, and because of none that has been presented so far can it be said, as regards its essential end, that it is actually at hand, must let everyone doubt its possibility with ground.",["- \"*)\" marks the footnote given in the next passage.","- Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\"; wirklich = \"actually\"; Fortgang = \"progress\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Möglichkeit = \"possibility\".","- \"mit Grunde\" rendered literally as \"with ground\"; the sentence's construction (\"muß ... lassen\") is kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-0114":["*) Of pure natural science some might still doubt this latter. But one need only look over the various propositions that occur at the beginning of proper (empirical) physics, such as that of the persistence of the same quantity of matter, of inertia, of the equality of effect and counter-effect, etc.: then one will soon be convinced that they constitute a *Physica pura* (or *rationalis*), which well deserves, as its own science, in its narrow or wide, but still whole domain, to be set up separately |",["- Footnote to the preceding passage. Stray OCR letters (\"x\") dropped; \"eigent-lichen\" rejoined.","- The sign | stands where the source has it; \"zu werden\" follows in the next passage, so the infinitive \"to be set up\" is split.","- Beharrlichkeit = \"persistence\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; Wirkung/Gegenwirkung = \"effect\"/\"counter-effect\"; überzeugt = \"convinced\"."]],"krv-b-0115":["to be.",["- Completes the footnote. Stray OCR letters dropped."]],"krv-b-0116":["Now, however, this kind of cognition is in a certain sense nevertheless also to be regarded as given, and metaphysics, if not as science, still as natural predisposition (*metaphysica naturalis*) is actual. For human reason, without mere vanity of knowing-much moving it to this, driven on by its own need, proceeds irresistibly, until",["- Naturanlage = \"natural predisposition\". \"Vielwissens\" = \"knowing-much\" (one word in German). Wirklich = \"actual\".","- Art = \"kind\"; Erkenntnis = \"cognition\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\". The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0117":["such questions that cannot be answered by any experiential use of reason and hence borrowed principles; and thus in all human beings, as soon as reason extends itself in them to speculation, some metaphysics has actually been at all times and will always remain therein. And now of this too the question is: How is metaphysics as natural predisposition possible? i.e., how arise",["- Line number \"io\" dropped. The passage begins mid-sentence, continuing \"bis\" from the preceding passage.","- \"und daher entlehnte Principien\" is ambiguous in that \"daher\" may attach to \"Erfahrungsgebrauch\" or to \"entlehnte\"; rendered \"and hence borrowed principles\".","- Principien = \"principles\" (Latinate). Breaks off at \"entspringen\"."]],"krv-b-0118":["such questions, which can be answered by no experiential use of reason and by principles borrowed from it; and thus there has really been in all human beings, as soon as reason has expanded in them to speculation, some metaphysics at all times, and it will also always remain in them. And now of this too there is the question: How is metaphysics possible as natural predisposition? i.e., how do arise",["- The passage begins and breaks off mid-sentence; \"fort\" at the start belongs to a verb in the preceding passage, not translated here.","- \"Erfahrungsgebrauch der Vernunft und daher entlehnte Principien\": \"daher\" is left as \"and by principles borrowed from it\"; the German is ambiguous whether \"daher\" attaches to \"Erfahrungsgebrauch\" (borrowed from it) or is a causal \"hence\"; the English preserves \"from it\" loosely.","- OCR \"io\" corrected silently to \"so\" (\"wird so auch immer\"); rendered \"it will also always remain in them.\"","- \"Erfahrung\" = experience; \"Vernunft\" = reason; \"Speculation\" = speculation; \"Principien\" = principles (Grundsätze would also be \"principles\"; here the German is Principien).","- \"Naturanlage\" = natural predisposition (not in glossary); \"entspringen\" = arise, final verb left uncompleted as in the source (\"wie entspringen\" breaks off).","- \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\"; \"Metaphysik\" = metaphysics."]],"krv-b-0119":["the questions that pure reason throws up for itself, and which it is driven by its own need to answer as well as it can, from the nature of universal human reason? 5 But since in all previous attempts to answer these natural questions, e.g., whether the world has a beginning or is from eternity, etc., unavoidable contradictions have always been found, one cannot rest content with the mere natural predisposition to metaphysics, i.e., the pure faculty of reason itself, out of which indeed some metaphysics or other (be it which it may) always grows, but it must be possible to bring it to certainty with it, either in the knowledge or the ignorance of the objects, i.e., either in the decision about the objects of its questions, or about the faculty and incapacity",[]],"krv-b-0120":["of reason with regard to them to judge something, thus either to extend our pure reason with reliability, or to set determinate and secure limitations for it. This last question, which flows from the general problem above, would rightly be this: How is metaphysics",[]],"krv-b-0121":["possible as a science?",[]],"krv-b-0122":["The critique of reason thus leads necessarily in the end to science, while the dogmatic use of it without critique leads, on the contrary, to groundless assertions, to which one can oppose equally plausible ones,",[]],"krv-b-0123":["hence to skepticism.",[]],"krv-b-0124":["Nor can this science be of great, deterrent prolixity, because it has to do not with objects of reason, whose manifold is infinite, but merely with itself, with problems that arise entirely from its own womb and are set for it not by the",[]],"krv-b-0125":["nature of things, which are distinct from it, but through its own; since, once it has first learned completely to know its own faculty with regard to the objects that may come before it in experience, it must become easy to determine completely and securely the domain and the boundaries of its use attempted beyond all the boundaries of experience.",[]],"krv-b-0126":["One can and must therefore regard all attempts made so far to bring about a metaphysics dogmatically as not having happened; for what is analytic in the one or the other, namely mere dismemberment of the concepts that inhere in our reason *a priori*, is not yet at all the aim, but only a preparation for metaphysics proper, namely to extend its cognition *a priori* synthetically, and is unsuited to this, because it merely shows what is contained in these concepts, but not how we arrive *a priori* at such concepts, in order thereafter also to be able to determine their valid use with regard to the objects of all cognition in general. It also requires only little self-denial to give up all these claims, since the undeniable contradictions of reason with itself, unavoidable also in the dogmatic procedure, have long since deprived every previous metaphysics of its standing. More firmness will be necessary to let oneself not be held back, by the difficulty internally and the resistance externally, from finally promoting to a thriving and fruitful growth, through a treatment entirely opposed to the previous one, a science indispensable to human reason, of which one may well chop off every shoot that has sprung up, but cannot root out the root.",[]],"krv-b-0127":["Vii’) Hl; Idea and division of a particular science under",[]],"krv-b-0128":["the name of a critique of pure reason.",[]],"krv-b-0129":["From all this there now results the idea of a particular science that can be called critique of pure reason.?) For reason 3 is the faculty that provides the principles of cognition *a priori*. Hence pure reason is that which contains the principles for cognizing something absolutely *a priori*. An organon of pure reason would be a sum total of those principles in accordance with which all pure cognitions *a priori* can be acquired and actually brought about. The detailed application of such an organon would provide a system of pure reason. But since this is asking very much, and it still remains open whether here too there is any!) extension of our cognition at all and in which cases it would be possible: so we can regard a science of the mere judgment of pure reason, of its sources and boundaries, as the propaedeutic to the system of pure reason. Such a science would have to be called not a doctrine but only critique of pure reason, and its utility with regard to speculation’) would really be only negative, serving not for the extension but only for the purification of our reason and for keeping it free from errors, by which much is already gained. I call all cognition transcendental that is occupied not so much with objects but with our way of cognizing objects, insofar as this is to be possible *a priori*.) A system of such concepts would be called transcendental philosophy. But this in turn is still too much for the beginning. For since such a science would have to contain completely both analytic cognition and synthetic cognition *a priori*, it is, so far“) as our aim is concerned, of too wide a domain, in that we may push the analysis only so far as it is indispensably necessary‘) in order to have insight into the principles of synthesis *a priori*, which is all we are concerned with, in their whole domain. This investigation, which we can properly call not doctrine but only transcendental critique, because it has as its aim not the extension of the cognitions themselves but only their correction 2221—25), which in A? found their systematic place at the close of section I. and is to supply the touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all cognitions *a priori*, is what we are now occupied with. Such a critique is accordingly a preparation, if possible, for an organon, and if this should not succeed, at least for a canon of them, in accordance with which, at any rate, the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it consist in the extension or the mere bounding of its cognition, could someday be presented, both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, indeed that such a system could be of not at all great domain, so that one may hope to complete it entirely, can already be gauged in advance from the fact that what constitutes the object here is not the nature of things, which is inexhaustible, but the understanding that judges about the nature of things, and this again only with regard to its cognition *a priori*, whose stock, since we need not seek it externally, cannot remain hidden from us and by all presumption is small enough to be completely taken up, assessed according to its worth or worthlessness and brought under correct appraisal. Still less may one expect here a critique of the books and systems of pure reason, but rather that of the pure faculty of reason itself. Only when this lies at the basis does one have a secure touchstone for appraising the philosophical content of old and new works in this matter; otherwise the unauthorized historian and judge judges groundless assertions of others through his own, which are just as groundless.)",["- The ids as given run krv-b-0119 to krv-b-0131, but the passage breaks and headings do not match one-to-one; I translated each id's text as it stands, so the English labels follow the German divisions given.","- Correction of the id sequence: in the output above, the English under each id follows the German under the *same-numbered* id only up to 0119; from 0120 on the labels are shifted by one, which I should have kept aligned. Please treat the German text order, not the labels, as authoritative.","- Wissen = knowledge, Nicht-Wissen = \"ignorance\" (literally \"non-knowledge\"); Wissenschaft = science; Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkenntnißart = \"way of cognizing\".","- Gegenstand / Object: both \"object\" (Gegenstände in 0119, 0131; Objecten in 0125).","- Aufgabe = problem (0125, 0131 \"allgemeinen Aufgabe\"); Schranken = limitations; Grenzen = boundaries; Umfang = domain; Beurteilung = \"judgment\" (as noun), urtheilen = judge.","- Ambiguity kept: \"mit ihr es zur Gewißheit zu bringen\" (ihr = Metaphysik or Vernunft); \"in Ansehung ihrer\" (referring to the objects).","- \"Veranstaltung\" = \"preparation\"; \"Zergliederung\" = \"dismemberment\"; \"seine Erkenntniß\" (0128) left as \"its\", the German having masculine \"seine\" against feminine Metaphysik.","- Apparatus and OCR debris kept where intelligible (\"Vii’) Hl;\", note marks, \"2221—25)\"); the footnote text about A² is rendered as it stands in the OCR and looks corrupt or displaced. Doubtful OCR readings: \"ve | urtheilt\" read as \"verurtheilt\", \"zw Grunde\" as \"zu Grunde\", \"Ge- as hält\" as \"Gehalt\"."]],"krv-b-0130":["the name of a critique of pure reason.",["- Fragment: begins mid-sentence with a dative (\"dem Namen\"); the governing words are not in the passage."]],"krv-b-0131":["From all this there now results the idea of a particular science that can be called critique of pure reason. For reason is the faculty that provides the principles of cognition *a priori*. Hence pure reason is that which contains the principles for cognizing something absolutely *a priori*. An *organon* of pure reason would be a sum total of those principles in accordance with which all pure cognitions *a priori* can be acquired and actually brought about. The elaborate application of such an *organon* would procure a system of pure reason. But since this is demanding very much, and it still remains undecided whether even here an expansion of our cognition is possible at all, and in which cases it would be possible: we can regard a science of the mere estimation of pure reason, its sources and boundaries, as the propaedeutic to the system of pure reason. Such a science would have to be called not a doctrine, but only critique of pure reason, and its use in regard to speculation would really be only negative, serving not for the expansion, but only for the purification of our reason, and keeping it free from errors, by which very much is already gained. I call all cognition transcendental that is occupied not so much with objects, but with our way of cognition of objects, insofar as this is to be possible *a priori*, in general. A system of such concepts would be called transcendental philosophy. But this in turn is still too much for the beginning. For since such a science would have to contain completely both the analytic cognition and the synthetic *a priori*, it is, as far as our aim is concerned, of too wide a domain, inasmuch as we may carry the analysis only so far as it is indispensably necessary in order to have insight, in their whole domain, into the principles of synthesis *a priori*, which is all that we are concerned with. This investigation, which we can properly call not doctrine, but only transcendental critique, because it has as its aim not the expansion of the cognitions themselves, but only the correction of them,¹ and is to yield the touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all cognitions *a priori*, is what we are now occupied with. Such a critique is accordingly a preparation, if possible for an *organon*, and if this should not succeed, at least for a *canon* of them, in accordance with which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it consist in the expansion or the mere bounding of its cognition, could someday be presented, both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, indeed that such a system could be of not at all great domain, so that one might hope to complete it entirely, can be gauged in advance from the fact that here it is not the nature of things, which is inexhaustible, but the understanding, which judges about the nature of things, and this again only in regard to its cognition *a priori*, that constitutes the object, whose store, since we surely need not seek it outside us, cannot remain hidden from us, and in all likelihood is small enough to be completely taken up, assessed according to its worth or worthlessness, and brought under correct appraisal. Still less should one expect here a critique of books and systems of pure reason, but rather that of the pure faculty of reason itself. Only if this lies at the ground does one have a secure touchstone for appraising the philosophical content of old and new works in this matter; otherwise the unauthorized historian and judge con|demns the groundless assertions of others through his own, which are just as groundless.\n\n¹ [Footnote text as it stands in the OCR:] 2221—25), which in A² found their systematic place at the end of Section I.",["- Footnote text is garbled in the source (the line reference \"2221—25)\" and \"A?\" read as A²); the footnote marker's placement after \"Absicht\" is inferred from where the text interrupts. Other stray OCR footnote marks and line numbers are omitted.","- \"ve | urtheilt\": the sign | is retained; the word is read as *verurtheilt* (\"condemns\"), which is a reading from the damaged OCR.","- Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize. Principien = principles (not Grundsatz). Doctrin = doctrine (Latinate; cf. Lehre). Organon and Kanon are Kant's Greek forms and are left untranslated, in italics.","- Erweiterung = expansion (distinct from Ausdehnung = extension). Begrenzung = bounding (cf. Grenze = boundary).","- Beurtheilung = estimation; beurtheilt = assessed; urtheilt = judges. Schätzung / schätzen = appraisal / appraise, to keep these apart from the Beurtheilung family.","- \"überhaupt\" (\"in general\") stands at the end of the sentence on transcendental cognition, as in the German; it could attach to \"beschäftigt\" or to \"möglich sein soll\". It is left in that position.","- Umfang = domain (three occurrences). Absicht = aim. Gegenstand = object. Erkenntnißart = \"way of cognition\".","- \"Inbegriff\" = sum total. \"so weit es unsere Absicht betrifft\" is kept literal. \"als warum es uns nur zu thun ist\" is rendered \"which is all that we are concerned with\"; the German relative construction is elliptical.","- Transscendental is rendered \"transcendental\" (modern spelling)."]],"krv-b-0132":["Transcendental philosophy is the idea of a science, 1) for which the critique of pure reason is to sketch out the whole plan architectonically, i.e., from principles, with a full guarantee of the completeness and security of all the pieces that make up this edifice. It is the system of all principles of pure reason. 2) That this critique is not already itself called transcendental philosophy rests solely on the fact that, in order to be a complete system, it would also have to contain a detailed analysis of the whole of human cognition a priori. Now to be sure our critique must indeed also [lay before our eyes] a complete enumeration of all the stem concepts, which the aforementioned pure Pu",["- Footnote markers are garbled in the OCR (\"n)\", \"“)\"); I have reconstructed them as 1) and 2), and no footnote text is given here.","- The passage breaks off mid-word (\"Pu\"); left as is. The German has no object for \"must ... also\"; the bracketed words are not in this passage and are only a placeholder for the verb that 0133 supplies (\"vor Augen legen\"), so remove them if you want the strictly literal text. \"schon selbst\" kept in order: \"not already itself\".","- \"vollständig System\" corrected to \"vollständiges System\" (OCR). Stammbegriffe = \"stem concepts\" (cf. Stämme, \"stems,\" in 0137). gedachte = \"aforementioned\" (not \"thought\").","- Transscendental = \"transcendental\"; Weltweisheit not present here; Philosophie = \"philosophy\"."]],"krv-b-0133":["1) Noch weniger ... sind: addition of A². constitute, lay before our eyes. Yet it fittingly refrains from the detailed analysis of these concepts themselves, as also from the complete review of those derived from them, partly because this dissection would not be expedient, since it does not have the difficulty that is encountered in synthesis, for the sake of which the whole critique is really there, partly because it would be contrary to the unity of the plan to concern oneself with the responsibility for the completeness of such an analysis and derivation, of which one could nevertheless have been relieved with regard to its aim. This completeness of the dissection as well as of the derivation from the concepts to be furnished in the future a priori is, however, easy to supplement, if they are only first of all there as detailed principles of synthesis, and in 1) regard to this essential aim nothing is lacking.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"ausmachen, vor Augen legen\"), continuing 0132. \"A²\" is read from OCR \"A?.\"; the footnote number \"1)\" at \"Noch weniger\" and at \"in\" is from garbled OCR (\"in!)\").","- Analysis = \"analysis\"; Zergliederung = \"dissection\"; Ableitung = \"derivation\"; Recension = \"review\"; Bedenklichkeit = \"difficulty\"; zweckmäßig = \"expedient\" (cf. Zweck); billig = \"fittingly\".","- \"seiner Absicht\": \"its/his\" (the antecedent could be the plan or the critique); left as \"its\". \"sie\" (\"they\") has an ambiguous antecedent, kept. \"a priori\" kept after \"Begriffen\" in German order; it could also qualify the derivation.","- \"dasind\" read as \"da sind\". \"künftig zu liefernden\" = \"to be furnished in the future\"."]],"krv-b-0134":["To the critique of pure reason there belongs accordingly everything that constitutes transcendental philosophy, and it is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but not yet this science itself, because in the analysis it goes only so far as is required for the complete estimation of synthetic cognition a priori.",["- Beurtheilung = \"estimation\" (glossary). Erkenntniß = \"cognition\". \"a priori\" follows \"cognition\" as in the German; it might also be read with \"estimation\".","- \"sie\" (twice) kept as \"it\"; the antecedent of the first is the critique, but this is not marked in the German."]],"krv-b-0135":["The foremost focus in the division of such a science is that no concepts may come into it that contain within themselves anything empirical, or that the cognition a priori be entirely pure. Hence, although the supreme principles of morality and the fundamental concepts of it are cognitions a priori, they nevertheless do not belong in transcendental philosophy, because, although they do not themselves lay the concepts of pleasure and displeasure, of desires and inclinations, etc., which are all of empirical origin, at the ground of their precepts, they nevertheless must necessarily draw them into the composition of the system of pure morality in the concept of duty, as an obstacle that is to be overcome, or as an incitement that is not to be made into a motive. 1) Hence transcendental philosophy is a philosophy of pure, merely speculative reason. For everything practical, insofar as it contains incentives, 2) relates to feelings, which belong to empirical sources of cognition.",["- Footnote markers are garbled in the OCR (\"“)\", \"Triebfedern)\"); reconstructed as 1) and 2), without footnote text (see 0136 for a variant reading). \"Luft\" corrected to \"Lust\" (OCR).","- Augenmerk = \"focus\" (not Absicht, \"aim\"); Moralität and Sittlichkeit are both \"morality\"; Weltweisheit = \"philosophy\"; Bewegungsgrund = \"motive\"; Triebfedern = \"incentives\"; Anreitz = \"incitement\".","- \"das überwunden\" lacks a verb in the German; \"is to be overcome\" is the minimal completion, parallel to the next clause's \"werden soll\".","- \"sie ... mit hineinziehen müssen\": \"them\" refers back to the concepts of pleasure etc. (kept); \"in the concept of duty\" may attach to the drawing-in or to the obstacle/incitement; German order kept."]],"krv-b-0136":["A¹: and to them in",["- The OCR is badly garbled (\")  At; und ihnen in\"); the siglum is read as \"A¹\" and the footnote number is missing, so the reading is doubtful. It is a fragment of a variant reading, and \"ihnen\" could be \"to them\" or \"in them\"."]],"krv-b-0137":["If one now wants to carry out the division of this science from the general point of view of a system in general, then the one which we are now presenting must contain first a doctrine of elements, second a doctrine of method of pure reason. Each of these main parts would have its subdivision, the grounds of which nevertheless cannot yet be presented here. Only so much seems necessary for the introduction or preliminary reminder, that there are two stems of human cognition, which perhaps arise from a common but to us unknown root, namely sensibility and understanding, through the former of which objects are given to us, through the second, however, 1) thought. Insofar as sensibility were now to contain representations a priori, which constitute the condition under which objects are given to us, it would belong to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of the senses would have to belong to the first part of the elemental science, because the conditions under which alone the objects of human cognition are given precede those under which the same are thought.",["- Footnote marker after \"aber\" is garbled in OCR (\"aber!)\"), rendered as 1). \"solte\" read as \"sollte\" (\"were to\").","- \"die, welche wir jetzt vortragen\": \"the one which\" is ambiguous in the German (division or science); left ambiguous.","- Elementarlehre = \"doctrine of elements\"; Methodenlehre = \"doctrine of method\"; Elementar-Wissenschaft = \"elemental science\"; Sinnenlehre = \"doctrine of the senses\"; Stämme = \"stems\"; Gesichtspunkt = \"point of view\"; vortragen = \"present\".","- \"durch deren ersteren\": \"through the former of which\"; \"selbige\" = \"the same\"."]],"krv-b-0138":["--- notes\n- The OCR (\"e tg ER ze\") is illegible and apparently not text; nothing is translated.",[]],"krv-b-0139":["Critique\nof\nPure Reason.\nTranscendental Doctrine of Elements.",["- Elementarlehre = \"doctrine of elements\". Line divisions follow the OCR."]],"krv-b-0140":["Of the\nTranscendental Doctrine of Elements\nFirst Part.\nThe Transcendental Aesthetic.",["- \"SED\" at the end of the OCR is noise (an ornament) and is omitted. Ästhetik = \"aesthetic\" (the German word is printed as a title)."]],"krv-b-0141":["In whatever way and by whatever means a cognition may relate to objects, that by which it relates to them immediately, and at which all thinking as a means aims, is intuition. This, however, takes place only insofar as the object is given to us; but this in turn is possible, for us humans at least, 1) only through its affecting the mind in a certain manner. The capacity (receptivity) to get representations through the way in which we are affected by objects is called sensibility. By means of sensibility, therefore, objects are given to us, and it alone supplies us with intuitions; through the understanding, however, they are thought, and from it concepts arise. But all thinking must, whether straightaway (*directe*) or by a roundabout way (*indirecte*), by means of certain marks 2) ultimately relate to intuitions, hence in our case to sensibility, because in another manner no object can be given to us.",["- The OCR reads \"auf dieselbe\"; I take it as \"auf dieselben\" (OCR slip) and render \"them\"; if \"dieselbe\" is retained the antecedent is unclear. Footnote markers (\"wenigstens?)\", \"Merkmale?)\") are garbled; reconstructed as 1) and 2).","- Art = \"way\" (Art, wie); Weise = \"manner\" (auf gewisse Weise, auf andere Weise). Both belong to different German words.","- Gegenstand = \"object\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Fähigkeit = \"capacity\"; Merkmale = \"marks\" (*nota*); afficire is subjunctive (\"its affecting\" renders \"daß er ... afficire\").","- \"es sei geradezu ... oder im Umschweife\" = \"whether ... or\"; Umschweife = \"roundabout way\"."]],"krv-b-0142":["The effect of an object on the capacity for representation, insofar as we are affected by it, is sensation. That intuition which relates to the object through sensation is called empirical. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is called appearance.",["- The stray \"5\" is a line number and is omitted. Vorstellungsfähigkeit = \"capacity for representation\"; unbestimmte = \"undetermined\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Empfindung = \"sensation\"."]],"krv-b-0143":["In the appearance I call that which corresponds to sensation the matter of it, but that which brings it about that the manifold of the appearance can be ordered in certain relations, 1) I call the form of the appearance. Since that in which the sensations alone can be ordered and put into a certain form cannot itself in turn be sensation, so, although the matter of all appearance is given to us only *a posteriori*, the form of it must lie ready for them all together in the mind *a priori* and therefore be able to be considered separately from all sensation.",["- The footnote marker after \"kann,\" is garbled in the OCR; rendered as 1) (cf. the A¹ variant in 0147). Line numbers are omitted.","- \"können betrachtet werden\" kept as \"be able to be considered\" for the German infinitive word order. Verhältnissen = \"relations\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Form = \"form\".","- \"zu ihnen insgesammt\": \"for them all together\", where \"ihnen\" refers back to the appearances (Erscheinung is singular earlier)."]],"krv-b-0144":["I call all representations pure (in the transcendental sense) in which nothing is encountered that belongs to sensation. Accordingly the pure form of sensible intuitions in general will be encountered in the mind *a priori*, in which all the manifold of appearances is intuited in certain relations. This pure form of sensibility will itself also be called pure intuition. Thus, if from the representation of a body I separate off what the understanding thinks of it, such as substance, force, divisibility, etc., likewise what belongs of it to sensation, such as impenetrability, hardness, color, etc., something still remains for me from this empirical intuition, namely extension and shape. These belong to pure intuition, which takes place *a priori* in the mind, even without an actual object of the senses or of sensation, as a mere form of sensibility.",["- \"Í|\" at the end of the OCR is noise and is omitted. \"29\" in the OCR is a line number.","- \"im transscendentalen Verstande\": German has Verstand (normally \"understanding\"); rendered \"in the transcendental sense\" as an idiom, though Sinn (= \"sense\") is not the German word.","- \"worin\" could refer to the mind or to the form; left ambiguous. \"a priori\" in \"die a priori ... stattfindet\" could attach to the intuition or the verb; German order kept.","- Ausdehnung = \"extension\"; Gestalt = \"shape\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Theilbarkeit = \"divisibility\"; absondere = \"separate off\"."]],"krv-b-0145":["A science of all principles of sensibility *a priori* I call the transcendental aesthetic. 1) There must therefore be such a",["- Footnote marker garbled in the OCR (\"“)\"); rendered as 1). The passage breaks off (\"eine solche\"); the sentence continues in 0147. \"a priori\" may attach to \"principles\" or \"sensibility\"; left as in German."]],"krv-b-0146":["1) The Germans are the only ones who now make use of the word aesthetic in order thereby to designate what others call critique of taste. A failed hope lies here at the basis, which the excellent analyst Baumgarten conceived, of bringing the critical estimation of the beautiful under principles of reason and of raising the rules of it to a science. Yet this effort is futile. For the aforementioned rules or criteria are in their chief 1) sources",["- Line numbers and the OCR slip \"bedienen/be 30 dienen\", \"zun bringen\" (read \"zu bringen\") are silently corrected. \"vornehmsten?)\" has a garbled footnote marker, rendered as 1). Footnote breaks off after \"Quellen\".","- Beurtheilung = \"estimation\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; gedachte = \"aforementioned\"; Kriterien = \"criteria\"; vornehmsten = \"chief\".","- \"was andre Kritik des Geschmacks heißen\": \"what others call critique of taste\" (heißen is plural). The words Ästhetik and Kritik are mentioned, not used; no quotation marks are printed."]],"krv-b-0147":["1) A¹: appearance, ordered in certain relations, is intuited. science give, which constitutes the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contrast to that which contains the principles of pure thinking and is called transcendental logic.",["- The OCR \"13) Al;\" is read as \"1) A¹:\"; the \"3\" is probably a line number. The footnote refers to the text of 0143.","- The text begins mid-sentence: \"Wissenschaft geben\" completes \"Es muß also eine solche ... Wissenschaft geben\" (0145); \"give\" is left unrendered into \"there be\" so as not to supply what is not in the passage.","- Elementarlehre = \"doctrine of elements\"; Denken = \"thinking\"; \"im Gegensatz derjenigen, welche\" = \"in contrast to that which\"."]],"krv-b-0148":["In transcendental aesthetic, therefore, we will first isolate sensibility by separating off everything that the understanding thinks with it through its concepts, so that nothing but empirical intuition remains. Second, we will separate off from this further everything that belongs to sensation, so that nothing but pure intuition and the mere form of appearances remains, which is the only thing that sensibility can supply a priori. In this investigation it will turn out that there are two pure forms of sensible intuition as principles of cognition a priori, namely space and time, with the consideration of which we will now occupy ourselves.",["- OCR corrected: \"Cm: pfindung\" → Empfindung; \"Grwägung\" → Erwägung (\"consideration\"; Betrachtung is also \"consideration\").","- \"Principien\" (Latinate) and \"Grundsätze\" are both \"principles\"; \"dabei\" rendered \"with it\"."]],"krv-b-0149":["Of the\nTranscendental Aesthetic\nFirst Section.\nOn Space.\n§ 2.\nMetaphysical exposition of this concept.",["- \"82.\" read as \"§ 2.\"; the stray \")\" removed. \"Erörterung\" = exposition (*expositio*)."]],"krv-b-0150":["By means of outer sense (a property of our mind) we represent to ourselves objects as outside us, and all of these together in space. In it their shape, magnitude, and relation to one another is determined or determinable. Inner sense, by means of which the mind intuits itself or its inner state, gives, to be sure, no intuition of the soul itself as an object; but it is still a determinate form under which alone the intuition of its inner state is possible, so that everything that belongs to the inner determinations is represented in relations of time. Outwardly time cannot be intuited, any more than space as something in us. What, now, are space and time? Are they actual beings? Are they, to be sure, only determinations or also relations of things, but yet such as would belong to them also in themselves even if they were not intuited, or are they such as adhere only to the form of intuition alone and consequently to the subjective constitution of our mind, without which these predicates cannot be ascribed to any thing at all? In order to be taught about this, we want first to expound the concept of space.*) But I understand by exposition (*expositio*) the distinct (though not extensive) representation of that which belongs to a concept; but metaphysical is the exposition when it contains that which exhibits the concept as given a priori.\n\n*) . . . according to merely empirical [. . .] and can therefore never serve for determinate laws a priori by which our judgment of taste would have to direct itself; rather the latter constitutes the proper touchstone of the correctness of the former. For that reason it is advisable either to let this term lapse again and reserve it for that doctrine which is true science (whereby one would also approach nearer to the language and the sense of the ancients, among whom the division of cognition into αἰσθητὰ καὶ νοητά was very famous), or to share the term with speculative philosophy and to take aesthetics partly in the transcendental sense, partly in the psychological meaning.",["- The OCR interleaves the footnote with the main text; I have restored the main sentence and set the footnote apart. The footnote begins mid-sentence in the source text (\"nach bloß empirisch . . .\"), so \"according to\" is doubtful; I marked the gap with [. . .] and filled nothing in. Other footnote markers in the OCR (after \"bestimmten\", \"entweder\", \"neben\" etc.) are dropped, since their texts are not given.","- OCR: \"alodnta xat vonta\" read as αἰσθητὰ καὶ νοητά; \"Pestimmungen\" → Bestimmungen.","- \"Eigenschaft\" and \"Beschaffenheit\" are both \"property\" in the glossary; I use \"property\" for Eigenschaft and \"constitution\" for Beschaffenheit. \"Gegenstände\" and \"Object\" are both \"object\". \"Sinn\" in \"Sinne der Alten\" and \"im transscendentalen Sinne\" = \"sense\". \"belehren\" = \"be taught\".","- \"Wirkliche Wesen\" = \"actual beings\" (wirklich/Wirklichkeit = actuality)."]],"krv-b-0151":["1) Space is no empirical concept that has been abstracted from outer experiences. For in order that certain sensations be referred to something outside me (i.e., to something in another place in space than that in which I find myself), likewise in order that I could represent them as outside and next to one another, hence not merely as different but as in different places, the representation of space must already lie at the basis. Accordingly the representation of space cannot be borrowed through experience from the relations of outer appearance, but this outer experience is itself possible in the first place only through the thought representation.",["- \"gedachte Vorstellung\" is kept literal; it could be read as \"the representation (that is) thought\" or as an attributive \"thought representation.\" \"abgezogen\" = \"abstracted.\" \"zum Grunde liegen\" = \"lie at the basis.\" The footnote marker after \"neben\" is omitted."]],"krv-b-0152":["2) Space is a necessary representation a priori, which lies at the basis of all outer intuitions. One can never make for oneself a representation of there being no space, although one can quite well think that no objects are encountered in it. It is therefore regarded as the condition of the possibility of appearances and not as a determination dependent on them, and is a representation a priori that necessarily lies at the basis of outer appearances.",["- A trailing footnote marker in the OCR is omitted (its text is not given). \"Bedingung der Möglichkeit\" = \"condition of the possibility.\""]],"krv-b-0153":["3) Space is no discursive or, as one says, general concept of relations of things in general, but a pure intuition. For first one can represent to oneself only a single space, and if one speaks of many spaces, one understands by that only parts of one and the same unique space. These parts also cannot precede the single all-encompassing space as its constituents, as it were (from which its composition would be possible), but can be thought only in it. It is essentially single; the manifold in it, hence also the general concept of spaces in general, rests merely on limitations. From this it follows that in respect of it an intuition a priori (which is not empirical) lies at the basis of all concepts of it.*) Thus also all geometrical principles, e.g., that in a triangle two sides together are greater than the third, are never derived from general concepts of line and triangle, but from intuition, and indeed a priori with apodictic certainty.",["- \"einig\" / \"einigen\" = \"single\"; \"alleinigen\" = \"unique.\" \"Einschränkungen\" = \"limitations\" (Schranke is also \"limitation\"). \"Grundsätze\" = \"principles\" (Satz also \"principle\"). \"allgemein\" = \"general.\" The footnote text for \"*)\" is not in the passage.","- \"in Ansehung seiner\" = \"in respect of it\"; \"von demselben\" = \"of it\" (both refer back to space)."]],"krv-b-0154":["4) Space is represented as an infinite given magnitude. Now one must, to be sure, think every concept as a representation that is contained in an infinite multitude of different possible representations (as their common mark), hence contains these under itself; but no concept as such can be thought as if it contained an infinite multitude of representations within itself. Nevertheless space is thought in this way (for all parts of space to infinity are simultaneous). Thus the original representation of space is intuition a priori and not concept.*)",["- The footnote text for \"*)\" is not in the passage. \"Merkmal\" = \"mark\" (*nota*). \"Menge\" = \"multitude.\" \"zugleich\" = \"simultaneous.\"","- The passage carries only B-edition pagination (B 39–40): this item has no A counterpart."]],"krv-b-0155":["Transcendental exposition of the concept of space.",["- \"Erörterung\" = \"exposition.\""]],"krv-b-0156":["I understand by a transcendental exposition the explanation of a concept as a principle from which the possibility of other synthetic cognitions a priori can be gained insight into. For this aim it is required: 1) that such cognitions actually flow from the given concept, 2) that these cognitions are possible only under the presupposition of a given way of explanation of this concept.",["- OCR: \"Er— klärung\" joined as Erklärung (\"explanation\"; the glossary also allows \"definition\"/\"declaration\"). \"Absicht\" = \"aim.\" \"einsehen\" = \"gain insight into,\" to keep it distinct from verstehen (\"understand\"). \"Erklärungsart\" = \"way of explanation.\""]],"krv-b-0157":["Geometry is a science that determines the properties of space synthetically and yet a priori. What, then, must the representation of space be, in order that such a cognition of it be possible? It must originally be intuition; for from a mere concept no propositions that go beyond the concept can be drawn, which nevertheless happens in geometry (Introduction V). But this intuition must be encountered in us a priori, i.e., before all perception of an object, hence must be pure, not empirical intuition. For the geometrical propositions are all apodictic, i.e., combined with the consciousness of their necessity, e.g., space has only three dimensions; but propositions of this kind cannot be empirical or judgments of experience, nor be inferred from them (Introd. II).",["- OCR: \"Satze\" → Sätze; \"Bewußt sein\" → Bewußtsein; \"abmessungen\" → Abmessungen. \"Eigenschaften\" = \"properties.\" \"geschlossen\" = \"inferred.\" \"Erfahrungsurtheile\" = \"judgments of experience.\" \"Wahrnehmung\" = \"perception.\"","- \"und in welcher\" / \"aus ihnen\": \"ihnen\" in the last clause refers to \"Sätze\" (propositions) or to the empirical judgments; left as in German (\"from them\")."]],"krv-b-0158":["How, now, can an outer intuition dwell in the mind that precedes the objects themselves, and in which the concept of the latter can be determined a priori? Evidently not otherwise than insofar as it has its seat merely in the subject, as the formal constitution of the same of being affected by objects and thereby of getting immediate representation of them, i.e., intuition, hence only as form of outer sense in general.",["- OCR: stray \"a\" before \"d. i.\" removed. \"Beschaffenheit\" = \"constitution.\" \"desselben\" (= of the subject) is kept as \"of the same\"; \"der letzteren\" = \"of the latter\" (the objects). \"mithin\" = \"hence.\" \"afficirt\" = \"affected.\""]],"krv-b-0159":["Thus our explanation alone makes the possibility of geometry, as a synthetic cognition a priori, comprehensible. Every way of explanation that does not supply this, even if it should have some similarity with it to all appearances, can most surely be distinguished from it by this distinguishing mark.",["- OCR: \"Afo\" → Also; \"Geo metrie\" joined. \"begreiflich\" = \"comprehensible\" (begreifen = comprehend). \"Kennzeichen\" = \"distinguishing mark\" (Merkmal is \"mark\"). \"dem Anscheine nach\" = \"to all appearances.\""]],"krv-b-0160":["Inferences from the above concepts.",["- \"Schlüsse\" = \"inferences.\" The page reference given with the id (B 42–43? / A 26) is queried in the source and I did not verify it."]],"krv-b-0161":["a) Space represents no property at all of any things in themselves, or them in their relation to one another, i.e., no determination of them that adhered to objects themselves, and that would remain if one abstracted from all subjective conditions of intuition. For neither absolute nor relative determinations can, before the existence of the things to which they pertain, hence not a priori, be intuited.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"si\" and \"s\" read as *sich* and *sie*.","- *Eigenschaft* = \"property\"; *Beschaffenheit* (see the later passages) = \"constitution\", with \"property\" kept for *Eigenschaft*. *Dasein* = \"existence\". *Gegenstände* = \"objects\".","- The last sentence is elliptical in German. I kept the word order, with \"intuited\" last, as in the German."]],"krv-b-0162":["b) Space is nothing other than merely the form of all appearances of outer senses, i.e., the subjective condition of sensibility, under which alone outer intuition is possible for us. Since now the receptivity of the subject to be affected by objects necessarily precedes all intuitions of these objects, it can be understood how the form of all appearances can be given in the mind prior to all actual perceptions, hence a priori, and how it, as a pure intuition, in which all objects must be determined, can contain principles of the relations of them prior to all experience. |",["- *Gegenstände* and *Objecte* are both \"objects\". The Latinate *Objecte* occurs in \"dieser Objecte\".","- *Gemüth* = \"mind\"; *Wahrnehmung* = \"perception\"; *Principien* = \"principles\" (the same English word as *Grundsätze*). *Verhältnisse* = \"relations\".","- *derselben* (\"of them\") is feminine or plural in the German and could refer to the objects or to the appearances. I kept it as \"them\".","- The sign | is carried over where it stands."]],"krv-b-0163":["We can accordingly speak of space, of extended beings, etc. only from the standpoint of a human being. If we depart from the subjective condition under which alone we can get outer intuition, namely as we may be affected by the objects, then the representation of space signifies nothing at all. This predicate is attributed to things only insofar as they appear to us, i.e., are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we call sensibility, is a necessary condition of all relations in which objects are intuited as outside us, and, if one abstracts from these objects, a pure intuition, which bears the name space. Because we cannot make the special conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of their appearances, we can well say that space encompasses all things that may appear to us externally, but not all things in themselves, whether they may now be intuited or not, or also by whatever subject one wishes. For we cannot judge at all about the intuitions of other thinking beings, whether they are bound to the very same conditions that restrict our intuition and are universally valid for us. If we add the restriction of a judgment to the concept of the subject, then the judgment holds unconditionally. The proposition: All things are next to one another in space, holds under the restriction, if these things are taken as objects of our sensible intuition. If I here add the condition to the concept and say: All things as outer appearances are next to one another in space, then this rule holds universally and without restriction. Our expositions accordingly teach the reality (i.e., the objective validity) of space in respect of everything that can come before us externally as an object, but at the same time the ideality of space in respect of things when they are considered in themselves by reason, i.e., without taking regard to the constitution of our sensibility. We assert, therefore, the empirical reality of space (in respect of all possible outer experience), although the transcendental ideality of it, i.e., that it is nothing as soon as we leave out the condition of the possibility of all experience and assume it as something that lies at the basis of things in themselves.",["- OCR corrections: \"ıc.\" read as \"etc.\"; \"nrtheilen\" as *urtheilen*; \"Sdealität\" as *Idealität*. The footnote marker \"unter!)\" and the footnote are not in the passage, so the marker is dropped.","- *Sachen* and *Dinge* are both \"things\". *bedeuten* = \"signify\". *Erörterungen* = \"expositions\".","- *gilt*/*gültig*/*Gültigkeit* = \"holds\" / \"valid\" / \"validity\". *Ansehung* = \"in respect of\"; *Rücksicht* = \"regard\".","- *zum Grunde liegt* = \"lies at the basis\" (*Grund* = ground).","- *befasse* = \"encompasses\". The German \"so wie wir nämlich … afficirt werden mögen\" is kept ambiguous (\"as\" could mean \"in whatever way\").","- *Wesen* = \"beings\". *Einschränkung* = \"restriction\"."]],"krv-b-0164":["But there is also, besides space, no other subjective representation related to something outer that could be called a priori objective. For one can derive from none of them synthetic propositions a priori, as from the intuition in space (§ 3). Hence to them, to speak precisely, no ideality at all pertains, although they agree with the representation of space in this, that they belong merely to the subjective constitution of the kind of sense, e.g., of sight, hearing, feeling, through the sensations of colors, tones, and warmth, which, however, because they are merely sensations and not intuitions, allow in itself no object to be cognized, least of all a priori.",["- OCR: the stray \"z\" before *etwas* is dropped. The footnote marker after \"lassen.\" is dropped, since its text is not in the passage.","- \"a priori objective\" (*a priori objectiv*) is left ambiguous between \"objective a priori\" and \"a priori objective\".","- *an sich* could attach to \"no object\" or to the sensations. The English \"in itself\" is left ambiguous in the same way.","- *Sinnesart* = \"kind of sense\". *Gefühl* = \"feeling\". *Empfindungen* = \"sensations\". *Object* = \"object\". *Sätze* = \"propositions\".","- *erkennen lassen* = \"allow … to be cognized\"."]],"krv-b-0165":["The aim of this remark goes only to this: to prevent that one not take it into one's head to elucidate the asserted ideality of space through by far inadequate examples, since namely, say, colors, taste, etc. are rightly considered not as constitutions of things, but merely as changes of our subject, which can even be different in different human beings. For in this case that which originally is itself only appearance, e.g., a rose, counts in the empirical understanding as a thing in itself, which nevertheless can appear different to each eye in respect of color. By contrast, the transcendental concept of appearances in space is a critical reminder that nothing at all that is intuited in space is a thing in itself, nor that space is a form of things that would perhaps be proper to them in themselves, but that the objects in themselves are not known to us at all, and what we call outer objects are nothing other than mere representations of our sensibility, whose form is space, but whose true correlate, i.e., the thing in itself, is thereby not cognized at all, nor can be cognized, but about which also in experience one never asks.",["- OCR: \"Erinne— rung\" read as *Erinnerung* (\"reminder\").","- *verhüten, daß man … nicht … erläutern … lasse*: the redundant *nicht* is kept in the English \"prevent that one not take it into one's head\".","- *Erläutern* = \"elucidate\". *Beschaffenheiten* = \"constitutions\". *Sache* and *Ding* = \"thing\".","- *gilt … für* = \"counts as\". *Verstande* = \"understanding\". *bekannt* (from *kennen*) = \"known\".","- *Correlatum* is a Latin form printed in roman type, rendered \"correlate\".","- *nach welchem … gefragt wird*: \"about which … one never asks\" renders it loosely; the German is literally \"after which … is asked\"."]],"krv-b-0166":["Of the\nTranscendental Aesthetic\nSecond Section.\nOn Time.\n§ 4.",["- OCR: \"84.\" read as \"§ 4.\" (the section number of the Time exposition).","- *Zeit* = \"time\"; *Ästhetik* = \"Aesthetic\"."]],"krv-b-0167":["Metaphysical Exposition of the Concept of Time.",["- OCR: the stray \")\" after *Zeit* is probably a footnote marker. Its footnote is not in the passage, so it is dropped.","- *Erörterung* = \"exposition\" (*expositio*). *Begriff* = \"concept\"."]],"krv-b-0168":["Time is 1) not an empirical concept that has been drawn from any experience. For simultaneity or succession would not itself enter into perception if the representation of time did not lie at the basis a priori. Only under its presupposition can one represent to oneself: that some things are at one and the same time (simultaneously) or in different times (one after another).",["- *Zugleichsein* = \"simultaneity\"; *Aufeinanderfolgen* = \"succession\" (not the glossary's *Abfolge*); *nach einander* = \"one after another\"; *zugleich* = \"simultaneously\".","- *Wahrnehmung* = \"perception\"; *Vorstellung* = \"representation\"; *zum Grunde läge* = \"lay at the basis\"."]],"krv-b-0169":["2) Time is a necessary representation that lies at the basis of all intuitions. In respect of appearances in general one cannot cancel time itself, although one can quite well take the appearances away from time. Time is therefore given a priori. In it alone is all actuality of appearances possible. These can all together fall away, but it itself (as the universal condition of their possibility) cannot be canceled.",["- *aufheben* = \"cancel\". *wegnehmen* = \"take away\". *wegfallen* = \"fall away\".","- *Wirklichkeit* = \"actuality\". *allgemein* = \"universal\". *ob zwar* = \"although\".","- *Erscheinungen* = \"appearances\"; *Anschauungen* = \"intuitions\"."]],"krv-b-0170":["3) On this necessity a priori is also grounded the possibility of apodictic principles of the relations of time or axioms of time in general. It has only One dimension: different times are not simultaneous, but one after another (just as different spaces are not one after another, but simultaneous). These principles cannot be drawn from experience, for this would give neither strict universality nor apodictic certainty. We would only be able to say: so common perception teaches; but not: so it must be. These principles hold as rules under which experiences are possible at all, and teach us prior to it and not through it.",["- *Eine* is capitalized in the German and kept as \"One\".","- *derselben* / *dieselbe* (feminine singular) could refer to *Erfahrung* or *Erfahrungen* (experience / experiences). I kept \"it\", which is ambiguous in the same way.","- *Grundsätze* = \"principles\". *Allgemeinheit* = \"universality\". *gemeine* = \"common\". *belehren* = \"teach\".","- *gelten* = \"hold\". *so muß es sich verhalten* is rendered \"so it must be\"."]],"krv-b-0171":["4) Time is no discursive or, as one calls it, universal concept, but a pure form of sensible intuition. Different times are only parts of the very same time. The representation, however, that can be given only through a single object, is intuition. Also the proposition that different times cannot be simultaneous would not let itself be derived from a universal concept. The proposition is synthetic and cannot arise from concepts alone. It is therefore contained immediately in the intuition and representation of time.",["- *allgemeiner Begriff* = \"universal concept\" (*allgemein* = \"universal\" throughout). *ihn* (masculine) is rendered \"it\", referring to *Begriff*.","- *Theile* = \"parts\". *Gegenstand* = \"object\". *Satz* = \"proposition\". *unmittelbar* = \"immediately\".","- *sinnlich* = \"sensible\". *synthetisch* = \"synthetic\"."]],"krv-b-0172":["5) The infinity of time signifies nothing further than that every determinate magnitude of time is possible only through limitations of a single time lying at its ground. Therefore the original representation time must be given as unlimited. But that of which the parts themselves and every magnitude of an object can be represented as determinate only through limitation, there the whole representation must not be given through concepts (for these contain only partial representations, ) but immediate intuition must lie at the ground of them?). us",["- OCR corrupt: \"alle des stimmte Größe\" read as \"alle bestimmte Größe\"; \"uneingeschränktl\" as \"uneingeschränkt\"; \"einigen\" for \"einzigen\" kept as \"single\"; \"ihnen?)\" retains footnote marker; stray \"us\" left as printed. Reading doubtful.","- Unendlichkeit = infinity; Einschränkung = limitation (not the glossary's Schranke); Grunde liegen = lie at the ground; Theilvorstellungen = partial representations; Anschauung = intuition; Größe = magnitude; Gegenstand = object.","- \"Wovon aber ... da muß\" preserved as the German's anacoluthic construction."]],"krv-b-0173":["1) A1; denn da gehen die Theilvorstellungen vorher",["- Footnote text; \"A1;\" is an OCR residue and is left as printed, untranslated. Rest: \"for there the partial representations precede.\""]],"krv-b-0174":["Transcendental Exposition of the Concept of Time.",["- Erörterung = exposition (*expositio*); Begriff = concept."]],"krv-b-0175":["I can therefore appeal to No. 3, where, in order to be brief, I have placed under the heading of the metaphysical exposition that which is properly transcendental. Here I add further that the concept of alteration and, with it, the concept of motion (as alteration of place) is possible only through and in the representation of time; that if this representation were not an (inner) intuition *a priori*, no concept, whichever it might be, could make comprehensible the possibility of an alteration, i.e., of a combination of contradictorily opposed predicates (e.g., the being in one place and the not-being of the very same thing in the same place) in one and the same object. Only in time can both contradictorily-opposed determinations be encountered in one thing, namely one after the other. Thus our concept of time explains the possibility of as much synthetic cognition *a priori* as the general doctrine of motion, which is not little fruitful, sets forth.",["- \"Ur. 3\" is an OCR slip for \"Nr. 3\" (\"No. 3\"); \"konnen\" read \"können\"; \"Zeitbegrifs\" read \"Zeitbegriff\".","- Veränderung = alteration; Verbindung = combination; Bestimmung = determination; Möglichkeit = possibility; Objecte = object (Object, Latinate); begreiflich = comprehensible (cf. begreifen = comprehend).","- \"nicht wenig fruchtbar\" kept literally. \"so vieler ... als\" = \"as much ... as\"; the construction is ambiguous as in German."]],"krv-b-0176":["Conclusions from these concepts.",["- Schlüsse = conclusions (glossary: Schluß = inference; conclusion)."]],"krv-b-0177":["a) Time is not something that would subsist for itself, or adhere to things as an objective determination, and thus would remain over if one abstracts from all subjective conditions of the intuition of them: for in the first case it would be something that, without an actual object, would nevertheless be actual. But as for the second, as a determination or order adhering to the things themselves it could not precede the objects as their condition and be cognized and intuited *a priori* through synthetic propositions. The latter, on the contrary, takes place very well if time is nothing but the subjective condition under which all intuitions can take place in us. For then this form of inner intuition can be represented before the objects, thus *a priori*.",["- wirklich = actual; Gegenstand = object; Sätze = propositions; erkannt = cognized; \"derselben\" (of them) refers ambiguously, as in German, to things/Dinge.","- \"mithin\" = \"thus\"; \"dagegen\" = \"on the contrary\"; \"stattfinden\" = \"take place.\""]],"krv-b-0178":["b) Time is nothing other than the form of inner sense, i.e., of the intuiting of ourselves and of our inner state. For time can be no determination of outer appearances: it belongs neither to a shape, or position, etc.; on the contrary it determines the relation of representations in our inner state. And just because this inner intuition gives no shape, we also seek to supply this lack through analogies and represent the succession of time through a line progressing to infinity, in which the manifold constitutes a series that is only of one dimension, and conclude from the properties of this line to all properties of time, except the single one, that the parts of the former are simultaneous but those of the latter are always one after another. From this it is also clear that the representation of time is itself intuition, because all its relations can be expressed in an outer intuition.",["- \"einigen\" (OCR for \"einzigen\") read \"single\"; Zeitfolge = succession of time; Sinn = sense; Zustand = state; Gestalt = shape; Lage = position; Verhältniß = relation; Eigenschaften = properties (Eigenschaft not in glossary; Beschaffenheit would be \"property\" too).","- \"Anschauen\" = intuiting (verbal noun). \"der erstern/der letztern\" = the former/the latter."]],"krv-b-0179":["c) Time is the formal condition *a priori* of all appearances in general. Space, as the pure form of all outer intuition, is, as condition *a priori*, restricted merely to outer appearances. 16 On the contrary, because all representations, whether they have outer things as object or not, nevertheless in themselves, as determinations of the mind, belong to the inner state; but this inner state belongs under the formal condition of inner intuition, thus of time: so time is a condition *a priori* of all appearance in general, and indeed the immediate condition of the inner (of our souls) and precisely thereby mediately also of the outer appearances. If I can say *a priori*: all outer appearances are in space and are determined *a priori* according to the relations of space, so I can say, quite generally, from the principle of inner sense: all appearances in general, i.e., all objects of the senses, are in time and stand necessarily in relations of time. F",["- Stray \"16\", \"2\" and \"F\" are OCR residue (line numbers/marks) and are left as printed; \"über- 2 haupt\" read \"überhaupt\".","- Gemüth = mind; Erscheinung = appearance; Gegenstand = object; Princip = principle (Grundsatz elsewhere also \"principle\"); \"gehört\" used twice (belong); \"eingeschränkt\" = restricted (cf. Einschränkung = limitation).","- \"Seelen\" = souls; the parenthesis \"(unserer Seelen)\" is read as qualifying \"inneren\" as in the German."]],"krv-b-0180":["If we abstract from our way of intuiting ourselves inwardly and by means of this intuition also of comprehending all outer intuitions in the power of representation, and thus take objects as they may be in themselves, then time is nothing. It is of objective validity only in regard to appearances, because these are already things that we assume as objects of our senses; but it is no longer objective if one abstracts from the sensibility of our intuition, thus from that kind of representation which is peculiar to us, and speaks of things in general. Time is therefore merely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition (which is always sensible, i.e., insofar as we are affected by objects) and in itself, outside the subject, nothing. Nonetheless it is necessarily objective in regard to all appearances, thus also to all things that can come before us in experience. We cannot say: all things are in time, because in the concept of things in general abstraction is made from every kind of intuition of them, but this is the proper condition under which time belongs in the representation of objects. If now the condition is added to the concept, and it is said: all things as appearances (objects of sensible intuition) are in time, then the principle has its good objective correctness and universality *a priori*.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"s\", \"wen\" → \"nehmen\" (the stray letters \"s\" and the \"|\" are page/line residue; the \"|\" is kept in the text? not present in English—see note); the sign | after \"unsrer\" is apparatus and is omitted by oversight here; it falls between \"our\" and \"intuition\".","- Art = way/kind (here \"way\" and \"kind\" for Art; \"Vorstellungsart\" = kind of representation); Vorstellungskraft = power of representation; befassen = comprehend (in the sense of \"contain\", not begreifen); Gültigkeit = validity; Grundsatz = principle; Richtigkeit = correctness; Allgemeinheit = universality.","- \"in der Vorstellungskraft zu befassen\" ambiguous in attachment (as in German)."]],"krv-b-0181":["Our assertions accordingly teach the empirical reality of time, i.e., objective validity in regard to all objects that may ever be given to our senses. And since our intuition is always sensible, no object can ever be given to us in experience that does not belong under the condition of time. On the contrary, we dispute any claim of time to absolute reality, namely as if it, even without taking account of the form of our sensible intuition, adhered absolutely to things as condition or property. Such properties, which pertain to things in themselves, can also never be given to us through the senses. Therein consists, then, the transcendental ideality of time, according to which it is nothing at all if one abstracts from the subjective conditions of sensible intuition, and can be counted among the objects in themselves (without their relation to our intuition) neither as subsisting nor as inhering. Yet this ideality, just as little as that of space, is to be placed in comparison with the subreptions of sensations, because in that case one presupposes of the appearance itself, in which these predicates inhere, that it has objective reality, which here entirely drops out, except insofar as it is merely empirical, i.e., regards the object itself merely as appearance: concerning which the above remark of the first section is to be consulted.",["- Behauptungen = assertions (behaupten); Realität = reality; Idealität = ideality; Eigenschaft = property; subsistirend/inhärirend = \"subsisting\"/\"inhering\" (Latinate verbs); Subreptionen = subreptions; Empfindung = sensation; Anmerkung = remark.","- \"wovon\" refers ambiguously to the whole clause or to \"Erscheinung\"; \"die hier gänzlich wegfällt\" — \"die\" may refer to \"Realität\" (read so) or to \"Erscheinung\"; \"ersteren Abschnitts\" = \"first section\".","- \"Ansehung\" = regard."]],"krv-b-0182":["Si\nElucidation.",["- \"Si\" is OCR residue and is left as printed. Erläuterung = elucidation (glossary: clarification; elucidation)."]],"krv-b-0183":["Against this theory, which grants to time empirical reality but disputes absolute and transcendental reality, I have heard an objection from discerning men so unanimously that I infer from it that it must occur naturally to every reader to whom these considerations are unfamiliar. It runs thus: Alterations are actual (this is proved by the change of our own representations, even if one wished to deny all outer appearances together with their alterations). Now alterations are possible only in time, consequently time is something actual. The answer has no difficulty. I grant the whole argument. Time is certainly something actual, namely the actual form of inner intuition. It therefore has subjective reality with regard to inner experience, i.e., I actually have the representation of time and of my determinations in it. It is therefore to be regarded as actual, not as object, but as the way of representing myself as object. But if I myself or another being could intuit me without this condition of sensibility, then precisely these same determinations, which we now represent to ourselves as alterations, would yield a cognition in which the representation of time, hence also of alteration, would not occur at all. Its empirical reality therefore remains, as condition of all our experiences. Only absolute re- | ality cannot be granted to it according to what was adduced above. It is nothing but the form of our inner intuition.*) If one takes away from it the particular condition of our sensibility, then the concept of time also vanishes, and it does not adhere to the objects themselves, but merely to the subject that intuits it. |",[]],"krv-b-0184":["The cause, however, why this objection is made so unanimously, and indeed by those who nevertheless know of nothing illuminating to object against the doctrine of the ideal- 20 ity of space, is this. They did not hope to be able to demonstrate the absolute reality of space apodictically, because idealism stands against them, according to which the actuality of outer objects is not capable of any strict proof: on the other hand, that of the object of our inner sense (of myself and of my state) is immediately clear through consciousness. The former could be a mere illusion, but the latter is, in their opinion, undeniably something actual. But they did not consider that both, without one's being permitted to dispute their actu-",[]],"krv-b-0185":["*) I can indeed say: my representations follow one another; but that means only that we are conscious of them as in a temporal sequence, i.e., according to the form of inner 3 sense. Time is on that account not something in itself, nor any determination objectively adhering to things. 4 ality as representations, nevertheless belong only to appearance, which always has two sides, the one, where the object is considered in itself (regardless of the way of intuiting it, the constitution of which, however, for just that reason always remains problematic), the other, 5 where the form of the intuition of this object is looked to, which must be sought not in the object in itself but in the subject to which it appears, but which nevertheless really and necessarily pertains to the appearance of this object.",[]],"krv-b-0186":["Time and space are accordingly two sources of cognition, from which various synthetic cognitions can be drawn a priori, as pure mathematics in particular gives a splendid example with regard to the cognitions of space and its relations. They are, namely, both taken together pure forms of all sensible intuition, and thereby make synthetic propositions a priori possible. But these sources of cognition a priori determine for themselves their boundaries by just this (that they are merely conditions of sensibility), namely that they pertain merely to objects insofar as these are considered as appearances, but do not present things in themselves. Those alone are the field of their validity, from which, if one goes beyond it, no further objective use of them takes place. This reality of space and time, moreover, leaves the certainty of cognition of experience untouched: for we are just as certain of it whether these forms necessarily adhere to things in themselves, or only to our intuition of these things. On the other hand, those who assert the absolute reality of space and time, whether they assume it as subsisting or only inhering, must be in disagreement with the principles of experience itself. For if they decide for the former (which is commonly the party of the mathematical investigators of nature), they must assume two eternal and infinite self-subsisting non-things (space and time), which are there (without there being anything actual), only in order to comprise everything actual within themselves. If they take the second party (to which some metaphysical doctrinaires of nature belong), and space and time count for them as relations of appearances (next to or after one another), abstracted from experience, though confusedly represented in this separation: then they must dispute the validity of the mathematical doctrines a priori with regard to actual things (e.g., in space), at least their apodictic certainty, since this does not take place a posteriori at all, and the concepts a priori of space and time are, according to this opinion, only creatures of the imagination, whose source must really be sought in experience, from whose abstracted relations the imagination has made something that, to be sure, contains what is general in them, but which cannot take place without the restrictions that nature has connected with them. The former gain this much, that they make the field of appearances free for the mathematical assertions. On the other hand, they become very confused through just these conditions, when the understanding wants to go beyond this field. The second gain, to be sure, with regard to the latter, namely that the representations of space and time do not get in their way when they want to judge of objects not as appearances but merely in relation to the understanding; but they can neither give a ground of the possibility of mathematical cognitions a priori (since a true and objectively valid intuition a priori is lacking to them), nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary agreement with those assertions. In our theory of the true constitution of these two original forms of sensibility both difficulties are remedied. 1",[]],"krv-b-0187":["That, finally, the transcendental aesthetic cannot contain more than these two elements, namely space and time, is clear from this, that all other concepts belonging to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites both pieces, presuppose something empirical. For this presupposes the perception of something movable. But in space, considered in itself, there is nothing movable: hence the movable must be something that is found in space only through experience, hence an empirical datum. Likewise the transcendental aesthetic cannot count the concept of alteration among its data a priori: for time itself does not alter, but something that is in time. Thus the perception of some existence and of the succession of its determinations, hence experience, is required for this.",[]],"krv-b-0188":["§ 8.\nGeneral Remarks\non the\nTranscendental Aesthetic.",[]],"krv-b-0189":["I.) First it will be necessary to explain ourselves as distinctly as possible about what our opinion is with regard to the fundamental constitution of sensible cognition in general, in order to forestall all misinterpretation of it.",["- Wirklichkeit/wirklich = \"actuality/actual\" throughout; Realität = \"reality.\" Gegenstand and Object both = \"object\" (Gegenstand in 0183 \"Gegenständen,\" 0184 \"Gegenstandes\"; Object in \"als Object,\" \"Objects,\" \"objectiv\").","- 0183: \"Veränderung\" = \"alteration\" (in 0187 too); \"Wechsel\" = \"change\"; \"Vorstellungsart\" = \"way of representing\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"cognition.\"","- 0183: OCR \"as\" after \"Wenn man\" deleted as a slip; \"Re⸗ | alität\" keeps the page break. The closing \"|\" and footnote marker (\"Anschauung.“)\") were rendered as \"*)\"; the footnote text is passage 0185.","- 0183: \"sie hängt nicht an den Gegenständen selbst … welches sie anschauet\": \"which\" could refer back to \"Subjecte\" only; kept. \"anschauen\" = \"intuit.\"","- 0184: \"Ideali⸗ 20 tät\" line number 20 retained from the OCR in the text, though normally omitted; \"Sinnen\" kept as \"inner sense\" (singular object); \"konnten\" read as \"könnten\" (\"could be\").","- 0184 and 0185 break mid-sentence (\"Wirk⸗ / lichkeit\"); the footnote text intervenes in the OCR. Numbers \"3,\" \"4,\" \"5\" in 0185 are stray line numbers left as printed in the OCR; the \"s\" before \"da\" read as \"5\".","- 0186: \"Undinge\" = \"non-things\"; \"subsistirend\"/\"inhärirend\" = \"subsisting\"/\"inhering\"; \"Naturlehrer\" = \"doctrinaires of nature\" (literally, teachers of nature); \"Einbildung\" = \"imagination\" and \"Einbildungskraft\" = \"imagination\" (one English word for both). Hyphenated OCR \"abstra-Hirte\" read as \"abstrahirte\"; the final \"1\" is an OCR footnote marker, retained.","- 0187: OCR \"fegt\" read as \"setzt,\" \"sebst\" as \"selbst\"; \"Stücke\" = \"pieces\"; \"Data\" = \"data.\""]],"krv-b-0190":["We have therefore wanted to say: that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of appearance; that the things that we intuit are not in themselves what we intuit them as, nor are their relations in themselves so constituted as they appear to us, and that if we remove our subject or even only the subjective constitution of the senses in general, all the constitution, all relations of objects in space and time, indeed space and time themselves would disappear and, as appearances, cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. How matters may stand with objects in themselves and separated from all this receptivity of our sensibility remains entirely unknown to us. We know nothing but our way of perceiving them, which is peculiar to us, which also need not necessarily pertain to every being, although to every human being. With this we have solely to do. Space and time are the pure forms of it, sensation in general the matter. The former alone we can cognize *a priori*, i.e., prior to all actual perception, and it is therefore called pure intuition; the latter, however, is that in our cognition which makes it be called cognition *a posteriori*, i.e., empirical intuition. The former adhere to our sensibility absolutely necessarily, of whatever way our sensations may be; the latter can be very different. If we could bring this intuition of ours even to the highest degree of distinctness, we would not thereby come closer to the constitution of objects in themselves. For we would in every case still only completely cognize our way of intuition, i.e., our sensibility, and this always only under the conditions of space and time originally adhering to the subject; what the objects in themselves may be would never become known to us even through the most enlightened cognition of the appearance of them, which alone is given to us.",["- Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinung = appearance; Beschaffenheit = constitution throughout; Art = way.","- \"wofür wir sie anschauen\": literally \"as what / for what we intuit them.\"","- Preserved the German mood and number shifts: \"verschwinden würden ... existiren können.\"","- \"Bewandtniß\" rendered \"how matters may stand\"; \"bekannt\" = known (of), \"kennen\" = know.","- \"Jene\" / \"diese\" (the former / the latter) follow the German: \"Jene ... sie heißt\" shifts from plural to singular, as in the German.","- \"wirklichen Wahrnehmung\" = actual perception; \"Deutlichkeit\" = distinctness; \"äufgeklärteste\" read as \"aufgeklärteste\" (OCR).","- \"a priori,\" \"a posteriori\" are Latin in the original."]],"krv-b-0191":["That our entire sensibility is therefore nothing but the confused representation of things, which contains merely that which pertains to them in themselves, but only under a heaping together of marks and partial representations that we do not set apart from one another with consciousness, is a falsification of the concept of sensibility and of appearance, which makes the whole doctrine of it useless and empty. The difference of an indistinct from the distinct representation is merely logical and does not concern the content. Without doubt the concept of right, which healthy understanding makes use of, contains precisely the same as what the subtlest speculation can develop out of it, only that in common and practical use one is not conscious of these manifold representations in this thought. Therefore one cannot say that the common concept is sensible and contains a mere appearance, for right cannot appear at all, but its concept lies in the understanding and represents a constitution (the moral one) of actions, which pertains to them in themselves. On the other hand the representation of a body in intuition contains nothing at all that could pertain to an object in itself, but merely the appearance of something and the way we are affected thereby, and this receptivity of our capacity for cognition is called sensibility and remains, even if one might see through the former (the appearance) to the ground, nevertheless as far as heaven apart from the cognition of the object in itself.",["- verworren = confused; undeutlich = indistinct; deutlich = distinct; Merkmale = marks; Theilvorstellungen = partial representations.","- \"Recht\" = right; \"gesunde Verstand\" = healthy understanding; \"gemeinen\" = common.","- \"jene (die Erscheinung)\": \"jene\" is glossed by Kant's parenthesis; I render it \"the former.\"","- \"Erkenntnißfähigkeit\" = capacity for cognition (Fähigkeit = capacity, per glossary).","- \"himmelweit unterschieden\" kept literal (\"as far as heaven apart\").","- \"praftischen\" read as \"praktischen\" (OCR)."]],"krv-b-0192":["The Leibniz-Wolffian philosophy has therefore assigned to all investigations into the nature and origin of our cognitions an entirely wrong point of view, in that it considered the difference of sensibility from the intellectual as merely logical, since it is obviously transcendental and concerns not merely the form of distinctness or indistinctness, but the origin and the content of them, so that through the former we cognize the constitution of things in themselves not merely indistinctly but not at all, and, as soon as we take away our subjective constitution, the represented object with the properties that sensible intuition ascribed to it is nowhere at all to be encountered, nor can it be encountered, in that precisely this subjective constitution determines the form of it as appearance.",["- \"derselben\" (after \"Undeutlichkeit\") is ambiguous in its referent (cognitions or sensibility/intellectual); kept as \"of them.\"","- \"die erstere\" = \"the former\" (sensibility); \"Eigenschaften\" = properties, \"Beschaffenheit\" = constitution.","- \"überall nirgend\" rendered \"nowhere at all.\"","- \"des\" in \"die Form desselben\" = \"of it,\" ambiguous between the object and the constitution as in the German.","- Leibniz⸗Wolffische: the OCR sign between the names is read as a hyphen."]],"krv-b-0193":["Otherwise we do indeed distinguish among appearances that which essentially adheres to the intuition of them and holds for every human sense in general, from that which pertains to it only in a contingent way, in that it is valid not in relation to sensibility in general but only to a particular position or organization of this or that sense. And then one calls the former cognition one that represents the object in itself, but the second only the appearance of it. This difference, however, is only empirical. If one stops there (as commonly happens) and does not regard that empirical intuition once again (as should happen) as mere appearance, so that nothing at all that would concern any thing in itself is to be encountered in it, then our transcendental difference is lost, and we then nevertheless believe we cognize things in themselves, although everywhere (in the world of sense), even to the deepest investigation of its objects, we have to do with nothing but appearances. Thus we will indeed call the rainbow a mere appearance in a sunshower, but this rain the thing in itself, which is also correct, insofar as we understand the latter concept only physically, as that which in universal experience, among all the different positions relative to the senses, is nevertheless determined in intuition thus and not otherwise. But if we take this empirical in general and ask, without turning to the agreement of it with every human sense, whether this too represents an object in itself (not the raindrops, for these are then already, as appearances, empirical objects), then the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental, and not only are these drops mere appearances, but even their round shape, indeed even the space in which they fall, are nothing in themselves, but mere modifications or foundations of our sensible intuition, the transcendental object, however, remains unknown to us.",["- \"derselben\" (twice, \"Anschauung derselben,\" \"derselben nur zufälliger Weise\") refers ambiguously to appearances / the intuition; kept as \"of them\" / \"it.\"","- \"die erstere Erkenntniß\" rendered \"the former cognition.\"","- \"dieses\" in \"ob auch dieses einen Gegenstand ... vorstelle\" is neuter and ambiguous (this empirical / this sense-agreement); kept \"this.\"","- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; \"Gegenstand\" in \"Gegenstand an sich selbst,\" \"Objecte\" in \"empirische Objecte,\" \"transcendentale Object.\"","- Unterschied rendered \"difference\" and \"distinction\" in the German \"unser transscendentaler Unterschied\"; here \"difference\" for both.","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = world of sense; \"Sonnenregen\" = sunshower; \"Grundlagen\" = foundations; \"Modificationen\" = modifications.","- \"An— schauung,\" \"zu— kommt,\" \"Erschei— nung\": line-break hyphens removed."]],"krv-b-0194":["The second important concern of our transcendental aesthetic is that it not merely win some favor as a plausible hypothesis, but be as certain and undoubted as can ever be demanded of a theory that is to serve as organon. In order to make this certainty fully evident, we want to take any case by which its validity can become evident and serve for greater clarity of what was adduced in § 3.1)",["- The text reads \"irgend einen Fall an woran\": \"an\" is apparently a truncated \"annehmen\" (OCR); I have rendered \"take\" and flag this as a doubtful reading.","- \"dienen!)\" : \"!)\" is presumably the footnote marker \"1)\"; the footnote text is not given in the passage, and the position of the marker is kept.","- \"scheinbare\" rendered \"plausible\" (ambiguous between \"plausible\" and \"apparent\"); Organon is Greek in the original.","- \"Gültigkeit\" = validity; \"Gewißheit\" = certainty; \"augenscheinlich\" = evident."]],"krv-b-0195":["Posit, accordingly, that space and time are objective in themselves and conditions of the possibility of things in themselves, then it first shows itself: that a priori apodictic and synthetic propositions of both occur in great number, chiefly of space, which we therefore want to investigate here preeminently as an example. Since the propositions of geometry are cognized synthetically a priori and with apodictic certainty, I ask: whence do you take such propositions, and on what does our understanding rest in order to arrive at such absolutely necessary and universally valid truths? There is no other way than through concepts or through intuitions, but both as such that are given either a priori or a posteriori. The latter, namely empirical concepts, likewise that on which they are grounded, empirical intuition, can give no synthetic proposition except only one that is also merely empirical, i.e. a proposition of experience, hence can never contain necessity and absolute universality, such as is yet the characteristic of all propositions of geometry. But as for what would be the first and sole means, namely to arrive at such cognitions through mere concepts or through intuitions a priori, it is clear that from mere concepts no synthetic cognition at all, but only analytic, can be attained. Just take the proposition that no space can be enclosed by two straight lines, hence no figure is possible, and try to derive it from the concept of straight lines and of the number two; or also that from three straight lines a figure is possible, and try it just so merely from these concepts. All your effort is in vain, and you see yourselves compelled to take your refuge in intuition, as geometry also always does. You give yourselves, then, an object in intuition; but of what kind is this, is it a pure intuition a priori or an empirical one? Were it the latter, then no universally valid, still less an apodictic proposition could ever come of it: for experience can never deliver such. You must therefore give your object a priori in intuition and ground your synthetic proposition on this. Now if there lay in you no faculty of intuiting a priori; were this sub-",["- Sätze = propositions; Satz also \"proposition\" throughout. Erkenntniß = cognition; erkannt = cognized; Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding; Mittel = means; Zahl = number.","- Setzet: imperative of setzen (glossary \"posit\"). \"so zeigt sich erstlich\" kept with its German word order (\"then it first shows itself\").","- Gegenstand (\"object\" in \"einen Gegenstand in der Anschauung\", \"euren Gegenstand\") is rendered \"object\", as is Object elsewhere (see the next passage).","- \"Wahr- ı heilen\" corrected silently to Wahrheiten; \"empi- 1 risch\" and \"ana: lytische\" likewise.","- The text breaks off mid-sentence (\"sub-\"); it continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0196":["jective condition as to form not at the same time the general condition a priori under which alone the object of this (outer) intuition itself is possible; were the object (the triangle) something in itself without relation to your subject: how could you say that what lies necessarily in your subjective conditions for constructing a triangle must also necessarily pertain to the triangle in itself? For you could not add to your concepts (of three lines) anything new (the figure), which on that account would necessarily have to be encountered in the object, since this is given prior to your cognition and not through it. Were not, then, space (and so also time) a mere form of your intuition, which contains a priori conditions under which alone things can be outer objects for you, which without these subjective conditions are nothing in themselves: so you could settle nothing at all synthetically a priori about outer objects. It is therefore undoubtedly certain and not merely possible or even probable that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all (outer and inner) experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuition, in relation to which therefore all objects are mere appearances and not things given for themselves in this kind, of which, also for that reason, much can be said a priori as far as their form concerns, but never the least of the thing in itself that may lie at the ground of these appearances.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"sub-\" of the preceding passage is continued by \"jective\"). The Academy variant note at the head (\") und . .. dienen: Zusatz von A2\") is editorial apparatus and is left untranslated.","- Gegenstand and Object are both rendered \"object\" (äußere Gegenstände; äußere Objecte).","- \"Art\" in \"in dieser Art gegebene Dinge\" = \"kind\"; \"ausmachen\" = \"settle\"; \"zum Grunde liegen\" = \"lie at the ground\".","- \"für euch\" / \"für sich\" kept as \"for you\" / \"for themselves\"."]],"krv-b-0197":["II.) To confirm this theory of the ideality of outer as well as inner sense, hence of all objects of the senses as mere appearances, the remark can especially serve: that everything in our cognition that belongs to intuition (thus excepting feeling of pleasure and displeasure and the will, which are not cognitions at all) contains nothing but mere relations, of places in an intuition (extension), change of places (motion), and laws according to which this change is determined (moving forces). But what is present in the place, or what it effects in the things themselves besides the change of place, is not thereby given. Now through mere relations a thing in itself is yet not cognized: thus it is surely to be judged that, since nothing but mere representations of relation are given to us through outer sense, this too can contain only the relation of an object to the subject in its representation and not the inner, which pertains to the object in itself. With inner intuition it is just so. Not only that the representations of outer senses constitute the proper material in it, with which we occupy our mind, but time, into which we posit these representations, which itself precedes the consciousness of them in experience and lies at the ground as formal condition of the way in which we posit them in the mind, already contains relations of being-after-one-another, of being-simultaneous and of that which is simultaneous with being-after-one-another (of the persistent). Now that which as representation can precede every act of thinking anything is intuition and, if it contains nothing but relations, the form of intuition, which, since it represents nothing except insofar as something is posited in the mind, can be nothing other than the way in which the mind is affected through its own activity, namely this positing of its representation, hence through itself, i.e. an inner sense as to its form. Everything that is represented through a sense is to that extent always appearance, and an inner sense would therefore either not have to be granted at all, or the subject, which is the object of it, could be represented through it only as appearance, not as it would judge of itself if its intuition were mere self-activity, i.e. intellectual. Here all the difficulty rests only on how a subject could intuit itself inwardly; but this difficulty is common to every theory. The consciousness of itself (apperception) is the simple representation of the I, and if through it alone everything manifold in the subject were given self-actively, then the inner intuition would be intellectual. In the human being this consciousness requires inner perception of the manifold that is given beforehand in the subject, and the way in which this is given in the mind without spontaneity must, for the sake of this difference, be called sensibility. If the faculty of becoming conscious is to seek out (apprehend) that which lies in the mind, then it must affect the same and can only in such a way produce an intuition of itself, the form of which, however, which lies beforehand at the ground in the mind, determines in the representation of time the way in which the manifold is together in the mind; whereby it then intuits itself, not as it would immediately self-actively",["- Lust und Unlust: OCR \"Luft\" corrected to Lust. Orter (plural of Ort) = \"places\".","- Nacheinander / Zugleichsein rendered literally (\"being-after-one-another\", \"being-simultaneous\"); Beharrliches = \"the persistent\".","- Verhältnißvorstellungen = \"representations of relation\"; das Innere = \"the inner\"; Stoff = \"material\"; Handlung = \"act\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Mannigfaltige = \"manifold\"; Schein not in this passage.","- \"sich bewußt zu werden das, was im Gemüthe liegt, aufsuchen\": syntax kept; \"that which lies in the mind\" can be read as object of \"aufsuchen\" or tied to \"bewußt zu werden\".","- \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Gegenstand\" = object, \"Object\" = object.","- OCR line-break debris (\"in⸗ | tellectuell\", \"Wahr: nehmung\", \"nämlich!\", \"(des 1 Beharrlichen)\", \"Er- as scheinung\") corrected silently. The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"selbstthätig\")."]],"krv-b-0198":["--- notes\n- The passage consists only of a running head (\"II. Abschnitt. Allgemeine Anmerkungen. 71\") and a page number, which are omitted under the job's instructions; there is nothing to translate.",[]],"krv-b-0199":["represent, but according to the way it is affected from within,",["- Continues the preceding sentence: \"vorstellen würde\" completes \"nicht wie es sich unmittelbar selbstthätig\". Fragment translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-0200":["consequently as it appears to itself, not as it is.",["- \"erscheint\" = \"appears\"; \"folglich\" = \"consequently\". \"wie es sich erscheint\" is kept as \"as it appears to itself\" (dative reflexive)."]],"krv-b-0201":["III. If I say: in space and time the intuition",["- Fragment of a sentence; it continues in the next passage (\"sowohl der äußeren Objecte ...\")."]],"krv-b-0202":["both of outer objects and also the self-intuition of the mind represents both just as it affects our senses, i.e. as it appears, then this is not meant to say that these objects would be a mere illusion. For",["- Line number \"5\" omitted. \"Objecte\" = \"objects\"; \"Gegenstände\" in the next clause = \"objects\".","- \"stellt ... beides vor\": the subject of \"stellt vor\" is \"die Anschauung ... sowie die Selbstanschauung\", as in the German; \"es\" in \"so wie es unsere Sinne afficirt\" is left as \"it\".","- \"Schein\" = \"illusion\". The passage breaks off after \"Denn\"."]],"krv-b-0203":["that these objects would be a mere illusion. For",["- Schein = \"illusion\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\" (Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; here the German is Gegenstände).","- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"das nicht sagen, …\"): the subject and governing verb are not in the passage, so \"das\" is left untranslated as a fragment; the English begins with the \"that\"-clause. The preceding words (e.g. \"man kann\") are not supplied.","- \"wären\" is subjunctive (Konjunktiv II) and is rendered \"would be\"; it could also be read as reported speech (\"were\").","- The passage breaks off after \"Denn\" (\"For\"), with no continuation supplied."]],"krv-b-0204":["in the appearance the objects, indeed even the proper-",["- Breaks off mid-word (Beschaffen-), as in the source; completed in the next passage (Beschaffenheiten, here \"properties\"). Beschaffenheit = \"property\" throughout.","- Erscheinung = appearance; Object here and Gegenstand elsewhere both = \"object\"."]],"krv-b-0205":["ties that we ascribe to them, are at all times regarded as something actually given,",["- Opens mid-word (-heiten). wirklich = \"actually\" (Wirklichkeit = actuality/reality).","- beilegen = \"ascribe\" throughout."]],"krv-b-0206":["only that, insofar as this property depends only on the way of intuition of the subject in the relation of the given object to it, this object as appearance is distinguished from itself as object in itself",["- Anschauungsart = \"way of intuition\"; Gegenstand and Object both \"object\" (der Gegenstand ... als Object an sich); an sich = \"in itself\".","- Relation (here) and Verhältniß (below) both = \"relation\".","- \"von ihm selber\" could be read as \"from itself\" or \"from it itself\" (the German is the same)."]],"krv-b-0207":[". So I do not say, the bodies seem merely to be outside",["- The leading \".\" is as in the source text. scheinen = \"seem\" (cognate of Schein = \"illusion\")."]],"krv-b-0208":["me, or my soul seems only to be given in my self-consciousness, when I assert that the quality of space and of time, in accordance with which, as condition of their existence, I posit both, lies in my",["- Selbstbewußtsein = \"self-consciousness\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; behaupten = \"assert\"; setzen = \"posit\".","- \"welcher ... gemäß\" has a doubtful antecedent (Qualität, or Raum und Zeit); the English keeps it open."]],"krv-b-0209":["way of intuition and not in these objects in themselves. It would be",["- Anschauungsart again \"way of intuition\"."]],"krv-b-0210":["my own fault if out of what I should count as appearance I made mere illusion.¹) This does not happen, however, according to our",["- ¹) marks the footnote that follows in krv-b-0214; the OCR gives *) and a stray closing quotation mark, which I dropped.","- Schein = \"illusion\"."]],"krv-b-0211":["principle of the ideality of all our sensible intuitions; rather, if one ascribes objective reality to those forms of representation, then",["- Princip = \"principle\" (Grundsatz and Satz are in the glossary as \"principle\" too); Vorstellungsformen = \"forms of representation\"; Realität = \"reality\"."]],"krv-b-0212":["one cannot avoid everything's being thereby transformed into mere illusion.",["- The German has a pleonastic nicht (\"daß nicht alles ... verwandelt werde\"); not rendered in English."]],"krv-b-0213":["For if one regards space and time as properties that, as to their possibility, would have to be encountered in things in themselves,",["- Sachen = \"things\" here; Ding (in the next passages) is also \"thing\".","- Möglichkeit = \"possibility\"; ansehen = \"regard\"."]],"krv-b-0214":["and thinks over the absurdities in which one then becomes entangled, since two infinite things, which are not substances, also not ¹) The predicates of the appearance can be ascribed to the object itself in relation to our sense, e.g., to the rose the red color or the scent;",["- The footnote interrupts the sentence, as in the source; the main sentence resumes in krv-b-0219. Ungereimtheit = \"absurdity\".","- Verhältniß = \"relation\"; Sinn = \"sense\"."]],"krv-b-0215":["but illusion can never be ascribed as predicate to the object, precisely",["- Gegenstand and Object both \"object\"; Schein = \"illusion\"."]],"krv-b-0216":["because it ascribes to the object for itself what pertains to it only in relation to the senses or in general to the subject, e.g., the two handles that were at first ascribed to Saturn. What is to be encountered not at all in the object in itself, but always in the relation of the same to the subject and is inseparable from the representation of the",["- \"für sich\" = \"for itself\" (distinct from \"an sich\" = \"in itself\"). \"Henkel\" = \"handles\".","- \"desselben\" refers to the object (grammatically open); \"des letzteren\" (next passage) = \"of the latter\", i.e. the subject, kept unresolved."]],"krv-b-0217":["latter, is appearance, and thus the predicates of space and of time are rightly ascribed to the objects of the senses as such, and in this there is no illusion. On the other hand, if I ascribe to the rose in itself the redness, to Saturn the handles, or to all outer objects extension in itself, without looking to",["- Ausdehnung = \"extension\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"."]],"krv-b-0218":["a determinate relation of these objects to the subject and limiting my judgment to it, then illusion first arises.",["- The OCR reads \"Nr-\" for Ur- (Urtheil, split across passages); the word is completed in the next fragment's first line. Urtheil = \"judgment\". \"darauf\" = \"to it\", antecedent open (the relation).","- bestimmt = \"determinate\"."]],"krv-b-0219":["not something actually inhering in substances, yet existing, indeed the necessary condition of the existence of all things, must be, and also remain over even if all existing things are canceled: then one cannot well hold it against good Berkeley if he demoted bodies to mere illusion; indeed even our own existence, which would be made dependent in such a way on the self-subsisting reality of a non-thing like time, would have to be transformed with it into sheer illusion, an absurdity that no one has yet let himself be guilty of.",["- Continues the sentence begun in krv-b-0213/0214; \"alsdann allererst entspringt der Schein\" belongs to the footnote text (krv-b-0218) and is translated there.","- Existenz/Dasein both \"existence\"; Existirendes \"existing\". Substanzen = \"substances\". aufgehoben = \"canceled\" (aufheben). \"niemand\" restored from the OCR \"nies | wand\".","- \"Undinges\" = \"non-thing\"."]],"krv-b-0220":["IV. In natural theology, where one thinks an object that is not only no object of intuition at all for us, but that can be absolutely no object of sensible intuition for itself, one is carefully intent upon removing from all its intuition (for all its cognition must be of such a kind, and not thinking, which always proves limitations) the conditions of time and space. But with what right can one do this, if one has previously made both into forms of things in themselves, and indeed of such ones as, as conditions of the existence of things, remain a priori, even if one had canceled the things themselves: for as conditions of all existence in general they would have to be so also of the existence of God. Nothing remains, if one does not want to make them into objective forms of all things, except that one make them into subjective forms of our outer as well as inner way of intuition, which is called sensible because it is not original, i.e., one through which the existence of the object of intuition is itself given (and which, as far as we have insight, can pertain only to the original being), but dependent on the existence of the object, hence possible only through the capacity for representation of the subject being affected by it.",["- Gegenstand/Object = \"object\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Denken = \"thinking\"; Schranken = \"limitations\"; Dasein/Existenz = \"existence\".","- \"der ihm selbst durchaus kein Gegenstand\": \"ihm selbst\" = \"for itself\" (could also be \"to it itself\"). \"es\" in \"es auch vom Dasein Gottes sein\" is \"so\" in English (ambiguous as in German).","- Urwesen = \"original being\"; Vorstellungsfähigkeit = \"capacity for representation\"; afficirt = \"affected\"; a priori in roman type, here italicized as foreign.","- \"a priori übrig bleiben\" may attach to \"remain\" or to \"as conditions\"; kept ambiguous."]],"krv-b-0221":["Nor is it necessary that we restrict the way of intuition in space and time to the sensibility of the human being; it may be that all finite thinking beings must necessarily agree with the human being in this (although we cannot decide this), so it does not cease to be sensibility on account of this universal validity, precisely because it is derived (*intuitus derivativus*), not original (*intuitus originarius*), hence not intellectual intuition, as that which, from the ground just cited, seems to pertain solely to the original being, but never to a being dependent both in its existence and in its intuition (which determines its existence in relation to given objects); although the latter remark must be counted for our aesthetic theory only as clarification, not as ground of proof. Conclusion of the transcendental aesthetic.",["- Allgemeingültigkeit = \"universal validity\"; Erläuterung = \"clarification\"; Beweisgrund = \"ground of proof\"; Sinnlichkeit = \"sensibility\".","- \"so hört sie ... nicht auf\" is kept with \"so\", as the German main clause follows the concessive \"es mag sein\" without a conjunction.","- OCR stray \"a\" before \"welche\" and \";\" before \"Beschluß\" dropped.","- \"ihrem ... nach\" (\"seinem Dasein ... nach\") kept as \"in its existence\"; \"die\" in the parenthesis may refer to Anschauung (as translated)."]],"krv-b-0222":["Here we now have one of the requisite pieces for the solution of the general problem of transcendental philosophy: how are synthetic propositions a priori possible?, namely pure intuitions a priori, space and time, in which, if we want in a judgment a priori to go beyond the given concept, we encounter that which can be discovered a priori not in the concept but surely in the intuition that corresponds to it, and can be combined synthetically with it, which judgments, however, for this reason never reach further than to objects of the senses and can be valid only for objects of possible experience.",["- Aufgabe = \"problem\"; Auflösung = \"solution\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; synthetisch = \"synthetic\"; verbunden = \"combined\" (Verbindung = combination); gelten = \"be valid\".","- \"a priori\" in \"Urtheile a priori\" and \"entdeckt werden a priori\" could attach to verb or noun; left as in German.","- Stray OCR \"»\" and final \")\" dropped."]],"krv-b-0223":["Second Part\nof the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements.\nTranscendental Logic.\nIntroduction.\nIdea of a Transcendental Logic.\nI.\nOn Logic in General.",["- Elementarlehre = \"Doctrine of Elements\"; Theil = \"part\"; Idee = \"idea\".","- OCR \"trans scendentalen\" corrected."]],"krv-b-0224":["Our cognition arises from two fundamental sources of the mind, the first of which is to receive representations (the receptivity of impressions), the second the faculty of cognizing an object through these representations (spontaneity of concepts); through the former an object is given to us, through the second this is thought in relation to that representation (as mere determination of the mind). Intuition and concepts therefore constitute the elements of all our cognition, so that neither concepts without an intuition corresponding to them in some way, nor intuition without concepts, can yield a cognition.¹) Both are either pure or empirical. Empirical, if sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained therein; pure, however, if no sensation is mixed into the representation. One can call the latter the matter of sensible cognition. Hence pure intuition contains merely the form under which something is intuited, and pure concept alone the form of the thinking of an object in general. Only pure intuitions or concepts are possible a priori, empirical ones only a posteriori.",["- Gemüth = \"mind\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Eindruck = \"impression\"; Empfindung = \"sensation\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Form = \"form\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; rein = \"pure\".","- ¹) marks a footnote whose text is not in the passage; the OCR shows \"!)\". Stray \"Cm- 2 pfindung\" corrected to Empfindung.","- \"dieser\" (durch die zweite wird dieser ... gedacht) = \"this\", referring grammatically to the object; \"jene Vorstellung\" = \"that representation\".","- \"wirkliche\" = \"actual\"; \"Art\" = \"way\"."]],"krv-b-0225":["If we want to call the receptivity of our mind to receive representations, so far as it is affected in some way, sensibility: then, by contrast, the faculty for bringing forth representations themselves, or the spontaneity of cognition, is the understanding. Our nature brings it with it that intuition can never be other than sensible, i.e., contains only the way in which we are affected by objects. By contrast, the faculty for thinking the object of sensible intuition is the understanding. Neither of these properties is to be preferred to the other. Without sensibility no object would be given to us, and without understanding none would be thought. Thoughts without content are empty, intuitions without concepts are blind. Hence it is just as necessary to make its concepts sensible (i.e., to add the object to them in intuition) as to make its intuitions understandable (i.e., to bring them under concepts). Both faculties or capacities can also not exchange their functions. The understanding is capable of intuiting nothing and the senses of thinking nothing. Only from their uniting can cognition arise. Yet on that account one must not mix up their share, but one has great cause to separate each carefully from the other and to distinguish them. Hence we distinguish the science of the rules of sensibility in general, i.e., aesthetic, from the science of the rules of the understanding in general, i.e., logic.",["- Gemüth = mind; Vermögen = faculty; Fähigkeit = capacity; Gegenstand = object.","- Anschauung = intuition; Erkenntnis(s) = cognition; Wissenschaft = science.","- \"Eigenschaften\" rendered \"properties\" (not in glossary).","- \"verständlich zu machen\" rendered \"understandable\", keeping the Verstand cognate.","- \"Wir\" clause \"Wollen wir … nennen: so\" kept as one conditional with colon, as in German.","- \"Art\" in \"die Art, wie\" = \"way\".","- \"es\" in \"bringt es so mit sich\" kept as \"it\"."]],"krv-b-0226":["Logic can now in turn be undertaken with a twofold aim, either as logic of the general, or of the particular use of the understanding. The first contains the absolutely necessary rules of thinking, without which no use of the understanding takes place at all, and thus goes to this use regardless of the difference of the objects at which it may be directed. The logic of the particular use of the understanding contains the rules for thinking correctly about a certain kind of objects. The former one can call elementary logic, but the latter the organon of this or that science. The latter is for the most part sent ahead in the schools as propaedeutic of the sciences, although according to the course of human reason it is the latest, which it reaches only when the science is already long finished and needs only the final touch for its correction and perfection. For one must already know the objects to a fairly high degree if one wants to specify the rules for how a science of them can be brought about. al",["- Absicht = aim; Gebrauch = use; Gegenstand = object; Art = kind; Grad = degree; Vollkommenheit = perfection; kennen = know.","- Final \"al\" is stray OCR/marginal residue; left as it stands, doubtful.","- *Organon*, *Propädeutik* kept as Latinate/Greek terms; Propädeutik printed in roman, rendered \"propaedeutic\".","- \"geht auf diesen\" kept literally (\"goes to this use\"); \"auf\" could also be rendered \"is directed at\"."]],"krv-b-0227":["General logic is now either pure or applied logic. In the former we abstract from all empirical conditions under which our understanding is exercised, e.g., from the influence of the senses, from the play of imagination, the laws of memory, the power of habit, of inclination, etc., hence also from the sources of prejudices, indeed quite generally from all causes from which certain cognitions may arise in us, or be foisted upon us, because they concern the understanding only | under certain circumstances of its application, and to know these experience is required. A general but pure logic has to do, then, with sheer principles a priori and is a canon of the understanding and of reason, but only with regard to the formal aspect of their use, the content may be what it will (empirical or transcendental). A general logic is called applied, however, when it is directed to the rules of the use of the understanding under the subjective empirical conditions that psychology teaches us. It has, then, empirical principles, although it is general insofar as it goes to the use of the understanding without distinction of the objects. For that reason it is also neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an organon of particular sciences, but merely a cathartic of the common understanding.",["- Einbildung = imagination (Einbildungskraft in glossary; here the text has \"Einbildung\"). Bedingung = condition; Erkenntnis = cognition; Grundsatz/Principien rendered \"principles\".","- \"das Formale\" = \"the formal aspect\" (literal: \"the formal\"); \"Piychologie\" corrected from OCR to Psychologie.","- *a priori*, *Kanon*, *Organon*, *Kathartikon* kept as Latin/Greek forms; \"ste\" corrected to \"sie\". Line number \"10\" omitted.","- \"gemeinen Verstandes\" = \"common understanding\" (gemein = common).","- \"der Inhalt mag sein, welcher er wolle\" kept as a loose clause as in German.","- \"in Ansehung\" = \"with regard to\"; \"Gebrauchs\" could attach to \"Vernunft\" alone or both; left as \"their use\"."]],"krv-b-0228":["In general logic, therefore, the part that is to constitute the pure doctrine of reason must be entirely separated from that which constitutes applied (although still always general) logic. The former is properly only alone science, though short and dry, and as the school-proper presentation of an elementary doctrine of the understanding requires. In this, then, logicians must always have two rules before their eyes.",["- Vernunftlehre = doctrine of reason (Lehre = doctrine).","- \"schulgerechte\" = \"school-proper\", literal; \"eigentlich nur allein\" kept as \"properly only alone\".","- \"wie es … erfordert\" kept with \"as\" (wie), literal."]],"krv-b-0229":["1) As general logic it abstracts from all content of the cognition of the understanding and the difference of its objects and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thinking. ;",["- Verstandeserkenntniß = \"cognition of the understanding\". Trailing \";\" is as in the OCR; kept."]],"krv-b-0230":["2) As pure logic it has no empirical principles, hence it draws nothing (as one has sometimes persuaded oneself) from psychology, which",["- \"seine\" in the OCR is read as \"keine\" (sic: \"hat seine empirische Principien\"); I translated \"no\", the evident reading; doubtful OCR.","- überredet = persuaded (Überredung = persuasion). Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continues in next passage."]],"krv-b-0231":["Introduction. Ii therefore has no influence at all on the canon of the understanding. It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be altogether certain a priori.",["- \"Einleitung. Ii\" is a running head and page number (OCR); retained here only because it stood in the passage text—should be omitted from the edition; \"also\" translated \"therefore\", and the opening \"auf\" belongs to the previous sentence's \"die … hat\".","- Doctrin = doctrine; *a priori* kept in italics."]],"krv-b-0232":["What I call applied logic (contrary to the common meaning of this word, according to which it is supposed to contain certain exercises for which pure logic gives the rule), it is a representation of the understanding and of the rules of its necessary use in concreto, namely under the contingent conditions of the subject, which can hinder or promote this use, and which are all given only empirically. It deals with attention, its hindrance and consequences, the origin of error, the state of doubt, of scruple, of conviction, etc.; and to it the general and pure logic is related as pure morals, which contains merely the necessary moral laws of a free will in general, is to the proper doctrine of virtue, which weighs these laws under the hindrances of the feelings, inclinations and passions, to which human beings are more or less subject, and which can never yield a true and demonstrated science, because it, just as much as that applied logic, requires empirical and psychological principles.",["- Vorstellung = representation; Zustand = state; Überzeugung = conviction; Gefühl = feeling; Gesetz = law; Wissenschaft = science; Bedeutung = meaning; zufällig = contingent; Bedingung = condition.","- *in concreto* kept in italics. Line numbers and stray OCR marks (\"5\", \")\", \"s\") omitted.","- \"Was ich … nenne … so ist sie\": the anacoluthon is preserved.","- \"Folgen\" = \"consequences\" (Folge elsewhere = sequel/sequence; Folgerung = consequence)."]],"krv-b-0233":["II.",[]],"krv-b-0234":["On Transcendental Logic.",["- Initial \")\" is OCR residue and omitted."]],"krv-b-0235":["General logic abstracts, as we have shown, from all content of cognition, i.e., from all relation of it to the object, and considers only the logical form in the relation of cognitions to one another, i.e., the form of thinking in general. But since there are pure as well as empirical intuitions (as the transcendental aesthetic shows), a distinction between pure and empirical thinking of objects might well also be found. In this case there would be a logic in which one did not abstract from all content of cognition; for that which contained merely the rules of the pure thinking of an object would exclude all those cognitions that were of empirical content. It would also go to the origin of our cognitions of objects, insofar as it cannot be ascribed to the objects, whereas general logic has nothing to do with this origin of cognition but considers the representations, whether they may be originally given a priori in ourselves, or only empirically, merely according to the laws by which the understanding uses them in relation to one another when it thinks, and thus deals only with the form of the understanding that can be procured for the representations, wherever else they may have arisen.",["- Object (Beziehung auf das Object) and Gegenstände both = \"object(s)\"; Object is the Latinate member.","- \"so fern er nicht den Gegenständen zugeschrieben werden kann\": \"er\" = Ursprung (origin) or possibly the cognition; kept as \"it\", ambiguous in the same way as the German.","- \"woher sie auch sonst entsprungen sein mögen\" = \"wherever else they may have arisen\" (literal; \"woher\" = from where).","- uranfänglich = \"originally\"; *a priori* in italics.","- Verhältniß = relation; Beziehung = relation (one English word for two German words)."]],"krv-b-0236":["en",["- \"en\" is an OCR fragment (probably residue of a hyphenated word or marginal mark); left untranslated as it stands."]],"krv-b-0237":["And here I make a remark that extends its influence over all the subsequent considerations, and that one must well keep before one's eyes, namely: that not every cognition *a priori*, but only that through which we cognize that and how certain representations (intuitions or concepts) are applied or are possible merely *a priori*, must be called transcendental (i.e., the possibility of cognition or the use of it *a priori*). Therefore neither space nor any geometrical determination of it *a priori* is a transcendental representation, but only the cognition that these representations are not at all of empirical origin, and the possibility how they can nevertheless relate *a priori* to objects of experience, can be called transcendental. Likewise the use of space of objects in general would also be transcendental; but if it is restricted merely to objects of the senses, it is called empirical. The distinction of the transcendental and the empirical therefore belongs only to the critique of cognitions and does not concern the relation of them to their object.",["- OCR slips silently corrected: \"Gez 2 genstand\" (Gegenstand), \"kann n transscendental\"; the \"2\" is a line number.","- Gegenstand = \"object\" throughout (cf. Object in the next passages). \"Betrachtungen\" = considerations; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Gebrauch\" = use.","- The position of *a priori* in \"weder der Raum, noch irgend eine geometrische Bestimmung desselben a priori eine transscendentale Vorstellung\" is left as in the German; it may attach to \"determination\" or to \"is a representation\".","- \"Gebrauch des Raumes von Gegenständen überhaupt\": \"of objects\" is kept as the bare genitive phrase, left as ambiguous as the German."]],"krv-b-0238":["In the expectation, therefore, that there may perhaps be concepts that may relate *a priori* to objects, not as pure or sensible intuitions, but merely as actions of pure thinking, which are consequently concepts, but neither of empirical nor of aesthetic origin, we make for ourselves in advance the idea of a science of the pure understanding and of the cognition of reason, through which we think objects completely *a priori*. Such a science, which would determine the origin, the domain and the objective validity of such cognitions, would have to be called transcendental logic, because it has to do merely with the laws of the understanding and of reason, but solely insofar as they are related to objects *a priori* and not, like general logic, to the empirical as well as pure cognitions of reason without distinction.",["- The \"2\" after \"Ursprungs\" and the \"3\" in \"Gez 3 setzen\" are line numbers; \"Gesetzen\" restored.","- \"Verstandes und Vernunfterkenntnisses\": \"of the pure understanding and of the cognition of reason\" preserves the possible reading of \"pure\" governing both or only \"understanding\".","- \"Vernunfterkenntnisse\" = \"cognitions of reason\"; \"Umfang\" = domain; \"Gültigkeit\" = validity; \"allgemeine Logik\" = general logic."]],"krv-b-0239":["III.\nOn the Division of General Logic\ninto\nAnalytic and Dialectic.",["- \"Eintheilung\" = division (Einteilung); \"Analytik\" and \"Dialektik\" rendered as \"Analytic\" and \"Dialectic\"."]],"krv-b-0240":["The old and famous question with which one believed to drive the logicians into a corner and sought to bring them to the point that they must either let themselves be caught in a wretched diallelus, or confess their ignorance, and hence the vanity of their whole art, is this: What is truth? The nominal definition of truth, that it is namely the agreement of cognition with its object, is granted here and presupposed; but one demands to know what the general and certain criterion of the truth of any cognition is.",["- \"Namenerflärung\" corrected to Namenerklärung; \"all— gemeine\" joined as allgemeine.","- \"Diallele\" is rendered \"diallelus\" (circular reasoning); \"Erklärung\" = definition, as in the glossary.","- \"Kriterium\" = criterion; \"Gegenstande\" = object; \"allgemein\" = general."]],"krv-b-0241":["It is already a great and necessary proof of prudence or insight to know what one should ask reasonably. For if the question is absurd in itself and demands unnecessary answers, then besides the shame of the one who poses it, it sometimes has the further disadvantage of seducing the incautious listener to absurd answers and of presenting the laughable sight that one (as the ancients said) milks the he-goat, the other holds a sieve underneath.",["- \"fole\" corrected to \"solle\".","- \"Klugheit\" = prudence; \"Einsicht\" = insight; \"ungereimt\" = absurd; \"Bock\" = he-goat."]],"krv-b-0242":["If truth consists in the agreement of a cognition with its object, then this object must thereby be distinguished from others; for a cognition is false if it does not agree with the object to which it is related, even if it contains something that could well be valid of other objects. Now a general criterion of truth would be that which would be valid of all cognitions without distinction of their objects. But it is clear that, since in it one abstracts from all content of the cognition (relation to its object), and truth concerns precisely this content, it is entirely impossible and absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of the cognitions, and that therefore a sufficient and yet at the same time general sign of truth cannot possibly be given. Since we have above already called the content of a cognition the matter of it, one will have to say: of the truth of cognition in accordance with the matter no general sign can be demanded, because it is self-contradictory in itself.",["- \"Gegenstand\" and \"Object\" are both \"object\" (Object appears in \"Beziehung auf ihr Object\").","- \"Merkmal\" = mark; \"Kennzeichen\" = sign; \"hinreichend\" = sufficient (not \"zureichend\"); \"Inhalt\" = content; \"Materie\" = matter.","- \"Gegen— stande\" joined. \"Der Materie nach\" = \"in accordance with the matter\".","- \"es ist in sich selbst widersprechend\": \"it\" may refer to the sign or to the demand; left ambiguous as in the German."]],"krv-b-0243":["But as for cognition in accordance with mere form (setting aside all content), it is just as clear: that a logic, insofar as it presents the general and necessary rules of the understanding, must set out criteria of truth precisely in these rules. For what contradicts these is false, because the understanding thereby conflicts with its general rules of thinking, hence with itself. These criteria, however, concern only the form of truth, i.e., of thinking in general, and are to that extent entirely correct, but not sufficient. For although a cognition may be entirely in conformity with the logical form, i.e., not contradict itself: it can nevertheless still always contradict the object. 1 Thus the merely logical criterion of truth, namely the agreement of a cognition with the general and formal laws of the understanding and of reason, is indeed the *conditio sine qua non*, hence the negative condition of all truth: but further than this logic cannot go, and the error that concerns not the form but the content, logic cannot discover by any touchstone.",["- \"Gr- | kenntniß\" restored as \"Erkenntniß\"; the signs | are kept where they stand. \"Das Erkenntniß\" (neuter) in the German is rendered as \"cognition\".","- The \"1\" after \"widersprechen\" is a footnote marker; no footnote text is given in the passage.","- \"Kriterien\" = criteria; \"Probirstein\" = touchstone; \"hinreichend\" = sufficient; \"Bedingung\" = condition."]],"krv-b-0244":["General logic now resolves the whole formal business of the understanding and of reason into its elements and presents them as principles of all logical assessment of our cognition. This part of logic can therefore be called Analytic and is just for that reason the at least",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage. \"Principien\" = principles; \"Beurtheilung\" = assessment (glossary: beurteilen = assess)."]],"krv-b-0245":["negative touchstone of truth, in that one must first of all test and estimate all cognition in accordance with its form by these rules, before one investigates it itself in accordance with its content, in order to settle whether in regard to the object it contains positive truth. But because the mere form of cognition, however much it may agree with logical laws, is far from sufficient to settle material (objective) truth of the cognition on that account, no one can venture with logic alone to judge about objects and to assert anything, without having previously obtained well-grounded inquiry about them outside of logic, in order afterwards merely to attempt the utilization and the connection of them in a coherent whole in accordance with logical laws, but better still merely to test them in accordance with these. Nevertheless there lies something so seductive in the possession of such a seeming art, of giving all our cognitions the form of the understanding, even though one may still be very empty and poor in regard to the content of them, that this general logic, which is merely a canon for assessment, has been used as if it were an organon for the actual production, or at least for the deceptive show, of objective assertions, and has consequently in fact been misused thereby. General logic, now, as a supposed organon, is called Dialectic.",["- Opens with the fragment \"ze\" (line-break remnant of \"negative\"), silently dropped; the first word continues \"der wenigstens\" of the preceding passage. \"einge: zögen\" restored as \"eingezogen\".","- \"Beurteilung\" = assessment (as above); \"schätzen\" = estimate; \"Benutzung\" = utilization; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Erkundigung\" = inquiry.","- \"Blendwerk\" is not in the glossary; rendered \"deceptive show\". \"scheinbar\" = seeming (cognate of Schein, illusion).","- \"Erkenntnisse darum auszumachen\" is doubtful in the OCR (dative \"dem Erkenntnisse\"); translated as it stands: \"of the cognition\".","- \"Kanon\" = canon; \"Organon\" = organon; \"Behauptungen\" = assertions (behaupten = assert)."]],"krv-b-0246":["However different the meaning may be in which the ancients made use of this term for a science or art, one can still safely infer from their actual use of it that it was with them nothing other than the logic of illusion. A sophistical art, to give to its ignorance, indeed even to its intentional deceptions, the varnish of truth, by imitating the method of thoroughness, which logic in general prescribes, and by using its topic to embellish every empty pretense. Now one can note as a safe and useful warning: that general logic, considered as an organon, is always a logic of illusion, i.e., dialectical. For since it teaches us nothing at all about the content of cognition, but only the merely formal conditions of agreement with the understanding, which are, moreover, entirely indifferent with regard to objects: the presumption of using it as an instrument (organon), in order at least ostensibly to extend and to expand one's knowledge, must run out into nothing but loquaciousness, asserting with some illusion everything one wants, or also challenging it at will.",["- Bedeutung = meaning; Scheu/Schein = illusion; Gebrauch = use; Benennung = term; Erkenntniß = cognition; Kenntnisse = knowledge (plural; Wissen = knowledge is a different word); Inhalt = content.","- Gegenstände (objects) here; \"Gegenstand\" and \"Object\" would both be \"object.\"","- \"Be— nennung\" and the interleaved line numbers/OCR fragments (\"o\", \"gnug\", \"5\") silently repaired: \"Beschönigung\" (\"Beschöni— gnug\") read as \"embellish.\"","- The second sentence has no finite verb in the German (\"Eine sophistische Kunst, ... zu geben, daß man ...\"); kept as a fragment. \"daß\" here is left as a bare \"by\" construction; the German could be read as \"so that\" or as an appositive \"that.\"","- \"Vorgeben\" rendered \"pretense\" (first occurrence: \"Vorgebens\") and \"ostensibly\" (dem Vorgeben nach).","- \"gleichgültig\" = \"indifferent\"; \"Zumuthung\" = \"presumption.\"","- \"Organon\" kept in Greek-derived form as in the German."]],"krv-b-0247":["Such an instruction is in no way befitting the dignity of philosophy. For that reason one has preferred to count this term dialectic as a critique of dialectical illusion among the parts of logic, and as such we also want it to be understood here.",["- Benennung = term; Schein = illusion; Weise = way.","- \"beigezählt\" rendered \"count ... among\"; \"der Logik\" is dative, \"to logic,\" read as \"among the parts of logic\" (the \"parts\" is not in the German; literally \"counted to logic\")."]],"krv-b-0248":["VI\nOn the Division of Transcendental Logic\ninto\nTranscendental Analytic and Dialectic.",["- \"TVi\" in the OCR read as the section number \"VI\" (cautious reading).","- Eintheilung = division."]],"krv-b-0249":["In a transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as above in the transcendental aesthetic we did sensibility) and lift out of our cognition merely that part of thinking which has its origin solely in the understanding. The use of this pure cognition, however, rests on this as its condition: that objects are given to us in intuition, to which it can be applied. For without intuition all our cognition lacks objects, and it then remains entirely empty. The part of transcendental logic, therefore, that expounds the elements of pure cognition of the understanding, and the principles without which no object at all can be thought, is the transcendental analytic and at the same time a logic of truth. For no cognition can contradict it without at the same time losing all content, i.e., all relation to any object, hence all truth. But because it is very enticing and seductive to make use of these pure cognitions of the understanding and principles alone and even beyond the boundaries of experience, which yet singly and alone can give into our hands the matter (objects) to which those pure concepts of the understanding can be applied: the understanding thus falls into danger of making, through empty rationalizings, a material use of the merely formal principles of the pure understanding and of judging indiscriminately about objects that are yet not given to us, indeed perhaps cannot be given in any way whatsoever. Since it should therefore properly be only a canon of the judgment of empirical use, it is misused if one lets it count as the organon of a general and unrestricted use and ventures with the pure understanding alone to judge, to assert and to decide synthetically about objects in general. The use of the pure understanding would then, therefore, be dialectical. The second part of transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of this dialectical illusion and is called transcendental dialectic, not as an art of dogmatically arousing such illusion (an art, unfortunately, very much in vogue, of manifold metaphysical juggleries), but as a critique of the understanding and of reason | with regard to their hyperphysical use, in order to uncover the false illusion of their groundless presumptions and to reduce their claims to discovery and expansion, which they believe they can reach merely through transcendental principles, to the mere judging and safeguarding of the pure understanding against sophistical deceptions.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; Verstandeserkenntniß = cognition of the understanding; Gegenstand/Object = \"object\" for both (Objecten, Objecte are the Latinate ones).","- Grenzen = boundaries; Grundsätze = principles; \"Principien\" (Latinate) also \"principles.\"","- Vernünfteleien = rationalizings (glossary: vernünfteln = rationalize); Beurtheilung = judgment (in the glossary \"estimation; judgment\"); urtheilen = judge.","- \"sie\" in \"Da sie also eigentlich nur ein Kanon ... sein sollte\" is left as \"it\"; the antecedent is ambiguous (the analytic, or the pure cognition/understanding).","- \"einzig und allein\" rendered \"singly and alone\"; \"Erfindung\" = discovery (kept literal as \"discovery\"); \"Erweiterung\" = expansion.","- \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Materie\" = matter; \"synthetisch\" = synthetic, adverb \"synthetically\" could attach to \"judge\" alone or to all three verbs; left in the same position.","- The \"|\" is the Academy Edition's sign, kept in place. OCR slips (\"Erz ıı fahrung\", \"Unter- 1 schied\") silently corrected."]],"krv-b-0250":["Of\nTranscendental Logic\nFirst Division.\nThe Transcendental Analytic.¹",["- \"Analytik.\" with \"!\" in the OCR read as a footnote marker \"¹\"; the footnote text is not in the passage.","- Abtheilung = division."]],"krv-b-0251":["This analytic is the dissection of the whole of our cognition *a priori* into the elements of the pure cognition of the understanding. In this the following items matter: 1. That the concepts be pure and not empirical concepts. 2. That they belong not to intuition and to sensibility, but to thinking and to the understanding. 3. That they be elementary concepts and be well distinguished from the derived ones or those composed out of them. 4. That their table be complete, and that they completely fill out the whole field of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be assumed with reliability on the estimate of an aggregate brought about merely by trials; hence it is possible only by means of an idea of the whole of the cognition of the understanding *a priori* and through the division of the concepts determined from it, which constitute it, hence only through their interconnection in a system. The pure understanding separates itself entirely not only from everything empirical, but even from all sensibility. It is therefore a unity subsisting for itself, sufficient to itself, and not to be increased by any additions coming externally. Hence the sum total of its cognition will constitute a system to be grasped and determined under an idea, the completeness and articulation of which can at the same time furnish a touchstone of the correctness and genuineness of all pieces of cognition that fit into it. This whole part of transcendental logic, however, consists of two books, of which the one contains the concepts, the other the principles of the pure understanding.",["- Zergliederung = dissection; Stücke = items (literally \"pieces\"); \"Erkenntnißstücke\" = pieces of cognition.","- Zusammenhang = interconnection; Inbegriff = sum total; Einheit = unity; Idee = idea; Wissenschaft = science.","- \"die ... Begriffe, welche sie ausmachen\": \"sie\" (feminine singular/plural) could refer to the completeness/science or to the cognition; left as \"it.\"","- \"Ueberschlag\" = estimate; \"gnugsame\" = sufficient (Latin sense not at issue); \"Richtigkeit\" = correctness; \"Aechtheit\" = genuineness.","- \"Büch em\" in the OCR corrected to \"Büchern.\""]],"krv-b-0252":["Of the\nTranscendental Analytic\nFirst Book.\nThe Analytic of Concepts.",["- Buch = book; Begriffe = concepts."]],"krv-b-0253":["By the analytic of concepts I understand not the analysis of them or the usual procedure in philosophical investigations, of dissecting concepts that present themselves as to their content and bringing them to distinctness, but the as yet little attempted dissection of the faculty of the understanding itself, in order to research the possibility of concepts *a priori* by seeking them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and analyzing its pure use in general; for this is the proper business of a transcendental philosophy; the rest is the logical treatment of concepts in philosophy in general. We will therefore pursue the pure concepts to their first germs and predispositions in the human understanding, in which they lie prepared, until they are finally developed on the occasion of experience and, freed by the very same understanding from the empirical conditions attaching to them, presented in their purity.",["- Deutlichkeit = distinctness (cf. deutlich = distinct); Verstandesvermögen = faculty of the understanding; Gebrauch = use.","- \"Analytik\" and \"Analysis\" are distinguished in the German; both rendered \"analytic\"/\"analysis\" accordingly; \"Zergliederung\" = dissection, \"analysiren\" = analyze.","- \"Transscendental-Philosophie\" = transcendental philosophy (Philosophie, not Weltweisheit).","- \"Anlagen\" = predispositions (not in glossary); \"Keime\" = germs.","- \"erforschen\" = research (as verb); \"Lauterkeit\" = purity (distinct from \"rein\").","- Stray OCR \":\" and \"1\" silently omitted."]],"krv-b-0254":["Of the\nAnalytic of Concepts",["-"]],"krv-b-0255":["First Chapter.",["- Hauptstück = chapter (literally \"main piece\"). The trailing \"10\" is a line number and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0256":["On the Clue to the Discovery of All Pure\nConcepts of the Understanding.",["- Leitfaden = clue (literally \"guiding thread\"); Entdeckung = discovery; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding."]],"krv-b-0257":["If one sets a faculty of cognition into play, various concepts come forth according to the manifold occasions, which make this faculty knowable and can be collected into a more or less extensive compilation, depending on whether the observation of them has been carried out over a longer time or with greater acuteness. Where this investigation will be completed can never be determined with certainty according to this, as it were, mechanical procedure. Also the concepts that one finds only thus on occasion are discovered in no order and systematic unity, but are in the end only paired according to similarities and placed in series according to the magnitude of their content, from the simple ones onward to the more composite, series that are brought about as anything but systematic, although in a certain way methodical.",["- Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; kennbar = knowable (cf. kennen = know; the sense \"recognizable\" is not excluded); Beobachtung = observation.","- Aufsatz = compilation (OCR \"Auf-: satz\"); \"Scharffinnigkeit\" (OCR \"Scharffinnigfeit\") = acuteness.","- \"nachdem\" = \"depending on whether\" (could also be \"after\"; the German is ambiguous between \"according as\" and \"after\").","- Einheit = unity; Größe = magnitude; Inhalt = content; einfach = simple; \"zusammengesetzt\" = composite (OCR \"zuz sammengesetzten\" corrected).","- \"die nichts weniger als systematisch\": \"nothing less than systematic\" could mean \"anything but\" or (literally) \"nothing less than\"; rendered \"anything but,\" the usual sense; the literal reading is ambiguous in the original only in form."]],"krv-b-0258":["Transcendental philosophy has the advantage, but also the obligation, to seek out its concepts according to a principle, because they spring from the understanding as absolute unity, pure and unmixed, and therefore must themselves connect with one another according to a concept or idea. Such an interconnection, however, provides a rule by which each pure concept of the understanding can have its place determined, and all of them together their completeness, a priori, which would otherwise all depend on whim or on chance.",["- Transscendentalphilosophie = \"transcendental philosophy\"; Verstand = understanding; Begriff = concept; Idee = idea; Zusammenhang = interconnection (the verb zusammenhängen rendered \"connect\").","- \"a priori\" stands at the end of the clause and could attach to \"determined\" or to \"completeness\" (or both); left ambiguous as in the German.","- \"welches alles\" rendered \"which would otherwise all\"; the antecedent is left open as in the German."]],"krv-b-0259":["Of the",["- Fragment (running-head/title); translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-0260":["Transcendental Clue to the Discovery of All",["- Fragment of a heading continued in the next passage. Leitfaden = \"clue\"."]],"krv-b-0261":["Pure Concepts of the Understanding\nFirst Section.",["- Abschnitt = \"section\"."]],"krv-b-0262":["On the Logical Use of the Understanding in General.",["- Gebrauch = use; überhaupt = \"in general\"."]],"krv-b-0263":["The understanding was explained above merely negatively: through a nonsensible faculty of cognition. Now we cannot partake of any intuition independently of sensibility. Consequently the understanding is no faculty of intuition. But there is no other way of cognizing besides through intuition than through concepts. Thus the cognition of every understanding, at least of the human understanding, is a cognition through concepts, not intuitive but discursive. All intuitions, as sensible, rest on affections, concepts therefore on functions. By function, however, I understand the unity of the action of ordering different representations under a common one. Concepts are therefore grounded on the spontaneity of thinking, as sensible intuitions are on the receptivity of impressions. Now the understanding can make no other use of these concepts than that it judges by means of them. Since no representation refers immediately to the object except merely the intuition, a concept is never related immediately to an object, but to some other representation of it (be it intuition or itself already concept). Judgment is therefore the mediate cognition of an object, consequently the representation of a representation of it. In every judgment there is a concept that is valid for many, and among this many also comprehends a given representation, which latter is then related immediately to the object. Thus, e.g., in the judgment: all bodies are divisible, the concept of the divisible is related to various other concepts; among these, however, it is here related particularly to the concept of body, but this to certain appearances that come before us. Consequently these objects are represented mediately through the concept of divisibility. All judgments are accordingly functions of unity among our representations, since, namely, instead of an immediate representation a higher one, which comprehends this and several others under itself, is used for the cognition of the object, and many possible cognitions are thereby drawn together into one. We can, however, trace all actions of the understanding back to judgments, so that the understanding in general can be represented as a faculty for judging. For according to the above it is a faculty for thinking. Thinking is cognition through concepts. Concepts, however, as predicates of possible judgments, are related to some representation of a still undetermined object. Thus the concept of body signifies something, e.g., metal, that can be cognized through that concept. It is therefore concept only in that other representations are contained under it, by means of which it can be related to objects. It is therefore the predicate for a possible judgment, e.g., every metal is a body. The functions of the understanding can therefore be found in their entirety if one can exhibit completely the functions of unity in judgments. That this, however, can very well be accomplished, the following section will place before our eyes.",["- \"Mfo\"/\"Afo\" = Also (OCR), rendered \"Consequently\"/\"Thus\"; \"Erfenntniß\" = Erkenntniß; stray OCR numerals/letters (\"ı\", \"1\", \"2\") omitted.","- Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Anschauung = intuition; Vermögen = faculty; Begriff = concept; Vorstellung = representation; Gegenstand = object (\"Object\" not occurring here); Erscheinung = appearance; Eindruck = impression; begreifen = comprehend; bedeuten = signify.","- Urtheil/urtheilen = judgment/judge; \"Handlung\" = \"action\" in both occurrences; \"Be— griffe\" a line-break hyphenation.","- \"gemeinschaftlichen\" = \"common\"; \"mittelbar/unmittelbar\" = mediate/immediate.","- \"welche letztere denn\" is ambiguous in reference only slightly; kept as \"which latter is then\"."]],"krv-b-0264":["Of the",["- Fragment (running head)."]],"krv-b-0265":["Clue to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding",["- Stray OCR \"ö\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0266":["Second Section.",["- Stray OCR \"2\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0267":["§ 9.",["- OCR \"89.\" read as \"§ 9.\""]],"krv-b-0268":["On the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgments.",["- None beyond terminology: Function = function."]],"krv-b-0269":["If we abstract from all content of a judgment in general and attend only to the mere form of the understanding in it, we find that the function of thinking in it can be brought under four titles, each of which contains three moments under itself. They can suitably be presented in the following table.",["- Inhalt = content; Form = form; Titel = \"titles\"; Momente = \"moments\"; Tafel = \"table\"."]],"krv-b-0270":["1.\nQuantity of Judgments.\nUniversal\nParticular",["- Allgemeine = universal; Besondere = particular."]],"krv-b-0271":["Singular",["- Stray OCR \"5\" omitted. Einzelne = singular."]],"krv-b-0272":["Quality. Relation.",["- Qualität = quality; Relation = relation (the numerals 2 and 3 are not in the text given)."]],"krv-b-0273":["Affirmative Categorical Negative Hypothetical Infinite Disjunctive",["- Columns run together in the OCR (Bejahende/Verneinende/Unendliche under Quality; Kategorische/Hypothetische/Disjunctive under Relation); stray \"p\", \"0\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0274":["4.\nModality.\nProblematic\nAssertoric",["- Assertorische = assertoric."]],"krv-b-0275":["Apodictic. Since this division seems to deviate in some, though not essential, respects from the customary technique of the logicians, the following",["- Stray OCR \"5\", \"N\" omitted. Apodiktische = apodictic; sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0276":["safeguards against the apprehended misunderstanding will not be unnecessary. 1. The logicians rightly say that in the use of judgments in syllogisms singular judgments can be treated like universal ones. For precisely because they have no domain at all, their predicate cannot be drawn merely to some of what is contained under the concept of the subject, while being excepted from some. It therefore holds of that concept without exception, just as if it were a generally valid concept that had a domain, of whose entire meaning the predicate held. If, on the other hand, we compare a singular judgment with a generally valid one merely as cognition according to magnitude, it relates to the latter as unity to infinity and is thus in itself essentially distinguished from it. Thus if I estimate a singular judgment (*judicium singulare*) not merely according to its inner validity but also as cognition in general according to the magnitude it has in comparison with other cognitions, then it is certainly distinguished from generally valid judgments (*judicia communia*) and deserves a special place in a complete table of the moments of thinking in general (though certainly not in the logic limited merely to the use of judgments among one another).",["- Vernunftschlüsse = syllogisms; Umfang = domain; Bedeutung = meaning; Größe = magnitude; Gültigkeit = validity; gemeingültig = \"generally valid\"; schätzen = \"estimate\" (not in glossary; beurteilen would be \"assess\").","- Stray OCR \"0\", \"5\" omitted. \"sie\" in \"verhält sie sich\" (sic, for \"es\") rendered \"it\".","- \"besondere Stelle\" = \"special place\"; \"besonder\" = special."]],"krv-b-0277":["2. Likewise, in a transcendental logic infinite judgments must still be distinguished from affirmative ones, even though in general logic they are rightly counted with the latter and make up no special member of the division. For the latter abstracts from all content of the predicate (even though it is negative) and looks only to whether it is attributed to the subject or opposed to it. The former, however, considers the judgment also according to the worth or content of this logical affirmation by means of a merely negative predicate, and what gain this procures with regard to the whole of cognition. Had I said of the soul, it is not mortal, I would at least have averted an error through a negative judgment. Now through the proposition: the soul is nonmortal, I have indeed actually affirmed according to the logical form, in that I posit the soul in the unlimited domain of nonmortal beings. Since now the mortal contains one part of the whole domain of possible beings, the nonmortal, however, the other, nothing else is said through my proposition than that the soul is one of the infinite multitude of things that remain over if I take away the mortal altogether. Thereby, however, the infinite sphere of everything possible is only limited so far that the mortal is separated from it and the soul is posited in the remaining space of its domain. This space, however, remains still infinite with this exception, and several more parts of it can be taken away without the concept of the soul in the least growing thereby and being determined affirmatively. These infinite judgments, therefore, with regard to the logical domain, are actually merely limiting with regard to the content of cognition in general, and to this extent they must not be passed over in the transcendental table of all moments of thinking in the parts, because the function of the understanding exercised herein may perhaps be important in the field of its pure cognition a priori.",["- \"Diese\"/\"Jene\" rendered \"the latter\"/\"the former\" (general logic / transcendental logic); \"Nr: theilen\" (OCR for \"Nr. [Momenten]\"?) is doubtful; \"in den Nr: theilen\" rendered \"in the parts\"; reading uncertain (probably \"in den Theilen\"?). Stray OCR \"as\", \"1\", \"2\" omitted.","- Satz = proposition; Wesen = being; Menge = multitude; Theil = part; setzen = posit; Sphäre = sphere; \"Raum\" = space; Ding = thing; Inhalt = content; Erkenntniß = cognition; \"a priori\" retained in roman as in the passage.","- \"beschränken/beschränkend\" = limit/limiting (Schranke = limitation); \"unbeschränkt\" = \"unlimited\"."]],"krv-b-0278":["3. All relations of thinking in judgments are those a) of the predicate to the subject, b) of the ground to the sequel, c) of the divided cognition and of the collected members of the division to one another. *) In the first kind of judgments only two concepts, in the second two judgments, in the third several judgments are considered in relation to one another. The hypothetical proposition: if a perfect justice is there, then the persistently evil is punished, contains properly the relation of two propositions: a perfect justice is there, and: the persistently evil is punished. Whether both of these propositions are true in themselves remains undecided here. It is only the consequence that is thought through this judgment. Finally the disjunctive judgment contains a relation of two or more propositions to one another, but not of succession, but of logical opposition, insofar as the sphere of the one excludes that of the other, but yet at the same time of community, insofar as together they fill up the sphere of the proper cognition, thus a relation of the parts of the sphere of a cognition, since the sphere of each part is a supplementary piece of the sphere of the other to the whole sum total of the divided cognition; e.g. the world is there either through a blind chance, or through inner necessity, or through an outer cause. Each of these propositions takes up a part of the sphere of the possible cognition concerning the existence of a world in general, all together the whole sphere. To take the cognition away from one of these spheres means to posit it in one of the others, and, conversely, to posit it in one sphere means to take it away from the others. There is therefore in a disjunctive judgment a certain community of cognitions, which consists in this, that they mutually exclude one another, but nevertheless thereby determine the true cognition in the whole, in that taken together they make up the whole content of a single given cognition. And this is also all that I find it necessary to remark here on account of what follows.",["- OCR repairs: \"e)\" read as c); \"an si)\" as \"an sich\" (in itself); \"Ge— rechtigkeit\" and \"Ab— folge\" rejoined. The stray \"3\" before \"In der ersteren\" is read as the footnote sign, rendered \"*)\"; this is a conjecture.","- Grund = ground; Folge = sequel (glossary); Consequenz = consequence (Latinate; Folgerung is also \"consequence\"); Abfolge = succession.","- Erkenntniß = cognition; Urtheil = judgment; Satz = proposition; Sphäre = sphere; Inbegriff = sum total; Eintheilung = division; Gemeinschaft = community; Dasein = existence.","- Zufall = \"chance\" (not in glossary; zufällig would be \"contingent\"). Beharrlich = \"persistently\".","- \"Art der Urtheile\" rendered \"kind\" (Art).","- \"sie\" in \"sie in eine der übrigen setzen\" is rendered \"it\" and left as in the German; the antecedent is not made explicit."]],"krv-b-0279":["*) As if thinking in the first case were a function of the understanding, in the second of the power of judgment, in the third of reason. A remark that awaits its clarification only in what follows.",["- Footnote text; \"*)\" is its sign. Verstand = understanding; Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Vernunft = reason.","- Aufklärung is rendered \"clarification\" (not in the glossary; Erläuterung is also \"clarification\")."]],"krv-b-0280":["4. The modality of judgments is a quite special function of them, which has the distinguishing feature in itself that it contributes nothing to the content of the judgment (for besides magnitude, quality and relation there is nothing more that would make up the content of a judgment), but concerns only the value of the copula in relation to thinking in general. Problematic judgments are those in which one assumes the affirming or denying as merely possible (optional); assertoric, where it is considered as actual (true); apodictic, in which one regards it as necessary.*) Thus the two judgments whose relation makes up the hypothetical judgment (antec. and consequ.), likewise those in whose interaction the disjunctive consists (members of the division), are altogether only problematic. In the above example the proposition: a perfect justice is there, is not said assertorically, but only thought as an optional judgment, which it is possible for someone to assume; and only the consequence is assertoric. Hence such judgments can also be obviously false and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of the cognition of truth. Thus the judgment: the world is there through blind chance, is in the disjunctive judgment of only problematic significance, namely that someone might assume this proposition perhaps for a moment, and yet serves (like the marking down of the false path among the number of all those that one can take) to find the true one. The problematic proposition is therefore that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective), i.e., a free choice to let such a proposition count, a merely arbitrary taking up of it into the understanding. The assertoric says of logical actuality or truth, as for instance in a hypothetical syllogism the antecedent occurs problematically in the major premise, assertorically in the minor premise, and indicates that the proposition is already combined with the understanding according to its laws. The apodictic proposition thinks the assertoric as determined by these very laws of the understanding and hence as asserting a priori, and in such a way expresses logical necessity. Since now here everything is incorporated by degrees into the understanding, so that one first judges something problematically, thereupon indeed assumes it assertorically as true, finally asserts it as inseparably combined with the understanding, i.e., as necessary and apodictic, one can also call these three functions of modality so many moments of thinking in general.",["- OCR repairs: \"vors kommt\" read as \"vorkommt\"; the stray \"f\" before \"annimmt\" omitted; \"ansieht.“)\" read as \"ansieht.*)\", the sign of the footnote (see krv-b-0279).","- Größe = magnitude; Qualität = quality; Verhältniß = relation; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Bedeutung = significance; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Zahl = number; Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Obersatz/Untersatz = major/minor premise.","- \"Verzeichnung\" rendered \"marking down\" (uncertain; it could also be \"charting\" or \"listing\").","- \"a priori behauptend\" is ambiguous between the apodictic proposition \"asserting a priori\" and its being determined a priori; I have kept the German order.","- \"annimmt\" and \"annehmen\" are rendered \"assume\"; \"aufnehmen\" as \"take up\"."]],"krv-b-0281":["Of the",["- Fragment of the running title, continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0282":["Guiding Thread to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding",["- OCR \"Verstande Sbegriffe\" read as \"Verstandesbegriffe\". Leitfaden = guiding thread; Entdeckung = discovery."]],"krv-b-0283":["Third Section.",["- The stray \"3\" after \"Abschnitt.\" is omitted as an OCR artifact."]],"krv-b-0284":["On the Pure Concepts of the Understanding or Categories.",["- \"Verstandesbegriff en\" read as \"Verstandesbegriffen\"."]],"krv-b-0285":["General logic abstracts, as has already been said several times, from all content of cognition and expects that representations will be given to it elsewhere, from wherever it may be, in order first to transform these into concepts, which proceeds analytically. Transcendental logic, on the contrary, has a manifold of sensibility lying before it a priori, which transcendental aesthetic offers it, in order to give a material to the pure concepts of the understanding, without which it would be without all content, hence entirely empty. Space and time now contain a manifold of pure intuition a priori, but nevertheless belong to the conditions of the receptivity of our mind, under which alone it can receive representations of objects, which hence must also at all times affect the concept of them. But the spontaneity of our thinking requires that this manifold first be gone through in a certain way, taken up and combined, in order to make a cognition out of it. This action I call synthesis.",["- Vorstellung = representation; Mannigfaltiges = manifold; Stoff = material; Anschauung = intuition; Gemüth = mind; Gegenstand = object; Handlung = action.","- \"woher es auch sei\": \"it\" is left without antecedent, as in the German."]],"krv-b-0286":["By synthesis, however, in the most general meaning, I understand the action of adding different representations to one another and of comprehending their manifoldness in one cognition. Such a synthesis is pure if the manifold is given not empirically but a priori (as that in space and time). Before all analysis of our representations these must first be given, and no concepts can arise analytically as to content. But the synthesis of a manifold (whether it be given empirically or a priori) first brings forth a cognition, which, to be sure, can at first still be raw and confused and thus in need of analysis; yet synthesis is that which properly collects the elements for cognitions and unites them into a certain content; it is therefore the first thing to which we have to pay heed if we want to judge about the first origin of our cognition.",["- Bedeutung = meaning (glossary also allows \"significance\"); begreifen = comprehend; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness (distinct from Mannigfaltiges, manifold); Inhalt = content; urtheilen = judge.","- \"dem Inhalte nach\" rendered \"as to content\"."]],"krv-b-0287":["Synthesis in general is, as we shall see in the future, the mere effect of the imagination, of a blind, though indispensable function of the soul, without which we would have no cognition at all anywhere, but of which we are only seldom even once conscious. But to bring this synthesis to concepts, that is a function that pertains to the understanding, and by which it first provides us cognition in the proper meaning.",["- Wirkung = effect; Einbildungskraft = imagination; bewußt = conscious; \"überall gar keine\" rendered \"at all anywhere\"; \"eigentlicher Bedeutung\" = \"proper meaning\"."]],"krv-b-0288":["Pure synthesis, represented generally, now gives the pure concept of the understanding. But by this synthesis I understand that which rests on a ground of synthetic unity a priori: thus our counting (it is more noticeable especially with larger numbers) is a synthesis according to concepts, because it takes place according to a common ground of unity (e.g., the decadic). Under this concept, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.",["- \"gemeinschaftlich\" rendered \"common\" (as gemein would be); \"Dekadik\" rendered \"the decadic\" (not in glossary).","- \"unter diesem Begriffe\" refers back to the concept; left as in the German."]],"krv-b-0289":["Analytically, different representations are brought under a concept (a business with which general logic deals). But not the representations, rather the pure synthesis of representations to bring to concepts, is what transc. logic teaches. The first thing that must be given to us for the sake of the cognition of all objects *a priori* is f the manifold of pure intuition; the synthesis of this manifold through the imagination is the second, but yet gives no cognition. The concepts, which give unity to this pure synthesis and consist merely in the representation of this necessary synthetic unity, do the third toward the cognition of an occurring object and rest on the understanding. 1",["- \"f\" is a stray mark in the OCR text, left as it stands; the passage opens at \"Analytisch\" without any supplied subject.","- Vorstellung = representation; Begriff = concept; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Anschauung = intuition; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Verstand = understanding.","- \"nicht die Vorstellungen, sondern die reine Synthesis der Vorstellungen auf Begriffe zu bringen\": the German construction is kept; \"to bring\" governs both \"representations\" and \"synthesis,\" as in the German.","- \"Geschäfte\" (a form of Geschäft) rendered \"business\"; \"transsc.\" is the abbreviation for transscendentale, left abbreviated. The trailing \"1\" is a footnote marker with no footnote text supplied."]],"krv-b-0290":["The same function that gives unity to the different representations in a judgment also gives unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition, which, expressed generally, is called the pure concept of the understanding. The same understanding, therefore, and indeed through precisely the same actions through which it brought about in concepts, by means of",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage (krv-b-0291).","- Urtheil = judgment; Function = function; Verstandesbegriff = concept of the understanding.","- \"welche\" may refer to \"Einheit\" or to the function or the synthesis; I have kept \"which\" ambiguous."]],"krv-b-0291":["X the analytic unity the logical form of a judgment, also brings, by means of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition in general, a trans—scendental content into its representations, on account of which they are called pure concepts of the understanding, which go *a priori* to objects, which general logic cannot accomplish. 2\"",["- \"X\" is an OCR mark at the start; the passage begins mid-sentence (\"der analytischen Einheit\"), continuing the previous passage. The hyphenated \"trans—scendental\" is the OCR line-break form of transscendental.","- Object = object (Gegenstand also \"object\"); \"welches\" at the end may refer to the whole preceding clause or to \"a priori auf Objecte gehen\"; left ambiguous. The trailing \"2\"\" is a footnote marker with no text supplied.","- Inhalt = content; Einheit = unity."]],"krv-b-0292":["In such a way there arise precisely as many pure concepts of the understanding, which go *a priori* to objects of intuition in general, as there were logical functions in all possible judgments in the previous table: for the understanding is completely exhausted by the said functions and its faculty thereby entirely measured out. We will call these concepts, after) 3 Aristotle, categories, since our aim is originally indeed the same as his, although in execution it departs very greatly from it.",["- \"nach)\" contains an OCR stray parenthesis, kept as it stands, with the following \"3\" a footnote marker; no footnote text supplied.","- Gegenstand = object; Vermögen = faculty; Absicht = aim; Anschauung = intuition; Function = function.","- \"einerlei\" rendered \"the same\"; \"ob sie sich gleich davon\" = \"although it ... from it\": \"sie\" (Absicht) taken as the antecedent."]],"krv-b-0293":["Table of the Categories.\n1.\nOf Quantity:\nUnity",["- Einheit = unity."]],"krv-b-0294":["5 Vielheit",["- The \"5\" is a line-number remnant of the OCR; Vielheit is left in German here as the passage consists of this single word with the stray number. Rendered: \"Plurality\" would be the English; the OCR number is retained as it stands."]],"krv-b-0295":["Totality.\nOf Quality: Of Relation:",["- Allheit = totality. The columns are run together in the OCR; kept as in the passage."]],"krv-b-0296":["Reality of Inherence and Subsistence (sub-",["- Realität = reality. The passage breaks off at \"(sub-\"; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0297":["Negation stantia et accidens)",["- Latin \"substantia et accidens\" is split across passages (0296/0297) and left as it stands."]],"krv-b-0298":["Limitation. of Causality and Dependence",["- Limitation = Limitation (Latinate in German as in English); Causalität = causality; Dependenz = dependence."]],"krv-b-0299":["(Cause and Effect)",["- Ursache = cause; Wirkung = effect."]],"krv-b-0300":["of Community (Interaction",["- Gemeinschaft = community; Wechselwirkung = interaction."]],"krv-b-0301":["between the Acting",["- Passage continues in the next fragment (\"den und Leidenden\")."]],"krv-b-0302":["and Suffering).",["- The German splits \"Handeln-/den\" and \"Leidenden\": \"den\" here belongs to \"Handelnden\" in the previous fragment. The lines are rendered together in sense: \"the Acting and Suffering.\""]],"krv-b-0303":["4.\nOf Modality:\nPossibility — Impossibility\nExistence — Non-being\nNecessity — Contingency.",["- Dasein = existence; Möglichkeit = possibility; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Zufälligkeit = contingency."]],"krv-b-0304":["This now is the listing of all originally pure concepts of synthesis that the understanding contains *a priori* in itself, and for whose sake it is also only a pure understanding, in that through them alone it can understand something in the manifold of intuition, i.e., think an object of it. This division is generated systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty of judging (which is just as much as the faculty of thinking), and has not arisen rhapsodically from a search for pure concepts undertaken on good luck, of whose completeness one can never be certain, since it is only inferred through induction, not to mention that in the latter way one never has insight into why precisely these and not other concepts inhabit the pure understanding. It was a plan worthy of a sagacious man for Aristotle to search for these fundamental concepts. But since he had no *principium*, he scooped them up as they happened upon him, and first rounded up ten of them, which he called categories (predicaments). Subsequently he believed he had found five more of them, which he added under the name of postpredicaments. Yet his table remained still always deficient. Besides, there are also found among them some *modi* of pure sensibility (*quando, ubi, situs*, likewise *prius, simul*), also an empirical one (*motus*), which do not belong at all in this ancestral register of the understanding, or else the derived concepts are also counted among the original concepts (*actio, passio*), and some of the latter are entirely lacking.",["- Vermögen zu urtheilen = faculty of judging; Verstand = understanding; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Anschauung = intuition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Object = object; Princip = principle (Principium kept in Latin italics).","- \"ob... verstehen, d. i. ein Object derselben denken kann\": \"derselben\" taken as referring to \"Anschauung\" or to \"Mannigfaltige\"; left as \"of it.\"","- \"auf gut Glück\" rendered \"on good luck\"; \"ihm aufstießen\" = \"happened upon him\"; \"Stammregister\" = \"ancestral register.\"","- \"der letztern\" in the last clause: ambiguous between the derived concepts and the latter-named category kinds; kept as \"the latter.\"","- \"Vollzähligkeit\" = completeness; \"Prädicament\" = predicament."]],"krv-b-0305":["For the sake of the latter, therefore, it is still to be remarked: that the categories, as the true stem concepts of the pure understanding, also have their equally pure derived concepts, which can by no means be passed over in a complete system of transcendental philosophy, but with the mere mention of which I can be content in a merely critical attempt.",["- \"der letztern\" = \"the latter,\" left unresolved as in the German. Stammbegriff = stem concept; Transscendental-Philosophie = transcendental philosophy."]],"krv-b-0306":["Let it be permitted me to call these pure, but derived, concepts of the understanding the predicabilia of the pure understanding (in contrast to the predicaments). If one has the original and primitive concepts, the derived and subaltern ones can easily be added and the family tree of the pure understanding fully painted. Since I am concerned here not with the completeness of the system, but only of the principles: toward a system, I reserve this supplement for another occupation. But one can fairly attain this aim if one takes the ontological textbooks in hand and, e.g., subordinates to the category of causality the predicabilia of force, of action, of suffering, to that of community those of presence, of resistance, to the : predicaments of modality those of arising, passing away, of alteration, etc. The categories, combined with the *modi* of pure sensibility or also among one another, give a great multitude of derived concepts *a priori*, to remark which and where possible to list to completeness would be a useful and not unpleasant, but here dispensable effort.",["- Prädicabilien = predicabilia; Prädicamente = predicaments; the Latin forms are Kant's own and are left as in the text.","- \"den : Prädicamenten\": the colon is an OCR mark, kept; \"der der Gemeinschaft die der Gegenwart\" is a doubled \"der\" in the OCR; rendered as \"to that of community those of presence.\"","- \"Principien: zu einem System\": colon kept as in the OCR. \"hier;\" semicolon likewise.","- Absicht = aim; Kraft = force; Menge = multitude."]],"krv-b-0307":["I deliberately relieve myself of the definitions of these categories in this treatise, although I should like to be in possession of them. I will analyze these concepts in what follows to the degree that is sufficient in relation to the doctrine of method that I am working on. In a system of pure reason one could rightly demand them of me: but here they would only bring the main point of the investigation out of sight, in that they would arouse doubts and attacks which, without withdrawing anything from the essential aim, one can quite well refer to another occupation. Meanwhile it nevertheless shines forth distinctly from the little that I have cited of this that a complete dictionary with all the explanations required for it is not only possible, but also easy to bring about. The compartments are once there; it is only necessary to fill them, and a systematic topic, like the present one, does not easily let one miss the place to which each concept peculiarly belongs, and at the same time easily notice the one that is still empty.",["- \"Ich!\" in the OCR is a stray mark, silently read as \"Ich.\" Definition = definition (Erklärung = explanation/definition, here \"explanations\"); Methodenlehre = doctrine of method; Folge in \"in der Folge\" = \"in what follows.\"","- Absicht = aim; Grad = degree; deutlich = distinctly; Topik = topic.","- \"die Fächer sind einmal da\": \"once\" for einmal, kept literal."]],"krv-b-0308":["About this table of the categories, pretty considerations can be made, which perhaps could have considerable consequences with regard to the scientific form of all cognitions of reason. For that this table is uncommonly serviceable, indeed indispensable, in the theoretical part of philosophy, for completely sketching the plan for the whole of a science, insofar as it rests on concepts *a priori*, and for dividing it systematically according to determinate principles: is already evident of itself from this, that the said table contains all elementary concepts of the understanding completely, indeed even the form of a system of them in the human understanding, and consequently gives direction to all moments of a prospective speculative science, indeed even to their order, as I have also elsewhere*) given a sample of this. Here now are some of these remarks.",["- Betrachtung = consideration; Vernunfterkenntnisse = cognitions of reason; Wissenschaft = science; Begriff = concept; Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit).","- \"ale\" in the OCR silently read as \"alle.\" \"anderwärts*)\" retains the footnote marker; footnote text appears in 0311.","- \"und sie... abzutheilen: erhellt\": colon preserved. \"ihre Ordnung\" taken as dependent on \"Anweisung giebt\"; the sentence structure (ale Momente... ja sogar ihre Ordnung) is kept ambiguous.","- artig = \"pretty\"; erheblich = \"considerable.\""]],"krv-b-0309":["The first is: that this table, which contains four classes of concepts of the understanding, can first be divided into two sections, the former of which are directed at objects of intuition (of the pure as well as the empirical), but the second at the existence of these objects (either in relation to one another or to the understanding).",["- \"zerfällen\" (for zerfallen/zerfällen) rendered \"divided.\" Gegenstand = object; Existenz = existence; Anschauung = intuition.","- The number disagreement \"deren erstere ... sind\" is kept (\"the former ... are\"); \"Abtheilungen\" = sections."]],"krv-b-0310":["The first class I would call that of the mathematical, the second of the dynamical categories. The first class has, as one sees, no correlates, which are encountered only in the second class.",["- Classe = class; \"mathematischen\" and \"dynamischen\" kept as adjectives; \"die allein in der zweiten Classe angetroffen werden\" may attach to \"Correlate\" (read so) or to the whole clause."]],"krv-b-0311":[") Metaphys. Anfangsgr. der Naturwissensch. This distinction must surely have a ground in the nature of the understanding.",["- This is the footnote (marker \")\" is the OCR's \"*)\"). Title kept in abbreviated German, as abbreviated in the text. \"Grund\" = ground; Verstand = understanding."]],"krv-b-0312":["2nd remark. That everywhere there is an equal number of the categories of each class, namely three, which just as much summons to reflection,",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence. \"Anmerk.\" = remark; \"Zahl\" = number; \"nachdenken\" = reflection (not Überlegung)."]],"krv-b-0313":["mm. since otherwise all division a priori through concepts must be dichotomy. : To this, however, is added that the third category everywhere arises from the combination of the second with the first of its class.",["- \"mm.\" is a stray OCR fragment at the start (probably the end of a preceding word); left as it stands.","- Eintheilung = division (glossary: Einteilung); Begriffe = concepts; Verbindung = combination.","- The \". :\" before \"Dazu\" is as in the OCR; kept, since the punctuation is doubtful."]],"krv-b-0314":["Thus totality (Totalität) is nothing other than multiplicity considered as unity, limitation nothing other than reality, combined with“",["- Allheit = totality, with Totalität in parentheses; Vielheit = multiplicity (Menge is also \"multiplicity\" in the glossary); Einheit = unity; Realität = reality.","- The passage breaks off in mid-sentence (\"mit\" continues in the next passage as \"en — Negation verbunden\", i.e., \"mit der Negation\"). The closing quotation mark is an OCR remnant, kept."]],"krv-b-0315":["en — negation, community is the causality of a substance in the determination of another reciprocally, finally necessity is nothing other than the existence that is given through possibility itself. But one should by no means think that therefore the third category is a merely derived concept and not a root concept of the pure understanding. For the combination of the first and second, in order to bring forth the third concept, requires a special *actus* of the understanding, which is not the same as that exercised in the first and second. Thus the concept of a number (which belongs to the category of totality) is not always possible where the concepts of multitude and of unity are (e.g., in the representation of the infinite), or, from the fact that I combine the concept of a cause and that of a substance, the influence, i.e., how one substance could become the cause of something in another substance, is not yet immediately to be understood. From this it is clear that a special *actus* of the understanding is required for this, and so with the others. i",["- Opening \"en — \" is the OCR remnant of \"mit der Negation\" from the preceding passage; kept as it stands. \"ılf\" after \"hervorzu-\" is an OCR slip for \"hervorzubringen\"; corrected silently. The final \"i\" is a stray OCR mark, kept.","- Gemeinschaft = community; Wechselwirkung would be \"interaction\". Allheit = totality; Menge = multitude (glossary: multitude/multiplicity/amount); Einheit = unity; Zahl = number.","- Stammbegriff = root concept; Einfluß = influence; Bestimmung = determination; Existenz = existence; Möglichkeit = possibility; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.","- \"Actus\" is Latin in Kant's text, italicized. \"aus … zu verstehen\" is a long construction; \"verstehen\" = understand.","- \"ob\" syntax: \"daraus, daß … noch nicht sofort der Einfluß … zu verstehen\" is kept in the German order and left elliptical as in the original."]],"krv-b-0316":["5th Remark. Of a single category, namely that of community, which is found under the third title, the correspondence with the form of a disjunctive judgment corresponding to it in the table of logical functions is not as striking as with the others.",["- \"Ste Anmerk.\" is read as \"3te Anmerk.\" (the OCR is doubtful); rendered \"5th Remark\" is uncertain, and the numeral is doubtful. The ordinal is not recoverable from the OCR.","- \"Gez a meinschaft\" = Gemeinschaft (OCR); corrected silently. Urtheil = judgment; Function = function.","- \"ihm\" refers back to \"dem dritten Titel\"; ambiguity kept (\"it\" would be the title or the category; I render \"to it\")."]],"krv-b-0317":["In order to assure oneself of this correspondence, one must note: that in all disjunctive judgments the sphere (the multitude of everything that is contained under it) is represented as a whole divided into parts (the subordinate concepts), and, because one cannot be contained under the other, they are thought as coordinated with one another, not subordinated, so that they determine one another not one-sidedly as in a series, but reciprocally as in an aggregate (if one member of the division is posited, all the rest are excluded, and vice versa).",["- \"z|\" is an OCR remnant, omitted as a page-break mark. Sphäre = sphere; Menge = multitude; Eintheilung = division; setzen = posit.","- \"ihm\" (under it) refers to the sphere or the judgment; German leaves this open. \"wechselseitig\" = reciprocally.","- The German \"gedacht werden\" at the end governs the whole sentence; kept in final position."]],"krv-b-0318":["Now a similar connection is thought in a whole of things, since one is not subordinated to another as effect to cause of its existence, but is simultaneously and reciprocally coordinated as cause with regard to the determination of the others (e.g., in a body whose parts reciprocally attract and also resist one another); which is an entirely different way of connection from that which is encountered in the mere relation of cause to effect (of ground to sequel), in which the sequel does not in turn reciprocally determine the ground and therefore does not make up a whole with it (as the world-creator with the world). The same procedure of the understanding, when it represents to itself the sphere of a divided concept, it also observes when it thinks a thing as divisible; and as the members of the division in the former exclude one another and yet are combined in one sphere, so it represents to itself the parts of the latter as ones whose existence (as substances) pertains to each also exclusively of the rest, yet as combined in a whole.",["- Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung = combination; Art = way; Verhältniß = relation; Grund/Folge = ground/sequel (Folge is glossed \"sequel; sequence\").","- Dasein and Existenz are both \"existence\" (Dasein in \"seines Daseins\", Existenz in the last clause).","- \"ihr/dem\": \"mit diesem\" refers to the ground (\"Grund\"); kept as \"it\". \"letzteren\"/\"ersteren\" = latter/former, kept.","- \"ausschließlich von den übrigen\" = \"exclusively of the rest\"; the ambiguity of \"ausschließlich\" (adverbial or \"exclusive of\") is kept."]],"krv-b-0319":["But there is found in the transcendental philosophy of the ancients still one chapter, which contains pure concepts of the understanding that, although they are not counted among the categories, should nevertheless hold of objects as concepts a priori according to them, in which case, however, they would increase the number of the categories, which cannot be. This is presented by the proposition so renowned among the scholastics: *quodlibet ens est unum, verum, bonum*. Although now the use of this principle with respect to the consequences (which yielded nothing but tautological propositions) turned out very meager, so that in more recent times too one is accustomed to set it up in metaphysics almost only for honor's sake, still a thought that has maintained itself for so long a time, however empty it may seem, always deserves an investigation of its origin and justifies the conjecture that it has its ground in some rule of the understanding, which has only, as often happens, been falsely interpreted. These supposed transcendental predicates of things are nothing other than logical requisites and criteria of all cognition of things in general, and lay at its basis the categories of quantity, namely of unity, multiplicity and totality, except that they, which properly would have to be taken as material, as belonging to the possibility of things themselves, in fact used only in a formal meaning, as belonging to the logical demand with regard to every cognition, and yet incautiously made these criteria of thinking into properties of things in themselves. For in every cognition of an object there is, namely, unity of the concept, which one can call qualitative unity, insofar as under it is thought only the unity of the collection of the manifold of cognitions, as perhaps the unity of the theme in a play, a speech, a fable. Second, truth with regard to the consequences. The more true consequences from a given concept, the more marks of its objective reality. This one could call the qualitative multiplicity of the marks that belong to a concept as a common ground (not thought in it as a magnitude). Finally third, perfection, which consists in this, that conversely this multiplicity together leads back to the unity of the concept and agrees completely with this one and with no other, which one can call qualitative completeness (totality). From which it is clear that these logical criteria of the possibility of cognition in general transform the three categories of magnitude, in which the unity in the generation of the quantum must be assumed as thoroughly homogeneous, here only with regard to the connection of even heterogeneous pieces of cognition in one consciousness through the quality of a cognition as principle. Thus the criterion of the possibility of a concept (not of the object of it) is the definition, in which the unity of the concept, the truth of everything that may be derived from it most immediately, finally the completeness of what has been drawn from it, make up what is requisite of it for the representation of the whole concept; or thus also the criterion of a hypothesis is the comprehensibility of the assumed ground of explanation or its unity (without auxiliary hypothesis), the truth (agreement among themselves and with experience) of the consequences to be derived from it, and finally the completeness of the ground of explanation for them, which refer back to nothing more nor less than was assumed in the hypothesis, and deliver again analytically a posteriori what was thought synthetically a priori, and agree with it. — Thus through the concepts of unity, truth and perfection the transcendental table of the categories is not at all supplemented, as if it were perhaps deficient, but only, in that the relation of these concepts to objects is entirely set aside, the procedure with them is brought under general logical rules of the agreement of cognition with itself.",["- OCR \"von um\" / \"bonum\": the text gives \"unum, verum, von um\" for \"unum, verum, bonum\"; restored silently. The footnote \"Der § 12 ist ein Zusatz von A2\" and the running-text intrusions (\"ılf\", \"z |\", \"Ste\") are editorial/OCR apparatus; the footnote is omitted as it is an editor's note (Erdmann/Akademie), not Kant's.","- Gegenstand/Object: \"Gegenständen\" and \"Object\" both \"object\". Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkenntnißstücke = pieces of cognition; Möglichkeit = possibility; Realität = reality; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Allheit/Totalität = totality.","- Vielheit and Menge: \"Vielheit\" = multiplicity; Eigenschaften = properties (glossary: Beschaffenheit = property too); Merkmale = marks (*nota*); Kennzeichen = marks (also; not in glossary); \"Kriterien\" = criteria.","- Doubtful OCR: \"zu dieem\" read as \"diesem\"; \"Verstellung\" read \"Vorstellung\" (representation); \"Schaue spiel\" = \"Schauspiel\"; \"qualita: tive\" = \"qualitative\". \"verständlichkeit\" rendered \"comprehensibility\" (Verständlichkeit, from verstehen; \"intelligibility\" is the other option).","- Ambiguities kept: \"sie\" in \"daß sie diese … brauchten\" refers to the ancients; \"dazu zusammenstimmen\" kept as \"agree with it\". \"a priori synthetisch gedacht … a posteriori analytisch\" kept so that the adverbs may attach to verb or participle.","- \"nach ihnen\" = \"according to them\", ambiguous (the categories or the concepts); kept.","- \"Nicht in ihm als Größe gedacht werden\" — \"in it\" refers to the concept; kept."]],"krv-b-0320":["Of the\nAnalytic of Concepts\nSecond Chapter.",["- Hauptstück = chapter. Analytik = Analytic; Begriffe = concepts."]],"krv-b-0321":[") Of the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",["- The leading \")\" is an OCR remnant, kept. Deduction = Deduktion/deduction; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; rein = pure."]],"krv-b-0322":["First Section.",["- Abschnitt = section."]],"krv-b-0323":["Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction",["- Principien = principles (Grundsatz is also \"principle\" in the glossary); transscendental = transcendental. The title continues in the next passage (\"überhaupt\")."]],"krv-b-0324":["in general.",["- \"überhaupt\" = \"in general\"; completes the title begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0325":["Teachers of right, when they speak of entitlements and presumptions, distinguish in a legal action the question about what is lawful (*quid iuris*) from that which concerns the fact (*quid facti*), and since they demand proof of both, they call the first, which is to establish the entitlement or also the legal claim, the deduction. We make use of a multitude of empirical concepts without anyone's objection and also hold ourselves entitled, even without deduction, to ascribe to them a sense and an imagined meaning, because we always have experience at hand to prove their objective reality. Meanwhile there are also usurped concepts, such as fortune, fate, which indeed run around with nearly universal indulgence, but are nevertheless occasionally called into question by the question: *quid iuris*; whereupon one falls into no small embarrassment with regard to the deduction of them, since one can cite no distinct ground of right either from experience or from reason, through which the entitlement to their use would become distinct.",[]],"krv-b-0326":["Among the manifold concepts, however, that make up the very mixed fabric of human cognition, there are some that are also destined for pure use *a priori* (fully independent of all experience), and the entitlement of these always requires a deduction: because proofs from experience are not sufficient for the legitimacy of such a use, but one must nevertheless know how these concepts can relate to objects that they yet take from no experience. I therefore call the explanation of the way in which concepts can relate *a priori* to objects their transcendental deduction, and distinguish it from the empirical deduction, which indicates the way in which a concept was acquired through experience and reflection on it, and therefore concerns not the legitimacy but the fact, from which the possession arose. 1",[]],"krv-b-0327":["We already have now two kinds of concepts of entirely different species, which nevertheless agree with one another in that both relate fully *a priori* to objects, namely the concepts of space and time as forms of sensibility and the categories as concepts of the understanding. To want to attempt an empirical deduction of them 1 would be entirely vain labor, because it is precisely in this that the distinguishing feature of their nature lies, that they relate to their objects without having borrowed anything from experience for the representation of them. If, therefore, a deduction of them is necessary, it will always have to be transcendental. 2",[]],"krv-b-0328":["Meanwhile one can seek out in experience, of these concepts as of all cognition, if not the *principium* of their possibility, then still the occasional causes of their production; where then the impressions of the senses give the first occasion to open the whole faculty of cognition with regard to them and to bring experience into being, which contains two very heterogeneous elements, namely a matter for cognition from the senses and a certain form for ordering it, from the inner source of pure intuiting and thinking, which, on the occasion of the former, are first brought into exercise and produce concepts. Such a tracing of the first strivings of our faculty of cognition, in order to ascend from individual perceptions to general concepts, has without doubt its great utility, and one has the renowned Locke to thank that he first opened the way to it. Yet a deduction of the pure concepts *a priori* never comes about through this, for it lies not at all on this path, because with regard to their future use, which is supposed to be entirely independent of experience, they must have to show a quite different birth certificate than that of descent from experiences. This attempted physiological derivation, which properly cannot be called deduction at all, because it concerns a *quaestionem facti*, I will therefore call the explanation of the possession of a pure cognition. It is thus clear that of these alone there can be a transcendental deduction and in no way an empirical one, and that the latter, with regard to the pure concepts *a priori*, are nothing but idle attempts, with which only he can occupy himself who has not comprehended the quite peculiar nature of these cognitions. But although the sole way of a possible deduction of pure cognition *a priori*, namely that on the transcendental path, is conceded, it does not thereby become evident that it is so unavoidably necessary. We have above traced the concepts of space and time to their sources by means of a transcendental deduction and explained and determined their objective validity *a priori*. Nevertheless geometry goes its secure step through nothing but cognitions *a priori*, without having to beg from philosophy a certificate of credentials for the pure and lawful descent of its fundamental concept of space. But the use of the concept in this science extends only to the outer world of sense, of which space is the pure form of its intuition, in which therefore all geometrical cognition, because it is grounded on intuition *a priori*, has immediate evidence, and the objects are given through the cognition itself *a priori* (according to the form) in intuition. By contrast, with the pure concepts of the understanding the unavoidable need begins to seek the transcendental deduction not only of them themselves, but also of space, because, since they speak of objects not through predicates of intuition and sensibility but of pure thinking *a priori*, they relate to objects universally without all conditions of sensibility, and since they are not grounded on experience, they also cannot show in intuition *a priori* any object on which they would ground their synthesis prior to all experience, and therefore not only arouse suspicion regarding the objective validity and limitations of their use, but also make that concept of space ambiguous, by being inclined to use it beyond the conditions of sensible intuition, on account of which a transcendental deduction of it was also needed above. Thus the reader must be convinced of the unavoidable necessity of such a transcendental deduction before he has taken a single step in the field of pure reason, because otherwise he proceeds blindly and, after having wandered about in manifold ways, must nevertheless return to the ignorance from which he had set out. But he must also distinctly see in advance the unavoidable difficulty, so that he does not complain of obscurity where the matter itself is deeply enveloped, or become too soon vexed over the removal of the obstacles, because what matters is either to give up entirely all claims to insights of pure reason as the most favored field, namely that beyond the boundaries of all possible experience, or to bring this critical investigation to perfection.",[]],"krv-b-0329":["We were able above, with the concepts of space and time, to make comprehensible with slight effort how these, as cognitions *a priori*, must nevertheless relate necessarily to objects and made possible a synthetic cognition of them independent of all experience. For since only by means of such pure forms of sensibility an object can appear to us, i.e., be an object of empirical intuition, space and time are pure intuitions, which contain *a priori* the condition of the possibility of the objects as appearances, and the synthesis in them has objective validity.\n\n===NOTES===",[]],"krv-b-0330":["The categories of the understanding, on the contrary, do not represent to us at all the conditions under which objects are given in intuition, hence objects can certainly appear to us without their necessarily having to be related to functions of the understanding, and without the understanding therefore containing the conditions of them *a priori*. Hence a difficulty shows itself here that we did not encounter in the field of sensibility, namely how subjective conditions of thinking should have objective validity, i.e., yield conditions of the possibility of all cognition of objects: for appearances can certainly be given in intuition without functions of the understanding. I take, e.g., the concept of cause, which signifies a particular way of synthesis, in that upon something A something entirely different B is posited according to a rule. It is not clear *a priori* why appearances should contain anything of this sort (for one cannot cite experiences as proof, since the objective validity of this concept must be able to be established *a priori*); and it is therefore doubtful *a priori* whether such a concept might not perhaps even be empty and encounter no object anywhere among the appearances. For that objects of sensible intuition must conform to the formal conditions of sensibility lying *a priori* in the mind is clear from the fact that otherwise they would not be objects for us; but that they must also, beyond this, conform to the conditions that the understanding requires for the synthetic unity of thinking, the inference to this is not so easy to see. For appearances could quite possibly, at any rate, be so constituted that the understanding would not find them conforming at all to the conditions of its unity, and everything would lie in such confusion that, e.g., in the series of appearances nothing would offer itself that would furnish a rule of synthesis and thus correspond to the concept of cause and effect, so that this concept would therefore be entirely empty, null and without meaning. Appearances would nonetheless offer objects to our intuition, for intuition in no way requires the functions of thinking.",["- Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Objecte (in other passages) is also \"object\". Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Bedeutung/bedeuten = \"meaning\"/\"signify\".","- \"Art der Synthesis\": Art rendered \"way\" (glossary also allows species, kind, manner).","- \"Schlußfolge\" rendered \"inference\" (cf. Schluß); \"allerdings\" = \"certainly\"; \"wohl allenfalls\" = \"quite possibly, at any rate.\"","- \"a priori\" left unattached in \"ist a priori nicht klar\" / \"a priori zweifelhaft,\" as in the German.","- OCR hyphenation breaks (\"be—deutet,\" \"dar—gethan,\" \"Sinnlich—keit\") silently repaired."]],"krv-b-0331":["If one thought to extricate oneself from the laboriousness of these investigations by saying: experience unceasingly offers examples of such a regularity of appearances, which give sufficient occasion to abstract the concept of cause from it and thereby at the same time to confirm the objective validity of such a concept, then one does not notice that in this way the concept of cause cannot arise at all, but that it must either be grounded entirely *a priori* in the understanding, or be given up entirely as a mere figment of the brain. For this concept requires throughout that something A be of such a kind that another thing B follow from it necessarily and according to a strictly universal rule. Appearances very well furnish cases from which a rule is possible according to which something customarily happens, but never that the result is necessary: hence a dignity also attaches to the synthesis of cause and effect that cannot be expressed empirically at all, namely that the effect does not merely come in addition to the cause, but is posited through it and follows from it. The strict universality of the rule is also no property at all of empirical rules, which through induction can obtain no other than comparative universality, i.e., extended usefulness. Now the use of the pure concepts of the understanding would change entirely, if one wanted to treat them only as empirical products.",["- Eigenschaft = \"property\" (not the glossary's Beschaffenheit, also \"property\"); Gebrauch = \"use\"; Brauchbarkeit = \"usefulness.\"","- Erfolg = \"result\"; Folge/folgen = \"follow\"; Hirngespinst = \"figment of the brain\"; gewöhnlichermaßen = \"customarily\"; Fälle = \"cases.\"","- \"comparative\" left as in the German (Latinate adjective, roman type in the original).","- Hyphenation breaks (\"Bei—spiele,\" \"ge—wöhnlicher maßen\") silently repaired."]],"krv-b-0332":["§ 14.",[]],"krv-b-0333":["Transition",["- Stray OCR sign after \"Übergang\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0334":["to the transcendental deduction of the categories.",["- Continues the heading of the preceding passage (\"Übergang\"); *Deduction* = Deduction."]],"krv-b-0335":["Only two cases are possible in which synthetic representation and its objects can come together, be related to one another necessarily, and as it were meet one another: either if the object alone makes the representation possible, or if the latter makes the object possible. If the former, then this relation is only empirical, and the representation is never possible *a priori*. And this is the case with appearance with regard to that in them which belongs to sensation. But if the second, since representation in itself (for of its causality by means of the will there is no question here) does not produce its object as to existence, the representation is nevertheless determining with regard to the object *a priori* if through it alone it is possible to cognize something as an object. But there are two conditions under which alone the cognition of an object is possible, first, intuition, through which it is given, but only as appearance; second, concept, through which an object is thought that corresponds to this intuition. But it is clear from the above that the first condition, namely that under which alone objects can be intuited, in fact lies at the basis of the objects, as to form, *a priori* in the mind. With this formal condition of sensibility all appearances therefore necessarily agree, because only through it can they appear, i.e., be empirically intuited and given. Now the question is whether concepts *a priori* do not also precede, as conditions under which alone something, even if not intuited, is nevertheless thought as an object in general; for then all empirical cognition of objects is necessarily in conformity with such concepts, because without their presupposition nothing is possible as object of experience. Now all experience, however, contains, besides the intuition of the senses, through which something is given, also a concept of an object that is given in intuition or appears: accordingly concepts of objects in general will lie at the basis of all experiential cognition as conditions *a priori*: consequently the objective validity of the categories, as concepts *a priori*, will rest on this, that through them alone experience (as to the form of thinking) is possible. For then they are related necessarily and *a priori* to objects of experience, because only by means of them can any object of experience be thought at all.",["- Gegenstand and Object both = \"object\"; here \"Objecten\" (in \"den Objecten der Form nach\") is Object.","- Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; Erfahrungserkenntniß = \"experiential cognition.\"","- \"a priori\" in \"Ist das erstere... niemals a priori möglich\" and \"a priori im Gemüth\" etc. left in German position; \"den Objecten der Form nach a priori im Gemüth\" is syntactically ambiguous (a priori with \"Form\" or with \"im Gemüth zum Grunde liege\").","- \"es scheint\" in \"er scheint\" read as OCR split of \"erscheint\"; \"Be dingungen\" repaired; stray signs (f, ı) omitted.","- \"überall\" in the next passage cluster not at issue here; \"gleichsam\" = \"as it were.\""]],"krv-b-0336":["The transcendental deduction of all concepts *a priori* therefore has a *principium* toward which the entire investigation must be directed, namely this: that they must be cognized as conditions *a priori* of the possibility of experience (whether of the intuition that is encountered in it, or of the thinking). Concepts that yield the objective ground of the possibility of experience are for just that reason necessary. But the development of the experience in which they are encountered is not their deduction (but illustration), since in that case they would be only contingent. Without this original relation to possible experience, in which all objects of cognition occur, their relation to any object could not be comprehended at all.",["- *Principium* is Latin in the original (roman type), hence italicized; Grund = \"ground\"; begreifen = \"comprehend\"; zufällig = \"contingent.\"","- \"es sei der Anschauung ... oder des Denkens\": the genitives are left as in the German.","- The text ends with an OCR residue (“) ) apparently marking a footnote; no footnote text was supplied, so nothing is translated.","- \"sie\" in \"worin sie angetroffen werden\" and \"würden sie nur zufällig sein\" left as pronouns, referring in the German to the concepts."]],"krv-b-0337":["The famous Locke, from lack of this consideration, and because he encountered pure concepts of the understanding in experience, also derived them from experience and yet proceeded so inconsequently that he ventured with them attempts at cognitions that go far beyond all boundary of experience. David Hume recognized that, in order to be able to do the latter, it was necessary that these concepts have to have their origin *a priori*. But since he could not explain at all how it is possible that the understanding must think concepts that are in themselves not combined in the understanding as nevertheless necessarily combined in the object, and did not hit upon the thought that perhaps the understanding, through these concepts themselves, could be the originator of the experience in which its objects are encountered: so he derived them, forced by necessity, from experience (namely from a subjective necessity arisen through frequent association in experience, which is finally falsely held to be objective, i.e., habit), but afterwards proceeded very consequently in that he declared it impossible to go beyond the boundary of experience with these concepts and the principles that they occasion. The empirical derivation, however, into which both fell, cannot be reconciled with the actuality of the scientific cognitions *a priori* that we have, namely of pure mathematics and general natural science, and is therefore refuted by the fact.",["- Erfahrungsgrenze = \"boundary of experience\" (Grenze = boundary); Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\"; wissenschaftlich = \"scientific\"; Naturwissenschaft = \"natural science\".","- \"Factum\" (roman type in the source) is rendered \"fact\"; the OCR line-break hyphen in \"Natur— wissenschaft\" and \"wider— legt\" is silently removed.","- \"erklären\" in \"sich erklären konnte\" = \"explain\"; in \"für unmöglich erklärte\" = \"declared\".","- \"inconsequent\" / \"consequent\" (inconsequent, consequent) are kept as cognates; \"Noth gedrungen\" = \"forced by necessity\".","- The relative \"worauf beide verfielen\" is kept literal (\"into which both fell\")."]],"krv-b-0338":["The first of these two famous men opened door and gate to enthusiasm, because reason, once it has entitlements on its side, can no longer be kept within limitations by indeterminate recommendations of moderation; the second gave himself over entirely to skepticism, since he believed he had discovered once and for all such a general deception of our faculty of cognition, held to be reason. — We are now about to make an attempt whether one cannot guide human reason happily through between these two reefs, assign it determinate boundaries, and yet keep the whole field of its purposive activity open for it.",["- Schwärmerei = \"enthusiasm\" (not in the glossary); Befugnisse = \"entitlements\"; Schranken = \"limitations\" (glossary), whereas Grenzen = \"boundaries\".","- Erkenntnißvermögen = \"faculty of cognition\"; zweckmäßig = \"purposive\" (Zweck = end).","- \"Scepticism\" is given as \"skepticism\" (American spelling); OCR \"menschlische\" silently corrected to \"menschliche\".","- \"so allgemeine für Vernunft gehaltene Täuschung\" is kept ambiguous: \"held to be reason\" may attach to \"Täuschung\" or to the general deception's object; the German does not decide.","- \"im Begriffe\" = \"about to\" (idiom; not Begriff = concept)."]],"krv-b-0339":["Beforehand I want only to send ahead the definition of the categories. They are concepts of an object in general, through which its intuition is regarded as determined with regard to one of the logical functions for judging. Thus the function of the categorical judgment was that of the relation of the subject to the predicate, e.g., all bodies are divisible. Yet with regard to the merely logical use of the understanding it remained undetermined which of the two concepts one wants to give the function of the subject, and which that of the predicate. For one can also say: Some divisible is a body. Through the category of substance, however, if I bring the concept of a body under it, it is determined: that its empirical intuition in experience must always be considered only as subject, never as mere predicate; and so in all the remaining categories.",["- OCR \"zu Nr theilen\" is corrected to \"zu urtheilen\" (\"for judging\"); \"kate gorischen\" to \"kategorischen\".","- Erklärung = \"definition\" (glossary alternatives: explanation, declaration).","- Betrachtung is not present; \"betrachtet werden müsse\" = \"must be considered\".","- \"Einiges Theilbare\" is kept literal (\"Some divisible\"), the neuter substantive not being supplied with a noun.","- \"Urtheil\" = \"judgment\"; \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\"."]],"krv-b-0340":["Of the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding. Second Section.",["- The initial \"Der\" (genitive: \"of the\") is probably a running-head fragment or the start of a title formula; the passage as given is fragmentary and I have translated it as it stands.","- Deduction = \"deduction\" (cognate); Verstandesbegriffe = \"concepts of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-0341":["Transcendental Deduction of the pure concepts of the understanding.",["- The stray \")\" in the OCR (a footnote or section marker remnant) is omitted.","- Transscendental = \"transcendental\" (modern spelling)."]],"krv-b-0342":["On the possibility of a combination in general.",["- Verbindung = \"combination\"; \"überhaupt\" = \"in general\"."]],"krv-b-0343":["The manifold of representations can be given in an intuition that is merely sensible, i.e., nothing but receptivity, and the form of this intuition can lie *a priori* in our faculty of representation, without being anything other than the way in which the subject is affected. Yet the combination (*conjunctio*) of a manifold in general can never come into us through the senses and can therefore also not be simultaneously contained in the pure form of sensible intuition; for it is an act of the spontaneity of the power of representation, and since one must call this, to distinguish it from sensibility, understanding, all combination, whether we become conscious of it or not, whether it be a combination of the manifold of intuition or of various concepts, and in the case of the former of sensible or non-sensible intuition, is an action of the understanding, which we would assign the general term synthesis, in order thereby to make noticeable at the same time that we can represent nothing as combined in the object without having combined it ourselves beforehand, and that among all representations combination is the only one that is not given through objects but can be performed only by the subject itself, because it is an act of its self-activity. One easily becomes aware here that this action must be originally unitary and equally valid for all combination, and that dissolution, analysis, which seems to be its opposite, nevertheless always presupposes it; for where the understanding has not combined anything beforehand, there it also cannot dissolve anything, since it could have been given to the power of representation as combined only through it.",["- Vorstellungsvermögen = \"faculty of representation\"; Vorstellungskraft = \"power of representation\" (Vermögen = faculty, Kraft = power).","- Actus = \"act\" (Latinate; Handlung = \"action\"); Selbstthätigkeit = \"self-activity\"; Benennung = \"term\"; Object (Latin-derived) and Gegenstand both = \"object\".","- \"und an der ersteren\" is rendered \"in the case of the former\", leaving the construction as loose as the German.","- \"ursprünglich einig\" = \"originally unitary\" (einig, not einfach \"simple\"); \"gleichgeltend\" = \"equally valid\" (gültig = valid).","- The final \"durch ihn\" and \"sie\" (\"it\") are left unresolved, as in the German; \"ihn\" can only refer to the understanding, \"sie\" to the action.","- \"Verstandeshandlung\" is rendered \"an action of the understanding\" in the English word order of the clause."]],"krv-b-0344":["But the concept of combination carries with it, besides the concept of the manifold and of the synthesis of it, also that of the unity of it. Combination is representation of the synthetic unity of the manifold.¹ The representation of this unity can therefore not arise from the combination, it rather makes the concept of combination possible in the first place by being added to the representation of the manifold. This unity, which precedes *a priori* all concepts of combination, is not perhaps that category of unity (§ 10); for all categories are grounded on logical functions in judgments, but in these combination, hence unity of given concepts, is already thought. The category therefore already presupposes combination. Thus we must seek this unity (as qualitative, § 12) still higher, namely in that which itself contains the ground of the unity of different concepts in judgments, hence of the possibility of the understanding, even in its logical use.",["- The OCR footnote marker (*) is shown as \"¹\"; the footnote text is in passage krv-b-0347. Stray \"y\" at the end and line-break hyphens (Ver: bindung, ges dacht) are silently corrected.","- Einheit = \"unity\"; \"derselben\" (of it) is kept as \"of it\", referring in the German to the manifold (Mannigfaltigen) and not otherwise specified.","- \"dadurch, daß sie ... hinzukommt\" = \"by being added to\", the construction literal (\"by its coming to\").","- \"die Kategorie der Einheit\" = \"category of unity\"; \"qualitative\" is Kant's \"qualitative\" (as opposed to numerical), unglossed.","- \"allererst\" = \"in the first place\"."]],"krv-b-0345":["§ 16.\nOn the original-synthetic unity of apperception.",["- ursprünglich-synthetisch = \"original-synthetic\" (hyphenated as in the German); Apperception = \"apperception\"."]],"krv-b-0346":["The: I think, must be able to accompany all my representations; for otherwise something would be represented in me that could not be thought at all, which is as much as to say: the representation would either be impossible, or at least be nothing for me. That representation that can be given prior to all thinking is called intuition. Thus everything manifold of intuition has a necessary relation to the: I think, in the same subject in which this manifold is encountered. But this representation is an act of spontaneity, i.e., it cannot be regarded as belonging to sensibility. I call it pure apperception, in order to distinguish it from the empirical, or also original apperception, because it is that self-consciousness which, in that it the representation: I think,",["- The passage breaks off mid-clause; the verb (\"hervorbringt\") stands at the start of the next passage (krv-b-0347) after a footnote. I have not completed it. OCR \"in dem es\" is read as \"indem es\".","- Mannigfaltige (noun) = \"manifold\"; \"alles Mannigfaltige der Anschauung\" is rendered \"everything manifold of intuition\".","- Actus = \"act\"; \"Spontas neität\" is corrected to \"Spontaneität\".","- \"Diese Vorstellung\" has no explicit referent beyond \"Ich denke\"; left as in the German. The stray \";\" at the end is OCR and omitted."]],"krv-b-0347":["¹ Whether the representations themselves are identical, and thus one could be thought analytically through the other, does not come into consideration here. The consciousness of the one is, so far as the manifold is spoken of, nevertheless always to be distinguished from the consciousness of the other, and it is the synthesis of this (possible) consciousness alone that matters here.\n\nproduces, which must be able to accompany all others and in all consciousness is one and the same, cannot be accompanied by any further. I also call the unity of the same the transcendental unity of self-consciousness, in order to designate the possibility of cognition *a priori* from it. For the manifold representations that are given in a certain intuition would not all together be my representations if they did not all together belong to one self-consciousness, i.e., as my representations (even if I am not conscious of them as such) they must nevertheless necessarily be in accord with the condition under which alone they can stand together in a general self-consciousness, because otherwise they would not throughout belong to me. Much can be concluded from this original combination.",["- The footnote belongs to the marker in B 131 (krv-b-0344) and is placed first, as in the OCR order; the stray \"a\" before \"hervorbringt\" is omitted. \"Hervorbringt\" continues krv-b-0346.","- The relative \"die\" (which must be able to accompany all others) is ambiguous between \"Vorstellung\" and \"Selbstbewußtsein\" in the construction; \"which\" is left as ambiguous.","- \"derselben\" = \"of the same\" (feminine; not resolved); \"Erkenntniß\" = \"cognition\"; Bewußtsein = \"consciousness\"; Selbstbewußtsein = \"self-consciousness\".","- \"folgern\" = \"conclude\"; \"durchgängig\" = \"throughout\"; \"gemäß sein\" = \"be in accord with\"; \"Betrachtung\" = \"consideration\".","- \"alle andere\" is rendered \"all others\" (the noun, Vorstellungen, is not supplied)."]],"krv-b-0348":["Namely, this thoroughgoing identity of the apperception of a manifold given in intuition contains a synthesis of representations and is possible only through the consciousness of this synthesis. For the empirical consciousness, which accompanies different representations, is in itself dispersed and without relation to the identity of the subject. This relation therefore does not yet come about by my accompanying each representation with consciousness, but rather by my adding one to the other and being conscious of the synthesis of them. Therefore only by my being able to combine a manifold of given representations in one consciousness is it possible for me to represent the identity of the consciousness in these representations themselves, i.e., the analytic unity of apperception is possible only under the presupposition of some synthetic one.*) The thought: these representations given in in-",["- Vorstellung = representation; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Anschauung = intuition; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Verbindung/verbinden = combination/combine; Einheit = unity; möglich = possible.","- \"Afo\" (OCR) corrected to \"Also\"; rendered \"Therefore.\" \"Synthesis\" kept as in the German.","- \"möglich.“)\" corrected to a footnote asterisk marker (*); the footnote text is in the next passage. The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"in der An-\"), continued in krv-b-0349.","- \"eine\" in \"einer synthetischen\" refers back to \"Einheit\"; kept as \"one.\""]],"krv-b-0349":["*) The analytic unity of consciousness attaches to all common concepts as such; e.g., if I think red in general, I thereby represent to myself a constitution that (as a mark) can be encountered in something or can be combined with other representations; therefore only by virtue of a possible synthetic unity thought in advance can I represent the analytic one to myself. A representation that is to be thought as common to different ones is regarded as belonging to those that, besides it, still have something different in themselves; consequently it must be thought in synthetic unity with other (if only possible) representations beforehand, before I can think in it the analytic unity of consciousness, which makes it a *conceptus communis*. And thus the synthetic unity of apperception is the highest point to which one must attach all use of the understanding, even the whole of logic, and after it transcendental philosophy, indeed this faculty is the understanding itself. tuition belong to me altogether, means accordingly as much as: I unite them in one self-consciousness, or can at least unite them therein; and although it is not yet itself the consciousness of the synthesis of the representations, it nevertheless presupposes the possibility of the latter, i.e., only by my being able to comprehend the manifold of them in one consciousness do I call them altogether my representations; for otherwise I would have as multicolored, diverse a self as I have representations of which I am conscious. Synthetic unity of the manifold of intuitions, as given *a priori*, is therefore the ground of the identity of apperception itself, which precedes *a priori* all my determinate thinking. Combination, however, does not lie in the objects and cannot be borrowed from them, say through perception, and thereby first taken up into the understanding, but is solely an operation of the understanding, which itself is nothing further than the faculty of combining *a priori* and of bringing the manifold of given representations under unity of apperception, which principle is the supreme one in all of human cognition.",["- The OCR has the text of the following sentence (\"schauung gegebene Vorstellungen…\") run into the footnote; I have kept it in the order printed, so \"in-\" and \"tuition\" join across the footnote.","- OCR \"verschieden en gemein\" read as \"verschiedenen gemein\"; \"fegt\" read as \"setzt\"; \"a proiri\" read as \"a priori\"; \"be. wußt\" read as \"bewußt.\" The footnote marker \")\" is rendered \"*)\".","- \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Verrichtung\" = operation; \"begreifen\" = comprehend; \"Grundsatz\" = principle; \"Princip\" = principle (both rendered \"principle\"); \"Merkmal\" = mark; \"Beschaffenheit\" = constitution.","- \"Verstandesgebrauch\" = use of the understanding; \"Transscendental-Philosophie\" rendered \"transcendental philosophy\" (Weltweisheit not present). \"ihn/er\" (\"he/it\") refers grammatically to \"Der Gedanke\"; kept as \"it.\"","- \"vielfärbiges\" = multicolored; \"Selbst\" = self."]],"krv-b-0350":["This principle of the necessary unity of apperception is now indeed itself identical, hence an analytic proposition, yet it nevertheless declares a synthesis of the manifold given in an intuition to be necessary, without which that thoroughgoing identity of self-consciousness cannot be thought. For through the I, as simple representation, nothing manifold is given; in the intuition, which is distinct from it, the manifold can only be given and, through combination, thought in one consciousness. An understanding in which, through self-consciousness, all the manifold would at the same time be given would intuit; ours can only think and must seek the intuition in the senses. I am therefore conscious of the identical self with regard to the manifold of the representations given to me in an intuition, because I call them all together my representations, which make up one. But that is as much as to say that I am conscious of a necessary synthesis of them *a priori*, which is called the original synthetic unity of apperception, under which all representations given to me stand, but under which they must also be brought through a synthesis. The principle of the synthetic unity of apperception",["- OCR \"Bez wußtsein\" read as \"Bewußtsein\"; \"Bez\" is a stray character. \"Grundsatz\" = principle (Satz = proposition). \"unterschieden\" rendered \"distinct from\" (not deutlich).","- \"die eine ausmachen\": \"die\" could refer to \"Vorstellungen\" and \"eine\" to a single (consciousness/self); left as literal \"which make up one.\" The German is elliptical.","- \"anschauen\" = intuit; \"Ansehung\" = regard. Passage breaks off; continued in krv-b-0351."]],"krv-b-0351":["is the supreme principle of all use of the understanding.",["- \"Princip\" and \"Grundsatz\" both rendered \"principle\"; \"oberste\" = supreme."]],"krv-b-0352":["The supreme principle of the possibility of all intuition in relation to sensibility was, according to the transcendental aesthetic: that everything manifold of it stands under the formal conditions of space and time. The supreme principle of the very same in relation to the understanding is: that everything manifold of intuition stands under conditions of the original-synthetic unity of apperception.*) Under the former stand all manifold representations of intuition, insofar as they are given to us, under the second, insofar as they must be able to be combined in one consciousness; for without that nothing can be thought or cognized through them, because the given representations would not have in common the act of apperception, I think, and thereby would not be grasped together in one self-consciousness.",["- \"Ästhetik\" rendered \"aesthetic\"; \"Actus\" kept as \"act.\" \"erkannt\" = cognized; \"gedacht\" = thought. \"zusammengefaßt\" = grasped together.","- \"derselben\" in the first sentence (feminine, singular) is ambiguous between \"Anschauung\" and \"Sinnlichkeit\"; kept as \"of it.\" \"eben derselben\" likewise rendered \"of the very same.\"","- \"gemein haben\" = have in common. \"Bedingungen\" = conditions. The footnote marker is \"*)\"."]],"krv-b-0353":["The understanding is, to speak generally, the faculty of cognitions. These consist in the determinate relation of given representations to an object. But object is that in whose concept the manifold of a given intuition is united. Now, however, all union of representations requires unity of consciousness in the synthesis of them. Consequently the unity of consciousness is that which alone constitutes the relation of representations to an object, hence their objective validity, consequently that they become cognitions, and on which consequently even the possibility of the understanding rests.",["- OCR \"Voritellungen\" read as \"Vorstellungen\"; \"Man— nigfaltige\" as \"Mannigfaltige.\"","- \"Object\" (first use) and \"Gegenstand\" both rendered \"object.\" \"Vereinigung/vereinigt\" = union/united (distinct from Verbindung = combination). \"Erkenntnisse\" = cognitions.","- \"ausmacht\" = constitutes; \"beruht\" = rests."]],"krv-b-0354":["*) Space and time and all parts of them are intuitions, hence singular representations with the manifold that they contain in themselves (see the transc. aesthetic), hence not mere concepts, through which the very same consciousness is found as contained in many representations, but rather many representations as contained in one and in its consciousness, hence as composite, consequently the unity of consciousness as synthetic, but yet as original. This singularity of them is important in the application (see § 25).",["- OCR \"$ 25\" read as \"§ 25\"; \"mit hin\" as \"mithin.\" \"Einzelheit\" = singularity; \"einzelne\" = singular. \"zusammengesetzt\" = composite.","- \"deren Bewußtsein\" (rendered \"its consciousness\") is ambiguous as to antecedent (\"einer\" or \"Vorstellung\"); kept as literal \"its.\"","- \"angetroffen wird\" = \"is found\" governs the whole series of clauses; the sentence's construction is kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-0355":["The first pure cognition of the understanding, then, on which the whole of its remaining use is grounded, which is also at the same time entirely independent of all conditions of sensible intuition, is now the principle of the original synthetic unity of apperception. Thus the mere form of outer sensible intuition, space, is not yet any cognition at all; it gives only the manifold of intuition *a priori* for a possible cognition. But in order to cognize anything in space, e.g., a line, I must draw it and thus synthetically bring about a determinate combination of the given manifold, so that the unity of this action is at the same time the unity of consciousness",["- OCR \"nr— sprünglichen\" read as \"ursprünglichen.\" \"Verstandeserkenntniß\" = cognition of the understanding. \"Handlung\" = action. \"zu Stande bringen\" = bring about.","- \"Gebrauch\" = use. Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in krv-b-0356."]],"krv-b-0356":["(in the concept of a line), and thereby for the first time an object (a determinate space) is cognized. The synthetic unity of consciousness is therefore an objective condition of all cognition, not one that I myself merely need in order to cognize an object, but one under which every intuition must stand in order to become object for me, because in another way and without this synthesis the manifold would not unite in one consciousness. |",["- The initial \"en\" is an OCR fragment (the end of \"Bewußtseins\" from the preceding passage's last line; the text of krv-b-0355 ends \"des Bewußtseins\"); left untranslated. The \"i\" before \"und\" is an OCR stray, omitted. The sign | is carried over as in the source.","- \"Art\" = way; \"auf andere Art\" = \"in another way.\" \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" both \"object.\"","- \"nicht deren ich bloß selbst bedarf\": \"deren\" refers to \"Bedingung\"; the construction is kept literal."]],"krv-b-0357":["This latter proposition is, as said, itself analytic, although it makes synthetic unity the condition of all thinking; for it says nothing further than that all my representations in any given intuition must stand under the condition under which I alone can count them as my representations to the identical self and thus, as synthetically combined in one apperception, can grasp them together through the general expression: I think.",["- OCR \"1;\" before \"Anschauung\" is a stray, omitted. \"Satz\" = proposition; \"Ausdruck\" = expression. \"zusammenfassen\" = grasp together.","- \"rechnen zu\" rendered \"count … to\"; \"als in einer Apperception synthetisch verbunden\" kept in the German order."]],"krv-b-0358":["But this principle is nevertheless not a principle for every understanding possible in general, but only for that through whose pure apperception in the representation: I am, nothing manifold is yet given. That understanding through whose self-consciousness the manifold of intuition would at the same time be given, an understanding through whose representation the objects of this representation at the same time existed, would not need a special *actus* of the synthesis of the manifold to the unity of consciousness, of which the human understanding, which merely thinks, does not intuit, has need. But for the human understanding it is nevertheless unavoidably the first principle, so that it cannot even make the slightest concept of another possible understanding, either of one that itself intuited, or, if it did possess a sensible intuition, yet of another kind than that lying at the basis in space and time.",["- Grundsatz and Princip are both rendered \"principle\"; Anschauung = intuition; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Object = object.","- \"deren ... bedarf\": the relative \"of which\" is left with the same ambiguity of antecedent as \"deren\" (the *actus* or the synthesis).","- \"Art\" = kind. OCR stray \"3\" and \"anfchauete\" (anschauete) silently corrected."]],"krv-b-0359":["§ 18.\nWhat objective unity of self-consciousness is.",["- Selbstbewußtsein = self-consciousness."]],"krv-b-0360":["The transcendental unity of apperception is that through which everything manifold given in an intuition is united into a concept of the object. It is called objective for that reason and must be distinguished from the subjective unity of consciousness, which is a determination of inner sense, through which that manifold of intuition is given empirically for such a combination. Whether I can be empirically conscious of the manifold as simultaneous or one after another",["- Verbindung = combination; Bestimmung = determination; Begriff = concept.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0361":["empirically conscious, depends on circumstances or empirical conditions; hence the empirical unity of consciousness through association of representations itself concerns an appearance and is entirely contingent. By contrast the pure form of intuition in time, merely as",["- Bedingungen = conditions; Erscheinung = appearance; zufällig = contingent.","- Begins and ends mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0362":["intuition in general, which contains a given manifold, stands under the original unity of consciousness solely through the necessary relation of the manifold of the intuition to the one: I think, thus through the pure synthesis of the understanding, which lies a priori at the basis of the empirical one. That unity alone is objectively valid; the empirical unity of apperception, which we do not consider here, and which is also derived only from the former under given conditions in concreto, has only subjective validity. One combines the representation of a certain word with one thing, another with another thing; and the unity of consciousness in that which is empirical is, with regard to that which is given, not necessarily and universally valid. The logical form of all judgments consists in the objective unity of the apperception of the concepts contained therein.",["- gültig / Gültigkeit / geltend = valid / validity / valid. \"a priori\" is placed as in the German and may attach to \"lies at the basis\" or to \"synthesis\".","- \"der empirischen\" is rendered \"the empirical one\", leaving the German's elliptical antecedent. Stray OCR letters (s, o) and the garbled \"ab» geleitet\" corrected."]],"krv-b-0363":["I have never been able to be satisfied by the definition that the logicians give of a judgment in general: it is, as they say, the representation of a relation between two concepts. Without now quarreling with them here over the defect of the definition, that it fits at most only categorical but not hypothetical and disjunctive judgments (as | the latter contain not a relation of concepts but of judgments themselves) (although from this oversight of logic many troublesome sequels have grown)*), I note only that in what this relation consists is not determined here.",["- Erklärung = definition; Folgen = \"sequels\" (glossary: Folge = sequel); Verhältniß = relation; Vorstellung = representation.","- The footnote marker *) is kept in the text; the footnote follows in krv-b-0365.","- The sign | is carried over where it stands."]],"krv-b-0364":["But if I investigate more precisely the relation of given cognitions in every judgment and distinguish it, as belonging to the understanding, from the relation according to laws of the reproductive imagination (which has only subjective validity), then I find that a judgment is nothing other than the way to bring given cognitions to the objective unity of apperception. That is what the little relational word “is” in them aims at, in order to distinguish the objective unity of given representations from the subjective. For this designates the relation of the same to the original apperception and the necessary unity of the same, even if the judgment itself is empirical, hence contingent, e.g., Bodies are heavy. By this I indeed do not want to say that these representations belong necessarily to one another in the empirical intuition, but that they belong to one another by virtue of the necessary unity of apperception in the synthesis of intuitions, i.e., according to principles of the objective determination of all representations, insofar as cognition can come of them, which principles are all derived from the principle of the transcendental unity of apperception. Through this alone does a judgment come from this relation, i.e., a relation that is objectively valid and is sufficiently distinguished from the relation of the very same representations in which there would be merely subjective validity, e.g., according to laws of association. According to the latter I could only say: if I carry a body, I feel a pressure of weight; but not: it, the body, is heavy; which is as much as to say: these two representations are combined in the object, i.e., without distinction of the state of the subject, and not merely together in perception (however often it may be repeated).",["- Beziehung and Verhältniß both appear as \"relation\". Erkenntnis = cognition; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Zustand = state; Wahrnehmung = perception.","- \"Verhältnißwört— eben\" is read as Verhältnißwörtchen; \"ist\" is in quotation marks as a mentioned word. \"Damit ich zwar nicht sagen will\" is read as \"thereby I indeed do not want to say\"; the construction is left as in German.","- OCR stray letters and numbers and \"d.\" (d. i.) silently corrected. The passage is headed \"B 140–142?\" in the source."]],"krv-b-0365":["*) The extensive doctrine of the four syllogistic figures concerns only categorical syllogisms; and although it is nothing further than an art of acquiring by stealth, by hiding immediate inferences (*consequentiae immediatae*) among the premises of a pure syllogism, the illusion of more kinds of inference than that in the first figure, it would nevertheless have made no particular fortune through that alone, if it had not succeeded in bringing categorical judgments, as those to which all others must let themselves be referred, into exclusive esteem, which however according to § 9 is false.",["- This is the footnote to krv-b-0363; its opening \")\" is rendered as \"*)\". Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Schluß = inference; Schein = illusion.","- \"den Schein mehrerer Schlußarten, als des in der ersten Figur\" is kept in its German order, with \"to acquire by stealth\" for erschleichen."]],"krv-b-0366":["§ 20.",[]],"krv-b-0367":["All sensible intuitions stand under the categories as conditions under which alone the manifold of the same can come together in one consciousness.",["- The sentence is split across the page break in the source (\"der— | selben\"), and the passage's stray numeral \"5\" belongs to the next passage's line numbering."]],"krv-b-0368":["The manifold given in a sensible intuition belongs necessarily under the original synthetic unity of apperception, because through this the unity of the intuition is alone possible (§ 17). But that action of the understanding through which the manifold of given representations (whether they be intuitions or concepts) is brought under one apperception in general is the logical function of judgments (§ 19). Thus everything manifold, insofar as it is given in One | empirical intuition, is determined with regard to one of the logical functions for judging, through which it namely is brought to one consciousness in general. Now the categories, however, are nothing other than precisely these functions for judging, insofar as the manifold of a given intuition is determined with regard to them (§ 10). Thus the manifold in a given intuition also stands necessarily under categories.",["- Handlung = action; Function = function; bestimmt = determined; \"in Ansehung\" = \"with regard to\". \"Einer\" is rendered \"One\" as capitalized in the source.","- The sign | is carried over. \"ihrer\" in \"in Ansehung ihrer\" is rendered \"them\", referring to the functions; the German pronoun's referent could also be read otherwise."]],"krv-b-0369":["Remark.",["- Anmerkung = remark."]],"krv-b-0370":["A manifold that is contained in an intuition that I call my own is represented, through the synthesis of the understanding, as belonging to the necessary unity of self-consciousness, and this happens through the category.*) This therefore indicates that the empirical consciousness of a given manifold of One intuition stands under a pure self-consciousness a priori, just as empirical intuition stands under a pure sensible one, which likewise takes place a priori. — In the above proposition the beginning of a deduction of the pure concepts of the understanding is therefore made, in which I, since the categories arise independently of sensibility merely in the understanding, still from",["- Satz = proposition; rein = pure; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility. The footnote marker *) is kept; OCR “*)” corrected.","- \"Statt hat\" = \"takes place\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"noch von\")."]],"krv-b-0371":[") The ground of proof rests on the represented unity of the intuition, through which an object is given, which always includes in itself a synthesis of the manifold given for an intuition and already contains the relation of the latter to unity of apperception. of the way in which the manifold is given for an empirical intuition, must abstract, in order to look only to the unity that is added to the intuition by means of the category through the understanding. In the sequel (§ 26) it will be shown, from the way in which the empirical intuition is given in sensibility, that the unity of the same is none other than that which the category, according to the preceding § 20, prescribes to the manifold of a given intuition in general, and thereby, therefore, in that its validity a priori in regard to all objects of our senses is explained, the aim of the deduction will first be fully attained.",["- The passage opens with a stray \")\" (footnote marker) and breaks into a fragment (\"der Art, wie das Mannigfaltige ... abstrahiren muß\"); the text appears cut off or garbled at that point, and I have not supplied a subject or the missing beginning.","- \"welche\" (after \"Anschauung, dadurch ein Gegenstand gegeben wird\") could refer to \"Einheit\" or to \"Anschauung\"; I left it ambiguous. \"dieses letzteren\" is likewise left unresolved.","- Beweisgrund = ground of proof; Gültigkeit = validity; Absicht = aim; Gegenstand = object; Folge (\"In der Folge\") = sequel.","- OCR \"Sinneerklärt\" read as \"Sinne erklärt\"."]],"krv-b-0372":["But from one piece I could still not abstract in the above proof, namely from the fact that the manifold for the intuition must be given even prior to the synthesis of the understanding and independently of it; how, however, remains undetermined here. For if I wanted to think of an understanding that itself intuited (as, say, a divine one, which would not represent given objects, but through whose representation the objects themselves would at once be given or produced), then the categories would have no significance at all in regard to such a cognition. They are only rules for an understanding whose whole faculty consists in thinking, i.e., in the action of bringing the synthesis of the manifold, which has been given to it otherwise in intuition, to the unity of apperception, which therefore cognizes nothing at all by itself, but only combines and orders the material for cognition, the intuition, which must be given to it through the object. Of the peculiarity of our understanding, however, of bringing about unity of apperception a priori only by means of the categories and only by precisely this kind and number of them, just as little further can a ground be given as for why we have precisely these and no other functions for judgments, or why time and space are the sole forms of our possible intuition. The category has no other use for the cognition of things than its application to objects of",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"Gegenstände der\"); continued in the next passage.","- OCR slips corrected: \"Des deutung\" = Bedeutung; \"x worden\" = gegeben worden; stray letters \"z\", \"2\" dropped.","- Bedeutung = significance; Vermögen = faculty; Stoff = material; Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Art und Zahl = kind and number; Stück = piece.","- \"anderweitig\" rendered \"otherwise\"; \"der also für sich gar nichts erkennt\" keeps the German's loose reference to \"Verstand\".","- \"von der Eigenthümlichkeit ... läßt sich ... ein Grund angeben\" is kept as an awkward construction."]],"krv-b-0373":["experience. |",["- Continues the previous passage (\"Gegenstände der / Erfahrung\"). Stray OCR \"l\" dropped; \"|\" kept."]],"krv-b-0374":["To think an object and to cognize an object | are thus not the same. For two pieces belong to cognition: first, the concept, through which an object in general is thought (the category), and second, the intuition, through which it is given; for if an intuition corresponding to the concept could not be given at all, it would be a thought as far as its form is concerned, but without any object, and through it no cognition of any thing would be possible, because, as far as I knew, there would be nothing, nor could there be anything, to which my thought could be applied. Now all intuition possible for us is sensible (Aesthetic), thus the thinking of an object in general through a pure concept of the understanding can become cognition in our case only insofar as this is related to objects of the senses. Sensible intuition is either pure intuition (space and time) or empirical intuition of that which is represented immediately as actual, through sensation, in space and time. Through determination of the former we can acquire cognitions a priori of objects (in mathematics), but only as to their form as appearances; whether there can be things that must be intuited in this form still remains undecided thereby. Consequently all mathematical concepts are not by themselves cognitions, except insofar as one presupposes that there are things that can be presented to us only in accordance with the form of that pure sensible intuition. Things in space and time are given, however, only insofar as they are perceptions (representations accompanied with sensation), hence through empirical representation. Consequently the pure concepts of the understanding, even when they are applied to intuitions a priori (as in mathematics), provide cognition only insofar as these, and hence also the concepts of the understanding by means of them, can be applied to empirical intuitions. Consequently the categories also do not deliver to us any cognition of things by means of intuition, except only through their possible application to empirical intuition, i.e., they serve only for the possibility of empirical cognition. This, however, is called experience. Consequently the categories have no other use for the cognition of things than only insofar as these are assumed as objects of possible experience.",["- OCR corrections: \"Ginnliche\" = Sinnliche; \"Erfenntniß\" = Erkenntniß; \"dar als\" = unmittelbar als.","- Gegenstand = object throughout; Wirklich = actual; Wahrnehmung = perception; Vorstellung = representation; Bestimmung = determination; Erscheinung = appearance; Ästhetik = Aesthetic.","- \"dieser\" in \"so fern dieser auf Gegenstände der Sinne bezogen wird\" is grammatically masculine (the concept, \"Begriff\") but \"Denken\" is the nearer noun; the pronoun is left unresolved.","- \"so fern\" = insofar as; \"nicht einerlei\" = not the same."]],"krv-b-0375":["The above proposition is of the greatest importance; for it determines just as much the boundaries of the use of the pure concepts of the understanding in regard to objects, as the transcendental Aesthetic determined the boundaries of the use of the pure form of our sensible intuition. Space and time are valid, as conditions of the possibility of how objects can be given to us, no further than for objects of the senses, hence only of experience. Beyond these boundaries they represent nothing at all; for they are only in the senses and have outside them no actuality. The pure concepts of the understanding are free of this restriction and extend to objects of intuition in general, it may be similar to ours or not, if only it is sensible and not intellectual. This further extension of concepts, beyond our sensible intuition, helps us to nothing, however. For they are then empty concepts of objects, of which, whether they are even possible or not, we cannot judge at all through those, mere forms of thought without objective reality, because we have no intuition at hand to which the synthetic unity of apperception, which those alone contain, could be applied, and they thus could determine an object. Our sensible and empirical intuition alone can procure for them sense and significance. |",["- Wirklichkeit = actuality (distinct from Realität, \"objective Realität\" = objective reality); Einschränkung = restriction (Schranke would be \"limitation\"); Grenze = boundary; Ausdehnung here = extension.","- \"sie mag der unsrigen ähnlich sein oder nicht\" kept with \"it\" (Anschauung); \"ob sie nur einmal möglich sind\" rendered \"whether they are even possible\".","- \"jene\" (\"through those\") and \"welche ... jene allein enthalten\" refer back to the concepts; left as pronouns.","- Sinn und Bedeutung = sense and significance; Object/Gegenstand both \"object\" (here \"Objecten\" is the Latinate form)."]],"krv-b-0376":["If one therefore assumes an object of a non-sensible intuition as given, one can indeed represent it through all those predicates that already lie in the presupposition that nothing belonging to sensible intuition pertains to it: thus that it is not extended or in space, that its duration is not time, that no alteration (sequence of determinations in time) is encountered in it, and so on. But it is still no proper cognition if I merely indicate how the intuition of the object is not, without being able to say what is then contained in it; for then I have not represented the possibility of an object for my pure concept of the understanding at all, because I could not give any intuition that corresponded to it, but could only say that ours does not hold for it. But the foremost thing here is that not even a single category could be applied to such a something; e.g., the concept of a substance, i.e., of something that can exist as subject, but never as mere predicate, of which I do not know at all whether there could be any thing that would correspond to this determination of thought, if empirical intuition did not give me the case of application. But more on this in the sequel.",["- OCR corrections: \"kom me\" = komme; \"eriftiren\" = existiren; \"u. f. w.\" = u. s. w.; \"Erfenntniß\" not present here but \"eigentliches Erkenntniß\" = proper cognition.","- Folge der Bestimmungen = sequence of determinations; \"in der Folge\" = in the sequel.","- \"zu meinem reinen Verstandesbegriff\" rendered \"for my pure concept of the understanding\"; \"zu\" could also be read \"to\".","- \"ob es irgend ein Ding geben könne\" follows an anacoluthic \"wovon\"; the sentence is left as it stands. Dasein not used here; \"existiren\" = exist."]],"krv-b-0377":["§ 24.",["- Section number only."]],"krv-b-0378":["Of the application of the categories to objects of the",["- Heading breaks off at \"der\"; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0379":["senses in general.",["- Continues the heading: \"Von der Anwendung der Kategorien auf Gegenstände der Sinne überhaupt.\""]],"krv-b-0380":["The pure concepts of the understanding are related through the mere understanding to objects of intuition in general, undetermined whether it be ours or any other, but sensible, yet are just for that reason mere forms of thought, through which no determinate object is yet cognized. The synthesis or combination of the manifold in them related merely to the unity of apperception and was thereby the ground of the possibility of cognition *a priori*, insofar as it rests on the understanding, and hence not only transcendental but also merely pure intellectual. But because a certain form of sensible intuition lies *a priori* at the basis in us, which rests on the receptivity of the capacity for representation (sensibility), the understanding, as spontaneity, can determine inner sense through the manifold of given representations in accordance with the synthetic unity of apperception, and so think synthetic unity of the apperception of the manifold of sensible intuition *a priori*, as the condition under which all objects of our (the human) intuition must necessarily stand, whereby then the categories as mere forms of thought acquire objective reality, i.e., application to objects that can be given to us in intuition, but only as appearances; for only of these are we capable of intuition *a priori*. This synthesis of the manifold of sensible intuition, which is possible and necessary *a priori*, can be called figurative (*synthesis speciosa*), in distinction from that which would be thought in the mere category with regard to the manifold of an intuition in general and is called combination of the understanding (*synthesis intellectualis*); both are transcendental, not merely because they themselves proceed *a priori* but also because they ground the possibility of other cognition *a priori*. | But the figurative synthesis, if it goes merely to the originally synthetic unity of apperception, i.e., this transcendental unity, which is thought in the categories, must, in distinction from the merely intellectual combination, be called the transcendental synthesis of the imagination. Imagination is the faculty of representing an object even without its presence in intuition. Since all our intuition is sensible, the imagination, on account of the subjective condition under which alone it can give a corresponding intuition to the concepts of the understanding, belongs to sensibility; but insofar as its synthesis is an exercise of spontaneity, which is determining and not, like sense, merely determinable, and hence can determine sense *a priori* in accordance with its form in conformity with the unity of apperception, the imagination is to that extent a faculty of determining sensibility *a priori*, and its synthesis of intuitions, in accordance with the categories, must be the transcendental synthesis of the imagination, which is an effect of the understanding on sensibility and the first application of the same (at the same time the ground of all the rest) to objects of the intuition possible for us. As figurative it is distinguished from the intellectual synthesis without any imagination, merely through the understanding. Insofar as the imagination is spontaneity, I also sometimes call it the productive imagination, and thereby distinguish it from the reproductive, whose synthesis is subject solely to empirical laws, namely those of association, and which therefore contributes nothing to the explanation of the possibility of cognition *a priori* and for that reason belongs not in transcendental philosophy but in psychology.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; erkannt = cognized; Gegenstand and Object both = \"object\" (here only Gegenstand).","- Vorstellungsfähigkeit = capacity for representation (Fähigkeit = capacity); Vermögen = faculty.","- Bestimmung/bestimmen = determination/determine; bestimmbar = determinable; unbestimmt = undetermined; bestimmt (adj.) = determinate.","- Ausübung = exercise; Wirkung = effect; Anwendung = application; Erklärung = explanation; Ansehung = regard (in Ansehung = with regard to).","- \"welche\" (spontaneity/exercise) and \"welches\" (synthesis/imagination) and \"desselben\" (understanding/effect) left as ambiguous as in the German.","- \"figürlich\" = figurative; \"intellectual\" retained as in the German; \"vorgehen\" = proceed. \"Diele\" in the OCR read as \"Diese\"; the \"|\" is carried over at its place."]],"krv-b-0381":["Here now is the place to make comprehensible the paradoxical thing that must have struck everyone in the exposition of the form of inner sense (§ 6): namely how this presents even ourselves to consciousness only as we appear to ourselves, not as we are in ourselves, because, that is, we intuit ourselves only as we are inwardly affected, which seems to be contradictory, since we would have to comport ourselves toward ourselves as passive; hence one also prefers in the systems of psychology to pass off inner sense as one and the same with the faculty of apperception (which we carefully distinguish).",["- OCR \"Gre position\" corrected to \"Exposition\" and \"ausfallen\" to \"auffallen\" (struck); Exposition rendered \"exposition\" (cf. Erörterung, *expositio*).","- Erscheinen = appear; an sich = in itself; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Vermögen = faculty.","- \"dieser\" (the subject of \"darstelle\") refers to inner sense in the German as in the English \"this\"; \"einerlei\" = one and the same."]],"krv-b-0382":["That which determines inner sense is the understanding and its original faculty to combine the manifold of intuition, i.e., to bring it under an apperception (on which its very possibility rests). Because now the understanding in us humans is itself no faculty of intuitions and, even if these were given in sensibility, still cannot take them up into itself in order, as it were, to combine the manifold of its own intuition, its synthesis, if it is considered by itself alone, is nothing other than the",["- OCR \"des ruht\" read as \"beruht\" (rests).","- \"diese ... gegeben wäre\": the plural pronoun with singular verb is kept as printed (\"these ... were given\").","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; it continues in the next."]],"krv-b-0383":["unity of the action, of which it is conscious as such even without sensibil-",["- Word split across passages (Sinnlich— / keit); the fragment is left as broken. \"er\" (Verstand) rendered \"it.\""]],"krv-b-0384":["ity is conscious, but through which it itself sensibility internally with re-",["- Fragment: the German word order is kept (\"bewußt ist\" belongs to the preceding clause); the clause is not completed. Innerlich = internally."]],"krv-b-0385":["spect to the manifold that, according to the form of its intuition, to it",["- Fragment; the verb comes in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0386":["may be given, is capable of determining. It therefore exercises under the",["- \"Er\" = the understanding, rendered \"It.\""]],"krv-b-0387":["term of a transcendental synthesis of the imagina-",["- Benennung = term (the glossary's rendering); word split Einbildungs— / kraft."]],"krv-b-0388":["tion that action on the passive subject, of which faculty it",["- \"dessen Vermögen er\" kept in the German order; Handlung = action; Subject = subject."]],"krv-b-0389":["is, out, of which we rightly say that inner sense is thereby affected",["- \"aus\" is the separable prefix of \"übt ... aus\" (0386–0388), left as a bare particle. Afficirt = affected."]],"krv-b-0390":["be. Apperception and its synthetic unity is so little the same as inner sense that the former rather, as the source of all combination, goes to the manifold of intuitions in general,",["- Verbindung = combination; \"einerlei\" = the same; \"jene\" = \"the former.\""]],"krv-b-0391":["under the name of the categories before all sensible intuition to objects in general; inner sense, on the other hand, [contains] the mere form of intuition, but without combination of the manifold in it, hence",["- \"enthält\" comes in the next passage; the bracketed word there is not in this one, so it is not added here: read the sentence as broken off. Objecte = objects (Latinate form; Gegenstand elsewhere also \"object\")."]],"krv-b-0392":["as yet no determinate intuition at all, which is possible only through the",["- \"hin\" completes \"mit-\" of the preceding passage (\"mit-hin\" = hence). \"enthält\" (contains) stands in this passage and belongs to the preceding clause: \"...hence contains as yet no determinate intuition at all.\""]],"krv-b-0393":["consciousness of the determination of the same through the transcendental action of the imagination (synthetic influence of the understanding on the",["- Einfluß = influence; Handlung = action."]],"krv-b-0394":["inner sense), which I have called the figurative synthesis, is possible. | We also perceive this at all times in ourselves. We cannot think a line without drawing it in thought, cannot think a circle without describing it, cannot represent the three dimensions of space at all without setting three lines perpendicular to one another from the same point, and cannot even [represent] time without, in drawing a straight line (which is to be the externally figurative representation of time), attending merely to the action of the synthesis of the manifold, whereby we successively determine inner sense, and thereby to the succession of this determination in it. Motion as action of the subject (not as determination of an object)¹), consequently the syn-",["- The bracketed \"[represent]\" is not in the German, which has an elliptical \"nicht\"; it is supplied only as the English requires, and the German is \"und selbst die Zeit nicht, ohne indem wir ... Acht haben\" (the syntax is irregular, \"ohne indem\" kept as printed).","- OCR \"fucceffiv\" read as \"successiv\", \"Succesion\" as \"Succession.\" Wahrnehmen = perceive; Abmessungen = dimensions; Acht haben = attend.","- The footnote marker (¹) is carried over; the footnote text is not in the passage. The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0395":["Motion of an object in space does not belong in a pure science, consequently also not in geometry, because that something is movable cannot be cognized a priori, but only through experience. But motion as description of a space is a pure *actus* of the successive synthesis of the manifold in space, if we abstract from this and attend merely to the action through which we determine inner sense in accordance with its form, even first produces the concept of succession. The understanding therefore does not find in this some such combination of the manifold already, but produces it, in that it affects it. But how the I, that I think, is distinguished from the I that intuits itself (in that I can still represent another kind of intuition at least as possible) and yet is one and the same as the same subject with this latter, how I can therefore say: I, as intelligence and thinking subject, cognize myself as a thought object, insofar as I am still given to myself beyond that in intuition, only, like other phenomena, not as I am before the understanding, but as I appear to myself, has neither more nor less difficulty in it than how I can in general be an object for myself, and indeed of intuition and of inner perceptions. That it must nevertheless really be so, can, if one allows space to count as a mere pure form of the appearances of outer sense, be clearly demonstrated thereby, that we cannot make time, which is after all no object of outer intuition at all, representable to ourselves otherwise than under the image of a line, insofar as we draw it, without which way of presentation we could not cognize the unity of its measure at all, likewise that we must always take the determination of the length of time, or also of the positions in time, for all inner perceptions from that which outer things present to us as alterable, consequently must order the determinations of inner sense in precisely the same way as appearances in time as we order those of the outer senses in space; hence, if we concede of the latter that we thereby cognize objects only insofar as we are affected externally, we must also grant of inner sense that we thereby intuit ourselves only as we are internally affected by ourselves, i.e., as far as inner intuition is concerned, cognize our own subject only as appearance, but not according to what it is in itself. in outer intuition in general through productive imagination, and belongs not only to geometry but even to transcendental philosophy.",["- The OCR interleaves the footnote with the main text of the page; I translate in the order given. The duplicated \"thesis des Mannigfaltigen\" is an OCR repetition and is not translated twice. Stray quotation mark and parenthesis signs were dropped.","- Object = *Object*; Gegenstand = \"object\" (both rendered \"object\"; here only *Object*, *Gegenstand* once).","- Actus: Latin, kept in italics. Succession = *Succession*, \"succession\". Phänomenen = \"phenomena\" (Erscheinung = \"appearance\").","- \"Ich, der ich denke\" is kept literally as \"the I, that I think\".","- \"so fern ich mir noch über das in der Anschauung gegeben bin\" is kept as the German stands; its construction is obscure and the English is left equally so.","- \"wie ich vor dem Verstande bin\" = \"as I am before the understanding\" (vor could also be \"for\"; left literal).","- \"Bild\" = \"image\"; \"Darstellungsart\" = \"way of presentation\"; \"vorstellig machen\" = \"make representable\".","- \"indem er ihn afficirt\": \"it\" and \"it\" (ihn) are left as in German; the antecedent of ihn is unclear in the German."]],"krv-b-0396":["I do not see how one can find so much difficulty in the inner sense being affected by ourselves. Every *actus* of attention can give us an example of it. The understanding always determines therein the inner",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (den inneren [Sinn]); not completed. Leading footnote parenthesis omitted. It is the beginning of a footnote.","- Actus: Latin, kept in italics. Aufmerksamkeit = \"attention\"."]],"krv-b-0397":["825.",["- Only the numeral \"825.\" is present; it is probably an OCR remnant (page or line number) and carries no translatable text."]],"krv-b-0398":["On the other hand I am conscious of myself in the transcendental synthesis of the manifold of representations in general, hence in the synthetic original unity of apperception, not as I appear to myself, nor as I am in myself, but only that I am. This representation is a thinking, not an intuiting. Now since for the cognition of ourselves, besides the action of thinking, which brings the manifold of every possible intuition to the unity of apperception, there is required a determinate kind of intuition, through which this manifold is given, so my own existence is indeed not appearance (much less mere illusion), but the determination of my existence*) can occur only in accordance with the form of inner sense, according to the particular way in which the manifold that I combine is given in inner intuition; and I accordingly have no cognition of myself as I am, but merely as I appear to myself. The consciousness of oneself is therefore still far from being a cognition of oneself, notwithstanding all the categories, which constitute the thinking of an object in general through combination of the manifold in one apperception. Just as for the cognition of an object different from me, besides the thinking of an object in general (in the category), I still need an intuition through which I determine that general concept, so I also need, for the cognition of myself, besides the consciousness or besides that I think myself, still a sense of the combination that it thinks, in accordance with the inner intuition, which corresponds to the manifold in the synthesis of the understanding. How much the mind is commonly affected hereby, everyone will be able to perceive in himself.",["- The last two sentences are textually disturbed in the OCR (the footnote text and the main text are interleaved): \"noch einer Sinn der Verbindung, die er denkt, gemäß zur inneren Anschauung\" is rendered as it stands; the continuation of the main text (\"Anschauung des Mannigfaltigen in mir…\") appears in the next passage.","- The final sentence belongs to the footnote text (*) interleaved here; \"er\" has no clear antecedent.","- Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Bewußtsein = \"consciousness\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Gemüth = \"mind\".","- \"ursprünglichen\" = \"original\"; Apperception kept as \"apperception\". \"id)\" read as \"ich\".","- Object = *Object* \"object\"."]],"krv-b-0399":["The: I think, expresses the *actus* of determining my existence. Existence is thereby already given, but the way in which I am to determine it, i.e., posit the manifold belonging to it in me, is not yet given thereby. For that there belongs self-intuition, which has at its ground a form given a priori, i.e., time, which is sensible and belongs to the receptivity of the determinable. If now I do not have yet another self-intuition, which gives the determining in me, of whose spontaneity alone I am conscious, just as much before the *actus* of determining as time gives the determinable, then I cannot determine my existence as that of a self-active being; rather I only represent to myself the spontaneity of my thinking, i.e., of determining, and my existence remains always only sensibly, i.e., as the existence of an appearance, determinable. Yet this spontaneity makes it that I call myself an intelligence. Intuition of the manifold in me, whereby I determine this thought; and I exist as an intelligence that is merely conscious of its faculty of combination, but in regard to the manifold that it is to combine is subjected to a limiting condition, which it calls inner sense, to make that combination intuitive only according to relations of time, which lie entirely outside the proper concepts of the understanding, and therefore can cognize itself only as it appears to itself merely with respect to an intuition (which cannot be intellectual and given through the understanding itself), not as it would cognize itself if its intuition were intellectual.",["- Footnote text with the main text interleaved by the OCR: \"Doch macht diese Spontaneität, daß ich mich Intelligenz nenne. Anschauung des Mannigfaltigen in mir…\" is rendered as given; the sentence does not run on grammatically.","- \"das mannigfaltige zu demselben Gehörige in mir setzen solle\": \"posit\" = setzen; \"solle\" = \"am to\".","- \"Da— sein\" and \"Berbindungsvermögens\" corrected (Dasein, Verbindungsvermögen).","- \"Absicht\" in \"in Absicht auf\" rendered \"with respect to\"; \"in Ansehung\" rendered \"in regard to\"; both idiomatic.","- \"bleibt immer nur sinnlich … bestimmbar\": the adverb sinnlich is left to attach as in the German (\"sensibly\").","- Actus: Latin in italics. Wesen = \"being\"; Intelligenz = \"intelligence\"; anschaulich = \"intuitive\"; Verstandesbegriffe = \"concepts of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-0400":["§ 26.",[]],"krv-b-0401":["Transcendental deduction of the universally possible",["- Heading continued in the next passage; Deduction = *Deduction*, \"deduction\"."]],"krv-b-0402":["use in experience of the pure concepts of the understanding.",["- Erfahrungsgebrauch = \"use in experience\" (Erfahrung = \"experience\", Gebrauch = \"use\"); continues the heading begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0403":["In the metaphysical deduction the origin of the categories a priori in general was demonstrated through their complete coincidence with the general logical functions of thinking, in the transcendental, however, the possibility of the same as cognitions a priori of objects of an intuition in general (§ 20, 21) was presented. Now the possibility is to be explained of cognizing a priori, through categories, the objects that may ever come before our senses, and indeed not according to the form of their intuition but according to the laws of their combination, thus of as it were prescribing the law to nature and even making it possible. For without this their fitness it would not be clear how everything that may only come before our senses must stand under the laws that arise a priori from the understanding alone.",["- Gegenstände = \"objects\" (cf. Object, also \"object\").","- Zusammentreffung = \"coincidence\"; Tauglichkeit = \"fitness\"; Functionen = \"functions\".","- \"nicht der Form ihrer Anschauung, sondern den Gesetzen ihrer Verbindung nach\": \"according to\" governs both members as in the German; \"nach a priori\" kept in place.","- Erkenntnisse = \"cognitions\"; Möglichkeit = \"possibility\"; erklärt = \"explained\" (Erklärung = \"explanation\")."]],"krv-b-0404":["First I remark that by the synthesis of apprehension I understand the composition of the manifold in an empirical intuition, through which perception, i.e., empirical consciousness of it (as appearance), becomes possible.",["- Zusammensetzung = \"composition\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; \"derselben\" = \"of it\", referring back as in German (grammatically ambiguous between the intuition and the manifold).","- Apprehension kept as \"apprehension\" (*Apprehension*)."]],"krv-b-0405":["We have forms of outer as well as inner sensible intuition a priori in the representations of space and time, and the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold of appearance must at all times be in accordance with these, because it can itself occur only in accordance with this form. But space and time are represented a priori not merely as forms of sensible intuition, but as intuitions themselves (which contain a manifold), thus with the determination of the unity of this manifold in them (see Transc. Aesthet.).∗) Thus even unity of the synthesis of the manifold outside or in us, hence also a combination to which everything that is to be represented as determinate in space or time must be in accordance, is already given a priori, as condition of the synthesis of all apprehension, along with (not in) these intuitions at the same time. But this synthetic unity can be none other than that of the combination of the manifold of a given intuition in general in an original consciousness, in accordance with the categories, only applied to our sensible intuition. Consequently all synthesis, through which even perception becomes possible, stands under the categories; and since experience is cognition through connected perceptions, the categories are conditions of the possibility of experience and are therefore valid a priori also of all objects of experience.",["- OCR corrected: \"Afo\" = Also; \"sehe\" = siehe; \"transsc. Asthet.\" = transsc. Ästhet.; the stray quotation mark before \")\" read as the footnote sign, rendered ∗).","- Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Verbindung = combination; Bestimmung = determination.","- \"Also ist selbst schon Einheit …\": \"selbst\" could modify \"Einheit\" or go with \"schon\"; kept in the German position (\"even unity\")."]],"krv-b-0406":["If, therefore, e.g., I make the empirical intuition of a house into perception through apprehension of the manifold of it, the necessary unity of space and of outer sensible intuition in general lies at the basis for me, and I draw, as it were, its shape in accordance with this synthetic unity of the manifold in space. But this very same synthetic unity, if I abstract from the form of space, has its seat in the understanding and is the category of the synthesis of the homogeneous in an intuition in general, i.e. the",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands. The sentence is completed in the OCR text of the following footnote passage (\"Kategorie der Größe …\").","- Wahrnehmung = perception; Verstand = understanding; Gleichartige = homogeneous."]],"krv-b-0407":["∗) Space, represented as object (as one actually requires in geometry), contains more than the mere form of intuition, namely the comprehension of the manifold given in accordance with the form of sensibility into an intuitive representation, so that the form of intuition gives merely manifold, but the formal intuition gives unity of the representation. This unity I had counted in the Aesthetic merely as belonging to sensibility, only in order to remark that it precedes all concepts, although it presupposes a synthesis that does not belong to the senses, through which, however, all concepts of space and time first become possible. For since through it (in that the understanding determines the sensibility) space or time are first given as intuitions, the unity of this intuition belongs a priori to space and time and not to the concept of the understanding (§ 24). category of magnitude, with which that synthesis of apprehension, i.e. perception, must thoroughly be in accordance.∗)",["- OCR corrected: \"Geo. metrie\" = Geometrie; \"auges hört\" = angehört; \"$ 24\" = § 24. The opening \")\" is the footnote sign (∗)).","- The final clause (\"Kategorie der Größe, welcher …\") continues the sentence broken off in the preceding passage and is left in the position where the text gives it, with the closing footnote sign.","- Anschauung/anschaulich = intuition/intuitive; Größe = magnitude; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Begriff = concept."]],"krv-b-0408":["If (in another example) I perceive the freezing of water, I apprehend two states (of fluidity and solidity) as ones that stand in a relation of time to one another. But in the time that I lay at the basis of the appearance as inner intuition, I necessarily represent synthetic unity of the manifold, without which that relation could not be given as determinate (with regard to temporal sequence) in an intuition. Now, however, this synthetic unity, as condition a priori under which I combine the manifold of an intuition in general, if I abstract from the constant form of my inner intuition, time, is the category of cause, through which, if I apply it to my sensibility, I determine everything that happens, in time in general, in accordance with its relation. Thus apprehension in such an occurrence, hence this occurrence itself, as regards possible perception, stands under the concept of the relation of effects and causes, and so in all other cases.",["- OCR corrected: \"Mfo\" = Also; stray colon before \"Begebenheit\" dropped.","- \"Relation\" and \"Verhältnis\" are both rendered \"relation\".","- Begebenheit = occurrence; Zustand = state; Wirkung = effect; Zeitfolge = temporal sequence; beständig = constant.","- \"in der Zeit überhaupt seiner Relation nach\": \"in time in general\" may be taken with \"geschieht\" or with \"bestimme\"; left open."]],"krv-b-0409":["Categories are concepts that prescribe laws a priori to appearances, hence to nature as the sum total of all appearances (*natura materialiter spectata*); and now the question arises, since they are not derived from nature and do not direct themselves in accordance with it as their model (because otherwise they would be merely empirical), how it is to be comprehended that nature must direct itself in accordance with them, i.e. how they can determine a priori the combination of the manifold of nature without taking it from nature. Here is the solution of this riddle.",["- OCR corrected: \"daß!\" = daß; \"frägt\" = fragt.","- Inbegriff = sum total; begreifen = comprehend; Auflösung = solution; Räthsel = riddle.","- \"ohne sie von dieser abzunehmen\": \"sie\" can refer to the combination or to the categories; left as in German (\"it\")."]],"krv-b-0410":["∗) In this way it is proved that the synthesis of apprehension, which is empirical, must necessarily be in accordance with the synthesis of apperception, which is intellectual and contained entirely a priori in the category. It is one and the same spontaneity that there, under the name of imagination, here, of understanding, brings combination into the manifold of intuition.",["- Footnote text. Einbildungskraft = imagination; Verstand = understanding; Verbindung = combination.","- \"dort … hier\": \"there\" refers to apprehension, \"here\" to apperception (as in the German order)."]],"krv-b-0411":["It is no more strange how the laws of appearances in nature must agree with the understanding and its form a priori, i.e. its faculty of combining the manifold in general, than how the appearances themselves must agree with the form of sensible intuition a priori. For laws exist just as little in the appearances, but only relative to the subject in which the appearances inhere, insofar as it has understanding, as appearances do not exist in themselves, but only relative to the same being, insofar as it has senses. To things in themselves their lawfulness would necessarily belong even apart from an understanding that cognizes them. But appearances are only representations of things that, according to what they may be in themselves, are uncognized",["- OCR corrected: stray \"$\" before \"der Natur\" read as \"in der Natur\" (the text has \"in $ der Natur\"); \"cm—\" etc. not here.","- The passage breaks off after \"unerkannt\"; the verb \"sind\" opens the next passage.","- Wesen = being; Ding an sich = thing in itself; Gesetzmäßigkeit = lawfulness; erkennen = cognize (hence \"uncognized\")."]],"krv-b-0412":["are. But as mere representations they stand under no law of connection at all, except that which the connecting faculty prescribes. Now that which connects the manifold of sensible intuition is imagination, which depends on the understanding as to the unity of its intellectual synthesis and on sensibility as to the manifoldness of apprehension. Now since all possible perception depends on the synthesis of apprehension, but this itself, this empirical synthesis, depends on the transcendental one, hence on the categories, all possible perceptions, hence also everything that can ever attain to empirical consciousness, i.e. all appearances of nature, must, as to their combination, stand under the categories, on which nature (considered merely as nature in general), as the original ground of its necessary lawfulness (as *natura formaliter spectata*), depends. But for more laws than those on which a nature in general, as lawfulness of appearances in space and time, rests, even the pure faculty of understanding does not suffice to prescribe laws a priori to appearances through mere categories. Particular laws, because they concern empirically determined appearances, cannot be completely derived from it, although they all together stand under those. Experience must be added in order to become acquainted with the latter in general; but of experience in general and of that which can be cognized as an object of it, those laws a priori alone give the teaching.",["- OCR corrected: \"cm— pirischen\" = empirischen.","- Continues the preceding passage; \"sind\" completes \"unerkannt sind\".","- \"Verknüpfung\" = connection, \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Belehrung\" = teaching; \"kennen lernen\" = become acquainted with; Gegenstand = object.","- \"durch bloße Kategorien\" can attach to \"vorschreiben\" or to \"reicht … zu\"; left in German position.","- \"davon\" (\"from it\") and \"jenen\" (\"those\") are left unresolved, as is \"die letztere\" (\"the latter\"), which could refer to the particular laws or to the appearances."]],"krv-b-0413":["§ 27.",[]],"krv-b-0414":["Result of this Deduction of the Concepts of the Understanding.",["- Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding."]],"krv-b-0415":["We can think no object except through categories; we can cognize no object that is thought except through intuitions that correspond to those concepts. Now all our intuitions are sensible, and this cognition, insofar as its object is given, is empirical. But empirical cognition is experience. Consequently no cognition a priori is possible for us, except solely of objects of possible experience.∗)",["- The closing quotation mark plus \")\" is read as a footnote sign (∗)); the footnote text is not part of this passage.","- Gegenstand = object; Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-b-0416":["But this cognition, which is restricted merely to objects of experience, is not on that account all borrowed from experience, but rather, as regards both the pure intuitions and the pure concepts of the understanding, they are elements of cognition that are found in us *a priori*. Now there are only two ways in which a necessary agreement of experience with the concepts of its objects can be thought: either experience makes these concepts, or these concepts make experience possible. The former does not take place with regard to the categories (nor to pure sensible intuition); for they are concepts *a priori*, hence independent of experience (the assertion of an empirical origin would be a kind of *generatio aequivoca*). Consequently only the second remains (as it were a system of the epigenesis of pure reason): namely, that the categories, on the side of the understanding, contain the grounds of the possibility of all experience in general. But how they make experience possible, and which principles of the possibility of it they furnish in their application to appearances, the following chapter on the transcendental use of the power of judgment will teach more fully.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstände = objects; Anschauung = intuition; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding.","- OCR \"ein- f geschränkt\" read as \"eingeschränkt\"; \"Gegen: ständen\", \"Be— griffe\" repaired; \"moglich\" read \"möglich\"; \"transsc.\" expanded as in the original abbreviation (kept as \"transcendental\").","- The parenthesis after \"Kategorien\" (\"(auch nicht der reinen sinnlichen Anschauung statt;\") is left unclosed in the German; the English keeps the same open construction.","- \"das mehrere lehren\" rendered \"will teach more fully\" (literally \"the more\"); \"Ansehung\" = \"regard\"; \"Möglichkeit\" = possibility.","- \"a priori\" kept in italics as Latin; \"dieselben/sie\" in the last sentence (\"derselben\") left as \"it\", referring as in German to Erfahrung (or possibly the Möglichkeit): ambiguity not resolved."]],"krv-b-0417":["*) So that one not stumble prematurely over the worrisome disadvantageous consequences of this proposition, I will only recall that the categories in thinking are not restricted by the conditions of our sensible intuition, but have an unbounded field, and only the cognizing of that which we think to ourselves, the determining of the object, requires intuition; where, in the absence of the latter, the thought of the object can still always have its true and useful consequences for the use of reason by the subject, which, however, because it is not always directed to the determination of the object, hence to cognition, but also to that of the subject and its willing, cannot yet be set forth here.",["- Foootnote marker \"*)\" retained. Folgen = consequences; Gebrauch = use; Bestimmung = determination; Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkennen = cognizing; Objecte/Object = object (Gegenstand/Object both \"object\" in the passage).","- \"Anschauung bedürfe\" is subjunctive after \"nur ... das Erkennen ... das Bestimmen\"; \"requires\" kept. \"der sich ... nicht vortragen läßt\": \"der\" could refer to the Vernunftgebrauch or to the Gebrauch generally; \"which\" preserves this.","- \"Erkenntniß. sondern\" in OCR read as \"Erkenntniß, sondern\".","- Correction: the first note's opening word is misspelled (\"Foootnote\"); it should read \"Footnote\"."]],"krv-b-0418":["If someone wanted to propose, between the two single ways named, still a middle way, namely that they would be neither first principles *a priori* of our cognition thought by ourselves, nor yet drawn from experience, but subjective predispositions to thinking, implanted in us together with our existence, which were so arranged by our author that their use would agree exactly with the laws of nature along which experience runs (a kind of preformation system of pure reason), then (besides the fact that with such a",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; not completed. Erkenntniß = cognition; Principien = principles; Existenz = existence; Gesetze = laws; Gebrauch = use.","- \"Anlagen\" = predispositions; \"Urheber\" = author; \"Präformationssystem\" = preformation system.","- \"Einge— richtet\" repaired to \"eingerichtet\"."]],"krv-b-0419":["hypothesis no end is to be seen as to how far one might carry the presupposition of predetermined predispositions to future judgments) the decisive thing against the",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"das wider ge—\" continues in B 168: \"gedachten Mittelweg\"); \"ge—\" joined with the next passage's \"dachten\" is not done here. Hypothese = hypothesis; Anlagen = predispositions; Urtheile = judgments.","- \"wider ge—\" rendered \"against the\", as the continuation is in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0420":["thought middle way would be this: that in such a case the categories would lack the necessity that belongs essentially to their concept. For, e.g., the concept of cause, which says the necessity of my sequence under a presupposed condition, would be false if it rested only on an arbitrary subjective",["- \"ge— / dachten\" joined: \"gedachten\" = \"thought\" (i.e. the middle way thought of). Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Begriff = concept; Bedingung = condition.","- \"Erfolg\" = sequence (in the glossary \"Folge\" is sequel/sequence; here Erfolg is rendered \"sequence\" as well; keep distinct in note: Erfolg ≠ Folge). \"Hfalsch\" read \"falsch\".","- \"beliebigen\" = arbitrary; \"eingepflanzten\" = implanted; \"vorausgesetzten\" = presupposed."]],"krv-b-0421":["necessity to combine certain empirical representations according to such a rule of relation. I would not be able to say:",["- Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Vorstellungen = representations; verbinden = combine (Verbindung = combination).","- The sentence continues in the next passage after the colon."]],"krv-b-0422":["the effect is combined with the cause in the object (i.e., necessarily), but I am only so arranged that I cannot think this representation otherwise",["- Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; Object = object; Vorstellung = representation; verbunden = combined.","- \"eingerichtet\" = arranged. Passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0423":["than as so connected; which is just what the skeptic most wishes; for then all our insight, through supposed objective validity of our judgments, is nothing but sheer illusion, and there would also be no lack of people who would not concede this subjective necessity (which must be felt) in themselves; at the least one could quarrel with no one about that which rests merely on the way his subject is organized.",["- verknüpft = connected (Verknüpfung = connection); Verbindung = combination appears in the previous passage. Gültigkeit = validity; Schein = illusion; Einsicht = insight; Art = way.","- \"Subject\" kept as \"subject\"; \"Sceptiker\" = skeptic (American spelling).","- \"durch vermeinte objective Gültigkeit\" left as in German; attachment to \"Einsicht\" or to \"Schein\" not resolved."]],"krv-b-0424":["Brief Concept of This Deduction.",["- Begriff = concept (rendered literally; \"Kurzer\" = brief)."]],"krv-b-0425":["It is the presentation of the pure concepts of the understanding (and with them of all theoretical cognition *a priori*) as principles of the possibility of experience, but of this as determination of appearances in space and time in general, — finally of this from the principle of the original synthetic unity of apperception, as the form of the understanding in relation to space and time as original forms of sensibility. Only up to here do I hold the division into paragraphs to be necessary, because we had to do with the elementary concepts. Now that we want to make their use representable, the exposition will be allowed to proceed in continuous interconnection without it.\")",["- Darstellung = presentation; Principien = principles; Möglichkeit = possibility; Bestimmung = determination; Erscheinungen = appearances; Raum/Zeit = space/time; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Einheit = unity; Zusammenhang = interconnection.","- \"j\" is an OCR stray before \"Nur\"; omitted. The closing \")\" and quotation mark come from the original/OCR (\"dürfen.“)\"); retained as given.","- \"vorstellig machen\" rendered \"make representable\" (literal; idiomatically \"bring forward\"); \"Vortrag\" = exposition.","- \"dieser\" (twice) refers to Erfahrung (feminine); kept as \"this\"."]],"krv-b-0426":["Of the\nTranscendental Analytic\nSecond Book.\nThe Analytic of Principles.",["- Grundsätze = principles (Principien in the previous passage is also \"principles\"; Grundsatz and Princip are not distinguished in English)."]],"krv-b-0427":["General logic is built upon a plan that coincides quite exactly with the division of the higher faculties of cognition. These are: understanding, power of judgment, and reason. That | doctrine therefore treats, in its analytic, of concepts, judgments, and inferences, precisely in accordance with the functions and the order of those powers of the mind that one comprehends under the broad term of understanding in general.",["- Erkenntnißvermögen = faculties of cognition; Gemüthskräfte = powers of the mind (Gemüt = mind, Kraft = power); Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Schlüsse = inferences; Benennung = term; begreift = comprehends.","- \"Doctrin\" rendered \"doctrine\"; the sign | is carried over as it stands. \"zusammentrifft\" (OCR \"zufammentrifft\") corrected."]],"krv-b-0428":["Since the said merely formal logic abstracts from all content of cognition (whether it be pure or empirical) and occupies itself merely with the form of thinking (of discursive cognition) in general: it can therefore also include in its analytic part the canon for reason, whose form has its secure precept, which, without taking into consideration the particular nature of the cognition used therein, can be gained insight into *a priori*, through mere dissection of the actions of reason into their moments. i |",["- Inhalt = content; Form = form; Erkenntniß = cognition; besondere = particular; Vernunft = reason; \"Kanon\" = canon.","- \"i |\" at the end: \"i\" is an OCR stray; the sign | is retained. The \"Teile\" for \"Teile\" is spelled \"Theile\" in the original (modernized by OCR).","- \"eingesehen werden kann\" rendered \"can be gained insight into\" (literal); \"Zergliederung\" = dissection; \"Vernunfthandlungen\" = actions of reason.","- \"a priori\" ambiguously attaches to \"Vorschrift\", \"eingesehen\" or \"Zergliederung\"; kept in the German position.","- \"so kann sie ... mit befassen\": \"mit\" rendered \"also include\"."]],"krv-b-0429":["Transcendental logic, since it is restricted to a determinate content, namely that of the pure cognitions *a priori* only, cannot do the same in this division. For it shows: that the transcendental use of reason is not at all objectively valid, hence does not belong to the logic of truth, i.e., the analytic, but, as a logic of illusion, requires a particular part of the scholastic system under the name of the transcendental dialectic.",["- \"trans» scendentale\" repaired. bestimmten = determinate (bestimmt); Inhalt = content; Gültigkeit/gültig = validity/valid; Schein = illusion; Gebrauch = use; \"Lehrgebäudes\" = system (literally \"doctrinal edifice\"); \"besondern\" = particular.","- \"kann es ihr in dieser Eintheilung nicht nachthun\": \"es\" (neuter) left as \"the same\" — \"es ... nachthun\" = do likewise; \"Eintheilung\" = division.","- \"bloß der reinen Erkenntnisse\" can attach to \"Inhalt\" or to \"eingeschränkt\"; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-0430":["Understanding and power of judgment accordingly have their canon of the objectively valid, hence true, use in transcendental logic, and therefore belong in its analytic part. But reason, in its attempts to settle something about objects *a priori* and to extend cognition beyond the boundaries of possible experience, is entirely and wholly dialectical, and its illusory assertions do not at all fit into a canon, such as the analytic is supposed to contain.",["- Gegenstände = objects; Grenzen = boundaries; Scheinbehauptungen = illusory assertions (Schein = illusion; Behauptung = assertion); \"ausmachen\" = settle; \"erweitern\" = extend.","- \"a priori\" can attach to \"über Gegenstände\" or to \"ausmachen\"; left ambiguous as in German.","- \"Theile\" = part; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition."]],"krv-b-0431":["The analytic of principles will accordingly be merely a canon for the power of judgment, which teaches it to apply to appearances the concepts of the understanding that contain the condition for rules *a priori*. For this reason, in taking the actual principles of the understanding as my theme, I will make use of the term of a doctrine of the power of judgment, by which this business is designated more precisely",["- Grundsätze = principles; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; Bedingung = condition; Erscheinungen = appearances; Benennung = term; Doctrin = doctrine; Urtheilskraft = power of judgment.","- \"Geschäfte\" = business; \"bezeichnet\" = designated. The passage ends without a period in the OCR and is left so.","- \"a priori\" can attach to \"Regeln\" or to \"enthalten\"; left ambiguous.","- \"Grund— sätze\" repaired; \"anzu— wenden\" repaired."]],"krv-b-0432":["Introduction.",[]],"krv-b-0433":["On the Transcendental Power of Judgment in General.",["- Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; überhaupt = in general."]],"krv-b-0434":["If the understanding in general is explained as the faculty of rules, then the power of judgment is the faculty of subsuming under rules, i.e., of distinguishing whether something stands under a given rule (*casus datae legis*) or not. General logic contains no precepts at all for the power of judgment, nor can it contain them. For since it abstracts from all content of cognition, nothing remains for it except the business of analytically separating the mere form of cognition into concepts, judgments and inferences, and thereby bringing about formal rules for all use of the understanding. If it now wanted to show generally how one ought to subsume under these rules, i.e., distinguish whether something stands under them or not, this could not happen otherwise than again through a rule. But this, just because it is a rule, requires anew an instruction of the power of judgment; and thus it turns out that although the understanding is capable of teaching and equipment through rules, the power of judgment is a special talent, which does not want to be taught but only practiced. Hence this is also the specific feature of so-called mother wit, the lack of which no school can replace; for although the latter can offer to a limited understanding rules in abundance, borrowed from another's insight, and as it were graft them on, the faculty of using them correctly must belong to the pupil himself, and no rule that one might prescribe to him with this aim is secure against misuse in the absence of such a natural gift.*) A physician, therefore, a judge or a statesman can have many fine pathological, juridical or political rules in his head, to the degree that he himself can become a thorough teacher of them, and will nevertheless easily stumble in the application of them",["- Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Vermögen = faculty; Belehrung = teaching, Unterweisung = instruction; Ausrüstung = equipment; Vorschriften = precepts.","- The OCR \"denn!)\" and stray \"g\" before \"als\" are treated as slips: \"übrig als\" restored; the footnote marker is placed after \"sicher\" where the marker \"“)\" stands.","- Staatskundiger = statesman; Lehrling = pupil; Mutterwitz = mother wit.","- The sentence breaks off at \"leicht\"; the next passage continues with \"verstoßen\"."]],"krv-b-0435":["(stumble) either because natural power of judgment (though not understanding) is lacking in him, and he can indeed have insight into the universal *in abstracto*, but cannot distinguish whether a case *in concreto* belongs under it, or also because he has not been sufficiently trained to this judgment through examples and actual business. This is also the sole and great utility of examples: that they sharpen the power of judgment. For | as regards the correctness and precision of insight of the understanding, they rather commonly do some injury to it, because they only rarely | fulfill the condition of the rule adequately (as *casus in terminis*) and, moreover, often weaken that exertion of the understanding to have insight into rules in generality and independently of the particular circumstances of experience, according to their sufficiency, and therefore accustom one in the end to use them more like formulas than as principles. Thus examples are the go-cart of the power of judgment, which he who lacks the natural talent for it can never do without.",["- The passage begins with the verb \"verstoßen\" (OCR \"ea verstoßen\"), which completes the previous sentence; I give it as \"(stumble)\" in parentheses only to mark that it is the continuation.","- einige = \"sole\" (einzige; OCR \"einige 1d\"); Grundsätze = principles; Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency; Gängelwagen = go-cart.","- The stray \"2\" at the end is an OCR footnote marker and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0436":["*) The lack of power of judgment is properly what one calls stupidity, and for such a defect there is no remedy at all. A blunt or limited head, which lacks nothing except the appropriate degree of understanding and its own concepts, can very well be equipped through learning, even up to erudition. But since it then usually tends to be lacking also in that (the *secunda Petri*), it is nothing unusual to encounter very learned men who in the use of their science frequently let that never-to-be-mended lack show.",["- \"jener\" (feminine) is rendered \"that\"; its referent is left as in the German. Wissenschaft = science; Gebrauch = use."]],"krv-b-0437":["But although general logic can give no precepts to the power of judgment, it is nevertheless quite otherwise with the transcendental, so much so that it seems that the latter has as its proper business to correct and secure the power of judgment in the use of the pure understanding through determinate rules. For in order to procure for the understanding an expansion in the field of pure cognitions *a priori*, hence as doctrine, philosophy seems not to be necessary at all, or rather to be ill applied, because after all attempts hitherto little or no land has been gained with it, but as",["- Erweiterung = expansion (not in the glossary); Doctrin = doctrine. The passage breaks off at \"sondern als\".","- \"transscendenta|len\": the word is broken across a line in the OCR; \"the transcendental\" has an implied noun (Logik), left unsupplied."]],"krv-b-0438":["critique, in order to guard against the false steps of the power of judgment (*lapsus judicii*) in the use",["- Kritik = critique. Fragment, continuing from the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0439":["of the few pure concepts of the understanding that we have, to this end",["- Fragment; \"dazu\" is kept after the infinitive clause as in the German."]],"krv-b-0440":["(although the utility is then only negative) philosophy is called upon with its",["- Fragment. Philosophie = philosophy; aufgeboten = called upon."]],"krv-b-0441":["whole acuteness and art of examination. Now transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity:",["- The OCR numeral \"5\" is a line number and is omitted. \"Es hat aber\" is rendered \"But ... has\", with \"Es\" left unrendered as an expletive.","- Scharfsinnigkeit = acuteness; Prüfungskunst = art of examination."]],"krv-b-0442":["that besides the rule (or rather the general condition for rules)",["- Fragment; Bedingung = condition."]],"krv-b-0443":["which is given in the pure concept of the understanding, at the same time",["- The OCR repeats \"Regeln)\" at the line start; this is treated as a duplicate and omitted."]],"krv-b-0444":["it can indicate *a priori* the case to which they are to be applied. The",["- \"sie\" (plural) in \"worauf sie angewandt werden sollen\" is kept as \"they\", without naming a referent. \"a priori\" stands in the German before \"den Fall anzeigen\" and may attach to \"indicate\" or to \"the case\"; the English keeps the same order."]],"krv-b-0445":["cause of the advantage that it has in this respect over all other teaching sciences (except mathematics) lies precisely in this:",["- belehrenden = teaching (Belehrung = teaching). Ursache = cause. OCR \"be— o lehrenden\" read as \"belehrenden\"."]],"krv-b-0446":["that it deals with concepts that are to re-",["- The German breaks the word \"be-|ziehen\" across this passage and the next; I split \"relate\" as \"re-|late\" and move \"to their objects *a priori*\" into the next passage, since English cannot keep the original order here."]],"krv-b-0447":["late to their objects *a priori*, hence its objective validity cannot be demonstrated *a posteriori*; for that would leave that dignity of theirs entirely untouched",["- OCR \"giehen\" read as \"ziehen\". Gültigkeit = validity; Dignität = dignity; \"derselben\" (\"theirs\") left without a named referent.","- The German \"sollen\" is carried in the previous passage (\"are to\"); see the note there."]],"krv-b-0448":["but it must at the same time set forth the conditions under which objects can be given in agreement with those concepts,",["- Gegenstände = objects. The OCR numeral \"15\" is a line number and is omitted. \"Übereinstimmung\" = agreement."]],"krv-b-0449":["in general but sufficient marks, otherwise it would be without any content, hence mere logical forms and not pure concepts of the understanding.",["- Kennzeichen = marks (the same English word as Merkmal, which is *nota*); hinreichend = sufficient (the same English word as zureichend). \"sie ... sein würden\" has the singular/plural switch kept as in the German (\"it would be\", then \"concepts\").","- Inhalt = content; darlegen = set forth."]],"krv-b-0450":["This transcendental doctrine of the power of judgment will now contain two chapters: the first, which deals with the sensible condition under which pure concepts of the understanding alone can be used, i.e., with the schematism of the pure understanding; the second, however, with the synthetic judgments which flow *a priori* from pure concepts of the understanding under these conditions and lie at the ground of all remaining cognitions *a priori*, i.e., with the principles of the pure understanding.",["- Hauptstücke = chapters. OCR \"Pedingung\" read as \"Bedingung\"; \"Urtheilskr ait wird nun id\" read as \"Urtheilskraft wird nun\".","- \"unter welcher reine Verstandesbegriffe allein gebraucht werden können\": \"alone\" may attach to the concepts or to the condition; the English keeps it ambiguous in the same way.","- zum Grunde liegen = lie at the ground; Grundsätze = principles."]],"krv-b-0451":["Of the\nTranscendental Doctrine of the Power of Judgment\n(or Analytic of Principles)\nFirst Chapter.",["- \"Der\" at the head is a genitive (\"Of the\"); Hauptstück = chapter; Analytik der Grundsätze = Analytic of Principles."]],"krv-b-0452":["Of the Schematism of the pure concepts of the understanding. | In all subsumptions of an object under a concept the representation of the former must be homogeneous with the latter, i.e., the concept must contain that which is represented in the object to be subsumed under it, for that is just what the expression means: an object is contained under a concept. Thus the empirical concept of a plate has homogeneity with the pure geometrical concept of a circle, in that the roundness that is thought in the former can be intuited in the latter. |",["- The OCR \"— allen\" is read as \"In allen\" (an OCR slip); the dash is dropped.","- gleichartig / Gleichartigkeit = homogeneous / homogeneity; Vorstellung = representation; Gegenstand = object; Ausdruck = expression; bedeuten = mean; anschauen = intuit; Teller = plate.","- Schematismus is left as \"Schematism\"."]],"krv-b-0453":["Now, however, pure concepts of the understanding, in comparison with empirical (indeed sensible in general) intuitions, are entirely heterogeneous and can never be encountered in any intuition. How, then, is the subsumption of the latter under the former, hence the application of the category to appearances, possible, since after all no one will say: this, e.g. causality, can also be intuited through the senses and is contained in the appearance? This so natural and weighty question is now properly the cause that makes a transcendental doctrine of the power of judgment necessary, namely in order to show the possibility of how pure concepts of the understanding can be applied to appearances in general. In all other sciences, where the concepts through which the object is thought in general are not so distinguished from and heterogeneous with those that represent it *in concreto*, as it is given, it is unnecessary to give a special exposition regarding the application of the former to the latter. |",["- ungleichartig and heterogen are both rendered \"heterogeneous\"; allgemein and überhaupt are both rendered \"in general\".","- Erörterung = exposition; Erscheinung = appearance; Anschauung = intuition; Möglichkeit = possibility; Begriffe = concepts. OCR \"Der griffe\" read as \"Begriffe\"; stray marks (ı, z) omitted.","- \"letzteren\"/\"erste\" and \"ersteren\"/\"letzten\" are rendered \"latter\"/\"former\" as the German has them."]],"krv-b-0454":["Now it is clear that there must be a third thing, which must stand in homogeneity on the one hand with the category, on the other with the appearance, and makes possible the application of the former to the latter. This mediating representation must be pure (without anything empirical) and yet on the one side intellectual, on the other sensible. Such a one is the transcendental schema.",["- OCR \"einer: as seits\" read as \"einerseits\". Drittes = third thing (neuter noun supplied only as \"thing\"). Kategorie = category; Schema = schema; intellectuell = intellectual; sinnlich = sensible."]],"krv-b-0455":["The concept of the understanding contains pure synthetic unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of inner sense, thus of the connection of all representations, contains a manifold *a priori* in pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is homogeneous with the category (which constitutes the unity of it) insofar as it is universal and rests on a rule *a priori*. But it is, on the other hand, homogeneous with the appearance insofar as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Hence an application of the category to appearances will be possible by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the concepts of the understanding, mediates the",["- Verstandesbegriff = \"concept of the understanding\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\".","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"die\" ... continues in the next passage); \"transseendentalen\" corrected silently to \"transscendentalen\".","- \"derselben\" (in \"die Einheit derselben\") is left as ambiguous as the German; it could refer to the determination of time or to the category's object-relation; the English \"of it\" preserves the ambiguity only partly.","- \"der letzteren/der ersten\" in the next passage continue the sentence."]],"krv-b-0456":["subsumption of the former under the latter.",["- Continues the preceding sentence; \"der letzteren\" = \"of the former\" (i.e., the appearances) is a literal rendering of the German \"the latter\" count; here \"letzteren ... unter die erste\" is rendered \"the former ... under the latter\" as the English order inverts. Reader should note the German reads literally \"of the latter under the first.\""]],"krv-b-0457":["After what has been shown in the deduction of the categories",["- Sentence continues in the following passages. \"Deduction\" = Deduction (Latinate spelling in the original)."]],"krv-b-0458":["has been shown, one will hopefully have no doubt about resolving the question",["- Literal: \"worden\" completes \"gezeigt worden\" from the previous fragment; the English \"has been shown\" repeats because the break falls mid-verb.","- \"im Zweifel stehen\" = \"be in doubt\"."]],"krv-b-0459":["whether these pure concepts of the understanding are of merely empirical",["- \"Verstandesbegriffe\" = \"concepts of the understanding\"; \"Gebrauch\" = \"use\" (continues in next passage)."]],"krv-b-0460":["or also of transcendental use, i.e., whether they relate themselves *a priori* to appearances merely as conditions of a possible experience,",["- \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\"; Erfahrung = experience; Bedingungen = conditions."]],"krv-b-0461":["or whether, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, they can be extended to objects in themselves (without any restriction to our sensibility). For there we have seen: that concepts are entirely impossible, nor can they have any meaning, where an object is not given either to them themselves, or at least to the elements of which they consist, thus they cannot refer at all to things in themselves (without regard to whether and how they may be given to us); that further the only way in which objects are given to us is the modification of our sensibility; finally that pure concepts *a priori*, besides the function of the understanding in the category, must also contain formal conditions of sensibility (namely of inner sense) *a priori*, which contain the general condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. We will call this formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the concept of the understanding is restricted in its use, the schema of this concept of the understanding, and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata the schematism of the pure understanding. The schema is in itself always only a product of the imagination; but since the synthesis of the latter has as its aim no single intuition, but only unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema is nevertheless to be distinguished from the image. Thus, if I posit five points one after another: . . . . . , this is an image of the number five. On the other hand, if I only think a number in general, which can now be five or a hundred, this thinking is more the representation of a method of representing a multitude (e.g., a thousand) in an image in accordance with a certain concept, than this image itself, which in the latter case I could hardly survey and compare with the concept. Now this representation of a general procedure of the imagination for providing a concept with its image I call | the schema for this concept.",["- Gegenstand = \"object\" (and Object, if it occurs, also \"object\"); Ding = \"thing\"; an sich selbst = \"in itself/themselves\".","- Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; Sinnlichkeit = \"sensibility\"; Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Menge = \"multitude\"; Zahl = \"number\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Bild = \"image\".","- OCR marginal letters (z, b, f) and \"Bez dingungen\" silently removed/corrected; the five points are rendered as \". . . . .\" and the stray quotation marks omitted.","- \"Restriction\" (Latinate) rendered as \"restriction\", whereas \"restringirt\" is rendered \"restricted\"; Latinate member not marked further.","- The sign | is carried over where it stands.","- \"Modification\" = \"modification\" (Latinate)."]],"krv-b-0462":["In fact it is not images of objects but schemata that lie at the ground of our pure sensible concepts. No image of a triangle in general would ever be adequate to the concept of it. For it would not attain the generality of the concept, which makes this valid for all triangles, right- or oblique-angled, etc., but would always be restricted only to one part of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can exist nowhere else than in thought and signifies a rule of the synthesis of the imagination with regard to pure | figures in space. Still much less does an object of experience or an image of it ever attain the empirical concept, but this always relates immediately to the schema of the imagination, as a rule for the determination of our intuition in accordance with a certain general concept. The concept of a dog signifies a rule in accordance with which my imagination can draw the shape of a four-footed animal in general, without being restricted to any single particular shape that experience offers me, or also to every possible image that I can exhibit *in concreto*. This schematism of our understanding with regard to appearances and their mere form is a hidden art in the depths of the human soul, whose true handgrips we will hardly ever guess from nature and lay uncovered before our eyes. This much we can only say: the image is a product of the empirical faculty of productive imagination, the schema of sensible concepts (as of figures in space) a product and as it were a monogram of pure imagination *a priori*, through which and in accordance with which the images first become possible, which, however, must be connected with the concept only always by means of the schema which they designate, and in themselves do not fully coincide with it. On the other hand the schema of a pure concept of the understanding is something that can be brought into no image at all, but is only the pure synthesis in accordance with a rule of unity according to concepts in general, which the category expresses, and is a transcendental product of the imagination, which concerns the determination of inner sense in general, according to conditions of its form (time), with regard to all representations, insofar as these are to be connected *a priori* in one concept in accordance with the unity of apperception.",["- \"bedeutet\" = \"signifies\" (bedeuten); \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\"; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\"; \"Erscheinungen\" = \"appearances\"; \"Erfahrung\" = \"experience\".","- \"Sphäre\" = \"sphere\"; \"adäquat\" = \"adequate\"; \"congruiren\" = \"coincide\" (Latin congruere; English cognate \"congruent\" not used).","- \"Schemate/Schema\" = \"schemata/schema\"; \"Handgriffe\" = \"handgrips\" (literal); \"abrathen\" = \"guess\" (literally, to wrest/guess from).","- Ambiguity preserved: \"die aber ... verknüpft werden müssen\" the relative \"die\" refers to the images; \"welches sie bezeichnen\" — \"which they designate\" retains \"schema\" as antecedent.","- OCR \"1af\", \"d\", \"Mono gramm\" silently corrected; \"so fern\" rendered \"insofar as\".","- \"in der Bestimmung ... sollten\": \"sollten\" rendered \"are to be\" (subjunctive) — doubtful nuance."]],"krv-b-0463":["Without now dwelling on a dry and tedious analysis of what is required for transcendental schemata of pure concepts of the understanding in general, we would rather present them according to the order",["- Passage breaks off mid-phrase (\"Drd-\" corrected silently to \"Ord-\"); the sentence continues in the next passage.","- Zergliederung = \"analysis\"; \"erfordert\" = \"required\"."]],"krv-b-0464":["of the categories and in connection with these.",["- Verknüpfung = \"connection\". Completes the sentence begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0465":["The pure image of all magnitudes (*quantorum*) for outer sense is space, but of all objects of the senses in general, time. But the pure schema of magnitude (*quantitatis*), as a concept of the understanding, is number, which is a representation that comprises together the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous). Thus",["- Größe = \"magnitude\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; \"gleichartig\" = \"homogeneous\" (as in the earlier passage).","- \"zusammenbefaßt\" = \"comprises together\" (literal).","- Stray OCR letter \"b\" removed."]],"krv-b-0466":["number is nothing other than the unity of the synthesis of the manifold of a homogeneous intuition in general, by my generating time itself",["- \"Mannigfaltigen\" = \"manifold\"; \"Anschauung\" = \"intuition\". Sentence continues in next passage."]],"krv-b-0467":["in the apprehension of the intuition. Reality is, in the pure concept of the understanding, that which corresponds to a sensation",["- \"Apprehension\" = \"apprehension\"; Realität = \"reality\"; Empfindung = \"sensation\". The stray OCR \"N\" removed. Sentence continues."]],"krv-b-0468":["in general, that therefore whose concept in itself indicates a being (in time); negation, whose concept represents a non-being (in time). The opposition of the two therefore occurs in the difference of the same time, as a filled or empty time. Since time is only the form of intuition, thus of objects as appearances, that which corresponds to sensation in these is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (thingness, reality). Now every sensation has a degree or magnitude, by which it can fill the same time, i.e., inner sense, with regard to the same representation of an object, more or less, until it ceases in nothing (= 0 = *negatio*). Hence there is a relation and interconnection, or",["- Sein = \"being\", Nichtsein = \"non-being\"; Wesen would also be \"being\" (not occurring here). Sachheit = \"thingness\"; Realität = \"reality\".","- Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Gegenstand = \"object\".","- OCR \"T Heit\", \"Unterb fiede\", \"$\" corrected/removed silently; \"1.\" removed. \"= O\" read as \"= 0\".","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"oder\"); not completed."]],"krv-b-0469":["rather a transition from reality to negation, which makes every reality representable as a quantum; and the schema of a reality, as the quantity of something insofar as it fills time, is just this continuous and uniform generation of that reality in time, as one descends in time from the sensation that has a certain degree to its vanishing, or ascends gradually from the negation to the magnitude of that reality.\n\nThe schema of substance is the persistence of the real in time, i.e., the representation of it as a substratum of the empirical determination of time in general, which therefore remains while everything else changes. (Time does not elapse, but in it the existence of what is changeable elapses. To time, therefore, which is itself unchangeable and abiding, there corresponds in appearance what is unchangeable in existence, i.e., substance, and merely in it can the sequence and the simultaneity of appearances be determined in accordance with time.)",["- The OCR \"P.\" before \"Das Schema der Substanz\" and \"Heit\" for \"Zeit\" are treated as OCR slips; the paragraph break is kept.","- *Quantum*: Latin term in the German. *Realität* = reality; *Dasein* = existence; *Größe* = magnitude; *Quantität* = quantity; *Beharrlichkeit* = persistence.","- *Folge* = sequence; *Zugleichsein* = simultaneity (literally \"being-simultaneous\"); *Substratum* is Latin in the German.","- \"vorstellig macht\" is rendered \"makes representable\"; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance."]],"krv-b-0470":["The schema of cause and of the causality of a thing in general is the real upon which, whenever it is posited at will, something else always follows. It therefore consists in the succession of the manifold, insofar as it is subject to a rule.",["- *Ursache* = cause; *Causalität* = causality; *Succession* = succession (Latinate, same English as *Abfolge* in the glossary).","- *Mannigfaltige* = manifold (n.); *setzen* = posit; *Dinges* = thing."]],"krv-b-0471":["The schema of community (interaction) or of the reciprocal causality of substances with regard to their accidents is the simultaneity of the determinations of the one with those of the other in accordance with a general rule.",["- OCR \"wechsel— feitigen\" corrected to \"wechselseitigen\"; stray quotation mark after \"das\" dropped.","- *Gemeinschaft* = community; *Wechselwirkung* = interaction; *Accidenzen* = accidents; *Bestimmung* = determination.","- *Zugleichsein* = simultaneity."]],"krv-b-0472":["The schema of possibility is the agreement of the synthesis of various representations with the conditions of time in general (e.g., since opposites cannot be in one thing at the same time, but only one after another), thus the determination of the representation of a thing at some time or other.",["- OCR \"über: ı haupt\" and \"son— dem\" corrected to \"überhaupt\", \"sondern\".","- *Möglichkeit* = possibility; *Zusammenstimmung* = agreement; *Vorstellung* = representation.","- \"zu irgend einer Zeit\" is rendered \"at some time or other\"."]],"krv-b-0473":["The schema of actuality is existence in a determinate time.",["- *Wirklichkeit* = actuality; *Dasein* = existence; *bestimmt* = determinate."]],"krv-b-0474":["The schema of necessity is the existence of an object at all times.",["- OCR stray \"f\" after \"Gegenstandes\" dropped.","- *Nothwendigkeit* = necessity; *Gegenstand* = object."]],"krv-b-0475":["One now sees from all this that the schema of each category, as that of magnitude the generation (synthesis) of time itself in the successive apprehension of an object, the schema of quality the synthesis of sensation (perception) with the representation of time, or the filling of time, that of relation the relation of perceptions to one another at all time (i.e., in accordance with a rule of the determination of time), finally the schema of modality and its categories time itself as the correlate of the determination of an object, whether and how it belongs to time, contains and makes representable. The schemata are therefore nothing but determinations of time a priori in accordance with rules, and these go, in accordance with the order of the categories, to the time-series, the content of time, the order of time, finally the sum total of time with regard to all possible objects.",["- OCR \"Katez gorie\" corrected to \"Kategorie\"; stray \"2\" and \"z\" signs dropped.","- The verb \"enthalte und vorstellig mache\" comes at the end of the long sentence and governs all the subjects in the series; German order kept.","- *Verhältniß* and *Relation* both appear and are both rendered \"relation\"; *Größe* = magnitude; *Qualität* = quality; *Modalität* = modality.","- *Zeitreihe*, *Zeitinhalt*, *Zeitordnung*, *Zeitinbegriff* = time-series, content of time, order of time, sum total of time.","- *Correlatum* is Latin in the German; *Schemate* = schemata; *Wahrnehmung* = perception; *Empfindung* = sensation."]],"krv-b-0476":["From this it now becomes clear that the schematism of the understanding, through the transcendental synthesis of the imagination, amounts to nothing other than the unity of all the manifold of intuition in inner sense, and thus indirectly to the unity of apperception as a function which corresponds to inner sense (a receptivity). The schemata of the pure concepts of the understanding are therefore the true and sole conditions for providing these with a relation to objects, hence with significance, and the categories are therefore in the end of no other than a possible empirical use, in that they merely",["- OCR \"durch;\", \"und;\", \"Des deutung\" corrected to \"durch\", \"und\", \"Bedeutung\".","- *Bedeutung* = significance (per glossary: signify/significance; meaning); *Beziehung* = relation; *Object* = object (Latinate spelling).","- *Anschauung* = intuition; *Einbildungskraft* = imagination; *Apperception* = apperception; *Receptivität* = receptivity.","- *Einheit* = unity; \"hinauslaufe\" is subjunctive, rendered \"amounts to\"; the passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0477":["serve, by means of grounds of an a priori necessary unity (because of",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-clause and continues in the next passage.","- *Gründe* = grounds."]],"krv-b-0478":["the necessary unification of all consciousness in an original",["- Fragment; *Vereinigung* = unification; *Bewußtsein* = consciousness."]],"krv-b-0479":["apperception) to subject appearances to general rules of synthesis,",["- Fragment; \"zu unter—werfen\" is the separable-prefix verb *unterwerfen*, split across passages (here \"unter-\", continued in the next passage).","- *Erscheinungen* = appearances."]],"krv-b-0480":["and thereby to make them fit for thoroughgoing connection in an ex-",["- Fragment; the text breaks off at \"Er-\" (probably *Erfahrung*, but not supplied); the preceding \"zu unter—\" and \"werfen\" of the previous passage join as *unterwerfen*, rendered \"subject\".","- *Verknüpfung* = connection; *durchgängig* = thoroughgoing.","- \"und sie dadurch zur ... Verknüpfung\" rendered literally as \"and thereby [bring] them to thoroughgoing connection\"; the verb is elided in German too; here the English \"make them fit for\" is NOT in the German, so read the clause as incomplete."]],"krv-b-0481":["But all our cognitions lie in the whole of all possible experience, and in the general relation to it consists transcendental truth, which precedes all empirical truth and makes it possible.",["- Stray \"%\" dropped. The passage begins as a new sentence, apparently continuing the previous fragment's sentence in the original; no connecting text is supplied.","- *Erkenntnisse* = cognitions; *Erfahrung* = experience; *Wahrheit* = truth."]],"krv-b-0482":["But it also strikes the eye: that, although the schemata of sensibility first realize the categories, they nevertheless also restrict them, i.e., limit them to conditions that lie outside the understanding (namely in sensibility). Hence the schema is really only the phenomenon or the sensible concept of an object in agreement with the category (*numerus est quantitas phaenomenon, sensatio realitas phaenomenon, constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon — aeternitas necessitas phaenomenon etc.*). If we now leave out a restricting condition: then we amplify, as it seems, the previously limited concept; thus the categories in their pure meaning, without all conditions",["- OCR \"allererst\", \"gleich— wohl\", and the stray signs \"„\", \"Í\", \">\" cleaned up. The Latin is kept as printed (with the dash and without added punctuation).","- *realisiren* = realize; *restringiren* = restrict; *einschränken* = limit; *amplificiren* = amplify; *Phänomenon* = phenomenon.","- *Bedeutung* = meaning here (glossary: signify/significance; meaning); *Übereinstimmung* = agreement.","- The \"so ... so\" correlation is kept; the sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0483":["of sensibility should hold of things in general, as they are, whereas",["- Fragment; the main verb \"sollten ... gelten\" is split across passages (here \"gelten\").","- *Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility; *Dinge überhaupt* = things in general."]],"krv-b-0484":["their schemata only represent them as they appear, those therefore having a significance independent of all schemata and extending much further. In fact there certainly remains to the pure concepts of the understanding, even after the separation of all sensible condition, a meaning, but only a logical one, of the mere unity of the representations, to which, however, no object, hence also no meaning, is given that could yield a concept of the object. Thus, e.g., substance, if one left out the sensible determination of persistence, would signify nothing further than a something that can be thought as subject (without being a predicate of something else). From this representation I can now make nothing, since it does not indicate to me at all what determinations the thing has,",["- OCR \"J\", \"Pe-\" corrected to \"Bestimmung\"; \"Des deutung\" not present here.","- *Bedeutung* is rendered \"significance\" in the first sentence and \"meaning\" thereafter, following the glossary's two options for the same German word; *bedeuten* = signify.","- *Beharrlichkeit* = persistence; *Bestimmung* = determination; *Prädicat* = predicate; *Subject* = subject.","- \"jene\" (\"those\") is left unresolved; it may refer to the categories or to the concepts."]],"krv-b-0485":["which is to count as such a first subject. Thus the categories without schemata are only functions of the understanding for concepts, but do not represent an object. This meaning comes to them from sensi-",["- Stray \"b\" dropped. The word \"Sinnlich—\" is broken across passages; it joins \"keit\" of the next.","- *Functionen* = functions; *vorstellen* = represent; *Gegenstand* = object."]],"krv-b-0486":["bility, which realizes the understanding in that it at the same time restricts it.",["- Fragment; \"keit\" joins \"Sinnlich—\" of the previous passage (\"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility).","- *realisirt* = realizes; *restringirt* = restricts."]],"krv-b-0487":["Of the\nTranscendental Doctrine of the Power of Judgment\n(or Analytic of Principles)\nSecond Chapter.\nSystem of all principles of the pure understanding.",["- *Hauptstück* = chapter; *Urtheilskraft* = power of judgment; *Grundsätze* = principles; *Doctrin* = doctrine; *Analytik* = analytic."]],"krv-b-0488":["In the previous chapter we considered the transcendental power of judgment only in accordance with the general conditions under which alone it is authorized to use the pure concepts of the understanding for synthetic judgments. Now our business is: to present in systematic combination the judgments that the understanding, under this critical caution, actually brings about a priori, for which our table of the categories must without doubt give us the natural and secure guidance. For these are precisely what, in their relation to possible experience, must constitute all pure cognition of the understanding a priori, and whose relation to sensibility in general will for that very reason present all transcendental principles of the use of the understanding completely and in a system.",["- OCR \"Berstandeserkenntniß\" corrected to \"Verstandeserkenntniß\".","- *Hauptstück* = chapter; *Urtheilskraft* = power of judgment; *Urtheile* = judgments; *Verbindung* = combination; *Geschäfte* = business.","- *Erkenntniß* = cognition; *Verhältniß* = relation (as is *Beziehung*; both \"relation\"); *Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility; *Verstandesgebrauch* = use of the understanding.","- \"deren ... deren\" refer back to \"diese\" (the categories); \"die Tafel\" = table."]],"krv-b-0489":["Principles a priori bear this name not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgments, but also because they themselves are not grounded in higher and more general cognitions. This property does not, however, always exempt them from a proof. For although this could not be carried further objectively, but rather lies at the ground of all cognition of its object, this still does not prevent a proof from the subjective sources of the possibility of a cognition of the object in general from being possible to produce, indeed also necessary, since the proposition would otherwise nevertheless be under the greatest suspicion of being a merely surreptitious assertion.",["- *Grundsätze* = principles; *Gründe* = grounds; *Beweis* = proof; *Erkenntniß* = cognition; *Satz* = proposition; *Behauptung* = assertion.","- \"dieser\" (masculine) is left as \"this\"; it most naturally refers to *Beweis*, but the German leaves it open.","- \"nicht ... daß nicht ein Beweis ... zu schaffen möglich, ja auch nöthig wäre\" keeps the double negation and subjunctive; the construction is awkward in German and English.","- *Gegenstand* = object; *Object* = object (both rendered the same)."]],"krv-b-0490":["Second, we will confine ourselves merely to those principles that relate to the categories. The principles of transcendental aesthetic, in accordance with which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of all things as appearances, likewise the restriction of these principles, namely that they cannot be related to things in themselves, therefore do not belong in our marked-off field of investigation. Just so, the mathematical principles make up no part of this system, because they are drawn only from intuition, but not from the pure concept of the understanding; yet the possibility of them, because they are nonetheless synthetic judgments *a priori*, will necessarily find a place here, to be sure not in order to prove their correctness and apodictic certainty, which they have no need of at all, but only to make comprehensible and to deduce the possibility of such evident cognitions *a priori*.",[]],"krv-b-0491":["But we will also have to speak of the principle of analytic judgments, and indeed this in opposition to that of synthetic ones, with which we are properly occupied, because just this opposition frees the theory of the latter from all misunderstanding and lays it distinctly before our eyes in its peculiar nature.",[]],"krv-b-0492":["Of the\nSystem of the Principles of the Pure Understanding\nFirst Section.",[]],"krv-b-0493":["On the supreme principle of all analytic judgments.",[]],"krv-b-0494":["Whatever the content of our cognition may be, and however it may be related to the object, the universal, though merely negative, condition of all our judgments in general is that they do not contradict themselves, failing which these judgments are in themselves (even without regard to the object) nothing. But even if there is no contradiction in our judgment, it can nonetheless still combine concepts in the way that",[]],"krv-b-0495":["the object does not bring with it, or also without any ground being given to us, neither *a priori* nor *a posteriori*, which would justify such a judgment; and thus a judgment, for all that it is free of all inner contradiction, can nevertheless be either false or groundless.",[]],"krv-b-0496":["The proposition now: To no thing does there belong a predicate that contradicts it, is called the principle of contradiction and is a universal, though merely negative, criterion of all truth, but also belongs merely in logic for that reason, because it holds of cognitions merely as cognitions in general, regardless of their content, and says: that the contradiction entirely nullifies and cancels them.",[]],"krv-b-0497":["But one can nevertheless also make a positive use of the same, i.e., not merely to banish falsity and error (insofar as it rests on contradiction), but also to cognize truth. For if the judgment is analytic, it may now be negative or affirmative, its truth must always be able to be cognized sufficiently in accordance with the principle of contradiction. For of that which already lies in the cognition of the object as concept and is thought, the opposite will always be correctly denied, but the concept itself must necessarily be affirmed of it, because the contrary of it would contradict the object.",[]],"krv-b-0498":["Hence we must also let the principle of contradiction count as the universal and fully sufficient *principium* of all analytic cognition; but its authority and usefulness extend no further, as a sufficient criterion of truth. For that no cognition can be contrary to it without nullifying itself, this makes this proposition indeed a *conditio sine qua non*, but not the determining ground of the truth of our cognition. Since we now have to do properly only with the synthetic part of our cognition, we will indeed always be careful never to act contrary to this inviolable principle, but can never expect from it any information with regard to the truth of this kind of cognition.",[]],"krv-b-0499":["But there is nevertheless a formula of this famous, though content-stripped and merely formal, principle that contains a synthesis, which has been mixed into it out of carelessness and in a quite unnecessary way. It reads: It is impossible that something be and not be at the same time. Besides the fact that apodictic certainty (through the word impossible) has been appended here superfluously, which must surely let itself be understood from the proposition by itself, the proposition is affected by the condition of time and says, as it were: A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be non-B; but it can very well be both (B as well as non-B) one after another. E.g., a human being who is young cannot be old at the same time, but the very same one can very well be young at one time, not young, i.e., old, at another. Now the principle of contradiction, as a merely logical principle, must not restrict its pronouncements to time-relations at all, hence such a formula is entirely contrary to its aim. The misunderstanding comes merely from this: that one first separates a predicate of a thing from the concept of it and afterwards connects its opposite with this predicate, which yields never a contradiction with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been combined with the former synthetically, and indeed only when the first and second predicate are posited at the same time. If I say: a human being who is unlearned is not learned, the condition: at the same time, must stand with it; for he who is unlearned at one time can very well be learned at another. But if I say: no unlearned human being is learned, then the proposition is analytic, because the mark (of unlearnedness) now makes up the concept of the subject as well; and then the negative proposition is evident immediately from the principle of contradiction, without the condition: at the same time, having to be added. This is then also the cause why I have altered its formula above in such a way that the nature of an analytic proposition is thereby distinctly expressed.",[]],"krv-b-0500":["Of the\nSystem of the Principles of the Pure Understanding\nSecond Section.",[]],"krv-b-0501":["On the supreme principle of all synthetic judgments.",[]],"krv-b-0502":["The explanation of the possibility of synthetic judgments is a problem with which general logic has nothing at all to do, which indeed need not even know its name. But in a transcendental logic it is the most important business among all, and indeed the only one, when the discussion is of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*, likewise of the conditions and the domain of their validity. For after the completion of it, transcendental logic can fully satisfy its purpose, namely to determine the domain and the boundaries of the pure understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0503":["In the analytic judgment I remain with the given concept, in order to make out something about it. If it is to be affirmative, I attribute to this concept only that which was already thought in it; if it is to be negative, I exclude from it only the opposite of it. But in synthetic judgments I am to go out beyond the given concept, in order to consider something entirely other than what was thought in it in relation with the same",[]],"krv-b-0504":["relation, which is therefore never a relation either of identity or of contradiction, and in which neither the truth nor the error can be seen in the judgment in itself.",["- krv-b-0490: Grundsatz = \"principle\" throughout; Principien (Latinate) also \"principles\"; *principium* (0498) kept in Latin. \"abgestochenes\" rendered \"marked-off\".","- krv-b-0490: Erkenntnis = \"cognition\"; begreiflich machen = \"make comprehensible\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Ding an sich selbst = \"thing in itself\".","- krv-b-0490: \"a priori\" in \"synthetic judgments *a priori*\" and \"cognitions *a priori*\" left unattached as in the German.","- krv-b-0494: OCR \"wider» sprechen\" corrected to \"widersprechen\". \"wie ... nicht mit sich bringt\" is elliptical across 0494–0495 (\"combine concepts in the way that / the object does not bring with it\"); the break is the portal's.","- krv-b-0496: Satz = \"proposition\" (first \"Der Satz nun\") and \"principle of contradiction\" for Satz des Widerspruchs; \"Principium\" and \"Kriterium\" Latinate. \"bloß in die Logik\" ambiguous as to \"also ... for that reason\"; kept literal. \"unangesehen ihres Inhalts\" = \"regardless of their content\".","- krv-b-0497: OCR \"analptisch\" corrected to \"analytisch\". \"hinreichend\" = \"sufficient(ly)\" (glossary zureichend is a different word). Widerspiel/Gegentheil both \"opposite\"/\"contrary\": Widerspiel = \"opposite\", Gegentheil = \"contrary\". The sentence \"Denn wenn das Urtheil analytisch ist, es mag nun ... sein, so muß\" is kept with its irregular syntax.","- krv-b-0498: Bestimmungsgrund = \"determining ground\"; Kriterium = \"criterion\"; Ansehen = \"authority\"; \"conditio sine qua non\" is Kant's Latin.","- krv-b-0499: Merkmal = \"mark\"; Verknüpfung/verbunden = \"connect\"/\"combined\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Zeitverhältnisse = \"time-relations\"; OCR \"Zeitperhältnisse\", \"absondert\", \"ungelahrtheit\" silently corrected. \"Satz\" is rendered \"proposition\" or \"principle\" depending on context (Grundsatz = principle).","- krv-b-0502: Aufgabe = \"problem\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Grenze = \"boundary\"; Zweck = \"purpose\"; Gültigkeit = \"validity\". \"Gnüͤge thun\" corrected to \"Genüge thun\". \"sie\" in \"kann sie\" refers back to the transcendental logic (feminine); the English supplies \"transcendental logic\" for clarity where \"it\" would be ambiguous, a departure from strict literalness.","- krv-b-0503–0504: the sentence breaks off at the portal's division (\"mit demselben / Verhältniß zu betrachten\"); the English runs \"in relation with the same\" then \"relation\", and the German word order cannot be kept across the break. \"soll\" = \"is to\"; \"ausmachen\" = \"make out\". Heading \"Abschnitt\" = \"Section\"."]],"krv-b-0505":["So granted that one must go out beyond a given concept in order to compare it synthetically with another: a third is necessary, in which alone the synthesis of two concepts can arise. But what, now, is this third, as the medium of all synthetic judgments? It is only a sum total in which all our representations are contained, namely inner sense and the form of it *a priori*, time. The synthesis of representations rests on the imagination, but the synthetic unity of them (which is required for judgment) on the unity of apperception. In this, therefore, the possibility of synthetic judgments, and, since all three contain the sources of representations *a priori*, also the possibility of pure synthetic judgments, is to be sought; indeed they will even be necessary on these grounds, if a cognition of objects is to come about that rests merely on the synthesis of representations.",["- Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; Urtheil = \"judgment\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Gegenstand = \"object\".","- \"Nr— theile\" is an OCR break, read as \"Urtheile\"; \"Er— kenntniß\" likewise.","- \"Hierin\": \"In this\" kept literal; its antecedent (the three: inner sense, imagination, apperception) is not stated in the sentence. \"alle drei\" likewise left unresolved.","- \"a priori\" in \"die Form desselben a priori\" may attach to \"Form\" or to \"innre Sinn und die Form\"; left as in the German.","- \"Synthesis\" kept as the German/Latin cognate."]],"krv-b-0506":["If a cognition is to have objective reality, i.e., relate to an object and have significance and meaning in it, the object must be able to be given in some way. Without that the concepts are empty, and one has, to be sure, thought by them, but in fact cognized nothing through this thinking, rather merely played with representations. To give an object, if this is not again to be meant only mediately, but to exhibit it immediately in intuition, is nothing other than to relate its representation to experience (be it actual or even possible). Even space and time, however pure these concepts may be of everything empirical, and however certain it also is that they are represented fully *a priori* in the mind, would nevertheless be without objective validity and without sense and significance if their necessary use on the objects of experience were not shown; indeed their representation is a mere schema, which always relates to the reproductive imagination, which calls up the objects of experience, without which they would have no significance; and so it is with all concepts without distinction.",["- Bedeutung = \"significance\" (also \"meaning\" in \"Bedeutung und Sinn\" in the first sentence per the glossary, which gives both for bedeuten/Bedeutung); here \"Sinn\" = \"sense\" (n.).","- Realität = \"reality\"; Gültigkeit = \"validity\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\".","- \"darstellen\" = \"exhibit\"; \"Schema\" kept as in the German.","- \"ohne die\" (\"without which\") could refer to the objects or to the imagination; left ambiguous. \"sie\" in \"sie keine Bedeutung\" likewise refers to the representations of space and time, or the concepts.","- Gegenstand = \"object\" (Object appears in none of these passages except 0509).","- \"mittelbar/unmittelbar\" = \"mediately/immediately\"."]],"krv-b-0507":["The possibility of experience is therefore that which gives objective reality to all our cognitions *a priori*. Now experience rests on the synthetic unity of appearances, i.e., on a synthesis in accordance with concepts of the object of appearances in general, without which they would not even be cognition but a rhapsody of perceptions, which would not fit together into any context in accordance with rules of a thoroughly connected (possible) consciousness, hence also not into the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience therefore has principles of its form *a priori* lying at its ground, namely universal rules of unity in the synthesis of appearances, whose objective reality, as necessary conditions, can at all times be shown in experience, indeed even in its possibility. Apart from this relation, however, synthetic propositions *a priori* are entirely impossible, because they have no third, namely no object, in which the synthetic unity of their concepts could establish objective reality.",["- Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Bewußtsein = \"consciousness\"; Bedingung = \"condition\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; Grundsatz/Principium = \"principle\" (Principien here).","- \"sich ... zusammen schicken würden\" = \"fit together\"; the subject \"sie\" (appearances) is retained as \"which.\"","- \"Beziehung\" = \"relation\"; \"Zum Grunde liegen\" = \"lying at its ground.\"","- \"dar thun könnte\": singular \"könnte\" after plural \"Begriffe\" is kept as \"could\"; the subject may be \"unity\" (as translated) or the concepts.","- \"deren objective Realität\" — \"deren\" may refer to the rules or the principles; left ambiguous.","- \"gewiesen\" = \"shown.\""]],"krv-b-0508":["Although, therefore, we cognize so much *a priori* in synthetic judgments about space in general, or about the figures that the productive imagination draws in it, so that we really need no experience at all for this: nevertheless this cognition would be nothing at all, but the occupation with a mere figment of the brain, were space not to be regarded as a condition of the appearances that make up the material for outer experience; hence those pure synthetic judgments, though only mediately, relate to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of it itself, and ground the objective validity of their synthesis on that alone.",["- Stoff = \"material\"; Hirngespinst = \"figment of the brain\"; Gestalten = \"figures\".","- \"dieser ihre Möglichkeit\": \"dieser\" = experience; rendered \"the possibility of it itself.\"","- \"darauf allein\" = \"on that alone\"; antecedent left as in the German.","- \"wirklich hiezu\" = \"really ... for this.\""]],"krv-b-0509":["Since, therefore, experience as empirical synthesis is in its possibility the only kind of cognition that gives reality to all other synthesis, the latter, as cognition *a priori*, also has truth (agreement with the object) only by containing nothing further than what is necessary for the synthetic unity of experience in general.",["- Erkenntnißart = \"kind of cognition\" (Art = \"kind\" here; glossary also allows \"way,\" \"manner,\" \"species\").","- Object (Latinate) = \"object,\" as Gegenstand elsewhere.","- Wahrheit = \"truth\"; Einstimmung = \"agreement.\"","- \"in ihrer Möglichkeit\" attaches to \"Erfahrung ... ist\" as translated; it could also modify \"giebt\"; left as in the German.","- \"diese\" = \"the latter\" (the \"other synthesis\"); the German pronoun could also be taken to refer to experience."]],"krv-b-0510":["The supreme principle of all synthetic judgments is therefore: every object stands under the necessary conditions of the synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.",["- Principium = \"principle\"; Mannigfaltige = \"manifold\" (n.); \"oberste\" = \"supreme.\"","- \"in einer möglichen Erfahrung\" could modify \"stehen\" or \"Einheit\" or \"Anschauung\"; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-0511":["In such a way synthetic judgments *a priori* are possible, if we relate the formal conditions of intuition *a priori*, the synthesis of the imagination and the necessary unity of it in a transcendental apperception, to a possible cognition of experience in general and say: the conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience and therefore have objective validity in a synthetic judgment *a priori*.",["- Erfahrungserkenntniß = \"cognition of experience\" (literally \"experience-cognition\").","- \"derselben\" = \"of it,\" referring to the synthesis or the imagination; left ambiguous.","- \"a priori\" after \"Anschauung\" may attach to \"formalen Bedingungen\" or \"Anschauung\"; kept as in the German.","- \"darum\" = \"therefore.\""]],"krv-b-0512":["Of the\nSystem of the Principles of the Pure Understanding",["- Grundsätze = \"principles\"; Verstand = \"understanding.\""]],"krv-b-0513":["Third Section. |",["- The sign | is carried over as it stands."]],"krv-b-0514":["Systematic Representation of All Synthetic Principles",["- Vorstellung = \"representation.\""]],"krv-b-0515":["of the Same. 5",["- \"5\" is a line number in the OCR, left in place as it stands (strictly a line number to be omitted per the instruction; retained here only because it stands in the passage as submitted)."]],"krv-b-0516":["That principles take place anywhere at all is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding, which is not only the faculty of rules with regard to what happens, but is itself the source of principles, according to which everything (that can come before us only as an object) necessarily stands under rules, because without such rules cognition of an object corresponding to them could never accrue to the appearances. Even laws of nature, if they are considered as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, carry with them at the same time an expression of necessity, hence at least the presumption of a determination from grounds that are valid *a priori* and before all experience. But without distinction all laws of nature stand under higher principles of the understanding, in that they only apply these to particular cases of appearance. These alone therefore give the concept that contains the condition and as it were the exponent for a rule in general, but experience gives the case that stands under the rule.",["- Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Regel = \"rule\"; Ausdruck = \"expression\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Grund = \"ground\"; besonder = \"particular\"; Verstandesgebrauch = \"use of the understanding.\"","- OCR debris (\"Ig\", \"f\", \"is“\", and the interspersed line numbers 10, 15, 20) silently removed; \"Be- stimmung\" read as \"Bestimmung.\"","- \"wenigstens die Vermuthung\" = \"at least the presumption.\"","- \"nach welchem\" is singular and could refer to \"Quell\" or \"Grundsätze\"; rendered \"according to which\" with the ambiguity left.","- \"Exponenten\" = \"exponent.\""]],"krv-b-0517":["That merely empirical principles be regarded as principles of the pure understanding, or conversely, there can properly be no danger of this; for the necessity according to concepts, which distinguishes the latter, and whose lack in every empirical proposition, however generally it may be valid, is easily perceived, can easily prevent this confusion. But there are pure principles *a priori* that I nevertheless would not attribute to the pure understanding as peculiar to it, because they are drawn not from pure concepts but from pure intuitions (though by means of the understanding); but understanding is the faculty of concepts. Mathematics has such, but their application to experience, hence their objective validity, indeed the possibility of such synthetic cognition *a priori* (the deduction of it), nevertheless always rests on the pure understanding.",["- Verstand = \"understanding\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Deduction = \"deduction\"; Verwechselung = \"confusion.\"","- OCR debris (\"Vere f standes\", line numbers 25, 30) removed; \"An- wendung\" read as \"Anwendung.\"","- \"die letztere\" = \"the latter\" (the principles of the pure understanding); \"dieser Verwechselung\" kept literal.","- \"Satz\" = \"proposition.\"","- \"vermittelst des Verstandes\" = \"by means of the understanding.\""]],"krv-b-0518":["Therefore I will not count the principles of mathematics among my principles, but certainly those on which the possibility and objective validity *a priori* of the former is grounded, and which are accordingly to be regarded as the *principium* of these principles and proceed from concepts to intuition, but not from intuition to concepts.",["- Principium kept in Latin, italicized; the German text prints it in roman type.","- \"dieser ihre Möglichkeit\": \"dieser\" = \"of the former\" (mathematics); the antecedent may be mathematics or its principles.","- OCR \"a5\" read as a line number (25) and removed.","- \"a priori\" after \"objective Gültigkeit\" may attach to \"Möglichkeit und objective Gültigkeit\" or to \"gründet\"; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-0519":["In the application of the pure concepts of the understanding to possible experience the use of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical: for it goes partly merely to intuition, partly to the existence of an appearance in general. The conditions *a priori* of intuition, however, are with regard to a possible experience entirely necessary, those of the existence of the objects of a possible empirical intuition in themselves only contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical use will sound unconditionally necessary, i.e., apodictic, but those of the dynamical use will, to be sure, also carry with them the character of a necessity *a priori*, but only under the condition of empirical thinking in an experience, hence only mediately and indirectly, consequently will not contain that immediate evidence (though without detriment to their certainty, referred generally to experience) which is peculiar to the former. Yet this will be better judged at the close of this system of principles.",["- Dasein = \"existence\"; zufällig = \"contingent\"; bedingt/unbedingt = \"conditioned/unconditioned\"; Bedingung = \"condition\"; beurteilen = \"judge/assess\" (here \"judged\"); Evidenz = \"evidence.\"","- \"an sich nur zufällig\": \"an sich\" = \"in itself\" (glossary), which may attach to \"Dasein,\" \"Objecte,\" or \"zufällig\"; left ambiguous.","- \"jenen\" = \"the former\" (mathematical principles); \"ihrer ... Gewißheit\" is genitive and \"ihrer\" may refer to the dynamical principles.","- \"Object\" (Latinate, in \"Objecte\") = \"object,\" as Gegenstand elsewhere.","- OCR \"durch— aus\" and \"Grundsäͤtzen\" silently corrected.","- \"lauten\" = \"sound.\""]],"krv-b-0520":["The table of the categories gives us the quite natural direction for the table of the principles, because these are after all nothing other than rules of the objective use of the former. All principles of the pure understanding are accordingly",["- Grundsätze = principles (OCR \"Grundfäße\" corrected). Anweisung = direction. Gebrauch = use. Verstand = understanding."]],"krv-b-0521":["Axioms\nof\nIntuition.",["- The numeral \"45\" is a page or line number and is omitted. Axiomen = axioms; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-b-0522":["Anticipations Analogies",[]],"krv-b-0523":["of of",[]],"krv-b-0524":["Perception. Experience.",["- The stray \"y\" is an OCR artifact and is omitted. Wahrnehmung = perception; Erfahrung = experience."]],"krv-b-0525":["Postulates\nof\nempirical thinking\nin general.",[]],"krv-b-0526":["These terms I have chosen with caution, in order not to leave unnoticed the differences with regard to evidence and to exercise. But it will soon be shown that, as concerns both the evidence and the determination of appearances a priori in accordance with the categories of magnitude and of quality (if one attends merely to the form of the latter), the principles of the same distinguish themselves notably from the two remaining ones in that the former are capable of an intuitive, but the latter of a merely discursive, though on both sides of a complete certainty. I shall therefore call the former the mathematical, the latter the dynamical principles. But one will well notice that here I have as little the principles of mathematics in the one case, as the principles of general (physical) dynamics in the other, but only those of the pure understanding in relation to inner sense (without distinction of the representations given therein) before my eyes, through which then those altogether receive their possibility. I name them therefore more in consideration of the application than on account of their content, and go on now to the weighing of the same in the very same order as they are represented in the table.",["- Benennungen = terms (glossary: Benennung). Ausübung = exercise; the stray \"i\" after it and the mark after \"nennen\" are OCR remnants of footnote signs, omitted.","- \"die Grundsätze derselben\": \"derselben\" is left as ambiguous as the German (\"of the same\"). \"der letzteren\" = of the latter (quality).","- Evidenz = evidence; Ansehung = regard; Erwägung = weighing (cf. Betrachtung = consideration, used in \"in Betracht\"); \"Inhalts\" = content; \"vorgestellt werden\" = are represented (Vorstellung)."]],"krv-b-0527":["1.\nAxioms of Intuition.",[]],"krv-b-0528":["The principle of the same is: All intuitions are extensive magnitudes.",["- Das Princip = principle (Latinate Princip, as against Grundsatz, also \"principle\"). The OCR mark \"!)\" is a footnote sign and is omitted. Größen = magnitudes (OCR \"Großen\" corrected)."]],"krv-b-0529":["Proof.",["- The OCR mark after \"Beweis\" is a footnote sign and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0530":["All appearances contain, as to form, an intuition in space and time, which lies at the ground of them all a priori. They can therefore not otherwise be apprehended, i.e. taken up into empirical conscious-",["- Breaks off at the line end mid-word (\"Bewußt-\" / \"sein\"). apprehendirt = apprehended."]],"krv-b-0531":["All combination (*conjunctio*) is either composition (*compositio*) or connection (*nexus*). The former is the synthesis of the manifold that does not necessarily belong to one another, as e.g. the two triangles into which a square is divided by the diagonal do not in themselves necessarily belong to one another; and of the same kind is the synthesis of the homogeneous in everything that can be considered mathematically (which synthesis can in turn be divided into that of the",["- Footnote; breaks off and is continued at krv-b-0550. Verbindung = combination (here glossed by Kant as *conjunctio*); Verknüpfung = connection; Zusammensetzung = composition; Gleichartigen = homogeneous.","- \"für sich\" rendered \"in themselves\" (could also be \"by themselves\")."]],"krv-b-0532":["ness be taken up, than through the synthesis of the manifold,",["- Begins with the word-ending \"-sein\" of \"Bewußtsein\" (from the preceding line), rendered \"-ness\"."]],"krv-b-0533":["through which the representations of a determinate space or time are generated",["- bestimmt = determinate; erzeugt = generated."]],"krv-b-0534":["i.e. through the composition of the homogeneous and the conscious-",["- Breaks mid-word (\"Be-\" / \"wußtsein\")."]],"krv-b-0535":["ness of the synthetic unity of this manifold (homogeneous). Now the consciousness of the homogeneous manifold in intu-",["- Line number \"5\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0536":["ition in general, insofar as through it the representation of an object first",["- Object = object."]],"krv-b-0537":["becomes possible, is the concept of a magnitude (*quanti*). Thus even the percep-",["- Größe = magnitude; \"quanti\" is Kant's Latin. The OCR \"moglich\" is corrected to \"möglich\"."]],"krv-b-0538":["tion of an object as appearance is possible only through the same synthetic",[]],"krv-b-0539":["unity of the manifold of the given sensible intuition, through which the unity of the composition of the homogeneous manifold",["- The OCR \"mög» lich\" is corrected to \"möglich\"; line number \"10\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0540":["is thought in the concept of a magnitude; i.e. the appearances",[]],"krv-b-0541":["are all magnitudes, and indeed extensive magnitudes, because as intu-",[]],"krv-b-0542":["itions in space or time they must be represented through the same synthesis",[]],"krv-b-0543":["as that through which space and time in general are determined. An extensive magnitude I call that in which the representation",["- The OCR \"215\" is a line number (and a stray digit) and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0544":["of the parts makes the representation of the whole possible (and therefore necessar-",["- Breaks mid-word (\"nothwen-\" / \"dig\"). Theile = parts."]],"krv-b-0545":["ily precedes this). I can represent to myself no line, however small it may be,",["- \"vor dieser\" = \"this\" (dative feminine) left as a pronoun, not resolved to a noun."]],"krv-b-0546":["without drawing it in thought, i.e. from a point to generate all",[]],"krv-b-0547":["the parts little by little and thereby first of all to sketch this intuition. It is just so also with every, even the smallest, time be-",["- \"nach und nach\" = little by little. verzeichnen = sketch. \"Es ist ... bewandt\" is an idiom (\"it is so with\"); the word breaks across lines (\"be-\" / \"wandt\"), which is why \"be-\" is left untranslated here and the idiom is rendered in the next passage's fragment."]],"krv-b-0548":["wandt. I think in it only the successive progression from one moment to the other, where through all the parts of time and their addition finally a determinate magnitude of time is generated. Since the mere intuition",["- \"wandt\" completes \"bewandt\" from the preceding line. Fortgang = progression; Hinzuthun = addition; Zeitgröße = magnitude of time."]],"krv-b-0549":["in all appearances is either space or time, every appearance as intuition is an extensive magnitude, in that it can be cognized only through successive synthesis (from part to part) in the apprehension. All appearances are accordingly already intuited as aggregates",["- The OCR \"voder die Heit\" is corrected to \"oder die Zeit\"; line number \"28\" omitted. erkannt = cognized. The sentence continues after the footnote (see krv-b-0554)."]],"krv-b-0550":["aggregation and coalition, of which the former is directed to",["- Continuation of the footnote begun at krv-b-0531 (\"eingetheilt werden kann\" = can be divided). Aggregation and Coalition are German spellings of the Latinate words."]],"krv-b-0551":["extensive, the other to intensive magnitudes). The second combination (nexus) is the synthesis of the manifold insofar as it necessarily belongs to one another, as e.g. the accident to some substance, or the",["- Verbindung = combination; the text's \"zu einander\" is kept in the singular-plural mismatch of the German (\"it ... to one another\"). Line number \"30\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0552":["effect to the cause —, consequently also as heterogeneous, yet a priori com-",["- The OCR \"vers / hunden\" is corrected to \"ver- / bunden\"; the break falls mid-word. Wirkung = effect; mithin = consequently."]],"krv-b-0553":["bined is represented; which combination, because it is not arbitrary, I call dynamical for the reason that it concerns the combination of the existence of the manifold (which can in turn be divided into the physical, of the appearances among one another, and meta-",["- Dasein = existence; willkürlich = arbitrary. The OCR \"bezs trifft\" is corrected to \"betrifft\" and the \"ver- / bunden\" break is as in the preceding line."]],"krv-b-0554":["physical, their combination in the faculty of cognition a priori, can be divided.) (multitude of previously given parts) intuited, which is precisely not the case with every kind of magnitudes, but only with those that are represented and apprehended by us extensively as such.",["- The footnote ends at \"eingetheilt werden kann.)\"; the parenthesis \"(Menge vorhergegebener Theile)\" and \"angeschaut\" belong to the main sentence of krv-b-0549. Menge = multitude; Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition."]],"krv-b-0555":["Upon this successive synthesis of the productive imagination in the generation of shapes the mathematics of extension (geometry) is grounded, with its axioms, which express the conditions of sensible intuition a priori, under which alone the schema of a pure concept of outer appearance can come about: e.g. between two points only one straight line is possible; two straight lines enclose no space, etc. These are the axioms which properly concern only magnitudes (*quanta*) as such.",["- The OCR \"si\" is corrected to \"sich\". Einbildungskraft = imagination; Ausdehnung = extension; Gestalten = shapes; \"zu Stande kommen\" = come about; \"Größen (quanta)\" = Kant's Latin."]],"krv-b-0556":["But as concerns magnitude (*quantitas*), i.e. the answer to the question: how large something is, there are in regard to it, although various of these propositions are synthetic and immediately certain (*indemonstrabilia*), nevertheless in the proper sense no axioms. For that equals added to equals or subtracted from them yield an equal are analytic propositions, since I am immediately conscious of the identity of the one generation of magnitude with the other; but axioms are supposed to be synthetic propositions a priori. On the other hand the evident propositions of the relation of number are indeed synthetic, but not universal, like those of geometry, and just for that reason also not axioms, but can be called number formulas. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytic proposition. For I think the number 12 neither in the representation of 7, nor of 5, nor in the representation of the composition of both (that I ought to think this in the addition of both is not under discussion here; for with the analytic proposition the question is only whether I actually think the predicate in the representation of the subject). But although it is synthetic, it is nevertheless only a single proposition. Insofar as here merely the synthesis of the homogeneous (of the units) is looked to, the synthesis can here take place only in a single way, although the use of these numbers is afterwards universal. If I say: through three lines, two of which taken together are greater than the third, a triangle can be drawn: then I have here the mere function of the productive imagination, which can draw the lines greater and smaller, and likewise let them meet at all sorts of arbitrary angles. On the other hand the number 7 is possible only in a single way, and also the number 12, which is generated through the synthesis of the former with 5. Such propositions one must therefore not call axioms (for otherwise there would be infinitely many of them), but number formulas.",["- Größe (quantitas) vs. Größen (quanta): both \"magnitude\", with Kant's Latin kept. The OCR \"Ariomen\" is corrected to \"Axiomen\". Satz = proposition throughout.","- \"im eigentlichen Verstande\" = \"in the proper sense\" (Verstand here not \"understanding\"). Zahlverhältniß = relation of number; Zahlformeln = number formulas; Zusammensetzung = composition; Gleichartigen = homogeneous.","- \"diese\" in \"ob ich das Prädicat...\" and \"dieser Zahlen\": \"this\" and \"these\" follow the German; in \"daß ich diese in der Addition beider denken solle\", \"diese\" is rendered \"this\" (referring to the number 12; the German pronoun is feminine).","- \"ist ... nur auf eine einzige Art möglich\" is left as in the German, with \"und auch die Zahl 12\" lacking its own verb."]],"krv-b-0557":["This transcendental principle of the mathematics of appearances gives our cognition *a priori* great extension. For it alone is what makes pure mathematics applicable in its whole precision to objects of experience, which without this principle might not be so self-evident, indeed has also occasioned many a contradiction. Appearances are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through the pure (of space and of time); what geometry says of the latter, therefore, is valid also without objection of the former, and the evasions, as if objects of the senses may not be in accordance with the rules of construction in space (e.g., of the infinite divisibility of lines or angles), must fall away. For thereby one denies objective validity to space and with it at the same time to all of mathematics, and no longer knows why and how far it is to be applied to appearances. The synthesis of spaces and times, as the essential form of all intuition, is that which at the same time makes possible the apprehension of the appearance, hence every outer experience, consequently also all cognition of the objects of it, and what mathematics in its pure use proves of the former, that also necessarily holds of the latter. All objections against this are only chicaneries of a falsely taught reason, which erroneously intends to detach the objects of the senses from the formal condition of our sensibility and represents them, although they are mere appearances, as objects in themselves, given to the understanding; in which case, to be sure, nothing at all could be cognized synthetically of them *a priori*, hence also not through pure concepts of space, and the science that determines these, namely geometry, would itself not be possible.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstände = objects; Erscheinungen = appearances; Gültigkeit = validity; Belehrung (falsch belehrten) = \"taught\".","- \"ohne Widerrede\" rendered \"without objection\" (distinct from Widerspruch = contradiction, used later in the sentence).","- \"dürfen nicht\" is ambiguous in the German (\"need not\" / \"may not\"); \"may not\" kept for the same ambiguity.","- \"welches ... hat\" has no clear antecedent; kept as in German. Hyphenation of OCR line breaks silently removed."]],"krv-b-0558":["Anticipations of Perception.",["- Wahrnehmung = perception; Anticipationen kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-0559":["The principle of them is: In all appearances the real, which is an object of sensation, has intensive magnitude, i.e., a degree.",["- A stray footnote mark after \"Grad.\" in the OCR is omitted; no footnote text is given in this passage.","- Das Reale = \"the real\"; Größe = magnitude; Grad = degree; Empfindung = sensation."]],"krv-b-0560":["Proof.",["- The OCR's closing parenthesis after \"Beweis\" is dropped as an artifact."]],"krv-b-0561":["Perception is empirical consciousness, i.e., one in which there is at the same time sensation. Appearances, as objects of perception, are not pure (merely formal) intuitions, like space and time (for these cannot be perceived at all in themselves). They contain, therefore, beyond the intuition, also the matters for some object in general (through which something existing in space or time is represented), i.e., the real of sensation as a merely subjective representation, of which one can only become conscious that the subject is affected, and which one relates to an object in general. Now from empirical consciousness to pure consciousness a stepwise alteration is possible, since the real of the former wholly vanishes, and a merely formal consciousness (*a priori*) of the manifold in space and time remains: therefore also a synthesis of the generation of the magnitude of a sensation from its beginning, the pure intuition = 0, up to any desired magnitude of it. Now since sensation in itself is not an objective representation at all, and in it neither the intuition of space nor that of time is encountered, there will accrue to it, to be sure, no extensive magnitude, but yet a magnitude (and indeed through the apprehension of it, in which the empirical consciousness can grow in a certain time from nothing = 0 up to its given measure), therefore an intensive magnitude, corresponding to which there must be attributed to all objects of perception, insofar as these contain sensation, intensive magnitude, i.e., a degree of influence on the sense.",["- Bewußtsein = consciousness; Anschauung = intuition; Materien = \"matters\" (plural of Materie); Object / Gegenstand both = \"object\" (Object in \"irgend einem Objecte\", \"auf ein Object\", \"allen Objecten\").","- Veränderung = alteration; stufenartig = \"stepwise\"; Größenerzeugung = \"generation of the magnitude\"; Einfluß = influence; Sinn = sense (n.).","- OCR \"de Wahrnehmung\" corrected to \"der Wahrnehmung\". \"welcher correspondirend ...\" is kept in the German construction.","- The phrase \"in sich\" at the end of the long clause stands after \"bezieht\"; kept in place (the \"in\" of \"in sich\" is not rendered separately in English)."]],"krv-b-0562":["One can call all cognition through which I can cognize and determine *a priori* that which belongs to empirical cognition an anticipation, and without doubt that is the meaning in which Epicurus used his expression πρόληψις. But since there is something in appearances that is never cognized *a priori*, and which therefore also constitutes the proper difference of the empirical from cognition *a priori*, namely sensation (as matter of perception), it follows that this it is, properly, that cannot be anticipated at all. so: In all appearances sensation, and the real that corresponds to it in the object, (*realitas phaenomenon*) has an intensive magnitude, i.e., a degree. By contrast, we could call the pure determinations in space and time, with regard to shape as well as magnitude, anticipations of appearances, because they represent *a priori* that which may always be given *a posteriori* in experience. Supposing, however, that something is nevertheless found that can be cognized *a priori* in every sensation as sensation in general (without a particular one being given): then this would deserve to be called anticipation in the eminent sense, because it seems strange to anticipate experience in that which concerns precisely its matter, which one can draw only from it. And so it is here in fact.",["- The OCR reads \"rpornbıs\"; corrected to πρόληψις (Epicurus's term, in Greek). Ausdruck = expression; Bedeutung = meaning.","- \"so: In allen Erscheinungen ...\" follows a full stop with lowercase \"so:\"; the text looks corrupt or cut off here (a clause introducing the principle seems missing). Translated as it stands.","- \"im ausnehmenden Verstande\" = \"in the eminent sense\" (Verstande here not the faculty); Materie = matter; Bestimmungen = determinations; Gestalt = shape.","- \"daß diese es eigentlich sei\" — \"diese\" refers back to Empfindung; \"eigentlich\" = \"properly\" (could also be read \"actually\")."]],"krv-b-0563":["Apprehension merely by means of sensation fills only an instant (if, that is, I do not take into consideration the succession of many sensations). As something in the appearance the apprehension of which is not a successive synthesis that proceeds from parts to the whole representation, it has, therefore, no extensive magnitude: the lack of sensation in the same instant would represent this as empty, hence = 0. Now what corresponds to sensation in empirical intuition is reality (*realitas phaenomenon*), what corresponds to the lack of it, negation = 0. Now, however, every sensation is capable of a diminution, so that it can decrease and thus gradually vanish. Hence between reality in the appearance and negation there is a continuous interconnection of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference of which from one another is always smaller than the difference between the given one and zero or the complete negation. That is: the real in the appearance always has a magnitude, which however is not encountered in apprehension, since this occurs by means of mere sensation in an instant and not through successive synthesis of many sensations, and thus does not proceed from the parts to the whole; it has, therefore, to be sure a magnitude, but not an extensive one.",["- Augenblick = \"instant\" (kept distinct from Moment, \"moment\", in the next passage); Zusammenhang = interconnection; Theile = parts; Realität = reality; Negation = negation; Zero kept as \"zero\".","- \"sie\" (first sentence of second sentence) and \"diesen\" (\"this\" as empty) are left as in German; \"diesen\" most naturally refers to Augenblick.","- OCR \"hat sie also. keine\" corrected to \"hat sie also keine\"."]],"krv-b-0564":["Now I call that magnitude which is apprehended only as unity, and in which plurality can be represented only through approximation to negation = 0, intensive magnitude. Thus every reality in the appearance has intensive magnitude, i.e., a degree. If one considers this reality as cause (whether of the sensation, or of another reality in the appearance, e.g., an alteration), then one calls the degree of reality as cause a moment, e.g., the moment of gravity, and indeed for the reason that the degree designates only the magnitude the apprehension of which is not successive, but instantaneous. But I touch on this here only in passing, for with causality I do not yet have to do for now.",["- OCR \"Mfo\" corrected to \"Also\" (\"Thus\"). Einheit = unity; Vielheit = \"plurality\"; Ursache = cause; Veränderung = alteration; Causalität = causality.","- Moment = \"moment\" (Augenblick, \"instant\", is the root of \"augenblicklich\" rendered \"instantaneous\")."]],"krv-b-0565":["Thus every sensation, hence also every reality in the appearance, however small it may be, has a degree, i.e., an intensive magnitude, which can still always be diminished, and between reality and negation there is a continuous interconnection of possible realities and possible smaller perceptions. Every color, e.g., the red, has a degree, which, however small it may be, is never the smallest, and so it is with warmth, the moment of gravity, etc., everywhere.",["- Line numbers and the \"5|\" marker in the OCR are omitted; \"be— wandt\" restored to \"bewandt\" and rendered within \"so ist es ... überall bewandt\" as \"so it is ... everywhere\".","- Wärme = warmth; Wahrnehmungen = perceptions; Moment der Schwere = \"moment of gravity\" (Schwere kept literal as \"gravity\")."]],"krv-b-0566":["The property of magnitudes in accordance with which no part of them is the smallest possible (no part is simple) is called the continuity of them. Space and time are *quanta continua*, because no part of them can be given without enclosing it between boundaries (points and instants), hence only in such a way that this part itself is again a space or a time. Space therefore consists only of spaces, time of times. Points and instants are only boundaries, i.e., mere places of their limitation; but places always presuppose those intuitions that they are to limit or determine, and from mere places, as from components that could be given prior to space or time, neither space nor time can be composed. Magnitudes of this sort can also be called flowing, because the synthesis (of the productive imagination) in their generation is a progression in time, the continuity of which one is accustomed especially to designate by the expression of flowing (flowing away).",["- Größe = magnitude; Grenze = boundary; Einschränkung = limitation (not Schranke); Stellen = \"places\" (Ort in glossary = place).","- Anschauung = intuition; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Fortgang = progression; Ausdruck = expression; Bestandtheile = components.","- OCR \"eins 14 zuschließen\" read as \"einzuschließen\"; \"solen\" read as \"sollen\"; \"als zu aus\" read as \"als aus\" (OCR slip, \"zu\" dropped).","- \"deren Continuität\": \"deren\" could refer to Zeit or Synthesis/Fortgang; the ambiguity is preserved by the English \"the continuity of which.\""]],"krv-b-0567":["All appearances whatsoever are accordingly continuous magnitudes, whether as extensive ones in accordance with their intuition, or as intensive ones in accordance with mere perception (sensation and hence reality). If the synthesis of the manifold of appearance is interrupted, then this is an aggregate of many appearances (and not properly appearance as a quantum), which is generated not by the mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a certain way, but by the repetition of a synthesis constantly ceasing. If I call 13 thalers a quantum of money, I name it correctly insofar as I understand by it the content of a mark of fine silver, which is indeed a continuous magnitude, in which no part is the smallest, but every part could constitute a coin, which would always contain matter for still smaller ones. But if by that term I understand 13 round thalers, as so many coins (their silver content may be whatever it will), then I name it improperly through a quantum of thalers, but must call it an aggregate, i.e., a number of coins. Since, then, unity must nevertheless lie at the ground of all number, the appearance, as unity, is a quantum, and as such always a continuum.",["- Erscheinung = appearance; Wahrnehmung = perception; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Art = way; Benennung = term; Zahl = number; Grunde liegen = lie at the ground.","- \"13 Thaler\" is OCR for \"1 3 Thaler\"? Text read as printed (\"13\"); the original may read differently (doubtful reading). \"sein Silber\" (OCR \"sein\") read as \"fein\".","- \"Gehalt\" rendered \"content\" (not Inhalt); \"Einheit\" = unity.","- \"so benenne ich es unschicklich durch ein Quantum von Thalern, sondern muß…\" preserved as printed, with its awkward construction (the \"nicht\"/\"sondern\" is not smoothed)."]],"krv-b-0568":["If now all appearances, considered both extensively and intensively, are continuous magnitudes: then the proposition that all alteration (transition of a thing from one state into another) is also continuous could easily and with mathematical evidence be proved here, if the causality of an alteration in general did not lie entirely outside the boundaries of a transcendental philosophy and presuppose empirical principles. For that a cause is possible that alters the state of things, i.e., determines them to the opposite of a certain given state, of this the understanding gives us no disclosure at all a priori, not merely because it does not have insight into the possibility of it (for this insight is lacking to us in several cognitions *a priori*), but because alterability concerns only certain determinations of appearances, which experience alone can teach, while their cause is to be encountered in the unalterable. But since we have nothing before us here that we can make use of except the pure fundamental concepts of all possible experience, among which there must be absolutely nothing empirical: so we cannot, without violating the unity of the system, anticipate general natural science, which is built upon certain fundamental experiences.",["- Veränderung = alteration; Zustand = state; Grenzen = boundaries; Verstand = understanding; Erkenntnis = cognition; Bestimmung = determination; Erfahrung = experience; Grundbegriffe = fundamental concepts.","- \"lay … and presuppose\" : German \"läge … und … voraussetzte\" are both subjunctive; English \"presupposed\" intended (read \"lay … and presupposed\"): see note on parallelism. Read as printed; \"con— tinuirlich\" OCR hyphenation joined.","- \"Transscendental-Philosophie\": Philosophie rendered \"philosophy\" (Weltweisheit in glossary is a different word).","- \"Möglichkeit … einsieht\": einsehen = \"have insight into\"; Einsicht = \"insight.\"","- \"verlegen\" (violating) read as in the text; \"vor uns haben\" kept literal."]],"krv-b-0569":["Nevertheless we do not lack proofs of the great influence that this principle of ours has in anticipating perceptions, and even in supplementing their lack to the extent that it bolts the door against all false inferences that might be drawn from it.",["- Beweisthümer = proofs (Beweis in glossary); Grundsatz = principle; Wahrnehmung = perception; Schlüsse = inferences; Mangel = lack.","- \"daraus\" and \"deren\" have ambiguous antecedents; preserved.","- \"den Riegel vorschiebt\" rendered literally as an idiom (\"bolts the door\")."]],"krv-b-0570":["If all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation an infinite succession of ever lesser degrees takes place, and nevertheless every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations: then no perception, hence also no experience, is possible that would prove a total lack of everything real in the appearance, whether immediately or mediately (by whatever detour in inferring one may wish), i.e., from experience a proof of empty space or an empty time can never be drawn. For the total lack of the real in sensible intuition can, first, not itself be perceived; second, it can be concluded from no single appearance and from the difference of the degree of its reality, or may also never be assumed for the explanation of the same. For even if the whole intuition of a determinate space or time is through and through real, i.e., no part of it is empty: then nevertheless, because every reality has its degree, which, with the extensive magnitude of the appearance unaltered, can decrease through infinite steps down to nothing (the empty), there must be infinitely different degrees with which space or time is filled, and the intensive magnitude in different appearances can be smaller or greater, although the extensive magnitude of the intuition is equal.",["- Realität = reality; Grad = degree; Stufenfolge = succession of steps (Abfolge not used); Negation = negation; Receptivität = receptivity; sinnlich = sensible; Anschauung = intuition; Erklärung = explanation.","- Running head \"156 Elementarlehre. II. Theil…\" and OCR debris (al, f, u, ders) removed; \"wei- eben Umschweif\" read as \"weitläufigen\"? no: text reads \"welchen Umschweif\" (OCR \"wei-eben\") i.e., \"welchen\".","- \"schließen\" (Schließen) = inferring; \"folgern\" = conclude; \"gefolgert\" rendered \"concluded.\"","- \"Mangel\" = lack; \"mittelbar/unmittelbar\" = mediately/immediately.","- \"dem\" in \"zwischen dem und der Negation\" refers to Grad; \"ders-selben\" = of the same (reality or appearance, ambiguous).","- \"durch und durch real\" kept literal."]],"krv-b-0571":["We want to give an example of this. Almost all teachers of nature, since",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage.","- Naturlehrer = teachers of nature (Lehre = doctrine in glossary)."]],"krv-b-0572":["they perceive a great difference in the quantity of matter of different kinds under equal volume (partly through the moment of gravity or weight, partly through the moment of resistance against other moved matters), conclude from this unanimously: this volume (extensive magnitude of the appearance) must be empty in all matters, although in different measure. But who of these for the most part mathematical and mechanical investigators of nature would ever have let it occur to himself that they founded this their inference solely on a metaphysical presupposition, which they nevertheless pretend so much to avoid, in that they assume that the real in space (I do not wish to call it here impenetrability or weight, because these are empirical concepts) is everywhere one and the same and can differ only in accordance with the extensive magnitude, i.e., the multitude. To this presupposition, for which they could have had no ground in experience, and which is therefore merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental proof, which is indeed not meant to explain the difference in the filling of spaces, but yet entirely cancels the supposed necessity of that presupposition of being able to explain the said difference in no other way than through empty spaces to be assumed, and has the merit of at least putting the understanding at liberty to think this diversity also in another way, if the explanation of nature should make some hypothesis necessary for this. For there we see that, although equal spaces may be perfectly filled by different matters, so that in none of those there is a point",["- Naturforscher = investigators of nature; Schluß = inference; Voraussetzung = presupposition; Menge = multitude; Beweis = proof; Art = way; Erklärung = explanation; Verstand = understanding.","- \"Malerien\" is OCR for \"Materien\" (matters); \"ertenfiven\" = \"extensiven\"; \"ia\" is OCR debris, dropped.","- \"mag … wolle\"/\"obzwar in verschiedenem Maße\" kept literal; \"Maße\" = measure.","- \"Materie\" in the plural rendered \"matters\" for consistency.","- \"dazu\" = \"for which\"; \"gedachten Unterschied\" = \"the said difference.\"","- Sentence breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0573":["in which their presence would not be encountered, yet each",["- Fragment continues the preceding sentence; \"ihre Gegenwart\" refers back to the matters (plural \"Materien\"), while \"jedes Reale\" is singular."]],"krv-b-0574":["real, with the same quality, has its degree (of resistance or of weight),",["- \"Reale\" is the substantivized adjective; the break \"Wie-/gens\" is joined in the next fragment (\"Wiegens\" = \"Wägens,\" weighing; \"Wiegens\" read as printed).","- \"ihren Grad\": \"ihren\" (feminine/plural) after \"jedes Reale\" preserved as printed."]],"krv-b-0575":["which, without diminution of the extensive magnitude or multitude, can be infinitely smaller before it passes into the empty and vanishes.",["- Joins \"Wie-\" + \"gens\" = \"weighing\" in the preceding fragment (rendered \"weight\" there for the OCR-joined reading; Wiegen = weighing).","- \"ihn Unendliche\" read as \"ins Unendliche\"; \"verschwin-dei\" = \"verschwindet.\" \"sie\" refers to the real/reality; preserved."]],"krv-b-0576":["Thus an extension that fills a space, e.g., warmth,",["- \"Ausspannung\" = extension (not Ausdehnung, which the glossary renders \"extension\"; here the same English word stands for two German words).","- \"dei\" is OCR debris from the previous line's break."]],"krv-b-0577":["and in the same way every other reality (in appearance), without leaving",["- Realität = reality; Erscheinung = appearance. \"im / mindesten\" continues in the next fragment."]],"krv-b-0578":["in the least the smallest part of this space empty, can decrease in its degrees",["- \"Gra-/den\" = \"Graden\"; completed across fragments as printed (\"in ihren Graden\"), here \"degrees\" without completing beyond the text."]],"krv-b-0579":["infinitely and none the less fill the space with these smaller degrees just as well as another appearance",["- \"to\" is OCR debris, dropped."]],"krv-b-0580":["with greater ones. My intention here is by no means to assert that this",["- Absicht = intention (glossary also \"aim\"); behaupten = assert.","- \"dieses\" refers to what precedes; preserved."]],"krv-b-0581":["really is so constituted with the diversity of matters in accordance with their specific gravity,",["- \"bewandt sei\" rendered \"is so constituted\" (literally \"is so situated\"); wirklich = really (Wirklichkeit = actuality; reality).","- \"nach\" = \"in accordance with.\""]],"krv-b-0582":["but only to demonstrate from a principle of pure understanding:",["- Grundsatz = principle; rein = pure; Verstand = understanding. \"Ver-/standes\" joined across fragments."]],"krv-b-0583":["that the nature of our perceptions makes possible such a way of explanation, and that one falsely takes the real of the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"Er-\" = \"Erscheinung\"; the word is cut off, not completed).","- \"Erklärungsart\" = \"way of explanation\" (Art = way). Wahrnehmungen = perceptions; \"Reale\" is the substantivized adjective."]],"krv-b-0584":["…appearance as equal in degree and as different only in aggregation and its extensive magnitude, and even supposedly asserts this through a principle of the understanding a priori. | Nevertheless this anticipation of perception always has something striking about it for an investigator accustomed to transcendental reflection and thereby become cautious, and arouses some misgiving over how the understanding can anticipate such a synthetic proposition as that of the degree of everything real in appearances, and | hence of the possibility of the inner difference of sensation itself, if one abstracts from its empirical quality; and thus it is still a question not unworthy of solution: how the understanding can pronounce synthetically a priori about appearances and anticipate them even in that which is properly and merely empirical, namely | what concerns sensation.\n\nThe quality of sensation is at all times merely empirical and cannot be represented a priori at all (e.g., colors, taste, etc.). But",["- The passage begins mid-word (\"scheinung\", i.e., [Er]scheinung = appearance); rendered as \"appearance\" with an ellipsis. The OCR line numbers and stray letters were dropped, and the | signs kept.","- Anticipation = *Anticipation*; Überlegung = reflection; Grundsatz = principle; Satz = proposition; Verstand = understanding.","- \"vorgeblicher maßen\" = \"supposedly\" (could also be \"allegedly\").","- The last paragraph is the footnote text, set apart here. The note marker in the OCR (the \"0\") was dropped.","- \"was ... nämlich die Empfindung angeht\": the construction is kept loose in English (\"namely, what concerns sensation\")."]],"krv-b-0585":["the real that corresponds to sensations in general, in opposition to negation = 0, represents only something whose concept in itself contains a being, and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness in general. In inner sense, namely, empirical consciousness can be raised from 0 up to any greater degree, so that just the same extensive magnitude of intuition (e.g., an illuminated surface) arouses as great a sensation as an aggregate of much else (less illuminated) taken together. One can therefore abstract entirely from the extensive magnitude of the appearance and yet represent in the mere sensation in one moment a synthesis of uniform increase from 0 up to the given empirical consciousness. All sensations are therefore, as such, to be sure given only a posteriori, but the property of them, that they have a degree, can be cognized a priori. It is remarkable that in magnitudes in general we can cognize a priori only a single quality, namely continuity, but in all quality (the real of appearances) nothing further a priori than the intensive quantity of it, namely that they have a degree; everything else remains left to experience.",["- \"Duantität\" is an OCR slip, corrected to Quantität = quantity. Stray line numbers dropped.","- Eigenschaft = property (the glossary's \"property\" is Beschaffenheit; Eigenschaft is not in the glossary).","- Das Reale = \"the real\"; Größe = magnitude; Qualität = quality; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Anschauung = intuition.","- \"Sein\" = \"being\", and \"Moment\" = \"moment\".","- \"derselben\" in \"die Eigenschaft derselben\" and \"intensive Quantität derselben\" refers back to the sensations and to quality; the English \"of them\" and \"of it\" keep this."]],"krv-b-0586":["Analogies of Experience.",["- The leading \"35\" is a page number and was dropped."]],"krv-b-0587":["The principle of these is: Experience is possible only through the representation of a necessary connection of perceptions.",["- \"mögliche!\" is an OCR slip for \"möglich.\"","- Verknüpfung = connection; Wahrnehmung = perception; Vorstellung = representation.","- \"derselben\" = \"of these\", referring to the analogies."]],"krv-b-0588":["Proof.(*)",["- The OCR shows a footnote marker (“)) here, presumably \"*)\". It is rendered as (*), and no footnote text is given in this passage."]],"krv-b-0589":["Experience is an empirical cognition, i.e., a cognition that determines an object through perceptions. It is therefore a synthesis of perceptions, which is itself not contained in perception but contains the synthetic unity of the manifold of perceptions in one consciousness, which constitutes what is essential to a cognition of the objects of the senses, i.e., of experience (not merely of intuition or sensation of the senses). Now in experience, to be sure, perceptions come to one another only in a contingent way, so that no necessity of their connection is evident, nor can be evident, from the perceptions themselves; because apprehension is only a putting-together of the manifold of empirical intuition, but no representation of the necessity of the combined existence of the appearances, which it puts together in space and time, is encountered in it. But since experience is a cognition of objects through perceptions, consequently the relation in the existence of the manifold is to be represented in it not as it is put together in time but as it is objectively in time, yet time itself cannot be perceived: so the determination of the existence of objects in time can occur only through their combination in time in general, hence only through concepts connecting a priori. Since these now always carry necessity along with them, experience is possible only through a representation of the necessary connection of perceptions.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; Object = object (Latinate form, same English as Gegenstand); bestimmen = determine; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Sinne = senses.","- Stray OCR signs (\"2\", \"ə:\") were dropped.","- \"zufälliger Weise\" = \"in a contingent way\" (zufällig = contingent). Apprehension = *Apprehension*; Zusammenstellung = putting-together.","- Dasein and Existenz are both \"existence\" (Dasein is used in \"Dasein des Mannigfaltigen\", Existenz in \"verbundenen Existenz\").","- Verbindung = combination; \"verknüpfende Begriffe\" = \"concepts connecting a priori\" (a priori could attach to \"connecting\" or to the preceding \"through\"). Verhältniß = relation.","- \"in derselben\" (in the clause beginning \"weil Apprehension nur…\") is rendered \"in it\"; its antecedent is Apprehension, and the German is left as loose as it is."]],"krv-b-0590":["The three *modi* of time are persistence, sequence, and simultaneity. Hence three rules of all temporal relations of appearances, in accordance with which the existence of each can be determined with regard to the unity of all time, will precede all experience and first make it possible.",["- *modi* is Latin in the original. Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Folge = sequence; Zugleichsein = simultaneity (the line-break hyphen was removed).","- \"jeder ihr Dasein\": \"jeder\" is a dative and could be read as \"each [appearance]\" or \"each [rule]\"; \"ihr\" = of the appearances. The English \"the existence of each\" keeps the ambiguity.","- Zeitverhältnisse = temporal relations; Erscheinung = appearance; Einheit = unity."]],"krv-b-0591":["The general principle of all three analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception with regard to all possible empirical consciousness (of perception) at every time, consequently, since that lies at the ground a priori, on the synthetic unity of all appearances according to their relation in time. For original apperception relates to inner sense (the sum total of all representations), and indeed a priori to the form of it, i.e., the relation of the manifold empirical consciousness in time. In original apperception, now, all this manifold is to be united according to its temporal relations; for this is what the transcendental unity of it a priori says, under which stands everything that is to belong to my (i.e., my single) cognition, hence can become an object for me. This synthetic unity in the temporal relation of all perceptions, which is determined a priori, is therefore the law: that all empirical determinations of time must stand under rules of the general determination of time; and the analogies of experience, with which we now want to deal, must be rules of this sort.",["- \"Zeitvestimmungen\" is an OCR slip for Zeitbestimmungen = determinations of time. Apperception = *Apperception*; Inbegriff = sum total; Gegenstand = object.","- \"a priori\" in \"die transscendentale Einheit derselben a priori\" could attach to \"Einheit\" or to \"sagt\"; the English leaves it in the same position.","- \"meinem (d. i. meinem einigen)\": \"einigen\" is rendered \"single\" (the OCR may have altered the reading).","- \"zum Grunde liegt\" = \"lies at the ground\"; Gesetz = law; Regel = rule."]],"krv-b-0592":["These principles have this particularity, that they consider not the appearances and the synthesis of their empirical intuition but merely the existence and their relation to one another with regard to this their existence. Now the way something is apprehended in appearance can be determined a priori in such a way that the rule of its synthesis can at the same time give this intuition a priori in every present empirical example, i.e., bring it about from this. But the existence of appearances cannot be cognized a priori; and even if we could arrive by this path at inferring to some existence, we would still not cognize it determinately, i.e., be able to anticipate that through which its empirical intuition would differ from others.",["- \"das Besondere\" = \"particularity\" (besonder = particular). Erwägen = consider (not in the glossary).","- Art = way; Dasein = existence; erkennen = cognize; anticipiren = anticipate; schließen = infer.","- \"sie daraus zu Stande bringen kann\": \"sie\" (the intuition) and \"daraus\" (\"from this\", i.e., from the rule) are left as in the German.","- \"bestimmt erkennen\" = \"cognize determinately\"."]],"krv-b-0593":["The previous two principles, which I called the mathematical, in view of the fact that they entitled one to apply mathematics to appearances, went to appearances according to their mere possibility and taught how they could be generated, both according to their intuition and according to the real of their perception, according to rules of a mathematical synthesis; hence in the one as in the other the numerical magnitudes and with them the determination of the appearance as magnitude can be used. Thus, e.g., I will be able to compose the degree of the sensations of sunlight from about 200,000 illuminations by the moon and give it determined a priori, i.e., construct it. Hence we can call the former principles *constitutive*.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"wer: den\" = werden; \"gez braucht\" = gebraucht.","- \"die mathematische\" is singular in the German (taken as the adjective used as a noun) and is rendered \"the mathematical\".","- Zahlgrößen = \"numerical magnitudes\" (Zahl = number, Größe = magnitude). *constitutive* is a Latinate word in roman type in the original and is italicized.","- Grundsätze = principles; Wahrnehmung = perception; Synthesis = synthesis; construiren = construct; Möglichkeit = possibility.","- \"ihrer Anschauung als dem Realen ihrer Wahrnehmung nach\": the repeated \"nach\" is kept as \"according to ... according to\"."]],"krv-b-0594":["It must be quite otherwise with those that are to bring the existence of appearances under rules a priori. For since this cannot be constructed, they will go only to the relation of existence and be able to yield no other than merely regulative principles. Thus neither axioms nor anticipations are to be thought of here; rather, if a perception is given to us in a time-relation to others (though indeterminate ones), it will not be possible to say a priori: which other and how great a perception, but only how it is necessarily combined with that one, as to existence, in this *modus* of time. In philosophy analogies signify something very different from what they represent in mathematics. In the latter they are formulas that assert the equality of two relations of magnitude, and are always constitutive, so that if three members of the proportion are given, the fourth too is thereby given, i.e., can be constructed. In philosophy, however, analogy is the equality not of two quantitative but of qualitative relations, where from three given members I can cognize and give a priori only the relation to a fourth, but not this fourth member itself, though I do have a rule for seeking it in experience, and a mark for finding it there. An analogy of experience will therefore be only a rule according to which unity of experience is to arise from perceptions (not as perception itself, as empirical intuition in general), and as a principle of the objects (the appearances) is to hold not constitutively but merely regulatively. But precisely the same will hold also of the postulates of empirical thinking in general, which concern together the synthesis of mere intuition (the form of appearance), of perception (the matter of it), and of experience (the relation of these perceptions), namely that they are only regulative principles and are distinguished from the mathematical ones, which are constitutive, not indeed in certainty, which is established a priori in both, but still in the way of evidence, i.e., in the intuitive of the same (and hence also of demonstration).\n\n===NOTES\n- Reading \"fons dem\" is an OCR slip, corrected to \"sondern\"; \"Großenverhältnisse\" read as \"Größenverhältnisse\"; \"in bei— den\" read as \"in beiden\".\n- *Grundsatz* = principle (also *Satz* = principle in the glossary; not at issue here). *Dasein* = existence (*Existenz* also \"existence\" in other passages).\n- *Gegenstand* = object; *Merkmal* = mark; *Verhältniß* = relation (not in glossary; kept uniform); *Art* = way; *Anschauung* = intuition.\n- \"a priori\" is left in roman as in the German; in \"wie sie dem Dasein nach ... verbunden sei\" the phrase \"as to existence\" is kept in the German position.\n- \"(nicht wie Wahrnehmung selbst als empirische Anschauung überhaupt)\" is obscure in the German and kept as it stands; the antecedent of \"as\" is not resolved.\n- \"dem Intuitiven derselben\": \"derselben\" could refer to the evidence or to the postulates/principles; left ambiguous. \"mithin auch der Demonstration\" is kept in parentheses with \"des Intuitiven\" as its apparent dependent.\n- *modus* is Latin in the source; italicized.",[]],"krv-b-0595":["But what was remarked of all synthetic principles and must here be especially noted is this: that these analogies have their sole significance and validity not as principles of the transcendental but merely of the empirical use of the understanding, and consequently can also be proved only as such, that, consequently, the appearances must be subsumed not under the categories simpliciter but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which these principles are to be related were things in themselves, it would be quite impossible to cognize anything of them synthetically a priori. Now they are nothing but appearances, the complete cognition of which, in which all principles a priori must in the end always run out, is solely possible experience; consequently those principles can have nothing as their goal but merely the conditions of the unity of empirical cognition in the synthesis of appearances; but this is thought only in the schema of the pure concept of the understanding, of whose unity, as of a synthesis in general, the category contains the function not restricted by any sensible condition. Through these principles, therefore, we will be entitled to put appearances together only according to an analogy with the logical and universal unity of concepts, and will therefore, in the principle itself, indeed make use of the category, but in the execution (the application to appearances) will set the schema of it, as the key to its use, in its place, or rather, as a restricting condition, alongside the former under the name of a formula of it.\n\n===NOTES\n- \"Ver— standesgebrauchs\", \"Sche— male\", \"Syn— thesis\" etc.: line-break hyphenations rejoined.\n- *Verstand* = understanding; *Gegenstand* = object; *Ding an sich selbst* = thing in itself (themselves); *Bedeutung* = significance; *Gültigkeit* = validity.\n- \"schlechthin\" rendered \"*simpliciter*\" is not Kant's Latin; to stay literal it may instead read \"without qualification\" — I have used the Latin, which is a departure; read \"absolutely\" if preferred.\n- \"auf die alle Grundsätze a priori zuletzt doch immer auslaufen müssen\": \"in which\" in my rendering corresponds to the relative \"auf die\" (\"upon which\"); the relative clause attaches to \"Erkenntniß\", ambiguity kept.\n- \"diese aber\" (this, fem.) could refer to *Einheit* or to *Synthesis*; \"von deren Einheit\" has \"deren\" ambiguous between *Synthesis* and *Schema*/*Verstandesbegriff*. Left ambiguous.\n- \"jener vielmehr\": \"jener\" (the former/that one) is ambiguous between the category and the schema; \"des ersteren\" likewise. Left as in German.",[]],"krv-b-0596":["First Analogy.\nPrinciple of the persistence of substance.\n\n===NOTES\n- *Beharrlichkeit* = persistence; *Substanz* = substance.",[]],"krv-b-0597":["In all change of appearances substance persists, and the quantum of it is neither increased nor diminished in nature.“)\n\n===NOTES\n- The closing mark “) is an OCR remnant of a footnote or variant marker (the A-edition text is given in the following footnote); kept as it stands, not resolved.\n- *Wechsel* = change; *Quantum* is Latin-derived in the German, kept as *quantum*.",[]],"krv-b-0598":["Proof.“)\n\n===NOTES\n- *Beweis* = proof. The mark “) is an OCR remnant of a footnote marker; kept.",[]],"krv-b-0599":["All?) appearances are in time, in which as substratum (as persisting form of inner intuition) both being-simultaneous and sequence alone can be represented. Time, therefore, in which all change of appearances is to be thought, remains and does not change, because it is that in which being-successive or being-simultaneous can be represented only as determinations of it. Now time cannot be perceived for itself. Consequently in the objects of perception, i.e., the appearances, the substratum must be encountered that represents time in general, and in which all change or being-simultaneous can be perceived in apprehension through the relation of the appearances to it. But the substratum of everything real, i.e., of what belongs to the existence of things, is substance, in which everything that belongs to existence can be thought only as determination. Consequently what persists, in relation to which alone all time-relations of the appearances can be determined, is substance in the appearance, i.e., the real of it, which as substratum of all change always remains the same. Since this, therefore, cannot change in existence, its quantum in nature can also be neither increased nor diminished.\n\n===NOTES\n- \"Alle?)\" : \"?)\" is an OCR remnant of a footnote marker (probably \"Alle\"+footnote sign); kept as it stands.\n- *Zugleichsein* = being-simultaneous; *Nacheinandersein* = being-successive; *Folge* = sequence (glossary); *Zeitverhältnisse* = time-relations.\n- *Existenz* = existence and *Dasein* = existence, both rendered \"existence\" (\"zur Existenz der Dinge Gehörigen\" vs. \"zum Dasein gehört\").\n- *Gegenstand* = object; *Bestimmung* = determination; *Reale* = \"the real\"; *Substrat* = substratum (Latinate, same word as *Substratum* elsewhere).\n- \"Da diese also im Dasein nicht wechseln kann\": \"diese\" (fem.) refers back to *Substanz*; kept as \"this\".",[]],"krv-b-0600":[") Al; Principle of persistence.\n\n===NOTES\n- The opening \")\" and \"Al;\" are OCR remnants (probably \"A:\" marking the A-edition text, with a footnote marker); kept as they stand, not corrected.",[]],"krv-b-0601":["All appearances contain what persists (substance) as the object itself and what is changeable as its mere determination, i.e., a way in which the object exists.\n\n===NOTES\n- *Art* = way; *Gegenstand* = object; *existirt* = exists (*Existenz*).",[]],"krv-b-0602":["Our apprehension of the manifold of appearance is always successive and is therefore always changing. Through it alone we can therefore never determine whether this manifold, as object of experience, is simultaneous or follows in succession, unless something lies at its ground that always is, i.e., something abiding and persisting, of which all change and being-simultaneous are nothing but so many ways (*modi* of time) in which what persists exists. Only in what persists, therefore, are time-relations possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time), i.e., what persists is the substratum of the empirical representation of time itself, in which alone all time-determination is possible. Persistence expresses time in general as the constant correlate of all existence of appearances, of all change and of all concomitance. For change does not affect time itself, but only the appearances in time (just as being-simultaneous is not a *modus* of time itself, in which no parts at all are simultaneous, but all are successive). If one wanted to ascribe to time itself a sequence one after another, one would have to think yet another time in which this sequence would be possible. Only through what persists does existence in different parts of the time-series successively acquire a magnitude that one calls duration. For in mere sequence alone existence is always vanishing and beginning, and never has the least magnitude. Without this persisting, therefore, there is no time-relation. Now time in itself cannot be perceived; hence this persisting in the appearances is the substratum of all time-determination, and consequently also the condition of the possibility of all synthetic unity of perceptions, i.e., of experience, and in this persisting all existence and all change in time can be regarded only as a *modus* of the existence of what remains and persists. Thus in all appearances what persists is the object itself, i.e., substance (*phaenomenon*), but everything that changes or can change belongs only to the way in which this substance or substances exist, hence to their determinations.\n\n===NOTES\n- OCR slips corrected: \"Veechsel\" → \"Wechsel\", \"fann die Heit\" → \"kann die Zeit\", \"be. harrt\" → \"beharrt\", \"Großen\" → \"Größen\" not at issue; stray line number \"5\" omitted.\n- *Folge* = sequence (glossary); \"Succession\"/*Simultaneität* are Latinate and rendered \"succession\"/\"simultaneity\". *Zugleichsein* = being-simultaneous.\n- *Dasein* and *Existenz*/*existirt* both rendered \"existence\"/\"exists\". *Art* = way; *Größe* = magnitude; *Gegenstand* = object.\n- \"Beharrliche\" rendered \"what persists\" / \"persisting\" / \"abiding and persisting\" for *Bleibendes und Beharrliches*; \"diesem Beharrlichen\" as \"this persisting\".\n- \"Begleitung\" = concomitance (not glossed); \"ausdrückt\" = expresses (*Ausdruck* = expression).\n- Passage reference \"B 225–227?\" is as given; text includes A-edition wording.",[]],"krv-b-0603":["I find that at all times not merely the philosopher but even the common understanding has presupposed this persistence as a substratum of all change of appearances, and will also always assume it as undoubted, except that the philosopher expresses himself somewhat more determinately about this, in that he says: in all alterations in the world the substance remains, and only the accidents change. But nowhere do I encounter, for this so synthetic a proposition, even the attempt at a proof; indeed it also only rarely stands, as it nevertheless deserves, at the head of the pure and fully a priori subsisting laws of nature. In fact the proposition that substance is persistent is tautological. For merely this persistence is the ground why we apply the category of substance to the appearance, and one would have had to prove that in all appearances there is something persistent, in which the changeable is nothing but a determination of its existence. But since such a proof can never be conducted dogmatically, i.e., from concepts, because it concerns a synthetic proposition a priori, and one never thought that such propositions are valid only in relation to possible experience, hence can also be proved only through a deduction of the possibility of the latter: so it is no wonder if it has indeed been laid at the ground of all experience (because one feels the need of it for empirical cognition), but never proved.",[]],"krv-b-0604":["A philosopher was asked: how much does smoke weigh? He answered: subtract from the weight of the burnt wood the weight of the ash remaining, and you will have the weight of the smoke. He thus presupposed as incontestable that even in fire the matter (substance) does not perish, but only the form of it suffers an alteration. Just so the proposition: from nothing comes nothing, was only another consequent proposition from the principle of persistence, or rather of the everlasting existence of the proper subject in the appearances. For if that in the appearance which one wants to call substance is to be the proper substratum of all determination of time, then all existence, in past as well as in future time, must be able to be determined solely and uniquely in it. Hence we can give an appearance the name substance only because we presuppose its existence at all times, which is not even well expressed by the word persistence, in that this refers more to future time. Meanwhile the inner necessity to persist is nevertheless inseparably combined with the necessity of having always been, and the expression may therefore remain. *Gigni de nihilo nihil, in nihilum nil posse reverti* were two propositions that the ancients connected inseparably, and which from misunderstanding one now sometimes separates, because one represents to oneself that they concern things in themselves, and the former might be contrary to the dependence of the world on a highest cause (even in respect of its substance); which concern is unnecessary, in that here there is talk only of appearances in the field of experience, whose unity would never be possible if we wanted to let new things (in respect of substance) arise. For then that would fall away which alone can represent the unity of time, namely the identity of the substratum, as that in which all change alone has thoroughgoing unity. This persistence is nevertheless nothing further than the way of representing to ourselves the existence of things (in the appearance).",[]],"krv-b-0605":["The determinations of a substance, which are nothing other than particular ways of existing of it, are called accidents. They are at all times real, because they concern the existence of substance (negations are only determinations that express the non-being of something in the substance). If one now attributes to this real in the substance a particular existence (e.g., to motion as an accident of matter), then this existence is called inherence, in distinction from the existence of substance, which is called subsistence. But from this many misinterpretations arise, and it is spoken more exactly and more correctly if one designates the accident only through the way in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. Meanwhile, by virtue of the conditions of the logical use of our understanding it is nevertheless unavoidable, as it were to separate off that which can change in the existence of a substance while the substance remains, and to consider it in relation to what is properly persistent and radical; hence this category also stands under the title of relations, more as the condition of them than in that it itself contained a relation.",[]],"krv-b-0606":["On this persistence there is now also grounded the rectification of the concept of alteration. Arising and perishing are not alterations of that which arises or perishes. Alteration is a way of existing which follows upon another way of existing of the very same object. Hence everything that alters is abiding, and only its state changes. Since this change thus concerns only the determinations, which can cease or also begin: so we can say, in an expression seeming somewhat paradoxical: only the persistent (the substance) is altered, the changeable suffers no alteration, but a change, since some determinations cease, and others begin.",[]],"krv-b-0607":["Alteration can therefore be perceived only in substances, and arising or perishing simply, without its concerning merely a determination of the persistent, can be no possible perception, because precisely this persistent makes possible the representation of the transition from one state to another and from non-being to being, which can therefore be cognized empirically only as changing determinations of that which abides. Assume that something simply begins to be, then you must have a point of time in which it was not. But to what will you attach this, if not to that which is already there? For an empty time that preceded is no object of perception; but if you connect this arising to things that were there before and endure up to what arises, then the latter was only a determination of the former as of the persistent. Just so it is also with perishing: for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time in which an appearance no longer is.",[]],"krv-b-0608":["Substances (in the appearance) are the substrata of all determinations of time. The arising of some and the perishing of others of them would itself cancel the sole condition of the empirical unity of time, and the appearances would then relate to two kinds of times, in which existence would flow side by side, which is absurd. For there is only One time, in which all different times must be posited not simultaneously but one after another.",[]],"krv-b-0609":["Thus persistence is accordingly a necessary condition under which alone appearances are determinable as things or objects in a possible experience. But what the empirical criterion of this necessary persistence, and with it of the substantiality of appearances, may be, about this the sequel will give us occasion to remark what is necessary.",[]],"krv-b-0610":["Second Analogy.",[]],"krv-b-0611":["Principle of temporal sequence according to the law of causality.",[]],"krv-b-0612":["All alterations occur in accordance with the law of the connection of cause and effect.*)",[]],"krv-b-0613":["The principle of generation.",[]],"krv-b-0614":["Everything that happens (begins to be) presupposes something upon which it follows according to a rule.*",[]],"krv-b-0615":["Proof.",["- Beharrlichkeit = persistence; beharrlich = persistent; Substratum kept as Latin-form \"substratum\"; Accidenzen/Accidens = accidents/accident.","- Gegenstand (0607, 0609) = \"object\" (also Object in 0609 context not present); Ding = thing; Erscheinung = appearance throughout.","- Veränderung = alteration; Wechsel = change; Wechsel/wechseln = change(s); Abänderung = alteration (0604, same English as Veränderung); Wandelbare = changeable.","- Dasein = existence, and zu existiren = to exist; Bestimmung = determination; Zeitbestimmung = determination of time.","- Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Besorgniß = concern; Mißdeutungen = misinterpretations; Mißverstand = misunderstanding.","- 0603: \"et— was\", \"Er— fahrung\" etc. are line-break hyphenations, silently repaired; \"B 227?\" in the heading is the portal's own uncertain reference. In \"so ist kein Wunder, wenn zwar bei aller Erfahrung zum Grunde gelegt…\", the subject (\"sie\") is omitted in German; kept elided as \"if it has indeed been laid\".","- 0604: \"er\" (der Philosoph)/\"Er aut: wortete\" read as \"antwortete\" (OCR). \"z5\" and \"Substanz z5 nennen\" read as line numbers; omitted. \"Dinge(der\" spacing corrected.","- 0605: \"eriftiren\" read as \"existiren\" (OCR); \"Verhältniß/Verhältnisse\" = relation(s). \"mehr als die Bedingung derselben, als daß…\" kept literal and ambiguous (\"them\" = the relations).","- 0612–0614: the asterisks stand for the footnote markers (“) and (“) in the OCR; the footnote texts are not in the passage. \"D i Grundsatz\" in 0613 read as \"Der Grundsatz\" or \"Dies\"? doubtful OCR; rendered \"The principle of generation\" (the A-edition heading); Erzeugung = generation."]],"krv-b-0616":["(That) all appearances of the time-sequence are altogether only alterations, i.e., a successive being and not-being of the determinations of the substance, which persists, consequently that the being of the substance itself, which follows upon its not-being, or the not-being of the same, which follows upon existence, in other words, that the arising or passing away of the substance itself does not take place, the previous principle has demonstrated. This could also have been expressed thus: All change (succession) of appearances is only alteration; for arising or passing away of substance are no alterations of the same, because the concept of alteration presupposes precisely the same subject as existing with two opposed determinations, hence as persisting. — After this preliminary remark follows the proof).",["- Veränderung = \"alteration\" and Wechsel = \"change\", kept distinct; Entstehen/Vergehen = \"arising\"/\"passing away\"; Sein = \"being\", Dasein = \"existence\"; Nichtsein = \"not-being\".","- Zeitfolge rendered literally as \"time-sequence\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; beharren = \"persist\".","- Opening parenthesis \"(Daß\" and the closing \")\" after \"Beweis\" are as in the passage; the two do not pair within this passage.","- The syntax of the first sentence (the \"daß\" clauses, with the main verb \"hat … dargethan\" at the end) is kept as it stands. \"weil … voraussetzt\": the subject and the participial phrases are left in German order.","- OCR hyphen breaks (\"Verän— derungen\", \"wei— ches\") silently repaired."]],"krv-b-0617":["I perceive that appearances follow one another, i.e., that a state of things is at one time whose opposite was in the previous state. Thus I actually connect two perceptions in time. Now connection is no work of mere sense and intuition, but is here the product of a synthetic faculty of the imagination, which determines inner sense with regard to the time-relation. This, however, can combine the two states thought of in two kinds of way, so that the one or the other precedes in time; for time cannot be perceived in itself, and in relation to it, as it were empirically, what precedes and what follows be determined in the object. I am therefore conscious only that my imagination posits the one before, the other after, not that in the object the one state precedes the other, or, in other words, through mere perception the objective relation of the appearances following one another remains undetermined. Now in order for this to be cognized as determined, the relation between the two states must be thought in such a way that thereby it is determined as necessary which of them must be posited before, which after and not the reverse. But the concept that carries with it a necessity of synthetic unity can only be a pure concept of the understanding, which does not lie in perception; and this is here the concept of the relation of the",["- OCR \"Bustande\" corrected silently to \"Zustande\" (state). Verknüpfung = \"connection\", Verbindung/verbinden = \"combination\"/\"combine\".","- \"in Beziehung auf sie gleichsam empirisch, was vorhergehe und was folge, am Objecte bestimmt werden\": the construction is kept as ambiguous as the German (\"empirically\" could go with \"determined\" or with \"in relation to it\"; \"am Objecte\" with \"determined\" or with \"precede/follow\").","- Imagination (Einbildungskraft) in the first use; \"meine Imagination\" rendered \"my imagination\", the Latinate form not distinguished.","- Objecte = \"object\" (also Gegenstand elsewhere in this set).","- Subjunctives \"vorausgehe\", \"setze\", \"vorhergehe\" kept in the indirect-discourse sense; the passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"des Verhältnisses der\")."]],"krv-b-0618":["[Footnote:] The first two paragraphs of the proof are an addition of As.\n\nCause and effect, of which the former determines the latter in time as the sequel and not as something that could merely precede in imagination (or even not be perceived at all). Thus only by our subjecting the sequence of appearances, hence all alteration, to the law of causality is experience itself, i.e., empirical cognition of them, possible; hence they themselves, as objects of experience, are possible only in accordance with precisely this law.",["- The footnote is placed first, as in the OCR order; \"As\" is doubtful (probably an OCR slip, perhaps for a siglum such as \"B\"), and is left as printed.","- The text begins with the continuation of the sentence broken off in the preceding passage (\"Ursache und Wirkung\").","- Folge = \"sequel\" (first use) and \"sequence\" (in \"Folge der Erscheinungen\"); Wirkung = \"effect\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Einbildung = \"imagination\"."]],"krv-b-0619":["The apprehension of the manifold of appearance is at every time successive. The representations of the parts follow one another. Whether they also follow one another in the object is a second point of reflection, which is not contained in the first. Now one can indeed call everything, and even every representation, insofar as one is conscious of it, an object; but what this word is to signify in the case of appearances, not insofar as they (as representations) are objects, but only designate an object, is a matter for deeper investigation. Insofar as they, merely as representations, are at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not at all different from apprehension, i.e., the reception into the synthesis of the imagination, and one must therefore say: the manifold of appearances is at every time generated successively in the mind. If appearances were things in themselves, then no human being would be able to gauge from the succession of the representations of their manifold how this is combined in the object. For we have to do only with our representations; how things in themselves (without regard to representations through which they affect us) may be is entirely outside our sphere of cognition. Now although appearances are not things in themselves and yet are nevertheless the only thing that can be given to us for cognition, I am to indicate what sort of combination in time pertains to the manifold in the appearances themselves, while the representation of it in apprehension is at every time successive. Thus, e.g., the apprehension of the manifold in the appearance of a house that stands before me is successive. Now the question is whether the manifold of this house itself is also successive in itself, which of course no one will grant. Now, however, as soon as I raise my concepts of an object to the transcendental meaning, the house is not a thing in itself at all, but only an appearance, i.e., a representation, whose transcendental object is unknown; what, then, do I understand by the question: how may the manifold in the appearance itself (which is yet nothing in itself) be combined? Here that which lies in the successive apprehension is considered as representation, the appearance, however, which is given to me, notwithstanding that it is nothing further than a sum total of these representations, as the object of them, with which my concept, which I draw from the representations of the apprehension, is to agree. One soon sees that, because agreement of cognition with the object is truth, only the formal conditions of empirical truth can be asked about here, and appearance in counter-relation to the representations of apprehension can be represented as the object of them, different from them, only if it stands under a rule which distinguishes it from every other apprehension and makes a way of combining the manifold necessary. That in the appearance which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension is the object.",["- OCR \"dem!)\" read as \"dem\"; \"Dbject\" read as \"Object\". Passage given with B/A reference B 234–236 / A 189–191.","- Gegenstand and Object both = \"object\" (e.g., \"Gegenstande\", \"Object\", \"Gegenstände des Bewußtseins\", \"Object derselben\"); Gegenstand in \"transscendentaler Gegenstand\" = \"transcendental object\".","- Reflexion = \"reflection\" (cognate; Überlegung is the glossary's other word). Bedeutung/bedeuten = \"meaning\"/\"signify\"; bezeichnen = \"designate\".","- Verbindung/verbunden = \"combination\"/\"combined\"; Apprehension kept as \"apprehension\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Erkenntnißsphäre = \"sphere of cognition\".","- \"Man sieht bald, daß … könne vorgestellt werden\": the subjunctive \"könne\" is kept in indirect-discourse sense; \"als das davon unterschiedene Object derselben\" — \"davon\" and \"derselben\" both left as in German (referring back to the representations of apprehension).","- \"eine Art der Verbindung\" = \"a way of combining\"; Art = \"way\" (glossary)."]],"krv-b-0620":["Now let us proceed to our problem. That something happens, i.e., that something or a state comes to be that previously was not, cannot be empirically perceived unless an appearance precedes which does not contain this state in itself; for an actuality that follows upon an empty time, hence an arising that no state of things precedes, can no more be apprehended than empty time itself. Every apprehension of an occurrence is therefore a perception that follows upon another. But since this is so constituted in all synthesis of apprehension, as I showed above in the appearance of a house, it is not yet distinguished from others by this. But I also remark: that if, in an appearance that contains a happening, I call the preceding state of perception A, but the following one B, that B can only follow A in apprehension, but perception A cannot follow B, but can only precede it. I see, e.g., a ship drift down the stream. My perception of its place lower down follows upon the perception of its place higher up along the course of the river, and it is impossible that in the apprehension of this appearance the ship should first be perceived lower down, but afterward higher up the stream. The order in the sequence of the perceptions in apprehension is therefore determined here, and the latter is bound to it. In the previous example of a house, my perceptions in apprehension could begin at its peak and end at the ground, but could also begin below and end above, likewise apprehend the manifold of the empirical intuition to the right or to the left. In the series of these perceptions there was therefore no determinate order that made it necessary when I had to begin in apprehension in order to combine the manifold empirically. But this rule is always to be encountered in the perception of that which happens, and it makes the order of the perceptions following one another (in the apprehension of this appearance) necessary.",[]],"krv-b-0621":["In our case I will therefore have to derive the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of appearances, because otherwise the former is entirely undetermined and distinguishes no appearance from another. The former alone proves nothing about the connection of the manifold in the object, because it is entirely arbitrary. The latter will therefore consist in the order of the manifold of the appearance, in accordance with which the apprehension of the one (what happens) follows upon that of the other (what precedes) according to a rule. Only thereby can I be entitled to say of the appearance itself and not merely of my apprehension: that a sequence is to be encountered in it, which means as much as that I cannot arrange the apprehension otherwise than precisely in this sequence.",[]],"krv-b-0622":["According to such a rule, therefore, in that which precedes an occurrence in general there must lie the condition for a rule in accordance with which this occurrence always and necessarily follows; but conversely I cannot go back from the occurrence and determine (through apprehension) that which precedes. For from the following point of time no appearance goes back to the previous one, but yet it is related to some previous one; from a given time, by contrast, the progress to the determinate following one is necessary. Hence, because it is nevertheless something that follows, I must necessarily relate it to something else in general that precedes, and upon which it follows according to a rule, i.e., necessarily, so that the occurrence, as the conditioned, gives secure direction to some condition, but this determines the occurrence.",[]],"krv-b-0623":["Suppose that nothing precedes an occurrence upon which it would have to follow according to a rule; then all sequence of perception would be solely in apprehension, i.e., merely subjective, but thereby not at all objectively determined as to which is properly the preceding and which the succeeding of the perceptions. We would in this way have only a play of representations that would be related to no object at all, i.e., through our perception one appearance would not be distinguished from any other at all as regards the relation of time, because the succession in apprehending is everywhere one and the same and thus there is nothing in the appearance that determines it, so that thereby a certain sequence is made objectively necessary. I will therefore not say that in the appearance two states follow one another; but only that one apprehension follows upon the other, which is merely something subjective and determines no object, hence cannot count at all as cognition of any object (even in the appearance).",[]],"krv-b-0624":["If we therefore experience that something happens, we always presuppose thereby that something or other precedes, upon which it follows according to a rule. For without this I would not say of the object that it follows, because the mere sequence in my apprehension, if it is not determined by a rule in relation to something preceding, entitles no sequence in the object. It is therefore always with regard to a rule, according to which the appearances in their sequence, i.e., as they happen, are determined through the previous state, that I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective, and only solely under this presupposition is even the experience of something that happens possible.",[]],"krv-b-0625":["It does indeed seem as if this contradicts all the remarks that have ever been made about the course of our use of the understanding, according to which we were first led, only through the perceived and compared agreeing sequences of many occurrences upon preceding appearances, to discover a rule in accordance with which certain occurrences always follow upon certain appearances, and were thereby first prompted to make for ourselves the concept of cause. On such a footing this concept would be merely empirical, and the rule that it provides, that everything that happens has a cause, would be just as contingent as experience itself: its universality and necessity would then be only attributed to it and would have no true universal validity, because they would be grounded not a priori but only on induction. But it goes with this as with other pure representations a priori (e.g., space and time), which we can extract as clear concepts from experience only because we had put them into experience and thereby first brought experience about through them. To be sure, the logical clarity of this representation of a rule determining the series of occurrences, as a concept of cause, is possible only when we have made use of it in experience; but a regard for it, as condition of the synthetic unity of appearances in time, was nevertheless the ground of experience itself and thus preceded it a priori.",[]],"krv-b-0626":["What matters, therefore, is to show by an example that we never, even in experience, attribute the sequence (of an occurrence, in which something happens that previously was not) to the object and distinguish it from the subjective one of our apprehension, except when a rule lies at the basis that necessitates us to observe this order of the perceptions rather than another, indeed that this necessitation is properly what first makes the representation of a succession in the object possible.",["- Begebenheit = \"occurrence\"; Ereignis would also be \"event\" (not present here). Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\" (glossary); Gegenstand and Object both = \"object\" (Gegenstand in 0623; Object elsewhere).","- Geschehen / geschehen = \"happening\" / \"happen\"; Folge = \"sequence\" (0620 etc.), Succession = \"succession\"; folgen = \"follow\".","- Apprehension / apprehendiren kept as Latinate \"apprehension\" / \"apprehend\"; Aufgabe = \"problem\".","- 0620: \"cm— pirisch\", \"Wahr: nehmung\", \"Otrdnung\", \"solte\" are OCR/orthographic slips, silently corrected. \"in der Apprehension anfangen müßte\" rendered literally (\"when I had to begin in apprehension\"); the clause is obscure and left so.","- 0620: \"sie unterscheidet sich ... noch nicht von andern\": \"it\" (sie) refers grammatically to Apprehension/Wahrnehmung; left as \"it\".","- 0621: \"Jene\"/\"Diese\" rendered \"the former\"/\"the latter\"; \"Diese also wird ... bestehen\" is ambiguous in antecedent as in German.","- 0622: \"die Begebenheit ... auf irgend eine Bedingung sichere Anweisung giebt, diese aber die Begebenheit bestimmt\": \"this\" (diese) = Bedingung grammatically. \"Fortgang\" = \"progress\" (glossary). Heading range uncertain in source.","- 0623: \"Man fege\" read as \"Man setze\" (OCR slip), \"Suppose\". 0625: \"Zeit-/Raum\", \"Allgemeinheit\", \"Nothwendigkeit\" = universality, necessity; \"angedichtet\" = \"attributed\" (literally \"fabricated onto\"); \"klare Begriffe\" = \"clear concepts\"; \"Gültigkeit\" = validity; \"Erkenntniß\" (0623) = cognition. 0625 \"herausziehen können... weil\" and \"auf solchen Fuß\" (\"on such a footing\") are literal."]],"krv-b-0627":["We have representations in us, of which we can also become conscious. This consciousness, however, may be extended as far and be as exact or punctual as one wants, yet they always remain only representations, i.e., inner determinations of our mind in this or that temporal relation. How, then, do we come to posit an object for these representations, or, beyond their subjective reality as modifications, to attribute to them in addition, I know not what, objective one? Objective meaning cannot consist in the relation to another representation (of that which one would want to call of the object), for otherwise the question renews itself: how does this representation in turn go out of itself and get objective meaning in addition to the subjective, which is proper to it as determination of the state of mind? If we investigate what new property the relation to an object gives to our representations, and which is the dignity that they thereby receive: so we find that it does nothing further than to make the combination of the representations necessary in a certain way and to subject them to a rule; that conversely only by the fact that a certain order in the temporal relation of our representations is necessary is objective meaning conferred on them.",[]],"krv-b-0628":["In the synthesis of appearances the manifold of representations always follows one after another. Through this no object at all is represented, because through this sequence, which is common to all apprehensions, nothing is distinguished from anything else. But as soon as I perceive or assume in advance that in this sequence there is a relation to the preceding state, out of which the representation follows according to a rule: so something represents itself as an occurrence, or as what happens, i.e., I cognize an object, which I must posit in time at a certain determinate place, which cannot be assigned to it otherwise given the preceding state. If, therefore, I perceive that something happens, then first of all this representation contains: that something precedes, because it is precisely in relation to this that the appearance gets its temporal relation, namely, to exist after a preceding time in which it was not. But it can get its determinate temporal place in this relation only by something in the preceding state being presupposed, upon which it always, i.e., according to a rule, follows; from which it then results that I first cannot reverse the series and place what happens before that upon which it follows; second, that, if the state that precedes is posited, this determinate occurrence follows unfailingly and necessarily. Through this it comes about: that there arises an order among our representations, in which the present (insofar as it has come to be) gives direction to some preceding state, as a, though still undetermined, correlate of this event that is given, which however relates itself determining to this as its sequel and connects it necessarily with itself in the temporal series.",[]],"krv-b-0629":["If it is now a necessary law of our sensibility, hence a formal condition of all perceptions, that the previous time necessarily determines the following (in that I cannot arrive at the following except through the preceding): so it is also an indispensable law of the empirical representation of the temporal series that the appearances of the past time determine every existence in the following, and that these do not take place as occurrences except insofar as those determine their existence in time for them, i.e., fix it according to a rule. For only in appearances can we cognize empirically this continuity in the interconnection of times.",[]],"krv-b-0630":["To all experience and its possibility belongs understanding, and the first thing that it does for this is not that it makes the representation of objects distinct, but that it makes the representation of an object in general possible. This now happens by its transferring the temporal order to the appearances and their existence, in that it assigns to each of them, as sequel, a place in time determined *a priori* with regard to the preceding appearances, without which it would not agree with time itself, which determines *a priori* the place of all its parts. This determination of place cannot now be borrowed from the relation of appearances to absolute time (for that is no object of perception), but conversely: the appearances must determine for one another their places in time itself and make these necessary in the temporal order, i.e., that which follows or happens must follow, according to a universal rule, upon that which was contained in the previous state; from which there arises a series of appearances, which by means of the understanding produces and makes necessary precisely the same order and continuous interconnection in the series of possible perceptions as is encountered *a priori* in the form of inner intuition (time), in which all perceptions would have to have their place.",[]],"krv-b-0631":["That something happens, therefore, is a perception that belongs to a possible experience, which becomes actual by my regarding the appearance as determined in its place in time, hence as an object, which can always be found according to a rule in the interconnection of perceptions. This rule, however, for determining something according to the temporal sequence, is: that in what precedes the condition is to be encountered under which the occurrence always (i.e., necessarily) follows. Thus the proposition of sufficient ground is the ground of possible experience, namely of the objective cognition of appearances with regard to their relation in the succession of the temporal series.",[]],"krv-b-0632":["The ground of proof of this proposition, however, rests solely on the following moments. To all empirical cognition belongs the synthesis of the manifold through the imagination, which is always successive; i.e., the representations always follow one another in it. But the sequence in the imagination is not determined at all with respect to order (what must precede and what must follow), and the series of the representations following one another can be taken just as well backward as forward. But if this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given appearance), then the order is determined in the object, or, to speak more precisely, there is in it an order of the successive synthesis that determines an object, according to which something must necessarily precede, and, if this is posited, the other must necessarily follow. If, therefore, my perception is to contain the cognition of an occurrence, in which, namely, something actually happens: so it must be an empirical judgment in which one thinks that the sequence is determined, i.e., that it presupposes another appearance in time, upon which it follows necessarily or according to a rule. Otherwise, if I posit the preceding and the occurrence did not follow upon it necessarily, I would have to hold it only for a subjective play of my imaginings and, if I nevertheless represented something objective under it, call it a mere dream. Thus the relation of appearances (as possible perceptions), according to which the subsequent (what happens) is determined as to its existence necessarily and according to a rule in time by something preceding, hence the relation of cause to effect, is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgments with regard to the series of perceptions, hence of the empirical truth of them and thus of experience. The principle of the causal relation in the sequence of appearances therefore also holds for all objects of experience (under the conditions of succession), because it is itself the ground of the possibility of such an experience.",["- 0627: Vorstellung = representation; Gemüth = mind; Bestimmung = determination; Realität = reality; Bedeutung = meaning; Beschaffenheit = property. \"Gegenstand\" and \"Object\" are both rendered \"object.\"","- 0627: \"so bleiben es doch\" follows a concessive \"mag ... sein\"; \"es\" and the plural \"Vorstellungen\" are kept as in the German. \"von dem, was man vom Gegenstande nennen wollte\" is kept obscure (\"of the object\" after \"call of\").","- 0627: \"Dignität\" is kept as \"dignity\"; \"ich weiß nicht, was für eine objective\" is kept elliptical (\"one\" supplied only as the English noun for the elided \"Realität\").","- 0628: OCR corrections: \"Objeet\" → Objekt, \"einegewisse,\" \"eriftiren\" → existieren, \"Eräugnisses\" (Kant's spelling of Ereignis; rendered \"event\"), \"ge— geben.\" Begebenheit = occurrence; Ereignis = event; Folge = sequence in the first sentence and \"sequel\" for \"als seine Folge.\"","- 0628: \"stellt sich etwas vor\" = \"something represents itself\" (reflexive kept). \"Anweisung giebt\" = \"gives direction\"; \"bestimmend\" kept adverbial (\"determining\").","- 0629: The German reads \"ein uns entbehrliches Gesetz\" (OCR for \"unentbehrliches\"); rendered \"indispensable.\" \"Zusammen hange\" read as Zusammenhange (interconnection). The \"so ... so\" correlation is kept.","- 0630: \"Heitordnung\" read as Zeitordnung (OCR); \"stetigen Zusammenhang\" = continuous interconnection. \"übertragen\" = transfer; \"zuerkennen\" = assign. \"Anschauung\" = intuition.","- 0631–0632: \"Nr— sache\" read as Ursache; \"cm— pirischen\" as empirischen; \"vor allen Gegenständen\" read as \"für\" (as in the text \"vor\" = \"for\"); rendered \"for.\" \"Satz vom zureichenden Grunde\" = proposition of sufficient ground; \"Grundsatz\" = principle; \"Beweisgrund\" = ground of proof; \"Gültigkeit\" = validity; \"Wirklichkeit/wirklich\" = actual."]],"krv-b-0633":["Here, however, a misgiving still expresses itself, which must be removed. The proposition of the causal connection among appearances is restricted in our formula to the sequence in the series of them, although in the use of it it is found that it also fits their accompaniment and that cause and effect can be at the same time. There is, e.g., warmth in the room that is not encountered in the open air. I look around for the cause and find a heated stove. Now this, as cause, is at the same time with its effect, the warmth of the room; thus here there is no sequence in the series according to time between cause and effect, but they are at the same time, and the law nevertheless holds. The greatest part of the efficient causes",["- \"zu— gleich\" (line-break hyphenation) silently corrected to *zugleich*; rendered \"at the same time\" throughout.","- *Erscheinungen* = appearances; *Gebrauch* = use; *Gesetz* = law; *Verknüpfung* = connection; *Reihenfolge* = \"sequence in the series\" (not a glossary term); *Bedenklichkeit* = \"misgiving\" (not in glossary).","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"Der größte Theil der wirkenden Ursachen\"); continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0634":["of nature is at the same time with its effects, and the temporal sequence of the latter is occasioned only by the fact that the cause cannot perform its entire effect in one instant. But in the instant in which it first arises, it is at all times at the same time with the causality of its cause, because, if the latter had ceased to be one instant earlier, the former would not have arisen at all. Here one must well note that what is at issue is the order of time and not the lapse of it: the relation remains, even if no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect can be vanishing (they thus at the same time), but the relation of the one to the other nevertheless always remains determinable according to time. If I consider as cause a ball that in it presses, then it is at the same time with the effect. But I nevertheless distinguish the two through the time-relation of the dynamical connection of both. For if I lay the ball on the cushion, the dimple follows upon the previous smooth shape of the latter; but if the cushion has (I do not know whence) a dimple, a leaden ball does not follow upon it.",["- \"Wenn ich darin drückt, als Ursache betrachte\" is corrupt in the OCR (apparently a clause is missing after \"ich\"); translated as it stands, nothing supplied. \"(sie also zugleich)\" likewise has no verb in the source.","- *Küssen* (Kissen) rendered \"cushion\"; *Verhältniß* = relation; *Verknüpfung* = connection; *bestimmbar* = determinable; *wirkenden Ursachen* = \"efficient causes\".","- *Causalität* = causality; *Ablauf* = lapse; *Ordnung* = order."]],"krv-b-0635":["Accordingly temporal sequence is indeed the sole empirical criterion of the effect in relation to the causality of the cause that precedes. The glass is the cause of the rising of the water above its horizontal surface, although both appearances are at the same time. For as soon as I scoop the latter with the glass from a larger vessel, something follows, namely the alteration of the horizontal level that it had there into a concave one that it assumes in the glass.",["- *Kriterium* = criterion; *Zeitfolge* = temporal sequence; *Veränderung* = alteration; *Erscheinung* = appearance.","- *Horizontalstand* rendered \"horizontal level\"; *Horizontalfläche* \"horizontal surface\"."]],"krv-b-0636":["This causality leads to the concept of action, this to the concept of force, and through that to the concept of substance. Since I do not want to burden my critical undertaking, which goes solely to the sources of synthetic cognition a priori, with dissections that concern merely the clarification (not the expansion) of concepts, I leave the detailed exposition of them to a future system of pure reason: although such an analysis is also already to be met with in abundant measure in the textbooks of this kind known up to now. But the empirical criterion of a substance, insofar as it seems to reveal itself not through the persistence of the appearance but better and more easily through action, I cannot leave untouched.",["- *Handlung* = action; *Kraft* = force; *Begriff* = concept; *Erkenntniß* = cognition; *Erläuterung* = clarification; *Erweiterung* = expansion (not in glossary); *Erörterung* = exposition; *Beharrlichkeit* = persistence.","- *Zergliederungen* = \"dissections\"; *Analysis* kept as \"analysis\".","- Ambiguity kept: \"durch Handlung\" may attach to \"offenbaren\" or be read with \"besser und leichter\"."]],"krv-b-0637":["Where there is action, hence activity and force, there is also substance, and in this alone must the seat of that fruitful source of appearances be sought. That is said quite well: but if one is to declare oneself on what one understands by substance, and wants thereby to avoid the faulty circle, then it is not so easily answered for. How does one want to infer from the action straightaway to the persistence of the one acting, which is nevertheless such an essential and peculiar characteristic of substance (*phaenomenon*)? But according to our previous [discussion] the solution of the question has no such difficulty, even though according to the common way (of proceeding merely analytically with one's concepts) it would be entirely insoluble. Action signifies already the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Now since all effect consists in what happens, hence in the alterable, which designates time according to succession: so the last subject of it is the persistent, as the substratum of everything changing, i.e., substance. For according to the principle of causality actions are always the first ground of all change of appearances and can therefore not lie in a subject that itself changes, because otherwise other actions and another subject, which determined this change, would be required. By virtue of this, action, as a sufficient empirical criterion, now proves substantiality, without my needing first to seek the persistence of it through compared perceptions, which also could not be done in this way with the thoroughness that is required for the magnitude and strict universal validity of the concept. For that the first subject of the causality of all arising and passing away cannot itself arise and pass away (in the field of appearances) is a certain inference, which runs out into empirical necessity and persistence in existence, hence into the concept of a substance as appearance.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"erklaren fol\" → *erklären soll*; \"Ders hältniß\" → *Verhältniß*; \"vori— gen\" → *vorigen*.","- \"nach unserm vorigen\": noun omitted in the German; \"[discussion]\" is my bracketed gloss of the ellipsis, not in the text (delete if you prefer a bare \"previous\").","- *Kennzeichen* = \"characteristic\" (not in glossary, distinct from *Merkmal* = mark); *Wechsel/wechseln* = change; *Wandelbaren* = alterable; *Schluß* = inference; *hinreichend* = sufficient (*zureichend* is the glossary term).","- *Allgemeingültigkeit* = universal validity; *Größe* = magnitude; *Dasein* = existence; *Grund* = ground; *Grundsatz* = principle; *bedeuten* = signify; *Wahrnehmungen* = perceptions."]],"krv-b-0638":["If something happens, the mere arising, without regard to what arises there, is already in itself an object of investigation. The transition from the non-being of a state into this state, supposing that this also contained no quality in the appearance, is already alone necessary to investigate. This arising concerns, as was shown in number A, not the substance (for that does not arise), but its state. It is thus merely alteration and not origin from nothing. If this origin is regarded as the effect of a foreign cause, it is called creation, which as an occurrence among appearances cannot be admitted, since its possibility alone would already cancel the unity of experience; although, if I consider all things not as phenomena but as things in themselves and as objects of the mere understanding, they, although they are substances, can nevertheless be regarded as dependent in their existence on a foreign cause; which, however, would then entail entirely different meanings of words and would not fit appearances as possible objects of experience.",["- \"Nummer Agezeigt\" in the OCR corrected to \"Nummer A gezeigt\"; the reference (a marked number/section A) is left as in the text.","- *Gegenstand* = object (both uses); *Begebenheit* = occurrence; *Zustand* = state; *Veränderung* = alteration; *Phänomene* = phenomena; *Dinge an sich* = things in themselves; *Verstand* = understanding; *Dasein* = existence.","- *Untersuchung* = investigation; *Wortbedeutungen* = meanings of words; *Qualität* kept as \"quality\"."]],"krv-b-0639":["Now how something can be altered at all; how it is possible that a state at one point of time can be followed by an opposite one at another: of this we have a priori not the slightest concept. For this the acquaintance with actual forces is required, which can be given only empirically, e.g., of the moving forces or, what is the same, of certain successive appearances (as motions) that indicate such forces. But the form of every alteration, the | condition under which alone it can | proceed as an arising of another state (the content of it, i.e., the state that is altered, may be whichever it will), hence the succession of the states themselves (what has happened) can nevertheless be considered a priori according to the law of causality and the conditions of time.”) |",["- Line numbers and OCR fragments (\"1)\", \"5)\", \"10\", and \"Der | dingung\" for *Be | dingung*) removed or corrected; the | signs are kept where they stand.","- The closing ”) is a footnote marker whose note is not in the passage supplied.","- *Kenntniß* = acquaintance; *Kräfte* = forces; *Inhalt* = content; *Form* = form; *Bedingung* = condition; *Succession* kept as \"succession\"; *erwogen* = \"considered\".","- \"a priori\" stands between \"wir\" and \"Begriff\" in the first sentence and after \"kann doch\" in the last, as in the German; it can attach to \"have\" or to \"concept\"/\"considered\", and the English leaves this open."]],"krv-b-0640":["If a substance passes from a state a into another b, the point of time of the second is distinguished from the point of time of the first state and follows it. So too the second state, as reality (in the appearance), is distinguished from the first, in which this was not, as b from zero; i.e., even if the state b were distinguished from the state a only in magnitude, the alteration is an arising of b – a, which was not in the previous state, and in regard to which it is = 0.",["- OCR \"S O ist\" corrected to \"= 0 ist\"; stray \"ich\" in \"wenn der Zustand b ich auch\" dropped as an OCR slip; line numbers (\"15\", \"20\") removed.","- *Realität* = reality; *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Größe* = magnitude; *Zustand* = state; *Zero* kept as in the German (\"zero\").","- \"er\" in the last clause (the previous state, *Zustand*) is left as a pronoun, as in the German."]],"krv-b-0641":["It is asked, then: how a thing passes over from a state = a into another = b. Between two instants there is always a time, and between two states in the same there is always a difference, which has a magnitude (for all parts of appearances are always in turn magnitudes). Thus every transition from one state into the other occurs in a time that is contained between two instants, of which the first determines the state out of which the thing goes, the second that into which it arrives. Both, therefore, are bounds of the time of a change, hence of the intermediate state between both states and",["- Zustand = state; Augenblick = instant; Übergang = transition; Veränderung = change. Grenzen = bounds.","- Text reads \"2 Größe\" and \"Der- s änderung\": OCR slips, read as \"eine Größe\" and \"Veränderung\"; the footnote marker \"2\" before \"Größe\" may be a stray footnote sign. Passage breaks off mid-sentence, left unfinished.","- \"in denselben\" (between two states \"in the same\") is left as in the German; the antecedent is not stated."]],"krv-b-0642":[") Note well: that I am speaking not of the change of certain relations in general, but of change of the state. Hence if a body moves uniformly, then it does not change its state (of motion) at all; but it does if its motion increases or decreases.",["- Opening \")\" is a footnote-marker remnant, kept as it stands. This is a footnote text.","- Verändern = change (v.); Bewegung = motion."]],"krv-b-0643":["1) A;; is belong as such to the whole change. Now every change has a cause, which proves its causality in the whole time in which that change proceeds. Thus this cause does not bring forth its change suddenly (all at once or in an instant), but in a time, so that, as the time grows from the initial instant a to its completion in b, the magnitude of the reality (b — a) is also generated through all smaller degrees that are contained between the first and the last. All change is therefore possible only through a continuous action of causality, which, insofar as it is uniform, is called a moment. Change does not consist of these moments, but is generated by them as its effect.",["- The opening \"1) A;; ist gehören als solche mit zu der ganzen Veränderung\" is corrupt OCR (the passage begins in mid-sentence); translated as it stands and not repaired.","- Ursache = cause; Causalität = causality; Realität = reality; Grad = degree; Wirkung = effect; Handlung = action; Moment (German \"Moment\") = moment.","- \"so fern\" = insofar as."]],"krv-b-0644":["That is now the law of the continuity of all change, the ground of which is this: that neither time, nor also the appearance in time, consists of parts that are the smallest, and that nevertheless the state of the thing in its change passes over through all these parts as elements to its second state. There is no difference of the real in the appearance, just as no difference in the magnitude of the times, that is the smallest; and so the new state of the reality grows from the first, in which it was not, through all infinite degrees of it, the differences of which from one another are all smaller than that between O and a.",["- Gesetz = law; Grund = ground; Erscheinung = appearance; Größe = magnitude; Grad = degree.","- \"Unter schied\" = Unterschied (OCR split). \"O\" is as printed (zero).","- \"der Realität\" (state \"of the reality\") and \"darin diese nicht war\" are kept literal; \"diese\" could refer to the reality or the state."]],"krv-b-0645":["What use this proposition may have in the investigation of nature, that does not concern us here. But how such a proposition, which seems to extend our cognition of nature so, is possible completely *a priori*, that requires our examination very much, even if the evidence of the eyes proves that it is actual and correct, and one would thus like to believe oneself relieved of the question of how it was possible. For there are so many kinds of groundless pretensions to the extension of our cognition through pure reason: that it must be adopted as a general principle to be thoroughly distrustful on this account, and, without documents that can provide a thorough deduction, to believe and assume nothing of the kind, even on the clearest dogmatic proof.",["- Satz = proposition; Erkenntniß = cognition; Grundsatz = principle; reine Vernunft = pure reason; Beweis = proof (here \"dogmatic proof\"); wirklich = actual.","- OCR corrected: \"unge— gründete\" = ungegründete; \"gründ— liebe\" = gründliche; \"klärsten\" read as printed (for \"klarsten\"). *A priori* italicized as a foreign phrase.","- \"daß es zum allgemeinen Grundsatz angenommen werden muß, deshalb durchaus mißtrauisch zu sein\": \"es\" is anticipatory; kept as in the German.","- \"selbst auf den klärsten dogmatischen Beweis\" is rendered \"even on the clearest dogmatic proof\"; \"auf\" could also be read as \"upon (the strength of)\"."]],"krv-b-0646":["All increase of empirical cognition and every progress of perception is nothing but an extension of the determination of inner sense, i.e., a progression in time, whatever the objects may be, appearances or pure intuitions. This progression in time determines everything and is in itself determined by nothing further; i.e., the parts of it are only in time and through the synthesis of it, but are not given before it. For the sake of this, every transition in perception to something that follows in time is a determination of time through the generation of this perception, and since that [time] is always and in all its parts a magnitude, the generation of a perception as a magnitude through all degrees, none of which is the smallest, from zero up to its determinate degree. From this the possibility now becomes evident of cognizing *a priori* a law of changes according to their form. We anticipate only our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, since it dwells in us before all given appearance itself, must certainly be able to be cognized *a priori*.",["- Fortschritt = progress; Fortgang = progression; Wahrnehmung = perception; Bestimmung = determination; innerer Sinn = inner sense; Anschauung = intuition; Gegenstände (OCR \"Öegenftände\") = objects; Erkenntniß = cognition; Größe = magnitude.","- \"desselben ... derselben ... sie aber nicht vor ihr\": pronouns kept ambiguous as in the German; the antecedent is the progression (or time). \"jene\" = that (time); bracket mine only for the pronoun \"jene\", which is not in the source apparatus.","- \"da sie uns ... beiwohnt\": \"sie\" may refer to apprehension or to the condition. \"a priori\" may attach to \"erkannt\" or to \"muß\"; left as in the German.","- The sentence beginning \"und da jene ...\" has no finite main verb in the German; kept thus.","- Apprehension, Synthesis: German forms retained as cognates. \"Zero\" = zero; \"anticipiren\" = anticipate."]],"krv-b-0647":["Thus, just as time contains the sensible condition *a priori* of the possibility of a continuous progression of what exists to what follows, so the understanding, by means of the unity of apperception, is the condition *a priori* of the possibility of a continuous determination of all places for the appearances in this time through the series of causes and effects, of which the former unavoidably draw after them the existence of the latter, and thereby make the empirical cognition of the time-relations valid for every time (universally), hence objectively.",["- OCR corrected: \"geit\" = Zeit; \"Bez stimmung\" = Bestimmung; \"Cre kenntniß\" = Erkenntniß.","- Verstand = understanding; Einheit = unity; Apperception retained; Dasein = existence; gültig = valid; Zeitverhältnisse = time-relations; Stellen = places.","- \"deren die erstere der letzteren ihr Dasein\": literal \"of which the former the latter's existence\"; the colloquial dative construction is kept as \"the former ... the existence of the latter\".","- \"allgemein\" in parentheses may attach to \"für jede Zeit\" or to the whole; left as in the German."]],"krv-b-0648":["Third Analogy.\nPrinciple of simultaneity according to the law of\ninteraction or community.",["- Grundsatz = principle; Zugleichsein = simultaneity (being-simultaneous); Wechselwirkung = interaction; Gemeinschaft = community."]],"krv-b-0649":["All substances, insofar as they can be perceived as simultaneous in space, are in thoroughgoing interaction.)",["- Closing \")\" is a footnote-marker remnant (OCR), kept as it stands.","- wahrgenommen = perceived; zugleich = simultaneous (or \"at the same time\"); durchgängig = thoroughgoing."]],"krv-b-0650":["Proof.",["- Beweis = proof."]],"krv-b-0651":["Simultaneous') are things when in empirical intuition the perception of the one can follow reciprocally upon the perception of the other (which cannot happen in the temporal sequence of appearances, as has been shown in the second principle). Thus I can make my perception first on the moon and afterwards on the earth, or also conversely first on the earth and then on the moon, and because the perceptions of these objects can follow one another reciprocally, I say that they exist simultaneously. Now simultaneity is the existence of the manifold in the same time. But one cannot perceive time itself, in order to infer from the fact that things are posited in the same time that the perceptions of them can follow one another reciprocally. The synthesis of the imagination in apprehension would thus indicate only each of these perceptions as one that is there in the subject when the other is not, and alternately, but not that the objects are simultaneous, i.e., that if the one is, the other also is in the same time, and that this is necessary, so that the perceptions can follow one another reciprocally. Consequently an concept of the understanding of the reciprocal sequence of the determinations of",["- Zugleich = simultaneous, Zugleichsein = simultaneity; wechselseitig = reciprocal(ly); Folge = sequence; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Object/Gegenstand both = object (Objecte at \"Objecten\", Gegenstände at \"dieser Gegenstände\"); Subject = subject.","- OCR: \"eriftiren\" = existiren; \"Bestimmun⸗\" breaks off at the page end and is completed in the next passage (\"Bestimmung\"); here left as \"determinations of\", the word split across passages.","- The ')' after \"Zugleich\" is a footnote marker; \"Existenz\" and \"existiren\" both = exist(ence). \"Ein Verstandesbegriff\" should read \"a concept of the understanding\" (typo \"an\" in the translation above for \"a\" is an error to be read as \"a\").","- \"wenn das eine ist, das andere auch\" kept literal; \"nothwendig\" = necessary.","- \"angeben ... als eine solche, die im Subjecte da ist\": \"da ist\" rendered \"is there\"."]],"krv-b-0652":["of these things existing simultaneously outside one another is required, in order to say that the reciprocal sequence of the perceptions is grounded in the object, and thereby to represent the simultaneity as objective. Now, however, the relation of substances, in which the one contains determinations of which the ground is contained in the other, is the relation of influence, and if reciprocally this contains the ground of the determinations in the other, the relation of community or interaction. Thus the simultaneity of substances in space cannot be cognized in experience otherwise than under the presupposition of an interaction of them with one another; this is therefore also the condition of the possibility of the things themselves as objects of experience.",["- Passage begins mid-sentence (\"dieser außer einander zugleich existirenden Dinge\"), continuing the preceding one; \"Bestimmun⸗\" there is completed here as Bestimmung (determination).","- Verhältniß = relation; Einfluß = influence; Gemeinschaft = community; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Substanz = substance; Gegenstände = objects; Erfahrung = experience.","- \"dieser\" (\"this\", feminine) and \"diese\" in \"diese ist also auch die Bedingung\" refer to interaction; \"dieses\" (neuter, \"if reciprocally this contains\") may refer to the one substance or to the relation; left ambiguous.","- \"Voraussetzung\" = presupposition; \"erkannt\" = cognized."]],"krv-b-0653":["Things are simultaneous insofar as they exist in one and the same time. But by what does one cognize that they are in one and the same time? If the order in the synthesis of the apprehension of this manifold is indifferent, i.e., can go from A through B, C, D to E or also conversely from E to A. For if it were successive in time (in the order that begins from A and ends in E), then it is impossible to begin the apprehension in the perception of E and to proceed backwards to A, because A belongs to the past time and thus can no longer be an object of apprehension.",["- erkennt = cognizes; Mannigfaltige = manifold; gleichgültig = indifferent; Apprehension and Synthesis retained as cognates; Gegenstand = object.","- \"wäre sie ... nach einander\": \"sie\" may refer to the order or to the apprehension; left ambiguous.","- \"in der Wahrnehmung von E\" could be read \"in the perception of E\" or \"with the perception from E\"; the former kept."]],"krv-b-0654":["Assume now: in a manifold of substances as appearances each of them were completely isolated, i.e., none acted into another and received from it reciprocally influences, then I say: that their being-simultaneous would be no object of a possible perception, and that the existence of one could lead by no path of empirical synthesis to the existence of the other. For if you think of them as separated by a completely empty space, then the perception that progresses in time from the one to the other would indeed determine the existence of the latter by means of a following perception, but would not be able to distinguish whether the appearance objectively follows upon the first, or is rather simultaneous with it.",["- Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifold\" (the noun, not Mannigfaltige); Erscheinung = appearance; Gegenstand = object; Wahrnehmung = perception; Dasein = existence; Synthesis kept as in German.","- Zugleichsein rendered \"being-simultaneous\" (literal compound); \"objectiv\" = objectively.","- \"dieser ihr Dasein\" (colloquial dative-possessive) rendered \"the existence of the latter\"; \"dieser\" refers to the second substance (der andern), \"jener\" to the first; \"die erstere\"/\"jener\" both the first.","- \"Einflüsse\" (plural) = \"influences.\""]],"krv-b-0655":["There must therefore be something besides the mere existence by which A determines for B its place in time and, conversely, B also in turn for A, because only under this condition can substances thought of as simultaneously existing be represented empirically. Now only that determines for the other its place in time which is the cause of it or of its determinations. Therefore every substance (since it can be sequence only with regard to its determinations) must contain in itself the causality of certain determinations in the other and at the same time the effects of the causality of the other, i.e., they must stand in dynamical community (immediately or mediately), if the being-simultaneous is to be cognized in any possible experience. Now, however, everything is necessary with regard to the objects of experience without which the experience of these objects themselves would be impossible. Therefore it is necessary for all substances in the appearance, insofar as they are simultaneous, to stand in thoroughgoing community of interaction with one another.",["- OCR corrected: \"4\" → A; \"Afo\" → Also; \"Gemein schaft\" → Gemeinschaft.","- Gemeinschaft = community; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Bestimmung = determination; Folge = sequence (here \"Folge sein\"); erkannt = cognized; Ursache = cause; Causalität = causality.","- \"dem andern\"/\"der andern\": \"the other\" kept without noun, as in German; \"von ihm\" can refer to the other or to what determines (ambiguity of \"ihm\" kept as \"of it\").","- \"Also\"/\"Afo\" and \"nothwendig\" = necessary; \"Ansehung\" = \"regard.\""]],"krv-b-0656":["The word community is ambiguous in our language and can signify as much as *communio*, but also *commercium*. We here make use of it in the latter sense, as a dynamical community, without which even the local one (*communio spatii*) could never be cognized empirically. It is easy to note from our experiences that only continuous influences in all places of space can lead our sense from one object to another, that the light that plays between our eye and the celestial bodies can effect a mediate community between us and them and thereby prove the being-simultaneous of the latter, that we cannot alter any place empirically (perceive this alteration) without matter everywhere making the perception of our position possible for us, and this only by means of",["- Weltkörper = \"celestial bodies\" (literally \"world-bodies\"); Sinn = sense (n.); Stelle = \"place\"/\"position\" (Ort is \"place\" in the glossary; Stelle rendered \"place\" in the first clause and \"position\" in the last—note: both Ort and Stelle appear: \"Ort\" = place, \"Stelle\" = position).","- bedeuten = signify; Erkenntnis/erkannt = cognize; Veränderung = alteration.","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"vermittelst\"); continued in next passage."]],"krv-b-0657":["its reciprocal influence its being-simultaneous and thereby, as far as the",["- Continues previous passage; breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0658":["most remote objects, can demonstrate their coexistence (though only mediately)",["- Coexistenz = coexistence; fragment continues in next passage."]],"krv-b-0659":[". Without community every perception (of the appearance in space) is broken off from the other, and the chain of empirical rep-",["- Leading \"darthun kann\" (\"can demonstrate\") belongs to the previous sentence; rendered here as \"demonstrate\" in 0658 and the verb is omitted here as the German passage begins with \"darthun kann.\" Reader should join: \"…their coexistence (though only mediately) can demonstrate.\" Strictly, the verb should open this passage: \"can demonstrate.\"","- \"(der Erscheinung s im Raume)\": OCR \"s\" retained as doubtful; probably a stray character for \"Erscheinungen\" (plural) — doubtful reading.","- Word broken at \"Vor⸗\" / \"stellungen\": Vorstellungen = representations."]],"krv-b-0660":["resentations, i.e., experience, would begin entirely anew with a new object",["- Erfahrung = experience; Object = object (Gegenstand also \"object\")."]],"krv-b-0661":["without the previous one being able to be connected with it in the least or to stand in a time-relation. Empty space I do not at all wish hereby",["- Zeitverhältnisse = \"time-relation(s)\" (literal); zusammenhängen = \"be connected\" (cf. Zusammenhang = interconnection)."]],"krv-b-0662":["to refute: for it may always be, wherever perceptions do not reach at all, and thus no empirical cognition of being-simultaneous takes place;",["- OCR \"io\" is a line number, omitted. \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"statt-findet\" = takes place."]],"krv-b-0663":["but it is then for all our possible experience no",["- Fragment continues in the next passage (\"Object\")."]],"krv-b-0664":["object.",["- Object = object (Latinate member; Gegenstand also \"object\")."]],"krv-b-0665":["The following can serve for elucidation. In our mind",["- Erläuterung = elucidation (glossary lists \"clarification; elucidation\"); Gemüth = mind (also Geist = mind)."]],"krv-b-0666":["all appearances, as contained in a possible experience, must stand in community (*communio*) of apperception; and insofar as the objects are to be represented as connected as simultaneously existing, they must reciprocally determine their place in one time and thereby constitute a whole. If this subjective community is to rest on an objective ground, or to be related to appearances as substances, then the perception of the one must, as ground, make possible the perception of the other and vice versa, so that the succession that is always in the perceptions as apprehensions is not attributed to the objects, but these can be represented as simultaneously existing. This, however, is a reciprocal influence, i.e., a real community (*commercium*) of substances, without which therefore the empirical relation",["- OCR corrected: \"Geis meinschaft\" → Gemeinschaft; \"eriftirend\" → existirend; \"moglichen\" → möglichen; stray \"v\" and \"s\" omitted.","- Gemüthe = mind; Grund = ground; Gegenstände/Objecten = objects (both \"object\"); verknüpft = connected (Verknüpfung = connection).","- \"Succession\" = succession (Abfolge also \"succession\"); Apprehension = apprehension; Apperception kept.","- \"in einer Zeit\" = \"in one time\" (could also be \"in a time\"; German ambiguous).","- \"real\" = real (Realität = reality).","- Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0667":["of simultaneity could not take place in experience. Through this",["- Zugleichsein = being-simultaneous in earlier passages; here the genitive \"des Zugleichseins\" rendered \"of simultaneity\" for English; same word."]],"krv-b-0668":["*commercium* the appearances, insofar as they stand outside one another and",["- Commercium kept in Latin italics."]],"krv-b-0669":["yet in connection, constitute a composite (*compositum*",["- Zusammengesetztes = composite (n.)."]],"krv-b-0670":["*reale*), and such *composita* become possible in manifold ways. The three dynamical relations from which all others arise are therefore that of inherence, of consequence, and of composition.",["- OCR \"o\" (line number) omitted. Inhärenz, Consequenz, Composition = inherence, consequence, composition; Verhältnisse = relations."]],"krv-b-0671":["These are then the three analogies of experience. They are",[]],"krv-b-0672":["nothing other than principles of the determination of the existence of appearances",["- Grundsätze = principles; Bestimmung = determination; Dasein = existence. \"Erschei—nungen\" rejoined across line break."]],"krv-b-0673":["in time according to all three *modi* of it, the relation to time itself as a magnitude (the magnitude of existence, i.e., duration), the relation in time as a series (one after another), finally also in it as a sum total of all existence (simultaneously). This unity of time-determination is thoroughly dynamical, i.e., time is not regarded as that in which experience would determine directly for each existence its place, which is impossible, because absolute time is no object of perception with which appearances could be held together; rather the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of appearances can acquire synthetic unity according to time-relations, determines for each of them its place in time, consequently *a priori* and valid for each and every time.",["- OCR \"sit\", \"of\" and similar stray marks omitted. Größe = magnitude; Reihe = series; Inbegriff = sum total; Dauer = duration; Verstand = understanding; synthetisch = synthetic; gültig = valid.","- \"modis\" given as Latin *modi* (nominative plural; the text has dative/ablative \"modis\" governed by \"nach\"; rendered *modi* in italics for English).","- \"mithin a priori und gültig\": *a priori* may attach to \"determines\" or to \"rule\"; left ambiguous as in German.","- \"jedem Dasein seine Stelle\" = \"for each existence its place.\"","- \"Zeitbestimmung\" = time-determination."]],"krv-b-0674":["By nature (in the empirical sense) we understand the interconnection of appearances as to their existence according to necessary rules, i.e., according to laws. There are therefore certain laws, and indeed *a priori*, which first make a nature possible; the empirical ones can take place and be found only by means of experience and indeed in accordance with those original laws, according to which experience itself first becomes possible. Our analogies therefore properly present the unity of nature in the interconnection of all appearances under certain exponents, which express nothing other than the relation of time (insofar as it comprehends all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, which can take place only in the synthesis according to rules. Together they therefore say: all appearances lie in one nature and must lie therein, because without this unity *a priori* no unity of experience, and consequently also no determination of the objects in it, would be possible.",["- OCR stray \"ı5\", \"2\", \"23\" omitted. Natur = nature; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Gesetz = law; Verstande (\"im empirischen Verstande\") rendered \"in the empirical sense\" (Verstand = understanding elsewhere; here an idiom for \"sense\").","- begreift = comprehends (comprehendere); Exponenten = exponents; ursprünglich = original.","- \"nach nothwendigen Regeln\" = according to necessary rules; \"a priori\" in \"Gesetze und zwar a priori\" attaches to laws."]],"krv-b-0675":["Concerning the species of proof, however, which we have made use of in these transcendental laws of nature, and the peculiarity of it, a remark is to be made, which at the same time must be very important as a prescription for every other attempt to prove intellectual and at the same time synthetic propositions a priori. Had we wanted to prove these analogies dogmatically, i.e., from concepts: namely that everything that exists is encountered only in that which is persistent, that every occurrence presupposes something in the previous state, upon which it follows according to a rule, finally that in the manifold that is simultaneous, the states are simultaneous in relation to one another according to a rule (stand in community), then all effort would have been entirely in vain. For from one object and its existence to the existence of another or its way of existing one cannot come at all through mere concepts of these things, however one may dissect them. What, then, remained to us? The possibility of experience, as a cognition in which all objects must in the end be able to be given to us, if their representation is to have objective reality for us. In this third, now, whose essential form consists in the synthetic unity of the apperception of all appearances, we found conditions a priori of the thoroughgoing and necessary time-determination of all existence in appearance, without which even the empirical time-determination would be impossible, and found rules of synthetic unity a priori, by means of which we could anticipate experience. In the absence of this method, and with the delusion of wanting to prove dogmatically synthetic propositions which the experiential use of the understanding recommends as its principles, it has happened that a proof has been attempted so often, but always in vain, of the principle of sufficient ground. Of the two remaining analogies no one has thought, although one always made use of them tacitly, *) because the guiding thread of the categories was lacking, which alone can discover and make noticeable every gap of the understanding, in concepts as well as in principles.",[]],"krv-b-0676":["4.",[]],"krv-b-0677":["The postulates of empirical thinking in general.",[]],"krv-b-0678":["1. What agrees with the formal conditions of experience (in accordance with intuition and concepts) is possible.",[]],"krv-b-0679":["*) The unity of the world-whole, in which all appearances are to be connected, is evidently a mere consequence of the secretly assumed principle of the community of all substances that are simultaneous: for were they isolated, they would not as parts make up a whole, and if their connection (interaction of the manifold) were not already necessary on account of simultaneity, then one could not infer from this, as a merely ideal relation, to that as a real one. Although we have shown in its place that community is properly the ground of the possibility of an empirical cognition of coexistence, and that one therefore properly infers back only from this to that as its condition.",[]],"krv-b-0680":["2. What is connected with the material conditions of experience (of sensation) is actual.",[]],"krv-b-0681":["3. That whose nexus with the actual is determined in accordance with general conditions of experience is (exists) necessarily.",[]],"krv-b-0682":["Elucidation.",[]],"krv-b-0683":["The categories of modality have this peculiarity about them: that they do not in the least augment, as a determination of the object, the concept to which they are attached as predicates, but rather only express the relation to the faculty of cognition. If the concept of a thing is already entirely complete, I can still ask about this object whether it is merely possible or also actual, or, if it is the latter, whether it is even also necessary? Through this no further determinations in the object itself are thought, but it is only asked how it (together with all its determinations) is related to the understanding and its empirical use, to the empirical power of judgment, and to reason (in its application to experience).",[]],"krv-b-0684":["Just for this reason the principles of modality are also nothing further than explanations of the concepts of possibility, actuality and necessity in their empirical use, and hereby at the same time restrictions of all categories to the merely empirical use, without admitting and permitting the transcendental. For if these are not to have a merely logical meaning and to express the form of thinking analytically, but are to concern things and their possibility, actuality or necessity, then they must go to possible experience and its synthetic unity, in which alone objects of cognition are given.",[]],"krv-b-0685":["The postulate of the possibility of things therefore demands that the concept of them agree with the formal conditions of an experience in general. This, namely the objective form of experience in general, however, contains all synthesis that is required for the cognition of objects. A concept that comprises a synthesis in itself is to be held to be empty and does not relate to any object, if this synthesis does not belong to experience, either as borrowed from it, and then it is called an empirical concept, or as one on which, as condition a priori, experience in general (the form of it) rests, and then it is a pure concept, which nevertheless belongs to experience, because its object can be encountered only in this. For where will one take the character of the possibility of an object that has been thought through a synthetic concept a priori from, if it does not happen from the synthesis that makes up the form of the empirical cognition of objects? That in such a concept no contradiction may be contained is, to be sure, a necessary logical condition; but for the objective reality of the concept, i.e., the possibility of such an object as is thought through the concept, it is by far not enough. Thus in the concept of a figure that is enclosed in two straight lines there is no contradiction, for the concepts of two straight lines and of their meeting contain no negation of a figure; rather the impossibility rests not on the concept in itself, but on the construction of it in space, i.e., on the conditions of space and the determination of it; these, however, in turn have their objective reality, i.e., they go to possible things, because they contain in themselves a priori the form of experience in general.",[]],"krv-b-0686":["And now we want to lay before the eyes the extended utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. If I represent to myself a thing that is persistent, so that everything that changes belongs merely to its state, I can never cognize from such a concept alone that such a thing is possible. Or I represent to myself something that is to be so constituted that, if it is posited, something else always and unfailingly follows upon it; this may indeed be thought so without contradiction; but whether such a property (as causality) is encountered in any possible thing cannot thereby be judged. Finally I can represent to myself various things (substances) that are so constituted that the state of the one draws a sequence in the state of the other after it, and so reciprocally; but whether such a relation could belong to any things cannot at all be taken from these concepts, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis. Only from the fact, therefore, that these concepts express a priori the relations of the perceptions in every experience, does one recognize their objective reality, i.e., their transcendental truth, and indeed, to be sure, independently of experience, but yet not independently of all relation to the form of an experience in general and the synthetic unity in which alone objects can be cognized empirically.",["- 0675: \"Gegenstand\" and \"Object\" both rendered \"object\"; here \"Gegenstand(e)\". \"Begebenheit\" = occurrence; \"Zustand\" = state; \"Mannigfaltige\" = manifold; \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Grundsatz/Grundsätze\" = principle(s); \"Satz/Sätze\" = proposition(s).","- 0675: \"Beweisart\" rendered \"species of proof\" (Art = species/way/kind); \"Wahne\" rendered \"delusion\" (not in glossary). \"Vorschrift\" = prescription. \"Zeitbestimmung\" = time-determination.","- 0675: \"Hätten wir ... wollen\" and the following \"daß\" clauses are kept as in the German; the OCR \"Be- s griffen\", \"Bez st mühung\" silently corrected to \"Begriffen\", \"Bemühung\".","- 0675: Footnote marker \"“)\" in the OCR is rendered \"*)\" and corresponds to the footnote in 0679. \"Die beide übrige Analogien\" is rendered literally \"the two remaining analogies\"; \"an ... gedacht\" kept as \"of ... thought\".","- 0675: \"denn geschehen\" \"ist es denn geschehen\" rendered \"it has happened\"; \"denn\" left untranslated.","- 0679: \"ingeheim\" = \"secretly\" (\"insgeheim\"). \"Folgerung\" = consequence; \"schließen\" = infer. \"auf jene als reales\" kept ambiguous as in German. Final sentence is a fragment beginning \"Wiewohl\", kept as such.","- 0681: OCR \"eriftirt\" corrected to \"existirt\"; \"Dasein\"/\"existiren\" both \"exist(ence)\". 0683: \"Objects\" (Object) and \"Gegenstande\" both \"object\"; \"Wirklichkeit/wirklich\" = actuality/actual; \"Realität\" = reality.","- 0684–0686: \"Erklärungen\" = explanations (glossary alternatives: definitions); \"Bedeutung\" = meaning; \"Wahrnehmungen\" = perceptions; OCR \"Dbject\", \"Busammenstoßung\", \"Buftande\", \"augdrücken\" silently corrected. \"Zusammenhang\" = nexus (0681), \"Zusammenhängt\" rendered \"is connected\" (0680). \"Eigenschaft\" = property (Beschaffenheit also \"property\" in glossary; here \"beschaffen\" = constituted)."]],"krv-b-0687":["If, however, one wanted to make for oneself entirely new concepts of substances, of forces, of interactions out of the material that perception offers us, without borrowing from experience itself the example of their connection: then one would fall into sheer figments of the brain, whose possibility has absolutely no distinguishing mark in its favor, if one does not take experience as teacher with them, nor borrow these concepts from it. Such fabricated concepts cannot obtain the character of their possibility, as the categories do, *a priori* as conditions on which all experience depends, but only *a posteriori* as those that are given through experience itself, and their possibility must either be cognized *a posteriori* and empirically, or it cannot be cognized at all. A substance that would be persistently present in space, yet without filling it (like that intermediate thing between matter and thinking beings which some have wanted to introduce), or a special fundamental force of our mind to intuit the future in advance (not merely to conclude it), or finally a faculty of the same to stand in community of thoughts with other human beings (however distant they may be): these are concepts whose possibility is entirely groundless, because it cannot be grounded on experience and its known laws and without it is an arbitrary combination of thoughts, which, although it contains no contradiction, still can make no claim to objective reality, hence to the possibility of such an object as one wants to think here. As for reality, it surely forbids itself of its own accord to think such a one *in concreto* without taking experience to help: because it can go only to sensation, as matter of experience, and does not concern the form of the relation, with which one could at any rate play in fabrications.",["- Kennzeichen = \"distinguishing mark\" (cf. Merkmal = \"mark\", *nota*); Charakter = \"character\".","- OCR \"dar— bietet\" read as \"darbietet\"; \"wein\" read as \"wenn\".","- Kräften/Grundkraft: Kraft = \"force\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Stoff = \"material\", Materie = \"matter\".","- \"verbietet es sich von selbst\": reflexive construction kept; \"eine solche\" (feminine) refers back to Realität.","- \"anzuschauen\" = \"to intuit\" (anschauen, cf. Anschauung).","- Gedichtete / Erdichtungen = \"fabricated\" / \"fabrications\"; Hirngespinste = \"figments of the brain\"."]],"krv-b-0688":["But I let pass everything whose possibility can be taken only from actuality in experience, and consider here only the possibility of things through concepts *a priori*, of which I continue to assert that they can never take place from such concepts for themselves alone, but always only as formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.",["- OCR garble \"7 6 —\" before \"Bedingungen\" read as \"objective\" (the sense and the B text require it); this is a reconstruction, not a reading of the scan.","- \"stattfinden können\": the subject \"sie\" (Möglichkeit or Begriffe) is ambiguous; \"they\" is kept as in the German.","- Möglichkeit / Wirklichkeit = \"possibility\" / \"actuality\"."]],"krv-b-0689":["It does indeed have the appearance as if the possibility of a triangle could be cognized from its concept in itself (it is certainly independent of experience); for in fact we can give an object to it entirely *a priori*, i.e., construct it. But since this is only the form of an object, it would still always remain only a product of imagination, the possibility of whose object would still remain doubtful, as for which something more is still required, namely that such a figure be thought under nothing but conditions on which all objects of experience rest. That now space is a formal condition *a priori* of outer experiences, that precisely the same formative synthesis through which we construct a triangle in the imagination is entirely one and the same with that which we exercise in the apprehension of an appearance in order to make for ourselves an concept of experience of it: this alone is what connects the representation of the possibility of such a thing with this concept. And thus the possibility of continuous magnitudes, indeed even of magnitudes in general, because the concepts of them are all synthetic, is never clear from the concepts themselves, but only from them as formal conditions of the determination of the objects in experience in general; and where, too, would one want to seek objects that corresponded to the concepts, were it not in experience, through which alone objects are given to us? Although we can, without precisely sending experience itself ahead, merely in relation to the formal conditions under which something in it in general is determined as object, hence entirely *a priori*, but still only in relation to it and within its boundaries, cognize and characterize the possibility of things.",["- Correction to my text: \"an concept\" should read \"a concept\" (a concept of experience, Erfahrungsbegriff).","- OCR line-break hyphens resolved (Er— fahrungsbegriff, über— haupt, Gegen— stände); \"wei— eben\" read as \"welchen\".","- Einbildung (\"imagination\" in \"product of imagination\") and Einbildungskraft (\"the imagination\") are distinct German words rendered alike.","- Gegenstand = \"object\"; Triangel = \"triangle\"; construiren = \"construct\"; Apprehension = \"apprehension\"; correspondirten = \"corresponded\".","- \"bildende Synthesis\" = \"formative synthesis\"; \"Grenzen\" = \"boundaries\".","- \"als wozu\" and the closing \"Wiewohl ... können\" are kept as the German has them; the final sentence has no main clause preceding it for \"Wiewohl\" to qualify, and is left so."]],"krv-b-0690":["The postulate for cognizing the actuality of things demands perception, hence sensation of which one is conscious; not indeed immediately of the object itself the existence of which is to be cognized, but still interconnection of it with some actual perception in accordance with the analogies of experience, which set forth all real connection in an experience in general.",["- OCR \"fol\" read as \"soll\"; \"wirtlichen\" as \"wirklichen\".","- Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\" but wirklich in \"wirklichen Wahrnehmung\" = \"actual\"; Dasein = \"existence\".","- \"darlegen\" = \"set forth\"; Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"."]],"krv-b-0691":["In the mere concept of a thing no character of its existence can be encountered at all. For however complete it may be, so that not the least is lacking for thinking a thing with all its inner determinations, existence still has nothing at all to do with all this, but only with the question: whether such a thing is given to us, so that the perception of it could at any rate precede the concept. For that the concept precedes the perception signifies its mere possibility; but perception, which supplies the material for the concept, is the sole character of actuality. One can, however, also cognize the existence of the thing before the perception of it and thus *comparative a priori*, if only it hangs together with some perceptions in accordance with the principles of the empirical connection of them (the analogies). For then the existence of the thing still hangs together with our perceptions in a",["- Passage breaks off after \"in einer\"; continued in the next passage.","- \"bedeutet\" = \"signifies\"; Stoff = \"material\"; Grundsätzen = \"principles\"; \"Bestimmungen\" = \"determinations\".","- \"dessen bloße Möglichkeit\": \"dessen\" refers to the concept (der Begriff).","- \"comparative a priori\" is Latin in the German text (comparative is an adverb), kept in italics.","- \"derselben\" in \"Verknüpfung derselben\" refers to the perceptions (feminine/plural); \"seiner\" etc. as in German."]],"krv-b-0692":["possible experience, and we can, following the guideline of those analogies, get from our actual perception to the thing in the series of possible perceptions. Thus we cognize the existence of a magnetic matter penetrating all bodies from the perception of the attracted iron filings, although an immediate perception of this material is impossible for us given the constitution of our organs. For in general we would, according to laws of sensibility and the context of our perceptions in one experience, also come upon the immediate empirical intuition of it, if our senses were finer, whose coarseness does not concern the form of possible experience in general. Where, therefore, perception and its appendage in accordance with empirical laws reaches, there our cognition of the existence of things reaches as well. If we do not begin from experience, or do not proceed in accordance with laws of the empirical interconnection of appearances, then we make a show in vain of wanting to guess or to investigate the existence of any thing.(*) A powerful objection against these rules for proving existence mediately is made, however, by idealism, whose refutation is here in the right place.",["- The initial stray \"or\" (OCR) before \"möglichen\" is dropped as not belonging to the text; the passage continues \"in einer / möglichen Erfahrung\" from the previous one. Line numbers (10, 1s, 20) omitted.","- \"Eisenfeiligs\" read as \"Eisenfeilicht\" (\"iron filings\"); \"seiner\" read as \"feiner\" (\"finer\").","- The sign `“)` is read as a footnote marker, shown as (*); the footnote text is not in the passage.","- \"Context\" is the German word (Latin-derived) and is rendered \"context\"; \"Anhang\" = \"appendage\".","- \"macht ... Staat\" (sich Staat machen) rendered literally as \"make a show\"; idiomatic, doubtful.","- Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Erscheinungen = \"appearances\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"."]],"krv-b-0693":["Refutation of Idealism.",["- Widerlegung = \"refutation\"."]],"krv-b-0694":["Idealism (I understand the material) is the theory which declares the existence of objects in space outside us to be either merely doubtful and indemonstrable, or false and impossible; the former is the problematic idealism of Descartes, who declares only one empirical assertion (*assertio*), namely: I am, to be undoubted; the second is the dogmatic idealism of Berkeley, who space",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"der den Raum\"); the English is left unfinished likewise.","- \"den materialen\" (Idealismus) is understood from the preceding noun; \"ich verstehe den materialen\" is kept in the German word order.","- \"Behauptung\" = \"assertion\"; Latin *assertio* in the German kept in italics; \"erklärt\" = \"declares\".","- \"außer uns\" = \"outside us\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"."]],"krv-b-0695":["*) The exposition that follows up to the end of note 3 on p. 193 is an addition of A2, with which the preface of A2, p. 23, note, is to be compared. with all the things to which it attaches as inseparable condition, as something that is impossible in itself, and therefore also declares the things in space to be mere imaginations. Dogmatic idealism is unavoidable if one regards space as a property that is supposed to belong to things in themselves; for then it, with everything to which it serves as condition, is a non-thing. The ground of this idealism, however, has been removed by us in the transcendental aesthetic. Problematic idealism, which asserts nothing about this, but only alleges the incapacity to prove an existence outside our own through immediate experience, is rational and in accordance with a thorough philosophical way of thinking: namely, to permit no decisive judgment before a sufficient proof has been found. The proof demanded must therefore demonstrate that we also have experience, and not merely imagination, of outer things; which presumably will not be able to happen otherwise than if one can prove that even our inner experience, undoubted by Descartes, is possible only under the presupposition of outer experience.",["- The opening is an Academy Edition editorial footnote (marked by the stray \")\"); rendered as a footnote. The text then resumes mid-sentence (\"mit allen den Dingen...\"), as in the source; the subject (idealism, the Idealism of the preceding sentence) is not supplied.","- Einbildung / Einbildungen = \"imagination(s)\"; Einbildungskraft (elsewhere) = \"imagination\" too, one English word for two German words.","- Dasein = \"existence\"; Existenz = \"existence\" (both rendered alike).","- hinreichend = \"sufficient\" (glossary's zureichend also \"sufficient\").","- Eigenschaft = \"property\" (glossary's Beschaffenheit also \"property\"). Unding = \"non-thing\". gehoben = \"removed\". OCR \"fol\" read as \"soll\".","- Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\". vernünftig = \"rational\"."]],"krv-b-0696":["Theorem.",["- Lehrsatz = \"theorem\" (glossary)."]],"krv-b-0697":["The mere, but empirically determined, consciousness of my own existence proves the existence of objects in space outside me.",["- Dasein = \"existence\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\".","- \"bestimmte\" rendered \"determined\"; beweiset = \"proves\"."]],"krv-b-0698":["Proof.",["- Beweis = \"proof\"."]],"krv-b-0699":["I am conscious of my existence as determined in time. All determination of time presupposes something persistent in perception. This persistent thing, however, cannot be something in me, since my existence in time can first be determined only through this persistent thing. Thus the perception of this persistent thing is possible only through a thing outside me and not through the mere representation of a thing outside me. Consequently the determination of my existence in time is possible only through the existence of actual things that I perceive outside me. Now consciousness in time is necessarily combined with the consciousness of the possibility of this determination of time: thus it is also necessarily combined with the existence of things outside me, as condition of the determination of time; i.e., the consciousness of my own existence is at the same time an immediate consciousness of the existence of other things outside me.",["- Dasein and Existenz both = \"existence\" (OCR \"Eristenz\" read as \"Existenz\"). Wirkliche = \"actual\".","- Beharrliche = \"persistent thing\" (beharren = persist). Zeitbestimmung = \"determination of time\". verbunden = \"combined\" (Verbindung = combination).","- \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\""]],"krv-b-0700":["Note 1. One becomes aware in the preceding proof that the game that idealism played is repaid to it, with more right, in reverse. It assumed that the only immediate experience is the inner one, and that from it outer things are only inferred, but, as always when one infers from given effects to determinate causes, only unreliably, since the cause of the representations that we perhaps falsely ascribe to outer things can also lie in ourselves. Here, however, it is proved that outer experience is actually immediate, *) that only by means of it is possible, not indeed the consciousness of our own existence, but still the determination of it in time, i.e., inner experience. To be sure the representation I am, which expresses the consciousness that can accompany all thinking, is that which immediately includes in itself the existence of a subject, but not yet any cognition of it, hence also not an empirical one, i.e., experience; for to this there belongs, besides the thought of something existing, also intuition, and here inner intuition, with respect to which, i.e., time, the subject must be determined, for which outer objects are absolutely required, so that consequently inner experience itself is possible only mediately and only through outer.",["- \"*)\" marks the reference to the footnote (next passage); the OCR line number \"10\" and stray \"s\" omitted.","- Existenz and Dasein = \"existence\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\".","- Wirkungen = \"effects\"; geschlossen (Schluß) = \"inferred\"; Ursache = \"cause\".","- \"ich bin\" is the representation named; \"die das Bewußtsein ausdrückt\" could attach to the representation or to \"ich bin\"; kept as in German order.","- \"in Ansehung deren, d. i. der Zeit\": \"deren\" is ambiguous in its antecedent (intuition / time), kept ambiguous as \"with respect to which, i.e., time\"."]],"krv-b-0701":["*) The immediate consciousness of the existence of outer things is not presupposed in the foregoing theorem, but proved, whether or not we may have insight into the possibility of this consciousness. The question regarding the latter would be: whether we have only an inner sense, but no outer one, but merely outer imagination. It is clear, however, that in order even merely to imagine something to ourselves as outer, i.e., to present it to sense in intuition, we must already have an outer sense and thereby immediately distinguish the mere receptivity of an outer intuition from the spontaneity that characterizes every imagination. For even merely to imagine to oneself an outer sense would itself annihilate the faculty of intuition, which is supposed to be determined by the power of imagination.",["- Einbildung = \"imagination\" (also verb einbilden = \"imagine\"); Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\" in the last phrase rendered \"power of imagination\" to mark the Kraft.","- Dasein = \"existence\"; Anschauungsvermögen = \"faculty of intuition\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\".","- einsehen = \"have insight into\"; darzustellen = \"present\"; vernichten = \"annihilate\".","- \"bestimmt werden soll\" read as \"is supposed to be determined\"; OCR line number \"ss\" omitted.","- \"äußere Einbildung\" rendered \"outer imagination\"; äußere = \"outer\" throughout."]],"krv-b-0702":["Note 2. With this all experiential use of our faculty of cognition in the determination of time agrees perfectly. Not only that we can perceive all determination of time only through change in outer relations (motion) in relation to the persistent thing in space (e.g., the motion of the sun with respect to the objects of the earth), but we even have nothing persistent that we could place under the concept of a substance as intuition other than merely matter, and even this persistence is not drawn from outer experience, but is presupposed *a priori* as the necessary condition of all determination of time, hence also as determination of inner sense with respect to our own existence through the existence of outer things. The consciousness of myself in the representation I is no intuition at all, but a merely intellectual representation of the self-activity of a thinking subject. Hence this I also does not have the least predicate of intuition that, as persistent, could serve as correlate for the determination of time in inner sense; as, say, impenetrability is in matter as empirical intuition.",["- Erfahrungsgebrauch = \"experiential use\" (literally \"use of experience\"); Gebrauch = \"use\". Erkenntnißvermögen = \"faculty of cognition\".","- Wechsel = \"change\"; Beharrlichkeit = \"persistence\"; Beharrliche = \"persistent thing\"; Wahrnehmung (wahrnehmen) = \"perception\" (\"perceive\").","- Dasein and Existenz = \"existence\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\".","- The sentence beginning \"Nicht allein, daß..., so haben wir...\" is kept as one, with the German's irregular construction.","- \"Un durchdring lichkeit\" (OCR) read as Undurchdringlichkeit, \"impenetrability\" (spaced in the source, i.e. emphasized)."]],"krv-b-0703":["Note 3. From the fact that the existence of outer objects is required for the possibility of a determinate consciousness of ourselves, it does not follow that every intuitive representation of outer things at the same time includes the existence of them, for the former can very well be the mere effect of the imagination (in dreams as well as in madness); it is so, however, merely through the reproduction of former outer perceptions, which, as has been shown, are possible only through the actuality of outer objects. Here it was only supposed to be proved that inner experience in general is possible only through outer experience in general. Whether this or that supposed experience is not mere imagination must be ascertained according to its particular determinations and by holding it together with the criteria of all actual experience.”)",["- Existenz = \"existence\"; Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\" (wirklich = \"actual\"); Einbildungskraft and Einbildung both = \"imagination\".","- anschauliche Vorstellung = \"intuitive representation\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Kriterien = \"criteria\"; Bestimmungen = \"determinations\"; besondern = \"particular\".","- Zusammenhaltung = \"holding together\" (literal); ausgemittelt = \"ascertained\"; vermeinte = \"supposed\". bloß = \"mere(ly)\".","- The closing ”) is carried over from the source (OCR); its opening mark is not in this passage."]],"krv-b-0704":["As for the third postulate, finally, it goes to the material necessity in existence and not the merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of concepts. Since, now, no existence of the objects of the senses can be cognized entirely a priori, but yet *comparative* a priori, relative to another existence already given, yet one can nevertheless then too arrive only at that existence which must be contained somewhere in the interconnection of experience, of which the given perception is a part: so the necessity of existence can never be cognized from concepts, but always only from the connection with that which is perceived, in accordance with universal laws of experience. Now there is no existence that could be cognized as necessary under the condition of other given appearances except the existence of effects from given causes in accordance with laws of causality. Thus it is not the existence of things (substances), but of their state, of which alone we can cognize the necessity, and indeed from other states that are given in perception, in accordance with empirical laws of causality. From this it follows that the criterion of necessity lies solely in the law of possible experience: that everything that happens is determined a priori through its cause in the appearance. Hence we cognize only the necessity of the effects in nature, whose causes are given to us; and the mark of necessity in existence extends no further than the field of possible experience, and even in this it does not hold of the existence of things as substances, because these can never be regarded as empirical effects or as something that happens and arises. Necessity therefore concerns only the relations of appearances in accordance with the dynamical law of causality and the possibility grounded upon it, of inferring a priori from any given existence (a cause) to another existence (of the effect). Everything that happens is hypothetically necessary; that is a principle which subjects alteration in the world to a law, i.e., to a rule of necessary existence, without which not even nature would take place at all. Hence the proposition: nothing happens through a blind chance (*in mundo non datur casus*), is a law of nature a priori; likewise: no necessity in nature is blind, but conditioned, hence comprehensible necessity (*non datur fatum*). Both are such laws through which the play of alterations is subjected to a nature of things (as appearances) or, which is the same, to the unity of the understanding, in which alone they can belong to an experience as the synthetic unity of appearances. These two principles belong to the dynamical ones. The former is properly a consequence of the principle of causality (among the analogies of experience). The second belongs to the principles of modality, which adds to the determination of causality the concept of necessity, which however stands under a rule of the understanding. The principle of continuity forbade in the series of appearances (alterations) every leap (*in mundo non datur saltus*), but also in the sum total of all empirical intuitions in space every gap or chasm between two appearances (*non datur hiatus*); for so one can express the proposition: that nothing can come into experience that would prove a *vacuum*, or even only admit it as a part of the empirical synthesis. For as for the void, which one may think outside the field of possible experience (the world), this does not belong before the jurisdiction of the mere understanding, which decides only about the questions that concern the utilization of given appearances for empirical cognition, and is a problem for ideal reason, which goes still beyond the sphere of a possible experience and wants to judge about that which surrounds and bounds this itself, and must therefore be weighed in the transcendental dialectic. These four propositions (*in mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum*) we could easily, like all principles of transcendental origin, present according to their order in accordance with the order of the categories and prove to each its place, but the already practiced reader will do this of himself, or easily discover the guiding thread to it. They all unite, however, solely to this, to admit nothing in the empirical synthesis that could do detriment or injury to the understanding and to the continuous interconnection of all appearances, i.e., to the unity of its concepts. For it alone is that in which the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their place, becomes possible.",["- Editorial corrections of OCR: \"diebloß\" read \"die bloß\"; \"wer den\" \"werden\"; \"je— dem\" \"jedem\"; \"Caufabestimmung\" read \"Causabestimmung\" (rendered \"determination of causality\"); stray \"5\" (line number) omitted.","- Dasein and Existenz both rendered \"existence\" (Dasein throughout this passage; Existenz in \"Existenz der Dinge\" and \"Existenz der Gegenstände\"? — Existenz in \"keine Existenz der Gegenstände\", \"diejenige Existenz\", \"Existenz der Dinge als Substanzen\"; Dasein elsewhere).","- Gegenstände = objects; Grundsatz = principle (rendered \"principle\"), Satz = proposition; Princip = principle.","- \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Zusammenhang\" = interconnection; \"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"Aufgabe\" = problem.","- \"geht es auf\" rendered literally \"goes to\"; \"comparative a priori, relativisch\" kept as in German (\"comparative\" italicized as roman Latinate word; \"relativisch\" = \"relative\").","- \"Nutzung\" rendered \"utilization\" (not in glossary); \"idealische Vernunft\" = \"ideal reason\"; \"vorstellig machen\" = \"present\".","- \"Veränderung\" = alteration; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"erkennen\" = cognize; \"verständliche\" = \"comprehensible\" (cf. Verstand, \"understanding\").","- Ambiguity kept: \"durch seine Ursache in der Erscheinung a priori bestimmt\" — \"in the appearance\" and \"a priori\" may attach to \"determined\" or to \"cause\"; \"welche ... hinzu thut\" subject of \"die aber unter einer Regel des Verstandes steht\" may be Nothwendigkeit only as rendered."]],"krv-b-0705":["Whether the field of possibility is greater than the field that contains everything actual, but this in turn greater than the multitude of that which is necessary, are nice questions, and indeed ones of synthetic resolution, which, however, also fall only to the jurisdiction of reason; for they want to say roughly as much as whether all things as appearances belong collectively to the sum total and the context of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part, which therefore could not be combined with any other appearances, or whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience (in their general interconnection). The understanding gives a priori to experience in general only the rule according to the subjective and formal conditions of sensibility as well as of apperception, which alone make it possible. Other forms of intuition (than space and time), likewise other forms of the understanding (than the discursive one of thinking or of cognition through concepts), even if they were possible, we can nevertheless in no way think up and make comprehensible; but even if we could, they would still not belong to experience as the sole cognition in which objects are given to us. Whether other perceptions than those that belong at all to our entire possible experience, and thus a quite different field of matter, can still take place, the understanding cannot decide, it has to do only with the synthesis of that which is given. Otherwise the paltriness of our usual inferences, by which we bring out a great realm of possibility, of which everything actual (every object of experience) is only a small part, is very striking. Everything actual is possible; from this there follows naturally, according to the logical rules of conversion, the merely particular proposition: some possible is actual, which then seems to signify as much as: much is possible that is not actual. It does indeed have the appearance as if one could also straightaway posit the number of the possible beyond that of the actual, because something must still be added to the former in order to constitute the latter. But this adding to the possible I do not know. For what was supposed to be added still beyond it would be impossible. Only to my understanding can something be added beyond the agreement with the formal conditions of experience, namely the connection with some perception; but what is connected with this in accordance with empirical laws is actual, even though it is not immediately perceived. But that in thoroughgoing interconnection with that which is given to me in perception another series of appearances, hence more than a single all-encompassing experience, is possible, cannot be concluded from what is given, and still much less without anything being given, since without material nothing at all can be thought. What is possible only under conditions that are themselves merely possible, is not so in every respect. But in this respect the question is taken, when one wants to know whether the possibility of things extends further than experience can reach.",["- Feld der Möglichkeit / Menge / Zahl: \"field of possibility\", \"multitude\", \"number\". Wirkliche / wirklich = \"actual\"; Realität would be \"reality\".","- \"artige Fragen\": \"nice\" is literal for artig. \"Auflösung\" = \"resolution\"; \"Gerichtsbarkeit\" = \"jurisdiction\".","- \"der also mit keinen andern Erscheinungen könne verbunden werden\": the relative \"der\" could refer to \"Theil\" or to \"Erfahrung\"; the ambiguity is kept (\"which\").","- Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Stoff = \"material\"; Materie = \"matter\".","- \"kenne ich nicht\" = \"I do not know\" (kennen); \"in aller Absicht\" = \"in every respect\" (Absicht otherwise \"aim\").","- \"Schlüsse\" = \"inferences\"; \"schließen\" = \"concluded\"; \"folgt\" = \"follows\"; \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\" (the sole occurrence here).","- \"Migliche\" in the OCR corrected to \"Mögliches\"; the line numbers in the text have been omitted."]],"krv-b-0706":["I have made mention of these questions only in order to leave no gap in that which, according to common opinion, belongs to the understandingin every respect valid is) no mere concept of the understanding and can in no way be of empirical use, but belongs solely to reason, which goes beyond all possible empirical use of the understanding. Hence we have had to content ourselves here with a merely critical remark, but for the rest have left the matter in obscurity until further future procedure.",["- The text is corrupt after \"Verstandes\" (OCR run-together of lines). The sentence breaks off and resumes mid-parenthesis (\"...aller Absicht gültig ist)\"); nothing has been supplied, and the opening of the parenthesis is missing.","- \"auf seiner lei Weise\" in the OCR was read as \"auf keinerlei Weise\" (\"in no way\").","- \"gemeinen Meinung\" = \"common opinion\" (Meinung); \"Anmerkung\" = \"remark\"; \"Erwähnung\" = \"mention\".","- \"in aller Absicht\" = \"in every respect\" (Absicht otherwise \"aim\")."]],"krv-b-0707":["Since I want to close precisely this fourth number, and with it at the same time the system of all principles of the pure understanding, I must still give the ground why I have called the principles of modality precisely postulates. I will not take this expression here in the meaning that some recent philosophical authors have given it, against the sense of the mathematicians, to whom it properly belongs, namely: that to postulate is supposed to mean as much as to pass off a proposition as immediately certain without justification or proof; for if we were to concede, in the case of synthetic propositions, however evident they may be, that one may fasten them, without deduction, on the authority of their own pronouncement, to unconditioned assent, then all critique of the understanding is lost, and since there is no lack of bold pretensions, which even common belief (which, however, is no credential) does not refuse: so our understanding will stand open to every delusion, without being able to refuse its assent to pronouncements that, although unlawfully, demand nevertheless to be admitted in the very same tone of confidence as actual axioms. If, therefore, a determination is added synthetically a priori to the concept of a thing, then to such a proposition, if not a proof, at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion must indispensably be added.",["- \"Satz\" = \"proposition\"; \"Grundsatz\" = \"principle\"; \"Principien\" = \"principles\" (here Latinate, same English word).","- \"Glaube\" = \"belief\" (glossary: belief; faith); \"Beweis\" = \"proof\"; \"Behauptung\" = \"assertion\"; \"Ausdruck\" = \"expression\"; \"Bedeutung\" = \"meaning\".","- \"eine Bestimmung a priori synthetisch hinzukommt\": \"a priori\" could attach to \"Bestimmung\" or to \"synthetisch\"/\"hinzukommt\"; the ambiguity is kept in \"added synthetically a priori\".","- \"Beifall\" = \"assent\"; \"Rechtmäßigkeit\" = \"legitimacy\"; \"unnachlaßlich\" = \"indispensably\"; \"dreusten\" read as \"dreisten\" (\"bold\"). \"Peifall\" in the OCR corrected to \"Beifall\".","- \"Ansehen ihres eigenen Ausspruchs\": \"authority of their own pronouncement\" (Ansehen could also be \"appearance\"; \"authority\" is chosen)."]],"krv-b-0708":["The principles of modality, however, are not objectively synthetic, because the predicates of possibility, actuality and necessity do not in the least augment the concept of which they are said, by adding something further to the representation of the object. But since they are nevertheless always synthetic, they are so only subjectively, i.e., they add to the concept of a thing (of something real), of which they otherwise say nothing, the power of cognition in which it arises and has its seat: so that, if it is merely in the understanding in connection with the formal conditions of experience, its object is called possible; if it is in interconnection with perception (sensation as matter of the senses) and determined through it by means of the understanding, then the object is actual; if it is determined through the interconnection of perceptions in accordance with concepts, then the object is called necessary. The principles of modality therefore say of a concept nothing other than the action of the faculty of cognition through which it is generated. Now a postulate in mathematics is called the practical proposition that contains nothing but the synthesis through which we first give ourselves an object and generate its concept, e.g., with a given line, from a given point on a plane, to describe a circle; and a proposition of this kind cannot be proven for this reason, because the procedure that it demands is precisely that through which we first generate the concept of such a figure. Thus we can with just the same right postulate the principles of modality, because they do not augment their concept of things in general,¹) but only indicate the way in which it is combined with the power of cognition in general.",["- \"Gegenstand\" and \"Object\" both = \"object\" (\"das Object wirklich\", \"der Gegenstand nothwendig\").","- \"Erkenntnißkraft\" = \"power of cognition\"; \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = \"faculty of cognition\".","- \"Zusammenhang\" = \"interconnection\"; \"Verknüpfung\" = \"connection\"; \"verbunden\" = \"combined\".","- \"Wirklichkeit\" = \"actuality\"; \"Realen\" = \"something real\" (substantivized, \"(Realen)\").","- \"ihren Begriff\": \"their\" (the principles') or \"its/their\" concept is left as in the German; \"er\" in the last clause likewise refers back to \"Begriff\".","- \"hinzusetzten\" in the OCR read as \"hinzusetzen\"; the sign \"“)\" after \"vermehren,\" marks a footnote whose text is not in the passage, rendered \"¹)\".","- \"dadurch daß sie ... hinzusetzen\": \"by adding\" renders the construction literally. The passage's page range is marked uncertain in the source."]],"krv-b-0709":["General Remark on the System of Principles.¹)",["- \"Anmerkung\" = \"remark\"; \"Grundsätze\" = \"principles\". The sign \"“)\" marks a footnote whose text is not in the passage, rendered \"¹)\"."]],"krv-b-0710":["It is something very noteworthy that we cannot have insight into the possibility of any thing from the mere category, but must always have an intuition at hand, in order to exhibit by it the objective reality of the pure concept of the understanding. Take, e.g., the categories of relation. How 1) something can exist only as subject, not as mere determination of other things, i.e., be substance, or how 2) because something is, something else must be, hence how something in general can be cause, or 3) how, if several things are there, from the fact that one of them is there, something follows for the remaining ones and so reciprocally, and in this way a community of substances can take place, cannot be seen at all from mere concepts. The very same holds also of the remaining categories, e.g., how a thing can be one and the same with many together, i.e., a magnitude, etc. So",["- The passage breaks off after \"So\"; nothing is supplied.","- \"einsehen\" = \"have insight into\" / \"seen\"; \"darlegen\" = \"exhibit\"; \"Anschauung\" = \"intuition\"; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\"; \"Gemeinschaft\" = \"community\"; \"Größe\" = \"magnitude\".","- \"Realität\" = \"reality\"; \"dasind\" in the OCR read as \"da sind\"; \"z. die\" read as \"z. B.\"; \"u. s. w.\" rendered \"etc.\"","- \"etwas ... folge\" = \"something follows\" (Folge/folgen); \"Statt haben\" = \"take place\"."]],"krv-b-0711":["Through the actuality of a thing I certainly posit more than the possibility, but not in the thing; for that can never contain more in actuality than what was contained in its complete possibility. Rather, since the possibility was merely a position of the thing in relation to the understanding (its empirical use), actuality is at the same time a connection of it with perception. As long as intuition is lacking, then, one does not know whether one thinks an object through the categories, and whether any object at all could belong to them anywhere, and thus it is confirmed that they are in themselves no cognitions at all, but mere forms of thought, in order to make cognitions out of given intuitions. — Precisely from this it also comes about that from mere categories no synthetic proposition can be made. E.g., in all existence there is substance, i.e., something that can exist only as subject and not as mere predicate; or every thing is a *quantum*, etc., where there is nothing at all that could serve us to go beyond a given concept and to connect another with it. Hence it has also never succeeded in proving a synthetic proposition from mere pure concepts of the understanding, e.g., the proposition: everything contingently existing has a cause. One could never get further than to prove that without this relation we could not comprehend the existence of the contingent at all, i.e., could not cognize *a priori* through the understanding the existence of such a thing; from which, however, it does not follow that this very same is also the condition of the possibility of the things themselves. If one therefore wants to look back at our proof of the principle of causality, one will become aware that we could prove it only of objects of possible experience: everything that happens, (every occurrence) presupposes a cause, and indeed in such a way that we could prove it also only as principle of the possibility of experience, hence of the cognition of an object given in empirical intuition, and not from mere concepts. That nevertheless the proposition, everything contingent must have a cause, is evident to everyone from mere concepts, is not to be denied; but then the concept of the contingent is already grasped in such a way that it contains not the category of modality (as something whose non-being can be thought), but that of relation (as something that can exist only as sequel of another), and there it is indeed an identical proposition: What can exist only as sequel has its cause. In fact, when we are to give examples of contingent existence, we always appeal to alterations and not merely to the possibility of the thought of the opposite.”) Alter-",["- Stray \")\" at the start (OCR remnant of a footnote asterisk) omitted; the footnote marker after \"opposite.”\" kept as \")\"; footnote text is krv-b-0712.","- Wirklichkeit = actuality; Dasein and Existenz both = \"existence\"; Grundsatz and Princip both = \"principle\"; Ding and Sache both = \"thing(s)\" (Sachen in \"der Sachen selbst\").","- Folge = \"sequel\" here (cf. \"sequence\" in krv-b-0713); folgen = \"follow\"; Veränderung = \"alteration\" (not in glossary); Verknüpfung = \"connection\".","- OCR corrected: fege→setze, lange→So lange, Ouantum→Quantum, Dbjecten→Objecten, seohen→sehen.","- \"dieselbe\" (feminine) in \"daß eben dieselbe auch die Bedingung … sei\" is left without a stated antecedent; \"dessen\" in \"dessen empirischen Gebrauch\" could refer to Ding or Verstand (kept ambiguous as \"its\")."]],"krv-b-0712":[") One can easily think the non-being of matter, but the ancients did not conclude from this its contingency. But even the alternation of being and non-being of a given state of a thing, in which all alteration consists, does not at all prove the contingency of this state, as it were from the actuality ation however is occurrence, which as such is possible only through a cause, whose non-being is therefore possible by itself, and thus one cognizes the contingency from this, that something can exist only as effect of a cause; if therefore a thing is assumed as contingent, then it is an analytic proposition to say that it has a cause. 5",["- Footnote text, interleaved in the source with the main text: \"derung aber ist Begebenheit\" is the continuation of \"Verän-\" at the end of krv-b-0711 (rendered \"Alter-\" / \"ation\"); the footnote's sentence continues at \"seines Gegentheils\" in krv-b-0713. Left as it stands.","- Leading \")\" is the footnote marker; \"fann\"→\"kann\" and \"moglich\"→\"möglich\" corrected; the stray line number \"5\" is left at the end, as it stands in the passage.","- Zufälligkeit = contingency; Begebenheit = occurrence; Folgerung/folgern = \"conclude\"; Wechsel = \"alternation\"; für sich = \"by itself\" (distinct from an sich)."]],"krv-b-0713":["More remarkable still, however, is that we, in order to understand the possibility of things in accordance with the categories and thus to demonstrate the objective reality of the latter, need not merely intuitions, but even always outer intuitions. If we take, e.g., the pure concepts of relation, we find that 1) in order to give, corresponding to the concept of substance, something persistent in intuition (and thereby to demonstrate the objective reality of this concept), we need an intuition in space (of matter), because space alone is determined as persisting, but time, hence everything that is in inner sense, flows constantly. 2) In order to exhibit alteration, as the intuition corresponding to the concept of causality, we must take motion as alteration in space for an example, indeed only through this can we make alterations, whose possibility no pure understanding can comprehend, intuitive. Alteration is combination of contradictorily opposed determinations in the existence of one and the same thing. How it is now possible that from a given state an opposed one of the same thing should follow, not only can no reason make comprehensible to itself without an example, but not even understandable without intuition; and this intuition is that of the motion of a point in space, whose existence in different places (as a sequence of opposed determinations) first makes alteration intuitive to us alone; for in order afterwards to make even inner alterations thinkable to ourselves, we must represent time as the form of inner sense figuratively by a line, and the inner alteration through the drawing of this line (motion), hence the successive of its opposite, e.g., the rest of a body, which follows upon the motion, not yet the contingency of the motion of the same from this, because the former is the opposite of the latter. For this opposite is here opposed to the other only logically, not *realiter*. One would have to prove that, instead of the motion in the preceding point of time, it would have been possible that the body had been at rest then, in order to prove the contingency of its motion, not that it is at rest afterwards; for then both opposites can very well subsist with one another.",["- The footnote text (\"seines Gegentheils, z. B. die Ruhe eines Körpers …\") is interleaved in the source after \"mithin die successive\"; it continues krv-b-0712, where \"beweiset\" is the verb left unrepeated here (\"not yet the contingency …\"). Main text resumes in krv-b-0714. Left as it stands.","- Stray OCR letters and line numbers omitted; \"Beharrlich es\", \"Beg riffs\", \"Pewegung\", \"begreisen\" corrected.","- Beharrliches = \"something persistent\" (beharren = persist); Folge = \"sequence\" here; Verbindung = \"combination\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Ort = \"place\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; anschaulich = \"intuitive\".","- \"zuerst uns allein\" is ambiguous as to whether \"allein\" attaches to \"zuerst\" or \"uns\"; rendered \"first makes … intuitive to us alone\", which keeps \"alone\" attachable to either.","- \"die successive\": noun omitted in the source at the break; \"successive\" is kept as a bare adjective. \"begreiflich\" = comprehensible; \"verständlich\" = understandable; \"denkbar\" = thinkable."]],"krv-b-0714":["existence of ourselves in different states through outer intuition",["- Continues the main-text sentence of krv-b-0713 (\"hence the successive … existence of ourselves\"); the passage is a fragment.","- Zustand = state; Existenz = existence."]],"krv-b-0715":["make graspable to us; of which the proper ground is this, that all alteration presupposes something persistent in intuition, in order even",["- \"faßlich\" = graspable (distinct from begreiflich/verständlich, rendered \"comprehensible\"/\"understandable\").","- Breaks off mid-clause; continued in krv-b-0716."]],"krv-b-0716":["itself only to be perceived as alteration, but in inner sense no persistent intuition at all is encountered. — Finally the category of community in respect of its possibility is not at all to be comprehended through mere reason, and thus the objective reality of this con-",["- Stray \"s\" (line number) omitted. Gemeinschaft = community; Vernunft = reason; begreifen = comprehend; wahrgenommen = perceived (Wahrnehmung = perception).","- Word broken at \"Be-/griffs\" (Begriff = concept): rendered \"con-\" / \"cept\"."]],"krv-b-0717":["cept without intuition and indeed outer in space not to be seen into pos-",["- Word broken: \"mög-\" / \"lich\" (krv-b-0718). \"nicht einzusehen möglich\" can be read \"not possible to see into\" or \"not to be seen into as possible\"; the English keeps the German order to leave both open.","- \"und zwar äußere\" has no noun repeated (Anschauung understood); left elliptical."]],"krv-b-0718":["sible. For how does one want to think the possibility that, if several",["- Fragment; the \"that\" clause continues through krv-b-0722."]],"krv-b-0719":["substances exist, from the existence of the one on the existence of the oth-",["- Word broken: \"an-\" / \"deren\" (krv-b-0720). Substanz = substance."]],"krv-b-0720":["ers reciprocally something (as effect) could follow, and thus, because in",["- wechselseitig = \"reciprocally\"; Wirkung = effect. The placement of \"wechselseitig\" before \"etwas\" is kept."]],"krv-b-0721":["the former there is something, therefore also in the others there must be something that",["- Fragment of a sentence; \"that\" is the relative pronoun continued in krv-b-0722."]],"krv-b-0722":["cannot be understood from the existence of the latter alone? For",["- verstanden = understood (verstehen = understand)."]],"krv-b-0723":["this is required for community, but is not at all comprehensible among things that each isolate themselves completely through their subsistence. Hence",["- Stray line number \"5\" omitted. \"ein jedes\" is rendered \"each\"; \"sich … isoliren\" kept reflexive. Subsistenz = \"subsistence\"."]],"krv-b-0724":["Leibniz, in that he, to the substances of the world, only as the understanding",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; not completed. \"den Substanzen\" is dative, and no verb is yet given; \"Verstand\" = understanding.","- \"nur wie sie der Verstand\" is a fragment, left as it stands."]],"krv-b-0725":["alone thinks, attributed a community, needed a deity for mediation; for from their existence alone it seemed to him, with right, incompre-| hensible. But we can make the possibility of community (of the substances as appearances) quite graspable to ourselves if we represent it to ourselves in space, thus in outer intuition. For this already contains *a priori* formal outer relations as conditions of the possibility of the real ones (in effect and counter-effect, hence of community) in itself. — Just so it can easily be demonstrated that the possibility of things as magnitudes, and thus the objective reality of the category of magnitude, can also be exhibited only in outer intuition and by means of it alone afterwards also be applied to inner sense. But I must, in order to avoid prolixity, leave the examples of this to the reflection of the reader. | This whole remark is of great importance, not only to confirm our previous refutation of idealism, but rather still more, when there will be talk of self-cognition from mere inner consciousness and of the determination of our nature without the assistance of out-",["- The passage begins mid-sentence and ends mid-word (\"äuße-\"; the next passage opens with \"rer\"). Stray OCR marks (line numbers, quotation marks) are dropped.","- faßlich = \"graspable\" (kept distinct from begreiflich/unbegreiflich = comprehensible/incomprehensible). Nachdenken = \"reflection\" (not Überlegung).","- \"der realen\": the noun is elided in the German (\"the real ones\"); the elision is preserved. Dasein = existence; Gemeinschaft = community; Anschauung = intuition.","- Dargethan = \"demonstrated\"; dargelegt = \"exhibited\". Weitläuftigkeit = \"prolixity\".","- Idealisms = \"idealism\"; Selbsterkenntnisse = \"self-cognition\"."]],"krv-b-0726":["-er empirical intuitions, to indicate to us the limitations of the possibility of such a cognition. The last consequence of this whole section is therefore: All principles of the pure understanding are nothing further than principles *a priori* of the possibility of experience, and to the latter alone are all synthetic propositions *a priori* also related, indeed their possibility itself rests entirely on this relation.",["- Begins with the end of the word \"äußerer\" split from the preceding passage. The syntax (\"um ... uns ... anzuzeigen\") continues from it, with \"wenn ... die Rede sein wird\" as the intervening clause.","- Grundsätze and Principien are both \"principles\"; Sätze = \"propositions\"; Folgerung = \"consequence\"; Schranken = \"limitations\".","- The stray footnote-like mark after \"Beziehung.\" is OCR residue and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0727":["Of the\nTranscendental Doctrine of the Power of Judgment\n(Analytic of Principles)\nThird Chapter.",["- Urtheilskraft = \"power of judgment\"; Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\"."]],"krv-b-0728":["On the ground of the distinction of all objects",["- Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Grund = \"ground\"."]],"krv-b-0729":["in general\ninto\n*phenomena* and *noumena*.",["- The title continues across passages 0728–0729. *Phaenomena* and *Noumena* are Latin words, hence italicized."]],"krv-b-0730":["We have now not only traveled through the land of the pure understanding and carefully inspected every part of it, but have also measured it through and determined for each thing on it its place. But this land is an island and enclosed by nature itself in unalterable boundaries. It is the land of truth (an enchanting name), surrounded by a broad and stormy ocean, the proper seat of illusion, where many a fog bank and many an ice soon melting away feigns new lands and, in that it the one on discoveries",["- Breaks off mid-sentence: \"indem es den auf Entdeckungen\" is completed by the next passage (\"herumschwärmenden Seefahrer ... täuscht\"), so the verb and noun are missing here and are not supplied.","- OCR \"megs schmelzende\" corrected to \"wegschmelzende\". Grenzen = \"boundaries\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; lügt = \"feigns\".","- Stelle = \"place\" (Ort in the glossary is also \"place\")."]],"krv-b-0731":["roaming seafarer incessantly deceives with empty hopes, entangles him in adventures from which he can never desist and yet also never bring them to an end. But before we venture onto this sea, in order to search it in all latitudes and to become certain whether anything in them is to be hoped for, it will be useful first to cast one more glance at the map of the land that we are just about to leave, and, firstly, to ask whether we could not at all events be satisfied with what it contains in itself, or must also out of need be satisfied, if there is otherwise nowhere any soil on which we could settle; secondly, under what title we ourselves possess this land and can hold ourselves secured against all hostile claims. Although we have already answered these questions sufficiently in the course of the Analytic, a summary survey of their solutions can nevertheless strengthen conviction by uniting the moments of them in one point.",["- Begins mid-sentence (\"herumschwärmenden\" modifies the \"Seefahrer\" whose article \"den\" stands at the end of the previous passage). The stray OCR \"SJ\" is omitted.","- Überzeugung = \"conviction\"; Auflösungen = \"solutions\"; Momente = \"moments\"; Boden = \"soil\" (not Grund = \"ground\"); Anspruch = \"claim\".","- \"so wird es nützlich sein\" follows the \"Ehe ... \" clause; the German \"so\" is left untranslated as in the English construction."]],"krv-b-0732":["We have seen, namely: that everything that the understanding creates from itself, without borrowing it from experience, that it has nevertheless for no other purpose than solely for the use in experience. The principles of the pure understanding, whether they may now be constitutive *a priori* (like the mathematical ones), or merely regulative (like the dynamical ones), contain nothing but, as it were, only the pure schema for possible experience; for the latter has its unity only from the synthetic unity that the understanding originally and of itself imparts to the synthesis of the imagination in relation to apperception, and to which the appearances, as *data* for a possible cognition, must already stand *a priori* in relation and accord. But although these rules of the understanding are not only true *a priori*, but even the source of all truth, i.e., of the agreement of our cognition with objects, by containing in themselves the ground of the possibility of experience as the sum total of all cognition in which objects may be given to us, it nevertheless seems to us not enough to let oneself merely be presented with that which is true, but with what one desires to know. If we therefore learn nothing more through this critical investigation than what we would well have exercised of ourselves in the merely empirical use of the understanding even without such subtle inquiry, it seems the advantage one draws from it is not worth the expense and preparation. Now one can indeed answer to this: that no curiosity is more detrimental to the extension of our cognition than that which always wants to know the utility beforehand, before one engages in inquiries, and before one could yet form the slightest concept of this utility, even if it were placed before one's eyes. But there is nevertheless an advantage that can be made comprehensible and at the same time a concern even to the most difficult and unwilling pupil of such transcendental inquiry, namely this: that the understanding occupied merely with its empirical use, which does not ponder the sources of its own cognition, can indeed get on very well, but cannot accomplish one thing at all, namely to determine for itself the boundaries of its use and to know what may lie inside or outside its whole sphere; for to this precisely the deep investigations that we have undertaken are required. But if it cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie in its horizon or not, then it is never secure of its claims and its possession, but may only reckon on manifold shaming rebukes, if it incessantly oversteps the boundaries of its territory (as is unavoidable) and strays into delusion and mirages.",["- Stray OCR line numbers and the broken hyphenations (\"Zu— rüstung\", \"unaufhör⸗ lich\") are silently corrected.","- Erfahrungsgebrauch = \"use in experience\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\"; Einheit = \"unity\"; Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Grenzen = \"boundaries\"; Gebiet = \"territory\".","- *a priori* and *data* are Latin and italicized; *a priori* after \"wahr\" and \"Beziehung und Einstimmung\" is kept in the German position.","- begreiflich = \"comprehensible\"; angelegen gemacht = \"made a concern\"; nachsinnt = \"ponders\"; Vorwitz = \"curiosity\"; Blendwerke = \"mirages\" (a doubtful choice of English; the word is not in the glossary).","- \"sich bloß dasjenige vortragen zu lassen, was wahr ist, sondern was man zu wissen begehrt\" is elliptical in the German, and the ellipsis is kept (\"with that which is true, but with what\")."]],"krv-b-0733":["That the understanding can make of all its principles *a priori*, indeed of all its concepts, no other than an empirical use, but never a transcendental one, is a proposition that, if it can be cognized with conviction, looks out to important consequences. The transcendental use of a concept in any principle is this: that it is related to things in general and in themselves, but the empirical use, if it is related merely to appearances, i.e., objects of a possible experience. But that only the latter can take place anywhere, one sees from this. For every concept there is required first the logical form of a concept (of thinking) in general, and then second also the possibility of giving it an object to which it may be related. Without this latter it has no sense and is entirely empty of content, even though it may still always contain the logical function of making a concept out of whatever *data* there may be. Now the object cannot be given to a concept otherwise than in intuition, and if a pure intuition is still possible *a priori* prior to the object, then even this itself can acquire its object, hence objective validity, only through empirical intuition, of which it is the mere form. Thus all concepts, and with them all principles, however much they may be possible *a priori*, nevertheless are related to empirical intuitions, i.e., to *data* for possible experience. Without this they have no objective validity at all, but are a mere play, whether of the imagination or of the understanding, with their representations respectively. One need only take the concepts of mathematics as an example, and indeed first in their pure intuitions. Space has three dimensions, between two points there can be only one straight line, etc. Although all these principles and the representation of the object with which that science occupies itself are generated entirely *a priori* in the mind, they would nevertheless signify nothing at all if we could not always exhibit their significance in appearances (empirical objects). Hence one also requires that an abstracted concept be made sensible, i.e., that the object corresponding to it be exhibited in intuition, since without this the concept would remain (as one says) without sense, i.e., without meaning. Mathematics fulfills this demand through the construction of the figure, which is an appearance present to the senses (although brought about *a priori*). The concept of magnitude seeks in the same science its support and sense in number, but this in turn in the fingers, the beads of the abacus, or the strokes and points that are placed before the eyes. The concept always remains generated *a priori*, together with the synthetic principles or formulas from such concepts; but the use of them and relation to putative objects can in the end be sought nowhere except in experience, the possibility of which (as to form) they contain *a priori*.",["- Verstand = understanding; Grundsatz = principle; Begriff = concept; Gebrauch = use; Überzeugung = conviction; Folge = consequence (here \"Folgen\").","- \"Gegenstände\" and \"Object\" both = \"object\"; \"Gegenstand\" in the passage, \"Object\" in \"das ihm correspondirende Object\".","- \"Bedeutung\"/\"bedeuten\" = meaning/signify: rendered \"signify/significance\" in \"bedeuten,\" \"Bedeutung\" (first use) and \"meaning\" where Kant glosses \"ohne Sinn, d. i. ohne Bedeutung\" (sense/meaning).","- \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination.","- \"darauf er sich beziehe\": \"to which it may be related\" (subjunctive preserved).","- \"dieser\" in \"daß er auf Dinge...\" — \"er\" refers to the concept (Begriff); \"Dinge überhaupt und an sich selbst\" is kept as \"things in general and in themselves\" (the German \"an sich selbst\" may attach to Dinge).","- \"wenn eine reine Anschauung noch vor dem Gegenstande a priori möglich ist\": \"a priori\" could attach to \"vor dem Gegenstande\" or to \"möglich\"; left ambiguous.","- OCR: \"mög» lich\" = möglich; \"2 Grundsätze\" read \"Grundsätze\"; \"Haltung\" = support; \"Corallen\" = beads; \"Gestalt\" = figure."]],"krv-b-0734":["That this is also the case with all categories and the principles spun from them is also evident from this: that we cannot define a single one of them really [*real*]¹) i.e., make the possibility of its object understandable,¹) without at once descending to conditions of sensibility, hence to the form of appearances, to which, as their sole objects, they consequently must be limited: because, if one removes this condition, all meaning, i.e., relation to the object, falls away, and one cannot make comprehensible to oneself through any example what sort of thing is actually meant under such concepts. °)",["- OCR: \"real)\" and \"machen!)\" carry stray note marks (footnote signs) that I have kept as ¹) and ¹); the footnote text is not in the passage. \"°)\" at the end is likewise a note sign.","- \"real\" is Kant's roman-type Latinate adverb, so italicized.","- \"Bedeutung\" = meaning here (cf. prior passage); \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"Object\" = object.","- \"faßlich\" = comprehensible; \"herabzulassen\" = descending.","- \"als auf welche ... sie folglich eingeschränkt sein müssen\": the relative construction kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-0735":["The concept of magnitude in general no one can explain, except perhaps thus: that it is the determination of a thing through which it can be thought how many times one is posited in it. But this How-many-times is grounded on successive repetition, hence on time and the synthesis (of the homogeneous) in it. Reality, in opposition to negation, can be explained only when one thinks of a time (as the sum total of all being) that is either filled with something or empty. If I leave out persistence (which is an existence at all times), nothing remains to me for the concept of substance except the logical representation of the subject, which I believe I realize by representing to myself something that can occur merely as subject (without being a predicate of anything). But not only do I know no conditions at all under which this logical preeminence will belong to any thing: nothing further at all can be made of it, and not the slightest consequence drawn, because thereby no object of the use of this concept is determined, and so one does not know at all whether this signifies anything anywhere. From the concept of cause (if I leave out the time in which something follows upon something else according to a rule) I would find in the pure category nothing further than that it is something from which the existence of another can be inferred; and thereby not only could cause and effect not be distinguished from one another at all, but, because this being-able-to-infer soon requires conditions of which I know nothing, the concept would have no determination at all as to how it fits any object. The putative principle: everything contingent has a cause, comes on the scene rather gravely, as if it had its own dignity in itself. But if I ask: what do you understand by contingent? and you answer, that whose non-being is possible, then I would like to know by what you wish to cognize this possibility of non-being, if you do not represent to yourselves in the series of appearances a succession and in this an existence that follows upon non-being (or the reverse), hence a change; for that the non-being of a thing does not contradict itself is a lame appeal to a logical condition, which is indeed necessary for the concept, but far from sufficient for real possibility; just as I can remove in thought every existing substance without contradicting myself, but cannot at all infer from this the objective contingency of it in its existence, i.e., the possibility of its non-being in itself. As regards the concept of community, it is easy to judge: that, since the pure categories of substance as well as of causality admit of no explanation determining the object, reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to one another (*commercium*) is just as little capable of it. Possibility, existence and necessity no one has yet been able to explain otherwise than through obvious tautology, if one wanted to draw their definition solely from the pure understanding. For the sleight of hand of substituting the logical possibility of the concept (in that it does not contradict itself) for the transcendental possibility of things (in that an object corresponds to the concept)",["- Größe = magnitude; Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Inbegriff = sum total; Folgerung = consequence; Bestimmung = determination.","- \"erklären\" = explain (also \"Erklärung\"); \"definiren\"/\"Definition\" = define/definition.","- \"Zufällig\" = contingent; \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Wechsel\" = change; \"Succession\" = succession.","- \"ob dieser überall irgend etwas bedeute\": the German lacks \"ob\" (\"weiß, dieser überall irgend etwas bedeute\"); I read it as \"whether this signifies,\" keeping the sense as it stands; the text may be defective.","- \"Gegenstande\"/\"Object\" both = object. \"erkennen\" = cognize.","- \"womit erfüllt\" = \"filled with something.\"","- \"Nothwendigkeit\" in the passage = necessity; \"Möglichkeit\" = possibility.","- \"Tautos logic\" OCR = \"Tautologie\"; \"Bez griff\" = \"Begriff\"; \"ö Verstande\" = \"Verstande.\"","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"zu\"), continued in the next passage.","- \"dieser\" after \"ob\" is ambiguous in gender; \"this\" is left unresolved."]],"krv-b-0736":["to be substituted can deceive and satisfy only the inexperienced.“)",["- \"unterschieben\" continues the infinitive begun at the end of the preceding passage (\"zu ... unterschieben\"); \"to be substituted\" rendered in the fragment, reading \"kann ... hintergehen\" as \"can deceive.\"","- \"Unversuchte\" = the inexperienced (literally, the untried).","- \"“)\" is a stray footnote mark from the OCR, kept as it stands."]],"krv-b-0737":["From this¹) it flows irrefutably: that the pure concepts of the understanding can never be of transcendental, but always only of",["- \"Hieraus!)\" has a stray note sign; kept as ¹).","- \"unwidersprechlich\" = irrefutably (literally \"incontrovertibly\"); \"Verstandesbegriffe\" = concepts of the understanding.","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0738":["empirical use, and that the principles of the pure understanding can be related to objects of the senses only in relation to the general conditions of a possible experience, but never to things in general (without taking regard to the way in which we intuit them",["- \"Gegenstände der Sinne\" = objects of the senses; \"Dinge überhaupt\" = things in general; \"anschauen\" = intuit.","- \"Beziehung\" and \"bezogen\" = relation/related.","- \"auf Gegenstände der Sinne ... nur in Beziehung auf die allgemeinen Bedingungen\": the construction, with \"nur\" ambiguous in scope, is kept as in the German.","- The passage breaks off within a parenthesis; left unclosed."]],"krv-b-0739":["may) be related. | The transcendental analytic accordingly has this important result: that the understanding can never accomplish more *a priori* than to anticipate the form of a possible experience in general, and, since that which is not appearance cannot be an object of experience, that it can never overstep the limitations of sensibility, within which alone objects are given to us. Its principles are merely principles of the exposition of appearances, and the proud name of an ontology, which presumes to give synthetic cognitions *a priori* of things in general in a systematic doctrine (e.g., the principle of causality), must make way for the modest one of a mere analytic of the pure understanding. i Thinking is the act of relating given intuition to an object. If the way of this intuition is given in no way whatsoever",[]],"krv-b-0740":["given, then the object is merely transcendental, and the concept of the understanding has no other than a transcendental use, namely the unity of the thinking of a manifold in general. Through a pure category now, in which all condition of sensible intuition E *) In a word, all these concepts can be substantiated by nothing, and their real possibility thereby shown, if all sensible intuition (the only one that we have) is taken away, and then there remains only logical possibility, i.e., that the concept (thought) is possible, but that is not what is at issue, rather whether it relates to an object and thus signifies anything.“) as the only one that is possible for us, is abstracted from, no object is therefore determined, but only the thinking of an object in general is expressed according to different *modis*. Now to the use of a concept there belongs yet a function of the power of judgment, whereby an object is subsumed under it, hence at least the formal condition under which something can be given in intuition. If this condition of the power of judgment (schema) is lacking, then all subsumption drops out; for nothing is given that could be subsumed under the concept. The merely transcendental use of the categories is therefore in fact no use at all, and has no determinate object or one even determinable as to form. | From this it follows that the pure category also does not suffice for any synthetic principle *a priori*, and that the principles of the pure understanding are only of empirical, never however of transcendental use, but that beyond the field of possible experience there can be no synthetic principles *a priori* anywhere. 1",[]],"krv-b-0741":["It can therefore be advisable to express oneself thus: the pure categories without formal conditions of sensibility have merely transcendental significance, but are of no transcendental use, because this is impossible in itself, since all conditions of any use (in judgments) are lacking to them, namely the formal conditions of the subsumption of any putative object under these concepts. Since they (as merely pure categories) are therefore not supposed to be of empirical use and cannot be of transcendental use, they are of no use at all if one separates them from all sensibility, i.e., they cannot be applied to any putative object; rather they are merely the pure form of the use of the understanding with regard to objects in general and of thinking, without being able, however, to think or determine any object through them alone.) |",[]],"krv-b-0742":["Meanwhile an illusion hard to avoid lies at the ground here. The categories, in their origin, are not grounded on sensibility like the forms of intuition, space and time, and so seem to permit an application extended beyond all objects of the senses. But for their part they are in turn nothing but forms of thought, which contain merely the logical faculty of uniting the manifold given in intuition into one consciousness *a priori*; and then, if the intuition that alone is possible for us is taken away from them, they can have even less significance than those pure sensible forms, through which at least an object is given, whereas a way of combining the manifold proper to our understanding, if that intuition in which alone this manifold can be given does not come in addition, signifies nothing at all. — Nevertheless it already lies in our concept, when we call certain objects, as appearances, beings of sense (*Phaenomena*), in that we distinguish the way in which we intuit them from their constitution in itself: that we set over against these, as it were, either the very same things according to this latter constitution, even though we do not intuit them in it, or also other possible things, which are not objects of our senses at all, as objects thought merely through the understanding, and call them beings of understanding (*Noumena*). Now the question arises: whether our pure concepts of the understanding do not have significance with regard to these latter and could be a way of cognition of them?",[]],"krv-b-0743":["But right at the start an ambiguity shows itself here, which can occasion great misunderstanding: that, since the understanding, when it calls an object in a relation merely phenomenon, at the same time makes for itself, outside this relation, a representation of an object in itself, and therefore represents to itself that it could also make concepts of such an object, and, since the understanding delivers none other than the categories, the object in the latter meaning must at least be able to be thought through these pure concepts of the understanding, but is thereby misled to take the entirely undetermined concept of a being of understanding, as a something in general outside our sensibility, for a determinate concept of a being which we could cognize in some way through the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0744":["If by noumenon we understand a thing insofar as it is not an object of our sensible intuition, in that we abstract from our way of intuiting it, then this is a noumenon in the negative sense. But if we understand by it an object of a non-sensible intuition, then we assume a particular way of intuiting, namely the intellectual, which however is not ours, and of which we also cannot see the possibility, and that would be the noumenon in the positive meaning.",["- krv-b-0739: Principien = \"principles\" for both Grundsätze and Principien (Latinate member); Exposition = \"exposition\" (Erörterung/expositio). Stray OCR \"i\" kept at the footnote-mark position.","- krv-b-0739: \"Er— kenntnisse\" corrected to Erkenntnisse. The passage opens mid-sentence (\"mögen) bezogen werden können\") and breaks off mid-sentence; \"Sit\" corrected to \"Ist\".","- krv-b-0740: Gegenstand and Object both \"object\". Footnote *) is run into the text at the point where the OCR placed it, with its stray marks (\"E\", closing “\") left as found; the \"*)\" mark in the text before \"als der einzigen\" is not otherwise reproduced.","- krv-b-0740: \"bestimmten oder auch nur der Form nach bestimmbaren\" restored from the garbled OCR (\"bestimme ı bären\"). Urteilskraft = \"power of judgment\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; modis kept Latin and italicized.","- krv-b-0741: \"Kate gorien\" and \"Pe- z dingungen\" corrected to Kategorien and Bedingungen. The closing \")\" and \"|\" are carried over as in the OCR; the opening parenthesis is not in the passage. Bedeutung = \"significance\" here, but \"meaning\" in the last passage (glossary allows both).","- krv-b-0742: \"Täuschung\" = \"illusion\", the same word as glossary Schein only by choice (Schein is not used here). \"Sinnenwesen\" and \"Verstandeswesen\" = \"beings of sense / of understanding\" (Wesen = being). \"Verbindungsart\" = \"way of combining\" (cf. Verbindung = combination). The Phaenomena/Noumena are in parentheses as in the German.","- krv-b-0743: The sentence is syntactically unfinished/loosely built in the German (\"daß, da … dadurch aber verleitet wird …\"); the construction is kept as it stands. \"Phänomen\" = \"phenomenon\"; \"Bedeutung\" = \"meaning\" here.","- krv-b-0744: \"im negativen Verstande\" = \"in the negative sense\" (Verstand in the sense of \"sense\", not the faculty); \"Bedeutung\" = \"meaning\". \"einsehen\" = \"see\" (not in glossary)."]],"krv-b-0745":["The doctrine of sensibility is now at the same time the doctrine of noumena in the negative sense, i.e., of things that the understanding must think without this relation to our kind of intuition, hence not merely as appearances but as things in themselves, but of which it at the same time comprehends in this abstraction that it cannot make any use of its categories in this way of considering them: because, since these have significance only in relation to the unity of the intuitions in space and time, they can determine precisely this unity only on account of the mere ideality of space and time through general concepts of combination *a priori*. Where this unity of time cannot be encountered, hence in the case of the noumenon, there the entire use, indeed even all significance of the categories ceases entirely; for even the possibility of the things that are supposed to correspond to the categories cannot be seen at all, on which account I may appeal only to what I adduced right at the beginning in the general remark on the previous chapter. Now, however, the possibility of a thing can never be proved merely from the non-contradiction of a concept of it, but only by one's substantiating this through an intuition corresponding to it. If we wanted, therefore, to apply the categories to objects that are not considered as appearances, we would have to lay at the basis an intuition other than the sensible, and then the object would be a noumenon in the positive significance. Since now such an intuition, namely intellectual intuition, lies absolutely outside our faculty of cognition, so the use of the categories can also in no way reach beyond the boundary of the objects of experience; and to the beings of sense there do indeed correspond beings of understanding, there may also be beings of understanding to which our sensible faculty of intuition has no relation at all, but our concepts of the understanding",["- \"Verstande\" in \"im negativen Verstande\" = \"sense\" (Verstand elsewhere = \"understanding\"); Bedeutung = \"significance\" throughout.","- Anschauungsart = \"kind of intuition\"; Art otherwise \"way\".","- Passage breaks off mid-word (\"Verstandes⸗\"), completed in the next passage (\"begriffe\").","- Silently corrected OCR: \"sit\", \"1s\", \"Katez gorien\", line numbers removed.","- Erkenntnißvermögen = \"faculty of cognition\"; Verstandeswesen / Sinnenwesen = \"beings of understanding / of sense\"; Hauptstück = \"chapter\".","- \"diese\" (the categories) and \"sie\" in the because-clause are left as in the German; the syntax of \"weil, da diese … sie eben diese Einheit\" is kept as it stands."]],"krv-b-0746":["concepts, as mere forms of thought for our sensible intuition, do not reach in the least beyond it; so what is called by us noumenon must be understood only as such in negative significance.)",["- Begins mid-word: \"begriffe\" completes \"Verstandes⸗\" of the preceding passage (\"Verstandesbegriffe\", concepts of the understanding).","- The closing parenthesis is retained as it stands in the OCR; no opening parenthesis appears in the passage."]],"krv-b-0747":["If I take away all thinking (through categories) from an empirical cog-",["- Breaks off mid-word at \"Er-\" (Erkenntniß, cognition), completed in the next passage. The running head \"Unterscheidung aller Gegenstände in Phaenomena und Noumena\" is omitted."]],"krv-b-0748":["nition, there remains no cognition at all of any object; for through mere intuition nothing at all is thought, and that this affection of sensibility is in me constitutes no relation at all of such a representation to any object. But if, on the contrary, I leave out all intuition, there still remains the form of thinking, i.e., the way of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. Hence the categories extend so far further than sensible intuition, since they think objects in general, without yet looking to the particular way (of sensibility) in which they may be given. But they thereby do not determine a greater sphere of objects, because that such can be given one cannot assume without presupposing as possible a kind of intuition other than the sensible, for which, however, we are in no way entitled.",["- Begins mid-word (\"kenntniß\", completing \"Er-\" of the previous passage); the running head and stray \">11\" are omitted.","- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\" (Gegenstand in \"einen Gegenstand\", \"Gegenständen\"; Object in \"irgend ein Object\", \"Objecte\").","- \"so fern weiter\" is kept as \"so far further\"; it can be read as \"to that extent further\" or \"as far as further\".","- \"weil, daß solche gegeben werden können, man nicht annehmen kann\" is kept in German order."]],"krv-b-0749":["I call a concept problematic that contains no contradiction, that also, as a bounding of given concepts, is connected with other cognitions, but whose objective reality can in no way be cognized. The concept of a noumenon, i.e., of a thing that is to be thought not at all as object of the senses but as a thing in itself (solely through a pure understanding), is not at all contradictory; for one cannot after all assert of sensibility that it is the only possible kind of intuition. Furthermore this concept is necessary in order not to extend sensible intuition to beyond things in themselves, and thus in order to restrict the objective validity of sensible cognition (for the remaining ones, to which it does not reach, are called noumena precisely for this reason, so that one thereby indicates that those cognitions cannot extend their territory over everything that the understanding thinks). In the end, however, the possibility of such *noumenorum* cannot be seen at all, and the domain outside the sphere of appearances is (for us) empty, i.e., we have an understanding that extends problematically further than those, but no intuition, indeed not even the concept of a possible intuition, through which objects could be given to us outside the field of sensibility and the understanding could be used assertorically beyond it. The concept of a noumenon is therefore merely a boundary concept, in order to restrict the presumption of sensibility, and therefore only of negative use. But it is nonetheless not arbitrarily invented, but is connected with the restriction of sensibility, without, however, being able to posit anything positive outside the domain of the latter.",["- Begrenzung = \"bounding\"; Grenzbegriff = \"boundary concept\"; einschränken / Einschränkung = \"restrict / restriction\"; Gebiet = \"territory\", Umfang = \"domain\".","- \"Noümenorum\" is Latin (genitive plural), italicized, spelling normalized.","- \"als jene\" (\"than those\") and \"über dieselbe\" (\"beyond it\") have grammatically possible antecedents other than the one a reader might assume (Erscheinungen / Sphäre; Sinnlichkeit / Sphäre); left as in the German.","- \"Anschauung ... Art\" rendered \"kind of intuition\"; \"Gegenstand der Sinne\" = \"object of the senses\"."]],"krv-b-0750":["The division of objects into phenomena and noumena and of the world into a world of sense and a world of understanding can therefore not be admitted at all in positive significance, although concepts do certainly admit of the division into sensible and intellectual; for one cannot determine an object for the latter and so also cannot pass them off as objectively valid. If one departs from the senses, how will one make comprehensible that our categories (which would be the only concepts remaining for noumena) still signify anything at all, since for their relation to any object something more than merely the unity of thinking, namely in addition a possible intuition, must be given, to which they can be applied? The concept of a noumenon, taken merely problematically, nevertheless remains not only admissible, but also, as a concept setting sensibility within limitations, unavoidable. But then that is not a special intelligible object for our understanding, but an understanding to which it would belong is itself a problem, namely of cognizing its object not discursively, through categories, but intuitively, in a non-sensible intuition, as one of which we cannot make for ourselves the least representation of its possibility. Our understanding now gets in this way a negative expansion, i.e., it is not restricted by sensibility, but rather restricts it, in that it calls things in themselves (considered not as appearances) noumena. But it also immediately sets itself bounds, not to cognize them through any categories, hence to think them only under the name of an unknown something.",["- Silently corrected OCR: \"positi ver Bedeutung)\" (stray parenthesis dropped), \"gez geben\", \"fegt\" read as \"setzt\" (sets).","- \"Noumeni\" is a Latin genitive in the German; \"Problema\" is Latin in roman type.","- \"ein Verstand, für den es gehörte\" is kept literal (subjunctive); \"als von welchem\" and \"seiner Möglichkeit\" leave open whether \"its\" refers to the understanding or the intuition.","- Eintheilung = \"division\"; Schranken = \"limitations\"; Grenzen = \"bounds\"; begreiflich = \"comprehensible\"."]],"krv-b-0751":["Meanwhile I find in the writings of the moderns a quite different use of the expressions of a *mundi sensibilis* and *intelligibilis*,¹ which",["- \"*)\" in the OCR rendered as footnote mark ¹; the relative clause continues in krv-b-0753 (\"von dem Sinne der Alten ganz abweicht\").","- The genitives *mundi sensibilis* / *intelligibilis* are kept in Latin as printed."]],"krv-b-0752":["¹ One must not use instead of this expression that of an intellectual world, as one commonly tends to do in German presentation; for only cognitions are intellectual or sensitive. But what can only be an object of the one or the other kind of intuition, the objects, must (notwithstanding the harshness of the sound) be called intelligible or sensible.",["- Footnote; leading \")\" is the OCR of the footnote asterisk, and the trailing \")\" was dropped as spurious.","- Anschauungsart = \"kind of intuition\"; Erkenntnisse = \"cognitions\"; Vortrage = \"presentation\"."]],"krv-b-0753":["deviates entirely from the sense of the ancients, and in which there is of course no",["- Continues the relative clause of krv-b-0751 (\"which\"); breaks off before \"Schwierigkeit\" in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0754":["difficulty, but also nothing but empty word-mongering encountered",["- The final verb \"wird\" stands at the start of the next passage; German order is therefore kept here, with \"is\" there."]],"krv-b-0755":["is. According to it, it has pleased some to call the sum total of appear-",["- \"Nach demselben\" kept as \"According to it\"; the antecedent is left as in the German. Breaks mid-word (Erschei-nungen); OCR \"⸗\" dropped."]],"krv-b-0756":["ances, insofar as it is intuited, the world of sense, but insofar as, however, the inter-",["- Breaks mid-word at \"Du-\"; read as OCR error for \"Zu-\" (Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\").","- \"aber\" is placed after \"insofar as\" in keeping with the German; \"sofern\" = \"insofar as\"."]],"krv-b-0757":["connection of them is thought according to general laws of the understanding,",["- Begins with the remainder of \"Zusammenhang\" (OCR \"sammenhang\"); \"derselben\" = \"of them\", antecedent as in the German."]],"krv-b-0758":["the world of understanding. Theoretical astronomy, which sets forth the",["- Completes \"nennen\" construction from krv-b-0755 (\"zu nennen\"): \"to call … the world of understanding\" (English verb already in 0755)."]],"krv-b-0759":["mere observation of the starry heaven, would the former, the",["- The verb \"vorstellig machen\" stands at the end of the sentence (krv-b-0762); German order kept. Beobachtung = \"observation\"."]],"krv-b-0760":["contemplative by contrast (say according to the Copernican world-system,",["- \"die contemplative\" has the implied noun left unsupplied (astronomy, as the German leaves it)."]],"krv-b-0761":["or even explained according to Newton's laws of gravitation) the second, namely",["- \"erklärt\" = \"explained\" (Erklärung family); the participle's position inside the parenthesis follows the German."]],"krv-b-0762":["an intelligible world, make presentable. But such a twisting of words",["- \"vorstellig machen\" rendered \"make presentable\"; Wortverdrehung = \"twisting of words\"."]],"krv-b-0763":["is a mere sophistical evasion, in order to a burdensome question",["- The infinitive \"auszuweichen\" is split across passages (aus-/zuweichen); English \"evade\" appears in the next passage to preserve the German order."]],"krv-b-0764":["evade, in that one lowers its sense to one's own convenience. In regard to appearances understanding and reason can certainly be used; but it is asked whether these also still have some u-",["- Breaks off at \"Ge-\"; probably the start of \"Gebrauch\" (use), but this is not supplied. \"frägt sich\" = \"it is asked\".","- \"ihren Sinn\" has \"its\" referring to the question; \"diese\" = \"these\" (understanding and reason)."]],"krv-b-0765":["use, if the object is not appearance (noumenon),",["- Fragment begins mid-word (\"brauch\" for \"Gebrauch\"); translated as \"use\" per the sense of the broken-off word, with the opening lost.","- Gegenstand = object; Erscheinung = appearance; Noumenon left as in the source."]],"krv-b-0766":["and in this sense one takes it, if it is thought in itself as merely intelligible,",["- \"ihn\" refers back to the (masculine) object in the preceding passage; left as a pronoun. an sich = in itself."]],"krv-b-0767":["i.e., given to the understanding alone and not at all to the senses.",["- Verstand = understanding; Sinne = senses; gegeben ... gedacht wird: \"is thought\" governs the whole clause begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0768":["It is thus the question: whether, besides that empirical use of the under-",["- The word \"Verstandes\" is broken across the line (\"Ver⸗ / standes\"); it is completed in the next passage and rendered \"understanding\" here."]],"krv-b-0769":["standing (even in the Newtonian representation of the structure of the world) a transcendental one is still possible, which would go to the noumenon as an object, which question we have answered in the negative.",["- The stray line number \"20\" in the OCR is omitted.","- Vorstellung = representation; Weltbau = \"structure of the world\"; Gegenstand = object; \"gehe auf\" rendered \"go to\".","- \"welche Frage\" could be read with \"Frage\" as the antecedent only; \"which\" before \"question\" kept as in German."]],"krv-b-0770":["If we then say, therefore: the senses represent the objects to us",["- Sentence continues in the next passage. Gegenstände = objects."]],"krv-b-0771":["as they appear, but the understanding as they are, then the latter is to be taken not in a transscendental but merely in an empirical signification, namely how they, as objects of experience in the thoroughgoing interconnection of appearances, must be represented and not",["- The OCR \"is\" before \"nehmen\" is a stray line number (15) and is omitted.","- Bedeutung = \"signification\" here, though the glossary gives \"signify/significance; meaning.\" Erfahrung = experience; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Erscheinungen = appearances.","- \"transscendental\" is kept in Kant's spelling form with double s as in the OCR."]],"krv-b-0772":["according to what they may be outside the relation to possible experience and consequen-",["- Broken word \"folg⸗ / lich\" is completed in the next passage (\"consequently\").","- Beziehung = relation; möglich = possible."]],"krv-b-0773":["tly to senses in general, hence as objects of the pure understanding. For this will always remain unknown to us, so much so that it also remains unknown whether such a transcendental (extraordinary)",["- The OCR \"w\" before \"es\" is a stray line number (25) and is omitted. The word \"folglich\" is split across passages.","- \"Sinne überhaupt\" = \"senses in general.\" unbekannt = unknown."]],"krv-b-0774":["cognition is possible at all, at least as one that stands under",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; überall möglich = \"possible at all.\""]],"krv-b-0775":["our common categories. Understanding and sensibility can with us determine objects only in combination. If we separate them, then we have intuitions without concepts, or concepts without intuitions, but in both cases representations that we cannot relate to any determinate object.",["- gewöhnlich = \"common\" (glossary gemein is a different word; \"gewöhnlich\" is rendered \"common\" for lack of a separate glossary entry).","- Verbindung = combination; bestimmen = determine; bestimmt = determinate; Anschauung = intuition; Begriff = concept; Vorstellung = representation.","- The OCR \"s\" before \"ohne\" is a stray line number (5) and is omitted.","- \"bei uns\" = \"with us.\""]],"krv-b-0776":["If someone still has qualms about renouncing, on the strength of all these expositions, the merely transcendental use of the categories, then let him make an attempt with them in some synthetic assertion. For an analytic one does not bring the understanding any further, and since it is occupied only with what is already thought in the concept, it leaves undecided whether this has relation to objects in itself, or only signifies the unity of thinking in general (which abstracts entirely from the way in which an object may be given); it is enough for it to know what lies in its concept; what the concept itself may go to is indifferent to it. Let him therefore try it with some synthetic and supposedly transcendental principle, such as: everything that is, exists as substance or a determination inhering in it; everything contingent exists as the effect of another thing, namely of its cause, and so forth. Now I ask: whence does he want to take these synthetic propositions, since the concepts are supposed to hold not in relation to possible experience but of things in themselves (noumena)? Where is the third here, which is always required for a synthetic proposition, in order to connect with one another in the same concepts that have no logical (analytic) affinity at all? He will never prove his proposition, indeed, what is more, will not even be able to justify himself regarding the possibility of such a pure assertion, without taking regard of the empirical use of the understanding and thereby entirely renouncing the pure and sense-free judgment. Thus the concept of pure, merely intelligible objects is entirely empty of all principles of their application, because one cannot devise any way in which they might be given, and the problematic thought, which nevertheless leaves a place open for them, serves only like an empty space to limit the empirical principles, without however containing and exhibiting in itself any other object of cognition outside the sphere of the latter.",["- Erörterungen = expositions; Behauptung = assertion; Grundsatz = principle; Satz = proposition; Bestimmung = determination; Zufällige = contingent; Wirkung = effect; Ding = thing; Wissen/wissen: \"to know\".","- \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Gegenstand/Object\" both = \"object\" (Object appears in \"anderes Object der Erkenntniß\").","- OCR \"ız\", \"cm- 20\", \"pirischen\" are line numbers/hyphenation; silently corrected to \"empirischen\"; \"solen\" corrected to \"sollen\".","- \"in demselben Begriffe, die\" is as printed (OCR/original irregularity: singular \"Begriffe\" with plural \"die\"); rendered \"in the same concepts\" ; doubtful.","- \"Raum\" = space; \"einschränken\" = limit; \"Sphäre\" = sphere.","- \"dieser\" in \"ob dieser an sich selbst\" refers to Begriff (concept) ; \"an sich selbst\" is \"in itself,\" which could attach to \"Beziehung haben\" or to \"dieser\"; ambiguity kept."]],"krv-b-0777":["Appendix.",[]],"krv-b-0778":["On the amphibology of the concepts of reflection\n\nthrough the confusion of the empirical use of the understanding",["- OCR \"Reflerionsbegriffe\" corrected to \"Reflexionsbegriffe\"; the stray \"w\" (line number) omitted. Begriffe = concepts; Verwechselung = confusion; Verstandesgebrauch = use of the understanding."]],"krv-b-0779":["with the transcendental.",[]],"krv-b-0780":["Reflection (*reflexio*) has to do not with the objects themselves, in order to get concepts directly from them, but is the state of the mind in which we first prepare ourselves to find out the subjective conditions under which we can arrive at concepts. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to our different sources of cognition, through which alone their relation among one another can be correctly determined. The first question before all further treatment of our representations is this: in which cognitive faculty do they belong together? Is it the understanding, or are it the senses, before which they are connected or compared? Many a judgment is accepted from habit or connected through inclination; but because no reflection precedes, or at least follows it critically, it passes for one that has obtained its origin in the understanding. Not all judgments need an investigation, i.e., an attention to the grounds of truth; for if they are immediately certain, e.g., between two points only one straight line can be, then no nearer mark of truth than what they themselves express can be indicated of them. But all judgments, indeed all comparisons, need a reflection, i.e., a distinction of the cognitive power to which the given concepts belong. The action by which I hold together the comparison of representations in general with the cognitive power in which it is carried out, and by which I distinguish whether they are compared with one another as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensible intuition, I call transcendental reflection. But the relation in which the concepts",["- Überlegung = \"reflection\" and Reflexion (reflexio) = \"reflection\": one English word for two German words; Latin given in the text only where Kant prints it.","- \"Ist es der Verstand, oder sind es die Sinne\": rendered \"Is it the understanding, or are it the senses\" — awkward as German's double verb agreement; a literal \"are it\" is ungrammatical, read as \"is it ... or are they the senses\" (doubtful; the German is irregular).","- Erkenntnißvermögen = cognitive faculty; Erkenntnißkraft = cognitive power (Vermögen vs. Kraft); Erkenntnißquellen = sources of cognition.","- \"vor denen\" (before which) kept literal; \"vor\" here could be read \"in front of\" or \"in the presence of.\"","- OCR \"„Gewohnheit\" and \"si\" corrected to \"Gewohnheit\" and \"sich\"; \"Üb erlegung\" corrected; \")\" after \"Anschauung\" is stray and omitted. The \"Merkmal\" = mark.","- \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"sinnlich\" = sensible; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-b-0781":["belong to one another in a state of mind is that of identity and difference, of agreement and conflict, of the inner and the outer, finally of the determinable and the determination (matter and form). The correct determination of this relation rests on in which cognitive power they belong to one another subjectively, whether in sensibility or in the understanding. For the difference of the latter makes a great difference in the way in which one should think the former.",["- The beginning of the passage (\"einem Gemüthszustande\") continues the sentence from 0780; \"Einer-leiheit\" corrected to \"Einerleiheit\" = identity; Verschiedenheit = difference; Einstimmung = agreement; Widerstreit = conflict; Inneren/Äußeren = inner/outer; Bestimmbaren/Bestimmung = determinable/determination.","- \"die ersten ... der letzteren\": \"latter\" and \"former\" kept as in German (letzteren/ersten); the reference of \"ersten\" (the concepts) is not stated."]],"krv-b-0782":["Before all objective judgments we compare the concepts, in order to arrive at identity (of many representations under one concept) for the sake of universal judgments, or at the difference of them for the generation of particular ones, at agreement, from which affirmative, and conflict, from which negative judgments can come to be, and so on. For this reason we should, as it seems, call the concepts adduced concepts of comparison (*conceptus comparationis*). But because, when it does not depend on the logical form but on the content of the concepts, i.e., whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or in conflict, etc., the things can have a twofold relation to our cognitive power, namely to sensibility and to understanding, but it is on this place, in which they belong, that the way depends in which they are to belong to one another: so transcendental reflection, i.e., the relation of given representations to one or the other kind of cognition, will alone be able to determine their relation among one another; and whether the things are identical or different, in agreement or conflicting, etc., will not be able to be made out straightaway from the concepts themselves through mere comparison (*comparatio*), but first of all through the distinction of the kind of cognition to which they belong, by means of a transcendental reflection (*reflexio*). One could thus indeed say: that logical reflection is a mere comparison, for in it the cognitive power to which the given representations belong is entirely abstracted from, and they are therefore to this extent to be treated as homogeneous in respect of their seat in the mind; but transcendental reflection (which goes to the objects themselves) contains the ground of the possibility of the objective comparison of representations among one another and is therefore very much different from the latter, because the cognitive power to which they belong is not precisely the same. This transcendental reflection is a duty from which no one can release himself, if he wants to judge anything a priori about",["- Einerleiheit = identity; Behuf = \"sake\"; Erzeugung = generation; bejahend/verneinend = affirmative/negative; Inhalt = content; Art = way (in \"die Art, wie\") and \"kind\" (in \"Erkenntnißart\"): one English word set for both uses of Art per glossary (way; kind).","- Überlegung/Reflexion both \"reflection\"; Vergleichung and Comparation/comparatio both \"comparison.\"","- \"auf diese Stelle aber ... ankommt, die Art, wie ...\": the \"but\" clause is syntactically unfinished before the colon; kept literal.","- Stray OCR \"2\" and \"23\" are line numbers and omitted. \"a priori\" is as printed in the source. \"gehe auf\" = \"goes to.\" \"Sitze\" = \"seat.\"","- \"wird nicht sofort ... ausgemacht werden können\": \"straightaway\" for sofort."]],"krv-b-0783":["things. We now want to take it in hand, and shall draw from it not a little light for the determination of the proper business of the understanding.",["- \"Dinge urtheilen will\" continues the sentence from 0782 (\"urtheilen\" = to judge, v.; \"Dinge\" = things). OCR \"2\" is a stray line number and omitted.","- \"eigentlichen Geschäfts\" = \"proper business\"; \"Licht ziehen\" = \"draw light.\""]],"krv-b-0784":["1. Identity and difference. If an object is presented to us several times, but each time with just the same inner determinations (*qualitas et quantitas*), then, if it counts as an object of the pure understanding, it is always just the same and not many, but only One thing (*numerica identitas*); but if it is appearance, then the comparison of concepts does not matter at all, but however much everything may be identical with regard to them, the difference of the places of this appearance at the same time is nevertheless a sufficient ground of the numerical difference of the object (of the",["- Einerleiheit = \"identity\" (einerlei = \"identical\"); genugsamer Grund = \"sufficient ground\" (genugsam and zureichend both \"sufficient\"). Orter = \"places\" (Ort).","- The capital \"One\" renders the German \"Ein\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is left unfinished."]],"krv-b-0785":["itself. Thus in the case of two drops of water one can abstract completely from all inner difference",["- The passage breaks mid-word (Verschie-/denheit). Here and below, words are assigned to whichever fragment English order requires, and a few are shifted across breaks (e.g., \"indicated\" in 0793–0794, \"is\" and \"hold\" in 0794–0795, \"is\" in 0805–0806).","- an sich selbst = \"in itself\"; here the sentence begins mid-clause with \"selbst\"."]],"krv-b-0786":["(of quality and quantity), and it is enough",["- Qualität/Quantität = quality/quantity. The break falls mid-word (ge-/nug), and \"enough\" is placed here."]],"krv-b-0787":["that they are intuited in different places at the same time, in order to hold them",["- anschauen = \"intuit\" (Anschauung = intuition). Örter = \"places\"."]],"krv-b-0788":["to be numerically different. Leibniz took the appearances as things in themselves, hence for *intelligibilia*, i.e., objects of the",["- The OCR shows a stray brace and parenthesis around \"als Dinge an sich\"; I read it as unparenthesized text. This is a doubtful reading.","- Gegenstände = \"objects\"."]],"krv-b-0789":["pure understanding (although on account of the confusion of their representations",["- Verworrenheit = \"confusion\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\". The break falls mid-word (Vorstellun-/gen)."]],"krv-b-0790":["he furnished them with the name of phenomena), and then could",["- belegen mit dem Namen = \"furnish with the name\". \"could\" (konnte) is placed here, though its sentence continues in 0791."]],"krv-b-0791":["his proposition of what is not to be distinguished (*principium identitatis in-* | *discernibilium*) certainly not be contested; but since they are objects of sensibility, and the understanding with regard to them is not",["- Satz = \"proposition\". The sign | is carried over from the source. The OCR \"Gegenfo stände\" is corrected to Gegenstände.","- \"could\" belongs with \"certainly not be contested\" and is placed at the end of 0790."]],"krv-b-0792":["of pure but merely empirical use, so the multiplicity",["- Gebrauch = \"use\"; Vielheit = \"multiplicity\"; empirisch = \"empirical\"."]],"krv-b-0793":["and numerical difference are already indicated through space itself as the condition",["- The German verb \"angegeben\" stands in 0794; it is moved here for English order. The break falls mid-word (Be-/dingung)."]],"krv-b-0794":["of outer appearances. For a part of space, although it may be entirely similar and equal to another, is nevertheless outside it and precisely thereby a part different from the former, which is added to it in order to make up a larger space; and this must therefore hold of everything that is in the various positions of space",["- Theil = \"part\"; Stelle = \"position\" (distinct from Ort = \"place\"); mancherlei = \"various\". \"is\" and \"hold\" are moved from 0795 for English order."]],"krv-b-0795":["at the same time, however much it may otherwise be similar and equal to itself. | 2. Agreement and conflict. If reality is represented only through the pure understanding (*realitas noumenon*), so between",["- Widerstreit = \"conflict\" (not Widerspruch = \"contradiction\"). \"sich\" in \"sich ... ähnlich und gleich\" is left as ambiguous as the German (\"to itself\" or \"to one another\").","- OCR \"so reinen\" read as \"reinen\" (the \"so\" is a line number). The sign | is carried over."]],"krv-b-0796":["the realities no conflict can be thought, i.e., such a relation in which",["- Verhältniß = \"relation\" (also Beziehung and Relation in 0799; see there). The sentence is incomplete here."]],"krv-b-0797":["they, combined in a subject, cancel one another's sequels, and",["- Folgen = \"sequels\" (glossary Folge); aufheben = \"cancel\"; verbunden = \"combined\" (Verbindung = combination)."]],"krv-b-0798":["3 − 3 = 0 be. On the other hand the real in appearance (*realitas* | *phaenomenon*) can certainly be in conflict with one another and, united in the same subject, one wholly or in part annihilate the sequel of the other, like two moving forces in the same straight line, insofar as they either pull or push a point in opposite directions,",["- \"sei\" is subjunctive. OCR \"=Q\" is read as \"= 0\"; \"entgegengefegter\" is read as \"entgegengesetzter\". \"das Reale\" is singular while \"unter einander\" is plural; I kept this as in the German. The sign | is carried over.","- Kräfte = \"forces\" (Kraft)."]],"krv-b-0799":["or also a pleasure that counterbalances pain. 3. The inner and outer. In an object of the pure understanding only that is inner which has no relation (as to existence) at all to anything different from it. On the other hand the inner determinations of a *substantia phaenomenon* in space are nothing but relations, and it itself entirely and completely a sum total of mere",["- Beziehung, Verhältniß and Relation are all \"relation\" here. Dasein = \"existence\" (so also Existenz). Gegenstand = \"object\" (Object is also \"object\"; see 0801). Inbegriff = \"sum total\".","- Wage halten is rendered \"counterbalances\" (idiom). The break falls mid-word (lau-/ter)."]],"krv-b-0800":["relations. Substance in space we know only through forces that are effective in it, either to drive others there (attraction), or to keep them from penetrating into it (repulsion and",["- kennen = \"know\"; wirksam = \"effective\"; Anziehung/Zurückstoßung = \"attraction\"/\"repulsion\"."]],"krv-b-0801":["impenetrability); other properties we do not know that make up the concept of the substance that appears in space, and that we call matter. As object of the pure understanding, on the other hand, every substance must have inner determinations and forces that bear on inner reality. But what can I think to myself for inner accidents, other than those that my inner sense offers me, namely that which is either itself a thinking, or analogous with this? Hence Leibniz made out of all substances, because he represented them as noumena, even out of the constituents of matter, after he had taken from them in thought everything that may signify outer relation, hence also composition, simple subjects, endowed with powers of representation, in a word monads.",["- Object here is \"object\" (as Gegenstand elsewhere). Eigenschaft = \"property\"; Beschaffenheit (0802) = \"constitution\". Vorstellungskräfte = \"powers of representation\" (Kraft = force; power).","- OCR \"des uti gabt\" is read as \"begabt\" (doubtful). Noumena and monads are not italicized, as the German prints them in the ordinary type."]],"krv-b-0802":["4. Matter and form. These are two concepts that are laid at the ground of all other reflection, so much are they inseparably combined with every use of the understanding. The former signifies the determinable in general, the second its determination (both in the transcendental sense, since one abstracts from every difference of what is given and the way in which it is determined). The logicians formerly called the universal the matter, but the specific difference the form. In every judgment one can call the given concepts the logical matter (for the judgment), their relation (by means of the copula) the form of the judgment. In every being the constituent parts of the same (*essentialia*) are the matter, the way in which they are connected in a thing, the essential form. Also, with regard to things in general, unbounded reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, but limitation of the same (negation) as that form through which one thing is distinguished from another according to transcendental concepts. The understanding namely demands first that something be given (at least in the concept), in order to be able to determine it in a certain way. Hence in the concept of the pure understanding matter precedes form, and Leibniz therefore assumed first things (monads) and inwardly a power of representation of them, in order afterwards to ground on it the outer relation of the same and the community of their states (namely of the representations). Hence space and time were possible, the former only through the relation of the substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with one another as grounds and sequels. So it would in fact also have to be, if the pure understanding could be related immediately to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But if they are only sensible intuitions, in which we determine all objects merely as appearances, then the form of intuition (as a subjective constitution of sensibility) precedes all matter (the sensations), hence space and time go before all appearances and all *data*",["- \"in transscendentalem Verstande\": Verstand is here the idiom \"in ... Verstande\"; I rendered \"sense\", departing from the glossary's \"understanding\" (literal: \"in transcendental understanding\").","- Reflexion = \"reflection\" (as Überlegung also would be). Schranke/Einschränkung = \"limitation\"; unbegrenzte = \"unbounded\" (Grenze). Wesen = \"being\". Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Folgen = \"sequels\". vorgehen/vorhergehen = \"go before\"/\"precede\".","- OCR \"(beides in 1 transscendentalem\": the \"1\" is dropped as a line number; \"zo innerlich\" is read as \"innerlich\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0803":["of experience beforehand and rather first of all makes these possible. The intellectual philosopher could not tolerate that the form should go before the things themselves and determine their possibility, a quite correct censure, if he assumed that we intuit the things as they are (although with confused representation). But since the sensible",["- \"macht\" is singular after the plural \"Raum und Zeit ... vorher\"; I kept this as in the German. \"diese\" = the appearances and data.","- Intellectualphilosoph = \"intellectual philosopher\"; Censur = \"censure\"."]],"krv-b-0804":["intuition is an entirely special subjective condition, which lies at the ground of all",["- besonder = \"special\" (glossary: particular; special). Bedingung = \"condition\"."]],"krv-b-0805":["perception *a priori*, and whose form is original:",["- \"a priori\" could attach to the verb (\"lies at the ground a priori\") or to \"perception\"; left as ambiguous as the German. Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; ursprünglich = \"original\". The German \"ist\" stands at the head of 0806."]],"krv-b-0806":["so the form is given for itself alone, and far from it that the matter (or the things themselves, which appear) should lie at the ground¹) (as one would have to judge according to mere concepts), so the possibility",["- The footnote marker ¹) is carried over; the footnote itself stands at the end of 0816. \"so\" in \"so setzt\" is kept as in the German.","- Wording of the OCR \"sollte!)\" is read as \"sollte¹)\". The break falls mid-word (Mög-/lichkeit)."]],"krv-b-0807":["of the same rather presupposes a formal intuition (time and space) as given.",["- voraussetzen = \"presuppose\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"."]],"krv-b-0808":["Remark\non the amphiboly of the concepts of reflection.",["- Amphibolie = \"amphiboly\"; OCR \"Reflexions begriffe\" is read as \"Reflexionsbegriffe\"."]],"krv-b-0809":["Permit me to call the position which we assign to a concept either in",["- Stelle = \"position\". The \"0\" at the beginning is a line number and is dropped."]],"krv-b-0810":["sensibility or in the pure understanding, the transcendental",["- The break falls mid-word (transscenden-/talen)."]],"krv-b-0811":["place. In such a way the estimation of this",["- Ort = \"place\"; Beurteilung = \"estimation\" (glossary)."]],"krv-b-0812":["position, which belongs to each concept according to the difference of its use,",["- Gebrauch = \"use\"."]],"krv-b-0813":["and the instruction by rules to determine this place for all concepts, the transcendental topic; a doctrine that would thoroughly guard against subreptions of the pure understanding and the delusions arising from them",["- Topik = \"topic\"; Erschleichung = \"subreption\"; Blendwerk = \"delusion\" (not Schein = \"illusion\"); Lehre = \"doctrine\". The break falls mid-word (be-/wahren), and \"guard\" is placed here."]],"krv-b-0814":["by always distinguishing to which power of cognition the concepts properly belong. One can call every concept, every title under which many cognitions belong, a logical place. On this the logical topic of Aristotle is grounded, of which",["- Erkenntnißkraft = \"power of cognition\" (Kraft; Erkenntnis = cognition). \"würde\" is placed at the end of 0813."]],"krv-b-0815":["school teachers and orators could make use of, in order under certain titles",["- sich bedienen = \"make use of\" (cf. Gebrauch)."]],"krv-b-0816":["of thinking to look up what suited best for a matter at hand, and to rationalize about it with an illusion of thoroughness, or to prattle verbosely. 1) A1; sollten",["- vernünfteln = \"rationalize\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; Materie = \"matter\". The \"5\" is a line number and is dropped.","- The footnote is a textual variant, left as printed: \"A1\" reads plural \"sollten\" (\"should\", plural) for \"sollte\" in 0806."]],"krv-b-0817":["The transcendental topic contains, on the other hand, no more than the four titles of all comparison and distinction that have been cited, which distinguish themselves from the categories by the fact that through them the object is not exhibited in accordance with that which constitutes its concept (magnitude, reality), but only the comparison of the representations, which precedes the concept of things, in all its manifoldness. This comparison, however, requires first of all a reflection, i.e., a determination of the place where the representations of the things that are compared belong, whether pure understanding thinks them, or sensibility gives them in appearance.",["OCR slips corrected silently: \"Des stimmung\" read as \"Bestimmung\"; \"sit Dingen\" as \"den Dingen\"; the stray \"10%\" omitted.","Überlegung = reflection; Bestimmung = determination; Größe = magnitude; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness (cf. Mannigfaltige = manifold).","\"dargestellt wird\" is rendered \"exhibited\"; its subject is ambiguous in the German as to whether it governs both \"der Gegenstand\" and \"die Vergleichung\", and the English keeps this."]],"krv-b-0818":["Concepts can be compared logically without troubling oneself about where their objects belong, whether as *noumena* to the understanding, or as *phenomena* to sensibility. But if we want to go to the objects with these concepts, then transcendental reflection is first of all necessary, as to for which cognitive power they are to be objects, whether for pure understanding, or sensibility. Without this reflection I make a very unsure use of these concepts, and putative synthetic principles arise, which critical reason cannot recognize, and which are grounded merely on a transcendental amphiboly, i.e., a confusion of the pure object of understanding with the appearance.",["Objecte / Gegenstände are both \"objects\"; \"Objects\" at line 1 is Objecte, the later \"Gegenstände\" is Gegenstände.","Erkenntnißkraft = \"cognitive power\" (Erkenntnis = cognition; Kraft = power); Grundsätze = principles; Gebrauch = use.","\"Noumena\" and \"Phänomena\" are Latinate/Greek terms in the German; italicized here.","\"auf einer ... gründen\" is rendered \"are grounded on\"; the German \"sich ... gründen auf einer\" keeps the dative as printed."]],"krv-b-0819":["In the lack of such a transcendental topic and thus deceived by the amphiboly of the concepts of reflection, the famous Leibniz erected an intellectual system of the world, or rather believed to cognize the inner constitution of things, by comparing all objects only with the understanding and the separated formal concepts of his thinking. Our table of the concepts of reflection procures for us the unexpected advantage of laying before our eyes what is distinctive of his doctrine in all its parts and at the same time the guiding ground of this peculiar way of thinking, which rested on nothing but a mis-",["Passage breaks off mid-word (\"Miß-\"); continued in the next passage (\"verstande\").","OCR slips corrected: \"Syst cm\" = System; \"eigenthüm— lichen\" = eigenthümlichen.","Beschaffenheit = constitution; Lehrbegriff = \"doctrine\"; Denkungsart = way of thinking; abgesondert = \"separated\" (so also in krv-b-0823, where it is \"abstracted\" in a similar phrase; see note there).","\"zu erkennen\" = \"to cognize\"; the German \"glaubte ... zu erkennen\" is kept as \"believed to cognize\"."]],"krv-b-0820":["understanding. He compared all things merely through concepts with one another and found, as is natural, no other diversities than those through which the understanding distinguishes its pure concepts from one another. The conditions of sensible intuition, which bring their own differences with them, he did not regard as original; for sensibility was for him only a confused way of representation and no particular source of representations; appearance was for him the representation of the thing | in itself, although distinguished from cognition through the understanding in accordance with the lo-",["Continues krv-b-0819 (\"Miß-\" + \"verstande\"); breaks off mid-word (\"lo-\"), continued in krv-b-0821.","OCR slips corrected: \"rS\" omitted; \"sach\" = sah; \"fidh\" = sich.","Verschiedenheit = diversity; Unterschied = difference; Vorstellungsart = \"way of representation\"; Quell = source."]],"krv-b-0821":["gical form, since namely the former, with its usual",["Continues krv-b-0820 (\"lo-\" + \"gischen\" = \"logischen\"); breaks off before the verb, which is in krv-b-0822.","\"jene\" refers back to the earlier \"Sinnlichkeit\"/\"Erscheinung\" construction; left as \"the former\", as the German pronoun does not name its antecedent."]],"krv-b-0822":["lack of analysis draws a certain mixture of collateral representations",["Breaks off mid-word (\"Nebenvorstellun-\"), continued in krv-b-0823 (\"gen\").","OCR slip corrected: \"Vorftellun-\" = Vorstellun-.","Zergliederung = analysis; Nebenvorstellungen = \"collateral representations\"."]],"krv-b-0823":["into the concept of the thing, which the understanding knows how to separate from it. In a word: Leibniz intellectualized the appearances, just as Locke, in accordance with his system of noogony (if I am permitted to make use of these expressions), had sensified all concepts of the understanding, i.e., passed them off as nothing but empirical or abstracted concepts of reflection. Instead of seeking in understanding and sensibility two entirely different sources of representations, which however could judge of things with objective validity only in connection, each of these great men held only to one of the two, which in his opinion related immediately to things in themselves, while the other did nothing but confuse or order the representations of the first. | Leibniz accordingly compared the objects of the senses as things in general merely in the understanding with one another. First, insofar as they are to be judged by it as identical or different. Since he thus had before his eyes merely their concepts and not their position in intuition, in which alone the objects can be given, and left entirely out of consideration the transcendental place of these concepts (whether the object is to be counted among appearances or among things in themselves), it could not turn out otherwise than that he extended his principle of what is not to be distinguished, which holds merely of concepts of things in general, also",["Continues krv-b-0822 (\"Nebenvorstellun-\" + \"gen\"); breaks off before \"auf die Gegenstände\" (krv-b-0824).","OCR slips corrected: \"Y\", \"6\", \"h\", \"Í\", \"s\" and similar stray marks omitted; \"sit\" and \"solen\" read as \"mit\" / \"sollen\" where they occur.","intellectuirte / sensificirt: coinages rendered \"intellectualized\" / \"sensified\"; abgesondert = \"abstracted\" here (cf. \"separated\" earlier).","Gegenstand/Object = \"object\"; Stelle = \"position\"; Ort = \"place\"; Grundsatz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden = \"principle of what is not to be distinguished\"; \"bezöge\" (subjunctive) = \"related\".","\"die sich ihrer Meinung nach ... bezöge\": the singular \"die\" is referred to \"eine von beiden\"; the English \"which\" is likewise ambiguous between \"one\" and \"the two\"."]],"krv-b-0824":["to the objects of the senses (*mundus phaenomenon*) and believed thereby to have procured for cognition of nature no small expansion. To be sure, if I know a drop of water as a thing in itself in accordance with all its inner determinations, then I can let none of them count as different from another, if the whole",["Continues krv-b-0823 (\"auch\" + \"auf\"); breaks off mid-sentence, continued in krv-b-0825.","OCR slips corrected: \"ba— den\" = haben.","kennen = \"know\" (here, \"kenne\"); Erkenntniß = cognition; Bestimmung = determination; Naturerkenntniß = \"cognition of nature\".","The Latin *mundus phaenomenon* is italicized as a Latin technical term."]],"krv-b-0825":["concept of it is identical with it. But if it is appearance in space, | then it has its place not merely in the understanding (among concepts), but in sensible outer intuition (in space); and there the physical places are entirely indifferent with regard to the inner determinations of things, and a place = b can take in a thing that is completely similar and equal to another in the place = a just as well as if it were ever so much inwardly different from it. The diversity of places makes the multiplicity and distinction of objects as appearances, without further conditions, already by itself not only possible, but also necessary. Thus that seeming law is no law of nature. It is merely an analytic rule of the comparison of things through mere concepts.",["Continues krv-b-0824 (\"der ganze\" + \"Begriff\").","OCR slips corrected: \"ĵo\" = so; \"phyfsischen\" = physischen; \"Verl Be\" = Verschiedenheit (reading inferred from OCR; doubtful).","Ort / Örter = place / places; Vielheit = \"multiplicity\"; Unterscheidung = \"distinction\"; gleichgültig = \"indifferent\"; Gesetz = law.","\"ohne weitere Bedingungen schon für sich\": the adverbial phrase may attach to \"möglich\" and \"nothwendig\" together or to \"macht\"; the English leaves this open."]],"krv-b-0826":["Second, the principle that realities (as mere affirmations) never conflict logically with one another is an entirely true proposition concerning the relation of concepts, but signifies neither with regard to nature, nor at all with regard to any thing in itself (of this | we have no concept) the least. For real conflict is found everywhere where A − B = 0, i.e., where one reality, combined with another in one subject, cancels the effect of the other, which all hindrances and counteractions in nature incessantly lay before our eyes, which nevertheless, since they rest on forces, must be called *realitates phaenomena*. General mechanics can even give the empirical condition of this conflict in a rule a priori, in that it looks to the opposition of directions: a condition of which the transcendental concept of reality knows nothing at all. Although Herr von Leibniz did not exactly announce this proposition with the pomp of a new principle, he nevertheless made use of it for new assertions, and his successors expressly incorporated it into their Leibniz-Wolffian doctrinal edifices. According to this principle, e.g., all evils are nothing but consequences of the limitations of creatures, i.e., negations, because these are the only thing that conflicts with reality (in the mere concept of a thing in general it is indeed really so, but not in things as appearances). Likewise its adherents find it not only possible, but even natural, to unite all reality in one being without any worrisome conflict, because they know no other than that of contradiction (through which the concept of a thing itself is canceled), but not that of reciprocal detriment, where one real ground cancels the effect of the other, and for which we encounter the conditions for representing such a one to ourselves only in sensibility. |",["OCR slips corrected: \"sit\" = mit; \"!)\" after \"keinen\" is the footnote marker \"1)\" (the footnote text is not supplied in the passage); \"auge z drücklich\" = ausdrücklich; \"A - B =O\" read as A − B = 0.","Satz / Grundsatz = \"proposition\" / \"principle\"; Widerstreit = \"conflict\"; Widerspruch = \"contradiction\"; Realgrund = \"real ground\"; Schranken = \"limitations\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Kräfte = \"forces\"; Lehrgebäude = \"doctrinal edifices\".","\"von diesem ... keinen Begriff\": the \"|\" falls inside the parenthesis in the OCR and is kept where it stands.","\"wirklich\" = \"really\"; Bejahungen = \"affirmations\"; *realitates phaenomena* is Latin in the original, italicized.","\"überall\" in \"noch überall in Ansehung\" is rendered \"at all\"; \"das mindeste\" is left standing as the object of \"bedeutet\", as in the German."]],"krv-b-0827":["Third, the Leibnizian monadology has no other ground at all than that this philosopher represented the difference of the inner and the outer merely in relation to the understanding. Substances in general must have something inner, which is therefore free of all outer",["OCR slip corrected: \"Suchen a\" = Substanzen.","Grund = \"ground\"; Verhältniß = \"relation\" (in the next passage); Verstand = \"understanding\".","Breaks off before \"Verhältnissen\" (krv-b-0828); the sentence continues there."]],"krv-b-0828":["relations, consequently also of composition. The simple is therefore the foundation of the inner of things in themselves. The inner",["Continues krv-b-0827 (\"allen äußeren\" + \"Verhältnissen\"); breaks off after \"Das Innere\".","Zusammensetzung = \"composition\"; Das Einfache = \"the simple\" (einfach = simple); Grundlage = \"foundation\"."]],"krv-b-0829":["however of their state also cannot consist in place, shape, contact or",["Continues krv-b-0828; breaks off mid-sentence, continued in krv-b-0830.","Zustand = \"state\"; Ort = \"place\"; Gestalt = \"shape\"; Berührung = \"contact\"."]],"krv-b-0830":["motion (which determinations are all outer relations),",["Continues krv-b-0829 (\"oder\" + \"Bewegung\"); the clause is completed by \"bestehen\" at the end of this line; the sentence goes on in krv-b-0831.","Bestimmungen = \"determinations\"; Bewegung = \"motion\"."]],"krv-b-0831":["and we can therefore ascribe to substances no other inner state than that through which we determine our sense itself inwardly, namely",["Continues krv-b-0830; breaks off before \"den Zustand der Vorstellungen\" (krv-b-0832).","Sinn = \"sense\" (n.); bestimmen = \"determine\"; beilegen = \"ascribe\" (the verb stands in krv-b-0832)."]],"krv-b-0832":["the state of representations. Thus the mo-",["Continues krv-b-0831; the verb \"beilegen\" is rendered \"ascribe\" in the previous passage, where the German places it here.","OCR slip: \"Wo⸗\" read as \"Mo-\" (for \"Monaden\"); breaks off mid-word.","Vorstellung = \"representation\"."]],"krv-b-0833":["…nades ready-made, which are supposed to constitute the basic material of the whole universe, whose active force, however, consists only in representations, through which they are properly only effective in themselves. | Just for that reason, however, his principle of the possible community of substances with one another also had to be a pre-established harmony and could not be a physical influence. For because everything is occupied only inwardly, i.e., with its representations, the state of the representations of the one substance could stand in no effective combination whatsoever with that of the other, but some third cause, influencing all of them together, had to make their states correspond to one another, though not precisely through occasional assistance, applied specially in each individual case (*Systema assistentiae*), but through the unity of the idea of a cause valid for all, in which they all together must receive their existence and persistence, hence also reciprocal correspondence with one another in accordance with universal laws.",[]],"krv-b-0834":["Fourth, his famous doctrine of time and space, in which he intellectualized these forms of sensibility, had arisen merely from the very same deception of transcendental reflection. If I want to represent outer relations of things through the mere understanding, this can happen only by means of a concept of their reciprocal effect, and if I am to connect one state of the very same thing with another state, this can happen only in the order of grounds and consequences. Thus Leibniz thought",[]],"krv-b-0835":["space, then, as a certain order in the community of substances, and time as the dynamical sequence of their states.",[]],"krv-b-0836":["But the peculiar and thing-independent character that both seem to have in",[]],"krv-b-0837":["themselves he ascribed to the confusion of these concepts,",[]],"krv-b-0838":["which brought it about that that which is a mere form of dynamical relations is taken for an intuition of its own, subsisting for itself and preceding the things themselves. Thus space and time were the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves. The things, however, were intelligible substances (*substantiae noumena*). Nonetheless he wanted to make these concepts valid for appearances, because he conceded to sensibility no peculiar way of intuition, but sought all, even the empirical representation of objects, in the understanding, and left to the senses nothing but the contemptible business of confusing and distorting the representations of the former.",[]],"krv-b-0839":["But even if we could say something synthetically about things in themselves through the pure understanding (which is nonetheless impossible), this still could not be extended at all to appearances, which do not represent things in themselves. In this latter case, then, I will always have to compare my concepts in transcendental reflection only under the conditions of sensibility, and so space and time will be determinations not of things in themselves but of appearances: what things in themselves may be, I do not know and also do not need to know, since a thing can never come before me otherwise than in appearance.",[]],"krv-b-0840":["I proceed in the same way with the remaining concepts of reflection. Matter is *substantia phaenomenon*. What pertains to it internally I seek in all parts of the space that it occupies, and in all effects that it exercises, which, to be sure, can always be only appearances of outer sense. Thus I have, to be sure, nothing absolutely, but only comparatively internal, which itself in turn consists of outer relations. But the absolutely internal of matter, according to the pure understanding, is also a mere fancy; for this is nowhere an object for the pure understanding; the transcendental object, however, which may be the ground of this appearance that we call matter, is a mere something, of which we would not even understand what it is, even if someone could tell us. For we can understand nothing except what brings with it something corresponding to our words in intuition. If the complaints: We have no insight at all into the inner of things, are supposed to signify as much as: we do not comprehend through the pure understanding what the things that appear to us may be in themselves: then they are quite unfair and unreasonable; for they want one to be able to cognize things without senses, hence to intuit them, consequently that we have a faculty of cognition entirely different from the human not merely in degree but even in intuition and way, thus that we should be not human beings but beings of which we ourselves cannot say whether they are even possible, much less how they are constituted | . Observation and dissection of appearances penetrates into the interior of nature, and one cannot know how far this will go in time. Those transcendental questions, however, which go beyond nature, we would nevertheless never be able to answer, even if all of nature were uncovered to us, since it is not even given to us to observe our own mind with another intuition than that of our inner sense. For in it lies the secret of the origin of our sensibility. Its relation to an object, and what the transcendental ground of this unity may be, lies without doubt too deeply hidden for us, who know even ourselves only through inner sense, hence as appearance, to be able to use such an unsuitable tool of our investigation to find anything other than ever again appearances, whose non-sensible cause we would nonetheless gladly investigate.",["- 0833: \"Grundstoff\" = \"basic material\" (Stoff = material); first word \"naden\" is a truncated \"Monaden\" (OCR), rendered \"…nades\"; stray \"o\"/\"So\" OCR artifacts dropped. \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Beharrlichkeit\" = persistence.","- 0833: \"gelegentlichen … Beistand\" rendered \"occasional assistance\"; \"Systema assistentiae\" kept in Latin italics.","- 0834: \"Täuschung\" = \"deception\"; \"Überlegung/Reflexion\" both appear (Reflexion = reflection; Überlegung = reflection, per glossary); \"intellectuirte\" = \"intellectualized\"; \"Verhältnisse\" = \"relations\"; \"Wirkung\" = \"effect\"; \"Folgen\" = \"consequences\" (Folge/Folgerung both \"consequence/sequence\" in glossary; Folge here rendered \"consequences\", in 0835 \"sequence\").","- 0838: \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Art\" = \"way\" (also in 0840 \"Anschauung und Art\" = \"intuition and way\"); \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\" (and \"Object\" also \"object\"); OCR debris (\"qf\", \"f\", \"10\") removed.","- 0839: \"synthetisch\" = synthetic; \"Überlegung\" = \"reflection\"; \"gezogen werden\" = \"extended\" (literally \"drawn\"); the reference of \"dieses\" (what is said) is as ambiguous as in the German. Passage ends \"kommen kann\" with OCR debris \"öl\" dropped.","- 0840: \"Innerliche/Innre\" = \"internal/inner\"; \"Grille\" = \"fancy\"; \"verstehen\" = understand; \"begreifen\" = comprehend; \"erkennen\" = cognize; \"kennen\" = know; \"wissen\" = know; \"Wesen\" = \"beings\"; \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = \"faculty of cognition\". \"Beobachtung\" = observation; \"Gemüth\" = mind.","- 0840: \"Wertzeug\" read as \"Werkzeug\" (tool). \"mit … beschaffen | seien\" keeps the | as in the source. \"schlechthin\" = \"absolutely\"; \"Schlechthin⸗\" hyphenated compound with \"Innerliches\" resolved as \"absolutely internal.\" \"ein bloßes Etwas\" rendered \"a mere something.\"","- 0840: \"Ihre Beziehung … Einheit\": the antecedent of \"Ihre\" (Sinnlichkeit) and of \"dieser Einheit\" is left as in the German; \"Wesen\" and \"Dinge\" distinct from \"Gegenstand.\""]],"krv-b-0841":["What makes this critique of inferences from the mere acts of reflection exceedingly useful is that it distinctly demonstrates the nullity of all inferences about objects that one merely compares with one another in the understanding, and at the same time confirms what we chiefly impressed: that, although appearances, as things in themselves, are not comprehended among the objects of the pure understanding, they are nevertheless the only ones in which our cognition can have objective reality, namely where intuition corresponds to the concepts. If we reflect merely logically, we merely compare our concepts with one another in the understanding, whether both contain precisely the same thing, whether they contradict each other or not, whether something is contained internally in the concept or is added to it, and which of the two is given, but which is to count only as a way of thinking the given one. But if I apply these concepts to an object in general (in the transcendental sense), without further determining this, whether it is an object of sensible or of intellectual intuition, then limitations immediately show themselves (not to go out beyond this concept), which pervert all empirical use of them and by that very fact prove that the representation of an object as a thing in general is not merely insufficient, but, without sensible determination of it and independently of empirical condition, in itself self-contradictory, so that one must therefore either abstract from every object (in logic), or, if one assumes one, think it under conditions of sensible intuition, hence the intelligible would require a quite particular intuition, which we do not have, and in the absence of it is nothing for us, but on the other hand appearances also cannot be objects in themselves. For if I think merely of things in general, then the difference of outer relations certainly cannot constitute a difference of the things themselves, but rather presupposes this, and if the concept of the one is not at all internally distinguished from that of the other, then I only posit one and the same thing in different relations. Further, through the addition of a mere affirmation (reality) to another the positive is indeed increased and nothing is withdrawn or removed from it; hence the real in things in general cannot conflict with one another, etc.",[]],"krv-b-0842":["The concepts of reflection, as we have shown, have through a certain misinterpretation such an influence on the use of the understanding that they have been capable of seducing even one of the most acute of all philosophers to a supposed system of intellectual cognition, which undertakes to determine its objects without the addition of the senses. Precisely for that reason the development of the deceptive cause of the amphiboly of these concepts, in occasioning false principles, is of great utility for determining and securing reliably the boundaries of the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0843":["One must indeed say: what belongs to or contradicts a concept universally also belongs to or contradicts everything particular that is contained under that concept (*dictum de omni et nullo*); but it would be absurd to alter this logical principle so that it read: what is not contained in a universal concept is also not contained in the particular ones that stand under it; for",[]],"krv-b-0844":["these are particular concepts precisely because they contain more in themselves",[]],"krv-b-0845":["than is thought in the universal. Now the whole intellectual system of Leibniz is in fact built on this latter",[]],"krv-b-0846":["principle; it therefore falls",[]],"krv-b-0847":["at the same time with it, together with all the ambiguity in the use of the understanding that springs from it.",[]],"krv-b-0848":["The principle of the indiscernible was actually grounded on the",[]],"krv-b-0849":["presupposition: that if in the concept of a thing in general",[]],"krv-b-0850":["a certain distinction is not encountered, then it is also not to be encountered in the things themselves; consequently all things are completely identical (*numero eadem*) that are not already distinguished from one another in their concept (in accordance with quality or",[]],"krv-b-0851":["quantity). But because in the mere",[]],"krv-b-0852":["concept of any thing abstraction has been made from many necessary conditions",[]],"krv-b-0853":["of an intuition, that from which abstraction is made is, by a singular",[]],"krv-b-0854":["precipitateness, taken to be something that is not to be encountered anywhere, and nothing is granted to the thing except what is contained in its",[]],"krv-b-0855":["concept.",[]],"krv-b-0856":["The concept of a cubic foot of space, wherever and however often I may think it,",[]],"krv-b-0857":["is in itself completely identical. But two cubic feet in space are nevertheless distinguished merely by their places (*numero diversa*); these are conditions of the intuition in which the object of this concept is given, which do not belong to the concept but still belong to the whole of sensibility. In the same way there is in the concept of a thing",[]],"krv-b-0858":["no conflict at all if nothing negating has been combined with an affirming",[]],"krv-b-0859":["one, and merely affirmative concepts can in combination effect no removal at all. But in sensible intuition, in which reality (e.g., motion) is given, there are found conditions (opposite directions), from which abstraction was made in the concept of motion in general, which make possible a conflict, one that is admittedly not logical, namely one that makes possible a zero = 0 out of purely positive things; and one could not say: that therefore all reality is in agreement with one another because no conflict is encountered among their concepts.*)",[]],"krv-b-0860":["*) If one wanted to avail oneself here of the usual evasion, that at least *realitates noumena* cannot work against one another: then one would have to\n\n---\nnotes\n- Obvious OCR slips silently corrected (e.g. \"si selbst\", \"Venn\", \"Orter\", \"Zero - O\"); \"Jüber\", \"geingeschärft\", \"weliher\", \"fol\" likewise.\n- Gegenstand and Object are both rendered \"object\" (0841: \"Gegenstand\", \"Objecten\"); \"Gegenstand überhaupt\"/\"Ding überhaupt\" = \"object/thing in general.\"\n- 0841: \"daß, obgleich Erscheinungen nicht als Dinge an sich selbst … begriffen sind\": the German is ambiguous between \"not as things in themselves\" modifying \"appearances\" or \"comprehended\"; preserved as in the German, hence the awkward English.\n- 0841: \"Verstande\" in \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" is rendered \"sense\" in the sense of \"meaning\" (as the German reads \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" literally \"in the transcendental understanding\"); doubtful, the literal rendering \"in the transcendental understanding\" is also possible.\n- 0841: \"Einschränkungen\" = limitations (Schranke is also \"limitation\" in the glossary); \"Bejahung\" = affirmation; \"Intelligibele\" = \"the intelligible.\"\n- 0841: \"einschärfen\" = \"impress\"; \"widerstreitend/Widerstreit\" = \"self-contradictory/conflict\" (Widerspruch = contradiction is kept distinct).\n- 0843: \"dictum de omni et nullo\" (OCR \"diotum de Omni\") in Latin italics; 0850/0857 \"numero eadem\", \"numero diversa\" in italics; 0860 \"realitates noumena\" in italics. The OCR noise \"r ee\" in 0843 is omitted.\n- 0847 and 0850: the OCR numerals \"5\", \"1o\" are line numbers and are omitted; 0859 \"|\" is the apparatus sign in the German (\"über:|haupt\") and is dropped there as a line-break hyphen artifact; 0860 breaks off mid-sentence.",[]],"krv-b-0861":["to adduce an example of such pure and sense-free reality, so that one might understand whether such a thing represents anything at all or nothing at all. But no example can be taken from anywhere other than from experience, which never According to mere concepts the inner is the substratum of all relation or outer determinations. If I therefore abstract from all conditions of intuition and hold solely to the concept of a thing in general, then I can abstract from all outer relation, and there must nevertheless remain a concept of that which signifies no relation at all, but merely inner determinations. Now it seems that it follows from this: in every thing (substance) there is something that is absolutely inner and precedes all outer determinations, in that it first makes them possible; hence this substratum would be something that no longer contains any outer relations in itself, consequently simple (for corporeal things are indeed always only relations, at least of the parts outside one another); and because we know no absolutely inner determinations except those through our inner sense, this substratum would be not only simple, but also (according to the analogy with our inner sense) determined through representations, i.e., all things would really be monads, or simple beings endowed with representations. All this would also have its correctness, if something more than the concept of a thing in general did not belong to the conditions under which alone objects of outer intuition can be given to us, and from which the pure concept abstracts. For there it shows that a persistent appearance in space (impenetrable extension) can contain nothing but relations and nothing at all absolutely inner and yet be the first substratum of all outer perception. Through mere concepts I certainly cannot think anything outer without something inner, precisely because concepts of relation indeed presuppose absolutely given things and without these are not possible. But since in intuition something is contained that does not lie at all in the mere concept of a thing in general, and this affords the substratum, which through mere concepts would not be cognized at all, namely a space, which with everything it contains consists of nothing but formal, or also real relations, I cannot say: because without an absolutely-inner no thing can be represented through mere concepts, there is also in the things themselves, which are contained under these concepts, and which offers more of them than phaenomena, and so this proposition signifies nothing further than that the concept, which contains nothing but affirmations, contains nothing negating; a proposition that we have never doubted. intuition nothing outer that does not have something absolutely inner lying at its ground. For if we have abstracted from all conditions of intuition, then certainly nothing remains for us in the mere concept except the inner in general and the relation of it among one another, through which alone the outer is possible. This necessity, however, which is grounded on abstraction alone, does not take place with things insofar as they are given in intuition with such determinations as express mere relations, without having something inner at the ground, because they are not things in themselves, but merely appearances. Whatever we know of matter at all are nothing but relations (that which we call inner determinations of it is only comparatively inner); but among them are self-subsisting and persistent ones, through which a determinate object is given to us. That, when I abstract from these relations, I have nothing further at all to think, does not abolish the concept of a thing as appearance, nor the concept of an object *in abstracto*, but does abolish all possibility of such a one as is determinable according to mere concepts, i.e., of a *noumenon*. Admittedly it is startling to hear that a thing is supposed to consist entirely and completely of relations, but such a thing is also mere appearance and cannot be thought through pure categories at all; it consists itself in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. Likewise one can hardly think the relations of things *in abstracto*, if one begins with mere concepts, otherwise than that one is the cause of determinations in the other; for that is our concept of understanding of relations itself. However, since we then abstract from all intuition, a whole way in which the manifold can determine to one another its place, namely the form of sensibility (space), drops out, which nevertheless precedes all empirical causality.",["- The text is disordered as it stands (OCR/transposition): \"die niemals\" runs straight into \"Nach bloßen Begriffen…\", and the clause \"und ihrer mehr als Phaenomena darbietet … niemals gezweifelt haben\" is lodged in mid-sentence before \"Anschauung nichts Äußeres…\". Translated in the order given; nothing supplied.","- Silent OCR corrections: \"man s\" to \"man's\"; \"Verhältnißoder\"; \"fennen\" to \"kennen\"; \"bez harrliche\"; \"gar sein Verhältniß\" read as \"gar kein Verhältniß\"; stray \"i\" and \"C.aausalität\" dropped/corrected. \"Außere\" read as \"Äußere\".","- Verhältniß = relation; Bestimmung = determination; Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinung = appearance; Wesen = being; Wahrnehmung = perception; Ausdehnung = extension; Gegenstand = object (Object also \"object\").","- \"kennen\" (know) and \"erkennen\" (cognize) are kept distinct; \"Phaenomena\" and \"Noumenon\" are Latinized/Greek forms in the text.","- \"zum Grunde läge\" rendered \"lying at its ground\"; \"Grunde haben\" likewise \"at the ground\".","- \"einander seinen Ort bestimmen kann\": rendered \"determine to one another its place\"; the German construction is awkward and kept so.","- \"sei\"/\"wären\" subjunctives rendered with \"would be\" where the German is indirect or hypothetical."]],"krv-b-0862":["If by merely intelligible objects we understand those things that are thought through pure categories without any schema of sensibility, then such are impossible. For the condition of the objective use of all our concepts of understanding is merely the way of our sensible intuition, through which objects are given to us, and if we abstract from the latter, then the former have no reference at all to any object. Indeed, even if one wanted to assume another way of intuition than this sensible one of ours, our functions for thinking would nevertheless be of no meaning at all with regard to it. If we understand by them only objects of a non-sensible intuition, of which our categories admittedly do not hold, and of which we can therefore never have any cognition (neither intuition nor concept), then *noumena* in this merely negative meaning must indeed be admitted: since they then say nothing other than that our way of intuition does not extend to all things, but merely to objects of our senses, consequently its objective validity is bounded, and hence room remains over for some other way of intuition and thus also for things as objects of it. But then the concept of a *noumenon* is problematic, i.e., the representation of a thing of which we can say neither that it is possible nor that it is impossible, since we know no way of intuition other than our sensible one and no way of concepts other than the categories, but neither of the two is adequate to an extra-sensible object. We can therefore not yet positively extend the field of the objects of our thinking beyond the conditions of our sensibility and assume, besides appearances, still objects of pure thinking, i.e., *noumena*, because those have no positive meaning that can be given. For one must concede of the categories that they alone do not yet suffice for the cognition of things in themselves and without the *data* of sensibility would be merely subjective forms of the unity of understanding, but without object. Thinking is to be sure in itself no product of the senses and insofar also not restricted by them, but for that reason not immediately of its own and pure use without the accession of sensibility, because it is then without object. One also cannot call the *noumenon* such an object; for this signifies precisely the problematic concept of an object for an entirely different intuition and an entirely different understanding than ours, which hence is itself a problem. The concept of the *noumenon* is therefore not the concept of an object, but the problem, unavoidably interconnected with the restriction of our sensibility, whether there might not be objects entirely released from the intuition of that, which question can be answered only indeterminately, namely: that, because sensible intuition does not extend to all things without distinction, room remains over for more and other objects, that they are therefore not absolutely denied, but, in the absence of a determinate concept (since no category thereto",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"da keine Kategorie dazu\"); left unfinished.","- Silent OCR corrections: \"Non mena\" to \"Noumena\"; \"proble matischen\"; \"Sinn: lichkeit\"; \"unde stimmt\" to \"unbestimmt\"; \"nicht— sinnlichen\".","- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; Gegenstand is the Germanic, Object the Latinate member.","- Beziehung = reference (distinct from Verhältniß = relation); Einschränkung = restriction; eingeschränkt = restricted; begrenzt = bounded; Aufgabe = problem; zureichen = suffice (zureichend = sufficient).","- \"von jener ihrer Anschauung\": \"jener\" refers back to Sinnlichkeit; rendered \"the intuition of that\" to keep the construction's ambiguity.","- \"von denen unsere Kategorien zwar freilich nicht gelten\": \"gelten\" rendered \"hold\"; \"die Gültigkeit\" = validity.","- \"jene\" in \"weil jene keine anzugebende positive Bedeutung haben\" left as \"those\"; the grammatical antecedent is not unambiguous (noumena, or the objects of pure thinking).","- \"Bedeutung\" rendered \"meaning\" and \"bedeutet\" \"signifies\"; \"Functionen zu denken\" rendered \"functions for thinking\"."]],"krv-b-0863":["is suited) also cannot be asserted as objects for our understanding.",["- Fragment begins mid-parenthesis (\"tauglich ist)\"); the opening of the parenthesis is not in the passage.","- *behaupten* = assert; *Gegenstände* = objects."]],"krv-b-0864":["can be.",["- Fragment: \"werden können\" completes the preceding passage's sentence (\"behauptet werden können\"); translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-0865":["The understanding accordingly bounds sensibility, without thereby expanding its",["- *begrenzt* rendered \"bounds\" (cf. *Grenze* = bound(ary)); *Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility; *Verstand* = understanding.","- \"its\" (*sein*) is grammatically the understanding's; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0866":["own field, and in that it warns the former not to presume to go to things in themselves, but merely to appearances, [continued]",["- Passage breaks off mid-word (\"Erschei⸗\"), completed by the next passage (\"Erscheinungen\"); translated as \"appearances.\"","- \"an— s maße\" is an OCR break for *anmaße* (*sich anmaßen*, \"presume\"); \"s\" is a stray line number.","- *Dinge an sich selbst* = things in themselves (*an sich selbst*; glossary *an sich*).","- *jene* = \"the former\"; the German reference is ambiguous in form but I retained the literal pronoun."]],"krv-b-0867":["so it thinks an object in itself, but only as a",["- *Gegenstand* = object; *denkt sich* rendered literally \"thinks (to) itself,\" here as \"thinks.\"","- *an sich selbst* = \"in itself.\""]],"krv-b-0868":["transcendental object, which is the cause of the appearance (hence itself not appearance) and can be thought neither as magnitude, nor as reality, nor as",["- *Object* (Latinate) and *Gegenstand* are both \"object\" (see previous passage).","- *Ursache* = cause; *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Größe* = magnitude; *Realität* = reality.","- The passage breaks off before \"Substanz\" (the next passage opens after \"|\")."]],"krv-b-0869":["substance etc. (because these concepts always require sensible forms, in which they determine an object); of which therefore",["- \"|\" is retained in the apparatus only as a line break; OCR \"Subftang ıc.\" read as *Substanz etc.*; stray line number \"10\" omitted.","- Here the text \"| Subftang\" occurs after \"als\"; the earlier passage ended at \"als\", so the \"|\" is not repeated.","- *bestimmen* = determine; *Formen* = forms; *sinnlich* = sensible."]],"krv-b-0870":["it is entirely unknown whether it is to be encountered in us or also outside us, whether",["- *wovon* ... *es*: \"of which ... it\" refers to the transcendental object (neuter *es*); kept literal.","- *unbekannt* = \"unknown.\""]],"krv-b-0871":["it would be sublated together with sensibility or, if we take",["- *aufgehoben* = \"sublated\" (literal \"cancelled/removed\"); chosen for consistency with the Latinate cognate.","- \"jene\" appears in the next passage (\"wenn wir jene\"), referring to sensibility."]],"krv-b-0872":["that away, would still remain left over. If we want to call this object noumenon, because the representation of it is not sensible, then this is up to us. But since we can apply none of our concepts of the understanding to it, this representation nevertheless remains empty for us and",["- *Noumenon* left as in the original (Latin/Greek term), italicized: *noumenon*; *Vorstellung* = representation; *Verstandesbegriffe* = concepts of the understanding.","- Stray \"is\" before \"steht\" in OCR is a line number (\"15\"); omitted.","- *übrig bleiben* = \"remain left over.\""]],"krv-b-0873":["serves for nothing except to designate the boundaries of our sensible cognition and to leave a space over, which we can fill neither through possible experience nor through the pure understanding. The critique of this pure understanding thus does not permit",["- *Grenzen* = boundaries; *Erkenntniß* = cognition; *Erfahrung* = experience; *rein* = pure.","- OCR \"Ro\" is a line number (\"20\"); omitted. The sentence continues in the next passage (\"sich ein neues Feld ... zu schaffen\")."]],"krv-b-0874":["oneself a new field of objects beyond those that can come before it as appearances,",["- *sich ein*: \"sich\" here is the reflexive of the infinitive clause, kept as \"oneself\"; the sentence began in the preceding passage.","- *ihm* = \"it\" (the understanding)."]],"krv-b-0875":["to create, and to digress into intelligible worlds, indeed not even into their concept. The fault which leads to this in the most apparent way and certainly excuses, though it cannot be justified, lies in this: that the use of the understanding is made transcendental contrary to its vocation, and the objects, i.e., possible intuitions, must conform to concepts, but not concepts to possible intuitions (as that on which alone their objective validity rests). The cause of this, however, is once again: that apperception and with it thinking precedes all possible determinate arrangement of representations. We therefore think something in general and determine it on one side sensibly, but yet distinguish the universal object represented in abstracto from this way of intuiting it; there there remains to us a way of determining it merely through thinking, which is, to be sure, a mere logical form without content, but which nevertheless seems to us to be a way in which the object exists in itself (noumenon), without looking to the intuition, which is limited to our senses.",["- Passage opens with \"zu schaffen\" completing the preceding sentence; stray \"hs\" and OCR \"fidh\" (= *sich*), \"ſo\" etc. silently corrected. \"so\" before \"die Apperception\" is in the OCR text as \"so\"; kept as \"that apperception\" would drop it—rendered: read \"daß so die Apperception\" as \"that thus the apperception\"; I kept literal \"that ... apperception,\" see ambiguity: *so* left unrendered.","- *Bestimmung* = vocation (glossary); *bestimmen* = determine; *Gebrauch* = use; *Gültigkeit* = validity; *Anschauung* = intuition; *Gegenstand* = object; *Object* also \"object\" (two German words).","- *in abstracto* and *noumenon* kept as Latin/Greek; *Noumenon* italicized.","- *Art* rendered \"way\" throughout; \"Art, ihn anzuschauen\" = \"way of intuiting it.\"","- \"daß so die Apperception\": \"so\" is doubtful in OCR; preserved without strong rendering.","- *zu des stimmen* is OCR for *zu bestimmen*; *ihre objective Gültigkeit*: \"their\" refers to the intuitions or concepts ambiguously; retained.","- \"welche zwar eine bloße logische Form\" etc.: *welche* may refer to *Art* or *Form*; kept ambiguous."]],"krv-b-0876":["Before we leave the transcendental analytic, we must still add something that, although in itself of no particular importance, nevertheless might seem required for the completeness of the system. The highest concept from which one is accustomed to begin a transcendental philosophy is commonly the division into the possible and the impossible. But since every division presupposes a concept to be divided, a higher one must still be given, and this is the concept of an object in general (taken problematically and left undecided whether it is something or nothing). Because the categories are the only concepts that relate to objects in general, the distinction of an object, whether it is something or nothing, will proceed according to the order and direction of the categories.",["- *Eintheilung* = division; *eingetheilten Begriff* = \"divided concept\" (rendered \"concept to be divided\").","- *Transscendentalphilosophie* = transcendental philosophy; *Gegenstand überhaupt* = object in general.","- *Mögliche/Unmögliche* = possible/impossible; *Kategorien* = categories."]],"krv-b-0877":["1) To the concepts of all, many, and one, that which cancels",[]],"krv-b-0878":["everything, i.e., none, is opposed, and thus the object",["- *aufhebt* rendered \"cancels\" here (cf. \"sublated\" earlier for *aufgehoben*); same German root, two English words."]],"krv-b-0879":["of a concept to which no intuition that can be given corresponds,",[]],"krv-b-0880":["= nothing, i.e., a concept without object, like the noumena,",["- *Nonmena* in OCR (*Non-mena*) read as *Noumena*; italicize *noumena* in the original sense."]],"krv-b-0881":["which cannot be counted among the possibilities,",[]],"krv-b-0882":["although they must also not on that account be passed off as impossible",[]],"krv-b-0883":["(*ens rationis*), or like perhaps certain new fundamental forces,",[]],"krv-b-0884":["which one thinks, to be sure without contradiction, but also without example",[]],"krv-b-0885":["from experience are thought and so not among the",[]],"krv-b-0886":["possibilities must be counted.",["- *Grundkräfte* = fundamental forces; *Möglichkeiten* = possibilities; *Widerspruch* = contradiction.","- OCR \"fiù\" (= *sich*) silently corrected. Word order of the two last passages is awkward in German too (\"gedacht werden ... gezählt werden müssen\")."]],"krv-b-0887":["2) Reality is something, negation is nothing, namely a concept",[]],"krv-b-0888":["of the lack of an object, like shadow, cold",[]],"krv-b-0889":["(*nihil privativum*).",[]],"krv-b-0890":["3) The mere form of intuition without substance is in itself no",[]],"krv-b-0891":["object, but the merely formal condition of it (as",[]],"krv-b-0892":["appearance), like pure space and pure time, which indeed",[]],"krv-b-0893":["are something as forms of intuiting, but are themselves no",[]],"krv-b-0894":["objects that are intuited (*ens imaginarium*).",["- *Anschauung* = intuition; *anschauen* = intuit; *Bedingung* = condition; *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Raum/Zeit* = space/time.","- *desselben* (\"of it\") refers to the object (*Gegenstand*)."]],"krv-b-0895":["4) The object of a concept that contradicts itself is",[]],"krv-b-0896":["nothing, because the concept is nothing, the impossible, like perhaps the",[]],"krv-b-0897":["rectilinear figure of two sides (*nihil negativum*).",["- *widerspricht* = contradicts (cf. *Widerspruch*, contradiction)."]],"krv-b-0898":["The table of this division of the concept of nothing (for the division of something running parallel to this follows of itself) would therefore have to be laid out thus:",["- \"da— her\" is an OCR line break for *daher*."]],"krv-b-0899":["Nothing,\nas\nEmpty concept without object,\n*ens rationis*.",["- Table rendered as printed, with the numeral labels (1–4) largely lost in the OCR; none supplied."]],"krv-b-0900":["Empty object of a        Empty intuition without",["- Two table cells run together in the OCR line; the left continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0901":["concept,                 object,\n*nihil privativum*.      *ens imaginarium*.\n4.\nEmpty object without concept,\n*nihil negativum*.",["- The numeral \"4.\" appears in the OCR where printed; numerals 1–3 are not in the text and are not supplied."]],"krv-b-0902":["One sees that the thought-entity (n. 1.) is distinguished from the non-thing (n. 4.) in that the former may not be counted among the possibilities, because it is a mere fiction (though not a contradictory one), while the latter is opposed to possibility, in that the concept even cancels itself. But both are empty concepts. On the other hand the *nihil privativum* (n. 2.) and *ens imaginarium* (n. 3.) are empty data for concepts. If light has not been given to the senses, one can also represent to oneself no darkness, and if extended beings have not been perceived, no space. The negation as well as the mere form of intuition are, without something real, no objects.",["- *Gedankending* = thought-entity (glossary; Latin *ens rationis*); *Undinge* = \"non-thing.\"","- *Objecte* (\"objects\") and *Gegenstände* both \"object.\"","- *aufhebt* here \"cancels\" (see earlier note); *wahrgenommen* = perceived (*Wahrnehmung* = perception); *Data* kept as Latin plural, \"data.\"","- \"dieses\" = \"the latter\"; *Wesen* = \"beings.\""]],"krv-b-0903":["The\nTranscendental Logic\nSecond Division.\nThe",["- *Abtheilung* rendered \"Division\"; *Eintheilung* = \"division\" also. Two German words, one English word.","- Heading continues in the next passage (\"Transcendental Dialectic\")."]],"krv-b-0904":["Transcendental Dialectic.",["- Stray \"5\" (line number) omitted."]],"krv-b-0905":["Introduction.\nI\nOn Transcendental Illusion.",["- *Schein* = illusion (glossary)."]],"krv-b-0906":["Above we called dialectic in general a logic of illusion. That does not mean that it is a doctrine of probability; for this is truth, but cognized through insufficient grounds, the cognition of which is therefore indeed deficient, but not on that account deceptive, and hence must not be separated from the analytic part of logic. Still less may appearance and illusion be held to be one and the same. For truth or illusion are not in the object, insofar as it is intuited, but in the judgment about it, insofar as it is thought. One can therefore indeed say correctly that the senses do not err, but not because they always judge correctly, but because they do not judge at all. Hence truth as well as error, thus also illusion as the lure to the latter, are to be encountered only in judgment, i.e., only in the relation of the object to our understanding. In a cognition that agrees thoroughly with the laws of the understanding there is no error. In a representation of the senses there is (because it contains no judgment at all) also no error. But no force of nature can deviate of itself from its own laws. Hence neither the understanding by itself alone (without the influence of another cause), nor the senses by themselves would err; the former not, because, if it acts merely according to its laws, the effect (the judgment) must necessarily agree with these laws. But in the agreement with the laws of the understanding consists the formal of all truth. In the senses there is no judgment at all, neither a true one nor a false. Since we now have, besides these two sources of cognition, no other, it follows:",["- *Wahrscheinlichkeit* = probability; *Schein* = illusion; *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Urtheil* = judgment; *Erkenntniß* = cognition; *erkannt* = cognized; *Gründe* = grounds; *unzureichend* = insufficient (glossary *zureichend* = sufficient).","- *Irrthum* = error; *irren* = err; *Kraft* = force; *Gesetz* = law; *Wirkung* = effect; *Verhältniß* = relation; *das Formale* = \"the formal.\"","- Stray line numbers and OCR \"zs\", \"a\" omitted/corrected; *dürfen ... gehalten werden* = \"may ... be held.\"","- *Verleitung* = \"lure\"; *trüglich* = \"deceptive\"; *Erkenntnißquellen* = \"sources of cognition.\"","- Sentence breaks off with a colon; completed by the next passage (\"daß der Irrthum ...\")."]],"krv-b-0907":["that error is effected only through the unnoticed influence of sensibility on",["- *Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility; *Verstand* = understanding; *bewirkt* = \"effected.\""]],"krv-b-0908":["the understanding, whereby it happens that the subjective",["- Stray \")\" in the OCR omitted."]],"krv-b-0909":["grounds of judgment flow together with the objective ones and these from",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands, not completed.","- *Gründe* = grounds; *zusammenfließen* = \"flow together.\""]],"krv-b-0910":["their determination”); just as a moving body would indeed by itself always hold to the straight line in the same direction, but which, if another force simultaneously flows in upon it in another direction, turns into curvilinear motion.",["- Opening quotation mark is closed without an opener in the passage: the passage begins mid-sentence (\"ihrer Bestimmung abweichend machen\").","- OCR \"s\" after \"zwar\" and \"für sich\" garbled; the stray \"s\" omitted. \"Kraft\" = force; \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- \"die aber\" has no explicit antecedent in the German (grammatically \"Linie\" or \"Richtung\"); kept as \"which\".","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence at \"auf\" and continues in the next passage; \"auf ... einfließt\" (split across passages) rendered \"flows in upon\"."]],"krv-b-0911":["it flows in upon it, into curvilinear motion. In order to distinguish the pecu-",["- Fragment: opens mid-sentence and breaks off mid-word (\"eigenthüm-\"), completed in the next passage; \"ihn\" = \"it\" (the moving body).","- Text as given begins \"ihn einfließt\"; the preceding \"auf\" belongs to the previous passage, so \"flows in upon\" is not repeated here in full."]],"krv-b-0912":["liar action of the understanding from the force that mingles in with it, it will therefore be necessary to regard the erroneous judgment as the diagonal between two forces that determine the judgment in two different",["- \"eigenthümliche Handlung\" = \"peculiar action\"; the word is split across passages 0911/0912 (OCR reads \"liebe\", corrected to \"liche\").","- \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Urtheil\" = judgment; \"Kraft\" = force.","- Breaks off mid-phrase (\"zwei verschiedenen\" continues in 0913)."]],"krv-b-0913":["directions, which as it were enclose an angle,",["- \"schiedenen Richtungen\" completes \"verschiedenen\"; \"gleichsam\" = \"as it were\"."]],"krv-b-0914":["and to resolve that composite effect into the simple one of the understanding and of sensibility;",["- \"Wirkung\" = effect; \"einfach\" = simple; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"auflösen\" = resolve.","- The sentence continues in 0915 (\"welches in reinen Urtheilen a priori...\")."]],"krv-b-0915":["which must happen in pure judgments *a priori* through",["- \"a priori\" is italicized as Latin. \"rein\" = pure. The sentence continues in 0916.","- \"welches\" has as its antecedent the whole preceding resolution; \"in reinen Urtheilen a priori\" may attach to \"geschehen\" or to \"reinen Urtheilen\"; kept as ambiguous as the German."]],"krv-b-0916":["transcendental reflection, whereby (as has already been indicated) each representation is assigned its place in the faculty of cognition appropriate to it, and consequently the influence of the latter upon the former is also distinguished.",["- \"Überlegung\" = reflection; \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Erkenntnißkraft\" = faculty of cognition (Kraft = force/power elsewhere; here rendered \"faculty\" for the compound).","- \"der letzteren auf jene\": \"the latter upon the former\" kept; the German referents are not specified.","- \"angezeigt\" = \"indicated\"; OCR \"Erfenntnißkraft\" corrected to \"Erkenntnißkraft\"; stray \"is\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0917":["Our business here is not to treat of empirical illusion (e.g., the optical), which is found in the empirical use of otherwise correct rules of the understanding, and through which the power of judgment is misled by the influence of imagination; rather we have to do with transcendental illusion alone, which influences principles",["- \"Schein\" = illusion (glossary); \"Urtheilskraft\" = power of judgment; \"Einbildung\" = imagination (Einbildungskraft in glossary, but the text has \"Einbildung\"); \"Gebrauch\" = use; \"Verstand\" = understanding.","- \"Geschäfte\" = business; \"Grundsätze\" = principles. Passage breaks off at \"ein-\" (continued in 0918: \"einfließt\").","- OCR stray marks \"}\" and \"fo\" removed."]],"krv-b-0918":["upon,",["- \"einfließt\" completes the verb begun in 0917 (\"ein-\"); the sentence continues in 0919. In 0917 I rendered \"influences principles\", so \"upon\" is left here only as a bare fragment: the German passage 0918 contains only \"transscendentalen Scheines allein zu thun, der auf Grundsätze ein-\". Properly: \"with transcendental illusion alone, which influences principles\" belongs here; see 0917."]],"krv-b-0919":["whose use is not even laid out for experience, in which case we would at least have a touchstone of their correctness, but which leads us away altogether, even against all the warnings of critique, beyond the empirical use of the categories, and",["- \"Probirstein\" = touchstone; \"Kategorien\" = categories; \"Erfahrung\" = experience; \"Kritik\" = critique.","- \"der ... wegführt\": the relative pronoun \"der\" refers to \"Schein\"; English \"which\" is ambiguous in the same way as I have kept it, though \"deren\" refers to \"Grundsätze\".","- \"als in welchem Falle\" rendered \"in which case\"; stray \"is\" removed."]],"krv-b-0920":["holds us in suspense with the illusion of an extension of the pure understanding. We will call the principles whose application keeps itself entirely within the limitations of possible experience *immanent*, but those which are supposed to fly beyond these boundaries *transcendent* principles",["- \"Schranken\" = limitations; \"Grenzen\" = boundaries; \"Blendwerk\" = illusion (same English as \"Schein\"; marked here: Blendwerk, not Schein); \"Erweiterung\" = extension (Ausdehnung is also \"extension\" in the glossary).","- \"hinhält\" = \"holds in suspense\" (also \"puts off\"); \"solen\" corrected to \"sollen\"; \"do\" stray omitted. \"Grundsätze\" = principles."]],"krv-b-0921":["call. But by these I do not understand the transcendental\n \n1) Sensibility, subordinated to the understanding as the object on which it applies its function, is the source of real cognitions. But the very same, insofar as it influences the action of the understanding itself and determines it to judge, is the ground of error. Use or misuse of the categories, which is a mere fault of the power of judgment not properly reined in by critique, which does not pay enough attention to the boundary of the ground on which alone the pure understanding is permitted its play; but real principles, which require of us that we tear down all those boundary-posts and arrogate to ourselves an entirely new ground that recognizes no demarcation anywhere. Hence *transcendental* and *transcendent* are not the same. The principles of the pure understanding that we presented above are supposed to be of merely empirical and not of transcendental use, i.e., of a use reaching beyond the boundary of experience. But a principle that removes these limitations, indeed even commands us to overstep them, is called *transcendent*. If our critique can succeed in uncovering the illusion of these arrogated principles, then those principles of merely empirical use can be called, in contrast with the latter, immanent principles of the pure understanding.",["- Footnote text follows the main text \"nennen. Ich verstehe aber unter diesen nicht den transscendentalen\" and the sentence is left broken off; the footnote is printed here in the original order of the passage. OCR marks \"l )\", \"s\", \"1\", \"1\" removed.","- \"Object\" = object; \"Quell\" = source; \"Erkenntnisse\" = cognitions; \"Grund\" = ground; \"Irrthum\" = error; \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Grenze\" = boundary, \"Schranken\" = limitations; \"Grenzpfähle\" = boundary-posts.","- \"Gebrauch oder Mißbrauch ... sondern wirkliche Grundsätze\": the syntax is obscure, with \"nicht den transscendentalen\" apparently lacking its noun (Gebrauch); kept as in German. The relative \"die\" after \"Urtheilskraft\" has \"Urtheilskraft\" as antecedent.","- \"wirklich\" = real (glossary: Wirklichkeit = actuality; reality); \"real\" in \"realer Erkenntnisse\" is Latinate \"real\"; both rendered \"real\".","- \"angemaßten\" = arrogated; \"anzumaßen\" = arrogate."]],"krv-b-0922":["The logical illusion, which consists in the mere imitation of the form of reason (the illusion of fallacies), arises solely from a lack of attentiveness to the logical rule. Hence as soon as this is sharpened for the case at hand, it vanishes entirely. The transcendental illusion, by contrast, nevertheless does not cease even though one has already uncovered it and has clearly seen its nullity through transcendental critique (e.g., the illusion in the proposition: the world must have a beginning in time). The cause of this is that in our reason (considered subjectively as a human faculty of cognition) there lie fundamental rules and maxims of its use, which have entirely the look of objective principles, and through which it happens that the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our concepts, in favor of the understanding, is taken for an objective necessity of the determination of things in themselves. An illusion that is not at all to be avoided, just as little as we can avoid the sea seeming to us higher in the middle than at the shore, because we see the former through higher rays of light than the latter, or still more, just as little as the astronomer himself can prevent the moon from seeming larger to him at its rising, although he is not deceived by this illusion.",["- \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Illusion\" (Latinate) also = illusion — one English word for both. \"Trugschlüsse\" = fallacies.","- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = faculty of cognition; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Dinge an sich selbst\" = things in themselves.","- \"Grundregeln\" = fundamental rules; \"Grundsätze\" = principles; \"Satz\" = proposition.","- \"diese\" (die logische Regel) / \"ihn\" ambiguities kept; \"zu Gunsten des Verstandes\" = \"in favor of the understanding\". OCR \"ob man ihn x schon\" read \"ob man ihn schon\"."]],"krv-b-0923":["Transcendental dialectic will therefore content itself with uncovering the illusion of transcendent judgments and at the same time preventing it from deceiving; but that it also (like logical illusion) even vanish and cease to be an illusion, that it can never bring about. For we have to do with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which itself rests on subjective principles and foists them in as objective, whereas logical dialectic, in the resolution of fallacies, has to do only with an error in following the principles, or with an artificial illusion in imitation of them. There is therefore a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason, not one in which some bungler entangles himself through a lack of knowledge, or which some sophist has artificially devised in order to confuse rational people, but one that attaches irremovably to human reason and, even after we have uncovered its illusion, will nevertheless not cease to delude it and unceasingly to push it into momentary aberrations that always need to be removed.",["- \"Schein\" and \"Illusion\" both = illusion; \"Blendwerk\" = illusion in 0920 and here \"Blendwerk\" = \"illusion\" again (its sense of \"deception\" is not marked in the text).","- \"Kenntnissen\" = knowledge (plural; Kenntnis = acquaintance/information/knowledge). \"Auflösung\" = resolution; \"Trugschlüsse\" = fallacies.","- \"vernünftige Leute\" = rational people. OCR \"|\" and \"‚\" removed."]],"krv-b-0924":["II.",["- Section number only."]],"krv-b-0925":["On pure reason as the seat of transcendental",["- Title breaks off at \"transscenden-\"; completed in 0926 (\"talen Scheins\")."]],"krv-b-0926":["illusion.\nOn reason in general.",["- \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Vernunft\" = reason; \"überhaupt\" = in general."]],"krv-b-0927":["All our cognition begins with the senses, goes from there to the understanding, and ends with reason, beyond which nothing higher is encountered in us for working up the material of intuition and bringing it under the highest unity of thinking. Since I am now to give a definition of this supreme faculty of cognition, I find myself in some embarrassment. There is, of it as of the understanding, a merely formal, i.e., logical, use, in which reason abstracts from all content of cognition, but also a real one, in which it itself contains the origin of certain concepts and principles that it borrows neither from the senses nor from the understanding. The former faculty has indeed long since been explained by the logicians as the faculty of inferring mediately (in distinction from immediate inferences, *consequentiis immediatis*); but the second, which itself generates concepts, is not thereby yet understood. Since a division of reason into a logical and a transcendental faculty occurs here, a higher concept of this source of cognition must be sought, which comprehends both concepts under itself, while we can expect, in analogy with the concepts of the understanding, that the logical concept will at the same time give into our hands the key to the transcendental one, and the table of the functions of the former at the same time the genealogical tree of the concepts of reason.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Erkenntnißkraft\" = faculty of cognition; \"Vermögen\" = faculty; \"Stoff\" = material; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Einheit\" = unity; \"Inhalt\" = content.","- \"Erklärung\" = definition (also explanation); \"erklärt\" = explained. \"befaßt\" = comprehends (not *begreifen*).","- \"einsehen\" = \"understood\" here (not *verstehen*); marked: one English word for two German words.","- \"den Stoff ... zu bearbeiten ...\" attaches to \"angetroffen\" or \"Höheres\": left ambiguous. \"Stammleiter\" = genealogical tree (literally \"ladder of descent\"). OCR \"Perstande\", \"Riß\", \"PVerstande\", \"oon\" corrected."]],"krv-b-0928":["In the first part of our transcendental logic we explained the understanding through the faculty of rules; here we distinguish reason from it by our wanting to call it the faculty of principles.",["- \"Vermögen der Regeln\" = faculty of rules; \"Vermögen der Principien\" = faculty of principles; \"Principien\" (Latinate) = principles, as \"Grundsätze\" also = principles.","- \"erklärten\" = explained (glossary also offers \"defined\")."]],"krv-b-0929":["The expression of a principle is ambiguous and commonly signifies only a cognition that can be used as a principle, although in itself and according to its own origin it is no *principium*. Every universal proposition, even if it be taken from experience (through induction), can serve as the major premise in a syllogism; but for that reason it is not itself a *principium*. The mathematical axioms (e.g., between two points only one straight line is possible) are even universal cognitions a priori and are therefore rightly called principles, relatively to the cases that can be subsumed under them. But I still cannot say for that reason that I cognize this property of straight lines in general and in itself from principles, but only in pure intuition.",[]],"krv-b-0930":["I would therefore call that cognition from principles in which I cognize the particular in the universal through concepts. Thus every syllogism is a form of the derivation of a cognition from a principle. For the major premise always gives a concept that brings it about that everything that is subsumed under the condition of it is cognized from it according to a principle. Since now every universal cognition can serve as the major premise in a syllogism, and the understanding offers such universal propositions a priori, these too can accordingly be called principles with regard to their possible use.",[]],"krv-b-0931":["But if we consider these principles of the pure understanding in themselves according to their origin, they are anything but cognitions from concepts. For they would not even be possible a priori if we did not bring in pure intuition (in mathematics), or conditions of a possible experience in general. That everything that happens has a cause cannot at all be concluded from the concept of that which",[]],"krv-b-0932":["happens in general; rather the principle shows—",[]],"krv-b-0933":["how one could first of all get a determinate concept of experience from that which happens. The understanding can therefore not at all provide synthetic cognitions from concepts,",[]],"krv-b-0934":["and these are what I properly call principles simpliciter: while all universal propositions in general can be called comparative principles.",[]],"krv-b-0935":["It is an old wish that, who knows how late, may perhaps someday be fulfilled: that one might for once, instead of the endless manifoldness",[]],"krv-b-0936":["of civil laws, seek out their principles; for in this alone can the secret consist of simplifying, as one says, legislation. But the laws here too are only limitations of our",[]],"krv-b-0937":["freedom to conditions under which it thoroughly agrees with itself; hence they pertain to something that is entirely our own",[]],"krv-b-0938":["work, and of which we ourselves can be the cause through those concepts.",[]],"krv-b-0939":["But how objects in themselves, how the nature of things, stands under principles and is to be determined according to mere concepts, is, if not something impossible, at least very nonsensical in its demand. But however it may stand with this (for we still have the investigation of it before us), it is at least evident from it: that cognition from principles (in itself) is something entirely different from mere cognition of the understanding, which can indeed also precede other cognitions in the form of a principle, but in itself (insofar as it is synthetic) does not rest on mere thinking, nor contain a universal according to concepts in itself. The understanding may be a faculty of the unity of appearances by means of rules, but reason is the faculty of the unity of the rules of the understanding under principles. It therefore never goes first to experience or to any object, but to the understanding, in order to give a priori to its manifold cognitions a unity",[]],"krv-b-0940":["through concepts, which may be called the unity of reason and is of an entirely different kind from that which can be accomplished by the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0941":["That is the general concept of the faculty of reason, so far as it could be made comprehensible in the complete lack of examples (which are to be given only in what follows).",[]],"krv-b-0942":["On the logical use of reason.",[]],"krv-b-0943":["One makes a distinction between what is cognized immediately and what is only inferred. That in a figure that is bounded by three straight lines there are three angles is cognized immediately; but that these angles together are equal to two right angles is only inferred. Because we constantly need inferring, and thereby finally become quite accustomed to it, we at last no longer notice this distinction and often, as in the so-called deception of the senses, take for immediately perceived something that we have nevertheless only inferred. In every inference there is a proposition that lies at the basis, and another, namely the consequence, which is drawn from the former, and finally¹) the inferential connection (consequence), according to which the truth of the latter is inevitably connected with the truth of the former. If the inferred judgment already lies in the first in such a way that it can be derived from it without the mediation of a third representation, then the inference is called immediate (*consequentia immediata*); I would rather call it the inference of the understanding. But if, besides the cognition laid at the basis, still another judgment is needed in order to bring about the sequence, then the inference is called a syllogism. In the proposition: all human beings are mortal, there already lie the propositions: some human beings are mortal, some mortals are human beings, nothing²) that is immortal is a human being, and these are therefore immediate consequences from the former. By contrast, the proposition: all scholars are mortal, does not lie in the judgment placed underneath (for the concept of scholars does not occur in it at all), and it can be concluded from this only by means of an intermediate judgment.",[]],"krv-b-0944":["In every syllogism I first think a rule (*major*) through the understanding. Second, I subsume a cognition under the condition of the rule (*minor*) by means of the power of judgment. Finally I determine my cognition through the predicate of the rule (*conclusio*), hence a priori through reason. The relation, therefore, which the major premise represents as the rule between a cognition and its condition, constitutes the different kinds of syllogisms. They are therefore precisely threefold, just like all judgments in general, insofar as they differ in the way they express the relation of the cognition in the understanding, namely:",[]],"krv-b-0945":["categorical or hypothetical or disjunctive syllogisms.",[]],"krv-b-0946":["If, as happens for the most part, the conclusion has been set as a judgment, in order to see whether it does not flow from judgments already given, through which, namely, an entirely different object is thought: then",[]],"krv-b-0947":["I seek out in the understanding the assertion of this conclusion, [to see] whether it",[]],"krv-b-0948":["is not found there under certain conditions according to a universal rule. If I now find such a condition, and the",[]],"krv-b-0949":["object of the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, then",[]],"krv-b-0950":["the latter is concluded from the rule, which is valid also for other objects of cognition. One sees from this: that reason in inferring",[]],"krv-b-0951":["seeks to bring the great manifoldness of the cognition of the understanding to the smallest number of principles (universal conditions)",[]],"krv-b-0952":["and thereby to bring about the highest unity of it.",[]],"krv-b-0953":["On the pure use of reason.",["- Ausdruck = expression; bedeuten = signify; Erkenntniß = cognition; Grundsatz/Princip: \"principle\" for both Princip and Grundsatz (0931), Latin *principium* kept.","- Satz = proposition throughout; Obersatz = major premise; Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Schluß = inference; Schlußsatz = conclusion; Folgerung = consequence; Folge = sequence; Schlußfolge (Consequenz) = inferential connection (consequence).","- 0929: \"relativisch\" rendered \"relatively\"; the German \"ob es zwar\" kept as \"although.\"","- 0932: OCR fragment \"Grund— 0 sag\" corrected to \"Grundsatz\"; rendered \"principle\" (Grundsatz). Dash after \"shows\" preserved at passage break. \"A\" and \"j\", \"b\" stray OCR marks in 0933, 0934, 0948 dropped.","- 0943: footnote markers ¹) and ²) retained as in the OCR, but the footnote texts are not in the passage; \"endlich!)\" read as \"endlich¹)\". Ambiguity: \"wahrgenommen\" = perceived (Wahrnehmung = perception).","- 0943: \"ba— den\" read \"haben\"; \"er: kannt\" read \"erkannt.\"","- 0944: \"Verhältniß des Erkenntnisses im Verstande\" kept literally; \"Urtheilskraft\" = power of judgment; \"Arten\" = kinds.","- 0939: \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"Widersinnisches\" rendered \"nonsensical\"; \"Verstandeserkenntniß\" = cognition of the understanding; \"Einheit der Erscheinungen\" = unity of appearances. Passage 0939 includes a \"|\" page break kept as in source only where it fell mid-word in OCR; others were omitted as page markers.","- 0950: \"Object\" (0949) and \"Gegenstand\" (0946, 0950) both = \"object.\"","- 0936: the \"|\" before \"simplificiren\" is retained in the source apparatus but omitted in the English as a page-break sign, not editorial apparatus."]],"krv-b-0954":["Can reason be isolated, and is it then still a special source of concepts and judgments that arise solely from it, and thereby it is related to objects, or is it a merely subaltern faculty for giving to given cognitions a certain form, which is called logical, and whereby the cognitions of the understanding are subordinated only to one another and lower rules to others, higher ones (whose condition comprises the condition of the former in its sphere), as far as this can be accomplished through the comparison of them? This is the question with which we are now occupied only provisionally. In fact, manifoldness of rules and unity of principles is a demand of reason, in order to bring the understanding into thoroughgoing interconnection with itself, just as the understanding brings the manifold of intuition under concepts and thereby brings the former into connection. But such a principle prescribes no law to the objects and does not contain the ground of the possibility of cognizing and determining them as such in general; rather it is merely a subjective law of economy with the stock of our understanding, by comparison of its concepts to bring the general use of the same to the smallest possible number of the same, without one's being entitled on that account to demand from the objects themselves such a unanimity as would further the convenience and extension of our understanding, and at the same time to give objective validity to that maxim. In a word, the question is: whether reason in itself, i.e., pure reason *a priori*, contains synthetic principles and rules, and in what these principles may consist?",["- Quell = source; Urtheile = judgments; Verstandeserkenntnisse = cognitions of the understanding; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness, das Mannigfaltige = the manifold.","- \"und dadurch sie sich auf Gegenstände bezieht\": \"thereby it\" kept in German word order; the antecedent of \"it\" (reason or the source) is left as ambiguous as in the German.","- Principien / Grundsatz both = \"principle\" (Principien is the Latinate member); Gegenstände and Objecten both = \"objects.\"","- \"seiner ie e\": OCR damaged; \"Begriffe\" (concepts) is restored, a doubtful reading. Stray \"$\" in the OCR omitted.","- Haushaltung = economy; Vorrath = stock; Gemächlichkeit = convenience; Einhelligkeit = unanimity."]],"krv-b-0955":["The formal and logical procedure of the same in syllogisms already gives us sufficient guidance as to upon what ground the transcendental principle of the same in synthetic cognition through pure reason will rest.",["- Vernunftschlüssen = syllogisms; Principium = principle (Latinate, as Princip); Erkenntniß = cognition.","- \"derselben\" (twice) is kept as \"the same\"; the antecedent (reason) is not specified."]],"krv-b-0956":["First, the syllogism does not go to intuitions in order to bring them under rules (as the understanding does with its categories), but to concepts and judgments. If, therefore, pure reason also goes to objects, it still has no immediate relation to these *) and their intuition, but only to the understanding and its judgments, which turn first to the senses and their intuition in order to determine their object. Unity of reason is therefore not unity of a possible experience, but is essentially distinguished from this as unity of the understanding. That everything that happens has a cause is no principle at all cognized and prescribed by reason. It makes the unity of experience possible and borrows nothing from reason, which, without this relation to possible experience, from mere concepts, could not have commanded such a synthetic unity.\n\n*) A: darauf",["- Footnote marker and text are OCR-damaged (\"“)\", \"Al; darauf\"); read as \"*)\" and \"A: darauf\" (reading of the A edition, i.e., \"thereto\"). Doubtful.","- \"Nr— theile\" corrected to Urtheile = judgments. Vernunftschluß = syllogism.","- Gegenstände / Gegenstand = objects, object. Anschauung = intuition.","- zunächst = \"first\" (could also be \"most immediately\"); Vernunfteinheit and Verstandeseinheit rendered \"unity of reason\" / \"unity of the understanding.\""]],"krv-b-0957":["Second, reason in its logical use seeks the universal condition of its judgment (of the conclusion), and the syllogism is itself nothing other than a judgment by means of the subsumption of its condition under a universal rule (major premise). Now since this rule is in turn exposed to the very same attempt of reason, and the condition of the condition (by means of a *prosyllogism*) must thereby be sought as long as it is feasible, one sees well that the peculiar principle of reason in general (in the logical use) is: to find for the conditioned cognition of the understanding the unconditioned, with which the unity of the same is completed.",["- Schlußsatz = conclusion; Obersatz = major premise; Bedingung = condition; bedingt = conditioned; Unbedingte = the unconditioned.","- \"all— gemeine\" and \"eigens thümliche\" are OCR splits, corrected.","- \"desselben\" at the end is kept as \"the same\"; its antecedent (cognition or understanding) is left open as in the German."]],"krv-b-0958":["*) A: thereto",["- Footnote text OCR-damaged (\"Al; darauf\"); read as \"A: darauf\" and rendered as above. Doubtful."]],"krv-b-0959":["This logical maxim, however, cannot become a principle of the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage. Principium = principle."]],"krv-b-0960":["pure reason otherwise than by one's assuming: if the conditioned is given, then the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another, which is consequently itself unconditioned, is also given (i.e., contained in the object and its connection).",["- Begins mid-sentence. Reihe = series; Verknüpfung = connection; Gegenstande = object. Stray OCR characters and line numbers omitted."]],"krv-b-0961":["But such a principle of pure reason is obviously synthetic; for the conditioned relates analytically to some condition, to be sure, but not to the unconditioned. There must also arise from it various synthetic propositions, of which the pure understanding knows nothing, as it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, the cognition and synthesis of which is always conditioned. The unconditioned, however, if it actually takes place, can be particularly weighed",["- \"Dedingte\" is OCR for Unbedingte. Stray OCR characters and the \"|\" page-break sign (given in the source as \"syn— | thetisch\") are not carried into the English since it falls inside a word.","- besonders = \"particularly\" (could also be \"separately\"); Sätze = propositions. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0962":["in accordance with all the determinations that distinguish it from everything conditioned, and must thereby give material for many synthetic propositions *a priori*. 5 The ... arising from this supreme principle of pure reason ... be, i.e., no empirical use adequate to it will ever be able to be made of the same. It will therefore distinguish itself from all principles",["- The text after \"a priori\" is corrupt or incomplete in the OCR (\"Die aus diesem obersten Princip der reinen Vernunft entspringende sein, d. i. ...\"): a subject and predicate are missing before \"sein\"; I have not filled the gap and mark it with an ellipsis. The leftover line number \"5\" is an OCR remnant.","- Stoff = material; Bestimmungen = determinations; Princip = principle. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-0963":["synthesis of appearances, or also of the thinking of things in general) extends to the unconditioned, has its objective correctness or not; what consequences flow from this for the empirical use of the understanding, or whether there is rather no such objectively valid proposition of reason at all, but only a merely logical precept, in ascending to ever higher conditions to approach their completeness and thereby to bring the highest unity of reason possible for us into our cognition; whether, I say, this need of reason has been taken, through a misunderstanding, for a transcendental principle of pure reason, which hastily postulates such an unrestricted completeness of the series of conditions in the objects themselves; but what misinterpretations and delusions may still creep, even in this case, into the syllogisms whose major premise has been taken from pure reason (and which is perhaps more petition than postulate), and which ascend from experience upward to its conditions: that will be our business in the transcendental dialectic, which we now want to develop out of its sources, which are hidden deep in human reason. We will divide it into two main divisions, the first of which is to deal with the transcendent concepts of pure reason, the second with transcendent and dialectical syllogisms of the same.",["- Begins mid-sentence and the opening clause is incomplete. \"Erfenntniß\" and \"Berblendungen\" corrected to Erkenntniß and Verblendungen; line numbers omitted.","- Erscheinungen = appearances; Vernunftschlüsse = syllogisms; Obersatz = major premise; Mißdeutungen = misinterpretations; Verblendungen = delusions; Bedürfniß = need; Hauptstücke = main divisions.","- \"deren ersteres ... das zweite\" and \"derselben\" (\"of the same\") preserve the German's open antecedents (the dialectic or reason).","- Petition and Postulat both given as \"petition\"/\"postulate\" (Latinate words); Geschäfte = business."]],"krv-b-0964":["Of the\nTranscendental Dialectic\nFirst Book.\nOn the Concepts of Pure Reason.",["- Begriffen = concepts; Buch = book."]],"krv-b-0965":["Whatever the situation may be with the possibility of concepts from pure reason, still they are not merely reflected but inferred concepts. Concepts of the understanding are also thought *a priori*, before experience and for the sake of it; but they contain nothing further than the unity of reflection on the appearances, insofar as they are necessarily to belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone cognition and determination of an object becomes possible. They therefore first give material for inferring, and no concepts *a priori* of objects precede them from which they could be inferred. Their objective reality, on the other hand, is grounded solely on this: that, because they constitute the intellectual form of all experience, their application must always be able to be shown in experience.",["- \"solen\", \"intelectuele\", \"ges zeigt\" are OCR slips, corrected to sollen, intellectuelle, gezeigt. Line numbers omitted.","- Bewandtniß = \"situation\" (literally what is the case with it); Behuf = \"sake\" (also \"behalf\"); geschlossene = inferred, Schließen = inferring (cf. Schluß = inference/conclusion).","- Reflexion = reflection (Latinate); reflectirte = reflected; Realität = reality.","- Sie / ihre in the last sentences refer to the concepts of the understanding as the German allows; \"Dagegen\" = \"on the other hand\" (could also be \"by contrast\")."]],"krv-b-0966":["The term for a concept of reason, however, already indicates provisionally that it does not want to let itself be confined within experience, because it concerns a cognition of which every empirical one is only a part (perhaps the whole of possible experience or of its empirical synthesis), up to which, to be sure, no actual experience ever fully suffices, but which nevertheless always belongs to it. Concepts of reason serve for comprehending, as concepts of the understanding serve for understanding (perceptions). If they contain the unconditioned, then they concern something under which all experience belongs, which itself, however, is never an object of experience: something to which reason leads in its inferences from experience, and by which it estimates and measures the degree of its empirical use, but which never constitutes a member of the empirical synthesis. If concepts of this kind nevertheless have objective validity, they can be called *conceptus ratiocinati* (correctly inferred concepts); if not, they are at least sneaked in by an illusion of inference and may be called *conceptus ratiocinantes* (rationalizing concepts). But since this can first be settled only in the chapter on the dialectical inferences of pure reason, we cannot yet take account of it, but will provisionally, just as we called the pure concepts of the understanding categories, give the concepts of pure reason a new name and call them transcendental ideas, but now clarify and justify this term.",["- Benennung = term (also \"name\"/Namen below); Begriff = concept; Erkenntniß = cognition; Begreifen = comprehending, Verstehen = understanding; Verstand = understanding.","- Schlüssen / Schließens = inferences / inference (Schluß); the dialectical *Schlüsse* are not further specified in the text.","- \"bis dahin\" rendered literally \"up to which\"; the antecedent (Erkenntniß vs. the whole) is left as ambiguous as in German.","- erschlichen = \"sneaked in\" (obtained by stealth); vernünftelnd = \"rationalizing\" (vernünfteln); Hauptstück = chapter.","- \"zureicht\" = suffices (zureichend = sufficient)."]],"krv-b-0967":["Of the First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic, First Section.",["- Buch = book; Abschnitt = section."]],"krv-b-0968":["On ideas in general.",["- \"überhaupt\" rendered \"in general\"."]],"krv-b-0969":["With the great wealth of our languages, the thinking mind often finds itself nevertheless at a loss for the expression that fits its concept exactly, and in the lack of which it can be made properly understandable neither to others nor even to itself. To coin new words is a presumption to legislating in languages that seldom succeeds, and before one",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage (\"zu diesem verzweifelten Mittel schreitet\").","- Ausdruck = expression; Begriff = concept; Gesetz-geben rendered \"legislating\".","- \"denkende Kopf\" rendered \"thinking mind\" (literally \"head\")."]],"krv-b-0970":["resorts to this desperate means, it is advisable to look around in a dead and learned language to see whether this concept together with its appropriate expression is not to be found there; and even if the old use of it had become somewhat wavering through the carelessness of its originators, it is still better to fix the meaning that was especially proper to it (even if it should remain doubtful whether one had exactly the same in mind at that time), than to spoil one's business merely by making oneself not understood. | For that reason, if for a certain concept only a single word should be found that in an already established meaning fits this concept exactly, the distinction of which from other related concepts is of great importance, then it is advisable not to deal prodi-",["- The passage begins mid-sentence and ends mid-word (\"ver-\" / \"schwenderisch\"); the word is completed in the next passage (\"gally with it\"), which I have split accordingly.","- Gebrauch = use; Bedeutung = meaning; Mittel = means; Begriff = concept.","- \"dessen Unterscheidung\" has an ambiguous antecedent (the word or the concept); I have kept \"the distinction of which\" ambiguous.","- \"im Sinne gehabt\" rendered \"had in mind\" (Sinn = sense)."]],"krv-b-0971":["gally with it, or merely for variety to use it synonymously in place of others, but carefully to reserve for it its peculiar meaning; because otherwise it easily happens that, after the expression does not especially engage attention but is lost among the heap of others of very divergent meaning, the thought too is lost that it alone could have preserved.",["- Begins with the second half of the word split in the previous passage (\"ver-\" / \"schwenderisch\").","- Ausdruck = expression; Bedeutung = meaning; Gedanke = thought."]],"krv-b-0972":["Plato made use of the expression idea in such a way that one readily sees he understood by it something that is not only never borrowed from the senses, but that even far surpasses the concepts of the understanding, with which Aristotle occupied himself, in that nothing congruent with it is ever encountered in experience. The ideas are with him archetypes of the things themselves and not merely keys to possible experiences, like the categories. In his opinion they flowed out of the highest reason, from where they came to share in the human, which however no longer finds itself in its original state, but must with effort recall the old, now very obscured ideas through recollection (which is called philosophy). I will not here engage in any literary investigation in order to settle the sense that the sublime philosopher connected with his expression. I only remark that it is nothing at all unusual, in common conversation as well as in writings, by comparing the thoughts that an author expresses about his object, to understand him even better than he understood himself, in that he did not sufficiently determine his concept and thereby sometimes spoke or even thought contrary to his own intention. |",["- Urbilder = archetypes; Meinung = opinion; Absicht = intention; Gegenstand = object; Sinn = sense; Verstand = understanding.","- OCR \"si\" corrected to \"sich\"; line numbers omitted.","- \"der menschlichen\" (sc. Vernunft) is left elliptical as in the German (\"the human\").","- The final | is carried over from the source."]],"krv-b-0973":["Plato noticed very well that our power of cognition feels a far higher need than merely to spell out appearances according to synthetic unity in order to be able to read them as experience, and that our reason naturally soars to cognitions that go much further than that any object that experience can give could ever be congruent with them, but which nevertheless have their reality and are by no means mere figments of the brain.",["- Erkenntnißkraft = power of cognition (Kraft = power; Erkenntnis = cognition); Erscheinungen = appearances; Gegenstand = object; Realität = reality.","- A stray \"P\" at the end of the OCR was dropped as a slip.","- \"Hirngespinste\" rendered \"figments of the brain\"."]],"krv-b-0974":["Plato found his ideas preeminently in everything that is practical,*) i.e., rests on freedom, which for its part stands under cognitions that are a",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"ein\" / \"a\"); the continuation is not in this passage.","- The \"*)\" marks the footnote given in the next passage.","- \"ein\" could be the article or the beginning of a longer phrase; translated as \"a\"."]],"krv-b-0975":["*) He extended his concept, to be sure, also to speculative cognitions, if only they were given pure and completely a priori, even beyond mathematics, although this has its object nowhere other than in possible experience. In this I cannot follow him, as little as in the mystical deduction of these ideas or the exaggerations by which he as it were hypostatized them, although … peculiar product of reason are. Whoever wanted to draw the concepts of virtue from experience, whoever wanted to make into a model for the source of cognition what can serve at most only as an example for imperfect clarification (as indeed many have done), would make of virtue an ambiguous non-entity, changeable according to time and circumstances, usable for no rule. On the other hand everyone",["- The OCR is corrupt: after \"wiewohl\" the text jumps to \"eigenthümliches Product der Vernunft sind\", which reads like a fragment of the main text (p. 371) displaced into the footnote; I translated it as it stands, marked with \"…\", and did not supply what is missing.","- Stray OCR signs (\"f\", \"j\", \"Ns\") and a line number omitted. \"Erkenntnisquell\" read for \"Erkenntnisgquell\".","- Erläuterung = clarification; Unding = non-entity; Muster = model; Beispiel = example; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object.","- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"Dagegen wird ein jeder\")."]],"krv-b-0976":["becomes aware that, if someone is presented to him as a model of virtue, he still always has the true original merely in his own head, with which he compares this alleged model and by which alone he estimates it. But this is the idea of virtue, with regard to which all possible objects of experience serve, to be sure, as examples (proofs of the practicability",["- Begins mid-sentence (\"inne, daß\" continues \"Dagegen wird ein jeder\" from the previous passage) and ends mid-parenthesis.","- Gegenstand = object; Muster = model; Beweise = proofs; Thunlichkeit = practicability.","- OCR stray letters (\"ii\", \"ilo\") dropped. \"bloß darnach schätzt\" rendered \"merely by it\" (\"alone\" is my wording for \"bloß\"; German has \"merely\" twice, so the repetition is kept as \"merely/alone\")."]],"krv-b-0977":["of that in a certain degree which the concept of reason requires),",["- Completes the parenthesis begun in the previous passage.","- \"heischt\" rendered \"requires\"; Grad = degree."]],"krv-b-0978":["but not as archetypes. That never a human being to the— nevertheless all judgment about moral worth or lack of worth only",["- The text as given is cut or corrupt: \"Daß niemals ein Mensch dem—\" breaks off and runs into \"gleichwohl alles Urtheil…\"; I have not filled the gap or supplied a verb.","- \"dem—\" is a hyphenated line break whose continuation is missing; \"dem\" translated \"to the\".","- Urbilder = archetypes; Urtheil = judgment; Unwerth = \"lack of worth\"."]],"krv-b-0979":["by means of this idea possible; consequently it lies necessarily at the ground of every approach to",["- Begins mid-sentence; \"möglich\" lacks its verb in the text as given, kept as it stands.","- Grunde = ground; notwendig = necessary."]],"krv-b-0980":["moral perfection, however far the",["- Vollkommenheit = perfection; the sentence continues in the next passages."]],"krv-b-0981":["obstacles, not to be determined as to their degree, in human",["- The German word order is kept (phrase between \"Hindernisse\" and \"in der menschlichen\"); Grad = degree."]],"krv-b-0982":["nature may keep us distant from it.",["- Completes the sentence begun at krv-b-0979; \"davon\" rendered \"from it\"."]],"krv-b-0983":["The Platonic republic has, as a supposedly striking",["- Breaks off mid-phrase; \"ist … geworden\" is split across passages and rendered with \"has … become\" in the next."]],"krv-b-0984":["example of dreamed-up perfection, which can have its seat only in the brain of the idle thinker, become a proverb,",["- \"zum Sprichwort geworden\" belongs with the auxiliary of the previous passage (\"ist\"); rendered \"has … become\" across the two.","- Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"krv-b-0985":["and Brucker finds it laughable that the philosopher asserted that never would a prince govern well if he were not partaking of the ideas. But",["- Breaks off after \"Allein\"; \"But\" is its literal rendering.","- behaupten = assert; Ideen = ideas; \"theilhaftig\" rendered \"partaking of\"."]],"krv-b-0986":["one would do better to pursue this thought further and (where the excellent man leaves us without help) to put it in a clear light through new efforts, than to set it aside as useless under the very wretched and harmful pretext of its impracticability¹. A constitution of the greatest human freedom in accordance with laws that make it so that each one's freedom can exist together with the other's | (not of the greatest happiness, for this will already follow of itself) is surely at least a necessary idea, which one must lay at the ground not merely in the first draft of a constitution of a state but also in all laws, and in doing which one must at first abstract from the present hindrances, which perhaps may not so much arise unavoidably from human nature as rather from the neglect of genuine ideas in legislation. For nothing more harmful and more unworthy of a philosopher can be found than the vulgar appeal to allegedly conflicting experience, which would not exist at all if those arrangements had been made at the right time in accordance with the ideas, and if in their place crude concepts, just because they were drawn from experience, had not frustrated every good intention. The more legislation and government were arranged in agreement with this idea, the rarer would punishments indeed become, and then it is quite reasonable (as Plato asserts) that in a perfect arrangement of them no such things at all would be necessary. Although the latter may never come to pass, the idea is nevertheless quite correct which sets up this maximum as archetype, in order to bring the lawful constitution of human beings ever nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For which may be the highest degree at which humanity must stop, and how great the gulf may therefore be that necessarily remains between the idea and its execution, that no one can and ought to determine, precisely because it is freedom that can overstep every boundary given.\n\n¹ [. . .] the lofty language that he used in this field is quite capable of a milder interpretation, appropriate to the nature of things.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence; the footnote marker is lost in the OCR, and I have placed ¹ after \"impracticability\" (*Unthunlichkeit*). The footnote text was misplaced into the main text in the OCR and is moved here; its beginning is not in the passage.","- OCR slips silently corrected: \"je des\" = *jedes*; \"nichis\" = *nichts*; \"Be— griffe\" = *Begriffe*. The stray \"bloß im\" before the footnote text belongs to the main sentence (\"bloß im ersten Entwurfe\").","- *Verfassung* = \"constitution\" throughout; *Grunde legen* = \"lay at the ground\"; *Grenze* = \"boundary\".","- \"wobei\" is rendered \"in doing which\"; the antecedent is ambiguous in the German as in the English.","- \"der andern ihrer\" is rendered \"the other's,\" as in the German's colloquial genitive."]],"krv-b-0987":["But not merely in that in which human reason shows genuine causality, and where ideas become efficient causes (of actions | and of their objects), namely in the moral, but also with regard to nature itself Plato rightly sees distinct proofs of its origin from ideas. A plant, an animal, the regular arrangement of the structure of the world (presumably therefore also the whole order of nature) show distinctly that they are possible only in accordance with ideas; that, although no individual creature under the individual conditions of its existence is congruent with the idea of the most perfect of its species (as little as the human being with the idea of humanity, which he even bears in his soul as the archetype of his actions), these ideas are nevertheless in the highest understanding individual, unalterable, thoroughly determined, and the original causes of things, and only the whole of their combination in the universe is alone and solely fully adequate to that idea. If one separates off what is exaggerated in the expression, the soaring of spirit of the philosopher, to climb up from the bodily consideration of the physical aspect of the world-order to the architectonic connection of it in accordance with ends, i.e., in accordance with ideas, is an effort that deserves respect and emulation; with regard to that, however, which concerns the principles of morality, of legislation and of religion, where the ideas first make the experience itself (of the good) possible, although they can never be fully expressed in it, it is a quite peculiar merit, which one fails to recognize only because one judges it through the very empirical rules whose validity as principles was supposed to be abolished by it. [The text breaks off here, continuing \"sie hat aufgehoben werden sollen\" in the next passage.]",["- The passage ends mid-sentence (\"durch\" is carried by the next passage). I have kept \"by it\" so far as it stands; the bracketed remark above is mine and not part of the text; the English is cut where the German is: \"...was supposed to be abolished by it\" translates the following passage's opening and should be read with that passage.","- \"copeilichen\" read as *körperlichen*; \"nr- ssk sprünglichen\" as *ursprünglichen*.","- *im höchsten Verstande*: \"understanding\" (*Verstand*) is kept; \"sense\" is the other possible reading, and the German leaves it open.","- *Nachfolge* = \"emulation\" (literally \"following after\"); *Ansehung* = \"regard\"; *einzeln* = \"individual\" (also used for *einzelnen*).","- *Gegenstände* = \"objects\"; *Zwecke* = \"ends\"; *Verknüpfung* = \"connection\"; *Verbindung* = \"combination\"; *Gültigkeit* = \"validity\"."]],"krv-b-0988":["it has been supposed to be abolished. For in consideration of nature, experience gives us",["- Joins the preceding passage: *durch / sie hat aufgehoben werden sollen*. The phrase \"by it\" there renders *durch sie*; \"it has been supposed to be abolished\" is *sie hat aufgehoben werden sollen*, where *sie* is ambiguous between the empirical rules and the ideas' effect; it is left as in the German.","- *Betracht* = \"consideration\" here (not *Betrachtung*)."]],"krv-b-0989":["the rule in hand and is the source of truth; with",["- \"Duell\" in the OCR read as *Quell*. *an die Hand geben* = \"give into the hand,\" rendered \"give . . . in hand.\""]],"krv-b-0990":["regard to moral laws, however, experience is (alas!) the mother of illusion, and it is highly reprehensible to take the laws about what I ought to do from that, or to want to limit them thereby, from that which is done,",["- *Schein* = \"illusion\"; *Ansehung* = \"regard.\" The clause \"von demjenigen . . . was gethan wird\" continues into the next passage; \"from that which is done\" belongs to it, and I have only translated up to the break: the final words here are the first part of that clause."]],"krv-b-0991":["what is done. Instead of all these considerations, the proper execution of which in fact constitutes the peculiar dignity of philosophy, we now occupy ourselves",["- Stray \"N\" in the OCR before *Statt* omitted. *Betrachtungen* = \"considerations\"; *Philosophie* = \"philosophy\" (not *Weltweisheit*).","- The first words repeat the break of the preceding passage; the German reads continuously (*von demjenigen . . . was gethan wird*)."]],"krv-b-0992":["with a less brilliant but nevertheless not meritless labor, namely: to make the ground level and firm for building for those majestic moral",["- \"verdienftilo lösen\" read as *verdienstlosen*. The word *Gebäuden* is split across the passage boundary (\"Ge-\" here); the OCR of the next passage reads \"händen,\" corrected to *Bäuden/Gebäuden*. I have put \"edifices\" in the next passage.","- *Boden* = \"ground\" (cf. *Grund*, \"ground,\" elsewhere)."]],"krv-b-0993":["edifices, in which all sorts of mole-tunnels",["- \"händen\" read as the end of *Gebäuden*. *Maulwurfsgänge* = \"mole-tunnels\"; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0994":["of a reason digging in vain, but with good confidence in treasures",["- Fragment; *Vernunft* is in the next passage's first line (\"Vernunft vorfinden\"), so \"of a reason\" here translates *einer . . . grabenden* with the noun supplied by the next passage's first word, as the German splits it."]],"krv-b-0995":["are to be found, and which make that structure unsafe. The transcendental use of pure reason, its principles and ideas, are therefore what it is now incumbent on us to know precisely, in order to be able to determine and estimate properly the influence of",["- The German's *Vernunft vorfinden* opens the passage: \"reason\" of the preceding passage is carried here by the verb \"are to be found.\"","- *Gebrauch* = \"use\"; *Principien* = \"principles\"; *kennen* = \"know\" (*noscere*); *obliegt* = \"is incumbent.\"","- *Bauwerk* = \"structure.\""]],"krv-b-0996":["pure reason and its value. But before I lay this preliminary introduction aside, I request those to whom philosophy is close to the heart (which is saying more than one commonly encounters), if they should find themselves convinced by this and what follows, to take the expression \"idea\" under protection according to its original meaning, so that it not henceforth fall in among the remaining expressions with which all sorts of species of representation are usually designated in careless disorder, and science suffer loss thereby.",["- \"diejenige\" (sic) is plural in sense; rendered \"those.\" *Ausdruck* = \"expression\"; *Bedeutung* = \"meaning\"; *Wissenschaft* = \"science\"; *Vorstellungsarten* = \"species of representation\" (*Art* = species here, cf. \"way,\" \"kind\").","- \"idea\" is in quotation marks because the word is mentioned (*den Ausdruck Idee*).","- The first words (\"pure reason and its value\") begin the sentence that the previous passage opened."]],"krv-b-0997":["Are we in fact lacking in terms that are properly suited to each species of representation, without our having need to encroach on the property of another? [Note: the German continues \"Hier ist eine Stufenleiter\" in the next passage.]",["- Translated as it stands: *Fehlt es uns doch nicht an Benennungen* is a rhetorical negation (\"Surely we do not lack terms\"); I keep the German's *doch* as \"in fact.\" *Benennungen* = \"terms\"; *Vorstellungsart* = \"species of representation\"; *Eigenthum* = \"property.\"","- The bracketed remark in my English is not part of the text."]],"krv-b-0998":["Here is a scale",["- *Stufenleiter* = \"scale\" (literally \"ladder of steps\"); *derselben* (\"of them\") begins the next passage."]],"krv-b-0999":["of them. The genus is representation in general (*repraesentatio*). Under it stands representation with consciousness (*perceptio*). A perception that refers merely to the subject as the modification of its state is sensation (*sensatio*), an objective perception is cognition (*cognitio*). This is either intuition or concept (*intuitus vel conceptus*). The former refers immediately to the object and is individual, the latter mediately, by means of a mark that can be common to several things. The concept is either an empirical or a pure concept, and the pure concept, insofar as it has its origin merely in the understanding (not in the pure image of sensibility), is called *notio*. A concept from *notions*, which goes beyond the possibility of experience, is the idea or the concept of reason. For one who has once become accustomed to this distinction, it must be intolerable to hear the representation of the color red called idea. It is not even to be called *notion* (concept of the understanding).",["- \"Perception\" (German *Perception*, roman type) = \"perception\" (*Wahrnehmung* elsewhere also \"perception\"); *Notio*/*Notionen*/*Notion* are rendered in italics as *notio*, *notions*, *notion* for the foreign word.","- *Gattung* = \"genus\"; *Begriff* = \"concept\"; *Merkmal* = \"mark\"; *Gegenstand* = \"object\" (and *objectiv* = \"objective\"); *Erkenntniß* = \"cognition\"; *Anschauung* = \"intuition\"; *Empfindung* = \"sensation\"; *Zustand* = \"state\"; *Verstand* = \"understanding\"; *Sinnlichkeit* = \"sensibility\"; *Möglichkeit* = \"possibility\"; *Vernunftbegriff* = \"concept of reason.\"","- *Jene* / *dieser* = \"the former\" / \"the latter.\"","- \"Er kenntniß\" in the OCR read as *Erkenntniß*. *einzeln* = \"individual.\"","- *Modification* = \"modification.\"","- The first word *derselben* joins the preceding passage (\"Here is a scale of them\")."]],"krv-b-1000":["Of the",["- Fragment of the heading; completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1001":["First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic |",["- *Buch* = \"book\"; *Dialektik* = \"dialectic.\""]],"krv-b-1002":["Second Section.\nOn the transcendental ideas.",["- *Abschnitt* = \"section\"; *Von* = \"On.\""]],"krv-b-1003":["The transcendental analytic gave us an example of how the mere logical form of our cognition can contain the origin of pure concepts *a priori*, which represent objects prior to all experience, or rather indicate the synthetic unity that alone makes possible an empirical cognition of objects. The form of the judgment (transformed into a concept of the synthesis of intuitions) brought forth categories, which guide all use of the understanding in experience. In just the same way we can expect that the form of the inferences of reason, if one applies it to the synthetic unity of intuitions in accordance with the measure of the categories, will contain the origin of particular concepts *a priori*, which we can call pure concepts of reason or transcendental ideas, and which will determine the use of the understanding in the whole of the entire experience in accordance with principles.",["- \"dem Urtheile\" in the OCR read as *des Urtheils*; the stray \"$\" and the line-number debris omitted. The \"|\" after *vorstellen* in the OCR (printed \"vors | stellen\") is a page break in the original and is carried here at the break: \"represent\" stands without sign, as the OCR gives \"vors | stellen\" within a word.","- *Vernunftschlüsse* = \"inferences of reason\" (glossary also allows \"syllogisms\"); *Urtheil* = \"judgment\"; *Maßgebung* = \"measure\" (literally \"giving of measure\"); *Gebrauch* = \"use\"; *Verstandesgebrauch* = \"use of the understanding\"; *besonder* = \"particular\"; *Erfahrung* = \"experience\"; *Grundsatz*/*Principien* = \"principles.\"","- *Ganzen der gesammten Erfahrung*: \"the whole of the entire experience.\"","- *Synthesis* = \"synthesis\"; *Anschauungen* = \"intuitions.\""]],"krv-b-1004":["The function of reason in its inferences consisted in the universality of cognition in accordance with concepts, and the inference of reason itself",["- \"Algemeinheit\" in the OCR read as *Allgemeinheit*. *Schlüssen* = \"inferences\"; *Vernunftschluß* = \"inference of reason\"; the sentence breaks off and is not completed."]],"krv-b-1005":["is a judgment that is determined *a priori* in the whole domain of its condition. The proposition: Caius is mortal, I could also draw merely through the understanding from experience. But I seek a concept that contains the condition under which the predicate (assertion in general) of this judgment is given (i.e., here the concept of man), and after I have subsumed under this condition, taken in its whole domain, (all men are mortal): so I determine accordingly the cognition of my object (Caius is",["- Passage begins and ends mid-sentence. Running head, page number, and OCR line-number debris (e.g., \"i\", \"i|\") omitted.","- *a priori* could attach to \"bestimmt wird\" or to \"in dem ganzen Umfange seiner Bedingung\"; left open as in the German.","- Umfang = domain; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object; Urtheil = judgment; Satz = proposition.","- \"Assertion\" is Latinate, distinct from behaupten (assert), and is rendered \"assertion.\""]],"krv-b-1006":["mortal). Accordingly we restrict in the conclusion of an inference of reason a predicate to a certain object, after we have previously thought it in the major premise in its whole domain under a certain condition. This completed magnitude of the domain in relation to such a condition is called universality (*Universalitas*). To this corresponds in the synthesis of intuitions the totality (*Universitas*) or totality of the conditions. Thus the transcendental concept of reason is none other than that of the totality of the conditions for a given conditioned. Since now the unconditioned alone makes the",["- Allheit and Totalität are both rendered \"totality.\" Allgemeinheit = universality.","- Conclusion = \"conclusion\" (Latinate; Schluß elsewhere in the passage is part of Vernunftschluß = inference of reason).","- Obersatz = major premise; Größe = magnitude; Anschauung = intuition.","- Line-number debris omitted. The sentence breaks off after \"macht die\", and the next passage supplies the rest."]],"krv-b-1007":["totality of conditions possible, and conversely the totality",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence. The preceding passage ends \"Da nun das Unbedingte allein die.\"","- \"macht ... möglich\" = \"makes ... possible.\""]],"krv-b-1008":["As many ways of the relation as there now are, which the understanding by—",["- The German breaks the word \"ver—mittelst\" (\"by means of\") across the passage boundary. \"Verhältniß\" = relation; Art = way."]],"krv-b-1009":["means of the categories represents to itself, so many sorts of pure concepts of reason will there also be; and first an unconditioned of the categorical synthesis in a subject, second of the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series, third of the disjunctive synthesis of the parts in a system will therefore have to be sought.",["- Continuation of \"ver—mittelst\" from the previous passage. The OCR \"Sͤynthesis\" is silently corrected.","- Glied = member; Reihe = series; Theil = part.","- The subject of \"zu suchen sein\" is \"ein Unbedingtes,\" which is repeated with each of the three genitives."]],"krv-b-1010":["For there are just as many kinds of inferences of reason, each of which proceeds through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned: the one to the subject, which is itself no longer a predicate, the other to the presupposition, which presupposes nothing further, and the third to an aggregate of the members of the division, to which nothing further is required in order to complete the division of a concept. Hence the pure concepts of reason of the totality in the synthesis of conditions are necessary, at least as problems, in order to continue the unity of the understanding where possible up to the unconditioned, and are grounded in the nature of human reason; even if, moreover, these transcendental concepts lack a use appropriate to them *in concreto*, and consequently have no other utility than to bring the understanding into the direction in which its use, in that it is extended to the utmost, is at the same time made thoroughly in agreement with itself.",["- Vernunftschluß = inference of reason; Aufgabe = problem; Totalität = totality; Einheit = unity.","- \"zu welchen nichts weiter erforderlich ist\" is kept as \"to which nothing further is required\"; the German does not say more precisely how \"zu\" is governed.","- \"indem er\" has \"Gebrauch\" as antecedent; \"einstimmig\" = \"in agreement.\"","- Line-number debris omitted."]],"krv-b-1011":["But in that we here speak of the totality of the conditions and of the unconditioned as the common title of all concepts of reason, we again come upon an expression that we cannot dispense with, and yet, owing to an ambiguity attached to it through long misuse, cannot use safely. The word absolute is one of the few words that in their original meaning have been adapted to a concept to which afterwards absolutely no other word of the very same language fits exactly, and whose loss, or what is just as much, whose wavering use must therefore also draw after it the loss of the concept itself, and indeed of a concept that, because it occupies reason very much, cannot be dispensed with without great detriment to all transcendental judgments. The word absolute is now more often used merely to indicate that something holds of a thing considered in itself and thus internally. In this meaning absolutely-possible would signify that which is possible in itself (*interne*), which is in fact the least that one can say of an object. On the other hand it is also sometimes used to indicate that something is valid in every relation (unrestrictedly) (e.g., absolute rule), and absolutely-possible would in this meaning signify that which is possible in every aim, in every relation, which again is the most that I can say about the possibility of a thing. Now these meanings do indeed sometimes coincide. Thus, e.g., what is internally impossible is also impossible in every relation, hence absolutely impossible. But in most cases they are infinitely far apart, and I can in no way infer that, because something is possible in itself, it is therefore also possible in every relation, hence absolutely possible. Indeed of absolute necessity I shall show in what follows that it by no means depends in all cases on the internal one and thus need not be regarded as equivalent in meaning with it. That whose opposite is internally impossible, its opposite is of course also impossible in every aim, hence it itself is absolutely necessary; but I cannot conversely infer, what",["- Beurteilung is rendered \"judgment\" per the glossary, as is Urtheil; the German words differ.","- Absicht = aim (glossary); \"in aller Absicht\" is rendered \"in every aim\" and parallels \"in aller Beziehung\" = \"in every relation.\"","- schließen = \"infer\" (not in glossary); Bedeutung = meaning; bedeuten = signify; gleichbedeutend = \"equivalent in meaning.\"","- \"in der Folge\" = \"in what follows.\"","- \"verbinden\" is not rendered here. \"Herrschaft\" = \"rule.\"","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; OCR \"Dec deutung,\" \"fidh,\" etc. silently corrected. Line numbers omitted.","- \"müsse\" with \"nicht\" is rendered \"need not.\"","- The subject of \"nicht ... sicher brauchen können\" is the earlier \"wir\"; \"brauchen\" = use (cf. Gebrauch)."]],"krv-b-1012":["absolutely necessary, its opposite is internally impossible, i.e.",["- Continuation of the sentence of the preceding passage. The OCR \"seit)\" is read as \"sei\"; the subjunctive is not carried over into the English.","- Fragment; breaks off before \"die absolute Nothwendigkeit.\""]],"krv-b-1013":["the absolute necessity of things be an internal necessi—",["- \"sei\" is subjunctive (continuing indirect statement). The word \"Nothwendig—keit\" is split across passages.","- innere = internal (as innerlich above)."]],"krv-b-1014":["ty; for this internal necessity is in certain cases a quite empty expression, with which we cannot combine the least concept, whereas that of the necessity of a thing in every rela—",["- Verbinden = combine (Verbindung = combination). Ausdruck = expression.","- \"der\" in \"dagegen der von der Nothwendigkeit\" is a demonstrative standing for \"Begriff.\"","- The word \"Bezieh—ung\" is split across passages; \"Beziehung\" = relation."]],"krv-b-1015":["tion (to everything possible) carries with it quite special determinations.",["- Bestimmungen = determinations; besonder = special (glossary: particular; special).","- \"auf alles Mögliche\" = \"to everything possible\"; Mögliche is nominalized."]],"krv-b-1016":["Since now the loss of a concept of great application in specu—",["- The word \"specu—lative\" is split across passages. Weltweisheit = philosophy."]],"krv-b-1017":["lative philosophy can never be indifferent to the philosopher, so I hope that the determination and careful preservation of the expression on which the concept hangs will also not be indifferent to him.",["- Bestimmung = determination; gleichgültig = indifferent; Ausdruck = expression. \"o\" in the OCR is a line number and is omitted."]],"krv-b-1018":["In this extended meaning I shall then avail myself of the word",["- \"erweiterten\" = \"extended.\" The genitive \"des Worts\" after \"sich bedienen\" is rendered by \"avail myself of.\""]],"krv-b-1019":["absolute and set it in opposition to what is valid merely comparatively or in particular re—",["- \"Rück—sicht\" is split across passages; the English \"re—gard\" is split at a corresponding point, but not at the same syllable. The word order is adapted: \"entgegensetzen\" stands at the head of 1020 in the German, while \"Gültigen\" there follows the break.","- OCR \"Rüd-\" read as \"Rück-.\""]],"krv-b-1020":["gard; for this latter is",["- The German continues \"auf Bedingungen restringirt\" in the next passage; \"to conditions\" is left to the next passage.","- Gültigen = \"valid\" (placed in the preceding passage because of English order)."]],"krv-b-1021":["restricted to conditions, but that holds without restriction.\n\nNow the transcendental concept of reason always goes only to the",["- restringirt = restricted; Restriction = restriction. A paragraph break is inserted before \"Nun\" (the OCR \"ö\" is a line number).","- Passage ends mid-sentence (\"auf die\")."]],"krv-b-1022":["absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions and never ends except at the simply, i.e. in every relation, unconditioned. For pure reason leaves everything to the understanding, which relates itself first of all to the objects of intuition or rather their synthesis in the imagi—",["- \"schlechthin\" is rendered \"simply\" (not in glossary). Gegenstand = object; Einbildungskraft = imagination, whose word is split (\"Ein—bildungskraft\") across passages.","- \"endigt niemals als bei\" = \"never ends except at.\""]],"krv-b-1023":["nation. The former reserves to itself alone the absolute totality in the",["- Continuation of Einbildungskraft. \"bezieht\" was moved to the preceding passage in English (\"relates itself\").","- \"Jene\" = \"the former\"; its antecedent is left as in the German."]],"krv-b-1024":["use of the concepts of the understanding and seeks the synthetic unity,",["- \"Gebrauche der Verstandesbegriffe\" = \"use of the concepts of the understanding\"; Einheit = unity; synthetisch = synthetic.","- The separable \"vor\" of \"behält ... vor\" is incorporated into \"reserves\" in 1023."]],"krv-b-1025":["which is thought in the category, to carry out as far as the simply-unconditioned. One can therefore call this the unity of reason of the appear—",["- \"hinauszuführen\" comes at the end of its clause in the German; the English places it after the relative clause, mirroring this. \"Schlechthin-Unbedingten\" = \"simply-unconditioned.\"","- Erscheinung is split across passages (\"Er—scheinungen\"); here \"appear—ances.\""]],"krv-b-1026":["ances, just as one calls that, which the category expresses, unity of the understanding. Thus reason accordingly relates itself only to the use of the understanding, and indeed not insofar as this contains the ground of possible",["- \"nennen\" governs both objects in the German; \"one calls\" is its English counterpart. Vernunfteinheit = \"unity of reason\"; Verstandeseinheit = \"unity of the understanding\"; Erscheinungen = appearances.","- Passage breaks off in mid-clause (\"möglicher\")."]],"krv-b-1027":["experience (for the absolute totality of the conditions is no",["- Erfahrung = experience. Breaks off mid-sentence (\"kein\")."]],"krv-b-1028":["concept usable in an experience, because no experience is unconditioned",["- The verb \"ist\" stands at the head of the next passage in the German, before the closing parenthesis; here it is placed in this passage, with the parenthesis in the next.","- brauchbar = usable (cf. Gebrauch = use)."]],"krv-b-1029":["), but in order to prescribe to it the direction toward a certain unity, of which the understanding has no concept, and which aims at gathering together all actions of the understanding, with regard to every object, into an absolute whole. Hence the objective use of the pure concepts of reason is always transcendent, whereas that of the pure concepts of the understanding must by its nature always be immanent, in that it limits itself merely to possible experience.",["- The leading \")\" closes the parenthesis opened in 1027. \"ihm\" refers to the \"Verstandesgebrauch\" of 1026.","- \"zusammen zu fassen\" = \"gathering together\" (not zu begreifen); Verstandeshandlungen = actions of the understanding; Ansehung = regard.","- einschränkt = limits (Schranke = limitation); transscendent = transcendent; immanent = immanent. The OCR \"imma—nent\" is joined."]],"krv-b-1030":["By an idea I understand a necessary concept of reason to which no congruent object can be given in the senses. Thus our pure concepts of reason now considered are transcendental ideas. They are concepts of pure reason; for they consider all cognition of experience as determined through an absolute totality of conditions. They are not invented arbitrarily, but are set as a task by the nature of reason itself, and therefore relate necessarily to the whole use of the understanding. They are, finally, transcendent and overstep the boundary of all experience, in which, therefore, an object can never occur that would be adequate to the transcendental idea. If one names an idea, one says a great deal in respect of the object (as of an object of the pure understanding), but in respect of the subject (i.e., regarding its actuality under empirical condition) for that very reason very little, because it, as the concept of a *maximum*, *in concreto*",["- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; \"Object\" appears once, in \"dem Object nach\".","- Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\"; Erfahrungserkenntniß = \"cognition of experience\"; Des dingungen corrected to Bedingungen.","- aufgegeben (\"set as a task\"; cf. Aufgabe, \"problem\"); the \"sie\" before \"in concreto\" could refer to the idea or to the concept.","- Sinnen = \"senses\" (plural of Sinn)."]],"krv-b-1031":["can never be given congruently. Since now the latter is actually the whole aim in the merely speculative use of reason, and the approximation to a concept that is nevertheless never reached in exercise is just as much as if the concept were altogether missed, it is said of such a concept: it is only an idea. Thus one could say: the absolute whole of all appearances is only an idea, for since we can never sketch the like in an image, it remains a problem without any solution. On the other hand, since in the practical use of the understanding it is solely a matter of exercise according to rules, the idea of practical reason can always be given actually, although only in part, *in concreto*, indeed it is the indispensable condition of every practical use of reason. Its exercise is always bounded and deficient, but under boundaries that cannot be determined, hence always under the influence of the concept of an absolute completeness. Accordingly the practical idea is always most fruitful and, with regard to actual actions, unavoidably necessary. In it pure reason even has causality to bring forth actually what its concept contains; hence one cannot say of wisdom, as it were disparagingly: it is only an idea; but precisely for that reason, because it is the idea of the neces- as original, at least limiting condition, to serve as rule.",["- The passage ends with displaced and broken lines (\"nothals ursprüngliche ... zur Regel dienen\"); translated as it stands, not completed. \"noth-\" is the beginning of a word, probably nothwendig-.","- Ausübung = \"exercise\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Auflösung = \"solution\"; Handlungen = \"actions\"; einschränkende = \"limiting\".","- Stray line numbers and OCR junk (\"Sr\", \"z\", \"Ders nunft\") silently removed or corrected."]],"krv-b-1032":["Although we must now say of the transcendental concepts of reason",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1033":["that they are only ideas, we shall nevertheless by no means have to regard them as super- | ject can be determined, they can nevertheless at bottom and unnoticed",["- The text is displaced here: \"über-\" continues in the next passage (\"flüssig und nichtig\"), and \"| ject bestimmt werden kann\" is the end of a clause begun there (\"kein Ob-\"). Translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1034":["fluous and null. For even if thereby no ob- the understanding as a canon of its extended and harmonious use, whereby it indeed cognizes no more of an object than it would cognize according to its concepts, but is nevertheless led better and further in this cognition. Not to mention that they perhaps make possible a transition from the concepts of nature to the practical ones, and can in such a way procure for the moral ideas themselves stability and interconnection with the speculative cognitions of reason. About all this one must await the disclosure in the sequel",["- The first lines are displaced in the source (\"kein Ob-\" and \"dem Verstande zum Kanon ... dienen\" are separated); translated as it stands.","- \"dadurch er\": \"er\" could refer to the understanding or to its use (both masculine); rendered \"it\".","- Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Naturbegriffe = \"concepts of nature\"; einhellig = \"harmonious\"; Aufschluß = \"disclosure\"."]],"krv-b-1035":[".",["- Fragment: \"warten\", completing \"er- | warten\" in the previous passage (\"await\"); here only the sentence's final word appears, already translated there."]],"krv-b-1036":["In accordance with our aim, however, we set the practical ideas aside here and therefore consider reason only in the speculative use and in this",["- Absicht = \"aim\"; Betrachtung (betrachten) = \"consider\"."]],"krv-b-1037":["still more narrowly, namely only in the transcendental use. Here we must now take the same path that we above in the deduction zusehen.",["- Corrupt: \"zusehen\" at the end is unintelligible and left untranslated; the sense of the clause is broken off and not supplied."]],"krv-b-1038":["Reason, as the faculty of a certain logical form of cogni-",["- Fragment; \"Erfennt-\" corrected to Erkenntniß (cognition). Vermögen = \"faculty\"."]],"krv-b-1039":["Subsumption of the condition of a possible judgment under the condi- tion of a given one) to judge. The given judgment is the universal rule (major premise, *Major*). The subsumption of the condition of an",["- Opening parenthesis is missing in the passage (breaks off mid-sentence); \"Bedin— gnug\" corrected to Bedingung. Obersatz = \"major premise\"; Urtheil = \"judgment\"; urtheilen = \"judge\"."]],"krv-b-1040":["other possible judgment under the condition of the rule is the minor premise (*Minor*). The actual judgment, which states the assertion of the rule",["- Untersatz = \"minor premise\"; Assertion (Latinate) = \"assertion\", cf. behaupten."]],"krv-b-1041":["in the subsumed case, is the conclusion (*Conclusio*). The rule, namely, says something universally under a certain condition. Now in an occurring case the condition of the rule obtains. Thus what held universally under that condition is also regarded as valid in the occurring case (which carries this condition with it). One easily sees that reason arrives at a cognition through actions of the understanding, which make up a series of conditions. If I arrive at the proposition: all bodies are alterable, only by beginning from the more remote cognition (in which the concept of body does not yet occur, but which nevertheless contains the condition of it): everything composite is alterable; from this go to a nearer one, which stands under the condition of the first: bodies are composite; and from this for the first time to a third, which now connects the remote cognition (alterable) with the one at hand: consequently bodies are alterable: then I have arrived through a series of conditions (premises) at a cognition (conclusion). Now every series whose exponent (of the categorical or hypothetical judgment) is given can be continued; hence precisely the same action of reason leads to the *ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*, which is a series of inferences that can be continued to indeterminate distances either on the side of the conditions (*per prosyllogismos*) or of the conditioned (*per episyllogismos*).",["- Schlußsatz = \"conclusion\"; Schluß = \"inference\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; gültig = \"valid\"; Prämissen/Conclusion Latinate, both rendered \"premises\"/\"conclusion\".","- veränderlich = \"alterable\"; Verstandeshandlungen = \"actions of the understanding\"; Vernunfthandlung = \"action of reason\"."]],"krv-b-1042":["But one soon becomes aware that the chain or series of prosyllogisms, i.e., of the concluded cognitions on the side of the grounds or of the conditions for a given cognition, in other words, the ascending series of syllogisms, must nevertheless behave differently toward the faculty of reason than the descending series, i.e., the progress of reason on the side of the conditioned through episyllogisms. For since in the former case the cognition (*conclusio*) is given only as conditioned: one cannot arrive at it by means of reason otherwise than at least under the presupposition that all members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of premises), because only under the presupposition of these is the judgment at hand possible *a priori*; on the other hand, on the side of the conditioned or of the consequences only a becoming series, and not one already wholly presupposed or given, hence only a potential progress, is thought. Hence if a cognition is regarded as conditioned, reason is compelled to regard the series of conditions in the ascending line as completed and given according to its totality. But if the very same cognition is regarded at the same time as the condition of other cognitions, which among one another make up a series of consequences in a descending line, reason can be wholly indifferent how far | this progress extends *a parte posteriori*, and whether totality of this series is possible at all; since it does not need such a series for the conclusion lying before it, in that this is already sufficiently determined and secured through its grounds |",["- gefolgerten = \"concluded\" (folgern); Folgerungen = \"consequences\"; Vernunftschlüsse = \"syllogisms\"; Fortgang = \"progress\"; Gründe = \"grounds\"; Totalität = \"totality\".","- Apparatus signs \"|\" kept in their places; OCR line numbers and junk removed."]],"krv-b-1043":["*parte priori* already sufficiently determined and secured. It may now",["- Fragment: the Latin phrase is the end of \"a parte priori\" begun in the previous passage; sentence continues in the next."]],"krv-b-1044":["be that on the side of the conditions the series of premises has a first as supreme condition or not, and is thus without boundaries *a parte priori* |; it must nevertheless contain totality of the condition, supposing that we could never succeed in grasping it; and the whole series must be unconditionally true if the conditioned, which is regarded as a consequence springing from it, is to count as true. This",["- The \"|\" is carried over where it stands in the source (\"a parte priori | ohne Grenzen\"), here placed before the semicolon after the sense-order was kept; it belongs after \"priori\".","- Grenze = \"boundary\"; gesetzt = \"supposing\"; \"fassen\" = \"grasp\"; OCR \"opne\", \"fegt\" corrected to ohne, gesetzt."]],"krv-b-1045":["is a demand of reason, which proclaims its cognition as determined *a priori*",["- \"a priori\" could attach to \"determined\" or to \"proclaims ... as\"; left as in the German."]],"krv-b-1046":["and as necessary: either in itself, and then it needs",["- an sich selbst = \"in itself\"."]],"krv-b-1047":["no grounds, or, if it is derived, as a member of a series",["- Fragment; \"es\" rendered \"it\", referring to cognition as in the German."]],"krv-b-1048":["of grounds, which itself is true in an unconditioned way.",["- \"die\" may refer to the series or to the grounds (plural); left ambiguous as in the German (\"which\" rendered unresolved).","- unbedingter Weise = \"in an unconditioned way\"."]],"krv-b-1049":["Of the\nFirst Book of the Transcendental Dialectic,\nThird Section.\nSystem of the transcendental ideas.",["Dialektik: dialectic. Idee: idea."]],"krv-b-1050":["We have here to do not with a logical dialectic, which",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1051":["abstracts from all content of cognition and merely uncovers the false",["Inhalt: content. Erkenntniß: cognition. Schein follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1052":["illusion in the form of syllogisms, but with a transcendental one, which entirely a priori the origin of certain cognitions",["Schein: illusion. Vernunftschlüsse: syllogisms (Schlüsse elsewhere: inferences).","The verb \"enthalten soll\" comes only in the second passage after this one. \"Völlig a priori\" could go with \"Ursprung\" or with the verb; the ambiguity is preserved."]],"krv-b-1053":["from pure reason and inferred concepts, whose object cannot be given empirically at all, which therefore lie wholly outside the facul-",["geschlossener: \"inferred\" (literally also \"closed\"). Gegenstand: object. Begriffe: concepts.","The German breaks the word Vermögen across the passages; the hyphen is mirrored in English. The same is done below for Vernunft and erreichen."]],"krv-b-1054":["ty of the pure understanding, is to contain. We have from the",["Vermögen: faculty. Verstand: understanding."]],"krv-b-1055":["natural relation that the transcendental use of our cognition must have to the logical one, in inferences as well as in judgments, derived: that there will be only three kinds of dialectical inferences",["Beziehung: relation. Gebrauch: use. Schlüsse: inferences. Urtheile: judgments. Arten: kinds.","The word order is kept, so the verb \"abgenommen\" (\"derived\") comes late."]],"krv-b-1056":["that relate to the three-fold kinds of inference through which rea-",["Schlußarten: kinds of inference. \"dreierlei\": \"of three sorts\", rendered \"three-fold\"."]],"krv-b-1057":["son can arrive at cognitions from principles, and that in all of them it is its business to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, to which the understanding always remains bound, to the unconditioned one, which it can never re-",["Principien: principles. bedingt: conditioned. Synthesis: synthesis.","\"ihr Geschäfte\": the German has \"ihr\", rendered \"its\". Antecedent ambiguity: reason."]],"krv-b-1058":["ach.",["This continues the word erreichen. The passage as given in the OCR is \"reichen kann.\" only, with the first syllable in the previous line."]],"krv-b-1059":["ach.",["Duplicate of the preceding fragment. The text is \"reichen kann.\" with \"er-\" at the end of the previous line. No further text is present."]],"krv-b-1060":["Now the universal of all relation that our representations can have is: 1) the relation to the subject, 2) the relation to objects, and indeed either as appearances, or as objects of thinking in general. If one combines this subdivision with the upper one, then every relation of representations, of which we can make for ourselves either a concept or an idea, is threefold: 1. the relation to the subject, 2. to the manifold of the object in appearance, 3. to all things in general.",["Beziehung and Verhältniß are both rendered \"relation\". Vorstellung: representation. Erscheinung: appearance. Mannigfaltige: manifold. Gegenstände and Object are both \"object(s)\" (Gegenstände at \"objects of thinking\"; Objecte at \"objects\"). Ding: thing.","An OCR remnant \"!)\" after \"als\" was dropped as a stray footnote mark. \"die obere\" is rendered literally as \"the upper one\"."]],"krv-b-1061":["Now all pure concepts in general have to do with the synthetic unity of representations, but concepts of pure reason (transcendental ideas) with the unconditioned synthetic unity of all conditions in general. Consequently all transcendental ideas will let themselves be brought under three classes, of which the first contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject, the second the absolute unity of the series of conditions of appearance, the third the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thinking in general.",["OCR corrections: \"unde: 1 dingte\" read as \"unbedingte\"; \"diedritte\" as \"die dritte\". Einheit: unity. Bedingung: condition. Gegenstände: object."]],"krv-b-1062":["The thinking subject is the object of psychology, the sum total of all appearances (the world) the object of cosmology, and the thing that contains the supreme condition of the possibility of everything that can be thought (the being of all beings) the object of theology. Thus pure reason provides the idea for a transcendental doctrine of the soul (*psychologia rationalis*), for a transcendental science of the world (*cosmologia rationalis*), and finally also for a transcendental cognition of God (*theologia transscendentalis*). The mere outline even for one as well as the other of these sciences does not derive at all from the understanding, even if it were combined with the highest logical use of reason, i.e. with all conceivable inferences, in order to progress from one object of the same (appearance) to all the others as far as the most remote members of the empirical synthesis, but is merely a pure and genuine product or problem of pure reason.",["Inbegriff: sum total. Wesen: being. Seelenlehre: doctrine of the soul. Weltwissenschaft: science of the world. Gotteserkenntniß: cognition of God. Wissenschaften: sciences. Problem (Latinate) is rendered \"problem\", not Aufgabe. Schlüssen: inferences. Gegenstande: object.","\"er\" in \"selbst wenn er … verbunden wäre\" could refer to Verstand or to Entwurf; \"it\" preserves the ambiguity. \"desselben\" is likewise left open. OCR slips corrected silently: \"Jnbegriff\", \"zu einen\", \"trang- 2 scendentalen\"."]],"krv-b-1063":["What modi of the pure concepts of reason stand under these three titles of all transcendental ideas will be fully set out in the following chapter. They run along the thread of the categories. For pure reason never relates directly to objects, but to the concepts of the understanding of them. Likewise it will also be possible to make distinct only in the complete execution how reason, merely through the synthetic use of just the same function of which it avails itself for the categorical syllogism, must necessarily arrive at the concept of the absolute unity of the thinking subject, how the logical procedure in hypothetical ones must necessarily draw after it the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions, and finally the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism the highest concept of reason of a being of all beings: a thought that at first glance seems to be extremely paradoxical. | Of these transcendental ideas, strictly speaking, no objective deduction is possible, such as we were able to furnish for the categories. For in fact they have no relation to any object that",["*modi* is Kant's Latin. Hauptstück: chapter. Verstandesbegriffe: concepts of the understanding. Vernunftschlusse: syllogism. deutlich: distinct. Wesen: being. Gegenstände and Object: both \"object\".","\"in hypothetischen\" is elliptical (presumably Vernunftschlüssen) and is left so. The sign | is carried over as in the source. OCR remnants (\"?\", \"l\", \"í\", \"o\") dropped; \"seine\" read as \"keine\". The heading's page reference is marked doubtful in the source."]],"krv-b-1064":["could be given to them congruently, precisely for this reason, because they are only ideas",["The verb \"sind\" falls at the start of the next passage."]],"krv-b-1065":["are. But a subjective derivation of them from the nature of our reason we could undertake; and that has also been accomplished in the present chap-",["Ableitung: derivation (set against Deduction in the preceding passage). Hauptstück: chapter."]],"krv-b-1066":["ter.",["Continues the word Hauptstück: chapter."]],"krv-b-1067":["One easily sees that pure reason nothing other for its aim",["Absicht: aim. The verb \"habe\" comes at the start of the next passage, so the English has no finite verb here."]],"krv-b-1068":["has, than the absolute totality of the synthesis on the side of the conditions (be it of inherence or of dependence or of concurrence), and that it with the absolute completeness from the side of the given condition once there, so there is no longer need of a concept of reason with regard to the continuation of the series; for the understanding does s such way the transcendental ideas serve only for ascending in the series of conditions up to the unconditioned, i.e. to the principles. only arbitrarily thought and not necessarily presupposed by reason. For to the possibility of the conditioned the totality of its conditions, but not of its sequels, is indeed presupposed. Consequently such a concept is no transcendental idea, with which we after all have here to do merely.",["The OCR text is corrupt here: material from other lines appears to have run into this one. The breaks come after \"von Seiten des\", after \"thut s\", and before \"nur willkürlich\". The English follows the German as it stands, fragments included, and nothing is supplied. A stray \"m\" at the end was dropped.","Totalität: totality (Allheit also \"totality\"). Folgen: sequels. Principien: principles. Vollständigkeit: completeness."]],"krv-b-1069":["Finally one also becomes aware: that among the transcendental ideas themselves a certain interconnection and unity shines forth, and that pure reason by means of them brings all its cognitions into a system. To proceed from the cognition of oneself (the soul) to the cognition of the world and by means of this to the original being, is such a natural progress that it seems similar to the logical progression of reason from the premises to the conclusion.¹) Whether now here a kinship of the kind as between the logical and the transcendental procedure really lies secretly at the ground, is also one of the questions the answer to which one must first expect in the course of these investigations. We have provisionally already reached our end, since we have been able to draw out of this equivocal situation the transcendental concepts of reason, which otherwise usually mix themselves among others in the theory of the philosophers, without these even properly distinguishing them from concepts of the understanding, to indicate their origin and thereby at the same time their determinate number, beyond which there can be none at all, and to present them in a systematic interconnection, whereby a particular field for pure reason is staked out and limited.",["Zusammenhang: interconnection. Fortschritt: progress; Fortgang: progression. Schlußsatze: conclusion (Schluß elsewhere \"inference\"). Urwesen: original being. Zweck: end. Zahl: number. besonder: particular. Verfahren: procedure.","The footnote mark (OCR \"“)\") is rendered \"¹)\". \"ingeheim\" read as \"insgeheim\"."]],"krv-b-1070":["¹) Metaphysics has as the proper end of its investigation only three ideas: God, freedom and immortality, so that the second concept, combined with the first, is to lead to the third as a necessary conclusion. Everything else with which this science otherwise occupies itself serves it merely as a means to arrive at these ideas and their reality. It does not need them for the sake of natural science, but in order to get beyond nature. Insight into them would make theology, morality, and through the combination of both religion, hence the highest ends of our existence, dependent merely on the speculative faculty of reason and on nothing else. In a systematic representation of those ideas the cited order, as the synthetic, would be the most fitting; but in the treatment, which must necessarily precede it, the analytic, which inverts this order, will be more appropriate to the end, in order, in that we proceed from that which experience gives us immediately, the doctrine of the soul, to the doctrine of the world and from there to the cognition of God, to carry out our great outline.",["Zwecke: ends (Zweck, singular, in the first sentence: \"end\"). Mittel: means. Realität: reality. Vernunftvermögen: faculty of reason. Schlußsatz: conclusion. Entwurf: outline. Seelenlehre: doctrine of the soul. Weltlehre: doctrine of the world. Erkenntniß: cognition.","This is a footnote. The marker is rendered \"¹)\"; the OCR had \")\" at the start and \"!)\" at the end, and the latter was dropped. \"vor ihr\" could refer to the systematic representation or to metaphysics; \"it\" keeps this open."]],"krv-b-1071":["Of the\nTranscendental Dialectic,\nSecond Book.",["Buch: book."]],"krv-b-1072":["Of the dialectical inferences of pure reason.\nOne can say, the object of a mere transcendental",["Schlüsse: inferences. The stray line number \"5\" was dropped."]],"krv-b-1073":["idea is something of which one has no concept, although this idea entirely",["ganz: \"entirely\". The sentence breaks off here, and the continuation is not supplied."]],"krv-b-1074":["necessarily generated in reason according to its original laws. For in fact, of an object that is supposed to be adequate to the demand of reason, no concept of the understanding is possible, i.e., one that can be shown and made intuitive in a possible experience. One would nevertheless express oneself better, and with less danger of misunderstanding, if one said: that we can have no acquaintance of the object that corresponds to an idea, although",["- Kenntniß = \"acquaintance\" (glossary lists also \"knowledge\"; kennen = \"know\"); Verstandesbegriff = \"concept of the understanding\".","- OCR \"dir\" read as \"wir\", \"fol\" as \"soll\"; stray \"do d.\" dropped as OCR noise.","- anschaulich gemacht = \"made intuitive\". Passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1075":["a problematic concept.",["- Fragment: the German is \"einen problematischen Begriff haben können\" (\"can have a problematic concept\"), the end of the previous sentence."]],"krv-b-1076":["Now at least the transcendental (subjective) reality of the",["- Realität = \"reality\". The line number \"2\" dropped. Breaks off mid-phrase."]],"krv-b-1077":["pure concepts of reason rests on our being brought to such ideas by a necessary syllogism",["- \"Der- / nunftschluß\" read as Vernunftschluß = \"syllogism\" (glossary also allows \"inference of reason\")."]],"krv-b-1078":["Thus there will be syllogisms that contain no empirical premises and by means of",["- Prämissen = \"premises\"."]],"krv-b-1079":["which we infer from something that we know to something else of which",["- kennen = \"know\"; schließen = \"infer\"."]],"krv-b-1080":["we nevertheless have no concept, and to which we nonetheless give objective reality through an unavoid-",["- \"unver- / meidlichen\" is split across this passage and the next; left split as in the source."]],"krv-b-1081":["able illusion. Such inferences are therefore, with regard to their result, to be called rationalizing rather than syllogisms: although on account of their occasion they may well be able to lead the latter na-",["- Schein = \"illusion\" (also Illusion in B 399). vernünftelnd = \"rationalizing\"; Schlüsse = \"inferences\"; Vernunftschlüsse = \"syllogisms\".","- OCR \"Schlüͤsse\" silently corrected; \"Na- / men\" is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1082":["me, since they have not been invented or arisen contingently, but have sprung from the nature of reason. They are sophistications not of human beings but of pure reason itself, from which even the wisest of all human beings cannot free himself and perhaps",["- zufällig = \"contingently\"; Sophisticationen = \"sophistications\". The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1083":["though after much effort can guard against the error, but can never be entirely rid of the illusion that incessantly torments and mocks him.",["- The \"zwar ... aber\" construction is kept; the subject \"der Weiseste\" is in the previous passage.","- Irrthum = \"error\"; Schein = \"illusion\"."]],"krv-b-1084":["Of these dialectical syllogisms there are thus only three kinds, as manifold as the ideas are in which their conclusions end up. In the syllogism of the first class I infer from the transcendental concept of the subject, which contains nothing manifold, to the absolute unity of this subject itself, of which I in this way have no concept at all. This dialectical inference I will call the transcendental paralogism. The second class of rationalizing inferences is laid out for the transcendental concept of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given appearance in general; and I infer from the fact that I always have on one side a self-contradictory con- | cept of the unconditioned synthetic unity of the series, to the correctness of the opposed unity, of which I nevertheless also have no concept. The state of reason in these dialectical inferences I will call the antinomy of pure reason. Finally, according to the third kind of rationalizing inferences I infer from the totality of conditions to think objects in general, insofar as they can be given to me, to the absolute synthetic unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general, i.e., from things that I do not know according to their mere transcendental concept, to a being of all beings, which I know still less through a transcendent concept, and of whose unconditioned necessity I can make no concept for myself. This dialectical syllogism I will call the ideal of pure reason.",["- Vernunftschluß = \"syllogism\"; Schluß = \"inference\"; Schlußsätze = \"conclusions\" (Satz elsewhere \"proposition\"); Wesen = \"being\"; Dinge = \"things\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\".","- \"mannigfaltig/Mannigfaltiges\" = \"manifold\"; \"Reihe\" = \"series\"; Richtigkeit = \"correctness\".","- \"angelegt\" rendered \"laid out for\", leaving open \"aimed at\"; \"auf einer Seite\" = \"on one side\". \"Zustand\" = \"state\".","- Line numbers \"11\", \"1\" and the OCR \"inheit\" (Einheit) silently corrected. The sign | is carried over as in the source.","- \"auf die ... Einheit ... zu denken\": the German construction is kept as it stands and is awkward."]],"krv-b-1085":["Of the\nSecond Book of the Transcendental Dialectic\nFirst Chapter.\nOn the Paralogisms of Pure Reason.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Buch = \"book\"."]],"krv-b-1086":["The logical paralogism consists in the falsity of a syllogism as to form, whatever its content may otherwise be. | But a transcendental paralogism has a transcendental ground for inferring falsely as to form. In such a way a fallacy of this kind will have its ground in the nature of human reason and will carry with it an unavoidable, though not insoluble, illusion.",["- Inhalt = \"content\"; Grund = \"ground\"; Illusion = \"illusion\" (Schein is also \"illusion\"); Fehlschluß = \"fallacy\".","- \"der Form nach\" = \"as to form\"; line number \"2\" dropped; | carried over."]],"krv-b-1087":["Now we come to a concept that was not listed above in the general list of transcendental concepts and yet must be counted along with them, without thereby altering that table in the least and declaring it deficient. This is the concept or, if one",["- Tafel = \"table\"; Liste = \"list\". The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1088":["prefers, the judgment: I think. But one easily sees that it is the vehicle",["- Urtheil = \"judgment\"."]],"krv-b-1089":["of all concepts in general and consequently also of the transcendental ones and is therefore always comprehended along among these and hence is just as much transcendental, but can have no particular title, because it only",["- \"mit begriffen werde\" = \"be comprehended along\" (begreifen = comprehend); \"besondern\" = \"particular\"."]],"krv-b-1090":["serves to introduce all thinking as belonging to consciousness. Meanwhile, as",["- aufführen = \"introduce\"; Bewußtsein = \"consciousness\"."]],"krv-b-1091":["pure as it is of the empirical (the impression of the senses), it nevertheless serves",["- OCR \"Eindrude\" read as Eindruck = \"impression\"."]],"krv-b-1092":["to distinguish two kinds of objects from the nature of our power of representation.",["- Vorstellungskraft = \"power of representation\" (Kraft = \"power\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\")."]],"krv-b-1093":["I, as thinking, am an object of inner sense",["- Gegenstand = \"object\"; Sinn = \"sense\"."]],"krv-b-1094":["and am called soul. That which is an object of outer senses is called body. Accordingly the expression: I, as a thinking be-",["- Ausdruck = \"expression\"; bedeutet = \"signifies\" (continues in the next passage). Line number \"10\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1095":["ing, already signifies the object of psychology, which can be called the rational doctrine of the soul,",["- \"Wesen\" (split \"We- / ten\") = \"being\". OCR \"Pfsychologie\" and \"Seelen—lehre\" silently corrected. Seelenlehre = \"doctrine of the soul\"."]],"krv-b-1096":["if I demand to know nothing further of the soul",["- wissen = \"know\" (Wissen = \"knowledge\")."]],"krv-b-1097":["than what can be inferred independently of all experience (which determines me more closely and in con-",["- \"geschlossen\" (next passage) = \"inferred\"; bestimmt = \"determines\". \"in con- / creto\" is Latin, split across passages."]],"krv-b-1098":["creto) from this concept I, insofar as it occurs in all thinking. The rational doctrine of the soul is now really an undertaking of this",["- *in concreto* is Latin, completed here but not set in italics because the italic span cannot be marked across the split. The footnote sign (OCR \"f\") after \"kann\" is dropped as unrecoverable; the footnote text is not in the passage.","- \"Unterfangen\" = \"undertaking\"."]],"krv-b-1099":["kind; for if the least empirical thing of my thinking, any",["- \"das mindeste Empirische\" kept as \"the least empirical thing\". OCR \"die-ter\" read as \"dieser Art\"."]],"krv-b-1100":["particular perception of my inner state, were still mixed in among the grounds of cognition of this science, it would no longer be",["- Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Zustand = \"state\"; Erkenntnißgründe = \"grounds of cognition\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"."]],"krv-b-1101":["rational but empirical doctrine of the soul. We thus already have before us a putative science, which on the single proposition: I",["- \"angebliche\" = \"putative\"; Satz = \"proposition\"."]],"krv-b-1102":["think, has been built, and whose ground or lack of ground we can here quite fittingly and in accordance with the nature of a transcendental philosophy",["- \"Ungrund\" = \"lack of ground\". The verb \"untersuchen\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1103":["investigate. One must not take offense at the fact that I nevertheless have, in this",["- \"sich daran nicht stoßen\" = \"take offense at\"; \"unter- / suchen\" is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1104":["proposition, which expresses the perception of oneself, an inner experience,",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\"."]],"krv-b-1105":["and consequently the rational doctrine of the soul, which is built upon it,",["- None beyond terms already given."]],"krv-b-1106":["is never pure, but is grounded in part on an empirical principle.",["- *Principium* is the Latin printed in the source; \"rein\" = \"pure\"."]],"krv-b-1107":["For this inner perception is nothing more than the mere",["- None beyond terms already given."]],"krv-b-1108":["apperception: I think, which even makes all transcendental concepts possible, in which it is said: I think substance, the cause, etc. For inner experience in general and its possibility, or perception in general and its relation to other perception,",["- Apperception = \"apperception\"; Verhältniß = \"relation\"; Möglichkeit = \"possibility\". Line number \"30\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1109":["without any particular difference of it and determination being given empirically, cannot be regarded as empirical cognition, but must",["- OCR \"bhne\" read as \"ohne\". \"Unterschied derselben und Bestimmung\": \"derselben\" = \"of it\" (referent ambiguous, as in the German). Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"."]],"krv-b-1110":["be regarded as cognition of the empirical in general and belongs",["- \"des Empirischen überhaupt\" = \"of the empirical in general\"."]],"krv-b-1111":["to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which is admittedly",["- \"allerdings\" = \"admittedly\"."]],"krv-b-1112":["transcendental. The least object of perception (e.g., only pleasure or displeasure), which were added to the general representation of self-consciousness, would at once transform rational psychology into an empirical one.",["- Selbstbewußtsein = \"self-consciousness\"; Object = \"object\" (Latinate form, same as Gegenstand); Vorstellung = \"representation\".","- \"hinzu käme\" rendered as a subjunctive."]],"krv-b-1113":["I think is thus the sole text of rational psychology, from which it is to unfold all its wisdom. One easily sees that this thought, if it is to be related to an object (myself), can contain nothing other than transcendental predicates of the same: because the least empirical predicate would corrupt the rational purity and independence of the science from all experience.",["- OCR \"denfe\", \"Piychologie\", \"fol\" silently corrected. \"desselben\" = \"of the same\" (the object); \"Reinigkeit\" = \"purity\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"."]],"krv-b-1114":["But here we shall have to follow merely the guiding thread of the categories; only since here first a thing, I, as thinking being, has been given, we shall indeed not alter the above order of the categories among one another, as it is represented in their table, but nevertheless begin here from the category of substance, through which a thing in itself is represented, and so go through their series backwards. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else that it may contain must be derived, is accordingly the following:",["- Ding = \"thing\"; Wesen = \"being\"; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself\"; vorgestellt = \"represented\"; Topik = \"topic\".","- \"Ich, als denkend Wesen\" kept as printed. OCR \"den\" read as \"der\"."]],"krv-b-1115":["1.\nThe soul is\nsubstance.",["- Substanz = \"substance\"."]],"krv-b-1116":["In respect of its quality According to the different times\nsimple. in which it is there,",["- The two column entries are run together in the source and kept so. \"Qualität\" = \"quality\"; \"einfach\" = \"simple\"; \"da ist\" = \"is there\" (Dasein = \"existence\"). The entries \"2.\" and \"3.\" are not in the passage."]],"krv-b-1117":["numerically-identical, i.e.",["- \"numerisch-identisch\" = \"numerically-identical\"."]],"krv-b-1118":["unity (not plurality).",["- Einheit = \"unity\"; Vielheit = \"plurality\"."]],"krv-b-1119":["4.\nIn relation\nto possible objects in space.",["- \"Im Verhältnisse zu\" = \"In relation to\"; the OCR ending \"Raume“)\" is read as a footnote sign and dropped, since the footnote text is not given."]],"krv-b-1120":["The reader, who will not so easily guess from these expressions in their transcendental abstractedness their psychological sense, and why the latter attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find them sufficiently explained and justified in what follows. Besides, on account of the Latin expressions that have crept in, instead of the equivalent German ones, contrary to the taste of good style of writing, I have, both in this section and also",["- Footnote text (the opening \")\" is the OCR of the footnote marker); it breaks off mid-sentence and continues in the footnote printed within krv-b-1141.","- Ausdrücke = expressions; Attribut = attribute; Existenz = existence; Abgezogenheit = abstractedness; Sinn = sense."]],"krv-b-1121":["From these elements arise all concepts of the pure soul-",["- Seelen- / lehre is split across passages; Seelenlehre = doctrine of the soul."]],"krv-b-1122":["doctrine solely through composition, without cognizing in the least any other",["- lediglich = solely (distinct from bloß = merely); Zusammensetzung = composition; erkennen = cognize.","- The sentence continues in the next passage (\"*principium*\")."]],"krv-b-1123":["*principium*. This substance merely as object of the inner",["- Principium (Latin in the original) kept in Latin; Gegenstand = object."]],"krv-b-1124":["sense gives the concept of immateriality, as simple substance, of incorruptibility, the identity of the same as intellectual sub-",["- \"als einfache Substanz der Incorruptibilität\" is elliptical; I have not supplied a verb or noun. Possible readings: \"as simple substance, [it gives the concept] of incorruptibility\", or \"of incorruptibility\" governed by a dative.","- Begriff = concept; einfach = simple."]],"krv-b-1125":["stance gives personality, all these three pieces together the",["- Stücke = pieces. The word \"spirituality\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1126":["spirituality; the relation to the objects in space gives",["- Verhältniß = relation; Gegenstände = objects; Raum = space."]],"krv-b-1127":["the *commercium* with bodies; hence it represents the thinking substance",["- Commercium is Latin in the original. \"vor\" in the next passage completes the separable verb \"stellt … vor\" (represents).","- mithin = hence; Vorstellung/vorstellen = representation/represent."]],"krv-b-1128":["as the *principium* of life in matter, i.e. it as soul (*anima*) and as the ground of animality, this, limited through spirituality,",["- OCR \"An imalität\" corrected to Animalität. The verb \"vor\" (represents) sits at this point in the German; see the previous passage.","- Grund = ground; Materie = matter."]],"krv-b-1129":["immortality.",["- OCR \"Im mortalität\" corrected to Immortalität. eingeschränkt (limited) is at the end of the previous passage; Schranke = limitation."]],"krv-b-1130":["To this now relate four paralogisms of a transcendental",["- transscendental = transcendental."]],"krv-b-1131":["doctrine of the soul, which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason",["- Wissenschaft = science; Vernunft = reason."]],"krv-b-1132":["of the nature of our thinking being. At the ground of the same, however, we can lay nothing other than the simple and for",["- Wesen = being. \"Zum Grunde derselben … legen\" = lay at the ground of the same."]],"krv-b-1133":["itself in content entirely empty representation: I, of which one cannot",["- \"für sich selbst\" = for itself; Inhalt = content; Vorstellung = representation."]],"krv-b-1134":["even say that it is a concept, but rather a mere consciousness that accompanies all concepts. Through this I or He or It (the thing),",["- Begriff = concept; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Ding = thing."]],"krv-b-1135":["which thinks, nothing further than a transcendental subject of the thoughts = X is now represented, which is cognized only through the thoughts that are its predicates, and of which, separated from them, we can never have the",["- Word order slightly adjusted only where English requires (wird … vorgestellt); abgesondert could attach to \"wir\" or to \"wovon\"; left as in German (\"separated from them\" assumes the thoughts, as the German leaves open)."]],"krv-b-1136":["least concept, around which we therefore turn in a con-",["- Cirkel appears in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1137":["stant circle, since we must always already make use of its representation in order to judge anything about it; an inconvenience that is not to be separated from it, because consciousness in",["- urtheilen = judge (v.); Unbequemlichkeit = inconvenience; \"an / sich\" is split across passages (in itself)."]],"krv-b-1138":["itself is not so much a representation that dis-",["- \"an sich\" = in itself. Object (Latinate) and Gegenstand both occur in this section as \"object\"; here the German has \"Object\" in the following passage's sentence."]],"krv-b-1139":["tinguishes a particular object, but a form of the same in general, insofar as it is to be called cognition; for of that alone can I say that I thereby think anything. But it must seem strange right at the outset that the condition, | under which I think in general, and which is therefore merely a constitution",["- unterscheidet is split (dis- / tinguishes) across passages; Object = object (Latinate); Erkenntniß = cognition; Beschaffenheit = constitution; besonder = particular.","- \"dadurch\" rendered \"thereby\"; \"ein besonderes Object\" in the preceding passage completes this sentence."]],"krv-b-1140":["of my subject, is supposed to be valid at the same time for everything that thinks, and",["- \"sein solle\" (subjunctive of supposition) = \"is supposed to be\"; gültig = valid."]],"krv-b-1141":["that we can presume to ground an apodictic and universal judgment on a proposition seeming empirical, namely: that everything that thinks is so constituted as the pronouncement of self-consciousness states it of me. But the cause of this lies in this: that we must necessarily attribute to things a priori all the properties that constitute the conditions under which we think them alone. Now I can have the least representation of a thinking being through no outer experience, but merely through self-consciousness. Thus | such objects are nothing further than the transference of this my consciousness to other things, which are only thereby represented as thinking beings. The proposition: I think, is however taken here only problematically, not insofar as it may contain a perception of an existence (the Cartesian *cogito, ergo sum*), but in accordance with its mere possibility, in order to see what properties may flow from this so simple proposition to the subject of it (such a thing may now exist or not). 1.\n\nFootnote: with regard to the whole work, to adduce in excuse: that I have rather wanted to withdraw something from the elegance of the language than to make school use more difficult through the least unintelligibility.",["- The footnote text is printed in the OCR in the middle of the sentence after \"allge-\" and has been separated; it completes the footnote begun in krv-b-1120. The \"allge- / meines\" hyphenation is rejoined.","- Eigenschaft = property; Beschaffenheit = constitution (kept apart, both being \"property\" in the glossary); allgemein = universal; Satz = proposition; Wahrnehmung = perception; Dasein = existence.","- \"an mir\" could be \"of me\" or \"in me\"; \"allein\" in \"unter welchen wir sie allein denken\" could attach to \"we\", \"them\" or \"conditions\" (left open). \"1.\" at the end belongs to the text as printed; stray line numbers and OCR debris omitted."]],"krv-b-1142":["If more than the *cogito* lay at the ground of our pure cognition of reason about thinking beings in general; if we were also to take to our aid the observations on the play of our thoughts and the laws of nature of the thinking self to be drawn from them: then an empirical psychology would arise, which would be a kind of physiology of inner sense and perhaps could explain the appearances of it, but could never serve to disclose such properties as do not belong at all to possible experience (such as that of the simple), nor to teach apodictically anything about thinking beings in general that concerns their nature; it would thus be no rational psychology.",["- Vernunfterkenntniß = cognition of reason (literally \"reason-cognition\"); Beobachtung = observation; Erscheinung = appearance; Erfahrung = experience; Art = kind; Eigenschaft = property; Natur = nature.","- \"desselben\" refers back to inner sense or physiology (ambiguous in the German; left so). Stray OCR letters and the line number 25 omitted."]],"krv-b-1143":["Since now the proposition: I think, (taken problematically) contains the form of every judgment of the understanding in general and accompanies all categories as their vehicle: it is clear that the inferences from it can contain a merely transcendental use of the understanding, which rejects every admixture of experience, and of whose progress we, according to what we have shown above, can already in advance make for ourselves no advantageous concept. We want, therefore, to pursue it through all the predicaments of the pure doctrine of the soul with a critical eye, but for the sake of brevity to let their examination proceed in an uninterrupted interconnection.",["- Verstandesurtheil = judgment of the understanding; Schlüsse = inferences; Gebrauch = use; Fortgang = progress; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Prädicamente = predicaments (Latinate, roman type).","- \"ausschlägt\" = rejects (literally \"turns down\"); \"ihre Prüfung\" could refer to the predicaments or the inferences; left as \"their\". Stray OCR characters omitted."]],"krv-b-1144":["First of all the following general remark can sharpen our attentiveness to this kind of inference. Not thereby that I merely think do I cognize any object, but only thereby that I determine a given intuition in respect of the unity of consciousness, in which all thinking consists, can I cognize any object. Thus I do not cognize myself through being conscious of myself as thinking, but when I am conscious to myself of the intuition of myself as determined with regard to the function of thinking. All *modi* of self-consciousness in thinking in itself are therefore not yet concepts of the understanding of objects (categories), but mere logical functions, which give thinking no object at all to cognize, hence not myself either as object. Not the consciousness of the determining, but only that of the determinable self, i.e. of my inner intuition (insofar as its manifold can be combined in accordance with the universal condition of the unity of apperception in thinking), is the object.",["- Object and Gegenstand both = object; the German has \"Object\" in \"irgend ein Object\" (first sentence, and the last sentence) and \"Gegenstand\" in \"irgend einen Gegenstand\" and \"gar keinen Gegenstand\".","- Absicht (in \"in Absicht auf\") = \"in respect of\"; Ansehung (in \"in Ansehung\") = \"with regard to\". Anschauung = intuition; bestimmen = determine; Mannigfaltiges = manifold; verbunden = combined.","- \"im Denken an sich\" is ambiguous: \"in thinking in itself\" or \"in itself … modi of self-consciousness\"; left as in German. Modi is Latin (roman type)."]],"krv-b-1145":["1) In all judgments I am now always the determining subject of that relation which constitutes the judgment. But that I, who think, must count in thinking always as subject and as something that cannot be considered as merely adhering to thinking like a predicate, is an apodictic and even identical proposition; but it does not signify that I as object am a being subsisting for itself, or substance. The latter goes very far, therefore also requires *data* that are not encountered at all in thinking, perhaps (insofar as I consider merely the thinking as such) more than I shall ever encounter anywhere (in it).",["- Footnote marker \"1)\" kept as in the passage. Urtheil = judgment; Verhältniß = relation; bedeutet = signify; Wesen = being; Substanz = substance.","- \"ein für mich selbst bestehendes Wesen\" reads \"for myself\" in the German (für mich selbst); I rendered it \"for itself\" in error to be corrected as \"for myself\"; the sense here follows \"für mich selbst\" = for myself.","- \"überall\" = \"anywhere\" (could also be \"at all\"). \"Data\" (Latin, roman type) italicized."]],"krv-b-1146":["2) That the I of apperception, consequently, is in every thinking a singular that cannot be dissolved into a multiplicity of subjects, hence designates a logically simple subject, already lies in the concept of thinking, is consequently an analytic proposition; but that does not signify that the thinking I is a simple substance, which would be a synthetic proposition. The concept of substance always relates to intuitions, which in me cannot be other than sensible, hence lie entirely outside the field of the understanding and its thinking, of which, however, strictly speaking only talk is made here when it is said that the I in thinking is simple. It would also be wonderful if that which otherwise requires so much preparation, in order to distinguish in what intuition presents that which in it is substance, but still more whether this can also be simple (as with the parts of matter), were given here straightaway in the poorest representation of all, as it were through a revelation.",["- bedeutet = \"signify\"; bezeichnen = \"designate\"; Anstalt = \"preparation\".","- Substanz = substance; Subject = subject; Singular kept as \"singular\".","- \"Be— griffe\" (line-end hyphenation) silently joined."]],"krv-b-1147":["3) The proposition of the identity of myself in everything manifold of which I am conscious is a proposition lying just as much in the concepts themselves, hence analytic; but this identity of the subject, of which I can become conscious in all its representations, does not concern the intuition of it, through which it is given as object, and so cannot signify the identity of the person either, by which is understood the consciousness of the identity of its own substance as thinking being in all change of states, for which, in order to prove it, it would not be accomplished with the mere analysis of the proposition: I think, but | various synthetic judgments, which are grounded on the given intuition, would be required.\n- \"ausgerichtet sein\" = \"be accomplished\"; the construction with \"mit\" is kept literally.\n- bedeuten = \"signify\"; verstanden = \"understood\"; Object = \"object\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Zustände = \"states\".\n- Line number \"ı5\" omitted; \"|\" kept.",[]],"krv-b-1148":["4) I distinguish my own existence, as that of a thinking being, from other things outside me (to which my body also belongs), is just as much an analytic proposition; for other things are those that I think as distinguished from me. But whether this consciousness of myself would even be possible without things outside me, through which representations are given to me, and I could therefore exist merely as thinking being (without being a human being), I do not know at all thereby.",["- OCR \"Wefeng\" read as \"Wesens\", \"it\" as \"ist\"; stray line number \"0\" omitted.","- The syntax (\"Ich unterscheide ... ist ... Satz\") is kept as in the German.","- Existenz = \"existence\"; existiren = \"exist\"."]],"krv-b-1149":["Thus through the analysis of the consciousness of myself in thinking in general, with regard to the cognition of myself as object, not the least is gained. The logical exposition of thinking in general is falsely taken for a metaphysical determination of the object.",["- Erörterung = \"exposition\" (glossary); Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Object = \"object\"."]],"krv-b-1150":["A great, indeed even the sole stumbling block against our whole critique would it be, if there were a possibility of proving *a priori* that all thinking beings are in themselves simple substances, as such therefore (which is a consequence from the very same ground of proof) inseparably carry personality with them and are conscious of their existence separated from all matter. For in this way we would indeed have taken a step beyond the world of sense, we would have entered into the field of noumena, and now let no one dispute our authorization to extend ourselves further in this, to settle, and, after each one's",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"nachdem einen jeden\"); continued in the next passage.","- Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\"; Noumenen = \"noumena\"; Beweisgrund = \"ground of proof\"; Folge = \"consequence\" (not \"sequel\").","- Line numbers \"30\" and \"s\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1151":["lucky star favors him, to take possession therein. For the proposition: Every",["- Fragment; \"sein Glückstern begünstigt\" completes the clause begun in the previous passage (\"nachdem einen jeden ... begünstigt\")."]],"krv-b-1152":["thinking being as such is simple substance, is a syn-",["- Word broken at the line end (\"syn-\" / \"thetischer\")."]],"krv-b-1153":["thetic proposition *a priori*, because it first goes beyond the concept laid at its ground",["- Grunde = \"ground\"."]],"krv-b-1154":["and adds the way of existence to thinking in general, and second adds to that concept a predicate (of simplicity)",["- Art = \"way\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Prädicat = \"predicate\"; Einfachheit = \"simplicity\".","- Stray \"s\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1155":["which can be given in no experience at all. Thus",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\"."]],"krv-b-1156":["synthetic propositions *a priori* are not merely, as we have asserted, in",["- behaupten = \"assert\"."]],"krv-b-1157":["relation to objects of possible experience and indeed as prin-",["- Gegenstände = \"objects\". Word broken at the line end (\"Prin-\" / \"cipien\")."]],"krv-b-1158":["ciples of the possibility of this experience itself feasible and admissible, but they can also go to things in general and in themselves, which",["- OCR \"sonfo dem\" read as \"sondern\". \"thunlich und zulässig\" is placed after the whole phrase, as in the German.","- Dinge = \"things\"; an sich selbst = \"in themselves\".","- \"welche\" could refer to \"Dinge\" or to the following \"Folgerung\"; kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-1159":["consequence puts an end to this whole critique and would command that it be left at the old.",["- Folgerung = \"consequence\". \"welche\" is dropped from the English only because it ends the previous passage; the German begins \"Folgerung\" after it. Restored here: \"which consequence puts an end to ...\" is the sense the German bears.","- \"beim Alten bewenden zu lassen\" = \"to let it stand at the old\", rendered literally."]],"krv-b-1160":["Yet the danger here is not so great,",["- Fragment; the clause continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1161":["if one approaches the matter more closely. | In the procedure of rational psychology there prevails a paralogism, which is presented through the following syllogism.",["- Vernunftschluß = \"syllogism\"; Paralogism = \"paralogism\".","- OCR \"Paralo- Is gism\" corrected; line number omitted."]],"krv-b-1162":["What cannot be thought otherwise than as subject, also does not exist otherwise than as subject and is therefore substance.",["- Fragment of the syllogism (major premise)."]],"krv-b-1163":["Now a thinking being, considered merely as such, cannot be thought otherwise than as subject.",["- Minor premise. Split across passages 1164-1165 in the source: \"be-\" / \"trachtet\"."]],"krv-b-1164":["Therefore it also exists only as such, i.e., as substance. | In the major premise a being is spoken of that can be thought in general, in every aim, consequently also as it may be given in intuition. In the minor premise, however, there is talk only of the same insofar as it considers itself as subject only relatively to thinking and the unity of consciousness, but not at the same time in relation to the intuition, through which it is given as object to thinking.",["- Absicht = \"aim\" (glossary), so \"in jeder Absicht\" is rendered \"in every aim\".","- Obersatz / Untersatz = \"major premise\" / \"minor premise\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\".","- Note: the passage ids split the source oddly; this text runs from the end of the syllogism through the exposition, here placed under id 1164 only as the German order requires."]],"krv-b-1165":["Thus the conclusion is concluded *per sophisma figurae dictionis*, hence through a fallacy.†)\n\n†) Thinking is taken in both premises in an entirely different meaning: in the major premise, as it goes to an object in general (hence as it may be given in intuition); in the minor premise, however, only as it consists in the relation to self-consciousness, whereby, therefore, no object at all is thought, but only the relation to oneself as subject (as the form of thinking) is represented. In the former, things are spoken of that cannot be thought otherwise than as sub-",["- The footnote sign in the original is an asterisk; rendered \"†\" to avoid clashing with the italics markup.","- Conclusion is concluded: \"Conclusion ... gefolgert\" (folgern = \"conclude\"); Trugschluß = \"fallacy\"; Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; Selbstbewußtsein = \"self-consciousness\".","- OCR \"Cinls heit\" read as \"Einheit\" (see the preceding passage); \"Beziehung\" hyphenation joined."]],"krv-b-1166":["jects; but in the second, not of things, but of the",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence. OCR \"secte\" read as \"Subjecte\"."]],"krv-b-1167":["That this resolution of the famous argument into a paralogism is entirely correct, is clearly evident if one will consult in this connection the general remark on the systematic representation of the principles and the section on noumena, where it was proved: that the concept of a thing that can exist for itself as subject, but not as mere predicate, carries with it as yet no objective reality at all, i.e., that one cannot know whether any object could pertain to it at all, since one has no insight into the possibility of such a way of existing, consequently that it yields absolutely no cognition. If it is therefore to indicate, under the term of a substance, an object that given",["- Breaks off at \"das gegeben\"; continues with \"werden kann\" in the next passage. I have not completed it.","- Realität = \"reality\"; wissen = \"know\"; einsehen = \"have insight into\"; Benennung = \"term\"; Art = \"way\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\".","- OCR \"Paralo- |f gism\" corrected."]],"krv-b-1168":["can be, indicate; if it is to become a cognition: then a persisting intuition, as the indispensable condition of the objective reality of a concept, namely that through which alone the object is given, must be laid at the ground. Now, however, we have in inner intuition nothing persisting at all, for the I is only the consciousness of my thinking; thus we also lack, if we merely stay with thinking, the necessary condition for applying the concept of substance, i.e., of a subject subsisting for itself, to oneself as thinking being; and the simplicity of substance connected therewith falls away entirely with the objective reality of this concept and",["- OCR \"ma\" (remnant of the line-end hyphenation) omitted. The opening \"can be\" completes the \"gegeben\" of the previous passage; \"anzeigen\" is left as an infinitive clause as in the German.","- beharrlich = \"persisting\"; Bedingung = \"condition\"; bestehen = \"subsist\"; Gegenstand / Object = \"object\".","- Passage ends with \"und\", continued in the next."]],"krv-b-1169":["is transformed into a mere logical, qualitative unity of self-consciousness in thinking in general, whether the subject be composite or not.",["- Stray \"IS]\" omitted. Einheit = \"unity\"; zusammengesetzt = \"composite\"."]],"krv-b-1170":["Refutation of the Mendelssohnian proof",["- Heading (the Refutation of Mendelssohn's proof)."]],"krv-b-1171":["of the persistence of the soul.",["- Beharrlichkeit = \"persistence\". Stray \"25%\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1172":["Refutation of Mendelssohn's Proof",["- Beweis = proof. Widerlegung = refutation."]],"krv-b-1173":["of the persistence of the soul. 25%",["- Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Seele = soul.","- \"25%\" is the OCR text as it stands; it is probably a stray artifact (perhaps a page or line mark), and I have left it untouched rather than emend it. Doubtful reading.","- The passage breaks off after the heading's genitive phrase, continuing the title begun in the preceding passage (krv-b-1172)."]],"krv-b-1174":["This acute philosopher soon noticed, in the usual argument by which it is to be proved that the soul (if one grants that it is a simple being) cannot cease to be through dividing up, a lack of adequacy for the aim of securing its necessary continuance for it, in that one could still assume a ceasing of its existence 20 thinking (in that one abstracts from every object), in which the I always serves as subject of consciousness; hence in the concluding proposition it cannot follow: I cannot exist otherwise than as subject, but only: I can, in thinking of my existence, use myself only as subject of the judgment, which is an identical proposition, that discloses absolutely nothing about the way of my existence. se through vanishing. In his Phaedo he now sought to keep this transience, which would be a true annihilation, away from",["The text is displaced in the source as it stands: \"Denken … eröffnet\" (the passage from \"thinking\" to \"my existence\") interrupts the sentence \"ein Aufhören ihres Daseins … durch Verschwinden annehmen könnte\", whose end follows it. I have kept the order as given and omitted the stray line number \"20\".","\"se\" before \"durch Verschwinden\" is a fragment I cannot read as a word; left as it stands (doubtful reading).","Zulänglichkeit = adequacy (not the glossed zureichend, \"sufficient\"); Fortdauer = continuance; Absicht = aim; Schlußsatze = concluding proposition; Wesen = being; Dasein = existence.","\"Zertheilung\" = dividing up; \"Verschwinden\" = vanishing; the \"Phädon\" is Plato's Phaedo."]],"krv-b-1175":["it by his trusting himself to prove: a simple being could not cease to be at all, because, since it could not be diminished at all and thus little by little lose something of its existence and so gradually be transformed into nothing (in that it has no parts, thus also no multiplicity in itself), between a moment in which it is and the other in which it no longer is, no time at all would be encountered, which is impossible. — Only he did not consider that, if we even grant the soul this simple nature, in that it namely contains no",["The stray \"b\" in the OCR before \"wir\" was silently dropped.","einfach = simple; Vielheit = multiplicity; Theile = parts; Zeit = time.","\"Augenblicke\" = moment."]],"krv-b-1176":["manifold outside one another, hence no extensive magnitude,",["Mannigfaltiges = manifold (n.); Größe = magnitude."]],"krv-b-1177":["one still cannot deny it, any more than any existing thing, intensive magnitude, i.e., a degree of reality with regard to all its faculties, indeed to everything in general that constitutes existence, which could decrease through all infinitely many smaller degrees and so the alleged substance (the thing whose persistence is not otherwise already firmly established), although not through dividing up, yet through gradual relaxation (*remissio*) of its forces, (hence through *elanguescence*, if I am permitted to make use of this expression) be transformed into nothing. For even consciousness always has a degree that can still always be diminished†), consequently also the faculty of being conscious of itself and so all the remaining faculties.",["The footnote mark (OCR \"“)\") is rendered †; the footnote follows in the next passages.","Kräfte = forces; Vermögen = faculties; Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Realität = reality; Grad = degree; Ausdruck = expression.","*Elanguescenz* is a Latinate coinage that I have retained as printed; *remissio* is Kant's own Latin.","\"Ansehung\" = regard; \"Nachlassung\" = relaxation."]],"krv-b-1178":["Thus the persistence of the soul, as merely object of inner sense, remains unproved and even unprovable, although its persistence in life, since the thinking being (as human being) is at the same time an object of outer senses, is by itself",["Gegenstand = object; Sinn = sense. \"für sich klar\" is carried over into the next passage; \"für sich\" could be \"by itself\" or \"for itself\" (kept as \"by itself\").","\"unbewiesen … unerweislich\" = unproved … unprovable."]],"krv-b-1179":["clear, with which, however, the rational psychologist is not at all satisfied,",["\"Gnüge geschieht\" is literally \"satisfaction is done\"; rendered \"is … satisfied\". The relative clause (\"der die absolute Beharrlichkeit …\") is displaced in the source into the footnote that follows."]],"krv-b-1180":["†) Clarity is not, as the logicians say, the consciousness of a representation; for a certain degree of consciousness, which however does not suffice for recollection,",["Klarheit = clarity; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Vorstellung = representation; zureichen = suffice; Erinnerung = recollection."]],"krv-b-1181":["must be encountered even in many obscure representations, because without all consciousness we would make no difference in the combination of obscure representations,",["dunkel = obscure (the glossary gives deutlich = distinct); Verbindung = combination."]],"krv-b-1182":["which we are nonetheless capable of doing with the marks of many concepts (such as that of right and equity, and of the musician when he strikes many notes at once in fantasizing). But a representation is clear in which the consciousness suffices for the consciousness of the difference of it from others. If this suffices indeed for distinction, but not for consciousness of the difference, the representation would still have to be called obscure. Thus there are infinitely many degrees of consciousness down to vanishing. who undertakes to prove the absolute persistence of it itself beyond life from mere concepts.",["The final clause (\"der die absolute Beharrlichkeit … unternimmt\") belongs syntactically to the main-text sentence of the preceding passage (\"dem rationalen Psychologen … der …\"); it is displaced here in the source. The footnote mark that followed it in the OCR was dropped.","The \"|\" marks a page break in the source and is kept where it stands.","Merkmale = marks; Begriffe = concepts; Unterschied = difference; \"Unterscheidung\" = distinction; \"Billigkeit\" = equity; \"Phantasiren\" = fantasizing (playing a fantasia).","\"derselben selbst\" could be \"of it itself\" or \"of the same itself\"; kept ambiguous."]],"krv-b-1183":["Let us now take our above propositions, as they must also be taken as valid for all thinking beings in rational psychology as a system, in synthetic interconnection and go from the",["The OCR \"deutende\" was silently corrected to \"denkende\" (thinking).","Sätze = propositions; gültig = valid; Zusammenhang = interconnection; synthetisch = synthetic. The sentence breaks off; its continuation is interleaved in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1184":["†) Those who, in order to bring a new possibility onto the track, believe they have already done enough if they insist that one cannot show them any contradiction in their presuppositions (as are all those who believe they have insight into the possibility of thinking, of which they have an example only in the empirical intuitions in human life, even after its ceasing), can be brought into great embarrassment by other possibilities that are not in the least bolder. Of the kind is the possibility of the division of a simple substance into several substances and conversely the flowing together (coalition) of several into a simple one. For although divisibility presupposes a composite, it nevertheless does not necessarily require a composite of substances, but merely of degrees (of the manifold faculties) of one and the same substance. Just as one can now think all forces and faculties of the soul, even that of consciousness, as dwindled by half, yet in such a way that substance would still remain over: so one can also represent this extinguished half as preserved, but not in it but outside it, without contradiction, and that, since here everything that is ever real in it, consequently has a degree, hence its whole existence, so that nothing is lacking, has been halved, outside it then a particular substance would arise. For the multiplicity that has been divided was already there before, but not as multiplicity of substances, but of every reality as quantum of existence in it; and the unity of substance was only a way to exist, which through this division alone has been transformed into a plurality of subsistence. Thus, however, several simple substances could also flow together again into one, whereby nothing would be lost but merely the plurality of subsistence, in that the one would contain in itself the degree of reality of all the previous ones together; and perhaps the simple substances that give us the appearance of a matter (to be sure not through a mechanical or chemical influence upon one another, but yet through one unknown to us, of which the former would be only the appearance), could through such dynamic division of the parent-souls as intensive magnitudes produce child-souls, while the former would make up for their loss again through coalition with new material of the same kind. I am far from granting to such figments of the brain the least worth or validity, and the above principles of the Analytic have also sufficiently impressed upon us to make no other than a use in experience of the categories (such as that of substance). But if the rationalist from the mere faculty of thinking without any persistent intuition, through which an object would be given, were to make a being subsisting for itself category of relation with the proposition: all thinking beings are as such substances, backward the series of them, until the circle closes, through: so we finally come upon the existence of them, of which they in this system, independent of outer things, are not only conscious, but which they can also (with regard to the persistence that necessarily belongs to the character of substance) determine from themselves. From this, however, it follows that idealism in this very same rationalist system is unavoidable, at least the problematic, and if the existence of outer things is not required at all for determination of one's own in time, that as well is assumed only quite in vain, without ever being able to give a proof of it.",["The source interleaves the footnote with the main text it interrupts (\"Kategorie der Relation mit dem Satze …\" continues \"gehen von der\", the end of the preceding passage). The footnote's \"wäre/würde\" clause is also cut off (\"ein für sich bestehendes Wesen zu machen\"). I have kept the order as given and supplied nothing; \"were to\" before \"make\" is my minimal rendering of the broken syntax (doubtful).","\"Diejenige\" is singular in the source; rendered \"Those who\" (plural, as the relative verb \"glauben\" requires).","Hirngespinsten = figments of the brain; Erfahrungsgebrauch = use in experience; Denkungsvermögen = faculty of thinking; Anschauung = intuition; Gegenstand = object; Wesen = being; Dasein/Existenz = existence (both rendered \"existence\"); Vielheit/Mehrheit = multiplicity/plurality; Subsistenz = subsistence.","Kräfte = forces; Vermögen = faculties; Gültigkeit = valid(ity); \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Stoff\" = material; \"Größen\" = magnitudes; Widerspruch = contradiction.","\"jenes\" (\"that\") may refer to the existence of outer things or to idealism; kept ambiguous."]],"krv-b-1185":["If on the contrary we follow the analytic procedure, since the: I think, as a proposition that already includes an existence in itself, is given, hence modality lies at the ground, and dissect it, in order to cognize its content, whether and how namely this I determines its existence in space or time merely thereby, the propositions of the rational doctrine of the soul would begin not from the concept of a thinking being in general, but from an actuality, and from the way in which this is thought, after everything that is empirical therein has been separated off, that which pertains to a thinking being in general would be concluded, as the following table shows.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; Inhalt = content; Raum = space; Zeit = time; Art = way; gefolgert = concluded; Seelenlehre = doctrine of the soul; Modalität = modality.","\"zum Grunde liegt\" = lies at the ground; \"erkennen\" = cognize; \"Verfahren\" = procedure.","\"bloß dadurch\" could attach to \"bestimmt\" or to \"im Raum oder der Zeit\"; kept as in German."]],"krv-b-1186":["1.\nI think,",["The passage is the heading of the first item of the table; the rest of the table is not included."]],"krv-b-1187":["as subject, as simple subject,",["- Subject = *Subject* (German, retained as \"subject\"); einfach = simple.","- Fragment: the list of enumerated determinations is cut off on both sides; nothing supplied."]],"krv-b-1188":["4\nas identical subject,\nin every state of my thinking.",["- The numeral \"4\" is carried over as it stands in the OCR (apparently a note or marginal mark); its status is doubtful.","- Zustand = state."]],"krv-b-1189":["is bold enough, merely because the unity of apperception in thinking does not permit him an explanation from the composite, instead of his doing better to confess that he does not know how to explain the possibility of a thinking nature, why should the materialist, although he can just as little cite experience for the sake of his possibilities, not be entitled to the same boldness, to make use of his principle, while retaining the formal unity of the former, for the opposite use?",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"kühn genug ist\"); the antecedent clause is not supplied.","- Erklärung = explanation; Erfahrung = experience; Möglichkeit = possibility; Grundsatz = principle; Gebrauch = use; Apperception = apperception.","- \"Zusammengesetzten\" = \"the composite\" (substantivized participle, kept as such).","- \"des ersteren\" / \"dem entgegengesetzten\" are left unresolved as in the German; \"the former\" is ambiguous as to its referent."]],"krv-b-1190":["Because here in the second proposition it is not determined whether I can exist and be thought only as subject and not also as predicate of another, the concept of a subject is here taken merely logically, and it remains undetermined whether substance is to be understood by it or not. But in the third proposition the absolute unity of apperception, the simple I, in the representation to which all combination or separation, which constitutes thinking, is related, also becomes important for itself, even if I have as yet settled nothing about the subject's constitution or subsistence. Apperception is something real, and the simplicity of it already lies in its possibility. Now in space there is nothing real that would be simple; for points (which constitute the only simple thing in space) are merely boundaries, but are not themselves something that serves to constitute space as a part. Thus there follows from this the impossibility of an explanation of my constitution, as merely thinking subject, from grounds of materialism. But because my existence is considered in the first proposition as given, in that it does not say: every thinking being exists (which would at the same time say absolute necessity and thus too much about them), but only: I exist thinking, it is empirical and contains the determinability of my existence merely with regard to my representations in time. But since for this I again first need something persistent, of which kind nothing at all is given to me in inner intuition insofar as I think myself: the way in which I exist, whether as substance or as accident, is not possible to determine at all through this simple self-consciousness. Thus if materialism is unsuited as a way of explaining my existence, spiritualism is just as insufficient for it; and the conclusion is that in no way, whichever it may be, can we cognize anything at all about the constitution of our soul that concerns the possibility of its separate existence in general.",["- OCR slips silently corrected: \"Mpperception\" → Apperception; \"Afo\"/\"Mfo\" → Also; \"ı\", \"a\", stray letters dropped; \"der; Spiritualism\" read \"der Spiritualism\".","- Satz = proposition; Verbindung = combination; Trennung = separation; Vorstellung = representation; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Anschauung = intuition; Selbstbewußtsein = self-consciousness; Bestimmbarkeit = determinability; Dasein = existence; Grenzen = boundaries (Grenze); Schranken not in this passage.","- \"Subsistenz\" = subsistence; \"Real\" = real (Realität not used); \"Wesen\" = being; \"Erklärungsart\" = way of explaining.","- \"von ihnen\" (zu viel von ihnen sagen) is left with its plural pronoun as in the German; the referent is unclear.","- \"Art\" appears twice, both as \"way\" / \"kind\" (\"dergleichen\" rendered \"of which kind\"); \"auf keine Art\" = \"in no way\".","- \"zu bestimmen möglich\" kept in German order: \"not possible to determine\".","- \"erkennen\" = cognize."]],"krv-b-1191":["And how should it even be possible, through the unity of consciousness, which we ourselves know only by using it as indispensable for the possibility of experience, to get beyond experience (our existence in life) and even to extend our cognition to the nature of all thinking beings in general through the empirical proposition, undetermined with regard to every kind of intuition: I think?",["- kennen = know; Erkenntniß = cognition; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Erfahrung = experience; Anschauung = intuition.","- \"Art der Anschauung\" = \"kind of intuition\"; \"Satz\" = proposition.","- \"solte\" (sic) read as \"sollte\"."]],"krv-b-1192":["There is thus no rational psychology as doctrine, which would procure for us an addition to our self-cognition, but only as discipline, which sets insurmountable boundaries to speculative reason in this field, on the one hand in order not to throw itself into the lap of soulless materialism, on the other hand not to lose itself roaming about in spiritualism, groundless for us in life, but rather reminds us to regard this refusal of our reason to give a satisfying answer to the curious",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; its continuation is krv-b-1193.","- OCR slips silently corrected: \"Shook\" → Schooß (\"lap\"), \"s ung\" → \"uns\"; the reading \"uns\" with \"erinnert\" is the editor's reconstruction; the subject of \"erinnert\" (the discipline) is as in the German.","- Doctrin = doctrine; Disciplin = discipline; Grenzen = boundaries; Selbsterkenntniß = self-cognition.","- \"sich ... zu verlieren\" / \"sich nicht dem ... zu werfen\" carry reflexive \"sich\" with unresolved reference, as in the German.","- The hyphenated line break \"neugieri⸗ / gen\" is joined across passages: \"neugierigen\" (\"curious\")."]],"krv-b-1193":["questions, reaching beyond this life, as a hint from it to apply our self-cognition away from fruitless transcendent speculation to fruitful practical use, which, even if it is directed always only to objects of experience, nonetheless takes its principles from higher up and determines conduct as if our vocation reached infinitely far beyond experience, hence beyond this life. I) One sees from all this that a mere misunderstanding gives rise to rational psychology. The unity of consciousness, which lies at the ground of the categories, is here taken for intuition of the subject as object, and the category of substance is applied to it. But it is only the unity in thinking, through which alone no object is given, to which therefore the category of substance, as",["- Opens mid-sentence, continuing krv-b-1192 (\"den neugierigen ... Fragen\"); ends mid-sentence, continued in krv-b-1194.","- Gebrauch = use; Bestimmung = vocation (here, per glossary; \"bestimmt\" = determines); Gegenstände = objects; Object = object (Gegenstand and Object both \"object\"); Grunde = ground.","- \"Gegen- b stände\" OCR break joined; stray \"b\" and \"Gie\" (→ Sie) corrected; \"I)\" is carried over as in the OCR, probably a footnote marker; its text is not present here.","- \"Kategorie\" = category; \"Mißverstand\" = misunderstanding.","- \"überschwenglich\" = transcendent (Wood's usage not in glossary; the plain rendering is a choice).","- \"ihren Ursprung gebe\" kept literal, \"gives rise to\" for \"give its origin\"; \"ihren\" refers ambiguously to the psychology."]],"krv-b-1194":["presupposes the always-given intuition, is not applied, hence this subject cannot be cognized at all. The subject of the categories can thus not, by thinking these, get a concept of itself as an object of the categories; for in order to think these, it must lay its pure self-consciousness, which was after all supposed to be explained, at the ground. Likewise the subject, in which the representation of time originally has its ground, cannot thereby determine its own existence in time, and if the latter cannot be, then the former too, as determination of itself (as thinking be-",["- Opens mid-sentence (continuing \"als ... voraussetzt\"); breaks off mid-word (\"We-\" = \"Wesen\", \"beings\"), rendered \"be-\" as a fragment; nothing completed.","- Stray \"b\" and \"vVorstellung\" corrected; \"diese\" and \"dieses\" are kept as \"these\" / \"this.\"","- erkennen = cognize; Bestimmung = determination; bestimmen = determine; Anschauung = intuition; Selbstbewußtsein = self-consciousness; Dasein = existence; Begriff = concept.","- \"das letztere ... das erstere\" kept as \"the latter ... the former\" with reference unresolved.","- \"Wesen\" fragment: \"We-\" is rendered \"be-\" to mirror the break; the reading \"Wesen\" is conjectural."]],"krv-b-1195":["Thus there vanishes an attempt beyond the boundaries of possible experience",["- Fragment; continues in krv-b-1196 (\"hinaus versuchtes ...\"): \"über die Grenzen möglicher Erfahrung / hinaus\" is split across passages, so \"beyond\" appears at the end here by the English order and is not repeated.","- Grenzen = boundaries; Erfahrung = experience; möglich = possible."]],"krv-b-1196":["made beyond and yet belonging to the highest interest of humanity | \") The: I think, is, as already said, an empirical proposition and holds the proposition: I exist, within itself. But I cannot say: everything that thinks exists; for there shall, in disappointed expectation; whereby nevertheless the rigor of critique, in that it proves at the same time the impossibility of settling anything dogmatically about an object of experience beyond the bound of experience, does reason the not unimportant service, in this its interest, of placing it equally in security against all possible assertions of the opposite; which cannot happen otherwise than by one's either proving one's proposition apodictically, or, if this does not succeed, seeking out the sources of this inability, which, if they lie in the necessary limitations of our reason, must then subject every opponent precisely to the same law of renunciation of all claims to dogmatic assertion.",["- The text is garbled: a footnote (beginning \"Das: Ich denke ...\", marked by the OCR as \"„)\", here carried as \") \", with the sign | before it) is interleaved with the main text, and the footnote breaks off at \"denn da soll,\" while the main text resumes at \"in getäuschte Erwartung\". The interleaving is kept as it stands; \"wobei\" connects to \"vanishes ... in disappointed expectation\" only in the reconstructed order.","- The footnote's sentence \"denn da soll,\" is cut off; nothing supplied. \"in getäuschte Erwartung\" is rendered as the German \"in disappointed expectation.\"","- \"Das: Ich denke\" is rendered literally, \"The: I think\"; \"Satz\" = proposition; \"Behauptung\" = assertion; \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Grenze\" = bound; \"Schranken\" = limitations (Schranke); \"Entsagung\" = renunciation.","- \"hinaus versuchtes\" = \"made beyond\" is a literal fragment; \"versucht\" = attempted, \"an attempt ... made beyond\" joined with krv-b-1195; \"gehöri—\" joined to \"gehörigen\" is incomplete: \"gehöri—\" is rendered \"belonging,\" which assumes \"gehöriges.\"","- The stray \"10\" before \"sprüche\" is a line number and is omitted; \"Unvermöͤgens\" = inability; \"apodiktisch\" = apodictically."]],"krv-b-1197":["Nevertheless nothing in the least is lost hereby for the warrant, indeed even the necessity, of the assumption of a future life in accordance with principles of the practical use of reason combined with the speculative; for the merely speculative proof has never been able to have any influence on common human reason anyway. It is so placed on a hair's point that even the school can keep it on the same only as long as it lets it spin ceaselessly around the same like a top, and it therefore provides in its own eyes no persisting ground on which anything could be built.\n\nFootnote: ... would make the property of thinking all beings that possess it into necessary beings. Hence my existence also cannot be regarded as concluded from the proposition: I think, as Cartesius held (because otherwise the major premise: everything that thinks exists, would have to precede), but is identical with it. It expresses an indeterminate empirical intuition, i.e., perception, (hence it nevertheless proves that sensation, which consequently belongs to sensibility, already lies at the ground of this existential proposition), but goes before the experience that is to determine the object of perception through the category with regard to time; and existence is here not yet a category, as that has relation not to an indeterminately given object, but only to one of which one has a concept, and about which one wants to know whether it is also posited outside this concept, or not. An indeterminate perception signifies here only something real that has been given, and indeed only for thinking in general, thus not as appearance, also not as thing in itself (noumenon), but as something that in fact exists and in the proposition: I think, is designated as such. For it is to be noted that when I have called the proposition: I think, an empirical proposition, I do not thereby want to say that the I in this proposition is an empirical representation; rather it is purely intellectual, because it belongs to thinking in general. But without some empirical representation that provides the material for thinking, the *actus*: I think, would nevertheless not take place, and the empirical is only the condition of the application or of the use of the pure intellectual faculty.",["- The OCR interleaves the footnote with the main text; I have separated them and set the footnote after the paragraph. The footnote begins mid-sentence (its beginning is on the preceding page), and I translate it as it stands.","- Befugniß = warrant; Annehmung = assumption; Beweis = proof; Grundlage = ground (Grund = ground also); \"auf derselben/um denselben\" left as \"on the same/around the same\".","- Eigenschaft = property (Beschaffenheit is also \"property\" in the glossary); Wesen = being; Obersatz = major premise; Existentialsatz = existential proposition; gefolgert (folgern) = concluded.","- Stoff = material; Anwendung = application; Gebrauch = use; Sache an sich selbst = thing in itself; *actus* is printed in roman in the source (Latin).","- \"Gegenstand\" does not occur here; \"Object\" = object."]],"krv-b-1198":["The proofs that are usable for the world all remain herein in their undiminished worth and rather gain in clarity and unartificial conviction through the removal of those dogmatic presumptions,",["- Überzeugung = conviction; Anmaßungen = presumptions."]],"krv-b-1199":["in that they transpose reason into its peculiar territory, namely the order of ends, which is yet at the same time an order of nature, which then, however, at the same time, as practical faculty in itself, without being restricted to the conditions of the latter, is entitled to extend the former and with it",["- OCR \"Drdnung\" corrected to \"Ordnung\".","- The relative \"die dann aber ... berechtigt ist\" could attach to reason or to the order; left as ambiguous as the German.","- Zwecke = ends; Vermögen = faculty; Bedingungen = conditions. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1200":["our own existence beyond the boundaries of experience and of life. To judge according to the analogy with the nature of living beings in this world, in which reason necessarily",["- Grenzen = boundaries; Existenz = existence. The passage breaks off in mid-sentence and is translated as it stands.","- \"zu urtheilen\" begins a construction completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1201":["must assume as principle that no organ, no faculty, no impulse, thus nothing dispensable or disproportionate to use, hence nothing non-purposive, is to be encountered, but everything is exactly commensurate to its determination in life, the human being, who yet alone can contain in himself the final end of all this, would have to be the sole creature that would be excepted from it. For his natural predispositions, not merely according to the talents and impulses to make use of them, but especially the moral law in him, go so far beyond all utility and advantage that he could draw from them in this life, that the latter even teaches one to esteem above all things the mere consciousness of the uprightness of the disposition in the absence of all advantages, even of the shadow-work of posthumous fame, and he feels himself inwardly called to make himself fit, through his conduct in this world with renunciation of many advantages, to be a citizen of a better one, which he has in the idea. This mighty ground of proof, never to be refuted, accompanied by a ceaselessly increasing cognition of purposiveness in everything we see before us, and by a prospect into the immensity of creation, hence also by the consciousness of a certain unboundedness in the possible extension of our knowledge together with an impulse commensurate to this, still always remains, even if we must give up having insight into the necessary continuation of our existence from the merely theoretical cognition of ourselves. Conclusion of the resolution of the psychological paralogism.",["- Hyphenation breaks (Unproportio-nirtes, Bestim-mung, widerle-gende) and a stray \"!\" in the OCR silently corrected.","- Grundsatz = principle; Bestimmung = determination; Antrieb = impulse, Triebe = \"impulse\" also (both rendered \"impulse\"); Endzweck = final end; Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness; Unzweckmäßiges = \"non-purposive\".","- Beweisgrund = \"ground of proof\"; Erkenntniß = cognition; Kenntnisse = \"knowledge\" (also Wissen's English); Bewußtsein = consciousness; einsehen = \"have insight into\"; Auflösung = resolution; Paralogism = paralogism.","- The syntax \"Nach der Analogie ... zu urtheilen, müßte der Mensch ...\" is kept as in the German; \"das letztere\" kept as \"the latter\" (antecedent unspecified)."]],"krv-b-1202":["The dialectical illusion in rational psychology rests on the confusion of an idea of reason (of a pure intelligence) with the in all respects indeterminate concept of a thinking being in general. I think myself for the sake of a possible experience, in that I still abstract from all actual experience, and conclude from this that I could become conscious of my existence also outside experience and the empirical conditions of the same. Consequently I confuse the possible abstraction from my empirically determined existence with the supposed consciousness of a separately possible existence of my thinking self and believe that I cognize the substantial in me as the transcendental subject, in that I merely have in thought the unity of consciousness, which lies at the ground of all determining as the mere form of cognition.",["- Schein = illusion; Vernunft = reason; Stücken = \"respects\"; schließen = conclude; Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize.","- OCR \"Er-kenntniß\" rejoined."]],"krv-b-1203":["The problem of explaining the community of the soul with the body does not properly belong to that psychology of which there is talk here, because it has the intention of proving the personality of the soul also outside this community (after death) and is therefore transcendent in the proper sense, although it occupies itself with an object of experience, but only insofar as it ceases to be an object of experience. Meanwhile a sufficient answer can also be given to this according to our doctrinal concept. The difficulty that has occasioned this problem consists, as is known, in the presupposed dissimilarity of the object of inner sense (of the soul) with the objects of outer senses, since to the former only time, to the latter also space, attaches as formal condition of their intuition. But if one considers that both kinds of objects distinguish themselves from one another herein not inwardly, but only insofar as one appears outer to the other, hence that what lies at the ground of the appearance of matter as thing in itself might perhaps not be so dissimilar, then this difficulty vanishes, and none other remains than how a community of substances is possible at all, which to resolve lies entirely outside the field of psychology and, as the reader, after what has been said in the Analytic about fundamental forces and faculties, will easily judge, lies without any doubt also outside the field of all human cognition.",["- \"Objecte\" and \"Gegenstand/Gegenstände\" are both rendered \"object(s)\"; Object occurs in the first sentence, Gegenstand in \"ein Gegenstand der Erfahrung\" and elsewhere.","- \"im eigentlichen Verstande\" rendered \"in the proper sense\" (Verstand is \"understanding\" in the glossary).","- Aufgabe = problem; Gemeinschaft = community; Absicht = intention; Lehrbegriff = doctrinal concept; Art = kind; Grundkräften = fundamental forces; Vermögen = faculties; Erkenntniß = cognition; Beurtheilen: urtheilen = judge; Substanzen = substances.","- OCR breaks \"Un: gleichartigkeit,\" \"unter: scheiden,\" \"mensch— lichen\" rejoined."]],"krv-b-1204":["General Remark,\nconcerning the transition from rational psychology\nto cosmology.",["- Anmerkung = remark; Übergang = transition; Kosmologie = cosmology."]],"krv-b-1205":["The proposition: I think, or: I exist thinking, is an empirical proposition. At the ground of such a one, however, lies empirical intuition, consequently also the thought object as appearance, and so it seems as if, according to our theory, the soul were transformed entirely and completely, even in thinking, into appearance, and in such a way our consciousness itself, | as mere illusion, would in fact have to go to nothing.",["- The OCR stray \"J\" after \"Anschauung, folglich auch das\" is dropped; the sign | is carried over where it stands. A full stop after \"Satz\" is added in the translation where the OCR has none (\"empirischer Satz Einem\").","- \"auf nichts gehen\" rendered literally as \"go to nothing\"; the sense of \"go\" (be directed to / come to) is left open.","- Satz = proposition; Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinung = appearance; Schein = illusion; Object = object."]],"krv-b-1206":["Thinking, taken for itself, is merely the logical function, hence sheer spontaneity of the combination of the manifold of a merely possible intuition, and does not at all present the subject of consciousness as appearance, merely because it takes no regard at all of the way of intuition, whether it is sensible or intellectual. Thereby I present myself to myself neither as I am, nor as I to myself er-",["- The passage breaks off mid-word (\"er-\") and is translated as it stands, not completed. The verb governing \"stelle ich mich\" (OCR \"Dadutch\" = \"Dadurch\") is not in the text; \"present\" renders \"stellt ... dar\" from the first sentence and \"stelle\", as in the German.","- Verbindung = combination; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Art = way; Rücksicht = regard; sinnlich = sensible; Spontaneität = spontaneity."]],"krv-b-1207":["appears to me, but I think myself only like any object in general, from whose way of intuition I abstract. If I here represent myself as subject of thoughts or also as ground of thinking, then these ways of representing do not signify the categories of substance or of cause, for these are those functions of thinking (of judging) already applied to our sensible intuition, which would of course be required if I wanted to cognize myself. Now, however, I want to become conscious of myself only as thinking; how my own self is given in intuition, that I set aside, and then it could be mere appearance to me, who think, but not insofar as I think; in the consciousness of myself in mere thinking I am the being itself, of which, to be sure, nothing is thereby yet given to me for thinking. | The proposition, however: I think, insofar as it says as much as: I exist thinking, is not a mere logical function, but determines the subject (which is then at the same time object) with regard to existence, and cannot take place without inner sense, whose intuition always supplies the object not as thing in itself, but merely as appearance. In it, therefore, there is already no longer mere spontaneity of thinking, but also receptivity of intuition, i.e., the thinking of myself applied to the empirical intuition of just the same subject. In this latter the thinking self would then have to seek the conditions of the use of its logical functions as categories of substance, of cause, etc., in order not merely to designate itself as object in itself through the I, but also to determine the way of its existence, i.e., to cognize itself as noumenon; which, however, is impossible, since inner empirical intuition is sensible and supplies nothing but data of appearance, which can furnish nothing to the object of pure consciousness for the acquaintance with its separate existence, but can serve merely for experience. |",[]],"krv-b-1208":["Suppose, however, that in the sequel there were found, not in experience, but in certain laws of the pure use of reason (not merely logical rules, but) standing firm a priori and concerning our existence, an occasion for presupposing ourselves fully a priori as legislating with regard to our own existence and as also themselves determining this existence: then a spontaneity would thereby be discovered through which our reality would be determinable, without needing for that the conditions of empirical intuition; and here we would become aware that in the consciousness of our existence something is contained a priori which can serve to determine our existence, determinable throughout only sensibly, nevertheless with regard to a certain inner faculty in relation to an intelligible (to be sure only thought) world.",[]],"krv-b-1209":["But this would nonetheless not advance all attempts in rational psychology in the least. For through that admirable faculty, which first reveals to me the consciousness of the moral law, I would indeed have a principle of the determination of my existence which is purely intellectual, but through which predicates? Through no others than those that must be given to me in sensible intuition; and so I would again land there where I was in rational psychology, namely in the need of sensible intuitions in order to procure significance for my concepts of the understanding, substance, cause, etc., through which alone I can have cognition of myself; those intuitions, however, can never help me out beyond the field of experience. Meanwhile I would nevertheless, with regard to practical use, which is nevertheless always directed to objects of experience, be authorized to apply these concepts, in accordance with the significance analogous to that in theoretical use, to freedom and the subject of it, in that I understand by them merely the logical functions of subject",[]],"krv-b-1210":["and predicate, of ground and consequence, in accordance with which",[]],"krv-b-1211":["the actions or the effects are so determined in accordance with those laws that, together with the laws of nature, they can always be explained in accordance with the categories of substance and of cause, although they | arise from an entirely different principle. This has been said only to prevent the misunderstanding to which the doctrine of our self-intuition as appearance is easily exposed. In what follows",[]],"krv-b-1212":["as appearance is easily exposed, should be said. In what follows",[]],"krv-b-1213":["one will have occasion to make use of this¹).",[]],"krv-b-1214":["The",[]],"krv-b-1215":["Transcendental Dialectic, second book.",[]],"krv-b-1216":["Second Main Section.\nThe Antinomy of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-b-1217":["We have shown in the introduction to this part of our work that all transcendental illusion of pure reason rests on dialectical inferences, whose schema logic gives in the three formal kinds of",[]],"krv-b-1218":["syllogisms in general, just as the categories",[]],"krv-b-1219":["find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgments. The",[]],"krv-b-1220":["first kind of these rationalizing inferences went to the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject or of the soul) in correspondence with categorical syllogisms,",[]],"krv-b-1221":["whose major premise as principle asserts the relation of a predicate to",[]],"krv-b-1222":["a subject. The second kind of dialectical argument will",[]],"krv-b-1223":["therefore, in analogy with hypothetical syllogisms, make the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in appearance its content, just as the third kind, which will occur in the following main section, has as theme the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.",[]],"krv-b-1224":["It is remarkable, however, that the transcendental paralogism effected a merely one-sided illusion with regard to the idea of the subject of our thinking, and for the assertion of the opposite not the least illusion from concepts of reason is willing to be found. The advantage is entirely on the side of pneumatism, although this cannot deny its hereditary defect of dissolving into sheer vapor in the fiery test of critique, for all the illusion favorable to it.",[]],"krv-b-1225":["Quite otherwise it turns out when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of appearances, where it indeed thinks to make its principle of unconditioned unity count with much illusion, but soon entangles itself in such contradictions that it is compelled, in cosmological aim, to desist from its demand.",[]],"krv-b-1226":["Here, namely, a new phenomenon of human reason shows itself, namely: a quite natural antithetic, on which no one needs to brood or to lay snares artificially, but into which reason falls of itself and indeed unavoidably, and is thereby indeed guarded against the slumber of an imagined conviction, which a merely one-sided illusion produces, but at the same time is brought into temptation either to abandon itself to a skeptical hopelessness, or to adopt a dogmatic defiance and to set its head stiffly on certain assertions, without granting a hearing and justice to the grounds of the opposite. Both are the death of a sound philosophy, although the former could at any rate still be called the euthanasia of pure reason.\n\n---notes\n- krv-b-1212: I erred above by first printing a garbled duplicate under that id; the second block (beginning \"as appearance is easily exposed\") is the intended rendering, the first should be disregarded. The German \"sollen\" (for \"sollte\") is an OCR slip, silently corrected.\n- krv-b-1207: \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Kenntniß\" = acquaintance (glossary); \"erkennen\" = cognize; \"Wesen\" = being. \"Behüte\" (OCR) read as \"Behufe\"; rendered \"for experience\" without the \"Behuf\" sense (\"for the use of\"), doubtful.\n- krv-b-1207: \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" both = \"object\" (Object in 1207, 1208, 1211; Gegenstände in 1209, 1223). \"Satz\" = proposition (1207), but \"Obersatz\" = major premise (1221).\n- krv-b-1207: \"so fern ich denke\" = \"insofar as I think\"; \"Data\" kept as in German.\n- krv-b-1208: \"Wirklichkeit\" = reality (glossary: reality/actuality); \"Existenz\" and \"Dasein\" both = \"existence\". Ambiguity kept: \"nur sinnlich durchgängig bestimmbare\" could attach \"only\" to \"sensibly\" or to \"throughout determinable\"; \"völlig a priori\" could attach to \"presuppose\" or to \"legislating\".\n- krv-b-1209: \"Bedeutung\" = significance (meaning in glossary); \"bedeuten\" in 1207 = signify. \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition.\n- krv-b-1211: \"Princip\" = principle; \"Wirkungen\" = effects; \"Folge\" = consequence (in 1210), per the glossary Folge = sequel/sequence, but here kept as logical \"consequence\" for ground–consequent.\n- krv-b-1217–1226: \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism; \"Schluß\" = inference; \"vernünftelnd\" = rationalizing; \"Absicht\" = aim; \"Behauptung\" = assertion; \"Überzeugung\" = conviction; \"Gründe\" = grounds. Footnote marker \"1)\" in 1213 carried over; the footnote text is not in the passage. Line numbers and OCR debris (\"10\", \"18\", \"25\", etc.) omitted; \"Moglichkeit,\" \"Der-hütung\" (Verhütung), \"gefunden\" (gesunden) silently corrected.",[]],"krv-b-1227":["Before we let the scenes of discord and disorders be seen, which this conflict of the laws (antinomy) of pure reason occasions, we want to give certain expositions, which can clarify and justify the method of which we avail ourselves in the treatment of our object. I call all transcendental ideas, insofar as they concern the absolute totality in the synthesis of appearances, world-concepts, partly on account of just this unconditioned totality, on which the concept of the world-whole also rests, which is itself only an idea, partly because they go merely to the synthesis of appearances, hence the empirical one, whereas the absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things in general will occasion an ideal of pure reason, which is entirely distinguished from the world-concept, although it stands in relation to it. Hence just as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the ground for a dialectical psychology, so the antinomy of pure reason will set before our eyes the transcendental principles of a putative pure (rational) cosmology, not in order to find it valid and appropriate it, but, as the very term of a conflict of reason already indicates,",["- OCR \"des treffen\" read as \"betreffen\" (Erscheinungen betreffen).","- Erörterungen = expositions (*expositio*); Erläuterung/erläutern = clarification/clarify; Gegenstand = object; Benennung = term; Grundsätze = principles.","- \"sie\" (… zu finden und sich zuzueignen) is left unresolved as in the German."]],"krv-b-1228":["in order to present it as an idea that cannot be reconciled with appearances, in",["- Fragment of the sentence begun in the preceding passage; \"sie\" is left unresolved."]],"krv-b-1229":["its dazzling but false illusion.",["- Schein = illusion."]],"krv-b-1230":["Of the\nAntinomy of Pure Reason\nFirst Section.\nSystem of the cosmological ideas.",["- Heading; \"Der\" is the genitive article continuing the title \"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft\"."]],"krv-b-1231":["In order now to be able to enumerate these ideas according to one principle with systematic precision",["- Princip = principle (Grundsatz is also \"principle\" elsewhere in this section).","- Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1232":["we must first remark that it is only the understanding from which pure and transcendental concepts can arise,",["- Line number \"10\" and the hyphenation \"Ver: stand\" silently corrected. Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1233":["that reason actually generates no concept at all, but at most only frees the concept of the understanding from the unavoidable restrictions of a possible experience and thus seeks to extend it beyond the boundaries of the empirical, yet still in connection with it. This happens by its demanding, for a given conditioned, on the side of the conditions (under which the understanding subjects all appearances to synthetic unity) absolute totality",["- Einschränkungen = restrictions (not Schranke = limitation); Grenzen = boundaries; Verknüpfung = connection.","- \"daß … erzeuge … frei mache … zu erweitern suche\": the construction continues the \"daß\" clauses of the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1234":["and thereby makes the category into the transcendental idea, in order",["- Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1235":["to give to the empirical synthesis absolute completeness through the continuation of the same up to the unconditioned (which is never encountered in experience, but only in the idea)",["- OCR \"Fortjegung\" read as \"Fortsetzung\"; \"Unde- dingten\" as \"Unbedingten\". The German verb \"zu geben\" falls in the next passage; the English order is kept as literally as possible but \"zu geben\" appears at the start of the next passage in the German (see there)."]],"krv-b-1236":["Reason demands",["- The German places \"absolute Vollständigkeit zu geben.\" here; the clause \"to give … absolute completeness\" is rendered in the preceding passage's English only to keep the English intelligible; strictly: \"absolute completeness to give. Reason demands\"."]],"krv-b-1237":["this according to the principle: if the conditioned is given, then",["- Grundsatz = principle."]],"krv-b-1238":["the whole sum of the conditions, hence the absolutely unconditioned, is also given, through which alone the former was possible. Thus, first, the transcendental ideas will actually be nothing but",["- Summe = sum; schlechthin = absolutely; \"jenes\" = \"the former\" (literally \"that\")."]],"krv-b-1239":["categories extended to the unconditioned, and these will let themselves be brought into a table that is arranged according to the titles of the latter. Sec-",["- \"jene\" / \"der letzteren\" rendered \"these\" / \"the latter\"; the word \"Zwei—tens\" is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1240":["ondly, however, not all categories will be suited for this either, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series, and indeed of conditions subordinated to one another (not coordinated) to a",["- einander untergeordnet / beigeordnet = subordinated / coordinated. \"diejenige\" is singular in the German."]],"krv-b-1241":["conditioned. Absolute totality is demanded by reason only insofar as it concerns the ascending series of the conditions to a given conditioned, hence not when there is talk of the descending line",["- Totalität = totality; Reihe = series."]],"krv-b-1242":["of the consequences, nor of the aggregate of coordinated conditions for these consequences. For conditions are already presupposed with regard to the given conditioned and are to be regarded as given along with it, whereas, since the consequences do not make their conditions possible but rather presuppose them, in the progression to the consequences (or in the descent from the given condition to the conditioned) one can be unconcerned whether the series ceases or not, and the question about its totality is in general no presupposition of reason at all.",["- Folgen = consequences (also Folge = sequel; sequence in the glossary); Fortgang = progression. beigeordnet (previous passage) and coordinirt here are both \"coordinated\"."]],"krv-b-1243":["Thus one necessarily thinks a time completely elapsed up to the given moment as also given (even if not determinable by us). But as concerns the future, since it is not the condition for arriving at the present, it is, in order to comprehend the latter, entirely a matter of indifference how we want to deal with future time, whether one wants to let it cease somewhere, or run on into the infinite. Let the series be m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned with regard to m, but at the same time as condition of o; let the series go upward from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i etc.), likewise downward from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r etc.): then I must presuppose the former series in order to regard m as given, and n is possible, according to reason (the totality of conditions), only by means of that series, but its possibility does not rest on the following series o, p, q, r, which therefore also could not be regarded as given, but only as *dabilis*.",["- OCR \"T\" read as \"r\"; \"mals\" as \"m als\"; \"im gleichen\" = \"likewise\".","- begreifen = comprehend; Ansehung = \"regard\".","- \"die\" before \"daher auch nicht als gegeben\" refers grammatically to the series (die Reihe); \"dabilis\" is Latin in the original."]],"krv-b-1244":["I will call the synthesis of a series on the side of the conditions, thus from that one which is nearest to the given appearance and so on to the more remote conditions, the regressive, but that which on the side of the conditioned proceeds from the nearest consequence to the more remote, the progressive synthesis. The former goes *in antecedentia*, the second *in consequentia*. The cosmological ideas therefore occupy themselves with the totality of the regressive synthesis and go *in antecedentia*, not *in consequentia*. If the latter happens, it is an arbitrary and not necessary problem of pure reason, because for the complete comprehensibility of what is given in the appearance we need grounds, to be sure, but not consequences.",["- Problem = problem (as against Aufgabe, also \"problem\"); Begreiflichkeit = comprehensibility; Gründe = grounds; willkürlich = arbitrary.","- \"diejenige … die\" in the German could refer to either \"Synthesis\" or \"Seite\"; kept as \"that which\"."]],"krv-b-1245":["Now in order to set up the table of ideas according to the table of categories, we take first the two original *quanta* of all our intuition, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series), and hence in it, with regard to a given present, the *antecedentia* as conditions (the past) are to be distinguished *a priori* from the *consequentibus* (the future).",["- Anschauung = intuition. \"a priori\" could attach to \"unterscheiden\" or to the preceding words; left as ambiguous as in the German."]],"krv-b-1246":["Consequently the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the",["- Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1247":["series of conditions for a given conditioned goes only to all past time. According to the idea of reason the whole elapsed time is necessarily thought as given, as condition of the given moment. But as concerns space, there is in it in itself no",["- Line number \"s\" silently dropped. verlaufen / vergangen / verflossen are all rendered \"elapsed\" / \"past\" / \"elapsed\" without distinction beyond context: verlaufen = elapsed, vergangen = past, verflossen = elapsed."]],"krv-b-1248":["difference of *progressus* from *regressus*, because it constitutes an aggregate, but",["- Aggregat = aggregate."]],"krv-b-1249":["no series, in that its parts are all together at the same time. The present point of time I could regard with regard to past time only as conditioned, but never as condition of it, because this moment first arises only through the elapsed time (or rather through the elapsing of the preceding time). But since the",["- OCR \"Dien\" read as \"Den\"; stray \"„0\" dropped. \"konnte\" printed; \"könnte\" (\"could\") intended, rendered as \"could\"."]],"krv-b-1250":["parts of space are not subordinated to one another, but coordinated,",["- The German of this passage (\"Verfließen … entspringt. Aber da die\") begins in the preceding one; translated as divided in the passages."]],"krv-b-1251":["so one part is not the condition of the possibility of the other, and it does not constitute a series, as time does in itself.",["- \"er\" in the German (the part, or space) is left as \"it\"."]],"krv-b-1252":["Yet the synthesis of the manifold parts of space, through which we apprehend it, is nevertheless successive, thus happens in time and contains a series. And since in this series of aggregated spaces (e.g., of the feet in a rod) the ones thought further added from a given one on are always the condition of the boundary of the preceding, so the measuring of a",["- Passage division follows the source; the English of passage 1251 corresponds to the German \"so ist ein Theil … an sich selbst eine Reihe aus.\"","- OCR \"apprehen⸗ diren\" read as \"apprehendiren\" (apprehend); \"Be- 0 din gnug\" read as \"Bedingung\" (singular noun with plural verb \"sind\", kept); Ruthe = rod; Grenze = boundary; Mannigfaltige = manifold."]],"krv-b-1253":["space is also to be regarded as a synthesis of a series of conditions for a",["- Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1254":["given conditioned; only that the side of the conditions is in itself not distinguished from",["- Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1255":["the side toward which the conditioned lies, consequently *regressus* and *progressus* in space seem to be one and the same. Since, however, a part of space is not by the other ge-",["- Passage breaks off mid-word (\"ge-\"); translated as it stands, not completed.","- Line number \"is\" silently dropped."]],"krv-b-1256":["[No text corresponds to this id in the source beyond what is rendered above.]",["- The German of this passage (\"der Seite, nach welcher das Bedingte hinliegt, an sich selbst nicht unter-\") is already included in the English of 1254–1255 above."]],"krv-b-1257":["[No separate text; see 1255.]",["- The German of this passage (\"schieden ist, folglich regressus und progressus im Raume einerlei zu sein scheint. Weil indessen ein Theil des Raums nicht durch den andern ge-\") is included in the English of 1255 above."]],"krv-b-1258":["give, but is only bounded, we must also regard every bounded space, to that extent, as conditioned, which presupposes another space as the condition of its boundary, and so on. With regard to the bound-",["- *Begrenzung* is broken after \"Be-\"; rendered \"bound-\" and continued as \"ing\" in the next passage. *Grenze* = boundary; *bedingt* = conditioned; *Bedingung* = condition.","- *in so fern* rendered \"to that extent\"; its attachment (\"also ... in so fern\" or \"ansehen ... in so fern\") is left as loose as in the German."]],"krv-b-1259":["ing, therefore, the progression in space is also a regress, and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in the series",["- Opening \"ing\" continues the broken *Begrenzung* of the previous passage. *Fortgang* = progression; *Regressus* = \"regress\".","- OCR \"So\" before *transscendentale* silently dropped."]],"krv-b-1260":["of conditions also concerns space, and I can just as well ask after the absolute totality of the appearance in space as of that in the elapsed | time. But whether an answer to it is possible at all,",["- OCR \"verflofje- | neu\" read as *verflossenen*; the sign | is carried over.","- *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Zeit* = time."]],"krv-b-1261":["will allow itself to be determined in the future. Secondly, the reality in space, i.e., matter, is something conditioned, whose inner conditions are its parts and the parts of the parts the remote conditions, so that here a regressive synthesis takes place, whose absolute totality reason demands, which can take place in no other way than through a completed division, through which the reality of matter vanishes either into nothing or else into that which is no longer matter, namely the simple. Consequently here too there is a series of conditions and a progress to the unconditioned.",["- Stray OCR \"5\" dropped. *Fortschritt* = \"progress\" (distinct from *Fortgang*, \"progression\").","- *Theilung* = division; *Einfache* = \"the simple\"; *Realität* = reality; *Bedingtes* = \"something conditioned\".","- The relative clause \"welche ... stattfinden kann\" could attach to *Totalität*, *Synthesis* or *Vernunft* (the German is ambiguous); the English preserves the \"which\"."]],"krv-b-1262":["Thirdly, as regards the categories of real relation among appearances, the category of substance with its accidents does not lend itself to a transcendental idea; i.e., reason has no ground to go regressively to conditions with regard to it. For accidents (insofar as they inhere in a single substance) are coordinated with one another and make up no series. With regard to the substance, however, they are not properly subordinated to it, but are the way of existing of the substance itself. What might still seem here to be an idea of transcendental reason would be the concept of the substantial. But since this signifies nothing other than the concept of the object in general, which subsists insofar as one thinks in it merely the transcendental subject without any predicates, but here there is talk only of the unconditioned in the series of appearances: so it is clear that the substantial can constitute no member in it. The very same holds also of substances in community, which are mere aggregates and have no exponent of a series, since they are not subordinated to one another as conditions of their possibility, which one could well say of spaces, whose boundary was never in itself but always determined through another space. There remains, therefore, only the category of causality, which offers a series of causes for a given effect, in which one can ascend from the latter as the conditioned to those as conditions and answer the question of reason.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"ein- 1 ander\", \"Sub— stantiale\", \"konne\" (*könne*), \"zoi\", \"gez 2 gebenen\", \"Kate— gorie\"; stray numerals dropped.","- *Substantiale* = \"the substantial\"; *Gegenstand* = \"object\"; *Gemeinschaft* = community; *Art zu existiren* = \"way of existing\"; *Vernunftfrage* = \"question of reason\".","- *einigen Substanz* rendered \"single substance\"; *bedeutet* = signifies; *an sich* = in itself.","- \"darbietet\" rendered \"offers\"; \"eigentlich\" rendered \"properly.\"","- The Academy page reference for this passage is uncertain."]],"krv-b-1263":["Fourthly, the concepts of the possible, the actual, and the necessary lead to no series, except only insofar as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned and, according to the rule of the understanding, points to a condition under which it is necessary to point this to a higher condition, until reason encounters unconditioned necessity only in the totality of this series.",["- OCR \"jeder- a zeit\" read as *jederzeit* (\"always\").","- *Zufällige* = contingent; *Dasein* = existence; *Verstand* = understanding; *Allheit/Totalität* = totality.","- \"darunter es nothwendig ist, diese ... zu weisen\" is kept as stands; the reference of \"darunter\" (a condition) and \"diese\" is left as in the German."]],"krv-b-1264":["There are accordingly no more than four cosmological ideas, according to the four titles of the categories, if one picks out those that necessarily bring with them a series in the synthesis of the manifold.",["- *aushebt* = \"picks out\"; *Mannigfaltige* = manifold."]],"krv-b-1265":["1.\nThe absolute completeness\nof the\ncomposition\nof the given whole of all appearances.",["- *Zusammensetzung* = composition; *Vollständigkeit* = completeness. Line breaks of the table kept."]],"krv-b-1266":["The absolute completeness The absolute completeness",["- The numerals 2 and 3 of the table are not in the passage as given; nothing supplied."]],"krv-b-1267":["of the division of the origination",["- *Theilung* = division; *Entstehung* = \"origination\". Table columns run together as in the source."]],"krv-b-1268":["of a given whole of an appearance",["- Two columns run together as in the source (\"eines gegebenen Ganzen\" / \"einer Erscheinung\")."]],"krv-b-1269":["in the appearance in general.\n4.\nThe absolute completeness\nof the dependence of the existence\nof the alterable in the appearance.",["- *Veränderlichen* = \"the alterable\"; *Abhängigkeit* = dependence; *überhaupt* = \"in general\". Table line breaks kept."]],"krv-b-1270":["First it is to be remarked here: that the idea of absolute totality concerns nothing other than the exposition of appearances, hence not the pure concept of the understanding of a whole of things in general. Here, therefore, appearances are considered as given, and reason demands the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, insofar as these make up a series, hence a synthesis that is absolutely (i.e., in every respect) complete, through which the appearance could be expounded according to laws of the understanding.",["- OCR \"über: haupt\" read as *überhaupt*. *Exposition* / *exponirt* = exposition / expounded (cf. *expositio*, *Erörterung*).","- *in aller Absicht*: *Absicht* (glossary \"aim\") rendered \"respect\" in this idiom. *schlechthin* = \"absolutely\".","- *Gesetze* = laws; *betrachtet* = considered."]],"krv-b-1271":["Secondly, it is properly only the unconditioned that reason seeks in this synthesis of conditions continued series-wise and indeed regressively, as it were the completeness in the series of premises, which together presuppose no further one. This unconditioned is now always contained in the absolute totality of the series, if one represents it to oneself in the imagination. Yet this absolutely completed synthesis is in turn only an idea; for one cannot know, at least in advance, whether such a synthesis is also possible in the case of appearances. If one represents everything through mere pure concepts of the understanding, without conditions of sensible intuition, one can say straight out: that for a given conditioned the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another is also given; for the former is given only through the latter. Yet with appearances a particular restriction of the way in which conditions are given is to be encountered, namely through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, which is supposed to be complete in the regress. Whether this completeness is now sensibly possible is still a problem. Yet the idea of this completeness lies nevertheless in reason, regardless of the possibility or impossibility of connecting empirical concepts adequate to it. Thus, since in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in the appearance (under the guidance of the categories, which represent it as a series of conditions for a given conditioned) the unconditioned is necessarily contained, one may also leave undecided whether and how this totality is to be brought about: so reason here takes the path of setting out from the idea of totality, although it properly has as its final aim the unconditioned, whether of the whole series or of a part of it.",["- OCR corrected silently: \"volendete\" (*vollendete*); stray \"10%\" dropped.","- *Einbildung* = \"imagination\" (not *Einbildungskraft*); *Anschauung* = intuition; *sinnlich* = sensible; *Aufgabe/Problem* = problem; *Endabsicht* = \"final aim\".","- \"ihr adäquat empirische Begriffe zu verknüpfen\": *adäquat* may go with *Begriffe* (\"concepts adequate to it\") or with *verknüpfen* (\"to connect adequately\"); the German is ambiguous, the English commits to the first reading.","- *wissen* = \"know\"; *Einschränkung* = restriction; *Regressus* = \"regress\"; *Prämissen* = premises."]],"krv-b-1272":["This unconditioned one can now think of: either as consisting merely in the whole series, in which therefore all members without exception would be conditioned and only the whole of it absolutely unconditioned, and then the regress is called infinite; or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the remaining members of it are subordinated, but which itself stands under no other condition.¹ In the former case the series is *a parte priori* without boundaries (without beginning), i.e., infinite, and nevertheless entirely given, but the regress in it is never completed and can only be called infinite *potentialiter*. In the second case there is a first of the series, which with regard to elapsed time is called the beginning of the world, with regard to space the boundary of the world, with regard to the parts of a whole given in its boundaries the simple, with regard to causes absolute self-activity (freedom), with regard to the existence of alterable things absolute natural necessity.",["- OCR \"uns endlich\" corrected silently to *unendlich* (\"infinite\"); stray \"25\" dropped. Footnote marker (printed as a parenthesis) rendered ¹; the footnote follows in the next passage.","- *Grenze* = boundary (in \"Weltgrenze\", \"Grenzen\"); *Selbstthätigkeit* = self-activity; *Naturnothwendigkeit* = natural necessity.","- \"sich gedenken\" rendered \"think of\"; *Glieder* = members."]],"krv-b-1273":["We have two expressions: world and nature, which sometimes run into one another. The first signifies the mathematical whole of all ap-",["- *Ausdruck* = expression; *bedeutet* = signifies. *Erscheinungen* is broken after \"Er-\"; rendered \"ap-\" (continued \"pearances\" in the next passage). Stray \"30\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1274":["¹ The absolute whole of the series of conditions for a given conditioned is always unconditioned, because outside it there are no more conditions with regard to which it could be conditioned. Yet this absolute whole of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematic concept, whose possibility must be investigated, and indeed in relation to the way in which the unconditioned, as the proper transcendental idea on which it depends, may be contained in it. pearances and the totality of their synthesis in the large as well as in the small, i.e., as well in the progress of the same through composition,",["- Footnote (to the marker in the previous passage) precedes the continuation of the main sentence, as in the source. OCR \"Dag\" (*Das*), \"unter- sucht\", stray \"&*\" corrected or dropped.","- \"pearances\" continues the broken *Erscheinungen* of the previous passage. *worauf es ankommt* rendered \"on which it depends\"."]],"krv-b-1275":["as through division. The very same world, however, is called nature² insofar as",["- Footnote marker after *Natur* rendered ², its note is not in the passage. *Theilung* = division; *Welt* = world.","- The sentence breaks off; not completed."]],"krv-b-1276":["it is considered as a dynamical whole, and one looks not to the aggregation in space or time, in order to bring it about as a magnitude,",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence and continues in krv-b-1277. \"Aggregation\" = Aggregation; \"Größe\" = magnitude; \"Raume\" = space; \"Zeit\" = time.","- \"betrachtet\" rendered \"considered\" (cf. Betrachtung = consideration). \"sie\" has no stated antecedent in this fragment."]],"krv-b-1277":["but to the unity in the existence of the appearances. Now the condition of that which happens is called the cause, and the unconditioned causality of the cause in the appearance freedom, but the conditioned, by contrast, is called, in the narrower sense, natural cause. The conditioned in existence in general is called contingent and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of appearances can be called natural",["- Dasein = existence; Erscheinungen = appearances; Verstande (\"im engeren Verstande\") = \"sense,\" not the faculty (Verstand = understanding).","- Ursache = cause; Causalität = causality; Naturursache = \"natural cause\"; zufällig = contingent; nothwendig = necessary; Bedingung/bedingt/unbedingt = condition/conditioned/unconditioned.","- Sentence breaks off at \"natural\" and continues in krv-b-1278 (Naturnothwendigkeit). The word \"Be—dingte\" is divided by a line break in the OCR; silently joined."]],"krv-b-1278":["necessity.",["- Naturnothwendigkeit = natural necessity (the compound begun in krv-b-1277)."]],"krv-b-1279":["The ideas with which we are now occupied I called above cosmological ideas, partly because by world is understood the sum total of all appearances, and our ideas are also directed only to the unconditioned among appearances, partly also because the word world, in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the sum total of | existing things, and we direct our attention solely to the completeness of the synthesis (although, properly, only in the regress to the conditions). In consideration of the fact that, moreover, these ideas are all transcendent and, although they do not exceed the object, namely appearances, in kind, but have to do merely with the sensible world (not with noumena), nevertheless drive the synthesis to a degree that surpasses all possible experience, one can, in my opinion, quite suitably call them all world-concepts",["- Idee = idea; Inbegriff = sum total; Totalität = totality; Erscheinung = appearance; Art = kind; Grad = degree; Meinung = opinion; Weltbegriffe = \"world-concepts,\" the compound is divided across the line break and continues in krv-b-1280 (\"griffe nennen\").","- \"eriftirender\" in the OCR corrected to \"existirender\" (existing); \"|\" carried over as in the source.","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = sensible world; \"Noümenis\" = *noumena* (Latin dative/ablative form kept as printed in the German; rendered \"noumena\").","- \"Verstande\" in \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" = \"sense.\"","- \"Der Art nach\" = \"in kind\"; \"Art\" could also be \"way/species\" per glossary."]],"krv-b-1280":[". With regard to the difference of the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned, at which the regress aims, I would nevertheless call the first two, in a narrower meaning, world-concepts (of the world in the large and in the small), but the two remaining ones transcendent concepts of nature",["- The sentence begins with the completion of the previous fragment (\"…griffe nennen\"); the initial full stop in the English reflects the OCR break, as the German here reads \"griffe nennen. In Ansehung…\". Strictly, \"griffe nennen\" is the end of krv-b-1279; it is not repeated here.","- Weltbegriffe = world-concepts; Naturbegriffe = concepts of nature; Bedeutung = meaning.","- Footnote marker \")\" at the end of this passage is attached to \"Naturbegriffe\" in the OCR and the footnote follows in krv-b-1281; the German \"nennen\" at the end of that footnote text is part of this sentence's continuation."]],"krv-b-1281":[") Nature, taken adjectivally (*formaliter*), signifies the interconnection of the determinations of a thing in accordance with an inner principle of causality. By contrast, under nature substantively (*materialiter*) one understands the sum total of appearances, insofar as these thoroughly hang together by virtue of an inner principle of causality. In the former sense one speaks of the nature of fluid matter, of fire, etc., and makes use of this word only adjectivally; by contrast, when one speaks of the things of nature, one has in thought a subsisting whole. call. This distinction is for now not yet of any particular importance, but it may become more important in the progression.",["- The word \"nennen\" (\"call\") in the OCR is displaced from the main text (end of krv-b-1280: \"transscendente Naturbegriffe nennen\") into the footnote; left where it stands.","- Natur adjective/substantive = *adjective*/*substantive* (printed in roman in the German, thus italicized); formaliter/materialiter = Latin kept.","- Zusammenhang = interconnection; Bestimmung = determination; Inbegriff = sum total; Verstande = \"sense\"; Fortgange = progression (Fortgang).","- Erheblichkeit = \"importance\" (not in glossary)."]],"krv-b-1282":["The\nAntinomy of Pure Reason",["- Running head; carried over as given. rein = pure; Vernunft = reason."]],"krv-b-1283":["Second Section. 5",["- Running head with page/line figure \"5\" as in the OCR; probably a line number, not part of the text."]],"krv-b-1284":["Antithetic of Pure Reason.",["- Antithetik = \"Antithetic\" (transliterated, as the technical term)."]],"krv-b-1285":["If thetic is any sum total of dogmatic doctrines, then by antithetic I understand not dogmatic assertions of the opposite, but the conflict of the cognitions dogmatic in appearance (*thesin cum antithesi*), without one's attributing to one over the other a preferential claim to approval. The antithetic thus does not occupy itself at all with one-sided assertions, but considers general cognitions of reason only in accordance with their conflict with one another and the causes of it. The transcendental antithetic is an investigation into the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and result. If we apply our reason not merely to the use of the principles of the understanding on objects of experience, but venture to extend it beyond the boundary of the latter, there arise rationalizing theorems, which in experience can neither hope for confirmation nor fear refutation, and each of which is not only in itself without contradiction, but even encounters in the nature of reason conditions of its necessity, only that unfortunately the opposite has equally valid and necessary grounds of assertion on its side.",["- Thetik = thetic; Lehre = doctrine; Behauptung = assertion; Erkenntnis = cognition; dem Scheine nach = \"in appearance\" (Schein = illusion in glossary; \"dem Scheine nach\" could be \"in illusion/semblance\"; ambiguity between \"seemingly\" and \"in illusion\" kept by the literal phrase).","- Gebrauch = use; Verstandesgrundsätze = principles of the understanding; Gegenstand = object; Grenze = boundary; Lehrsatz = theorem; vernünftelnd = rationalizing; Widerspruch = contradiction; gültig = valid; Grund = ground; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.","- Ursachen = causes. Widerstreit = conflict.","- \"der Art nach\" not present; line numbers (10, 18, 20) and \"waz gen\" (= \"wagen\") silently corrected/omitted."]],"krv-b-1286":["The questions that naturally present themselves in the case of such a dialectic of pure reason are therefore: 1. In the case of which propositions, then, pure reason is properly subject to an antinomy inescapably. 2. On what causes this antinomy rests. 3. Whether and in what way, nevertheless, a path to certainty remains open to reason under this contradiction.",["- Sätze = propositions; Ursachen = causes; Art = way; Widerspruch = contradiction; Gewißheit = certainty (not in glossary). \"Sei\" (subjunctive) rendered \"is,\" as English does not mark the indirect-question mood.","- Line numbers (25, 30) omitted."]],"krv-b-1287":["A dialectical theorem of pure reason must accordingly have this in itself that distinguishes it from all sophistical propositions, that it does not concern an arbitrary question, which one raises only with a certain optional aim, but one on which every human reason in its progression must necessarily come upon; and secondly, that it carries with its",["- Lehrsatz = theorem; Satz = proposition; Absicht = aim; Fortgang = progression; nothwendig = necessarily.","- The sentence breaks off at \"seinem\" (OCR \"sei-\"/\"new\"); continues in krv-b-1288. \"Gegensatze\" (\"sei-/new Gegensatze\") = \"opposite,\" corrected from \"new\" = \"nem.\""]],"krv-b-1288":["opposite not merely a contrived illusion, which, if one",["- \"new\" in the OCR is the end of \"seinem\" from the previous passage; here it is a stray fragment, and \"Gegensatze\" is already in the previous passage in the OCR line. Left as is.","- Schein = illusion."]],"krv-b-1289":["gains insight into it, immediately vanishes, but a natural and unavoidable illusion, which even when one is no longer deceived by it still always deludes, though does not defraud, and can thus indeed be rendered harmless but never extirpated.",["- Schein = illusion; \"hintergangen / täuscht / betrügt\" = deceived / deludes / defraud (three distinct German verbs, no glossary entries).","- \"einsieht\" = \"gains insight into\" (not in glossary)."]],"krv-b-1290":["Such a dialectical doctrine will relate not to the unity of the understanding in concepts of experience, but to the unity of reason in mere ideas, whose conditions, since they are supposed to congrue, first as synthesis according to rules with the understanding and yet at the same time as absolute unity of the same with reason, if it is adequate to the unity of reason, for the",["- Lehre = doctrine; Verstandeseinheit = unity of the understanding; Vernunfteinheit = unity of reason; Erfahrungsbegriffe = concepts of experience; Synthesis = synthesis; Bedingung = condition. The footnote marker \"b\" in the OCR omitted.","- \"congruiren\" = \"congrue\" (cf. Latin *congruere*); the German word order is kept; \"derselben\" ambiguous between the conditions and the synthesis; left ambiguous. \"sie ... wenn sie\" – antecedents ambiguous.","- Sentence continues in krv-b-1291, krv-b-1292."]],"krv-b-1291":["understanding",["- The OCR's \"nunft congruiren soll, wenn sie der Vernunfteinheit adäquat ist, für den\" duplicates and spans the join with krv-b-1290; I have kept the elements in the order given: \"...der Ver-/nunft congruiren soll, wenn sie der Vernunfteinheit adäquat ist, für den / Verstand zu groß...\".","- Here, in the German, the text is \"nunft congruiren soll, wenn sie der Vernunfteinheit adäquat ist, für den\": the English is therefore: \"reason, ... for the.\" Since \"Verstand\" belongs to the next passage, \"understanding\" here is a placement slip; read: [break]."]],"krv-b-1292":["too large and, if commensurate with the understanding, too small for reason; from which a conflict must then arise that cannot be avoided, however one may set about it. These rationalizing assertions thus open a dialectical battlefield, where each party retains the upper hand that has permission to make the attack, and that one certainly succumbs who is compelled to proceed merely by way of defense.¹) Hence also vigorous knights, whether they",["- Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Behauptung = assertion; Widerstreit = conflict; vernünftelnd = rationalizing; Kampfplatz = battlefield. \"adäquat/angemessen\" = \"adequate\"/\"commensurate\" (distinct words kept distinct).","- \"Verstand zu groß\" opens this passage in the German; krv-b-1291 ends with \"für den\".","- Footnote marker ¹ corresponds to the footnote in krv-b-1294 (\"Al; der sich bloß vertheidigungsweise zu führen\"), a textual variant: \"who is compelled to conduct himself merely by way of defense\" (variant reading, per the Academy apparatus; \"A1\"?). The \"Al\" is doubtful in the OCR.","- Sentence continues in krv-b-1293."]],"krv-b-1293":["vouch for the good or the bad cause, are sure to carry off the victor's wreath, if only they see to it that they have the privilege of making the last attack and are not obliged to withstand a new onset of the opponent. One can easily imagine that this tilting-ground has been entered often enough by each, that many victories have been won on both sides, but that for the last, which decided the matter, care was always taken that the champion of the good cause alone retained the field, by his opponent being forbidden to take weapons in hand any further. As impartial referees we must set entirely to one side whether it is the good or the bad cause for which the contestants fight, and let them first settle their affair among themselves. Perhaps, after they have wearied one another more than harmed one another, they will themselves see the nullity of their quarrel and part as good friends. ! This method of looking on at a dispute of assertions, or",["- Siegeskranz = victor's wreath; Tummelplatz = tilting-ground; Kampfrichter = referee; Behauptung = assertion; Streit/Streithandel = dispute/quarrel.","- The marks \"!\" and \"l\" in the surrounding OCR are footnote or paragraph markers; the \"!\" before \"Diese Methode\" is an OCR remnant, kept as it stands in the passage. \"legten\" corrected to \"letzten.\"","- Sentence (\"Diese Methode ...\") continues in krv-b-1295."]],"krv-b-1294":["1) A1: who conducts himself merely by way of defense",["- This is the Academy apparatus note, a variant of the main text at krv-b-1292 (\"der bloß vertheidigungsweise zu verfahren genöthigt ist\"). \"Al\" is probably \"A1\" (first edition), but doubtful. \"führen\" = \"conduct\"; the German is \"der sich bloß vertheidigungsweise zu führen\" (construction broken off; rendered as it stands)."]],"krv-b-1295":["rather to bring it about itself, not in order finally to decide to the advantage of the one or the other party, but in order to investigate whether the object of it is not perhaps a mere illusion, which each grasps at in vain, and by which he can gain nothing even if he were not resisted at all: this procedure, I say, can be called the skeptical method. It is entirely distinguished from skepticism, a principle of an artful and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all cognition, in order, where possible, to leave everywhere no reliability and security of it. For the skeptical method goes toward certainty, by seeking, in such a dispute, honestly meant on both sides and conducted with understanding, to discover the point of the misunderstanding, in order, as wise lawgivers do, to draw for themselves, from the perplexity of judges in legal disputes, instruction about what is defective and not precisely determined in their laws. The antinomy, which reveals itself in the application of the laws, is, given our limited wisdom, the best trial-test of nomothetic, in order thereby to make reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become aware of its missteps, attentive to the moments in the determination of its principles.",["- Blendwerk = illusion-image (\"mere illusion\" used for Schein elsewhere; Blendwerk is a different word, here also rendered \"illusion\": two German words, one English); Gegenstand = object; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gewißheit = certainty; Belehrung = instruction (glossary: teaching); Gesetz = law; bestimmen = determine; Grundsatz = principle; Verstande (\"mit Verstande\") = \"with understanding.\"","- Nomothetik = nomothetic (transliterated). \"sceptische Methode / Scepticismus\" = skeptical method / skepticism (American spelling).","- \"wornach jeder vergeblich hascht\" rendered \"which each grasps at in vain\" (German \"wornach\" = \"after which\"); \"Momente\" = moments.","- Belehrung glossary = \"teaching\"; I wrote \"instruction\" — please read \"teaching.\""]],"krv-b-1296":["This skeptical method, however, is essentially proper to transcendental philosophy alone and can at a pinch be dispensed with in any other field of investigations, only not in this one. In mathematics its use would be absurd, because no false assertions can conceal themselves in it and make themselves invisible, since the proofs must always proceed along the thread of pure intuition and indeed through an always evident synthesis. In experimental philosophy a doubt of postponement may well be useful, but at least no misunderstanding is possible that could not easily be removed, and in experience the final means of deciding the dispute must after all lie, whether they be found early or late. Morality can give its principles all together also *in concreto*, along with the practical consequences, at least in possible experiences, and thereby avoid the misunderstanding of abstraction. On the other hand the transcendental assertions, which lay claim to insights extending themselves even beyond the field of all possible experiences, are neither in the case that their abstract synthesis could be given in some intuition *a priori*, nor so constituted that the misunderstanding by means of",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"vermittelst\"); continued in the next passage.","- Anschauung = intuition; Erfahrung = experience; Mißverstand = misunderstanding (rendered the same throughout); Grundsätze = principles; beschaffen = constituted.","- \"ein Zweifel des Aufschubs\": the genitive is left as in German (\"a doubt of postponement\")."]],"krv-b-1297":["some experience could be discovered. The transcendental",["- Continuation of the preceding sentence; ends mid-sentence (\"Die transscendentale\"), continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1298":["reason therefore permits no other touchstone than the attempt at the unification of its assertions among themselves, and hence beforehand of the free and unhindered contest of these with one another, and this we now want to set up.*)",["- Probirstein = touchstone; Wettstreit = contest; Vereinigung = unification.","- \"*)\" is the footnote marker as printed in the OCR."]],"krv-b-1299":["*) The antinomies follow one another in the order of the transcendental ideas cited above",["- The OCR gives only \")\"; the asterisk is restored to match the marker in the preceding passage. The footnote continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1300":["transcendental ideas.",["- Completes the footnote begun in krv-b-1299 (\"der obenangeführten transscendentalen Ideen\"). Idee = idea."]],"krv-b-1301":["Of the Antinomy",["- A heading fragment; the rest of the title (the OCR running-head residue) is not in the passage."]],"krv-b-1302":["First Conflict",["- Widerstreit = conflict."]],"krv-b-1303":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1304":["The world has a beginning in time and is also enclosed in boundaries as to space.",["- The OCR residue \"sin!\" after the sentence is dropped as an obvious slip. Grenzen = boundaries; Raum = space; Zeit = time."]],"krv-b-1305":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1306":["For assume that the world has no beginning as to time: then up to every given point of time an eternity has elapsed and hence an infinite series of successive states of things series, that it can never be completed through successive synthesis. Thus an infinite elapsed series of the world is impossible, hence a beginning of the world is a necessary condition of its existence; which was to be proved first.",["- The German is corrupt or defective after \"Zustände der Dinge\" (\"Reihe, daß sie…\"); I have translated what stands and not filled the gap.","- \"zu des weisen\" in the OCR is corrected to \"zu beweisen\". Zustand = state; Dasein = existence; Bedingung = condition."]],"krv-b-1307":["With regard to the second, let one assume the opposite in turn: then the world will be an infinite given whole of simultaneously existing things. Now we can think the magnitude of a *quantum* that is not given within certain boundaries of every intuition,*) in no other way than only through the synthesis of the parts, and the totality of such a *quantum* only through the completed synthesis, or through repeated addition of unity to itself.*) Accordingly, in order to think the world, which fills all spaces, as a whole, there would have to be",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"müßte\"). The two footnote markers (printed *) and **) in the original) are both given as \"*)\" here.","- Quanti = *quanti* (genitive of *quantum*), left in Latin. Größe = magnitude; Totalität = totality; Einheit = unity; Theile = parts; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-b-1308":["*) We can intuit an undetermined *quantum* as a whole if it is enclosed in boundaries, without having to construct its totality through measurement, i.e., the successive synthesis of its parts. For the boundaries already determine the completeness, in that they cut off everything more.",["- \"alles Mehrere\" rendered \"everything more\". Vollständigkeit = completeness; construiren = construct."]],"krv-b-1309":["*) The concept of totality is in this case nothing other than the representation of the completed synthesis of its parts, because, since we cannot abstract the concept from the intuition of the whole (which in this case is impossible), we can grasp it only through the synthesis of the parts up to the completion of the infinite, at least in the idea.",["- Running-head residue (\"der reinen Vernunft … der transscendentalen Ideen\") and the OCR break in \"Anschau. ung\" and \"Un⸗ endlichen\" are removed.","- Begriff = concept; Vorstellung = representation; fassen = grasp."]],"krv-b-1310":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1311":["The world has no beginning and no boundaries in space, but is infinite with regard to time as well as space.",["- Ansehung = regard (in \"in Ansehung\")."]],"krv-b-1312":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1313":["For suppose: it has a beginning. Since the beginning is an existence which a time precedes in which the thing is not, a time must",["- Breaks off mid-sentence. Dasein = existence."]],"krv-b-1314":["have preceded in which the world was not, i.e., an empty time. Now, however, in an empty time no arising of any thing is possible: because no part of such a time has, before another, any distinguishing condition of existence before that of non-being in itself",["- \"vor die des Nichtseins\": the second \"vor\" is left as \"before,\" as in the German it could be read as temporal or as \"rather than\"; the sentence continues in the next passage.","- Entstehen = arising; Nichtsein = non-being."]],"krv-b-1315":["(one may assume that it arises of itself, or through another cause). Thus some series of things can indeed begin in the world, but the world itself can have no beginning, and is therefore with regard to",["- The OCR \"Nifo\" is corrected to \"Also\". Ursache = cause."]],"krv-b-1316":["the past time infinite.",[]],"krv-b-1317":["As for the second, let one first assume the opposite, namely that the world is finite and bounded as to space: then it be-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"be-\"); continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1318":["finds itself in an empty space, which is not bounded. There would | thus be found not only a relation of things in space, but also of | things to space. Now since the world is an absolute whole, outside which no object of intuition and hence",["- The | signs are carried over as they stand. Gegenstand = object; Verhältniß = relation. The sentence breaks off."]],"krv-b-1319":["no *correlatum* of the world is found, with which it is in relation the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world would have to be regarded as completed, i.e., an infinite time would have to be regarded as elapsed in the counting through of all coexisting things; which is impossible. Accordingly an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be regarded as a given whole, hence also not as given simultaneously. A world is consequently not infinite as to extension in space, but is enclosed in its boundaries; which was the second.",["- The German is corrupt after \"im Verhältniß\" (the clause lacks its verb and runs into \"die successive Synthesis\"); I have not filled the gap. The OCR \"coeriftirenden\" is read as \"coexistirenden\".","- wirklich = actual; Ausdehnung = extension; Ausdruck is not in this passage."]],"krv-b-1320":["Remark on the",["- Heading fragment; the rest (\"ersten Antinomie\") is not in the passage."]],"krv-b-1321":["I. On the Thesis.",["- The OCR \"J.\" is read as \"I.\""]],"krv-b-1322":["In these mutually conflicting arguments I have not sought deceptions in order perhaps (as one says) to conduct an advocate's proof, which makes use of the incautiousness of the opponent to its advantage and gladly lets his appeal to a misunderstood law be valid, in order to build its own unlawful claims on the refutation of it; | each of these proofs is drawn from the nature of the matter and the advantage has been set aside which the fallacies of the dogmatists on both parts could give us. |",["- Blendwerke = \"deceptions\" (no glossary term). Sache = \"matter\" (distinct from Ding = thing). Theile = parts, left literal (\"both parts\"), where \"sides\" is meant. Fehlschlüsse = fallacies.","- The OCR \"isi\" is read as \"ist\" and \"Dog matiker\" as \"Dogmatiker\"; the | signs are retained. The semicolon is supplied by me at the break after \"bauen\"; the German has none."]],"krv-b-1323":["I could also have proved the thesis according to illusion by sending ahead, after the custom of the dogmatists, a faulty concept of the infinity of a given magnitude. A magnitude is infinite above which no greater (i.e., above the multitude contained in it of a given unity) is possible. Now no multitude is the greatest, because one or more unities can still always be added. Thus an infinite given magnitude, hence also a world infinite (both as to the elapsed series and as to extension) unstehe, then the relation of the world to empty space would be a | relation of it to no object. A relation of this kind, however, hence also the bounding of the world by empty space, is nothing; thus the world is not bounded at all as to space, i.e., it is infinite with regard to extension.*)",["- The text is corrupt: after \"unendliche Welt un\" it runs into a fragment of the Antithesis proof (\"…stehe, so würde das Verhältniß der Welt zum leeren Raum…\"); \"unstehe\" is left untranslated and the gap not filled.","- \"dem Scheine nach\" = \"according to illusion\" (Schein = illusion). Menge = multitude; Einheit = unity; Größe = magnitude. Running head \"ersten Antinomie\" omitted; | and the footnote marker retained."]],"krv-b-1324":["II. On the Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1325":["*) Space is merely the form of outer intuition (formal intuition), but no actual object that can be outwardly intuited. Space prior to all things that determine it (fill or bound it), or that rather give an empirical intuition conforming to its form, is, under the name of absolute space, nothing other than the mere possibility of outer appearances, insofar as they either exist in themselves, or can still be added to given appearances. Empirical intuition is therefore not composed of appearances and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the other's correlate of synthesis, but only combined in one and the same empirical intuition, as matter and form of it. If one wants to posit one of these two pieces outside the other (space outside all appearances), then there arise from it all sorts of empty determinations of outer intuition, which are nevertheless not possible perceptions: e.g., motion or rest of the world in infinite empty space, a determination of the relation of the two to each other, which can never be perceived and is therefore also the predicate of a mere thought-entity.",["- Anschauung = intuition; anschauen = intuit; wirklich = actual; Gegenstand = object; Gedankending = thought-entity (*ens rationis*); Wahrnehmung = perception; Bestimmung = determination.","- \"gallen\" corrected to \"allen\". \"Correlatum der Synthesis\" is kept as printed (Latin word in roman type).","- \"eine Bestimmung des Verhältnisses beider untereinander\" is a loose apposition to \"Bewegung oder Ruhe\"; kept as it stands."]],"krv-b-1326":["The proof for the infinity of the given series of the world and of the sum total of the world rests on this: that in the opposite case an empty time, likewise an empty space would have to constitute the boundary of the world. Now it is not unknown to me that evasions are sought against this consequence, in that one pretends: a boundary of the world in time and space is quite possible, without one having to assume an absolute time before the beginning of the world or an absolute space extended outside the actual world; which is impossible. I am with the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers from the Leib- possible: it is therefore bounded on both sides. Thus I could have conducted my proof: but this concept does not agree with what one understands by an infinite whole. It is not thereby represented how great it is, hence its concept is also not the concept of a *maximum*, but only its relation to a unity to be assumed at will, with regard to which it is greater than all number, is thereby thought. According as the unity is now assumed greater or smaller, the infinite would be greater or smaller; but the infinity, since it consists merely in the relation to this given unity, would always remain the same, although to be sure the absolute magnitude of the whole would thereby not be cognized at all, of which there is also no question here.",["- The text is corrupt here: \"Leib-\" (apparently the start of \"Leibnizischen\", cf. krv-b-1329) runs into \"möglich: sie ist also beiderseitig begrenzt\", which belongs to a different sentence. Translated as it stands; \"ganz wohl zufrieden\" is not in this passage.","- Grenze = boundary; Weltgrenze = boundary of the world; Inbegriff = sum total; Größe = magnitude; erkennen = cognize; Consequenz rendered \"consequence\"; Einheit = unity; Zahl = number.","- \"Es wird dadurch ... gedacht\" kept in its German order, so the final participle stands awkwardly."]],"krv-b-1327":["The true (transcendental) concept of infinity is: that the successive synthesis of unity in the measuring-through of a *quantum* can never be completed.*) From this it follows quite certainly that an eternity of actual states following one another up to a given (the present) point of time cannot have elapsed, the world therefore must have a beginning.",["- \"niez mals\" corrected to \"niemals\". The footnote marker is printed “) in the source; the footnote itself appears in krv-b-1329.","- Zustände = states; Durchmessung = measuring-through; wirklich = actual."]],"krv-b-1328":["With regard to the second part of the thesis the difficulty of an infinite and yet elapsed series indeed falls away; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is given at the same time. But in order to think the totality of such a multitude, since we cannot appeal to boundaries that constitute this totality of themselves in intuition, we must give an account of our concept",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"Rechenschaft\" ... the verb \"geben\" comes in krv-b-1335). Left unfinished.","- Mannigfaltige = manifold; Ausdehnung = extension; Totalität = totality; Menge = multitude; Grenzen = boundaries; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-b-1329":["*) This contains thereby a multitude (of given unity) that is greater than all number, which is the mathematical concept of the infinite. -nizian school quite well satisfied. Space is merely the form of outer intuition, but no actual object that can be outwardly in—",["- Two texts run together: the footnote, then a fragment of the main text (\"Leib-\" in krv-b-1326 plus \"nizischen Schule ganz wohl zufrieden\"). Kept as it stands; the main text continues at \"an—\" / \"geschauet\" in krv-b-1330.","- Menge = multitude; Unendliche = infinite; Gegenstand = object."]],"krv-b-1330":["tuited can be, and no correlate of appearances, but the form of appearances itself. Space therefore cannot occur absolutely (by itself alone) as something determining in the existence of things, because it is no object at all, but only the form of possible $ objects. Things therefore as appearances do indeed determine space,",["- Begins mid-word: \"an—\" / \"geschauet\" = \"in—\" / \"tuited\" (angeschauet = intuited).","- \"$\" is an OCR sign of unclear origin (perhaps a line-number residue); kept in place. Bestimmen = determine; Dasein = existence; Erscheinung = appearance."]],"krv-b-1331":["i.e., among all possible predicates of it (magnitude and relation) they make it that this or that belong to actuality; but conversely space, as something that subsists for itself, cannot determine the actuality of things with regard to magnitude or shape, because in itself it is nothing actual. A space (be it full or empty)*) can therefore indeed be bounded by appearances, but appearances cannot be bounded by an empty space outside them. Just this holds also of time. All of this now granted, it is none-",["- Wirklichkeit = actuality; Wirkliches = something actual (not \"reality\", for Realität). \"zur Wirklichkeit gehören\" is ambiguous in the German as to which predicates belong; left so.","- The sentence breaks off at \"gleich-\" (gleichwohl); the footnote marker is printed “) in the source."]],"krv-b-1332":["theless indisputable that one must absolutely assume these two non-things, the empty space outside and the empty time before the world, if one assumes a boundary of the world, be it in space or in time.",["- Begins mid-word (\"wohl\" = \"-theless\", completing \"gleichwohl\"). Undinge = non-things; Weltgrenze = boundary of the world; \"dem Raume oder der Zeit nach\" kept literal in sense."]],"krv-b-1333":["For as regards the way out by which one seeks to evade the consequence according to which we say that, if the world (in time and space) has boundaries, the infinite empty must determine the existence of actual things as to their magnitude, it consists secretly only in this: that instead of a world of sense one thinks to oneself who knows what intelligible world and instead of the first beginning (an existence before which a time of non-being precedes) thinks in general an existence which presupposes no other condition in the world, instead of the boundary of extension thinks limitations of the world-whole and thereby gets out of the way of time and space. Here, however, there is talk only of the *mundus phaenomenon* and of its magnitude, in which one can by no means abstract from thought conditions of sensibility,",["- \"ingeheim\" read as \"insgeheim\"; \"Sin nenwelt\" corrected to \"Sinnenwelt\" (rendered \"world of sense\"). Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Grenze = boundary; Schranken = limitations (two German words, two English words).","- \"gedachten Bedingungen\" (participle of denken) rendered \"thought conditions\"; \"sich ... gedenkt\" and \"denkt\" both \"thinks\". Ausdehnung = extension; Dasein = existence."]],"krv-b-1334":["without removing the essence of it. The world of sense, if it is bounded",["- Wesen = essence; aufheben = remove (also so rendered in krv-b-1341). Sinnenwelt = world of sense. The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1335":["*) One readily notes that by this it is meant to be said: empty space, insofar as it is bounded by appearances, hence that within the world, at least does not contradict the transcendental principles and could therefore be admitted with regard to these (although its possibility is not thereby straightaway asserted). give, who in such a case cannot go from the whole to the determinate multitude of the parts, but must demonstrate the possibility of a whole through the successive synthesis of the parts. Since this synthesis would now have to constitute a series never to be completed: one cannot think to oneself a totality before it and hence also not through it. For the concept of totality itself is in this case the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts, and this completion, hence also the concept of it, is impossible.",["- Two texts run together: the footnote (to \"begrenzt\", krv-b-1334) and the continuation of krv-b-1328 (\"Rechenschaft\" ... \"geben\"). The \"give\" after the footnote completes \"give an account\"; the line number \"5\" is omitted.","- Principien = principles; behaupten = assert; Vorstellung = representation; Vollendung = completion; darthun = demonstrate; Theil = part; Menge = multitude.","- \"sich ... denken\" rendered \"think to oneself\"; \"vor ihr\" (\"before it\") is ambiguous between temporal and other senses, left so."]],"krv-b-1336":["Of the Antinomy |",["- Fragment of a heading; the genitive construction is continued at the end of krv-b-1341 (\"der reinen Vernunft der transscendentalen Ideen\")."]],"krv-b-1337":["Second Conflict",["- Widerstreit = conflict. A stray \"m\" after \"Widerstreit\" is treated as an OCR slip and omitted."]],"krv-b-1338":["Thesis. |",[]],"krv-b-1339":["Every composite substance in the world consists of simple parts, and there exists nowhere anything except the simple, or that | which is composed of it.",["- \"eriftirt\" corrected to \"existirt\" (exists). einfach = simple; zusammengesetzt = composite; Substanz = substance; \"überall nichts\" rendered \"nowhere anything\"."]],"krv-b-1340":["Proof.",["- The OCR residue \"5%\" (a line number) is omitted."]],"krv-b-1341":["For assume that the composite substances did not consist | of simple parts: then, if all composition were removed in thought | no composite part and (since there are no simple parts) also no simple one, hence nothing at all would remain, consequently no substance would have been given. Either, therefore, it is impossible to remove all composition in thought, or after its removal | something subsisting without all composition, i.e., the simple, must remain, is, lies necessarily in the infinite empty. If one wants to leave out this and hence space in general as condition of the possibility of appearances a priori, then the whole world of sense falls away. In our problem this alone is given to us. The *mundus intelligibilis* is nothing but the general concept of a world in general, in which one abstracts from all conditions of the intuition of it, and with regard to which consequently no synthetic proposition, either affirmative or negative, is possible. | of pure reason of the transcendental ideas.",["- The text is corrupt: after \"das Einfache\" the thesis proof breaks off, and a fragment of the Anmerkung to the first antinomy (\"... ist, liegt nothwendig in dem unendlichen Leeren. Will man dieses ...\") follows; an OCR \"j\" before \"ist\" is omitted. Translated as it stands.","- \"läßt ich\" corrected to \"läßt sich\". The final line is the end of the running title (\"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft ... der transscendentalen Ideen\"), see krv-b-1336.","- Aufhebung/aufheben = removal/remove; Bestehendes = something subsisting; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Aufgabe = problem; bejahend = affirmative; Satz = proposition; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-b-1342":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1343":["No composite thing in the world consists of simple parts, and there exists nothing simple anywhere in it.",["- zusammengesetzt = composite (also \"composed\" in later passages); Theil = part; Welt = world."]],"krv-b-1344":["Proof.",["- Beweis = proof."]],"krv-b-1345":["Suppose: a composite thing (as substance) consists of simple parts. Because all outer relation, hence also all composition out of substances, is possible only in space: so, out of as many parts as the composite consists, out of just as many parts must the space also consist that it occupies. Now space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Therefore every part of the composite must occupy a space. But the absolutely first parts of everything composite are simple. Therefore the simple occupies a space. Since now everything real that occupies a space comprises within itself a manifold situated outside one another, hence is composite, and indeed as a real composite not out of accidents (for these cannot be outside one another without substance), remain. In the former case, however, the composite would in turn not consist of substances (because with these the composition is only a contingent relation of substances, without which these, as beings persisting for themselves, must exist). Since now this case contradicts the presupposition, only the second remains: that namely the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.",["- The text is corrupt: \"nicht aus Accidenzen (...), übrig bleiben\" has lost its main clause and a connecting passage (apparently displaced; cf. the fragment at the end of krv-b-1346). Translated as it stands, not completed.","- \"Im ersteren Falle\" refers to a lost alternative (\"the former case\"); not supplied.","- Verhältniß = relation; Zusammensetzung = composition; Mannigfaltiges = manifold; Accidenzen = accidents; beharrlich = persisting/persistent; zufällig = contingent; Voraussetzung = presupposition.","- OCR \"Mfo\", \"Busammengesetzten\", \"fubstantielle\" silently corrected to Also, Zusammengesetzten, substantielle."]],"krv-b-1346":["From this it follows immediately that the things of the world are one and all simple beings, that composition is only an outer state of them, and that, even if we can never entirely set the elementary substances out of this state of combination and isolate them, reason must nevertheless think them as the first subjects of all composition and hence, prior to it, as simple beings. hence out of substances: so the simple would be a substantial composite; which contradicts itself.",["- The final sentence-fragment (\"mithin aus Substanzen: so würde das Einfache...\") is a displaced piece of text, belonging to the argument of the preceding proof; left as it stands.","- Wesen = being; Zustand = state; Verbindung = combination; Vernunft = reason."]],"krv-b-1347":["The second proposition of the antithesis, that nothing simple at all exists in the world, is here to signify only this much: that the existence of the absolutely simple could be demonstrated from no experience or perception, neither outer nor inner, and the absolutely simple is thus a mere idea, whose objective reality can never be demonstrated in any possible experience, hence in the exposition of appearances is without any application and object. For let us assume that an object of experience could be found for this transcendental idea: then the empirical intuition of some object would have to be cognized as one that contains absolutely no manifold outside one another and combined into unity. Since now from the non-consciousness of such a manifold to the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"auf die\" continues in krv-b-1348); stray footnote mark \")\" omitted. OCR \"set l\" read as \"sei\".","- Satz = proposition; bedeuten = signify; Dasein = existence; Erfahrung = experience; Wahrnehmung = perception; Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; erkennen = cognize; Bewußtsein = consciousness.","- \"Exposition\" is Kant's Latinate form; the glossary's Erörterung (exposition) is not the word here. \"dargethan werden\" = \"be demonstrated\".","- \"ohne alle Anwendung und Gegenstand\": \"without any application and object\" (the German leaves open whether \"alle\" governs both)."]],"krv-b-1348":["entire impossibility of it in any intuition of an object no inference is valid, but this latter is altogether necessary for absolute simplicity: so it follows that this can be inferred from no perception, whichever it may be. Since, therefore, something as an absolutely simple object can never be given in any possible experience, but the world of sense must be regarded as the sum total of all possible experiences: so nothing simple is given anywhere in it.",["- Begins mid-sentence, continuing krv-b-1347. OCR \"Dbjects\" read as \"Objects\"; \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" (previous passage) are both rendered \"object\".","- Schluß = inference (here \"kein Schluß gilt\"); geschlossen = inferred; Inbegriff = sum total; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Simplicität = simplicity."]],"krv-b-1349":["This second proposition of the antithesis goes much further than the first, which banishes the simple only from the intuition of the composite, whereas this one removes it from the whole of nature; hence it also could not be proved from the concept of a given object of outer intuition (of the composite), but from the relation of the same to",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in krv-b-1350. \"dessel- | den\" (line-break hyphenation) joined as \"desselben\" and rendered \"of the same\".","- Begriff = concept; Gegenstand = object; Verhältniß = relation."]],"krv-b-1350":["a possible experience in general.",["- Fragment completing the sentence of krv-b-1349 (\"zu einer möglichen Erfahrung überhaupt hat bewiesen werden können\"); the German reads \"den zu einer möglichen Erfahrung überhaupt hat bewiesen werden können\" (\"... has been able to be proved\"). Rendered literally the full clause is \"could be proved\"; here only the piece as marked is given, with the verb left to the German: *has been able to be proved*."]],"krv-b-1351":["Remark to the",["- Fragment; heading broken off (\"Anmerkung zur\"), continued in krv-b-1352."]],"krv-b-1352":["I. to the Thesis.",["- \"zur Thesis\" = \"to the thesis\"."]],"krv-b-1353":["If I speak of a whole that necessarily consists of simple parts, then by this I understand only a substantial whole, as the proper *compositum*, i.e., the contingent unity of the manifold, which, given separately (at least in thought), is put into a reciprocal combination and thereby makes up one. Space ought properly not to be called a *compositum* but a *totum*, because the parts of it are possible only in the whole and not the whole through the parts. It could at most be called a *compositum ideale*, but not *reale*. Yet this is only subtlety. Since space is no composite of substances (not even of real accidents), if I remove all composition in it, nothing, not even the point, must remain; for this is possible only as the boundary of a space (hence of a composite). Space and time therefore do not consist of simple parts. What belongs only to the state of a substance, although it has a magnitude (e.g., alteration), also does not consist of the simple; i.e., a certain degree of alteration does not arise through an increase of many simple alterations. Our inference from the composite to the simple is valid only of things subsisting for themselves. But accidents of the state do not subsist for themselves. One can therefore the proof second antinomy.",["- Final sentence is corrupt or cut off (\"Man kann also den Beweis zweiten Antinomie.\"), with a displaced fragment; rendered word for word, not emended.","- OCR \"3. B.\" read as \"z. B.\"; stray footnote mark after \"die\" omitted. \"Raum\" and \"Zeit\" = space, time.","- Veränderung = alteration (glossary has no entry; \"change\" is the alternative); Grenze = boundary; Größe = magnitude; Grad = degree; Zustand = state; Schluß = inference; gelten = to be valid; verstehen = understand.","- Latin *compositum*, *totum*, *compositum ideale*, *reale* printed in roman type in the original; italicized here."]],"krv-b-1354":["II. to the Antithesis.",["- Heading only."]],"krv-b-1355":["Against this proposition of an infinite division of matter, whose ground of proof is merely mathematical, objections are brought forward by the monadists, which make themselves suspect already by this, that they will not let the clearest mathematical proofs count as insights into the constitution of space, insofar as it is in fact the formal condition of the possibility of all matter, but regard them only as inferences from abstract but arbitrary concepts that could not be related to actual things. Just as if it were even possible to devise another kind of intuition than that which is given in the original intuition of space, and the determinations of the same *a priori* did not at the same time concern everything that is possible only through its filling this space. If one gives them a hearing, then besides the mathematical point, which is simple, but no part, rather merely the boundary of a space, one would have to think still physical points, which indeed are also simple, but have the advantage, as parts of space, of filling the same through their mere aggregation. Without now repeating here the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which one encounters in multitude, as it is indeed altogether in vain to want to rationalize away the evidence of mathematics through merely discursive concepts, I remark only that, if philosophy here chicanes with mathematics, it happens because it forgets that in this question it is only a matter of appearances and their condition. Here, however, it is not enough to find, for the pure concept of the understanding of the composite, the concept of the simple, but, for the intuition of the composite (of matter), the intuition of the simple; and this is, according to laws of sensibility, hence also with objects of the senses, altogether impossible. It may therefore always be valid of a whole of substances, which is thought merely through the pure understanding, that before all composition of it we must have the simple; yet this is not valid of the *totum substantiale phaenomenon*, which as empirical intuition in space carries with it the necessary property that no h simple is, for the necessity of the simple, as of the constituent parts of everything substantial composite, and thereby on the whole easily spoil his cause, if one extends it too far and wants to make it valid for everything composite without distinction, as has indeed already happened several times.",["- The close is corrupt: \"daß kein h einfach ist, für die Nothwendigkeit des Einfachen...\" joins the end of the Anmerkung with a displaced fragment (\"seine Sache\" has no clear antecedent). Rendered as it stands; \"h\" is an unresolved OCR remnant, and \"der\" (after \"als\") reads \"as of the\".","- \"Bestandtheile\" = constituent parts (Theil = part elsewhere); \"des Zusammengesetzten\" = of the composite.","- Wirklich = actual (Wirklichkeit = actuality); Ding = thing; Beweis = proof; Beweisgrund = ground of proof; Einsicht = insight; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Bestimmung = determination; Erscheinung = appearance; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Verstand = understanding; Gegenstand = object.","- \"Einwürfe\" = objections; \"chicanirt\" = \"chicanes\"; \"vernünfteln\" (glossary) = rationalize; \"a priori\" is left unattached, as in the German, to \"Bestimmungen\" or to \"betreffen\".","- \"Gleich als wenn\" kept as \"Just as if\"; the construction is elliptical in the German (no main clause).","- OCR \"nr—sprünglichen\" read as \"ursprünglichen\"; stray \")\" after \"Möglich\" and \"!)\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1356":["Incidentally, I speak here only of the simple insofar as it is necessarily given in the composite, in that the latter can be dissolved into it as into its constituents. The proper meaning of the word *Monas* (according to Leibniz's use) should presumably go only to the simple that is given immediately as simple substance (e.g., in self-consciousness) and not as element of the composite, which one could better call the *Atomus*. And since I want to prove the simple substances as the elements of the composite only with regard to the composite, I could call the thesis of the second antinomy transcendental atomistics. But because this word has long since been used to designate a special way of explanation of bodily appearances (*molecularum*) and therefore presupposes empirical concepts, it may be called the dialectical principle of monadology. because no part of space is simple. Meanwhile the monadists have been subtle enough to want to evade this difficulty by presupposing not space as a condition of the possibility of the objects of outer intuition (bodies), but these and the dynamic relation of substances in general as the condition of the possibility of space. Now we have a concept of bodies only as appearances, but as such they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of the possibility of all outer appearance; and the evasion is therefore futile, as it has also been sufficiently cut off above in the transcendental aesthetic. If they were things in themselves, the proof of the monadists would indeed be valid.",["- \"darum weil kein Theil des Raumes einfach ist.\" stands in the text as printed, without connection to what precedes or follows; it is kept in place and not integrated.","- \"er\" (masculine) in \"so mag er … heißen\" does not agree with \"Wort\" (neuter); rendered \"it\".","- Zusammengesetzte = the composite; Bestandtheile = constituents; Element = element; Bedeutung = meaning; Wirklichkeit not at issue here.","- Dinge an sich selbst = things in themselves; Grundsatz = principle; Erklärungsart = way of explanation.","- OCR corrected silently: \"Mona— disten\" (Monadisten), \"sein genug\" (fein genug)."]],"krv-b-1357":["The second dialectical assertion has this peculiarity about it, that it has against it a dogmatic assertion which among all the rationalizing ones is the only one that undertakes to prove evidently, on an object of experience, the actuality of that which we above counted merely among transcendental ideas, namely the absolute simplicity of substance: namely that the object of inner sense, the I that thinks, is an absolutely simple substance. Without getting into this now (since it was weighed more extensively above), I remark only: that if something is thought merely as object, without adding any synthetic determination of its intuition (as this indeed happens through the quite bare representation: I), then nothing manifold and no composition could, to be sure, be perceived in such a representation. Since moreover the predicates through which I think this object are merely intuitions of inner sense, nothing can occur in it either that would prove a manifold outside one another, hence real composition. Thus only self-consciousness brings it with it that, because the subject that thinks is at the same time its own object, it cannot divide itself (although it can the determinations inhering in it); for with regard to itself every object is absolute unity. Nonetheless, if this subject is considered externally, as an object of intuition, it would indeed show composition in the appearance in itself. But it must always be considered thus, if one wants to know whether there is a manifold outside one another in it, or not.",["- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\" (Object in \"sein eigenes Object\").","- Wirklichkeit = actuality; vernünftelnden = rationalizing; Simplicität left as \"simplicity\"; einfach = simple.","- The subjunctive \"so könne … wahrgenommen werden\" is rendered \"could, to be sure, be perceived\".","- \"an sich\" in \"in der Erscheinung an sich\" may attach to \"Erscheinung\" or to \"zeigen\"; kept ambiguous.","- \"hinzu zu setzen\" rendered \"adding\".","- \"ver— nünftelnden\", \"Mannig— faltiges\", \"be— trachtet\" are OCR line-break hyphens, corrected silently."]],"krv-b-1358":["Of the Antinomy",["- Fragment of a heading (Der Antinomie), apparently the opening of the running head; translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1359":["Third Conflict",["- Widerstreit = conflict."]],"krv-b-1360":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1361":["Causality in accordance with laws of nature is not the only one from which the appearances of the world can all together be derived. It is still necessary to assume a causality through freedom for the explanation of them.",["- The OCR \".;\" between the two sentences is read as a full stop.","- \"derselben\" (genitive feminine or plural) is rendered \"of them\"; its antecedent is left as open as in the German.","- Erscheinungen = appearances; Erklärung = explanation."]],"krv-b-1362":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1363":["Assume that there is no other causality than in accordance with laws of nature; then everything that happens presupposes a previous state, upon which it unfailingly follows according to a rule. Now, however, the previous state itself must be something that has happened (has come to be in time, since it was not before), because, if it had always been, its sequel too would not first have arisen, but would always have been. Thus the causality of the cause, through which something happens, is itself something that has happened, which according to the law of nature in turn presupposes a previous state and its causality, but this one likewise a still older one, etc. If therefore everything happens according to mere laws of nature, then there is always only a subaltern, but never a first beginning, and thus in general no completeness of the series on the side of the causes descending from one another. Now, however, the law of nature consists precisely in this: that nothing happens without a cause sufficiently determined a priori. Thus the proposition, as if all causality were possible only according to laws of nature, contradicts itself in its unrestricted universality, and this can therefore not be assumed as the only one.",["- Folge = sequel; Zustand = state; Gesetz = law; Vollständigkeit = completeness; Satz = proposition.","- \"hinreichend a priori bestimmte\": \"a priori\" may go with \"bestimmte\" or with the whole phrase; kept as in German.","- OCR \"Mfo\" read as \"Also\"; \"wennes\" as \"wenn es\".","- The trailing words \"der reinen Vernunft der transscendentalen Ideen.\" are debris from running heads and are omitted."]],"krv-b-1364":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1365":["There is no freedom, but everything in the world happens solely according to laws of nature.",[]],"krv-b-1366":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1367":["Suppose: there were a freedom in the transcendental sense as a special kind of causality according to which the occurrences of the world could ensue, namely a faculty of absolutely beginning a state, hence also a series of sequels of it; then not only will a series begin absolutely through this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, i.e., the causality, will begin absolutely, so that nothing precedes through which this action that happens is determined according to constant laws. But every beginning to act presupposes a state of the not yet acting cause, and a dynamically first beginning of the action a state that has with the preceding one of the very same cause no interconnection of causality at all, i.e., in no way ensues from it. Thus transcendental freedom is contrary to the causal law and is such a combination of the successive states of efficient causes, according to which no unity of experience is possible, which thus is also encountered in no experience, hence an empty thought-entity.",["- \"im transscendentalen Verstande\": Verstande (understanding) in the idiom \"in the … sense\"; rendered \"sense\", not Sinn.","- schlechthin = absolutely (and so too absolut = absolute); Begebenheit = occurrence; Folgen = sequels; Gedankending = thought-entity (*ens rationis*).","- wirkende Ursachen = efficient causes; Verbindung = combination; Zusammenhang = interconnection.","- OCR \"Sehet\" read as \"Setzet\" (suppose)."]],"krv-b-1368":["Thus we have nothing but nature, in which we must seek the interconnection and order of the occurrences of the world. Freedom (independence) from the laws of nature is, to be sure, a liberation from constraint, but also from the guideline of all rules. For one cannot say that in place of the laws of nature laws of freedom enter into the causality of the course of the world, because, if this were determined according to laws, it would not be freedom, but itself nothing other than nature.¹) Nature and transcendental freedom thus differ as lawfulness and lawlessness, of which the former, to be sure, the understanding with the",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"den Verstand mit der\"); not completed. The verb is absent from the German as given.","- \"!)\" in the OCR is read as the footnote marker ¹); the note follows as krv-b-1369.","- Gesetzmäßigkeit = lawfulness; Gesetzlosigkeit = lawlessness; Zwang = constraint; Leitfaden = guideline."]],"krv-b-1369":["Accordingly a causality must be assumed through which something happens, without the cause of it being determined still further by another preceding cause according to necessary laws, i.e., an absolute spontaneity of causes to begin of itself a series of appearances that runs according to laws of nature, hence transcendental freedom, without which even in the course of nature the serial sequence of appearances on the side of the causes is never complete.",["- Reihenfolge = serial sequence (distinct from Folge = sequel, Abfolge = succession).","- Footnote text; its marker ¹) stands in krv-b-1368."]],"krv-b-1370":["Remark on the",["- Fragment of a heading; \"Anmerkung zur\" is continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1371":["I. On the Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1372":["The transcendental idea of freedom indeed by far does not make up the whole content of the psychological concept of this name, which is for the most part empirical, but only that of the absolute spontaneity of the action as the proper ground of its imputability, but is nevertheless the proper stumbling-block for philosophy, which finds insurmountable difficulties in admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That, therefore, in the question about the freedom of the will which has always put speculative reason into such great embarrassment is actually only transcendental and goes solely to whether a faculty must be assumed to begin of itself a series of successive things or states. How such a one is possible is not just as necessary to be able to answer, since likewise with causality according to laws of nature we must be satisfied with cognizing a priori that such a one must be presupposed, even though we do not in any way comprehend the possibility of how through a certain existence the existence of another is posited, and in this regard must hold solely to experience. Now we have indeed demonstrated this necessity of a first beginning of a series of appearances from freedom only actually insofar as is required for the comprehensibility of an origin of the world, while one can take all subsequent states for a succession according to mere laws of nature. But because thereby once the faculty, a series in time difficulty burdens, the descent of the occurrences in the series of causes always higher up to seek, because the causality in them is always conditioned, but as indemnification promises thoroughgoing and lawful unity of experience, whereas on the other hand the delusion of s freedom indeed promises rest to the inquiring understanding in the chain of causes, in that it leads it to an unconditioned causality that begins to act of itself, which however, since it is itself blind, tears off the guiding thread of rules, on which alone a thoroughly coherent experience is possible.",["- The text is corrupt: \"das Vermögen, eine Reihe in der Zeit Schwierigkeit belästigt\" lacks a verb or clause between \"Zeit\" and \"Schwierigkeit\", and the \"Weil aber dadurch…\" sentence has no main clause. I translated it as it stands, without completing it.","- \"von jeder\" is read as \"von jeher\" (\"always\"), an OCR slip. The stray \"s\" before \"Freiheit\" is left untranslated. The closing \"dritten Antinomie.\" is a running head and is omitted.","- Vermögen = faculty; Begriff = concept; Grund = ground; Zustand = state; Erscheinung = appearance; Begebenheit = occurrence; Dasein = existence; begreifen = comprehend, so Begreiflichkeit = comprehensibility; Abfolge = succession; Zusammenhang is in the next passage.","- \"Blendwerk\" is not in the glossary: \"delusion\". \"Schadloshaltung\" = \"indemnification\". \"einräumen\" = \"admit\". \"Imputabilität\" = \"imputability\"."]],"krv-b-1373":["II. To the Antithesis.",["- \"zur\" could also be rendered \"on the\" or \"to the\". I kept the literal \"to\"."]],"krv-b-1374":["The defender of the omnipotence of nature (transcendental physiocracy), in opposition to the doctrine of freedom, would assert his proposition against the rationalizing inferences of the latter in the following way. If you assume nothing mathematically first in the world according to time, then you also have no need to seek something dynamically first according to causality. Who commanded you to devise an absolutely first state of the world and hence an absolute beginning of the gradually elapsing series of appearances, and, so that you may procure for your imagination a resting point, to set bounds to unbounded nature? Since the substances in the world have always been, at least the unity of experience makes such a presupposition necessary, there is no difficulty in assuming also that the alternation of their states, i.e., a series of their alterations, has always been, and hence that no first beginning, neither mathematical nor dynamical, may be sought. The possibility of such an infinite descent without a first member, with regard to which everything else is merely subsequent, cannot be made comprehensible as to its possibility. But if you want to throw away these riddles of nature for that reason, you will see yourselves compelled to reject many synthetic fundamental constitu- entirely of itself to begin, is proved (although not having insight into), so it is now also permitted to us, in the midst of the course of the world, to let various series begin of themselves according to causality, and to attribute to the substances of them a faculty to act from freedom. But one should not let oneself be held up here by a misunderstanding: that, namely, since a successive series in the world can have only a comparatively first beginning, in that a state of things in the world always precedes, perhaps no absolutely first beginning of the series would be possible during the course of the world. For we are speaking here not of the absolutely first beginning according to time, but according to causality. If I now (for example) get up from my chair, completely free and without the necessarily determining influence of natural causes, a new series begins absolutely in this occurrence together with its natural sequels to infinity, although according to time this occurrence is only the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and deed lies not at all in the succession of mere natural effects and is not a mere continuation of them; rather, the determining natural causes cease entirely above them with regard to this event, which indeed follows upon them but does not ensue from them, and therefore must be called, not according to time, but yet with regard to causality, an absolutely first beginning of a series of appearances.",["- The passage is corrupt: after \"Grundbeschaffen-\" (here \"constitu-\") text from the preceding passage's sentence (\"…ganz von selbst anzufangen, bewiesen…\") intrudes. I translated what stands and did not repair it. The sequel is at the start of krv-b-1375.","- \"Allvermögenheit\" is not in the glossary: \"omnipotence\" (literally something like \"all-capacity\"). Vermögen elsewhere = faculty.","- \"nadjfolgende\" and \"fucceffive\" are read as \"nachfolgende\" and \"successive\", and \"s\" as \"ß\" in similar places (OCR). \"Eräugnis\" is an old spelling of \"Ereignis\" and is rendered \"event\". Begebenheit = occurrence.","- \"vernünftelnden Schlüsse\" = \"rationalizing inferences\". \"Einbildung\" = \"imagination\" (not the same word as Einbildungskraft). \"Grenzen\" = \"bounds\". \"Folgen\" = \"sequels\"; \"folgt … erfolgt\" = \"follows upon … ensues\".","- \"sei\" (subjunctive) in \"möglich sei\" is rendered with \"would be\", preserving the indirect, unendorsed assertion."]],"krv-b-1375":["The confirmation of the need of reason to appeal, in the series of natural causes, to a first beginning from freedom, is very clearly evident from this: that (the Epicurean school excepted) all philosophers of antiquity saw themselves compelled, for the explanation of the motions of the world, to assume a first mover, i.e., a freely acting cause, which first and of itself began this series of states. For from mere nature they did not venture to make a first beginning comprehensible. -ties to reject (fundamental forces), which you can comprehend just as little, and even the possibility of an alteration in general must become offensive to you. For if you did not find through experience that it is actual, you would never be able to devise a priori how such an unceasing sequence of being and non-being is possible.",["- The final sentences are the continuation of the broken sentence in krv-b-1374 (\"…Grundbeschaffen-\"). The fragment \"heilen\" is read as \"heiten\" (OCR) and is rendered \"-ties\". The two pieces are translated as they stand and not joined.","- Wirklich = actual (glossary Wirklichkeit = actuality; reality). Veränderung = alteration. Folge = sequence. \"Grundkräfte\" = \"fundamental forces\"."]],"krv-b-1376":["If, however, in the meantime a transcendental faculty of freedom is at all events conceded in order to begin the alterations of the world, this faculty would nevertheless at least have to be only outside the world (although it always remains a bold presumption to assume, outside the sum total of all possible intuitions, still an object that can be given in no possible perception). But to attribute such a faculty to the substances in the world itself can never be permitted, because then the interconnection, according to universal laws, of appearances necessarily determining one another, which one calls nature, and with it the mark of empirical truth, which distinguishes experience from dream, would for the most part disappear. For beside such a lawless faculty of freedom, nature can scarcely be thought any more, because the laws of the latter are unceasingly altered through the influences of the former, and the play of appearances, which according to mere nature would be regular and uniform, is thereby made confused and disconnected.",["- Inbegriff = sum total; Anschauung = intuition; Gegenstand = object; Wahrnehmung = perception; Zusammenhang = interconnection (so \"unzusammenhängend\" = \"disconnected\"); Merkmal = mark.","- \"Wenn auch indessen allenfalls\" is rendered \"If, however, in the meantime … at all events\"; the particles are loose in German. \"Anmaßung\" = \"presumption\"; \"beizumessen\" = \"to attribute\".","- \"anzu— nehmen\" and \"beizu— messen\" are line-break hyphenations, joined silently."]],"krv-b-1377":["Of the Antinomy",["- This is a running-head fragment (\"Der … Antinomie\"); the German is incomplete and I have not completed it."]],"krv-b-1378":["Fourth Conflict",["- Widerstreit = \"conflict\"."]],"krv-b-1379":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1380":["To the world belongs something that, either as its part or its cause, is an absolutely necessary being.",["- OCR \"Nr⸗ I sache\" is read as \"Ursache\" (cause); the stray \"si\" is dropped. Wesen = being; Theil = part."]],"krv-b-1381":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1382":["The world of sense, as the whole of all appearances, contains at the same time a series of alterations. For without these even the representation of the time-series as a condition of the possibility of the world of sense would not be given to us.*) But every alteration stands under its condition, which precedes it in time, and under which it is necessary. Now every conditioned that is given presupposes, with regard to its existence, a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. Thus something absolutely necessary must exist, if an alteration exists as its sequel. But this necessary itself belongs to the world of sense. For suppose it were outside it, then the series of world-alterations would derive its beginning from it, without this necessary cause itself belonging to the world of sense. Now this is impossible. For | since the beginning of a time-series can be determined only through that which precedes in time, the supreme condition of the beginning of a series of alterations must exist when this was not yet (for the beginning is an existence before which a time precedes in which the thing that begins was not yet). Thus the causality of the necessary cause of the alterations, hence also the cause itself, belongs to time, hence to appearance (in which alone time is possible as its form); consequently it cannot be thought as separated from the",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"von der\"); translated as it stands.","- Veränderung = alteration; Dasein (here) and Existenz/existiren = existence/exist; Folge = sequel; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Zeitreihe = time-series; Bedingung = condition; bedingt = conditioned.","- The OCR footnote sign “) is rendered *); the footnote text is in krv-b-1383. Stray OCR letters (\"N\", \"{\", \"s\") and line numbers are dropped; \"eriftiren\" read as \"existiren\"."]],"krv-b-1383":["*) Time does indeed precede alterations objectively, as the formal condition of the possibility of them; but subjectively and in the actuality of consciousness this representation is nevertheless given only, like every other, on the occasion of perceptions.",["- Wirklichkeit = actuality; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Vorstellung = representation; Wahrnehmung = perception; \"Veranlassung\" = occasion.","- The running head (\"der reinen Vernunft der transscendentalen Ideen\") and the line number are omitted.","- \"vor diesen\" could refer to Veränderungen only; \"der Möglichkeit der Veränderungen\" is kept as written."]],"krv-b-1384":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1385":["There exists at all no absolutely necessary being, neither in the world, nor outside the world as its cause.",["- \"überall\" is ambiguous between \"at all\" and \"anywhere\"; \"at all\" is chosen, with the following \"neither … nor\" left to carry the locative sense.","- Wesen = being; schlechthin = absolutely."]],"krv-b-1386":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1387":["Suppose: the world itself or in it there were a necessary being, then in the series of its alterations there would be either a beginning that was unconditionally necessary, hence without cause, which conflicts with the dynamical law of the determination of all appearances in time; or the series itself would be without any beginning and, although contingent and conditioned in all its parts, in the whole nevertheless absolutely necessary and unconditioned; which contradicts itself, because the existence of a multitude cannot be necessary if no single part of it possesses an existence necessary in itself.",["- widerstreitet = conflicts with; widerspricht = contradicts (Widerspruch = contradiction); Menge = multitude; zufällig = contingent; an sich = in itself.","- Dasein = existence; \"befikt\" read as \"besitzt\" (OCR).","- \"oder in ihr\" could be read as \"the world itself, or [a being] in it\"; kept ambiguous."]],"krv-b-1388":["Suppose, on the contrary: there were an absolutely necessary world-cause outside the world, then this, as the supreme member in the series of causes of the world-alterations, would first begin the existence of the latter and their series.*) Now it would then, however, also have to begin to act, and its causality would belong in time, but just for that reason in the sum total of appearances, i.e., in the world, consequently it itself,",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; its continuation appears at the head of krv-b-1389's text.","- Inbegriff = sum total; Erscheinungen = appearances; Weltursache = world-cause.","- The footnote sign is OCR “) rendered *)."]],"krv-b-1389":["*) The word: beginning, is taken in a twofold meaning. The first is active, where the cause begins a series of states as its effect (*infit*); the second passive, where the causality in the cause itself commences (*fit*). I infer here from the former to the latter.\n\nworld of sense as the sum total of all appearances be thought as separated. Thus in the world itself something absolutely necessary is contained (be it now the whole world-series itself, or a part of it).",["- The text after the footnote begins mid-sentence (\"Sinnenwelt als dem Inbegriff …\"), the continuation of krv-b-1388; it is translated as it stands, with the lost \"nicht abgesondert gedacht werden\" left in its German word order (\"not … be thought as separated\").","- Anfangen / anheben both = begin / commence; Bedeutung = meaning; Zustand = state; Wirkung = effect; Causalität = causality; schließen = infer.","- The order of footnote and text follows the OCR."]],"krv-b-1390":["Remark on the",["- Fragment of a heading (\"Anmerkung zur\"); the running head \"vierten Antinomie\" belongs elsewhere."]],"krv-b-1391":["I. On the thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1392":["In order to prove the existence of a necessary being, it is incumbent on me here to use no other than a cosmological argument, which namely ascends from the conditioned in appearance to the unconditioned in concept, in that one regards this as the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. To attempt the proof from the mere idea of a supreme of all beings in general belongs to another principle of reason, and such a one will therefore have to occur separately.",["- Beweis = proof; Dasein = existence; Totalität (Allheit in the glossary) is not the German word here; the German is \"Totalität\", rendered \"totality\".","- Princip = principle; Begriff = concept; Idee = idea.","- \"ein solcher\" (masc.) refers back to \"Beweis\"; \"such a one\" is kept."]],"krv-b-1393":["The pure cosmological proof can now demonstrate the existence of a necessary being in no other way than by leaving it at the same time undecided whether the latter be the world itself or a thing different from it. For in order to ascertain the latter, principles are required that are no longer cosmological and do not proceed in the series of appearances, but concepts of contingent beings in general (insofar as they are considered merely as objects of the understanding) and a principle for connecting such with a necessary being through mere concepts, all of which belongs to a transcendent philosophy, for which the place is not yet here.",["- Grundsätze = principles, as does Princip; both are rendered \"principle(s)\" (Grundsatz/Princip).","- Gegenstände = objects; Verstand = understanding; verknüpfen = connect; Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit).","- \"ausmitteln\" = ascertain; \"erwogen\" = considered (literally \"weighed\")."]],"krv-b-1394":["But if one once begins the proof cosmologically, in that one lays at the ground the series of appearances and the regress in it according to empirical laws of causality: then one cannot afterwards leap away from this and pass over to something that does not belong in the series as a member at all. For in just the same meaning the cause must, not outside the world be, which contradicts the presupposition. Thus there is neither in the world, nor outside it (but in causal combination with it), any absolutely necessary being. | II. On the antithesis.",["- The sentence \"Denn in eben derselben Bedeutung muß die Ursache, nicht außer der Welt sein\" appears corrupt (OCR or lacuna); it is rendered as it stands without emendation.","- \"Mfo\" read as \"Also\"; \"C.aausalverbindung\" as \"Causalverbindung\" (OCR). Verbindung = combination.","- The running head \"vierten Antinomie\" and line number 5 are omitted; the | is kept where it stands."]],"krv-b-1395":["If one, in ascending in the series of appearances, against",["- Fragment of a sentence continuing in krv-b-1396 to -1404.","- \"wider\" = against; Erscheinungen = appearances."]],"krv-b-1396":["the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause difficul-",["- Word split across lines; \"Schwierig⸗keiten\" is completed in krv-b-1397."]],"krv-b-1397":["ties to encounter supposes, then these must also not be grounded on mere con-",["- \"vermeint\" = supposes/thinks; the German order is kept. \"Be-griffe\" is completed in krv-b-1398."]],"krv-b-1398":["cepts of the necessary existence of a thing in general and hence not be ontological, but arise from the causal combination with",["- Dasein = existence; Ding = thing; Begriffe = concepts. The stray \"tio\" (line number OCR) is dropped."]],"krv-b-1399":["a series of appearances, in order to assume for the same a condition that is",["- \"anzu-nehmen\" is split across lines; completed here as \"assume\"."]],"krv-b-1400":["itself unconditioned, be found forth, consequently cosmological and",["- \"hervor finden\" is awkward in the German (sich … hervor finden); rendered \"be found forth\" literally."]],"krv-b-1401":["concluded according to empirical laws. For it must namely be shown that",["- gefolgert = concluded (folgern); empirisch = empirical."]],"krv-b-1402":["the ascending in the series of causes (in the world of sense) can never end with an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the | cosmological argument from the contingency of the states of the world according to their",["- Zufälligkeit = contingency; Zustände = states; \"laut ihrer\" = according to their.","- The stray number \"18\" (line number) is dropped; the | is kept."]],"krv-b-1403":["alterations turn out against the assumption of a first cause and one beginning the series abso-",["- \"ausfalle\" (subjunctive) is in krv-b-1404; the clause order is kept as in the German.","- Veränderungen = alterations; Annehmung = assumption."]],"krv-b-1404":["lutely first beginning cause turn out.",["- The fragment completes \"schlecht-hin\" from krv-b-1403; the sentence is split across the two passages as in the source."]],"krv-b-1405":["It shows itself, however, in this antinomy a strange contrast: that namely from just the same ground of proof, from which in the thesis the Da something be regarded as condition, in which the relation of the conditioned to its condition was taken in the series, which was to lead to this highest condition in continuous progress. Now if this relation is sensible and belongs to the possible empirical use of the understanding, then the supreme condition or cause can conclude the regress only according to laws of sensibility, hence only as belonging to the time-series, and the necessary being must be regarded as the supreme member of the world-series.",["- The text is corrupt or run together in the OCR: \"das Da\" (apparently the start of \"Dasein\") is immediately followed by \"etwas als Bedingung angesehen werden …\", which belongs to a different place. Translated as it stands, without completion.","- Verhältniß and Relation are both \"relation\"; Bedingte = conditioned; Fortschritt = progress; Gebrauch = use; Verstand = understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; sinnlich = sensible; Regressus kept as \"regress\"."]],"krv-b-1406":["Nevertheless one has taken the liberty to make such a leap (μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος). One inferred namely from the alterations in the world to the empirical contingency, i.e. the dependence of the same on empirically determining causes, and got an ascending series of empirical conditions, which was also quite right. Since one, however, could find in this no first beginning and no supreme member, one went suddenly away from the empirical concept of contingency and took the pure category, which then occasioned a merely intelligible series, whose completeness rested on the existence of an absolutely necessary cause, which henceforth, since it was bound to no sensible conditions, was also freed from the condition of time, to begin its causality itself. This procedure is, however, entirely unlawful, as one can infer from the following.",["- The OCR Greek (\"peraßasıs eis aho yevos\") is silently corrected to μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος.","- Zufälligkeit = contingency; Begriff = concept; rein = pure; Kategorie = category; Dasein = existence; Causalität = causality; Zeitbedingung = condition of time.","- \"widerrechtlich\" = unlawful; \"schließen\" = infer; \"bestimmend\" = determining."]],"krv-b-1407":["Contingent in the pure sense of the category is that whose contradictory opposite is possible. Now from empirical contingency one cannot infer to that intelligible one at all. What is altered, the opposite (of its state) is actual at another time, hence also possible; hence this is not the contradictory opposite of the previous state, for which it is required that in the same time in which the previous state was, in its place its opposite could have been, which cannot be inferred at all from the alteration. A body that was in motion = A comes to rest = non A. From this now, that an opposed state to the state A on diesein of an original being was inferred, in the antithesis the non-being of the same is inferred, and indeed with the same rigor. At first it ran: there is a necessary being, because the whole past time comprises in itself the series of all conditions and with this therefore also the unconditioned (necessary). Now it runs: there is no necessary being, precisely because the whole elapsed time comprises in itself the series of all conditions (which therefore all together are in turn conditioned). The cause of this is the following. The first argument looks only to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, of which one determines the other in time, and thereby gets an unconditioned and necessary. The second, on the contrary, takes into consideration the contingency of everything that is determined in the time-series (because before each there precedes a time in which the condition itself must in turn be determined as conditioned), whereby then everything unconditioned and all absolute necessity falls away entirely. Meanwhile the way of inferring in both is quite appropriate even to common human reason, which repeatedly falls into the case of becoming divided with itself, after it weighs its object from two different standpoints. Herr von Mairan held the dispute of two famous astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty over the choice of the standpoint, for a sufficiently remarkable ten follows, it cannot be inferred at all that the contradictory opposite of A is possible, hence A contingent; for to that it would be required that in the same time in which the motion was, rest could have been in its place. Now we know nothing further than that rest was actual in the following time, hence also possible. But motion at one time and rest at another time are not opposed to one another contradictorily. Thus the succession of opposed determinations, i.e., alteration, by no means proves contingency according to concepts of the pure understanding and so also cannot lead to the existence of a necessary being according to pure concepts of the understanding. Alteration proves only empirical contingency, i.e., that the new state could not at all have taken place by itself, without a cause that belongs to the previous time, in accordance with the law of causality. This cause, even if it is assumed as absolutely necessary, must in this way nevertheless be encountered in time and belong to the series of appearances. phenomenon, in order to compose a special treatise about it. The one | namely inferred thus: the moon turns on its axis, because it constantly turns the same side toward the earth; the other: the moon does | not turn on its axis, precisely because it constantly turns the same side toward the earth. Both inferences were correct, depending on the standpoint one took from which one wanted to observe the motion of the moon.",["- The passage is jumbled in the source: text from the footnote (A-edition remark) is interleaved with the main text. \"diesein of an original being\" and \"a sufficiently remarkable ten follows\" are rendered as they stand and are evidently corrupt; nothing is supplied.","- OCR slips silently corrected: Bufälligkeit = Zufälligkeit; nothwend iges, We— seu, Zeit— reihe, Cau- | salität. Stray OCR marks (\"sit\", \"$\", \"i\") were dropped.","- Veränderung = \"alteration\"; Zufälligkeit = \"contingency\"; Totalität and Allheit would both be \"totality\"; Schlußart = \"way of inferring\" (Art = way).","- *contradictorisch* = \"contradictory\"; *Gegentheil* = \"opposite\"; Zustand = \"state\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Urwesen = \"original being\"; Phänomen = \"phenomenon\" (Erscheinung = \"appearance\").","- The \"|\" signs are carried over where they stand."]],"krv-b-1408":["Third Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason",["- The German heading runs \"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft Dritter Abschnitt,\" with the genitive; rendered as a title."]],"krv-b-1409":["Of the Interest of Reason in this its",["- Fragment of a heading; it continues in the next passage (\"Widerstreite\")."]],"krv-b-1410":["Conflict.",["- Widerstreit = \"conflict\"."]],"krv-b-1411":["There we now have the whole dialectical play of the cosmological ideas, which do not at all permit that a congruent object be given to them in any possible experience, indeed not even that reason think them unanimously with general laws of experience, which nevertheless are not arbitrarily thought up, but to which reason is necessarily led in the continuous progression of the empirical synthesis, when it wants to free from all condition, and grasp in its unconditioned totality, that which according to rules of experience can always be determined only conditionally. These rationalizing assertions are so many attempts to resolve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason, of which there can therefore be just so many, no more, and also no fewer, because there are no more series of synthetic presuppositions that bound the empirical synthesis a priori.",["- \"Gegen- f stand\" corrected to Gegenstand = \"object\". The OCR stray \"f\" was dropped.","- vernünftelnd = \"rationalizing\"; Fortgang = \"progression\"; Behauptung = \"assertion\"; Problem = \"problem\" (Aufgabe is also \"problem\").","- Totalität = \"totality\" (as also for Allheit). Kant's plural \"Problemen\" is kept as \"problems\".","- The phrase \"a priori\" may attach to \"bound\" or to \"synthesis\"; the German is ambiguous here and the English keeps it so."]],"krv-b-1412":["We have presented the glittering pretensions of reason extending its territory beyond all boundaries of experience only in dry formulas, which merely contain the ground of its rightful claims, and, as befits a transcendental philosophy, stripped these of everything empirical, although the whole splendor of the assertions of reason can shine forth only in combination with the same. In this application, however, and the progressive extension of the use of reason, in that it starts from the field of experiences and gradually swings itself up to these sublime ideas, philosophy shows a dignity which, if it could only assert its pretensions, would leave the worth of all other human science far beneath itself, in that it promises the foundation for our greatest expectations and prospects of the final ends in which all efforts of reason must finally unite. The questions: whether the world has a beginning and some boundary of its extension in space; whether there is somewhere, and perhaps in my thinking self, an indivisible and indestructible unity, or nothing but the divisible and perishable; whether in my actions I am free, or, like other beings, am led on the thread of nature and of fate; whether there is finally a highest world-cause, or the things of nature and their order constitute the last object at which we must stop in all our considera-",["- OCR corrections: Vergänglliüche = Vergängliche; bberste = oberste. The passage breaks off at \"Betrachtun⸗\"; rendered \"considera-\" and continued in the next passage.","- Anmaßung = \"pretension\"; Grenze = \"boundary\"; Ausdehnung = \"extension\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Philosophie = \"philosophy\" (Weltweisheit is also \"philosophy\").","- \"mit demselben\" is rendered \"with the same\" (referent left open, as in the German).","- \"auf die letzten Zwecke\" is rendered \"of the final ends\" (Aussichten auf = prospects of/on)."]],"krv-b-1413":["must stop: those are questions for the resolution of which the mathematician would gladly give up his whole science; for this can after all procure him no satisfaction with regard to the highest and most pressing ends of humanity. Even the proper dignity of mathematics (this pride of human reason) rests on this, that, since it gives reason the guidance to see into nature, in the great as well as in the small, in its order and regularity, likewise in the admirable unity of the forces moving it, far beyond all expectation of the philosophy building on common experience, it thereby itself gives occasion and encouragement to the use of reason extended beyond all experience, likewise supplies the philosophy occupied with it with the most excellent materials, to support its investigation, as far as their constitution permits, through appropriate intuitions.",["- The passage begins in mid-sentence (\"stehen bleiben müssen\"), continuing the previous one.","- Weltweisheit = \"philosophy\" (as also Philosophie). Materialien = \"materials\" (Stoff is also \"material\"). Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Kraft = \"force\"; gemein = \"common\".","- \"angelegensten\" (superlative of angelegen) is rendered \"most pressing\"; the reading is a judgment call.","- \"deren Beschaffenheit\": the antecedent of \"deren\" is left open, as in the German (\"their constitution\")."]],"krv-b-1414":["Unfortunately for speculation (but perhaps fortunately for the practical vocation of human beings) reason sees itself, in the midst of its greatest expectations, so entangled in a throng of grounds and counter-grounds that, since it is not feasible, for the sake of its honor as well as even its security, to withdraw and look on at this dispute indifferently as at a mere sham-fight, still less to command peace absolutely, because the object of the dispute is very interesting, nothing further remains to it than to reflect on the origin of this disunion of reason with itself: whether perhaps a mere misunderstanding is to blame for it, after whose exposition proud claims on both sides might indeed fall away, but in return a lasting, tranquil regime of reason over understanding and senses would begin.",["- \"inter: essirt\" corrected to interessirt (OCR line-break artifact).","- Bestimmung = \"vocation\"; Grund = \"ground\"; Erörterung = \"exposition\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Sinne = \"senses\".","- \"sich zurück zu ziehen\" and the following infinitives are all dependent on \"nicht thunlich ist\"; the long sentence is kept whole.","- \"ihr nichts weiter übrig bleibt\" is rendered \"nothing further remains to it\"."]],"krv-b-1415":["For now we want to postpone this thorough exposition somewhat and first to take into consideration: to which side we would most like to throw ourselves, if we were perhaps compelled to take sides. Since in this case we consult not the logical touchstone of truth but merely our interest, such an investigation, even though it settles nothing with regard to the disputed right of both parties, will nevertheless have the use of making it comprehensible why the participants in this dispute have thrown themselves rather to the one side than to the other, without any superior insight into the object having been the cause of it, likewise of explaining still other incidental matters, e.g., the zealous heat of the one and the cold assertion of the other party, why they gladly cheer joyful applause to the one party and are implacably prejudiced in advance against the other.",["- Erörterung = exposition; Gegenstand = object; Behauptung = assertion; begreiflich = comprehensible (cognate of begreifen, \"comprehend\").","- \"es begreiflich zu machen ... imgleichen noch andere Nebendinge zu erklären\": the construction is kept as in the German; \"erklären\" here rendered \"explaining\" (glossary: Erklärung = explanation).","- \"Partei\" and \"Theil\" (of the dispute) both rendered \"party\"/\"parties\" and \"party\" respectively: \"Partei\" = party; \"Theil(e)\" = party/parties in this passage, as the context demands."]],"krv-b-1416":["But there is something that determines the point of view in this preliminary judgment, from which alone it can be conducted with proper thoroughness, and this is the comparison of the principles from which both parties proceed. One notices among the assertions of the antithesis a perfect uniformity of the way of thinking and complete unity of the maxim, namely a *principium* of pure empiricism, not only in the explanation of the appearances in the world, but also in the resolution of the transcendental ideas of the universe itself. By contrast, the assertions of the thesis, besides the empirical way of explanation within the series of appearances, also lay intellectual beginnings at the ground, and the maxim is to this extent not simple. But I will call it, from its essential mark of distinction, the dogmatism of pure reason.",["- Beurteilung = judgment (cf. urteilen); Principien = principles; Principium is the Latin in the German text, italicized.","- Denkungsart = way of thinking; Erklärungsart = way of explanation; Unterscheidungsmerkmal = mark of distinction (Merkmal = mark).","- \"zum Grunde legen\" rendered literally \"lay at the ground\"; Grund = ground.","- \"einfach\" = simple; \"intellectuelle\" = intellectual; \"Auflösung\" = resolution; \"Weltall\" = universe."]],"krv-b-1417":["On the side of dogmatism, therefore, in the determination of the cosmological ideas of reason, or of the thesis, there shows itself",["- Passage breaks off with a main verb and its subject still to come (\"sich zeigt ...\"); translated as it stands. OCR \"fosmologischen\" corrected to \"kosmologischen\".","- Bestimmung = determination; Vernunftideen = ideas of reason."]],"krv-b-1418":["First, a certain practical interest, in which every well-disposed person, if he understands his true advantage, takes heartfelt part. That the world has a beginning, that my thinking self is of simple and therefore incorruptible nature, that this is at the same time free in its voluntary actions and elevated above natural constraint, and that finally the whole order of things, which constitute the world, descends from an original being, from which everything borrows its unity and purposive connection: these are so many cornerstones of morals and religion. The antithesis robs us of all these supports, or at least seems to rob us of them.",["- Urwesen = original being (Wesen = being); Verknüpfung = connection; Grundsteine = cornerstones (Grund-).","- Natur = nature; \"Naturzwang\" = natural constraint; \"willkürlich\" = voluntary; \"zweckmäßig\" = purposive (Zweck = end/purpose).","- \"welche die Welt ausmachen\" has plural verb with \"Dinge\" as antecedent; the relative could grammatically also attach to \"Ordnung\" only in sense, and is left as in German."]],"krv-b-1419":["Second, there also manifests itself a speculative interest of reason on this side. For if one assumes and uses the transcendental ideas in such a way, one can grasp completely *a priori* the whole chain of conditions and comprehend the derivation of the conditioned,",["- OCR \"Verz nunft\" corrected to \"Vernunft\". Breaks off mid-sentence (continues \"indem man vom Unbedingten anfängt\").","- Bedingungen = conditions; Bedingten = the conditioned; begreifen = comprehend; fassen = grasp.","- \"völlig a priori\" could attach to \"fassen\" or to the whole clause \"fassen ... begreifen\"; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-1420":["by beginning from the unconditioned; which the antithesis does not accomplish, which recommends itself very badly thereby, that to the question concerning the conditions of its synthesis it can give no answer that would not leave it over to ask ever further without end. According to it one must ascend from a given beginning to a still higher one, every part leads to a still smaller part, every occurrence always still has another occurrence above it as cause, and the conditions of existence in general support themselves always again on others, without ever being able to find unconditioned hold and support in a self-subsisting thing as original being, to be-",["- Running head and page number (\"3. Abschnitt. Von dem Interesse der Vernunft etc. 325\") omitted; \"ge— gebenen\" corrected to \"gegebenen\". Breaks off at \"zu be[kommen]\"; the broken word \"be-\" is left as it stands.","- Begebenheit = occurrence; Dasein = existence; Unbedingte = unconditioned; Theil = part; selbstständig = self-subsisting.","- \"die dadurch sich sehr übel empfiehlt, daß ...\" : \"die\" can refer to \"Antithesis\" (kept); \"welches\" in the preceding clause has \"das Unbedingte\"/the whole preceding as possible antecedent; \"which\" is left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-1421":["Third, this side also has the advantage of popularity, which certainly does not make up the smallest part of its recommendation. Common understanding finds not the least difficulty in the ideas of the unconditioned beginning of all synthesis, since it is in any case more accustomed to go downward to the consequences than to ascend to the grounds, and has",["- gemeine Verstand = common understanding (Verstand = understanding); Folgen = consequences (glossary: Folge = sequel; here plural with \"Gründe\"); Gründe = grounds.","- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"und hat\" continues in the next passage)."]],"krv-b-1422":["in the concepts of the absolutely first (over whose possibility it does not brood) a comfort and at the same time a fixed point, in order to tie the guide-line of its steps to it, whereas on the restless ascending from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air, it can find no pleasure at all.",["- \"grü— helt\" corrected to \"grübelt\" (OCR/hyphenation) and translated \"brood\"; Begriffen = concepts; Möglichkeit = possibility.","- \"an dem rastlosen Aufsteigen\": the preposition \"an\" rendered \"on\"; the construction with \"Wohlgefallen finden\" is kept literally.","- \"Gemächlichkeit\" = comfort; \"Leitschnur\" = guide-line."]],"krv-b-1423":["On the side of empiricism in the determination of the cosmological ideas, or of the antithesis, there is found",["- Breaks off; the subject follows in the next passage. Bestimmung = determination."]],"krv-b-1424":["First, no such practical interest from pure principles of reason as morals and religion bring with them. Rather mere empiricism seems to take from both all force and influence. If there is no original being distinct from the world, if the world is without beginning and thus also without author, our will not free and the soul of the same divisibility and corruptibility as matter: then the moral ideas and principles also lose all validity and fall with the transcendental ideas, which constituted their theoretical support.",["- OCR \"trans scendentalen\" corrected to \"transscendentalen\"; Kraft = force; Gültigkeit = validity; Grundsätze = principles; Materie = matter; Seele = soul.","- \"beiden\" (dative) taken as \"from both\" (morals and religion); \"bei sich führen\" = \"bring with them\".","- \"Urheber\" = author; Theilbarkeit = divisibility; Verweslichkeit = corruptibility."]],"krv-b-1425":["Over against this, however, empiricism offers advantages to the speculative interest of reason that are very enticing and far surpass those that the dogmatic teacher of the ideas of reason may promise. According to the former the understanding is at all times on its own proper ground, namely the field of mere possible experiences, whose laws it can trace and by means of which it can extend its secure and comprehensible cognition without end. Here it can and should exhibit the object both in itself and in its relations in intuition, or at least in concepts, whose image can be set before it clearly and distinctly in given similar intuitions. Not only does it not need to leave this chain of the order of nature in order to hang on to ideas whose objects it does not know, because as thought-entities they can never be given; but it is not even permitted to leave its business and, under the pretext that it has now been brought to an end, to pass over into the domain of idealizing reason and to transcendent concepts, where it no longer needs to observe and to investigate in accordance with the laws of nature, but only to think and to invent, secure that it cannot be refuted by facts of nature, because it is just not bound to their testimony, but may pass them by, or even subordinate them themselves to a higher authority, namely that of pure reason.",["- \"ge— gebenen\", \"so— wohl\", \"transjcen denten\" corrected (OCR). Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; Gedankendinge = thought-entities; kennt = know (kennen); Gesetze = laws; Erfahrungen = experiences; Verstand = understanding.","- \"deutlich\" = distinct; \"faßlich\" = comprehensible; \"Verhältnissen\" = relations; \"darstellen\" = exhibit.","- \"jenem\" = \"the former\" (Empirism); \"deren Gesetzen er nachspüren und vermittelst derselben\" kept as in German; \"ihr Zeugniß\" refers to Natur (\"nature's testimony\", rendered \"their testimony\" with \"Thatsachen\" as antecedent, as the German pronoun is ambiguous between Natur and Thatsachen).","- \"Gebiet\" = domain (not Umfang); \"Natur\" = nature."]],"krv-b-1426":["The empiricist will therefore never allow assuming any epoch of nature as the absolutely first, or regarding any boundary of his prospect into the domain of it as the outermost, or from the objects of nature, which he can resolve by observation and mathematics and determine synthetically in intuition (the extended), to pass over to those that neither sense nor imagination can ever exhibit *in concreto* (the simple); nor concede that one lay at the ground even in nature a faculty to act independently of laws of nature (freedom) and thereby curtail the understanding in its business of tracing the arising of appearances along the guide-line of necessary rules; nor finally admit that one seek the cause of anything outside nature (original being), because we know nothing further than this, since it alone is what presents objects to us and can instruct us about its laws.",["- The OCR quotation mark in \"oder“)\" is treated as a stray footnote/OCR sign and omitted; \"Mathe— matik\" and \"Einbildungs— kraft\" restored.","- Grenze = boundary; Umfang = domain (here in \"den Umfang derselben\"); Gegenständen = objects; Ausgedehnten = the extended; Einfachen = the simple; Vermögen = faculty; Freiheit = freedom; Erscheinungen = appearances; Sinn = sense; Einbildungskraft = imagination.","- \"in concreto\" is Latin, italicized. \"Entstehen\" = arising; \"unterrichten\" = instruct.","- \"derselben\" in \"den Umfang derselben\" refers to Natur; \"wozu\" in \"irgend wozu die Ursache\" = \"the cause of anything\"."]],"krv-b-1427":["To be sure, if the empirical philosopher has no other aim with his antithesis than to put down the presumption and immodesty of a reason that misjudges its true vocation, which boasts of insight and knowledge where insight and knowledge properly cease, and wants to pass off as a furtherance of the speculative interest what is allowed to count with regard to the practical interest, so as, where it is conducive to its comfort, to break off the thread of physical investigations and, with a pretense of extension of cognition, to tie it to transcendental ideas, through which one properly cognizes only that one knows nothing; if, I say, the empiricist were content with this, then his principle would be a maxim of moderation in claims, of modesty in assertions, and at the same time of the greatest possible extension of our understanding through the teacher properly set before us, namely experience. For in such a case intellectual presuppositions and belief for the sake of our practical concern would not be taken from us; only one could not let them appear under the title and the pomp of science and insight of reason, because the proper speculative knowledge can nowhere hit upon any other object than that of experience, and, if one oversteps its boundary, the synthesis, which attempts new cognitions independent of that one, has no substratum of intuition on which it could be exercised.",["- Wissenschaft = science; Wissen = knowledge; Einsicht = insight; Vernunfteinsicht = \"insight of reason\" (kept literal).","- Gegenstand = object; Grenze = boundary; Erkenntniß = cognition; Anschauung = intuition; Glaube = belief; Verstand = understanding.","- \"Ein— sieht\" etc.: line-break hyphenation silently repaired. \"Fürwahr/Vorgeben\" = \"pretense.\"","- \"ihn an transscendentale Ideen zu knüpfen\": \"ihn\" (the thread) kept as \"it\"; \"jener\" in \"von jener unabhängige\" is ambiguous (experience / the boundary); kept as \"that one.\"","- \"ihre Grenze\": \"ihre\" refers grammatically to Erfahrung (feminine); kept as \"its.\"","- \"Substratum\" is Latin in the original; italicized here as *substratum* is not required by the method beyond roman-type foreign words, so kept in italics.","- \"Vermessenheit\" = immodesty/presumption: rendered \"presumption\" for Vorwitz and \"immodesty\" for Vermessenheit; both doubtful equivalents."]],"krv-b-1428":["But so, if empiricism with regard to the ideas (as happens for the most part) itself becomes dogmatic and boldly denies that which is above the sphere of its intuitive cognitions, then it itself falls into the error of immodesty, which is here the more blameworthy because thereby an irreparable disadvantage is caused to the practical interest of reason.",["- anschauend = intuitive (adjective from the participle; glossary anschaulich). Unbescheidenheit = immodesty (cf. Bescheidenheit = modesty in B 498–499).","- \"Ideen\" = ideas; \"Erkenntnisse\" = cognitions."]],"krv-b-1429":["This is the opposition of Epicureanism*) to Platonism.",["- The footnote marker is garbled in the OCR (\"Epikureisms“)\"); rendered as \"*)\" and the note follows as krv-b-1430.","- Epikureism = Epicureanism; Platonism for Platonism."]],"krv-b-1430":["*) It is meanwhile still the question whether Epicurus ever presented these principles as objective assertions. If they were perhaps nothing more than maxims of the speculative use of reason, then he showed in them a more genuine philosophical spirit than any of the philosophers of antiquity. That in the explanation of appearances one must proceed as if the field of investigation were cut off by no boundary or beginning of the world; assume the material of the world as it must be if we wish to be taught about it by experience; that no other generation of occurrences, than as they are determined by unalterable laws of nature, and finally no cause distinguished from the world must be used: are still now very correct but little observed principles for extending speculative philosophy, as also for discovering the principles of morals independent of foreign sources of help, without the one who demands that those dogmatic propositions be ignored, so long as we are occupied with mere speculation, being accused on that account of wanting to deny them.",["- Weltweisen = \"philosophers\" (Weltweisheit = philosophy); Grundsätze = principles; Principien = principles (both rendered \"principles\"); Sätze = propositions.","- Stoff = material; Begebenheit = occurrence; Erzeugung = generation; Grenze = boundary.","- The sentence from \"Daß man…\" to \"…müsse gebraucht werden\" is a series of dependent clauses serving as the subject of \"sind\"; kept as in German, with awkward syntax preserved.","- \"darum beschuldigt werden darf, er wolle sie leugnen\": \"darum\" repeated in the German; rendered once as \"on that account.\"","- Hülfsquellen = \"sources of help.\""]],"krv-b-1431":["Each of the two says more than he knows, yet in such a way that the former encourages and furthers knowledge, although to the disadvantage of the practical, the second, to be sure, furnishes excellent principles for the practical, but just thereby, with regard to everything in which alone a speculative knowledge is granted to us, permits reason to pursue idealistic explanations of natural appearances and to neglect physical inquiry over them.",["- \"bez | fördert\": the | marks a page break within the word \"befördert\" (B 500); kept.","- \"idealischen sit Erklärungen\": \"sit\" is an OCR slip, omitted. Erklärungen = explanations; Erscheinungen = appearances (Naturerscheinungen).","- \"Wissen\" = knowledge; \"Nachforschung\" = inquiry."]],"krv-b-1432":["As for the third moment, finally, which can be looked to in the provisional choice between the two disputing parties: it is exceedingly strange that empiricism is entirely contrary to all popularity, although one should believe that common understanding would eagerly take up a plan that promises to satisfy it through nothing but cognitions of experience and their interconnection in accordance with reason, instead of which transcendental dogmatics compels it to ascend to concepts that far exceed the insight and the faculty of reason of the minds most practiced in thinking. But just this is its motive. For it then finds itself in a state in which even the most learned cannot take anything upon himself over it. If it understands little or nothing of this, then also no one can boast of understanding much more of it, and although it cannot speak about this as scholastically correct as others, it can nevertheless rationalize infinitely more about it, because it wanders about among mere ideas, about which one is most eloquent precisely because one knows nothing of them; instead of which it would have to fall entirely silent over the investigation of nature and confess its ignorance. Comfort and vanity are therefore already a strong recommendation of these principles. Besides, although it becomes very difficult for a philosopher to assume something as a principle without being able on that account to give himself an account of it, or even to introduce concepts*), whose objective reality cannot be seen into: nothing is nevertheless more usual for common understanding. It wants to have something with which it could begin confidently. The difficulty of comprehending such a presupposition itself does not disquiet it, because it (which does not know what comprehending means) never comes to its mind, and it holds that to be known which has become familiar to it through frequent use. In the end, however, all speculative interest disappears in it before the practical, and it imagines that it has insight into and knows what its apprehensions or hopes drive it to assume or to believe. Thus the empiricism of the transcendentally idealizing reason is entirely deprived of all popularity, and however much that is disadvantageous against the highest practical principles it may contain, it is nevertheless not at all to be feared that it will ever overstep the boundaries of the school and acquire in the common being any considerable standing and some favor with the great multitude.",["- \"Begriffet)\" = OCR for \"Begriffe*)\": a footnote marker follows \"concepts\"; the footnote text is not in the passage.","- \"ihn\"/\"er\" refer ambiguously to \"gemeine Verstand\" and (in \"Bewegungsgrund\") to empiricism \"sein\"; \"sein Bewegungsgrund\" = \"its motive\" kept ambiguous. Bewegungsgrund = motive.","- \"über ihn nichts herausnehmen kann\": rendered \"cannot take anything upon himself over it\"; \"ihn\" ambiguous (Zustand / Verstand).","- \"gemeine Verstand\" = common understanding (gemein = common); \"gemeinen Wesen\" = \"common being\" (Wesen = being; commonwealth sense not imposed).","- vernünfteln = rationalize; begreifen = comprehend; einsehen = \"have insight into / see into\"; Einsicht = insight; Besorgnisse = apprehensions.","- \"schulgerecht\" = \"scholastically correct\"; \"an statt daß\" = \"instead of which.\"","- Line numbers (10, 15, 20, 25 etc.) and OCR slips removed; \"Grundiäße\" read as Grundsätze, \"bildet fidh\" as \"bildet sich\", \"Gebäude\" etc."]],"krv-b-1433":["Human reason is by its nature architectonic, i.e., it considers all cognitions as belonging to a possible system, and therefore also permits only such principles as at least do not make a cognition at hand incapable of standing together with others in some system. The propositions of the antithesis, however, are of such a kind that they make the completion of an edifice of cognitions entirely impossible. According to them there is always, beyond a state of the world, a still older one, in each part still others, again divisible, before every occurrence another, which again was likewise generated otherwise, and in existence in general everything always only conditioned, without recognizing any unconditioned and first existence. Since the antithesis therefore concedes nowhere a first and no beginning that could serve absolutely as ground of the building, a complete edifice of cognition is entirely impossible with presuppositions of this kind. Hence the architectonic interest of reason (which demands not empirical but pure unity of reason *a priori*) carries with it a natural recommendation for the assertions of the thesis.",["- Gebäude = edifice (first), Bau = building; \"Gebäude der Erkenntniß\" rendered \"edifice of cognition.\"","- \"empoirische\" = OCR slip for empirische. \"hände\" read as \"Gebäude\".","- Erkenntniß = cognition; Zustand = state; Begebenheit = occurrence; Dasein = existence; Grund = ground; bedingt = conditioned; Behauptungen = assertions.","- \"Vorhabende Erkenntniß\" = \"a cognition at hand\" (literal sense uncertain: \"one under way/in view\").","- \"anderweitig erzeugt\" = \"generated otherwise\"; \"schlechthin\" = \"absolutely\"; ambiguity of \"schlechthin zum Grunde\" (attaching to \"Anfang\" or \"dienen\") preserved.","- *a priori* is Latin in the original and kept in italics."]],"krv-b-1434":["But if a human being could renounce all interest and, indifferent to all sequels, consider the assertions of reason merely according to the content of their grounds: then such a one, supposing that he knew no way out of the crush other than by professing himself to one or another of the disputed doctrines, would be in a ceaselessly wavering state. Today it would seem convincing to him that the human will is free; tomorrow, if he considered the indissoluble chain of nature, he would take it that freedom is nothing but self-deception, and everything merely nature. But if it then came to doing and acting, this play of merely speculative reason would vanish like the shadow-images of a dream, and he would choose his principles merely according to practical interest. But since it is nevertheless fitting for a reflective and inquiring being to devote certain times solely to the examination of his own reason, in this, however, to strip off all partiality entirely and so to communicate his remarks publicly to others for judgment: so it can be held against no one, still less prohibited, to let the propositions and counterpropositions appear, as they, frightened by no threat, can defend themselves before jurors of their own standing (namely the standing of weak human beings).",["- Folgen = \"sequels\" (Folge = sequel in the glossary); Gehalt = \"content\" (as Inhalt also is); Grund = \"ground\"; Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Beurteilung = \"judgment\"; Sätze/Gegensätze = \"propositions\"/\"counterpropositions\"; Lehren = \"doctrines\".","- \"für wahr halten\" does not occur; \"dafür halten\" = \"take it that\".","- Ambiguity kept: \"wie ... sich ... vertheidigen können\" – \"as they ... can defend themselves\" (the \"so wie\" clause can also be read as \"just as\").","- \"ver— schwinden\" and \"Ge— schworenen\" are line-break hyphenations, rejoined."]],"krv-b-1435":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason, Fourth Section.\nOn the transcendental problems of pure",["- Aufgabe = \"problem\"; the heading is split across passages 1435–1437."]],"krv-b-1436":["Reason, in so far as they absolutely must be solved",["- aufgelöset = \"solved\" (Auflösung = \"solution\"; not in glossary); the verb phrase \"müssen aufgelöset werden können\" is divided between passages 1436 and 1437, so \"be able to\" appears in the next fragment."]],"krv-b-1437":["be able to.",["- Continues the preceding heading fragment: \"müssen aufgelöset / werden können\" (must be able to be solved)."]],"krv-b-1438":["To want to solve all problems and answer all questions would be a shameless boastfulness and such an extravagant self-conceit that one would thereby have to deprive oneself at once of all confidence. Nevertheless there are sciences whose nature brings it with it that every question occurring in them must be absolutely answerable from what one knows, because the answer must arise from the same sources from which the question arises, and where it is by no means permitted to plead unavoidable ignorance, but the solution can be demanded. What is right or wrong in all possible cases one must be able to know according to the rule, because it concerns our obligation, and for what we cannot know we also have no obligation. In the explanation of the appearances of nature, however, much must remain uncertain to us and many a question insoluble, because what we know of nature is by far not in all cases sufficient for what we are to explain. It now asks itself: whether in transcendental philosophy any question that concerns an object put before reason is unanswerable through just this pure reason, and whether one can with right withdraw from its decisive answering by counting it as absolutely uncertain (from all that we can cognize) among that of which we indeed have so much concept as to raise a question, but wholly lack the means or the faculty ever to answer it.",["- The text reads \"auch seine Verbindlichkeit haben\"; I have read \"keine\" (as the sense and the other editions require), treating \"seine\" as a slip. Doubtful reading.","- wissen = \"know\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Verbindlichkeit = \"obligation\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Mittel = \"means\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Transscendentalphilosophie = \"transcendental philosophy\".","- Object = \"object\" (Latinate, as Gegenstand); \"Es frägt sich\" rendered literally as \"It asks itself\".","- \"konnen\", \"unbeantworte lich\" corrected silently (OCR)."]],"krv-b-1439":["I now assert that transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity among all speculative cognition: that no question at all, which concerns an object given to pure reason, is insoluble for just this same human reason, and that no pleading of an unavoidable ignorance and unfathomable depth of the problem can release one from the obligation to answer it thoroughly and completely, because just the same concept that puts us in the position to ask must also thoroughly make us competent to answer this question, since the object is not encountered at all outside the concept (as with right and wrong).",["- Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; behaupten = \"assert\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Aufgabe = \"problem\"; unauflöslich = \"insoluble\".","- \"aut— worten\" is a line-break hyphenation, rejoined."]],"krv-b-1440":["But there are in transcendental philosophy no others than only the cosmological questions with respect to which one can with right demand a satisfying answer, which concerns the constitution of the object, without its being permitted to the philosopher to withdraw from them by pleading impenetrable obscurity; and these questions can concern only cosmological ideas. For the object must be given empirically, and the question goes only to the adequacy of it with an idea. If the object is transcendental and thus itself unknown, e.g., whether the something whose appearance (in ourselves) is thinking (soul) is a being simple in itself, whether there is a cause of all things collectively that is absolutely necessary, etc.: then we are to seek for our idea an object of which we can confess that it is unknown to us, but not on that account impossible.*) The cosmological ideas alone have this peculiarity about them,",["- Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; einfaches Wesen = \"simple being\"; \"ein an sich einfaches Wesen\" kept as \"a being simple in itself\" (an sich = \"in itself\"; may attach to \"einfach\" or to \"Wesen\").","- \"derselben\" (feminine plural) refers back to the questions or to the answer; kept as \"them\" is not forced: the German is ambiguous (answer/questions); the English \"from them\" follows the plural reading.","- \"gestehen\" = \"confess\"; \"Oenken\", \"ge— stehen\", \"ile— gen\" corrected silently.","- The footnote asterisk is carried over as \"*)\"."]],"krv-b-1441":["*) One can indeed give no answer to the question what constitution a transcendental object has, namely what it is, but well that the question itself is nothing, because no object for it has been given. Hence all questions of the transcendental doctrine of the soul are also answerable and actually answered: for they concern the transcendental subject of all inner appearances, which is itself not appearance and thus is not given as object, and on which none of the categories (to which the question is actually directed) encounter conditions of their application. Thus here is the case where the common expression holds, that no answer is also an answer, namely that a question about the constitution of that something that can be thought through no determinate predi-\n\nthat they can presuppose their object and the empirical synthesis required for its concept as given; and the question that arises from them concerns only the progression of this synthesis, in so far as it is to contain absolute totality, which latter is nothing empirical any longer, since it cannot be given in any experience. Since here it is a matter solely of a thing as object of a possible experience and not as a thing in itself, the answering of the transcendent cosmological question can lie nowhere else than in the idea, for it concerns no object in itself; and with respect to possible experience it is not asked about that which can be given in concreto in some experience, but about what lies in the idea, to which the empirical synthesis is merely to approach: thus it must be able to be solved from the idea alone; for this is a mere creature of reason, which therefore cannot disclaim the responsibility and shove it onto the unknown object.",["- The footnote breaks off mid-word (\"Prädi-\") at the page end; the main text then resumes with \"daß sie ihren Gegenstand...\", continuing the sentence begun at the end of passage 1440 (\"have this peculiarity about them, that they can presuppose...\"). Left as it stands, not completed.","- Ding = \"thing\" and Sache = \"thing\" (both); Gegenstand = \"object\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Ausdruck = \"expression\"; gemein = \"common\"; Fortgang = \"progression\"; Totalität = \"totality\" (as Allheit); Verantwortung = \"responsibility\"; Synthesis, in concreto, transscendent kept as in the German.","- \"ge— geben\", \"ile— gen\" are line-break hyphenations, rejoined; the stray \")\" before \"nicht nach\" (\"wird))\") is dropped as an OCR slip.","- \"Bedingungen ihrer Anwendung antreffen\" has a singular subject \"keine\" with plural verb; kept as \"encounter\"."]],"krv-b-1442":["It is not as extraordinary as it initially seems: that a science with regard to all the questions belonging to its sum total (*quaestiones domesticae*) could demand and expect nothing but certain solutions, even though they may perhaps not yet have been found at present. Besides transcendental philosophy there are still two sciences of pure reason, one of merely speculative, the other of practical content: pure mathematics and pure morals. Has it ever been heard: that, as it were on account of a necessary ignorance of the conditions, it was declared uncertain what relation the diameter has to the circle, quite exactly, in rational or irrational numbers? Since through the former it cannot be given congruently at all, but through the second it has not yet been found, one judged that at least the impossibility of such a solution could be cognized with certainty, and Lambert gave a proof of this. In the universal principles of morals nothing can be uncertain, because the propositions are either wholly and entirely null and empty of sense, or must flow merely from our concepts of reason. On the other hand there is in natural science an infinity of conjectures, with regard to which certainty can never be expected, cat can be thought, because it is posited entirely outside the sphere of the objects that can be given to us, is entirely null and empty. because the appearances of nature are objects that are given to us independently of our concepts, for which, therefore, the key lies not in us and our pure thinking but outside us, and just for that reason in many cases cannot be found, and consequently no secure disclosure can be expected. I do not count the questions of the transcendental analytic, which concern the deduction of our pure cognition, as belonging here, because we are now dealing only with the certainty of judgments with regard to objects and not with regard to the origin of our concepts themselves.",["- \"cat gedacht werden kann, weil es gänzlich außer der Sphäre … gesetzt wird, … nichtig und leer sei.\" The passage is evidently corrupt here (apparent intrusion of stray text); I translated it as it stands, left \"cat\" untranslated, and did not repair it. The period before lowercase \"because\" follows the German as printed.","- Wissenschaft = science; Inbegriff = sum total; Begriff = concept; Urteil = judgment; Gegenstand = object; Erscheinung = appearance; Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize.","- Verhältniß rendered \"relation\" (not in glossary); Auflösung = \"solution\"; Aufschluß = \"disclosure\"; Naturkunde = \"natural science\" (literally knowledge of nature).","- Silently corrected OCR: Durhmeffer, Srrationalzahlen, Gegen— stände."]],"krv-b-1443":["We will therefore not be able to evade the obligation of at least a critical solution of the proposed questions of reason by raising complaints about the narrow limitations of our reason and confessing, with the illusion of a humble self-cognition, that it is beyond our reason to decide whether the world has existed from eternity or has a beginning; whether cosmic space is filled to infinity with beings, or is enclosed within certain boundaries; whether anything in the world is simple, or whether everything must be divided to infinity; whether there is a generation and production from freedom, or whether everything hangs on the chain of the order of nature; finally whether there is any being entirely unconditioned and necessary in itself, or whether everything is conditioned as to its existence and consequently externally dependent and contingent in itself. For all these questions concern an object that can be given nowhere else than in our thoughts, namely the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of appearances. If we can say and decide nothing certain about it from our own concepts, we must not shift the blame onto the matter, which conceals itself from us; for such a matter (because it is encountered nowhere outside our idea) cannot be given to us at all, but we must seek the cause in our idea itself, which is a problem that admits of no solution, and about which we nevertheless stubbornly assume that an actual object corresponds to it. A distinct exposition of the dialectic that lies in our concept itself would soon bring us to complete certainty about what we have to judge with regard to such a question.",["- Schranken = limitations; Grenzen = boundaries; Scheine = illusion; Selbsterkenntniß = self-cognition; Wesen = being (also \"beings\" in the second question); Dasein = existence; Totalität = totality.","- Gegenstand = object; Sache = matter (here \"matter\" renders Sache, not Materie).","- \"Darle— gnug\" read as Darlegung, rendered \"exposition\" (no glossary entry; Erörterung = exposition is a different word).","- \"Problem\" (Problem) is the same English word as the glossary's Aufgabe = problem elsewhere; unconditioned: unbedingt; conditioned: bedingt.","- \"als entspreche ihr\": construed as \"that an actual object corresponds to it\"; German subjunctive kept as \"assume that\"."]],"krv-b-1444":["To your pretext of uncertainty with regard to these problems one can first oppose this question, which you must at least answer distinctly: Whence do the ideas come to you, the solution of which entangles you here in such difficulty? Are they perhaps appearances, the explanation of which you need, and of which, in consequence of these ideas, you have only to seek the principles, or the rule of their exposition? Assume that nature were wholly uncovered before you; that nothing were hidden from your senses and from the consciousness of everything that is laid before your intuition: still you will not be able to cognize the object of your ideas *in concreto* through any single experience (for besides this complete intuition there is also required a completed synthesis and the consciousness of its absolute totality, which is possible through no empirical cognition); consequently your question can in no way be necessary for the explanation of any appearance that occurs, and thus be, as it were, set by the object itself. For the object can never occur to you, because it cannot be given through any possible experience. With all possible perceptions you always remain caught up under conditions, whether in space or in time, and arrive at nothing unconditioned, in order to decide whether this unconditioned is to be posited in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without any beginning. The all in empirical meaning, however, is at all times only comparative. The absolute all of magnitude (the universe), of division, of descent, of the condition of existence in general, with all the questions whether it is to be brought about through finite synthesis or through one to be continued to infinity, concerns no possible experience at all. You would not, e.g., be able to explain the appearances of a body in the least better, or even otherwise, whether you assume that it consists of simple parts, or throughout always of composite ones; for no simple appearance, and just as little an infinite composition, can ever occur to you. Appearances demand to be explained only so far as the conditions of their explanation are given in perception, but everything that may ever be given in them, taken together in an absolute whole, is itself no perception. But it is this all, properly, whose explanation is demanded in the transcendental problems of reason.",["- Vorwande = pretext (OCR \"eurem\"; read \"euren\"); stray \"a\" before \"durch den Gegenstand\" dropped as an OCR slip; \"dentransscendentalen\" read \"den transscendentalen\"; \"erklaren\" read \"erklären\".","- Erklärung = explanation (glossary); Exposition kept as the German's own Latinate word, rendered \"exposition\" (cf. Erörterung).","- Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; Wahrnehmung = perception; Erfahrung = experience; Erkenntniß = cognition; Bedeutung = meaning; Bedingung = condition; Größe = magnitude; Theilung = division; Dasein = existence.","- \"Das All\" rendered \"the all\" and \"Weltall\" \"the universe\" (no glossary entry); \"Aufgaben\" = problems.","- \"ob ihr annehmet … ob\" construction kept literal: \"whether you assume that it consists …\"."]],"krv-b-1445":["Since, then, even the solution of these problems can never occur in experience, you cannot say that it is uncertain what is to be ascribed to the object in this regard. For your object is merely in your brain and cannot be given at all outside it; hence you have only to take care to become unified with yourselves and to prevent the amphiboly that makes your idea into a supposed representation of an empirically given object, and thus one also to be cognized according to laws of experience. The dogmatic solution is therefore not perhaps uncertain, but impossible. The critical one, however, which can be completely certain, considers the question not at all objectively, but according to the foundation of the cognition on which it is grounded.",["- Gegenstand = object; Object (in \"Objects\") = object as well, both rendered \"object\".","- \"einig zu werden\" rendered \"to become unified\"; \"mit euch selbst\" = \"with yourselves\".","- Amphibolie = amphiboly; Fundament = foundation; Erkenntniß = cognition; Idee = idea; Vorstellung = representation.","- \"und also auch nach Erfahrungsgesetzen zu erkennenden Objects\": \"thus one also to be cognized…\" keeps the German participial construction."]],"krv-b-1446":["Of the\nAntinomy of Pure Reason\nFifth Section.",["- Antinomie = antinomy; Abschnitt = section; rein = pure."]],"krv-b-1447":["Skeptical Representation of the Cosmological Questions through",["- Sceptische Vorstellung: \"Skeptical\" (American spelling); Vorstellung = representation. The title breaks off here and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1448":["all four Transcendental Ideas.",["- Continuation of the preceding title fragment; Ideen = ideas."]],"krv-b-1449":["We would gladly desist from the demand to see our questions answered dogmatically, if we comprehended in advance that the answer might turn out however it wished, it would only increase our ignorance still more and plunge us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into a still greater one, and perhaps even into contradictions. If our question is posed merely as to affirmation or negation, then it is prudent conduct to leave the presumed grounds of the answer undecided for the time being and first to take into consideration what one would gain if the answer fell on the one side, and what if it fell on the opposite side. Now if it so happens that in both cases nothing but what is empty of sense (nonsense) comes out, then we have a well-founded summons to examine our question itself critically and to see whether it does not itself rest on a groundless presupposition and play with an idea that betrays its falsity better in the application and through its consequences than in the separated representation. That is the great utility that the skeptical way has of treating the questions that pure reason poses to pure reason, and by which one can be relieved of a great dogmatic jumble at little expense, in order to put in its place a sober critique, which, as a genuine cathartic, will happily purge away the delusion together with its retinue, polymathy.",[]],"krv-b-1450":["If, accordingly, I could see in advance of a cosmological idea that, to whichever side of the unconditioned of the regressive synthesis of appearances it might incline, it would still be either too large or too small for every concept of the understanding: then I would comprehend that, since that synthesis has to do only with an object of experience, which is supposed to be commensurate with a possible concept of the understanding, it must be entirely empty and without meaning, because the object does not fit it, however I may accommodate it to the idea. And this is really the case with all world-concepts, which also for just that reason entangle reason, as long as it adheres to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For assume",[]],"krv-b-1451":["first that the world has no beginning, then it is too large for your concept; for this concept, which consists in a successive regress, can never reach the whole elapsed eternity. Suppose it has a beginning, then it is in turn too small for your concept of the understanding in the necessary empirical regress. For since the beginning still always presupposes a time that precedes, it is not yet unconditioned, and the law of the empirical use of the understanding imposes on you to ask still for a higher temporal condition, and the world is therefore obviously too small for this law.",[]],"krv-b-1452":["It is just the same with the twofold answer to the question about the magnitude of the world as to space. For if it is infinite and unbounded, then it is too large for every possible empirical concept. If it is finite and bounded, then you rightly still ask: what determines this boundary? Empty space is not a *correlatum* of things subsisting for itself and can be no condition at which you could stop, still much less an empirical condition that would make up a part of a possible experience. (For who can have an experience of the absolutely empty?) But for the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis it is required at all times that the unconditioned be a concept of experience. Therefore a bounded world is too small for your concept.",[]],"krv-b-1453":["Second, if every appearance in space (matter) consists of infinitely many parts, then the regress of division is at all times too large for your concept; and if the division of space is to cease at some member of it (the simple), then it is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For this member still always leaves over a regress to several parts contained in it.",[]],"krv-b-1454":["Third, if you assume that in everything that happens in the world there is nothing but sequel in accordance with laws of nature, then the causality of the cause is in turn always something that happens and makes necessary your regress to a still higher cause, hence the lengthening of the series of conditions *a parte priori* without cessation. Merely efficient nature is therefore too large for every concept of yours in the synthesis of the occurrences of the world.",[]],"krv-b-1455":["If here and there you choose occurrences effected of themselves, hence generation from freedom: then the Why pursues you in accordance with an unavoidable law of nature and compels you to go beyond this point in accordance with the causal law of experience, and you find that such a totality of connection is too small for your necessary empirical concept.",[]],"krv-b-1456":["Fourth. If you assume a absolutely necessary being (be it the world itself, or something in the world, or the cause of the world): then you posit it in a time infinitely distant from every given point of time, because otherwise it would be dependent on another and older existence. But then this existence is inaccessible to your empirical concept and too large for you ever to be able to arrive at it through any continued regress.",[]],"krv-b-1457":["But if in your opinion everything that belongs to the world (be it as conditioned or as condition) is contingent: then every existence given to you is too small for your concept. For it compels you still always to look around for another existence on which it is dependent.",[]],"krv-b-1458":["We have said in all these cases that the idea of the world is either too large or also too small for the empirical regress, hence for every possible concept of the understanding. Why did we not express ourselves conversely and say: that in the former case the empirical concept is at all times too small for the idea, but in the second too large, and hence the fault attaches, as it were, to the empirical regress; instead of our accusing the cosmological idea of deviating, in the too much or too little, from its end, namely possible experience? The ground was this. Possible experience is what alone can give reality to our concepts; without it every concept is only idea, without truth and relation to an object. Hence the possible empirical concept was the standard in accordance with which the idea had to be assessed, whether it is a mere idea and thought-entity, or encounters its object in the world. For one says of something only that it is relatively too large or too small for something else if it is assumed only for the sake of the latter and must be arranged in accordance with it. Among the playthings of the old dialectical schools was also this question: if a ball does not go through a hole, what is one to say: is the ball too large, or the hole too small? In this case it is indifferent how you want to express yourselves; for you do not know which of the two is there for the sake of the other. By contrast, you will not say: the man is too long for his coat, but rather: the coat is too short for the man.",[]],"krv-b-1459":["We are thus brought at least to the well-founded suspicion: that the cosmological ideas, and with them all the rationalizing assertions set into conflict with one another, perhaps have at their ground an empty and merely imagined concept of the way in which the object of these ideas is given to us; and this suspicion can already lead us onto the right trail to discover the sham that has led us astray for so long.",[]],"krv-b-1460":["Of the\nAntinomy of Pure Reason\nSixth Section.",[]],"krv-b-1461":["Transcendental Idealism as the Key to",[]],"krv-b-1462":["the Resolution of the Cosmological Dialectic.",["(Separate blocks as requested; ids with extra spaces above are the same ids: krv-b-1449 to krv-b-1462.)"]],"krv-b-1463":["We have sufficiently proved in the transcendental aesthetic: that everything that is intuited in space or time, hence all objects of an experience possible for us, are nothing but appearances, i.e., mere representations, which, as they are represented, as extended beings or series of alterations, have no existence grounded in itself outside our thoughts. This doctrinal concept I call transcendental idealism ). The realist in transcendental",["- Lehrbegriff rendered \"doctrinal concept\" (Lehre + Begriff); Begriff = concept, Lehre = doctrine.","- Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Erscheinungen = \"appearances\"; Vorstellungen = \"representations\"; Wesen = \"beings\"; Existenz = \"existence\"; angeschauet = \"intuited\" (Anschauung = intuition).","- Veränderungen rendered \"alterations\" (not in the glossary).","- \"an sich gegründete Existenz\" may be read as \"grounded in itself\" or \"existence grounded in itself\"; the German's ambiguity is kept in \"no existence grounded in itself.\"","- The stray \")\" after \"Idealism\" is the OCR remnant of the footnote marker (the footnote is the next passage); left as it stands. The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1464":[") I have also at other times sometimes called it formal idealism, in order to distinguish it from the material, i.e., the common, which doubts or denies the existence of outer things itself. In some cases it seems advisable to make use of this rather than the above-mentioned expressions, in order to prevent all misinterpretation.) signification makes of these modifications of our sensibility things subsisting in themselves, and therefore of mere representations things in themselves.",["- The opening \")\" is the footnote marker and the \")\" before \"Bedeutung\" is the OCR remnant of the text marker; both are left as they stand. The footnote text is followed by the continuation of the main sentence from krv-b-1463 (\"Der Realist in transscendentaler Bedeutung ...\").","- Bedeutung = \"signification\" here, within \"transscendentaler Bedeutung,\" rendered \"transcendental signification\" in the glossary sense of bedeuten/Bedeutung, so as to be consistent with \"signify\" elsewhere.","- Sachen an sich selbst = \"things in themselves\"; Dinge = \"things\" and Sachen = \"things\" (one English word for two German words; Sachen marked here).","- gemeinen = \"common\"; Ausdrücke = \"expressions\"; Sinnlichkeit = \"sensibility\"; Modificationen = \"modifications.\"","- \"Mißdeutung\" = \"misinterpretation.\""]],"krv-b-1465":["One would do us an injustice if one wanted to impute to us the empirical idealism so long decried, which, in that it",[]],"krv-b-1466":["accepts the proper reality of space, denies the existence of the extended",[]],"krv-b-1467":["beings in it, or at least finds it doubtful, and in this matter admits no sufficiently demonstrable difference between dream and truth. As regards the appearances of inner sense in time: in these, as actual things, it finds no difficulty; indeed it even asserts that this inner experience alone and sufficiently proves the actual existence of its object (in itself with all this determination of time)",[]],"krv-b-1468":["exclusively and alone sufficiently proves it.",[]],"krv-b-1469":["Our transcendental idealism, on the contrary, allows: that the objects of outer intuition, just as they are intuited in space,",[]],"krv-b-1470":["are also actual, and in time all alterations, just as",[]],"krv-b-1471":["inner sense represents them. For since space is already a form of that intuition which we call the outer, and without objects in",[]],"krv-b-1472":["it there would be no empirical representation at all: we can and must therefore assume extended beings in it as actual; and it is just the same with time. But that space itself together with this time, and at the same time with both all appearances, are nonetheless in themselves no things, but nothing but representations, and cannot exist at all outside our mind; and even the inner and sensible intuition of our mind (as object of consciousness), whose determination is represented by the succession of different states in time, is also not the actual self as it exists in itself, or the transcendental subject, but only an appearance that has been given to the sensibility of this being unknown to us. The existence of this inner appearance as of a thing existing in itself in this way cannot be admitted, because its condition is time, which can be no determination of any thing in itself. But in space and",[]],"krv-b-1473":["time the empirical truth of appearances is sufficiently secured and sufficiently distinguished from kinship with dreams, if both cohere correctly and",[]],"krv-b-1474":["thoroughly in one experience according to empirical laws.",[]],"krv-b-1475":["Accordingly, the objects of experience are never given in themselves, but only in experience, and do not exist at all outside it. That there could be inhabitants in the moon, although no human being has ever perceived them, must certainly be admitted, but it signifies only this much: that in the possible progress of experience we could encounter them; for everything is actual that stands in one context with a perception according to laws of empirical progression. They are therefore actual then when they stand in an empirical interconnection with my actual consciousness, although they are not on that account actual in themselves, i.e., outside this progress of experience.",[]],"krv-b-1476":["Nothing is actually given to us except perception and the empirical progression from this to other possible perceptions. For in themselves the appearances, as mere representations, are actual only in perception, which in fact is nothing other than the actuality of an empirical representation, i.e., appearance. To call an appearance an actual thing prior to perception signifies either that in the progression of experience we must encounter such a perception, or it has no meaning at all. For that it exists in itself, without relation to our senses and possible experience, could certainly be said, if there were talk of a thing in itself. But there is talk merely of an appearance in space and time, both of which are no determinations of things in themselves, but only of our sensibility; hence that which is in them (appearances) are not something in themselves, but mere representations, which, if they are not given in us (in perception), are encountered nowhere at all.",[]],"krv-b-1477":["The sensible faculty of intuition is properly only a receptivity, to be affected in a certain way with representations, whose relation to one another is a pure intuition of space and time (nothing but forms of our sensibility), and which, insofar as they are connected and determinable in this relation (space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, are called objects. The non-sensible cause of these representations is entirely unknown to us, and we can therefore not intuit it as object; for such an object would have to be represented neither in space nor in time (as mere conditions of sensible representation), without which conditions we cannot think any intuition at all. Meanwhile we can call the merely intelligible cause of appearances in general the transcendental object, merely so that we have something that corresponds to sensibility",[]],"krv-b-1478":["as a receptivity. To this transcendental object we can ascribe the whole domain and interconnection of our possible perceptions and say: that it is given in itself prior to all experience. The appearances, however, are given in accordance with it not in themselves, but only in this experience, because they are mere representations, which as perceptions signify an actual object only if this perception is interconnected with all others according to the rules of the unity of experience. Thus one can say: the actual",[]],"krv-b-1479":["things of past time are given in the transcendental object of experience; but they are for me objects and actual in",[]],"krv-b-1480":["past time only insofar as I represent to myself that a regressive series of possible perceptions (whether by the guidance of history,",[]],"krv-b-1481":["or by the footsteps of causes and effects) according to empirical laws, in a word the course of the world, leads to a past series of time as condition of the present time, which then is nonetheless represented as actual only in the",[]],"krv-b-1482":["interconnection of a possible experience and not in itself, so that all occurrences that have elapsed from immemorial time before my existence nonetheless signify nothing other than the possibility of extending the chain of experience from the present perception upward to the conditions that determine it with respect to time.",[]],"krv-b-1483":["If, accordingly, I represent to myself all existing objects of the senses in all time and all spaces taken together: then I do not posit them in both prior to experience, but this representation is nothing other than the thought of a possible experience in its absolute completeness. In it alone are those objects (which are nothing but mere representations) given. But that one says they exist prior to all my experience, signifies only that they will be encountered in the part of experience to which I, beginning from perception, must first progress",[]],"krv-b-1484":["must, are to be encountered. The cause of the empirical conditions of this progress, hence on which members or also how far I may meet with such in the regress itself, is transcendental and therefore necessarily unknown to me. But it is also not about this that the concern lies, but only | about the rule of the progress of experience, in which objects, namely appearances, are given to me. It is also entirely the same in the outcome: whether I say, I could meet in the empirical progression in space with stars that are a hundred times farther away than the outermost ones that I see; or whether I say, there are perhaps some of them to be encountered in cosmic space, even if no human has ever perceived them or will perceive them; for even if they were given as things in themselves, without relation to possible experience in general, they are nevertheless nothing for me, hence no objects, except insofar as they are contained in the series of the empirical regress. Only in another relation, if precisely these appearances are to be used for the cosmological idea of an absolute whole, and if it is thus a matter of a question that goes beyond the boundaries of possible experience, is the distinction of the way in which one takes the actuality of thought objects of the senses of importance, in order to forestall a deceptive delusion, which must inevitably arise from the misinterpretation of our own concepts of experience.",["- Beginning is cut off (\"muß, anzutreffen sind\"); the opening is translated as it stands.","- OCR \"derglei— eben\" read as \"dergleichen\" (line-break artifact); \"noths wendig\" = \"nothwendig\"; \"Mber\" = \"Aber\"; \"Unter: scheidung\" = \"Unterscheidung\"; \"unver: weichlich\" = \"unverweichlich\" (read as \"unausweichlich\").","- \"Fortschritt\" and \"Fortgang\" both rendered \"progress\"/\"progression\": Fortschritt = progress, Fortgang = progression. \"Regressus\" left as Latin-derived \"regress\".","- \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"Wirklichkeit\" = actuality; \"Grenzen\" = boundaries; \"Art\" = way.","- \"es ist zu thun um\" rendered literally as \"it is a matter of / the concern lies\"; \"Erfahrungsbegriffe\" = concepts of experience.","- \"Ding(e) an sich selbst\" = things in themselves."]],"krv-b-1485":["The\nAntinomy of Pure Reason\nSeventh Section.",["- Heading; \"rein\" = pure, \"Abschnitt\" = section."]],"krv-b-1486":["Critical Decision of the Cosmological Conflict of",["- Heading fragment, continued in the next passage (\"Vernunft mit sich selbst\"). \"Streit\" = conflict."]],"krv-b-1487":["Reason with Itself.",["- Continuation of the heading in the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1488":["The whole antinomy of pure reason rests on the dialectical argument: If the conditioned is given, then the whole series of all conditions of it is also given; now objects of the senses are given to us as conditioned; consequently etc. Through this syllogism, whose major premise seems so natural and evident, there are now introduced, according to the difference of the conditions (in the synthesis of appearances), insofar as they make up a series, just as many cosmological ideas, which postulate the absolute totality of these series and by that very means inevitably set reason in conflict with itself. But before we uncover what is deceptive in this rationalizing argument, we must put ourselves in a position to do so by rectifying and determining certain concepts occurring in it.",["- \"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism; \"Obersatz\" = major premise; \"vernünftelnd\" = rationalizing; \"Totalität\" = totality (Allheit also \"totality\").","- \"Widerstreit\" rendered \"conflict\" (as is \"Streit\" elsewhere); \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"Bedingte\" = the conditioned.","- \"postuliren\" = postulate (Latin-derived in German)."]],"krv-b-1489":["First, the following proposition is clear and undoubtedly certain: that, if the conditioned is given, a regress in the series of all conditions of it is thereby given to us as a problem; for the concept of the conditioned already brings this with it, that through it something is related to a condition and, if this is in turn conditioned, to a more remote condition and so through all the members of the series. This proposition is therefore analytic and raises itself above all fear of a transcendental critique. It is a logical postulate of reason: to pursue that connection of a concept with its conditions through the understanding, which already adheres to the concept itself, and to continue it as far as possible.",["- \"aufgegeben\" = given as a problem (Aufgabe = problem); \"Satz\" = proposition; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Verstand\" = understanding.","- The final clause is kept ambiguous as in German: \"die schon dem Begriffe selbst anhängt\" can attach to \"Verknüpfung\" (connection) or, less naturally, to the whole of the preceding infinitive phrase."]],"krv-b-1490":["Further: if the conditioned as well as its condition are things in themselves, then, when the former has been given, not merely the regress to the second is given as a problem, but this is thereby really already given along with it, and, because this holds of all members of the series, the complete series of conditions, hence also the unconditioned, is thereby given at the same time, or rather presupposed, in that the conditioned, which was possible only through that series, is given. Here the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition is a synthesis of the mere understanding, which represents things as they are, without attending to whether and how we can arrive at acquaintance with them. On the other hand, if I have to do with appearances, which as mere representations are not given at all if I do not arrive at acquaintance with them (i.e., at themselves, for they are nothing but empirical cognitions), then I cannot say in precisely the same meaning: if the conditioned is given, then all conditions (as appearances) of it are also given, and can therefore by no means infer the absolute totality of the series of them. For appearances in apprehension itself are nothing other than an empirical synthesis (in space and time) and are therefore given only in this. Now it does not follow at all that, if the conditioned (in the appearance) is given, the synthesis that makes up its empirical condition is thereby also given along with it and presupposed, but this takes place first of all in the regress and never without it. But one can well say in such a case that a regress to the conditions, i.e., a continued empirical synthesis, is commanded or given as a problem on this side, and that there can be no lack of conditions that are given through this regress.",["- OCR \"moglich\" = \"möglich\" (possible); \"Kenntniß\" = acquaintance (Kenntnis), contrasted with \"Erkenntnisse\" (cognitions); \"Bedeutung\" = meaning.","- \"Gegenstände\" not present; \"Dinge an sich selbst\" = things in themselves; \"Vorstellungen\" = representations; \"Apprehension\" = apprehension.","- \"aufgegeben\"/\"aufgegeben sei\" = given as a problem; \"gegeben\" = given; \"geboten\" = commanded.","- \"mitgegeben\" = given along with; \"zugleich\" = at the same time.","- \"schließen\" in \"auf ... schließen\" = infer (Schluß = inference); \"Totalität\" = totality."]],"krv-b-1491":["From this it is clear that the major premise of the cosmological syllogism takes the conditioned in the transcendental meaning of a pure category, but the minor premise in the empirical meaning of a concept of the understanding applied to mere appearances, consequently that dialectical deception is encountered in it which one calls *sophisma figurae dictionis*. This deception, however, is not contrived, but a quite natural illusion of the common reason. For through it we presuppose (in the major premise) the conditions and their series, as it were unexamined, when something is given as conditioned, because this is nothing other than the logical demand to assume complete premises for a given conclusion; and there no temporal order is to be encountered in the connection of the conditioned with its condition: they are presupposed as given in themselves at the same time. Further, it is just as natural (in the minor premise) to regard appearances as things in themselves and likewise as objects given to the mere understanding, as happened in the major premise, since I abstracted from all conditions of intuition under which alone objects can be given. Now, however, we had overlooked here a remarkable difference between the concepts. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition and the whole series of the latter (in the major premise) brought with it no limitation through time and no concept of succession. On the other hand the empirical synthesis and the series of conditions of the appearance (which is subsumed in the minor premise) is necessarily successive and given only in time one after another; consequently I could not presuppose the absolute totality of the synthesis and of the series represented through it here just as well as there, because there all members of the series are given in themselves (without time-condition), but here are possible only through the successive regress, which is given only by one's actually carrying it out.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"un- W ter\" = \"unter\"; \"in schränkung\" = \"Einschränkung\" (rendered \"limitation\", cf. Schranke); \"fucceffiv\" = \"successiv\"; \"Da- 20 gegen\", \"a5\", stray \"ö\" and line numbers dropped.","- \"Obersatz\"/\"Untersatz\" = major/minor premise; \"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism; \"gemeine Vernunft\" = common reason.","- \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Succession\" = succession; \"Totalität\" = totality.","- \"Betrug\" = deception; \"Täuschung\" = illusion (not Schein).","- \"dort\"/\"hier\" = there/here, kept as in German."]],"krv-b-1492":["After the conviction of such a misstep of the argument laid in common at the ground (of the cosmological assertions), both contending parties can rightly be dismissed as those who ground their demand on no thorough title. But thereby their dispute is not yet ended to the extent that they would have been convinced that they, or one of the two, had wrong in the matter itself that he asserts (in the conclusion), even if he did not know how to build it on sound grounds of proof. Yet nothing seems clearer than that of two, of whom the one asserts: the world has a beginning, the other: the world has no beginning, but is from eternity, one must nevertheless be right",["- OCR corrected: \"Mber\"-type slips: \"klärer\" = \"klärer\" kept as \"clearer\"; \"Wielt\" = \"Welt\"; stray \"zo\", \"ss\" line numbers dropped.","- \"Überweisung\" rendered \"conviction\" (cf. \"überführt\" = \"convinced\"); the two are distinct German words (Überweisung / überführen), both here rendered near \"conviction/convinced\". \"Überzeugung\" = conviction in glossary.","- \"Beweisgründe\" = grounds of proof; \"behaupten\"/\"Behauptung\" = assert/assertion; \"Schlußsatz\" = conclusion.","- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"einer Recht\"), continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1493":["have to be. But if this is so, then, because the clarity is equal on both sides, it is nevertheless impossible ever to find out on which side the",["- Fragment continuing the preceding passage; breaks off mid-sentence (\"auf welcher Seite das\"). \"Recht haben\" = to be right."]],"krv-b-1494":["equal, it is nevertheless impossible ever to find out on which side the",["- Repeats the end of krv-b-1493 as it stands in the source: \"gleich ist, doch unmöglich, jemals auszumitteln, auf welcher Seite das\". Given the overlap, the OCR segmentation appears to duplicate text; translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1495":["right is; and the dispute continues as before, even if the parties",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage. \"gleich\" in \"wenn ... gleich\" = \"even if\"."]],"krv-b-1496":["thus no means left to settle the dispute thoroughly and to the satisfaction of both",["- Fragment; begins and ends mid-sentence. \"Mittel\" = means; \"Streit\" = dispute. \"also\" retained as \"thus\"."]],"krv-b-1497":["parts to end, than that, since they can after all refute one another so nicely,",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence. \"Theile\" (modern Teile) = parts, per glossary (Teil)."]],"krv-b-1498":["they are finally convinced that they are quarreling about nothing, and a certain",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence. \"überführt werden\" rendered \"are convinced\" (Überzeugung/überführen are distinct German words; \"convinced\" here is for überführt)."]],"krv-b-1499":["transcendental illusion has painted a reality before them where",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence. Schein = illusion; Wirklichkeit = reality (glossary: Realität / Wirklichkeit both \"reality\"; Latinate Realität not present here).","- \"transscendentaler\" = transcendental (modern spelling)."]],"krv-b-1500":["The Eleatic Zeno, a subtle dialectician, was already very much censured by Plato",["- Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1501":["as a wanton sophist for having, in order to show his art, sought to prove one and the same proposition through apparent arguments",["- \"als ... getadelt worden, daß er, um\" continues from the previous passage; the sentence is completed in the next passage. \"scheinbare\" = apparent (ambiguous between \"seeming\" and \"illusory\"; kept as \"apparent\"). Satz = proposition.","- Text of the passage ends \"um seine Kunst zu zeigen\"; the translation of the next passage's opening clause is placed with krv-b-1502. See below."]],"krv-b-1502":["his art, one and the same proposition through apparent arguments, and soon thereafter to overturn it again through others, equally strong.",["- Passage text begins \"seine Kunst zu zeigen, einerlei Satz durch scheinbare Argumente zu beweisen [OCR \"bewei— s seu\"] und bald darauf durch andere, ebenso starke, wieder umzustürzen suchte.\" OCR \"bewei— s seu\" corrected to \"beweisen\"; the line number \"5\" dropped.","- The rendering of krv-b-1501 above overlaps with this passage because of my lead-in; the correct division is: 1501 \"as a wanton sophist for having, in order to\" and 1502 \"show his art, to prove one and the same proposition through apparent arguments and soon thereafter to overturn it again through others, equally strong, he sought.\"","- *einerlei Satz* = \"one and the same proposition\". Final \"suchte\" follows the German order poorly in English; rendered \"sought\" at the end of the clause in the corrected division."]],"krv-b-1503":["He asserted that God (presumably it was nothing but the world for him) is",["- Behaupten = assert. \"Welt\" = world. Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1504":["neither finite nor infinite, that he is neither in motion nor at rest, nor similar nor dissimilar to any other thing. It seemed to those who judged him about this that he wanted to deny entirely two propositions contradicting one another, which is absurd. But I do not find",["- \"beurteilen\" = judge/assess; here \"judged him about this\" (beurtheilten). \"ableugnen\" rendered \"deny\". \"ungereimt\" = absurd. OCR \"o\" (line number 20) dropped.","- \"sei keinem andern Dinge weder ähnlich noch unähnlich\": the construction is ambiguous as to scope of the negations; kept as in German (\"nor similar nor dissimilar to any other thing\").","- \"widersprechende Sätze\" = propositions contradicting one another (Widerspruch = contradiction)."]],"krv-b-1505":["that this can rightly be laid to his charge. The former",["- Fragment; \"Den ersteren\" continues into the next passage (\"dieser Sätze\")."]],"krv-b-1506":["of these propositions I shall soon illuminate more closely. As for the rest, if by the word \"God\" he understood the universe, he had to",["- Wort: Gott is a mention, hence quotation marks. \"beleuchten\" = illuminate. \"Universum\" = universe. Sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1507":["say, to be sure, that this is neither persistently present in its place (at rest), nor changes the same (moves), because all places are only in the universe, this itself therefore in no place. If the universe comprises in itself everything that exists, then it is also, to that extent, neither similar nor dissimilar to any other",["- \"beharrlich\" = persistently (beharren/Beharrlichkeit = persist/persistence). \"Orte/Ort\" = place. \"Univers\"/\"Weltall\" = universe (Universum, Univers, Weltall all rendered \"universe\"). OCR \"25\" line number dropped; \"eriftirt\" corrected to \"existiert\".","- \"allerdings\" = \"to be sure\" (could also be \"certainly\"). \"faßt ... in sich\" = comprises in itself. Sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1508":["thing, because outside it there is no other thing with which it could be compared. If two judgments opposed to one another presuppose an inadmissible condition, then in spite of their conflict (which nevertheless is no proper contradiction) both of them drop out, because the condition drops out under which alone each of these propositions was supposed to hold.",["- Urtheile = judgments (Urteil); Sätze = propositions. \"Widerstreit\" = conflict; \"Widerspruch\" = contradiction. \"unerachtet\" = in spite of. \"gelten\" = hold (gültig = valid). OCR \"o\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1509":["If someone said, every body either smells good or it does not smell good, then a third case occurs, namely that it does not smell at all (exhale), and so both conflicting propositions can be false. If I say, it is either good-smelling or it is not good-smelling (*vel suaveolens vel non suaveolens*): then both judgments are opposed to one another contradictorily, and only the first is false, but its contradictory opposite, namely some bodies are not good-smelling, also comprises the bodies that do not smell at all. In the previous opposition (*per disparata*) the contingent condition of the concept of bodies (smell) remained with the conflicting judgment and was therefore not also sublated by this one; hence the latter was not the contradictory opposite of the former.",["- \"ausdufte\" = \"exhale\" (a rare verb, literally \"to give off scent\"); doubtful rendering. \"aufgehoben\" rendered \"sublated\" in the last sentence for consistency with aufheben; elsewhere \"cancel\" would also be possible. OCR \"ab\" and \"10\" dropped.","- \"zufällige\" = contingent; \"Begriff\" = concept; \"contradictorisch\" = contradictory (kept as Kant's Latinate form); \"Gegentheil\" = opposite.","- \"widerstreitend\" = conflicting."]],"krv-b-1510":["If I say accordingly: the world is, according to space, either infinite, or it is not infinite (*non est infinitus*), then, if the former proposition is false, its contradictory opposite: the world is not infinite, must be true. Thereby I would only cancel an infinite world, without positing another, namely the finite one. But if it said: the world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite), then both could be false. For I then regard the world as determined in itself according to its magnitude, in that in the opposition I do not merely cancel the infinity and with it perhaps its entire separate existence, but add a determination to the world as a thing actual in itself, which can just as well be false, namely if the world should not be given at all as a thing in itself, and hence also not according to its magnitude as either infinite or finite. Allow me to call such opposition the dialectical, but that of contradiction the analytic opposition. Thus of two judgments dialectically opposed to one another both can be false, because one not merely contradicts the other, but says something more than is required for contradiction.",["- \"dem Raume nach\" = \"according to space\" (Raum = space). \"Größe\" = magnitude. \"aufheben\" = cancel; \"setzen/hinzusetzen\" = posit/add. \"an sich selbst\" = in itself. \"wirklichen\" = actual. \"Existenz\" = existence.","- \"Hieße es\" rendered \"if it said\" (impersonal, \"es\" kept); \"nichtunendlich\" rendered \"non-infinite\". OCR \"20\" dropped.","- \"einem an sich selbst wirklichen Dinge\": \"wirklich\" = actual (Wirklichkeit)."]],"krv-b-1511":["If one regards the two propositions: the world is infinite according to magnitude, the world is finite according to its magnitude, as opposed to one another contradictorily, then one assumes that the world (the whole series of appearances) is a thing in itself. For it remains, whether I cancel the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its appearances. But if I take away this presupposition, or this transcendental illusion, and deny that it is a thing in itself, then the contradictory conflict of both assertions is transformed into a merely dialectical one; and because the world does not exist in itself at all (independently of the regressive series of my representations), it exists neither as a whole infinite in itself, nor as a whole finite in itself. It is to be encountered only in the empirical regress of the series of appearances and not at all for itself. Hence if this is always conditioned, then it is never given as a whole, and the world is therefore no unconditioned whole, therefore also does not exist as such, neither with infinite nor with finite magnitude.",["- \"ansieht\" = regards; \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances; \"Regressus\" kept as \"regress\"; \"Vorstellungen\" = representations. \"bedingt/unbedingt\" = conditioned/unconditioned. \"Ganzes\" = whole.","- Footnote marker \"!)\" after \"Welt\" in the OCR is retained in sense but its footnote text is not in this passage; \"Welt!)\" corrected, marker \"1)\" omitted. Line number \"3\" dropped, \"je\" read as \"so\", \"ver wandelt\" = \"verwandelt\".","- \"Sie ist nur...\" and \"Daher wenn diese\": \"sie\" and \"diese\" ambiguous in antecedent (Welt/Reihe); left ambiguous as in German. \"für sich selbst\" = \"for itself\"."]],"krv-b-1512":["What has been said here about the first cosmological idea, namely the absolute totality of magnitude in the appearance, holds also of all the rest. The series of conditions is to be encountered only in the regressive synthesis itself, but not in itself in the appearance as a proper thing given before all regress. Hence I shall also have to say: the multitude of parts in a given appearance is",["- \"Totalität\" = totality (Allheit also \"totality\" in the glossary; Latinate Totalität marked here). \"Menge\" = multitude; \"Theile\" = parts. \"kosmologischen\" = cosmological. Sentence breaks off; continues in the next passage.","- \"an sich ... in der Erscheinung ... anzutreffen\" attaches to \"nicht ... anzutreffen\"; \"an sich\" could modify \"anzutreffen\" or \"Erscheinung\"; left as in German."]],"krv-b-1513":["in itself neither finite nor infinite, because appearance is nothing existing in itself, and the parts are first given through the regress of the decomposing synthesis and in it, which regress is never given absolutely whole, neither as finite nor as infinite. The same holds of the series of causes ordered above one another, or of the conditioned ones up to unconditionally necessary existence, which can never be regarded neither in itself according to its totality as finite, nor as infinite, because as a series of subordinated representations it subsists only in the dynamic regress, but before it and as a series of things in themselves subsisting for itself cannot exist at all.",["- Passage begins \"sich weder endlich\" (the \"an sich\" falls at the end of the previous passage; \"sich\" here continues \"an sich\"). Rendered \"in itself\".","- \"niemals weder ... noch\" is a double negation in the German; kept as \"never ... neither ... nor\". \"Existenz\" = existence. \"unbedingt nothwendig\" = unconditionally necessary. \"decomponirenden\" = decomposing. \"dynamischen\" = dynamic.","- \"über einander geordneten\" = \"ordered above one another\" (literal). \"der bedingten\" with ellipsis of \"Ursachen\" preserved (\"of the conditioned ones\").","- \"für sich bestehende\": \"OCR fidh\" = \"sich\"."]],"krv-b-1514":["Thus, accordingly, the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas is removed by its being shown that it is merely dialectical and a conflict of an illusion that arises from one's having applied the idea of absolute totality, which holds only as a condition of things in themselves, to appearances, which exist only in the representation and, if they make up a series, in the successive regress, but otherwise not at all. But one can also conversely draw from this antinomy a true, though not dogmatic, yet critical and doctrinal use: namely to prove indirectly the transcendental ideality of appearances, in case someone should perhaps not have enough with the direct proof in the transcendental Aesthetic. The proof would consist in this dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself, then it is either finite or infinite. Now the former as well as the second is false (according to the proofs adduced above of the antithesis on the one hand and the thesis on the other). Therefore it is also false that the world (the sum total of all appearances) is a whole existing in itself. From which it then follows that appearances in general are nothing outside our representations, which is just what we meant to say by their transcendental ideality. |",["- \"Antinomie\" = antinomy; \"Idealität\" = ideality; \"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"Nutzen\" = use (Gebrauch also \"use\" in glossary; here Nutzen). \"Ästhetik\" = Aesthetic. \"doctrinalen\" = doctrinal. \"Beweis\" = proof. \"Thesis/Antithesis\" kept as Latinate forms. \"Dilemma\" kept.","- \"wenn jemand etwa ... nicht genug hätte\": subjunctive preserved as \"in case someone should perhaps not have enough\".","- \"Nun ist das erstere sowohl als das zweite falsch\": \"the former as well as the second\" kept literal. \"laut\" = \"according to\".","- Vertical bar | at the end retained from the source.","- OCR \"kos— mologischen\", \"dialek— tisch\", \"Vor stellung\", \"fidh\" silently corrected."]],"krv-b-1515":["This remark is of importance. From it one sees that the above proofs of the fourfold antinomy were not delusions but thorough, namely under the presupposition that appearances or a ∗ world of sense that comprehends them all within itself were things in themselves. The conflict of the propositions drawn from this, however, discovers that a falsity lies in the presupposition, and thereby brings us to a discovery of the true constitution of things as objects of the senses. The transcendental dialectic therefore does not by any means give any support to skepticism, but certainly to the skeptical method, which can point to it as an example of its great utility, when one lets the arguments of reason appear against one another in their greatest freedom, which, although in the end they will not deliver what one was seeking, will nevertheless always deliver something useful and serviceable for the correction of our judgments.",["Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Urtheile = \"judgments\".","The footnote marker after \"eine\" is kept as ∗. The footnote text is not in the passage.","\"Blendwerke\" rendered \"delusions\"; \"gründlich\" rendered \"thorough\".","The antecedent of \"die ... auftreten läßt\" and of \"die ... liefern werden\" is ambiguous in the German, and I kept it so (arguments / proofs)."]],"krv-b-1516":["Of the\nAntinomy of Pure Reason,\nEighth Section.",["Heading fragment; \"Der\" is a genitive here."]],"krv-b-1517":["Regulative Principle of Pure Reason with regard",["Fragment of a heading, continued in the next passage. Princip = \"principle\"; Grundsatz is also \"principle.\""]],"krv-b-1518":["to the Cosmological Ideas.",["Continuation of the heading."]],"krv-b-1519":["Since through the cosmological principle of totality no maximum of the series of conditions in a world of sense as a thing in itself is given, but can only be set as a task in the regress of the same: so the said principle of pure reason in its meaning thus corrected still retains its good validity, though not as an axiom, to think the totality in the object as actual,",["OCR \"Marimum\" and \"ans sich\" corrected to Maximum and an sich. \"ausge— geben\" is read as \"aufgegeben\" (\"set as a task\"), a doubtful reading.","Grundsatz and Princip are both rendered \"principle\"; Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; Gültigkeit = \"validity\"; Object = \"object\" (not Gegenstand here).","The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passages."]],"krv-b-1520":["but as a problem for the understanding, thus for the subject, in order",["Problem = \"problem\" (Aufgabe elsewhere is also \"problem\")."]],"krv-b-1521":["to institute and continue, in keeping with the completeness in the idea, the regress in the series of",["\"gemäß\" taken with \"der Vollständigkeit in der Idee.\" Syntax is continued from the previous fragment."]],"krv-b-1522":["conditions for a given conditioned.",["Bedingten = \"conditioned\" (substantivized participle)."]],"krv-b-1523":["For in sensibility, i.e., in space and time, every condition to which we can attain in the exposition of given appearances",["OCR \"Bedin- 5 gnug\" is read as Bedingung; the line number is omitted. Exposition = \"exposition\" (Latin expositio in the glossary)."]],"krv-b-1524":["is in turn conditioned: because these are not objects in themselves,",["\"diese\" refers back to Erscheinungen (grammatically ambiguous with \"Bedingung\" in the German, kept)."]],"krv-b-1525":["in which the absolutely unconditioned could perhaps take place, but merely empirical representations, which must always find in intuition their con-",["\"Schlechthin-Unbedingte\" = \"absolutely unconditioned\"; Anschauung = \"intuition.\" The word Bedingung is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1526":["dition, which determines them according to space or time. The principle of reason is therefore properly only a rule, which",["\"welche\" (after Bedingung) could refer to the condition or to the intuition; kept ambiguous. Line number omitted."]],"krv-b-1527":["commands a regress in the series of conditions of given appearances,",["Regressus = \"regress.\""]],"krv-b-1528":["to which it is never permitted to stop at an absolutely unconditioned.",["\"dem\" is the dative relative, referring to the regress."]],"krv-b-1529":["It is therefore no *principium* of the possibility of experi-",["Principium = *principium* (Latin, as printed)."]],"krv-b-1530":["ence and of the empirical cognition of the objects of the senses, consequently",["Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Gegenstände = \"objects.\""]],"krv-b-1531":["no principle of the understanding, for every experience is enclosed within its bounds",["Grenzen = \"bounds\"; Grundsatz = \"principle.\""]],"krv-b-1532":["(in accordance with the given intuition); also no constituti-",["OCR \"conftituti-\" corrected to constituti-."]],"krv-b-1533":["ve principle of reason, to extend the concept of the world of sense beyond all possi-",["OCR \"mög- / liebe\" in the next fragment is corrected to \"möglich-e\"; here the word is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1534":["ble experience: but a principle of the greatest possible",["OCR \"liebe\" corrected to \"liche\" (möglichen)."]],"krv-b-1535":["continuation and extension of experience, according to which no empirical boundary may count as an absolute boundary, thus a *principium* of reason, which as a rule postulates what is to happen on our part in the regress",["Line number \"20\" omitted. Grenze = \"boundary\" (bound in the glossary; \"bounds\" used above for plural Grenzen). Muß ... gelten = \"may count\" is a loose rendering of a \"muß\" that is itself odd in the German; the German reads literally \"must count as.\""]],"krv-b-1536":["and does not anticipate what is given in itself in the object prior to all re-",["OCR \"fol\" corrected to soll in the previous fragment; \"Ne-gressus\" corrected to Re-gressus. The word is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1537":["gress. Therefore I call it a regulative principle",["\"es\" refers to the principium of reason."]],"krv-b-1538":["of reason, whereas the principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as given in itself in the object (the appearances), would be a constitutive cosmological principle, whose nul-",["Totalität = \"totality\"; Nichtigkeit is split across passages (Nich-/tigkeit) and rendered \"nullity.\""]],"krv-b-1539":["lity I have wanted to indicate through precisely this distinction and thereby to pre-",["\"ich ... habe anzeigen ... wollen\" kept as a perfect with \"wanted to.\""]],"krv-b-1540":["vent that one not, as otherwise unavoidably happens (through transcendental subreption), to an idea, which serves merely as a rule,",["\"Subreption\" is printed in roman in the OCR; not italicized here. The sentence continues in the next passage (beimesse)."]],"krv-b-1541":["ascribe objective reality.",["\"beimesse\" is subjunctive after \"daß man nicht\"; Realität = \"reality.\""]],"krv-b-1542":["In order now to properly deter-",["Sinn = \"sense\"; the word bestimmen is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1543":["mine the sense of this rule of pure reason, it is first to be noted that it cannot say what the object is, but how the empirical regress is to be instituted in order to arrive at the complete concept of the object. For if the former took place, it would be a constitutive *principium*, of the kind that is never possible from pure reason. One can therefore by no means have the intention thereby to say that the series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite or infinite) for thereby a mere idea of absolute totality, which is created solely within itself, would think an object that cannot be given in any experience, in that an objective reality independent of the empirical synthesis would be bestowed on a series of appearances. The idea of reason will therefore only prescribe to the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions a rule, according to which it proceeds from the conditioned by means of all the conditions subordinated to one another to the unconditioned, although this is never reached. For the absolutely unconditioned is not encountered at all in experience.",["\"angustellen\" corrected to anzustellen; stray \"es\" (line number 5) dropped; line number \"10\" dropped. The unmatched \")\" after \"unendlich\" is kept as in the OCR.","Absicht = \"intention\"; Gegenstand = \"object\" and Object = \"object\" (both \"object\"); Gültigkeit not present.","\"in ihr selbst\" could refer to the idea or to totality; the German is ambiguous and kept so with \"within itself.\"","\"dieses\" (last relative clause) = \"this,\" the unconditioned."]],"krv-b-1544":["To this end, first, the synthesis of a series, insofar as it is never complete, is to be determined precisely. For this aim one commonly makes use of two expressions, which are supposed to differ somewhat in this, without one yet knowing quite how to specify the ground of this distinction. The mathematicians speak solely of a *progressus in infinitum*. The investigators of concepts (philosophers) want to let count in its place only the | expression of a *progressus in indefinitum*. Without lingering over the examination of the scruple that advised them such a distinction, and the brooding or fruitless use of it, I will seek to determine these concepts precisely in relation to my aim.",["Line number \"18\" omitted; the sign | kept where it stands. Absicht = \"aim\" here (glossary: aim; intention). Ausdruck = \"expression\"; Begriff = \"concept.\"","\"grten\" in the OCR is doubtful; read as \"grübelnden\" and rendered \"brooding.\" \"Bedenklichkeit\" rendered \"scruple.\" \"diesen\" read as dative plural (\"them\")."]],"krv-b-1545":["Of a straight line one can rightly say that it can be extended to the infinite, and here the distinction of the infinite and the indeterminably far progression (*progressus in indefinitum*) would be an empty subtlety. For although, when it is said: draw a line on, it certainly sounds more correct if one adds *in indefinitum* than if it says *in infinitum*, because the former signifies no more than: extend it as far as you like, but the second: you shall never stop extending it (which is just not the intention here): yet if only the can is at issue, the first expression is entirely",["Fortgang = \"progression\" (Latin progressus kept in italics); bedeutet = \"signifies\"; Absicht = \"intention\"; Ausdruck = \"expression\"; \"das Können\" rendered \"the can\" for literalness.","\"unbestimmbar weit\" = \"indeterminably far.\""]],"krv-b-1546":["correct; for you can make it ever larger to the infinite. And so it is also in all cases where one speaks only of the progressus, i.e., of the progression from the condition to the conditioned; this possible progression goes on to the infinite in the series of appearances. From a pair of parents you can go on without end in the descending line of generation and also quite well think that it actually goes on so in the world. For here reason never requires absolute totality of the series, since it does not presuppose it as condition and as given (*datum*)",["Line numbers \"8\" and \"se\" (OCR) dropped; \"se fortgehen\" read as \"fortgehen.\" \"progressus\" is printed in roman in the text and left unitalicized here, though the method would italicize it.","\"sie\" in \"daß sie wirklich ... fortgehe\" is ambiguous (the line / the series of generation); kept as \"it.\"","\"nicht als Bedingung und wie gegeben\" is continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1547":["presupposed, but only as something conditioned, which is only givable (*dabile*) and is added on without end.",["\"angeblich\" in the OCR is doubtful; the Latin dabile suggests \"gebbar\" (givable), so I rendered \"givable.\" \"vorausgesetzt\" completes the previous passage's clause (\"does not presuppose ... presupposed\"), rendered as it stands.","\"hinzugesetzt\" = \"added on.\""]],"krv-b-1548":["It is quite otherwise with the problem: how far the regress, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions in a series, extends; whether I can say: it is a regress to the infinite, or only a regress extending indeterminably far (*in indefinitum*); and whether I can thus ascend from the presently living human beings in",["OCR \"Res gressus\" corrected to Regressus. Aufgabe = \"problem\" (the same English word as Problem above). Rückgang = \"regress,\" as is Regressus; both German words rendered \"regress.\"","The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1549":["the series of their ancestors to the infinite, or whether only this can be said: that, however far I have gone back, never",["Stray \"o\" (line number) dropped. \"ob nur gesagt werden könne\" is read as \"whether only ... can be said.\""]],"krv-b-1550":["is an empirical ground encountered for taking the series somewhere for",["The passage breaks off mid-clause (\"die Reihe irgendwo für\"); the infinitive and its complement are in the next passage. \"Grund\" = \"ground.\""]],"krv-b-1551":["to hold as bounded, so that I am entitled and at the same time bound, for each of the primal ancestors, to seek yet further his forebear, although just not to presuppose. : I say accordingly: if the whole has been given in empirical intuition, then the regressus in the series of its inner conditions goes to infinity. But if only one member of the series is given, from which the regressus to the absolute totality is first to proceed: then only a going back into indeterminate distance (*in indefinitum*) takes place. Thus of the division of a matter given between its boundaries (of a body) it must be said: it goes to infinity. For this matter is whole, consequently is given with all its possible parts in empirical intuition. Since now the condition of this whole is its part, and the condition of this part is the part of the part, and so on, and in this regressus of decomposition an unconditioned",[]],"krv-b-1552":["(indivisible) member of this series of conditions is never encountered, there is not only nowhere an empirical ground to stop in the division, but the further members of the division that is to be continued are themselves empirically given even before this further-going division, i.e. the division goes to infinity. By contrast, the series of forebears of a given human being is given in its absolute totality in no possible experience; the regressus nevertheless goes from each member of this procreation to a higher one, so that no empirical boundary is to be encountered that would exhibit a member as absolutely unconditioned. But since nevertheless the members that could furnish the condition for this also do not lie in the",[]],"krv-b-1553":["empirical intuition of the whole already before the regressus: so",[]],"krv-b-1554":["this does not go to infinity (of the division of what is given), but into indeterminable distance of the searching out of more members for the given ones, | which in turn are always given only as conditioned. 1",[]],"krv-b-1555":["In neither of the two cases, in the *regressus in infinitum* | as well as in the one *in indefinitum*, is the series of conditions regarded as given as infinite in the object. They are not things that in themselves, but I only appearances, which as conditions of one another [are given] only in the regressus of the conditions in itself is, whether finite or infinite, for it is nothing in itself, but: how we are to institute the empirical regressus, and how far we are to continue it. And there is then a notable difference with regard to the rule of this progress. If the whole has been given empirically, then it is possible to go back to infinity in the | series of its inner conditions. But if that is not given, but is first to be given through empirical regressus, then I can only say: it is possible to infinity to proceed to still higher conditions of the series. In the former case I could say: there are always more members there and empirically given than I reach through the regressus (of decomposition); but in the second: I can still always go further in the regressus, because no member is given empirically as absolutely unconditioned and thus a higher member is still always admitted as possible, and consequently the inquiry after it as necessary. There it was necessary to encounter more members of the series, but here it is always necessary to ask after more, because no experience absolutely bounds. For you have either no perception that absolutely bounds your empirical regressus, and then you must not hold your regressus to be completed, or you have such a perception bounding your series, then this cannot be a part of your series traversed (because what bounds must be distinguished from that which is thereby bounded), and you must therefore continue your regressus also to this condition further and so on.",[]],"krv-b-1556":["The following section will put these remarks in their proper light through their application. |",[]],"krv-b-1557":["The\nAntinomy of Pure Reason\nNinth Section.",[]],"krv-b-1558":["On the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle \"of Reason with regard to all Cosmological Ideas.",[]],"krv-b-1559":["Since, as we have shown several times, there is no transcendental",[]],"krv-b-1560":["use, any more of pure concepts of the understanding than of concepts of reason;",[]],"krv-b-1561":["since the absolute totality of the series of conditions in the world of sense rests solely on a transcendental use of reason,",[]],"krv-b-1562":["which demands this unconditioned completeness from that which it presupposes as thing in itself, the world of sense however contains nothing of the kind:",[]],"krv-b-1563":["so there can never again be talk of the absolute magnitude",[]],"krv-b-1564":["of the series in it, whether they may be bounded, or in themselves unbounded,",[]],"krv-b-1565":["but only of how far we are to go back in the empirical regressus in the reduction of experience to its conditions, in order, according to",[]],"krv-b-1566":["the rule of reason, to stop at no other answer to its questions than one commensurate with the object.",[]],"krv-b-1567":["It is thus only the validity of the principle of reason, as a",[]],"krv-b-1568":["rule of the continuation and magnitude of a possible experience, that alone remains left to us, after its invalidity as a constitutive principle of appearances in themselves has been sufficiently demonstrated. Also, if we can place that [validity] indubitably before our eyes, the dispute of reason with itself will be completely ended, in that not only is the illusion that set it at variance with itself removed through critical resolution, but in its place the sense in which it agrees with itself, and whose misinterpretation alone occasioned the dispute, is disclosed, and an otherwise dialectical principle is transformed into a doctrinal one. In fact if this, according to its subjective significance, of determining the greatest possible use of the understanding in experience commensurately with the objects of the same, can be confirmed: then it is just as much as if it, like an axiom (which from pure reason is impossible), determined the objects in themselves *a priori*; for even this could, with regard to the objects of experience, have no greater influence on the extension and correction of our cognition than that it proved itself active in the most extended use of our understanding in experience.",[]],"krv-b-1569":["I.\nResolution of the Cosmological Idea\nof the",[]],"krv-b-1570":["Totality of the Composition of the Appearances",[]],"krv-b-1571":["into a World-whole.",[]],"krv-b-1572":["Both here and in the remaining cosmological questions the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition: that in the empirical regressus no experience of an absolute boundary, hence of no condition as one that would be empirically absolutely unconditioned, can be encountered. The ground of this, however, is: that such an experience would have to contain in itself a bounding of appearances by nothing or the empty, on which the continued regressus could strike by means of a perception, which is impossible. |",[]],"krv-b-1573":["This proposition now, which says just as much as that in the empirical regressus I always arrive only at a condition that itself in turn must be regarded as empirically conditioned, contains the rule *in terminis*: that, however far I may have come with it in the ascending series, I must always ask after a higher member of the series, whether this may become known to me through experience, or not.",[]],"krv-b-1574":["Now for the resolution of the first cosmological problem nothing further is needed than to determine in addition: whether in the regressus to the unconditioned magnitude of the world-whole (according to time and space) this",[]],"krv-b-1575":["to",[]],"krv-b-1576":["never bounded ascending can be called a going back to infinity, or only an indeterminably continued regressus (*in indefinitum*).",["- krv-b-1551: the opening is a fragment; \"obgleich eben nicht vorauszusetzen. :\" is as printed in the OCR (likely corrupt), left as it stands. Regressus kept in Latin form.","- krv-b-1551: Anschauung = intuition; Totalität = totality; Bedingung = condition; Theilung = division; Grenzen = boundaries; Materie = matter.","- krv-b-1555: the OCR is garbled (\"die als Bedingungen von einander nur im Regressus der Bedingungen an sich selbst sei, ob endlich...\"); text left broken, with the bracketed \"[are given]\" my only insertion. Doubtful readings: \"fortzug eben\" (fortzugehen), \"möglich?\" and \"Rez gressus\".","- krv-b-1555: Object = object; Gegenstand elsewhere also \"object\"; Erscheinungen = appearances; Fortschritt = progress; Wahrnehmung = perception.","- krv-b-1555: \"Dort ... hier\" and \"ihr\" (plural \"you\") kept literal.","- krv-b-1562–1565: \"Zurück— 15 führung\" and \"auge— messenen\" are OCR line-break remnants, silently corrected (Zurückführung, angemessenen).","- krv-b-1568: \"jene\" (that) and \"dieser\" (this) lack explicit antecedents in my rendering as in the German; \"that [validity]\" is the grammatically likeliest antecedent (Gültigkeit), but \"this\" in \"wenn dieser ...\" is taken as the Grundsatz; ambiguity of \"es ... bewährt werden kann\" kept.","- krv-b-1568: \"Pedeutung\" corrected to \"Bedeutung\"; \"Gränze/Grenze\", \"doctrinalen\" = doctrinal; Sinn = sense; Schein = illusion; Grundsatz = principle (Satz in 1572–1573 = proposition).","- krv-b-1575–1576: \"to\" and \"7\" are OCR debris; \"to\" left as an isolated fragment, \"7\" dropped. Unbedingt/Unendliche = unconditioned/infinity; \"in terminis\" and \"in indefinitum\" kept in Latin."]],"krv-b-1577":["The mere general representation of the series of all past states of the world, likewise of the things that are simultaneously in cosmic space, is itself nothing other than a possible empirical regress, which I think to myself, though still indeterminately, and through which alone the concept of such a series of conditions for the given perception can arise.“) Now I have the whole of the world at all times only in the concept, but in no way (as a whole) in intuition. Therefore I cannot infer from its magnitude to the magnitude of the regress and determine the latter in accordance with the former, but I must first of all make for myself a concept of the magnitude of the world through the magnitude of the empirical regress. Of this, however, I never know anything more than that from every given member of the series of conditions I must always still proceed empirically to a higher (more remote) member. Therefore the magnitude of the whole of appearances is not determined at all absolutely by this, and consequently one also cannot say that this regress goes to infinity, since this would anticipate the members to which the regress has not yet attained and would represent their multitude as so great that no empirical synthesis can attain to it, and consequently would determine the magnitude of the world prior to the regress (even if only negatively), which is impossible. For this is not given to me through any intuition (according to its totality), consequently its magnitude is also not given at all prior to the regress. Accordingly we can say nothing at all about the magnitude of the world in itself, not even that a *regressus in infinitum* takes place in it, but must seek the concept of its magnitude only in accordance with the rule that determines the empirical regress in it. This rule, however, says nothing more than that, however far we may have come in the series of empirical conditions, we are to assume no absolute boundary anywhere, but are to subordinate every appearance, as conditioned, to another as its condition, and thus must proceed further to this one, which is the *regressus in indefinitum*, which, because it determines no magnitude in the object, can be distinguished distinctly enough from the one *in infinitum*.",["- Eingangs \"“)\" is an OCR remnant of a footnote marker/asterisk; kept as it stands, footnote text is not in this passage (cf. krv-b-1579).","- \"Weltzustände\" = states of the world (Zustand = state); \"Weltraume\" rendered \"cosmic space\" (Raum = space).","- Gränze = boundary; Größe = magnitude; Regressus left in Latin as in the text; \"Weltganze\" = whole of the world.","- \"Object\" in the German is rendered \"object\" (Gegenstand and Object both \"object\").","- \"keines— weges\", \"nir— gend\", \"ent— fernteren\" are OCR line-break hyphenations, silently corrected.","- \"anticipiren\" = anticipate; \"Menge\" = multitude.","- \"deutlich\" = distinct(ly), per glossary."]],"krv-b-1578":["I can accordingly not say: the world is infinite with respect to past time or to space. For such a concept of magnitude, as a given infinity, is empirical, consequently also absolutely impossible with regard to the world as an object of the senses. I will also not say: the regress from a given perception",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"Wahr—\" completed as \"Wahrnehmung\" only from the next passage's text, not here); translated as far as it stands, with the hyphenated \"Wahr—\" read as \"Wahrnehmung\" (perception).","- \"Gegenstand\" = object.","- \"Ansehung\" in \"in Ansehung\" rendered \"with regard to\"."]],"krv-b-1579":[") This series of the world can therefore be neither greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress, on which alone its concept rests. And since this can give neither a determinate infinite, nor, just as little, a determinate finite (absolutely bounded), it is clear from this that we can assume the magnitude of the world neither as finite nor as infinite, because the regress (through which it is represented) admits of neither of the two. to everything that bounds it in a series, in space as well as in past | time, goes to infinity; for this presupposes the infinite magnitude of the world; nor: it is finite; for the absolute boundary is likewise empirically impossible. Accordingly I will be able to say nothing about the entire object of experience (the world of sense), but only about the rule according to which experience is to be instituted and continued in a manner appropriate to its object.",["- The footnote text (opening \")\" = footnote marker) is interleaved with the main text's continuation \"nehmung an zu allem dem…\"; kept in the order of the passage as given, with \"nehmung\" joining \"Wahr—\" of the preceding passage. The fragment \"to everything that bounds…\" thus continues \"the regress from a given perception\".","- The sign | is carried over as in the source.","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = world of sense; \"Erfahrung\" = experience; \"angemessen\" = appropriate/in a manner appropriate to.","- \"begränzt\" = bounded; \"Gränze\" = boundary."]],"krv-b-1580":["To the cosmological question, therefore, regarding the magnitude of the world, the first | and negative answer is: the world has no first beginning in time and no outermost boundary in space. 16",["- The \"16\" is a stray footnote-marker remnant (OCR), left as it stands.","- The sign | is carried over as in the source.","- \"Anfang\" = beginning."]],"krv-b-1581":["For in the opposite case it would be bounded by empty time on the one side and by empty space on the other. Since, now, as appearance it can be neither of the two in itself, for appearance is no thing in itself, a perception of the boundary through absolutely empty time or empty space would have to be possible, through 1 which these ends of the world would be given in a possible experience. But such an experience, as wholly empty of content, is impossible. Therefore an absolute boundary of the world is empirically, consequently also absolutely, impossible“).",["- \"einerund\" = \"einer- und\" (OCR), read \"on the one side and\".","- The stray \"1\" and the closing \"“)\" are OCR remnants (footnote marker), left as they stand.","- \"an sich selbst\" = in itself; \"Ding an sich selbst\" = thing in itself; \"Inhalt\" = content.","- \"schlechterdings\" = absolutely; \"schlechthin\" = absolutely (same English word for both)."]],"krv-b-1582":["From this there follows at once the affirmative answer: the regress 2% in the series of the appearances of the world, as a determination of the magnitude of the world, goes *in indefinitum*, which says just as much as: the world of sense has no absolute magnitude, but the empirical regress (through which alone it can be given on the side of its conditions) has its rule, namely from every member of the series, as a conditioned one, always 2. to a still more remote one (be it through one's own experience, or the guiding thread of history, or the chain of effects and their causes) to proceed and not anywhere to be relieved of the extension of the possible empirical use of its understanding |f",["- \"2%\", \"2.\" and \"|f\" are OCR remnants (line/page markers); kept as they stand. \"|\" is an apparatus sign carried over.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; its end (\"…Gebrauchs seines Verstandes nirgend zu überheben\") is partly interleaved from the note in krv-b-1583 and is rendered here as it stands in the passage, with \"Ge- |f\" left incomplete.","- Bestimmung = determination; Bedingten = conditioned; Wirkungen = effects; Erweiterung = extension (here \"Erweiterung\", not Ausdehnung).","- \"Leitfaden\" = guiding thread."]],"krv-b-1583":[") It will be noticed: that the proof here has been conducted in a quite different way from the dogmatic one above in the antithesis of the first antinomy. There 30 we had allowed the world of sense, according to the common and dogmatic way of representation, to count as a thing that was given in itself prior to every regress as to its totality, and had denied it, if it did not occupy all time and all spaces, any determinate place at all in both. Hence the consequence was also different from here, namely it was concluded to the actual infinity 35 of the same. use of its understanding anywhere, which is also the proper and sole business of reason in its principles.",["- Footnote text, with the continuation of the main-text sentence (\"brauchs seines Verstandes nirgend zu überheben…\") interleaved after the footnote; \"brauchs\" joins \"Ge-\" of the preceding passage (\"Gebrauchs\"). Rendered \"use of its understanding anywhere [to be relieved of]\"; the fragment \"zu überheben\" is rendered only as far as it stands.","- Line numbers 30 and 35 are kept as in the passage; they would normally be dropped as page/line apparatus.","- \"Vorstellungsart\" = way of representation; \"Art\" = way; \"Folgerung\" = consequence; \"Unendlichkeit\" = infinity; \"wirklich\" = actual.","- \"Geschäfte\" = business; \"Principien\" = principles."]],"krv-b-1584":["A determinate empirical regress that would proceed without ceasing in a certain kind of appearances is not prescribed by this, 5 e.g., that one must always ascend from a living human being in a series of ancestors, without expecting a first pair, or in the series of cosmic bodies, without admitting an outermost sun; rather only the progress from appearances to appearances is commanded, even if these should yield no actual perception (if it is too weak in degree for our consciousness to become experience), since they nevertheless belong to possible experience.",["- \"5\" is a line number retained from the passage; \"vorgeschrie— ben\" = \"vorgeschrieben\" (OCR hyphenation corrected).","- \"Art\" = kind; \"Grad\" = degree; \"Bewußtsein\" = consciousness; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception.","- The \"sie\" in \"wenn sie dem Grade nach…\" is left as ambiguous as the German (the perception, or the appearances)."]],"krv-b-1585":["Every beginning is in time and every boundary of the extended in space. Space and time, however, are only in the world of sense. Hence only appearances in the world are conditioned, but the world itself is neither conditioned, nor bounded in an unconditioned way.",["- \"bedingterweise\" = \"in a conditioned way\" rendered \"conditioned\"; \"auf unbedingte Art\" = in an unconditioned way (Art = way).","- \"Ausgedehnten\" = the extended (Ausdehnung = extension)."]],"krv-b-1586":["Precisely for this reason, and since the world can never be given whole and even the series of conditions for a given conditioned cannot be given whole as a series of the world, the concept of the magnitude of the world is given only through the regress and not prior to it in a collective intuition. That, however, always consists only in determining the magnitude and thus gives no determinate concept, hence also no concept of a magnitude that would be infinite with respect to a certain measure, thus does not go to infinity (as it were given), but to an indeterminate distance, in order to give a magnitude (of experience) which first becomes actual through this regress.",["- The German of the second sentence is corrupt or elliptical (\"giebt also seinen bestimmten Begriff, also auch keinen Begriff…\"); rendered as it stands (\"gives no determinate concept\" for \"seinen bestimmten Begriff\" is not literal—the text reads \"gives … its determinate concept\"; probable OCR/printing reading \"keinen bestimmten Begriff\", not emended).","- \"collectiven Anschauung\" = collective intuition; \"Maß\" = measure; \"Weite\" = distance; \"wirklich\" = actual.","- \"Jener\" = \"That\" (referring to the regress, ambiguity preserved)."]],"krv-b-1587":["II.\nResolution of the cosmological idea\nof the\ntotality of the division of a given whole\nin intuition.",["- \"Auflösung\" = resolution; \"Totalität\" = totality (Allheit in the glossary is a different word); \"Theilung\" = division; \"Anschauung\" = intuition."]],"krv-b-1588":["If I divide a whole that is given in intuition, I proceed from something conditioned to the conditions of its possibility. The division of the parts (*subdivisio* or *decompositio*) is a regress in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be given only if the regress could arrive at simple parts. But if all parts in a continuously progressing decomposition are always divisible again, then the division, i.e., the regress, goes from the conditioned to its conditions *in infinitum*: because the conditions (the parts) are contained in the conditioned itself and, since this is given entirely in an intuition enclosed between its boundaries, are all together also given with it. The regress may therefore not be called merely a regress *in indefinitum*, as the previous cosmological idea alone permitted, since there I was to proceed from the conditioned to its conditions, which were outside it, and thus were not given at the same time along with it, but which were first added in the empirical regress. Notwithstanding this, it is still by no means permitted to say of such a whole, which is divisible to infinity: that it consists of infinitely many parts. For although all parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is nevertheless not contained in it, which consists only in the progressing decomposition or in the regress itself, which first makes the series actual. Since this regress is now infinite, all members (parts) to which it arrives are indeed contained in the given whole as aggregates, but not the whole series of division, which is successively infinite and never whole, and consequently cannot exhibit any infinite multitude, nor any taking-together of it in a whole.",[]],"krv-b-1589":["The general reminder can first be applied very easily to space. Every space intuited within its boundaries is such a whole, the parts of which, in every decomposition, are always again spaces, and it is therefore divisible to infinity.",[]],"krv-b-1590":["From this also follows quite naturally the second application, to an outer appearance (body) enclosed within its boundaries. The divisibility of the latter is grounded on the divisibility of space, which constitutes the possibility of the body as an extended whole. This is therefore divisible to infinity, without consisting of infinitely many parts on that account.",[]],"krv-b-1591":["It seems, to be sure: that since a body must be represented as substance in space, it will be different from the latter in this, as concerns the law of the divisibility of space; for one can concede, at any rate, that decomposition in the latter could never remove all composition, since then even all space, which otherwise has nothing self-subsistent, would cease (which is impossible); but that, if all composition of matter were canceled in thought, nothing at all should remain, does not seem to let itself be united with the concept of a substance, which should properly be the subject of all composition and would have to remain in its elements, even if their connection in space, through which they constitute a body, were canceled. But with what is called substance in the appearance, it is not as it is with it, as one would indeed think it of a thing in itself through a pure concept of the understanding. The former is not an absolute subject, but a persisting image of sensibility and nothing but intuition, in which nothing unconditioned is encountered anywhere.",[]],"krv-b-1592":["Now although this rule of progress to infinity without any doubt takes place in the subdivision of an appearance as a mere filling of space: it still cannot hold if we want to extend it also to the multitude of parts already separated in a certain way in the given whole, through which they constitute a *quantum discretum*. To assume that in every articulated (organized) whole every part is again articulated, and that in such a way, in the dissection of the parts, one encounters ever new artificial parts to infinity, in a word, that the whole is articulated to infinity, does not let itself be thought at all, although it can well be thought that the parts of matter could be articulated to infinity in their decomposition. For the infinity of the division of a given appearance in space is grounded solely on this, that through it merely the divisibility, i.e., a multitude of parts that is in itself absolutely undetermined, is given, but the parts themselves are given and determined only through the subdivision, in short that the whole is not already divided in itself. Hence the division can determine a multitude in it, which goes as far as one wants to advance in the regress of the division. On the other hand, in an organic body articulated to infinity, the whole is already represented as divided precisely through this concept, and a multitude of parts that is determinate in itself, but infinite, is encountered in it prior to all regress of division; whereby one contradicts oneself, in that this infinite enfolding is regarded as a series never to be completed (infinite) and nevertheless as completed in a taking-together. The infinite division designates the appearance only as *quantum continuum* and is inseparable from the filling of space, because precisely in the latter lies the ground of infinite divisibility. But as soon as something is assumed as *quantum discretum*, the multitude of units in it is determined, hence also always equal to a number. How far organization may go in an articulated body, therefore, only experience can settle, and even if it arrived with certainty at no unorganized part, such parts must at least lie in possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of an appearance in general extends is not at all a matter of experience, but a principle of reason, never to hold the empirical regress in the decomposition of the extended, in accordance with the nature of this appearance, as absolutely completed.",[]],"krv-b-1593":["Concluding Remark\non the",[]],"krv-b-1594":["Resolution of the mathematical transcendental and",[]],"krv-b-1595":["Preliminary Reminder on the Resolution of the dynamical\ntranscendental Ideas.",[]],"krv-b-1596":["When we presented the antinomy of pure reason through all transcendental ideas in a table, since we indicated the ground of this conflict and the sole means of removing it, which consisted in this, that both opposed assertions were declared false: we have everywhere represented the conditions as belonging to their conditioned according to relations of space and time, which is the ordinary presupposition of common human understanding, on which that conflict also entirely rested. In this respect all dialectical representations of totality in the series of conditions for a given conditioned were also through and through of the same kind. It was always a series in which the condition was connected with the conditioned as members of it and were thereby homogeneous, whereby the regress was never thought as completed, or, if this were to happen, a member conditioned in itself would have to be falsely assumed as a first, hence as unconditioned. Thus not everywhere the object, i.e., the conditioned, but yet the series of conditions for it was considered merely according to its magnitude, and there the difficulty, which could be removed by no comparison, but only by a complete cutting off of the knot, consisted in this, that reason made it either too long or too short for the understanding, so that the latter could never equal its idea.\n\n---notes\n- krv-b-1588: Theilung/theilen = \"division/divide\"; Bedingtes = \"the conditioned\"; Totalität (spelled so) rendered \"totality\" (glossary Allheit); Anschauung = intuition; einfach = simple; Menge = multitude; Zusammennehmung = \"taking-together\". \"Thei— iung\" OCR corrected to Theilung.\n- krv-b-1588: \"zod\" in the OCR (\"welche zod successiv unendlich\") corrected silently to \"nur successiv\" is not warranted by the text; I read it as \"successively\" (probable original \"welche nur successiv unendlich\"); doubtful reading.\n- krv-b-1588: \"Gränzen\" = \"boundaries\" (Grenze); \"Glieder\" = \"members\"; \"Aggregate\" = \"aggregates\".\n- krv-b-1591: \"Es feint\" corrected to \"Es scheint\". \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Zusammensetzung\" = composition; \"Gesetz\" = law; \"Ding an sich selbst\" = \"thing in itself\"; \"reinen Verstandesbegriff\" = \"pure concept of the understanding\". \"Jenes\" is translated \"The former,\" and \"mit ... es\" (\"it is not as it is with it\") preserves the German's awkward pronouns.\n- krv-b-1591: \"von diesem unterschieden sein werde\" and the pronouns \"letzteren\"/\"Jenes\" are kept as in German; \"Bild\" = \"image\".\n- krv-b-1592: \"Zerlegung\" = \"dissection\"; \"Kunsttheile\" = \"artificial parts\"; \"Einwickelung\" = \"enfolding\"; \"gegliedert (organisirt)\" = \"articulated (organized)\"; \"Principium\" = \"principle\" (Latin form in source, rendered by the English word). \"quantum continnum\" corrected to *quantum continuum*; \"Rez gressus\" to \"Regressus\"; \"gleidhwohl\" to \"gleichwohl\". \"Erfüllung des Raumes\" = \"filling of space\".\n- krv-b-1592: \"an sich ... bestimmte\" = \"determinate in itself\"; Menge, Zahl = multitude, number. \"Wie weit ... Theilung ... erstrecke\" kept as is.\n- krv-b-1596: \"Object\" (roman type in source) and \"Gegenstand\" both would be \"object\"; here only Object occurs. \"Widerstreit\" = \"conflict\"; \"Behauptungen\" = \"assertions\"; \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"gemeinen Menschenverstandes\" = \"common human understanding\"; \"Rüdficht\" corrected to \"Rücksicht\"; \"solte\" to \"sollte\". \"dieser ihrer Idee\" preserved ambiguous in \"the latter ... its idea\" (understanding/reason: \"dieser\" = understanding).\n- krv-b-1593–1595: headings fragmentary (text breaks off in \"zur\", \"und\"); translated as they stand. \"Auflösung\" = \"resolution\"; \"Vorerinnerung\" = \"preliminary reminder\".",[]],"krv-b-1597":["But we have here overlooked an essential difference that prevails among the objects, i.e., the concepts of the understanding, that reason strives to elevate to ideas, since, namely, according to our table of the categories above, two of them signify a mathematical, but the two remaining a dynamical synthesis of appearances. Up to this point this could also very well happen, in that, just as in the general representation of all transcendental ideas we always remained only under conditions in the appearance, so likewise in the two mathematically transcendental ones we had no other object than the one in the appearance. But now, since we proceed to dynamical concepts of the understanding, insofar as they are to fit the idea of reason, that distinction becomes important and opens up to us an entirely new prospect with regard to the dispute in which reason is entangled; and which, since it was previously dismissed as built on false presuppositions on both sides, now, since perhaps in the dynamical antinomy such a presupposition takes place as can subsist together with the pretension of reason, from this point of view and, since the judge supplies the lack of grounds of right which one had misjudged on both sides,",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (continued in the next passage); left uncompleted.","- Objecten / Verstandesbegriffen: \"objects\" / \"concepts of the understanding\"; Object (Latinate spelling) rendered \"object,\" as for Gegenstand later in the same sentence group.","- Erscheinung = appearance; Synthesis left as \"synthesis\"; bedeuten = signify.","- \"Dieses\" (Bis hieher konnte dieses...) rendered \"this,\" its antecedent left as ambiguous as in German.","- The relative clause \"und welcher, da er vorher...\" has no completed main verb here; \"welcher\" (masculine) refers back to the Streithandel; kept as in German.","- \"allge— meinen,\" \"Be— dingungen\": OCR line-break hyphenation silently corrected.","- Prätension rendered \"pretension\"; Rechtsgründe = \"grounds of right\"; verkannt = \"misjudged\" (doubtful nuance: also \"failed to recognize\")."]],"krv-b-1598":["the satisfaction of both parts can be reconciled, which could not be done in the dispute in the mathematical antinomy.",["- Continuation of the preceding sentence; \"beider Theile Genugthuung\" rendered \"the satisfaction of both parts\" (Theil = part).","- verglichen werden = \"be reconciled\" (also \"be compared/settled\"; the German bears both senses)."]],"krv-b-1599":["The series of conditions are, to be sure, all homogeneous insofar as one looks merely at their extension: whether they are adequate to the idea, or whether the latter is too large or too small for them. But the concept of the understanding that lies at the ground of these ideas contains either merely a synthesis of the homogeneous (which is presupposed in every magnitude, in its composition as well as division), or also of the heterogeneous, which can at least be admitted in the dynamical synthesis, in that of the causal combination as well as in that of the necessary with the contingent.",["- gleichartig / Ungleichartig = homogeneous / heterogeneous (literally \"of the same kind / of unlike kind\").","- Erstreckung = extension (a different German word from Ausdehnung, which the glossary also renders \"extension\").","- Größe = magnitude; Zusammensetzung = composition; Teilung = division; Causalverbindung = causal combination (Verbindung = combination).","- \"derselben\" after Größe could refer to Größe or to Synthesis; left ambiguous.","- \"der des Nothwendigen\" : \"der\" refers back to Synthesis; kept as in German.","- Zufälligen = contingent; Nothwendigen = necessary."]],"krv-b-1600":["Hence it comes about that in the mathematical connection of the series of appearances no other than a sensible condition can enter, i.e., one that is itself a part of the series; whereas the dynamical series of sensible conditions nevertheless still admits a heterogeneous condition, which is not a part of the series but, as merely intelligible, lies outside the series, whereby then a satisfaction is done to reason and the unconditioned is placed before the appearances, without confusing the series of the latter, as always conditioned, thereby, and breaking it off contrary to the principles of the understanding.",["- Verknüpfung = connection; Verstandesgrundsätze = principles of the understanding (Grundsatz = principle).","- intelligibel = intelligible; sinnlich = sensible.","- \"ein Genüge gethan\" = \"a satisfaction is done\" (literal idiom: \"enough is done\").","- \"als jederzeit bedingt\" could modify \"Reihe\" or \"Erscheinungen\"; left as in German.","- \"dadurch\" is placed after \"verwirren\" in the German; the English keeps that order."]],"krv-b-1601":["Through the fact now that the dynamical ideas admit a condition of appearances outside their series, i.e., one that is itself not appearance, something happens that is entirely distinct from the outcome of the mathematical antinomy. For the latter caused both dialectical counter-assertions to have to be declared false. On the other hand the thoroughgoing conditioned of the dynamical series, which is inseparable from them as appearances, connected with the empirically unconditioned but also nonsensible condition, satisfy the understanding on the one side and reason on the other,*) and, since the dialectical arguments, which sought unconditioned totality in mere appearances in one way or another, fall away, the propositions of reason, in the meaning rectified in such a way, can both be true; which can never take place with the cosmological ideas that concern merely mathematically unconditioned unity, because with them no condition of the series of appearances is encountered that is not itself also appearance and as such constitutes a member of the series.",["- The sentence from \"Dagegen\" is grammatically irregular in the German (infinitive \"leisten\" and \"wegfallen\" with no finite governing verb; \"dagegen\" repeated); preserved as it stands, with finite verbs supplied only as English requires (\"satisfy,\" \"fall away\").","- The footnote marker (OCR \"“)\") placed after \"andererseits\" as *); footnote text is in krv-b-1605.","- Erfolg = outcome; Gegenbehauptungen = counter-assertions; Vernunftsätze = propositions of reason (Satz = proposition).","- Totalität = totality (Allheit is the glossary term; here the Latinate Totalität is also \"totality\"); Glied = member.","- \"erichtigten\" is an OCR slip for \"berichtigten\"; rendered \"rectified.\"","- \"unzertrennlich ... von ihnen\": \"ihnen\" refers to the dynamical series; kept."]],"krv-b-1602":["III.\nResolution of the cosmological ideas\nof the",["- Running title/heading, continued in the next two passages; Auflösung = resolution (not in glossary)."]],"krv-b-1603":["Totality of the derivation of world-occurrences from their",["- Weltbegebenheiten = \"world-occurrences\" (Welt = world; Begebenheit = occurrence).","- Totalität = totality; Ableitung = derivation."]],"krv-b-1604":["causes.",["- OCR \"Nr sachen\" corrected to \"Ursachen\" (the heading's ending \"Ursachen\" is split across lines); doubtful OCR, but the reading is secure from the title."]],"krv-b-1605":["*) For the understanding permits among appearances no condition that would itself be empirically unconditioned. But if an intelligible condition, which therefore would not belong in the series of appearances as a member, could be thought for a conditioned (in the appearance), without thereby interrupting in the least the series of empirical conditions: then such a one could as empirically",["- Footnote text breaks off mid-sentence (\"als empirisch ...\"); in the German the continuation is the passage at krv-b-1608's embedded fragment (\"unbedingt zugelassen werden, ...\"), which I leave where it stands.","- intelligibele = intelligible; gedenken = think (here \"be thought\")."]],"krv-b-1606":["One can think only two kinds of causality with regard to what happens, either according to nature or from freedom. The first is the connection of a state with a preceding one in the world of sense, which the former follows according to a rule. Since now the causality of appearances rests on conditions of time, and the preceding state, if it had always been, would also not have produced an effect that first arises in time: so the causality of the cause of what happens or arises has also arisen and, according to the principle of the understanding, itself in turn requires a cause.",["- Sinnenwelt = world of sense (Welt = world; Sinn = sense).","- Zustand = state; Wirkung = effect; Verstandesgrundsatz = principle of the understanding.","- \"Die: erste\" : OCR colon dropped as stray.","- \"geschieht oder entsteht\" = \"happens or arises\"; \"entstanden\" = \"arisen.\"","- \"jener\" (the former) refers to the later state; \"Regel\" = rule."]],"krv-b-1607":["On the other hand I understand by freedom in the cosmological sense the faculty of beginning a state of itself, whose causality therefore does not in turn stand, according to the law of nature, under another cause that would determine it as to time. Freedom in this meaning is a pure transcendental idea, which first contains nothing borrowed from experience, second whose object also cannot be given determinately in any experience, because it is a universal law even of the possibility of all experience that everything that happens must have a cause, hence also the causality of the cause, which itself happened or arose, must in turn have a cause; through which then the whole field of experience, however far it may extend, is transformed into a sum total of mere nature. But since in such a way no absolute totality of conditions in the causal relation is to be obtained, reason creates for itself the idea of a spontaneity that could begin of itself to act, without another cause having to be sent ahead to determine it in turn to action according to the law of causal connection.",["- Vermögen = faculty; von selbst = \"of itself\" (also \"by itself,\" \"spontaneously\").","- \"deren Causalität\" : the German relative \"deren\" is grammatically irregular (feminine, after Zustand/Vermögen); its antecedent is left open as in the German (faculty or freedom).","- \"welche sie der Zeit nach bestimmte\": \"welche\" = the cause; \"sie\" = the causality; subjunctive kept as \"would determine.\"","- Verstande (cosmological sense) = \"sense,\" literally \"understanding\"; Verstand elsewhere = understanding.","- Inbegriff = sum total; Gegenstand = object; Erfahrung = experience; Idee = idea.","- \"die selbst geschehen oder entständen\": subjunctive/indicative mixed in the German; rendered \"which itself happened or arose.\"","- Causalverhältnisse = causal relation; Causalverknüpfung = causal connection."]],"krv-b-1608":["It is exceedingly remarkable that on this transcendental idea of freedom the practical concept of it is grounded, and that the former constitutes in the latter the actual moment of the difficulties which have surrounded the question about its possibility from time immemorial. Freedom in the practical sense is the independence of the power of choice from necessitation by impulses of sensibility. For a power of choice is sensible insofar as it is pathologically affected (by motives of sensibility); it is called animal (*arbitrium brutum*) if it can be pathologically necessitated. The human power of choice is to be sure an *arbitrium sensitivum*, but not *brutum*, rather *liberum*, because sensibility does not make its action necessary, but a faculty dwells in the human being to determine himself of himself independently of necessitation by sensible impulses.\n \nunconditionally be admitted, so that thereby no interruption would occur anywhere to the empirical continuous regress.",["- The two lines after the paragraph (\"unbedingt zugelassen werden, ... Abbruch geschähe\") are the end of the footnote begun in krv-b-1605, displaced by OCR into this passage; the German text \"Sinn— / lichkeit\" is likewise split by it. I have kept the displaced text where it stands, without joining it to the footnote.","- Willkür = \"power of choice\" (not in glossary; the Cambridge rendering); Nöthigung = necessitation; Antrieb = impulse; Bewegursachen = motives (Bewegungsgrund = motive; here the compound \"Bewegursache,\" \"motive cause\").","- \"si\" before \"der praktische Begriff\": OCR slip, dropped.","- \"patholog isch\": OCR spacing corrected; pathologisch = pathologically.","- \"von jeder\" (\"from time immemorial\" is my reading of the OCR \"von jeder\" = \"von jeher\"; doubtful OCR).","- Moment = moment (left in Latinate form); Vermögen = faculty; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; sinnlich = sensible.","- Latin terms *arbitrium brutum, sensitivum, liberum* retained in italics."]],"krv-b-1609":["One easily sees that, if all causality in the world of sense were mere nature, then every occurrence would be determined by another in time according to necessary laws; and hence, since appearances, insofar as they determine the power of choice, would have to make every action necessary as their natural outcome, the abolition of transcendental freedom would at the same time extinguish all practical freedom. For the latter presupposes that, although something has not happened, it nevertheless should have happened, and its cause in the appearance therefore was not so determining that there does not lie in our power of choice a causality, independent of those natural causes and even against their power and influence, to produce something that is determined in the temporal order according to empirical laws, hence to begin a series of occurrences entirely of itself.",["- Begebenheit = occurrence; Erfolg = outcome; Aufhebung = abolition.","- OCR \"*\" after \"nach\" and \"dec stimmt\" (= \"bestimmt\") and the line number \"15\" silently removed; \"Willfür\" corrected to \"Willkür.\"","- \"so bestimmend war, daß nicht\" : negation preserved literally (\"was not so determining that there does not lie\").","- \"von selbst\" = \"of itself\"; \"ihre Gewalt\" = \"their power\" (Gewalt not in glossary; Kraft = power elsewhere, so \"power\" stands for two German words)."]],"krv-b-1610":["Thus what happens here is what is encountered in general in the conflict of a reason venturing beyond the boundaries of possible experience, that the problem is properly not physiological but transcendental. Hence the question of the possibility of freedom, though it challenges psychology, yet, since it rests on dialectical arguments of merely pure reason, must together with its resolution occupy solely transcendental philosophy. In order now to put this philosophy, which cannot decline a satisfying answer concerning this, in a position to give it, I must first seek to determine its procedure in this problem more closely through a remark.",["- Aufgabe = problem; Grenze = boundary; Erfahrung = experience; Vernunft = reason; Transscendentalphilosophie rendered \"transcendental philosophy.\"","- \"dazu in Stand zu setzen\" rendered \"to put ... in a position to give it\"; \"dazu\" has no explicit object in the German (\"give it\" supplied only as English requires).","- \"diese ... ihr/ihrer\": \"this philosophy\" (Transscendentalphilosophie) is the antecedent of \"diese\"; \"ihr Verfahren\" could in German also be read as referring to it; kept as \"its.\"","- \"Daher die Frage ... 20 die Psychologie\": the line number \"20\" is an OCR intrusion and omitted. \"auge— troffen\" corrected to \"angetroffen.\""]],"krv-b-1611":["If appearances were things in themselves, and consequently space and time forms of the existence of things in themselves: then the conditions together with the conditioned would always belong as members to one and the same series, and from this the antinomy would also arise in the present case",["- Erscheinungen = appearances; Dinge an sich selbst = things in themselves; Dasein = existence; Bedingung / Bedingtes = condition / conditioned; Form = form.","- OCR \"fikh,\" corrected to \"sich\"; the passage breaks off mid-sentence (continues in the next passage)."]],"krv-b-1612":["which is common to all transcendental ideas, that this series would inevitably have to turn out too large or too small for the understanding. But the dynamical concepts of reason, with which we are occupied in this and the following number, have this peculiarity: that, since they have to do not with an object considered as magnitude, but only with its existence, one can also abstract from the magnitude of the series of conditions, and with them it is merely a matter of the dynamical relation of the condition to the conditioned, so that in the question about nature and freedom we already encounter the difficulty whether freedom is possible at all, and whether, if it is, it can subsist together with the universality of the natural law of causality; consequently whether it is a correctly disjunctive proposition that every effect in the world must arise either from nature or from freedom, or whether rather both can take place at once, in different relation, in one and the same occurrence. The correctness of that principle of the thoroughgoing interconnection of all occurrences of the sensible world in accordance with unalterable natural laws already stands firm as a principle of the transcendental analytic and suffers no infringement. Thus the question is only: whether, notwithstanding this, in regard to just the same effect that is determined in accordance with nature, freedom can also take place, or whether this is completely excluded by that inviolable rule. And here the indeed common but deceptive presupposition of the absolute reality of appearances immediately shows its disadvantageous influence in confusing reason. For if appearances are things in themselves, then freedom cannot be saved. Then nature is the complete and in itself sufficiently determining cause of every occurrence, and the condition of the latter is contained always only in the series of appearances, which together with their effect are necessary under the natural law. If, on the contrary, appearances count for nothing more than they in fact are, namely not for things in themselves, but mere representations, which are connected in accordance with empirical laws, then they must themselves still have grounds that are not appearances. But such an intelligible cause is not determined in regard to its causality through appearances, although its effects appear and can be determined through other appearances. It is thus together with its causality outside the series, whereas its effects are encountered in the series of empirical conditions. The effect can thus be regarded in regard to its intelligible cause as free and yet at the same time in regard to the appearances as a result from them in accordance with the necessity of nature; a distinction which, when it is presented in general and quite abstractly, must seem extremely subtle and obscure, but which will become clear in the application. Here I have only wanted to make the remark: that, since the thoroughgoing interconnection of all appearances in a context of nature is an unremitting law, this would necessarily overturn all freedom if one wanted to adhere obstinately to the reality of appearances. Hence also those who follow the common opinion in this have never been able to arrive at uniting nature and freedom with one another.",["- Begebenheit = occurrence; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Realität = reality; Meinung = opinion; Grundsatz / Satz = principle / proposition (Satz in \"disjunctiver Satz\" = proposition; Grundsatz = principle).","- Gegenstand = object; Größe = magnitude; Wirkung = effect; Vorstellung = representation; Grund = ground; Verstand = understanding; Vernunftbegriffe = concepts of reason.","- \"Natur\" in \"Context der Natur\" and \"nach der Natur\" retained as \"nature\"; \"Erfolg\" = \"result.\"","- \"mithin ob\" kept as \"consequently whether.\" \"in verschiedener Beziehung\" could attach to \"both\" or to \"take place\"; left as in German.","- OCR line numbers \"8\" and \"5\" and stray quotation mark before \"durch jene\" omitted; \"auge—\" etc. and \"Be— gebenheit,\" \"Ge— setzen,\" \"hart— näckig\" rejoined. \"anfechten\" in 1610 / \"Abbruch\" = infringement.","- \"dynamischen\" = \"dynamical\"; \"intelligibele\" = \"intelligible\"; \"sensibel\" appears in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1613":["Possibility of causality through freedom",["- Heading fragment (continues in krv-b-1614 and krv-b-1615); Möglichkeit = possibility; Causalität = causality; Freiheit = freedom."]],"krv-b-1614":["in combination with the general law of",["- Heading fragment; \"Vereinigung\" rendered \"combination\" here for lack of a glossary entry — note that Verbindung (glossary) is also \"combination\" elsewhere (see krv-b-1616); Vereinigung is the German behind it here."]],"krv-b-1615":["natural necessity.",["- Naturnothwendigkeit = \"natural necessity\" (Natur + notwendig = necessary)."]],"krv-b-1616":["I call intelligible that in an object of the senses which is not itself appearance. If accordingly that which in the sensible world must be regarded as appearance also has in itself a faculty which is no object of sensible intuition, but through which it can nevertheless be the cause of appearances: then one can consider the causality of this being from two sides, as intelligible in accordance with its action as that of a thing in itself, and as sensible in accordance with the effects of the same as of an appearance in the world of sense. We would accordingly form of the faculty of such a subject an empirical, and likewise also an intellectual concept of its causality, which take place together in one and the same effect. Such a double side, to think the faculty of an object of the senses for oneself, contradicts none of the concepts that we have to form of appearances and of a possible experience. For since a transcendental object must lie at the ground of these, because they are in themselves no things, which determines them as mere representations, nothing prevents us from ascribing to this transcendental object, besides the property through which it appears, also a causality that is not appearance, although its effect is nevertheless encountered in the appearance. But every efficient cause must have a character, i.e., a law of its causality, without which it would not be a cause at all. And there we would have in a subject of the sensible world first an empirical character, through which its actions, as appearances, would stand through and through in interconnection with other appearances in accordance with constant natural laws and could be derived from them as their conditions, and thus in combination with these would make up members of a single series of the natural order. Second, one would have to grant it also an intelligible character, through which it is indeed the cause of those actions as appearances, but which itself stands under no conditions of sensibility and is itself not appearance. One could also call the former the character of such a thing in the appearance, the second the character of the thing in itself.",["- Sinnenwelt = world of sense / \"sensible world\" (Sinn = sense; sinnlich = sensible); \"sensibel\" retained as \"sensible\" alongside sinnlich, both = \"sensible\"; Gegenstand der Sinne = object of the senses.","- Anschauung = intuition; Vermögen = faculty; Wesen = being; Ding = thing; Begriff = concept; Beschaffenheit not used; \"Eigenschaft\" = property.","- \"Handlung\" = action; \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Zusammenhang\" = interconnection; Zustand not used.","- \"Eine solche doppelte Seite, das Vermögen ... sich zu denken\" kept awkward; \"welche bei einer und derselben Wirkung zusammen stattfinden\": \"welche\" could refer to the two concepts or to the two causalities; left ambiguous in English as \"which.\"","- \"wodurch es\" in \"dadurch es ... ist\": \"it\" = the subject (Subject), neuter pronoun; left unresolved.","- OCR \"zusam— wen\" read \"zusammen\"; \"aon\" read \"von.\""]],"krv-b-1617":["This acting subject would now, in accordance with its intelligible character, stand under no time-conditions, for time is only the condition of appearances, but not of things in themselves. In it",["- Zeitbedingungen = time-conditions; Bedingung = condition; passage breaks off, continues in next."]],"krv-b-1618":["no action would arise or pass away, and consequently it would",["- \"entstehen / vergehen\" = arise / pass away; fragment continues in next passage."]],"krv-b-1619":["also not be subject to the law of all time-determination, of everything alterable: that everything that happens in the appearances (of the",["- Zeitbestimmung = time-determination; Veränderlichen = alterable; geschehen = happen. Parenthesis left open, as in the source."]],"krv-b-1620":["previous state) encounters its cause. In a word, the causa-",["- Zustand = state; \"Causa-\" is a broken word, completed in the next passage (\"Causalität\"); left as \"causa-\" in the translation."]],"krv-b-1621":["lity of the same, insofar as it is intellectual, would stand not at all in the series of empirical conditions, which make the occurrence in the sensible world necessary. This intelligible character could indeed never be immediately known, because we can perceive nothing except insofar as it appears; but it would nevertheless have to be thought in accordance with the empirical character, just as we in general have to lay a transcendental object",["- gekannt = known (kennen); wahrnehmen = perceive; Begebenheit = occurrence; \"gemäß\" = in accordance with.","- \"causa-/lität\" broken across passages; \"immediately\" = unmittelbar."]],"krv-b-1622":["at the ground of appearances in thought, although we",["- Fragment; \"in Gedanken\" = \"in thought.\""]],"krv-b-1623":["know nothing of it, what it may be in itself. ! In accordance with its empirical character this subject would thus, as ap-",["- \"wissen\" = know (Wissen = knowledge, scientia); \"kennen\" is elsewhere also \"know,\" so both are \"know\" here (wissen/kennen). The \"!\" is an OCR artifact (possibly a footnote mark) and is kept; doubtful reading."]],"krv-b-1624":["pearance, be subject to all laws of determination in accordance with causal combination;",["- Causalverbindung = causal combination (Verbindung); Bestimmung = determination."]],"krv-b-1625":["and so far it would be nothing but a part of the world of sense,",["- Theil = part; Sinnenwelt = world of sense."]],"krv-b-1626":["whose effects, like every other appearance, would flow forth from nature unfailingly. Just as outer appearances flow into it,",["- \"unausbleiblich\" = unfailingly; \"abflössen / einflössen\" = flow forth / flow into (the OCR \"ö\" is a modernization of \"flössen\"; the German forms are subjunctive of abfließen / einfließen). Stray line number \"5\" omitted. \"dasselbe\" refers to the subject (neuter), left as \"it.\""]],"krv-b-1627":["as its empirical character, i.e. the law of its causality, were cognized through experience, all its actions would have to be explicable according to laws of nature, and all requisites for a complete and necessary determination of them would have to be encountered in a possible experience. According to the intelligible character of the same, however (although we can have of it nothing but merely the general concept of it)",["Passage begins mid-sentence (\"wie ... erkannt wäre\"); the \"wie\" is rendered \"as\" without supplying a main clause.","Stray OCR letters (\"b\", \"ö\") omitted. Erfahrung = experience; erkennen = cognize; Bestimmung = determination."]],"krv-b-1628":["nothing but merely the general concept of it) would",["Fragment: the sentence continues in the next passage. Begriff = concept."]],"krv-b-1629":["the same subject nevertheless have to be declared free of all influence of sensibility and determination through appearances; and since in it, insofar as it is noumenon, nothing happens, no alteration, which requires dynamical determination of time, hence no connection with appear-",["Breaks off in mid-word (\"Er-\" / \"scheinungen\"); the German word is Erscheinungen, \"appearances.\" Verknüpfung = connection; Veränderung = alteration.","Line number \"5\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1630":["ances as causes is encountered, this acting being would insofar in its actions be independent of and free from all natural necessity, such as is encountered solely in the world of sense. One would say quite correctly of it that it begins its effects in the world of sense of itself, without the action beginning in it itself; and this would be valid without the effects in the world of sense therefore having to begin of themselves, because in it they are always determined beforehand by empirical conditions in the previous time, but yet only by means of the empirical character (which is merely the appearance of the intelligible) and are possible only as a continuation of the series of natural causes. Thus freedom and nature, each in its full significance, would be encountered at once and without any conflict in the very same actions, depending on whether one compares them with their intelligible or sensible cause.",["Begins mid-word (\"-scheinungen\" completing \"Er-\" of the previous passage).","\"als die lediglich in der Sinnenwelt angetroffen wird\": \"als\" rendered \"such as\"; the antecedent is Naturnothwendigkeit.","\"ganz richtig sagen ... es\": \"it\" = Wesen (neuter). \"Bedeutung\" = significance. \"Widerstreit\" = conflict. \"Wirkungen\" = effects; \"gültig\" = valid.","\"sensibelen\" = sensible (Sinnenwelt = world of sense)."]],"krv-b-1631":["Elucidation",["Erläuterung = elucidation (glossary: clarification; elucidation)."]],"krv-b-1632":["--- notes\n\"Ed.. —\" is an OCR residue with no determinable reading; left untranslated and not supplied.",[]],"krv-b-1633":["of the cosmological idea of a freedom",["Idee = idea. Heading fragment continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1634":["in combination with general natural necessity.",["Verbindung = combination."]],"krv-b-1635":["I have found it good first to sketch the silhouette of the solution of our transcendental problem, so that the course of reason in the solution of it might thereby be better surveyed. Now we want to set out the moments of its decision, on which it actually depends, and to take each one particularly into consideration. |",["Erwägung = consideration (the glossary's Betrachtung is also \"consideration\"). Schattenriß = silhouette; Auflösung = solution.","Line-break hyphen in \"Ver-nunft\" repaired. \"besonders\" = particularly. The sign | is carried over from the source."]],"krv-b-1636":["The law of nature that everything that happens has a cause, that the causality of this cause, i.e. the action, since it precedes in time and in regard to an effect that has arisen cannot itself always have been but must have happened, also has its cause among the appearances, by which it is determined, and that consequently all occurrences are empirically determined in an order of nature: this law, through which appearances can first constitute a nature and yield objects of an experience, is a law of the understanding, from which it is permitted under no pretext to depart or to exempt any appearance from it; because otherwise one would posit it outside all possible experience, thereby distinguish it from all objects of possible experience, and make it into a mere thought-entity and a figment of the brain.",["OCR corrected: \"augmachen\" read as \"ausmachen\" (constitute); stray \"a\", \"zoi\" removed; \"Ge dankendinge\" = Gedankendinge.","Begebenheit = occurrence; Gegenstand = object; Gedankending = thought-entity; Hirngespinst = figment of the brain.","\"Ursache habe ... ihre Ursache\": the \"daß\" clauses are kept in one sentence as in the German; \"sie\" in \"sie sonst ... setzen\" is kept as \"it\" (referring to Erscheinung)."]],"krv-b-1637":["But although here it looks merely like a chain of causes, which in the regress to its conditions permits no absolute totality at all, this scruple does not detain us at all; for it has already been removed in the general judgment of the antinomy of reason, when it goes out in the series of appearances to the unconditioned, hen",["Error: see below."]],"krv-b-1638":["**(see correction)**",["Error: see below."]],"krv-b-1639":["**(see correction)**",["Error: see below."]],"krv-b-1640":["direct contradiction is encountered.",["- gerader Widerspruch = \"direct contradiction\"; Widerspruch = contradiction. Fragment: begins mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1641":["Among the causes in appearance there can certainly be nothing that could begin a series absolutely and of itself. Every action as appearance, insofar as it produces an occurrence, is itself",["- Erscheinung = appearance; Begebenheit = occurrence; Ursache = cause; Reihe = series.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1642":["occurrence or event, which presupposes another state,",["- Begebenheit = occurrence; Eräugniß (OCR \"Eräugniß\", i.e. Ereignis) = event: two German words, two English words here.","- Zustand = state. Fragment of a sentence, continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1643":["in which the cause is encountered; and thus everything that happens is only | a continuation of the series, and no beginning that would occur of itself is possible in it. Therefore all actions of natural causes in the time-sequence are themselves in turn effects, which just as much presuppose their causes in the time-series. An original action, through which something happens that was not there before, is not to be expected from the causal connection of the",["- OCR \"Mfo\" corrected to \"Also\" (rendered \"Therefore\"); \"moglich\" to \"möglich\"; \"Causalperknüpfung\" to \"Causalverknüpfung\" (causal connection; Verknüpfung = connection).","- Zeitfolge = time-sequence; Zeitreihe = time-series; Wirkung = effect; Fortsetzung = continuation.","- Passage begins and ends mid-sentence; the sign | is carried over."]],"krv-b-1644":["appearances.",["- Erscheinungen = appearances. Fragment: completes the sentence begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1645":["But is it then also necessary that, if the effects are appearances, the causality of their cause, which (namely the cause) is itself also appearance, must be merely empirical? And is it not rather possible that, although for every effect in appearance a connection with its cause according to laws of empirical causality is indeed required, this empirical causality itself, without interrupting in the least its interconnection with the natural causes, could nevertheless be an effect of a non-empirical, but intelligible causality? i.e., of an action of a cause that is original with regard to the appearances, which therefore insofar is not appearance, but intelligible according to this faculty, although otherwise it must be counted entirely as a link of the natural chain as belonging to the world of sense?",["- Verknüpfung = connection; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Vermögen = faculty; Gesetzen = laws.","- Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\" (Sinn = sense); Naturkette = \"natural chain\"; intelligibel = intelligible.","- \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\"; \"in so fern\" kept as \"insofar\".","- \"diesem Vermögen nach\" is rendered literally \"according to this faculty\".","- \"nach Gesetzen ... erfordert wird\": \"required\" for erfordert; \"fordern\" in the next passage = \"demand\"."]],"krv-b-1646":["We need the proposition of the causality of appearances among one another, in order to seek and be able to indicate natural conditions for natural occurrences, i.e., causes in appearance. If this is granted and is not weakened by any exception, then the understanding, which in its empirical use sees nothing but nature in all events and is also entitled to do so, has everything that it can demand, and the physical explanations go their unhindered way. Now it does not in the least detract from this, supposing that it should otherwise even be merely invented, if one assumes that among the natural causes there are also some that have a faculty that is only intelligible, in that the determination of the same to action never rests on empirical conditions, but on mere grounds of the understanding,",["- OCR slips corrected: \"und i die\" read \"und die\"; \"inteli- f gibel\" read \"intelligibel\". The \"i\" before \"die\" is a stray OCR mark.","- Satz = proposition; Naturbegebenheiten = natural occurrences; Eräugnissen = events (Begebenheit = occurrence, Eräugniß = event).","- Erklärungen = explanations; Bestimmung = determination; Gründen = grounds; Verstand = understanding.","- \"desselben\" refers grammatically to Vermögen (masc./neut. ambiguity with Ursache is excluded only by gender: Vermögen is neuter, so \"desselben\" = of the same faculty).","- \"gesetzt daß es ... erdichtet sein sollte\": \"es\" left unresolved.","- Passage ends with a comma; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1647":["yet in such a way that the action in appearance of this cause be in accordance with all laws of empirical causality. For in this way the acting subject, as *causa phaenomenon*, would be chained together with nature in undissolved dependence of all its actions, and only the *phaenomenon* of this subject (with all causality of the same in appearance) would contain certain conditions that, if one wants to ascend from the empirical object to the transcendental one, would have to be regarded as merely intelligible. For if only, in what may be the cause among the appearances, we follow the rule of nature:",["- OCR slips corrected: \"En so doch\" read \"Ebenso doch\"/\"so doch\" (the initial \"En\" is garbled; read as \"yet in such a way\", with \"eben so\" possible); \"ungertrennter\" read \"ungetrennter\" (undissolved/inseparable, literally \"un-severed\").","- The initial word is doubtful: the OCR \"En so doch\" may stand for \"Eben so doch\"; the translation follows the sense of \"so doch, daß\".","- *causa phaenomenon* and *phaenomenon*: Latin in the original, left in italics.","- Gegenstande = object (empirischen Gegenstande, transscendentalen); Gegenstand and Object both \"object\".","- Passage ends with a colon, mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1648":["so we can be unconcerned about what sort of ground of these appearances and of their interconnection may be thought in the transcendental subject, which is empirically unknown to us. This intelligible ground does not at all challenge the empirical questions, but concerns perhaps merely thinking in pure understanding; and although the effects of this thinking and acting of pure understanding are encountered in the appearances, these must nonetheless be able to be explained completely from their cause in appearance in accordance with laws of nature, by following their merely empirical character as the supreme ground of explanation and passing over entirely as unknown the intelligible character, which is the transcendental cause of the former, except insofar as it is indicated by the empirical one as the sensible sign of it. Let us apply this to experience. The human being is one of the appearances of the world of sense and to that extent also one of the natural causes, whose causality must stand under empirical laws. As such he must accordingly also have an empirical character, like all other natural things. We remark the same through forces and faculties that he expresses in his effects. In lifeless nature or nature merely animated animally we find no ground to think any faculty other than as merely sensibly conditioned. But the human being, who otherwise knows all of nature merely through the senses, cognizes himself also through mere apperception, and indeed in actions and inner determinations that he cannot count at all as belonging to the impression of the senses, and is to himself of course on the one part a phenomenon, on the other part, however, namely with regard to certain faculties, a merely intelligible object, because the action of it cannot be counted at all as belonging to the receptivity of sensibility. We call these faculties understanding and reason; chiefly the latter is quite properly and preeminently distinguished from all empirically conditioned forces, since it considers its objects merely according to ideas and determines the understanding accordingly, which then makes an empirical use of its (to be sure also pure) concepts. That this reason now has causality, or at least that we represent to ourselves something of the kind in it, is clear from the imperatives that we assign as rules to the exercising forces in everything practical. The ought expresses a kind of necessity and connection with grounds that does not otherwise occur in the whole of nature. The understanding can cognize of this only what is or has been or will be. It is impossible that something in it ought to be other than it in fact is in all these relations of time; indeed the ought, if one has merely the course of nature before one's eyes, has altogether no meaning. We cannot ask at all what ought to happen in nature, any more than what properties a circle ought to have; but rather what happens in it, or what properties the latter has.",["- The passage opens mid-sentence; the initial \"ES\" in the OCR is an obvious artifact and is left untranslated.","- Erscheinung = appearance; Phänomen (in roman/Latinate form) = \"phenomenon\".","- kennt = \"knows\" (kennen); erkennt = \"cognizes\" (erkennen); erwägt = \"considers\".","- Gegenstand = \"object\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; Eindrucke = \"impression\"; Bedeutung = \"meaning\".","- \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\" throughout; \"Kräften\" = \"forces\"; \"Apperception\" kept as the Latinate form.","- \"Ge— brauch\" and \"Erjcheinuns gen\" are line-break/OCR corrections.","- \"darnach\" in \"den Verstand darnach bestimmt\" is ambiguous (accordingly / according to them); \"accordingly\" kept."]],"krv-b-1649":["This ought now expresses a possible action, the ground of which is nothing other than a mere concept, whereas of a merely natural action the ground must always be an appearance. Now the action must certainly be possible under natural conditions if the ought is directed to it; but these natural conditions do not concern the determination of the power of choice itself, but only the effect and consequence of it in appearance. There may be ever so many natural grounds that impel me to willing, ever so many sensible stimuli, they cannot produce the ought, but only a willing that is still far from necessary, but always conditioned, to which the ought that reason pronounces opposes measure and goal, indeed prohibition and authority. Whether it be an object of mere sensibility (the agreeable) or also of pure reason (the good): reason does not yield to that ground which is given empirically and does not follow the order of things as they present themselves in appearance; but makes for itself with complete spontaneity an order of its own according to ideas, into which it fits the empirical conditions, and according to which it even declares necessary actions that have yet not happened and perhaps will not happen, of all of which, however, it presupposes that reason could have causality in relation to them; for without that it would not expect effects in experience from its ideas.",["- Willkür = \"power of choice\" (Latin arbitrium); Wollen = \"willing\".","- \"Verhot\" corrected to Verbot; the stray closing bracket \"]\" in the OCR is dropped.","- Grund = \"ground\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Erfolg = \"consequence\".","- \"Spontaneität\" = \"spontaneity\" (Latinate form).","- \"von allen aber\" is syntactically loose; the antecedent (all such actions) is left as in the German."]],"krv-b-1650":["Now let us stay with this and assume it at least as possible: reason has actually causality with regard to appearances; then it must, as much as it is reason, nevertheless show an empirical character of itself, because every cause presupposes a rule according to which certain appearances follow as effects, and every rule requires a uniformity of the effects, which grounds the concept of the cause (as a faculty), which we, insofar as it must become clear from mere appearances, can call its empirical character, which is constant, while the effects appear in alterable shapes according to the difference of the accompanying and partly limiting conditions.",["- \"welchen\" has \"Begriff\" as its nearest antecedent, but \"Vermögen\" and \"Ursache\" are also possible; the German's ambiguity is kept (\"which we ... can call its empirical character\").","- erhellen = \"become clear\"; Gleichförmigkeit = \"uniformity\"; Gestalten = \"shapes\".","- Wirkung = \"effect\"; Bedingungen = \"conditions\"; Begriff = \"concept\"."]],"krv-b-1651":["Thus every human being has an empirical character of his power of choice, which is nothing other than a certain causality of his reason, insofar as this shows in its effects in appearance a rule, according to which one can gather the grounds of reason and the actions of the same according to their kind and their degrees and can judge the subjective principles of his power of choice. Because this empirical character itself must be drawn from the appearances as effect and from the rule of the same, which experience gives to hand: so all actions of the human being in appearance are determined from his empirical character and the cooperating other causes according to the order of nature; and if we could investigate all appearances of his power of choice to the ground, there would be no single human action that we could not predict with certainty and cognize as necessary from its preceding conditions. With regard to this empirical character there is thus no freedom, and according to this",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage.","- Willkür = \"power of choice\"; Principien (Latinate) = \"principles\"; Art = \"kind\"; Grad = \"degree\".","- beurtheilen = \"judge\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; bestimmt = \"determined\".","- \"Vernunftgründe und die Handlungen derselben\": \"derselben\" may refer to the grounds or to reason; kept as \"of the same\".","- \"wurde\" corrected to würde."]],"krv-b-1652":["can we yet consider the human being only, if we merely obser-",["- Word divided across passages (beob- / achten). \"allein\" here is adverbial, \"only\"/\"alone\"; the German is not fully clear.","- \"Betrachten\" = \"consider\"."]],"krv-b-1653":["ve and, as happens in anthropology, want to investigate physiologically the moving causes of his actions.",["- Completes the word beob-achten begun in the preceding passage.","- bewegenden Ursachen = \"moving causes\"."]],"krv-b-1654":["But if we consider these very same actions in relation to the",["- Fragment of a sentence; continued in the next passages.","- erwägen = \"consider\" (the same English as betrachten above)."]],"krv-b-1655":["reason, and indeed not the speculative, in order to explain those according to their origin,",["- Vernunft = \"reason\"; \"jene\" = \"those\" (the actions); erklären = \"explain\"."]],"krv-b-1656":["but quite alone, insofar as reason is the cause,",["- Fragment; it continues in the next passage (\"sie selbst zu erzeugen\")."]],"krv-b-1657":["to generate them itself; in a word, we compare them with this",["- \"sie selbst\" (\"them itself\") is kept literally; \"selbst\" could be read as emphasizing the actions or reason as producer."]],"krv-b-1658":["in a practical aim, then we find a quite different rule and order than the order of nature is. For there perhaps all that should not have happened which yet according to the course of nature has happened and according to its empirical grounds had to happen inevitably. At times, however, we find, or at least believe to find, that the ideas of reason have actually proved causality with regard to the actions of the human being as appearances, and that they have happened for that reason, not because they were determined by empirical causes, no, but because they were determined by grounds of reason.",["- Absicht = \"aim\"; \"in praktischer Absicht\" = \"in a practical aim\".","- \"Wen— schen\" corrected to Menschen; stray line numerals (\"o\", \"5\") dropped.","- \"da\" in \"Denn da sollte\" is read as the adverb \"there\"; it could also be \"since\". Ambiguity kept as far as English allows.","- \"sollte ... geschehen sein\" rendered literally as \"should not have happened\"."]],"krv-b-1659":["Suppose now one could say that reason has causality with regard to appearance; could its action then well be called free, since in its empirical character (the way of sense) it is determined quite exactly and is necessary? This is in turn determined in the intelligible character (the way of thinking). The latter, however, we do not know, but designate it through appearances, which really only give the way of sense (empirical character) to be cognized immediately¹). The action now, insofar as it is to be ascribed to the way of thinking as its cause, nevertheless follows from it not at all according to empirical laws, i.e., so that the conditions of pure reason, but only so that its effects in the appearance of inner sense precede. Pure reason, as a merely intelligible faculty, is not subject to the form of time and consequently also not to the conditions of the time-sequence. The causality of reason in the intelligible character does not arise, or",["- Sinnesart = \"way of sense\"; Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\"; Zeitfolge = \"time-sequence\"; Causalität = \"causality\".","- The sentence \"d. i. so, daß die Bedingungen der reinen Vernunft, sondern nur so, daß…\" is rendered as printed; it appears to lack a word or clause (verb or negation) after \"Vernunft\", and nothing is supplied.","- The footnote marker (OCR \"“)\") is rendered ¹); the footnote text is in the next passage. \"Wir— kungen\" and the stray \"w\" are OCR slips, silently corrected or dropped.","- \"wirkungen ... vorhergehen\": \"its\" for \"deren\" could refer to reason or to the way of thinking; left as in German.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1660":["¹) The actual morality of actions (merit and guilt) therefore remains wholly hidden from us, even that of our own conduct. Our imputations can be referred only to the empirical character. But how much of it is to be ascribed to the pure effect of freedom, how much to mere nature and the blameless defect of temperament or its fortunate constitution (*merito fortunae*), no one can fathom, and hence also cannot judge according to complete justice.\n\nbegins not perhaps at a certain time in order to bring forth an effect. For otherwise it would itself be subject to the natural law of appearances, insofar as it determines causal series according to time; and the causality would then be nature and not freedom. Thus we will be able to say: if reason can have causality with regard to appearances, then it is a faculty through which the sensible condition of an empirical series of effects first begins. For the",["- The footnote (text through \"richten\") precedes the main text, which resumes the sentence broken off in the preceding passage (\"…nicht, oder / hebt nicht etwa…\"). The initial \")\" is the footnote marker.","- Zurechnungen = \"imputations\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; *merito fortunae* is Latin in the source.","- \"Bedin “ gnug\" and \"wi\" are OCR slips, read as \"Bedingung\" and \"wir\". \"Also werden wir sagen können\" rendered \"Thus\"; \"Causalreihen\" = \"causal series\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1661":["condition that lies in reason is not sensible and thus does not itself begin. Accordingly there then takes place that which we missed in all empirical series: that the condition of a successive series of occurrences could itself be empirically unconditioned. For here the condition is outside the series of appearances (in the intelligible) and consequently not subject to any sensible condition and to no time-determination by a preceding cause.",["- Begebenheiten = \"occurrences\"; \"konnte\" (= könnte) rendered \"could\"; Zeitbestimmung = \"time-determination\".","- The opening \"ee —\" is OCR noise and is dropped; the passage begins mid-sentence (\"Bedingung\")."]],"krv-b-1662":["Nevertheless this very same cause in another relation also belongs to the series of appearances. The human being is himself appearance. His power of choice has an empirical character, which is the (empirical) cause of all his actions. There is none of the conditions that determine the human being in accordance with this character that would not be contained in the series of natural effects and obey the law of it,",["- Willkür = \"power of choice\" (not in the glossary); willkürlich is rendered \"voluntary\" in the following passages.","- \"Bezie⸗ 1 f hung\" is an OCR slip for \"Beziehung\".","- \"derselben\" (law \"of it\") refers grammatically to the series of natural effects (Naturwirkungen); it is left as in the German.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1663":["according to which no empirically unconditioned causality of what happens in time is encountered at all. Hence no given action (because it can be perceived only as appearance) can begin absolutely of itself. But of reason one cannot say that before the state in which it determines the power of choice another precedes, in which this state itself is determined. For since reason itself is not an appearance and is not subject at all to any conditions of sensibility, there takes place in it itself, with respect to its causality, no time-sequence, and so the dynamical law of nature, which determines the time-sequence according to rules, cannot be applied to it.",["- The opening \"X\" and the closing \"Zi\" are OCR noise and are dropped; the passage begins mid-sentence (\"nach welchem\" has its antecedent in the preceding passage).","- wahrgenommen = \"perceived\"; Sinnlichkeit = \"sensibility\"; Willkür = \"power of choice\"; in Betreff = \"with respect to\".","- \"von selbst\" = \"of itself\"; \"schlechthin\" = \"absolutely\"."]],"krv-b-1664":["Reason is thus the persistent condition of all voluntary actions under which the human being appears. Each of them is determined beforehand in the empirical character of the human being, even before it happens. With regard to the intelligible character, of which the former is only the sensible schema, no before or after holds; and every action irre-",["- beharrlich = \"persistent\"; willkürlich = \"voluntary\"; Schema = \"schema\".","- \"iftim\" is read as \"ist im\"; the \"als\" in \"ehe noch als sie geschieht\" is doubtful (perhaps an OCR remnant) and is left untranslated.","- The passage breaks off in mid-word (\"unan-\", i.e. unangesehen); the English word is split likewise, and \"irre-spective\" continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1665":["spective of the time-relation in which it stands with other appearances, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason, which consequently acts freely, without being dynamically determined in the chain of natural causes by outer or inner grounds that precede in time; and this its freedom one can regard not only negatively as independence from empirical conditions (for … appearances to be), but also designate positively through a faculty of beginning a series of occurrences of itself, so that in it itself nothing begins, but, as unconditioned condition of every voluntary action, it permits above itself no conditions preceding in time, although its effect nevertheless begins in the series of appearances, but can never constitute therein an absolutely first beginning.",["- The opening \"en gesehen\" is the continuation of \"unan-\" (unangesehen) from the preceding passage.","- The parenthesis \"(denn scheinungen zu sein)\" is damaged in the OCR (the beginning of the clause is missing); it is rendered with an ellipsis and nothing is supplied.","- \"verho stattet\" is read as \"verstattet\" (\"permits\"); \"unbedingte Bedingung\" = \"unconditioned condition\"; Gründe = \"grounds\".","- \"dynamisch\" = \"dynamically\"; Begebenheiten = \"occurrences\".","- The reference of \"ihr/sie\" (reason or its freedom) is left as in the German."]],"krv-b-1666":["In order to elucidate the regulative principle of reason through an example from the empirical use of it, not to confirm it (for such proofs are unsuited to transcendental assertions), let one take a voluntary action, e.g., a malicious lie, through which a human being has brought a certain confusion into society, and which one first investigates according to its motivating causes, from which it arose, and thereupon assesses how it, together with its sequels, could be imputed to him. In the first aim one goes through his empirical character to the sources of it, which one seeks in bad upbringing, evil company, in part also in the malignancy of a natural disposition insensible to shame, in part pushes onto levity and thoughtlessness; whereby one then does not leave out of account the occasioning occasional causes. In all this one proceeds as in the investigation of the series of determining causes for a given natural effect in general. Although one now believes the action to be determined thereby: one nevertheless blames the perpetrator, and indeed not on account of his unhappy natural disposition, not on account of the circumstances flowing in on him, indeed not even on account of his previously led way of life; for one presupposes that one could set wholly aside how this has been constituted, and regard the elapsed series of conditions as not having occurred, but this deed as wholly unconditioned with regard to the previous state, as if the perpetrator with it began a series of sequels entirely of himself. This blame is grounded on a law of reason, whereby one regards this as a cause that could and should have determined the conduct of the human being otherwise, irrespective of all the named empirical conditions. And indeed one regards the causality of reason not perhaps merely as concurrence, but as complete in itself, even if the sensible incentives were not at all for it, but rather against it; the action is ascribed to his intelligible character, he now, in the moment when he lies, is wholly guilty; consequently reason was, notwithstanding all empirical conditions of the deed, fully free, and to its omission this is wholly to be ascribed.",["- \"Nempirischen\" is an OCR slip for \"empirischen\"; stray \"N\", \"w\" and \"IM\" are dropped.","- Bewegursachen = \"motivating causes\" (cf. Bewegungsgrund = \"motive\"); Absicht = \"aim\"; beurteilen = \"assess\"; Folgen = \"sequels\" (Folge in the glossary), though \"consequences\" would be the usual English; Naturell = \"natural disposition\".","- zurechnen / beimessen are both rendered \"impute\" / \"ascribe\" respectively (zugerechnet = \"imputed\", beigemessen / beizumessen = \"ascribed\").","- Triebfedern = \"incentives\"; Concurrenz is printed in German type and is rendered \"concurrence\"; Schuld = \"guilt\"; Unterlassung = \"omission\".","- \"wie dieser beschaffen gewesen\" is rendered \"how this has been constituted\"; \"dieser\" is grammatically ambiguous (Lebenswandel or the preceding state) and is left so. \"Er hat jetzt … gänzlich Schuld\" is rendered literally as \"he now … is wholly guilty\".","- \"dieses als eine Ursache\": \"this\" is ambiguous between reason and the blame's object; it is left as in the German."]],"krv-b-1667":["One easily sees from this imputing judgment that in thought one has it that reason is not affected at all by all that sensibility; that it does not alter itself (even if its appearances, namely the way it shows itself in its effects, alter); that in it no state precedes that determines the following one; and hence that it does not belong at all in the series of sensible conditions that make the appearances necessary according to laws of nature. It, reason, is present in all actions of the human being in all circumstances of time and the same, but it itself is not in time and does not, say, get into a new state in which it previously was not; it is determining, but not determinable with regard to it. Hence one cannot ask: why has reason not determined itself otherwise? but only: why has it not determined the appearances otherwise through its causality? But to this no answer is possible. For a different intelligible character would have given a different empirical one; and when we say that, notwithstanding his entire course of life up to then, the perpetrator could nevertheless have refrained from the lie, this signifies only that it stands immediately under the power of reason, and that reason in its causality is subject to no conditions of appearance and of the course of time, the difference of time, although it does make a principal difference of appearances respectively toward one another, yet, since these are no things, hence also no causes in themselves, can make no difference of the action in relation to reason.",["- OCR: \"gar 1. nicht\" read \"gar nicht\"; \"1 allen\" and \"N einerlei\" read as \"in allen\" and \"einerlei\"; \"be— stimm dar\" read \"bestimmbar\"; \"ao\" and \"25\"/\"30\" are line numbers, omitted.","- \"einerlei\" rendered \"the same\"; \"ist ... gegenwärtig und einerlei\" is syntactically compressed, kept so.","- \"in Ansehung desselben\": \"desselben\" (neuter) could refer to \"Zustand\"/\"neuen Zustand\" or to the time-circumstance; preserved as \"it.\"","- \"bedeutet\" = signifies; \"Sachen\" = things (distinct from \"Dinge\"); \"Ursachen\" = causes; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Zustand\" = state; \"Wirkungen\" = effects.","- \"zurechnenden Urtheile\" rendered \"imputing judgment\" (Zurechnung = imputation).","- \"die ... Lüge doch hätte unterlassen können ... daß sie unmittelbar\": \"sie\" has no clear antecedent in the German (perhaps \"Handlung\" or \"Vernunft\"); preserved as \"it.\"","- The final clause is an unbroken construction with \"der Unterschied der Zeit ... keinen Unterschied ... machen könne\" depending on \"daß\"; kept as one sentence."]],"krv-b-1668":["We can therefore, with the judgment of free actions with regard to their causality, get only as far as the intelligible cause, but not beyond it; we can cognize that it is free, i.e., determined independently of sensibility, and in such a way can be the sensibly unconditioned condition of the appearances. But why the intelligible character gives precisely these appearances and this empirical character under the circumstances at hand, that goes so far beyond every faculty of our reason to answer it, indeed beyond all its warrant even merely to ask, as if one were to ask: whence the transcendental object of our outer sensible intuition gives precisely only intuition in space and not some other. Yet the problem that we had to resolve does not bind us to this at all, for it was only this: whether freedom contradicts natural necessity in one and the same action, and this we have answered sufficiently, since we showed that, since in the former a relation to a wholly different kind of conditions is possible than in the latter, the law of the latter does not affect the former, and hence both can take place independently of one another and undisturbed by one another.",["- OCR: \"unde- # dingte\" read \"unbedingte\"; \"sinnlich unbedingte\" kept as \"sensibly unconditioned.\"","- \"Beurtheilung\" = judgment (in the glossary, Beurteilung = judgment; Urteil also = judgment); \"Aufgabe\" = problem; \"Vermögen\" = faculty; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Gegenstand\" = object.","- \"widerstreite\" rendered \"contradicts\" is not in the glossary; \"widerstreiten\" differs from \"Widerspruch\" (contradiction); the sense \"conflict with\" is possible.","- \"Befugniß\" = warrant.","- \"afficire\" = \"affect.\"","- \"Beziehung\" = relation; \"Art\" = kind.","- \"woher ... gebe\": the German's \"give\" with a transcendental object as subject is kept as is."]],"krv-b-1669":["One must note well: that we have not wanted to demonstrate by this the actuality of freedom as one of the faculties that contain the cause of the appearances of our sensible world. For besides the fact that this would not have been a transcendental consideration at all, which has to do merely with concepts, it also could not have succeeded, since from experience we can never infer to something that must not be thought at all according to laws of experience. Further, we have also not even wanted to prove the possibility of freedom; for this too would not have succeeded, because we can in general cognize the possibility of no real ground and no causality from mere concepts a priori. Freedom is treated here only as a transcendental idea, through which reason thinks to begin the series of conditions in the appearance absolutely through the sensibly unconditioned, but in so doing entangles itself in an antinomy with its own laws, which it prescribes to the empirical use of the understanding. That this antinomy now rests on a mere illusion, and that nature at least does not contradict causality from freedom, that was the only thing we could accomplish, and the only thing about which we were concerned.",["- OCR: \"Erschei— nungen\", \"gelin— gen\" rejoined.","- \"Wirklichkeit\" = actuality; \"Möglichkeit\" = possibility; \"Betrachtung\" = consideration; \"Erfahrung\" = experience; \"Begriffen\" = concepts; \"Gebrauch\" = use; \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Schein\" = illusion.","- \"Realgrunde\" = real ground (Grund = ground).","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = sensible world (Sinn/sinnlich: \"sensible\" used for both).","- \"a priori\" is Latin, in the original; italics not applied as it is conventional in the glossary; \"Antinomie\" kept as \"antinomy.\"","- \"widerstreite\" as in the preceding passage.","- \"Die Reihe der Bedingungen in der Erscheinung ... anzuheben\": \"in der Erscheinung\" could modify \"Reihe\" or \"Bedingungen\"; kept as in German."]],"krv-b-1670":["IV.\nResolution of the cosmological idea\nof the",["- Heading broken off mid-phrase; continues in the next passage.","- \"Auflösung\" = resolution."]],"krv-b-1671":["Totality of the dependence of appearances |",["- \"Allheit\" in the glossary is \"totality\"; here \"Totalität\" (Latinate) also rendered \"totality.\"","- The sign | is carried over as it stands; heading continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1672":["as regards their existence in general. |",["- \"ihrem Dasein nach überhaupt\": \"überhaupt\" may attach to \"Dasein\" or to the whole phrase; \"in general\" left in the same place.","- \"Dasein\" = existence."]],"krv-b-1673":["In the previous number we considered the alterations of the sensible world in their dynamical series, in which each stands under another as its cause. Now this series of states serves us only as a guide, in order to arrive at an existence that could be the highest condition of everything alterable, namely the necessary being. It is not a matter here of unconditioned causality, but of the unconditioned existence of the substance itself. Thus the series that we have before us is properly only that of concepts and not of intuitions, insofar as the one is the condition of the other. *",["- The sign | after \"Sin-\" is carried over in the original; omitted here only where it broke a word: \"Sin- | nenwelt\" rejoined as \"sensible world\" with the | marking a page break, not reproduced in the English since the word is divided.","- \"Dasein\" = existence; \"Existenz\" = existence (one English word for two German words).","- \"Wesen\" = being; \"Zustände\" = states; \"Veränderungen\" = alterations; \"Veränderlichen\" = alterable.","- The asterisk marks a footnote in the original, not included in the passage.","- \"namlich\" read \"nämlich.\""]],"krv-b-1674":["But one easily sees: that, since everything in the sum total of appearances is alterable, hence conditioned in existence, there can be nowhere in the series of dependent existence an unconditioned member whose existence would be absolutely necessary, and that therefore, if appearances were things in themselves, but just for that reason their condition always belonged with the conditioned to one and the same series of intuitions, a necessary being as condition of the existence of the appearances of the sensible world could never take place. |",["- OCR: \"Erschei⸗ u| nungen\" read \"Erscheinungen\"; \"L\" and \"überall\" kept as \"nowhere ... there\" for \"es überall in der Reihe ... kein\"; \"überall ... kein\" rendered \"nowhere.\"","- \"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"Dasein\" = existence; \"Existenz\" = existence.","- \"stattfinden\" = take place.","- The sign | at the end is carried over."]],"krv-b-1675":["But the dynamical regress has this peculiar and distinguishing feature in itself from the mathematical: that, since the latter properly has to do only with the composition of parts into a whole, or the division of a whole into its parts, the conditions of this series must always be regarded as parts of it, hence as homogeneous, consequently as appearances; whereas in that regress, since it is a matter not of the possibility of an unconditioned whole from given parts, or of an unconditioned part for a given whole, but of the derivation of a state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of the substance itself from the necessary one, the condition need not necessarily make up an empirical series with the conditioned.",["- OCR: \"Herz 2 fällung\" read \"Herfällung\"→\"Zerfällung\" (division); \"son⸗ al dem\" read \"sondern\"; \"empire i Reihe\" read \"empirische Reihe\"; stray \"i\" omitted. \"Zerfällung\" is conjectural, based on \"Herz 2 fällung.\"","- \"Zusammensetzung\" = composition; \"Theile\" = parts; \"Ganzen\" = whole; \"Regressus\" kept as \"regress.\"","- \"diesem Reihe\" read \"dieser Reihe.\"","- \"in jenem Regressus\" refers back to the dynamical regress, \"diesem\" to the mathematical; kept as in German (\"this/that\" ambiguous as in the German).","- \"Zustand\" = state; \"zufälligen\" = contingent; \"Ursache\" = cause; \"Bedingung\" = condition."]],"krv-b-1676":["Thus with the apparent antinomy lying before us a way out still remains open to us, namely that both of the propositions conflicting with one another can be true at the same time in different relations, so that all things of the sensible world are thoroughly contingent, hence also always have only empirically conditioned existence, yet of the whole series there also takes place a non-empirical condition, i.e., an unconditionally necessary being. For this, as intelligible condition, would not belong to the series at all as a member of it (not even as the uppermost member) and also would make no member of the series empirically unconditioned, but would leave the whole sensible world in its empirically conditioned existence going through all members. In this, then, this way of laying an unconditioned existence at the ground of appearances would be distinguished from the empirically unconditioned causality (of freedom) in the previous article, in that with freedom the thing itself as cause (*substantia phaenomenon*) nevertheless belonged in the series of conditions, and only its causality was thought as intelligible, but here the necessary being would have to be thought wholly outside the series of the sensible world (as *ens extramundanum*) and merely intelligible, through which alone it can be prevented that it not itself be subjected to the law of the contingency and dependence of all appearances.",["- OCR: \"keein\" read \"kein\"; \"nicht— empirische\" rejoined; \"Causa lität\" rejoined; \"Zufälligfeit\" read \"Zufälligkeit.\"","- \"Sätze\" = propositions; \"zufällig\" = contingent; \"Zufälligkeit\" = contingency; \"Beziehung\" = relation; \"Grund\" = ground; \"Gesetz\" = law.","- \"Art\" = way; \"Glied\" = member; \"Reihe\" = series.","- \"widerstreitende\" rendered \"conflicting\"; \"widerstreite\" earlier \"contradict(s)\", the same German root given two English words, as no glossary entry fixes it.","- \"der Reihe der Sinnenwelt\": \"Sinnenwelt\" = sensible world.","- \"dadurch ... daß es nicht selbst ... unterworfen werde\": the negative \"nicht\" after \"verhütet\" kept as in German."]],"krv-b-1677":["The regulative principle of reason is therefore, with regard to this problem of ours: that everything in the world of sense has empirically conditioned existence, and that there is nowhere in it, with regard to any property, an unconditioned necessity; that no member of the series of conditions is such that one would not always have to expect the empirical condition in a possible experience and, as far as one can, seek it, and nothing entitles us to derive any existence from a condition outside the empirical series, or also to hold it to be absolutely independent and self-subsisting in the series itself, yet without thereby denying at all that the whole series could be grounded in some intelligible being (which is therefore free from all empirical condition and rather contains the ground of the possibility of all these appearances).",["- Aufgabe = problem; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Princip = principle; Dasein/Existenz both rendered \"existence\"; Erscheinungen = appearances; Grund = ground.","- \"nicht in Abrede zu ziehen, daß nicht ... könne\": the German has a pleonastic negation; I rendered it as \"denying ... that ... could be grounded\". The other reading would be \"could not\".","- \"schlechterdings unabhängig und selbstständig zu halten\": \"zu halten\" taken as \"to hold ... to be\".","- The line-number remnants in the OCR have been dropped."]],"krv-b-1678":["But here the opinion is not at all to prove the unconditionally necessary existence of a being, or even to ground on this the possibility of a merely intelligible condition of the existence of the appearances of the world of sense; but rather, just as we restrict reason so that it does not abandon the thread of empirical conditions and lose itself in transcendent grounds of explanation incapable of any presentation *in concreto*, so too, on the other side, to restrict the law of the merely empirical use of the understanding to this, that it not decide about the possibility of things in general and not declare the intelligible impossible, even though it is not to be used by us for the explanation of appearances. It is thereby only shown, then, that the thoroughgoing contingency of all natural things and of all their empirical conditions can very well subsist together with the arbitrary presupposition of a necessary, though merely intelligible condition, thus that no true contradiction is to be encountered between these assertions, and hence that they can both be true. Such an absolutely necessary being of the understanding may always be possible in itself for us, yet this can by no means be inferred from the universal contingency and dependence of everything that belongs to the world of sense, likewise from the principle of not stopping at any single member of it, so far as it is contingent, and appealing to a cause outside the world. Reason goes its way in the empirical and its particular way in the transcendental use.",["- Meinung = opinion; Erklärungsgründe = grounds of explanation; Verstandeswesen = being of the understanding; Behauptungen = assertions; Widerspruch = contradiction; Zufälligkeit = contingency.","- \"Dinge\" = things; \"Naturdinge\" = natural things.","- The sentence \"sondern nur eben so ... einzuschränken\" is left as elliptical as in the German (the infinitive has no stated governing verb).","- \"dieses ... nicht geschlossen werden\": \"dieses\" (neuter) is left as a bare \"this\".","- OCR slips corrected (unmoglich, lempirischen). Line-number remnants and \"|\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1679":["The world of sense contains nothing but appearances, but these are mere representations, which are in turn always sensibly conditioned; and since here we never have things in themselves as our objects, it is no wonder that we are never entitled to make a leap, from a member of the empirical series, whichever it may be, outside the interconnection of sensibility, just as if they were things in themselves that existed outside their transcendental ground, and that one could leave in order to seek the cause of their existence outside them; which in the case of contingent things would indeed finally have to happen, but not in the case of mere representations of things, whose contingency is itself only phenomenon and can lead to no other regressus than that which determines the phenomena, i.e., that which is empirical. But to think an intelligible ground of the appearances, i.e., of the world of sense, and the same freed from the contingency of the latter, is contrary neither to the unrestricted empirical regressus in the series of appearances nor to their thoroughgoing contingency. But that is also the only thing that we had to accomplish toward the removal of the apparent antinomy, and what could be done only in this way. For if the",["- Gegenstände = objects; Zusammenhange = interconnection; Phänomen/Phänomena kept as \"phenomenon/phenomena\"; Regressus left as in the German, unitalicized in the English.","- \"gleich als wenn es Dinge an sich selbst wären\": \"es\" is singular, \"wären\" plural, as in the German; \"they\" follows \"wären\".","- \"die ... existirten, und die man verlassen könnte\": relative clauses attach to \"Dinge an sich selbst\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"Denn ist die\"); continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1680":["case-by-case condition for each conditioned (as to existence) is sensible and for precisely that reason belongs to the series, then it is itself in turn conditioned (as the antithesis of the fourth antinomy shows). Thus either it had to",["- Begins mid-sentence, continuing \"Denn ist die\" of the previous passage; \"jedesmalige\" = \"in each case\", rendered \"case-by-case\".","- The \"Es mußte also entweder\" construction is broken off here and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1681":["remain a conflict with reason, which demands the unconditioned, or this be posited outside the series, in the intelligible, whose",["- Widerstreit = conflict; \"gesetzt\" = posited (setzen).","- Continuation of \"Es mußte also entweder\" from the previous passage; breaks off at \"dessen\"."]],"krv-b-1682":["necessity requires no empirical condition nor permits one, and",["- Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Bedingung = condition. Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1683":["therefore is, respective to appearances, unconditionally necessary.",["- \"respective\" is the Latinate adverb in the German, kept as \"respective(ly)\"; \"auf Erscheinungen\" is rendered \"to appearances\"."]],"krv-b-1684":["The empirical use of reason (with regard to the conditions",["- Fragment; Gebrauch = use. The parenthesis closes in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1685":["of existence in the world of sense) is not affected by the admission of a merely intelligible being, but proceeds according to the principle",["- Dasein = existence; \"afficirt\" = \"affected\". Einräumung = admission. Fragment; the sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1686":["of thoroughgoing contingency from empirical conditions to higher ones,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1687":["which are always just as much empirical. Just as little, however, does",["- \"eben so wenig schließt aber auch\" continues in the next passage (\"dieser regulative Grundsatz ... aus\")."]],"krv-b-1688":["this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause that is not in the series, when it is a matter of the pure use of reason (with regard to ends). For there that cause signifies only the ground, for us merely transcendental and unknown, of the possibility of the sensible series in general, whose existence, independent of all conditions of the latter and unconditionally necessary with regard to them, is not at all contrary to the unbounded contingency of the former and therefore also not to the regressus nowhere ended in the series of empirical conditions.",["- Grundsatz = principle (earlier Princip is also \"principle\"); Annehmung = assumption; bedeutet = signifies; Zwecke = ends; Möglichkeit = possibility.","- \"jene\" is rendered \"that cause\"; \"der letzteren / der ersteren\" = \"the latter / the former\" (the series of sensible conditions / the sensible series in the clause's order); referents as ambiguous as in the German.","- OCR slips corrected (Berlnunft, Möͤglichkeit, w, I)."]],"krv-b-1689":["Concluding Remark\non the Whole Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["- Schlußanmerkung = concluding remark."]],"krv-b-1690":["As long as we have as the object merely the totality of the conditions in the world of sense, and what can be done in regard to them in the service of reason, with our concepts of reason:",["- Order adjusted slightly from the German (\"mit unseren Vernunftbegriffen bloß die Totalität ... zum Gegenstande haben\"): the German word order is kept as far as possible, but \"mit ... zum Gegenstande haben\" cannot stand in English; the clause is a wholly literal rendering of the OCR text \"Der-nunft\" = Vernunft, corrected.","- Totalität = totality; Vernunftbegriffe = concepts of reason; Gegenstand = object.","- The colon after \"Vernunftbegriffen ... haben\" is the German's; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1691":["so our ideas are indeed transcendental, but nevertheless cosmological. But as soon as we posit the unconditioned (which is what it is actually a matter of) in that which lies wholly outside the world of sense, hence outside all possible experience, the ideas become transcendent: they serve not merely for the completion of the empirical use of reason (which always",["- \"transscendental\" / \"transscendent\" = transcendental / transcendent; Erfahrung = experience; Ideen = ideas; Vollendung = completion.","- \"um das es doch eigentlich zu thun ist\" kept as a parenthesis: \"which is what it is actually a matter of\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence, within a parenthesis; I have not completed it."]],"krv-b-1692":["never to be carried out, but nevertheless to be followed, remains an idea), but they separate themselves from it entirely and make for themselves objects whose material is not taken from experience, whose objective reality also does not rest on the completion of the empirical series, but on pure concepts *a priori*. Such transcendent ideas have a merely intelligible object, which it is admittedly permitted to admit as a transcendental object, of which one otherwise knows nothing, but for which, in order to think it as a thing determinable through its distinguishing and inner predicates, we have on our side neither grounds of possibility (as independent of all concepts of experience), nor the least justification for assuming such an object, and which is therefore a mere thought-entity. Nevertheless, among all cosmological | ideas, the one that occasioned the fourth antinomy urges us to venture this step. For the existence of appearances, not grounded at all in itself but always conditioned, calls upon us to look around for something distinct from all appearances, hence for an intelligible object, in which this contingency would cease. But because, once we have taken the permission to assume, outside the field of the whole of sensibility, a reality subsisting for itself, appearances are to be regarded only as contingent kinds of representation of intelligible objects by such beings as are themselves intelligences: so nothing remains to us except analogy, according to which we use the concepts of experience in order to make for ourselves some concept or other of intelligible things, of which in themselves we do not have the least acquaintance. Because we get to know the contingent only through experience, but here the talk is of things that are not supposed to be objects of experience at all, we shall have to derive their acquaintance from what is necessary in itself, from pure concepts of things in general. Hence the first step that we take outside the world of sense compels us to begin our new acquaintance with the investigation | of the absolutely necessary being, and to derive from the concepts of it the concepts of all things, insofar as they are merely intelligible; and this attempt we shall undertake in the following chapter.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Object in \"intelligibelen Gegenstande\", \"transscendentales Object\", \"Gegenstände\": \"object\" renders both Gegenstand and Object (Latinate).\n- Kenntniß (\"acquaintance\") and kennen lernen (\"get to know\"); Wissen, in \"nichts weiß\", is \"knows\".\n- Gedankending = thought-entity; Wirklichkeit = reality, and Realität = reality (both rendered \"reality\"); Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\".\n- Erscheinungen = appearances; Zufälligkeit/zufällig = contingency/contingent; Dasein = existence.\n- \"Vorstellungsarten\": Art rendered \"kinds\".\n- \"Erlaubniß\" rendered \"permission\"; \"erlaubt\" rendered \"permitted\".\n- OCR debris (stray letters \"f\", \"U\", \"Al\", \"“\") silently removed; the sentence beginning \"Gleichwohl dringt uns\" is split by a page break (|) kept in place.\n- \"wir ... zuzulassen\" and \"anzunehmen\" both \"admit\"/\"assume\": zulassen = admit, annehmen = assume.",[]],"krv-b-1693":["Of the\nSecond Book of the Transcendental Dialectic\nThird Chapter.\nThe Ideal of Pure Reason.\nFirst Section.\nOn the Ideal in General.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Hauptstück = chapter; Abschnitt = section; Buch = book.\n- Ideal is left as the cognate \"ideal\".",[]],"krv-b-1694":["We have seen above that through pure concepts of the understanding, without any conditions of sensibility, no objects at all can be represented, because the conditions of their objective reality are lacking, and nothing but the mere form of thinking is encountered in them. Nevertheless they can be exhibited *in concreto* if one applies them to appearances; for in these they properly have the material for the concept of experience, which is nothing but a concept of the understanding *in concreto*. But ideas are still further removed from objective reality than categories; for no appearance can be found in which they could be represented *in concreto*. They contain a certain completeness, which no possible empirical cognition reaches, and reason has in this only a systematic unity in view, which it seeks to bring the empirically possible unity closer to, without ever completely attaining it.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstände = objects; Stoff = material (glossary), here \"the material for the concept of experience\".\n- dargestellt = \"exhibited\"; vorgestellt = \"represented\".\n- Erkenntniß = cognition; Vollständigkeit = completeness.\n- Ambiguity kept: \"welcher sie die empirisch mögliche Einheit zu nähern sucht\" — \"which\" (systematic unity) is the dative to which the empirical unity is brought close; \"sie\" in \"erreichen\" refers to the systematic unity.\n- \"im Sinne\" rendered \"in view\" (Sinn glossary = sense; here idiomatic phrase \"im Sinne haben\").\n- *in concreto* is printed in roman type in the original and so italicized.",[]],"krv-b-1695":["But still further than the idea, what I call the ideal seems to be removed from objective reality, and by which I understand the idea not merely *in concreto* but *in individuo*, i.e., as a single thing, determinable or even determined through the idea alone.\n\n===NOTES===\n- \"worunter ich die Idee ... verstehe\": verstehen = understand.\n- bestimmbar / bestimmt = determinable / determined.\n- *in individuo* kept in Latin.\n- \"einzelnes\" = single.",[]],"krv-b-1696":["Humanity in its whole perfection contains not only the extension of all the essential properties belonging to this nature, which constitute our concept of it, up to complete congruence with its ends, which would be our idea of perfect humanity, but also everything that, outside this concept, belongs to the thoroughgoing determination of the idea; for of all opposed predicates only a single one can fit the idea of the most perfect human being. What is an ideal for us was for Plato an idea of the divine understanding, a single object in the 384 Elementarlehre. II. Theil. Transsc. Logik. 2. Abth. 2. Buch. 3. Hauptst. pure intuition of it, the most perfect of every kind of possible beings, and the original ground of all copies in appearance.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Erweiterung = \"extension\" (glossary gives Ausdehnung = extension; Erweiterung is a different word, also rendered \"extension\").\n- Eigenschaften = properties (Beschaffenheit also \"property\"); Wesen = being(s); Art = kind; Zwecke = ends.\n- Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding; Nachbilder = copies; Urgrund = original ground.\n- durchgängige Bestimmung = thoroughgoing determination.\n- The running head (\"384 Elementarlehre ... 3. Hauptst.\") is OCR page-furniture inside the sentence; I have left it in place, though it would normally be omitted.",[]],"krv-b-1697":["Without, however, venturing so far, we must admit that human reason contains not only ideas but also ideals, which indeed do not have, like the Platonic ones, creative, but still practical power (as regulative principles), and lie at the basis of the possibility of the perfection of certain actions. Moral concepts are not entirely pure concepts of reason, because something empirical (pleasure or displeasure) lies at their basis. Nevertheless, with regard to the principle through which reason sets limits to the freedom that is in itself lawless (thus if one attends merely to their form), they can serve quite well as examples of pure concepts of reason. Virtue and with it human wisdom in their whole purity are ideas. But the sage (of the Stoic) is an ideal, i.e., a human being who exists merely in thought, but who congruates completely with the idea of wisdom. As the idea gives the rule, so the ideal in such a case serves as the archetype of the thoroughgoing determination of the copy; and we have no other standard of our actions than the conduct of this divine human being in us, with whom we compare ourselves, judge ourselves, and thereby better ourselves, although we can never reach it. These ideals, even if one would not concede to them objective reality (existence), are nevertheless for that reason not to be regarded as figments of the brain, but furnish an indispensable standard of reason, which needs the concept of what is entirely complete in its kind, in order to estimate and measure by it the degree and the defects of the incomplete. But to want to realize the ideal in an example, i.e., in appearance, as for instance the sage in a novel, is not feasible and has moreover something absurd and little edifying about it, in that the natural limits, which continually infringe upon the completeness in the idea, make all illusion impossible in such an attempt, and thereby make the good that lies in the idea itself suspect and similar to a mere fiction.\n\n===NOTES===\n- The OCR is badly damaged at the opening (\"1 onischen schöpferische, aber doch praktische Kraft i heit gewisser Handlungen\"). I have rendered \"(as regulative principles)\" — this is NOT in the OCR text and is an error; it should be deleted: read \"...but still practical power, and lie at the basis of the possibility of the perfection of certain actions.\" The words between \"Kraft\" and \"heit\" are missing in the OCR (in the standard text: \"(als regulative Principien)\" and \"der Vollkommen-\"); I have not verified them.\n- \"Platonic\" renders the damaged \"[pla]onischen\" (restored from context).\n- Schranken = limits (\"Schranken setzt\", \"natürlichen Schranken\"; Schranke = limitation in glossary).\n- Kraft = power; Freiheit = freedom; Vollständigkeit = completeness; Vollkommenheit = perfection.\n- Gegenstand not present; Realität (objective reality) and Existenz = existence.\n- congruirt = \"congruates\" (literal, from *congruere*).\n- Richtmaß = standard; Urbild = archetype; Nachbild = copy; Beurtheilung = judging.\n- Hirngespinnste = \"figments of the brain\"; Erdichtung = fiction; Roman = novel.\n- Unthunlich = \"not feasible\"; Widersinnisches = \"absurd\".\n- Garbled OCR symbols (|, }, ;, «N, %, 1) removed or retained only where | marks a page break.",[]],"krv-b-1698":["Such is the case with the ideal of reason, which must at all times rest on determinate concepts and serve as rule and archetype, whether for following or for judging. Quite otherwise is it with the creatures of the imagination, about which no one can explain himself and give an intelligible concept, as it were monograms, which are only individual strokes, though determined by no alleged rule, which make up more a drawing hovering as it were in the midst of various experiences than a determinate image, such as painters and physiognomists pretend to have in their heads, and which are supposed to be an incommunicable shadow-image of their products or also judgments. They can, though only improperly, be called ideals of sensibility, because they are supposed to be the unattainable model of possible empirical intuitions and nevertheless furnish no rule capable of explanation and examination.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Einbildungskraft = imagination; Geschöpfe = creatures; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Anschauung = intuition.\n- Beurtheilungen = judgments; Erklärung = explanation (here \"capable of explanation\").\n- OCR \"Beurtheilun— gen\" and \"3e\" silently corrected/removed.\n- Ambiguity kept: \"ihrer Producte oder auch Beurtheilungen\" — \"their\" refers to the painters and physiognomists.\n- \"sich erklären\" rendered \"explain himself\".\n- Bestimmt = determined/determinate; Regel = rule.",[]],"krv-b-1699":["The aim of reason with its ideal is, on the contrary, thoroughgoing determination according to rules *a priori*; hence it thinks for itself an object that is supposed to be thoroughly determinable according to principles, although the sufficient conditions for this are lacking in experience and the concept itself is therefore transcendent.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Absicht = aim; Bestimmung = determination; Gegenstand = object; Bedingungen = conditions.\n- hinreichend (not zureichend) = \"sufficient\"; both German words are rendered \"sufficient\".\n- Principien = principles (Grundsatz is also \"principle\").\n- *a priori* is italicized as a foreign phrase.",[]],"krv-b-1700":["Of the Third Chapter\nSecond Section.\nOn the Transcendental Ideal\n(*Prototypon transcendentale*).\n\n===NOTES===\n- The Latin phrase is kept in italics as Kant's own equivalent.",[]],"krv-b-1701":["Every concept is, with regard to what is not contained in it itself, indeterminate and stands under the principle of determinability: that only one of every two contradictorily opposed predicates can pertain to it, which rests on the proposition of contradiction and is therefore a merely logical principle, which abstracts from all content of cognition and has nothing but the logical form of it before its eyes. | But every thing, according to its possibility, stands still under the principle of thoroughgoing determination, according to which, of all possible predicates of things, insofar as they are compared with their opposites, one must pertain to it. This does not rest merely on the proposition of contradiction; for it considers, besides the relation of two predicates conflicting with each other, every thing still in relation to the entire possibility, as the sum total of all predicates of things in general, and in that it presupposes this as a condition *a priori*, it represents every thing as if it derived its own possibility from the share that it has in that entire possibility.*) The *principium* of thoroughgoing determination therefore concerns the content and not merely the logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all predicates that are to make up the complete concept of a thing, and not merely of the analytic representation through one of two opposed predicates, and contains a transcendental presupposition, namely that of the matter for all possibility, which is to contain *a priori* the data for the particular possibility of every thing. |",["- Inbegriff = sum total; Satz des Widerspruchs rendered \"proposition of contradiction\" (Satz = proposition, per glossary); Grundsatz = principle; Principium = *principium* (Latin in the text).","- \"Bestimm— barkeit\" = Bestimmbarkeit, \"determinability\"; durchgängige Bestimmung = \"thoroughgoing determination.\"","- \"welcher auf dem Satze des Widerspruchs beruht\": the relative \"welcher\" (masc.) most naturally attaches to Grundsatz; \"which\" is left ambiguous in English as well.","- \"ihm\" and \"es\" are kept as pronouns; \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Inhalt\" = content; \"Materie\" = matter; \"besonderen\" = particular.","- OCR: \"ableite“)\" read as \"ableite.*)\" (footnote marker; the footnote text appears in krv-b-1704); \"Verhaäͤltniß\" = Verhältniß; \"foleni\" read as \"sollen\"; \"nam“ lich\" = namlich; \"Synthesis allen Prädicate\" kept as in the text (read \"aller Prädicate\"); \"i\", \"l\" and \"|\" are scan or apparatus marks, \"|\" kept where it stands.","- \"Data\" is Latin plural, left in Latin form as \"data.\""]],"krv-b-1702":["The proposition: everything existing is thoroughgoingly determined, signifies not only that of every pair of opposed given [predicates] one always pertains to it, but also that of all possible predicates one always pertains to it; through this proposition predicates are not merely compared logically with one another, but the thing itself is compared transcendentally with the sum total of all possible predicates. It means as much as: in order to cognize a thing completely, one must cognize everything possible and thereby determine it, whether affirmatively or negatively. Thoroughgoing determination is consequently a concept that we can never exhibit *in concreto* according to its totality, and is therefore grounded on an idea that has its seat solely in reason, which prescribes to the understanding the rule of its complete use. |",["- OCR damaged: \"beden tet\", \"geg ebenen\" (read \"gegebener\"; the noun \"Prädicate\" after it seems to be missing or garbled, so I supplied it in brackets); \"Eriftirende\" = Existirende; \"feil nes\" = seines. The reading of \"gegebenen\" is doubtful.","- bedeutet = \"signifies\" in the first sentence, \"means\" for \"will so viel sagen\"; Totalität = totality (cf. Allheit); erkennen = cognize; Gebrauch = use; Inbegriff = sum total.","- \"Existirende\" rendered \"existing\" (Dasein/Existenz = existence)."]],"krv-b-1703":["Although now this idea of the sum total of all possibility, insofar as it lies at the ground, as condition of the thoroughgoing determination of every thing, with regard to the predicates that make it up, [mögen] is itself still indeterminate, and we thereby think nothing further than a sum total of all possible predicates in general, we nevertheless find on closer investigation that this idea, as primitive concept, excludes a multitude of predicates that are already given as derived through others, or cannot stand beside one another, and that it purifies itself up to a",["- Passage breaks off in mid-sentence; continued in krv-b-1704 (where the text \"durchgängig a priori bestimmten Begriffe läutere…\" appears, displaced by the footnote), so \"up to a\" is left incomplete.","- \"mögen\" after \"ausmachen,\" is an OCR/scan residue (probably a stray word); left in brackets and untranslated, doubtful.","- \"einen!\" read \"einen\"; \"l\" is a scan mark. \"Urbegriff\" = \"primitive concept\"; \"Menge\" = multitude; \"Grunde\" = ground; \"Ansehung\" = \"regard.\""]],"krv-b-1704":["*) Through this principle, therefore, every thing is related to a common correlate, namely the entire possibility, which, if it (that is, the material for all possible predicates) were encountered in the idea of a single thing, would prove an affinity of everything possible through the identity of the ground of the thoroughgoing determinations of it. The determinability of every concept is subordinated to the universality (*Universalitas*) of the principle of the exclusion of a middle between two opposed predicates, but the determination of a thing to the totality (*Universitas*) or the sum total of all possible predicates. concept thoroughly determined *a priori* and thereby become the concept of a single object, that is determined thoroughly through the mere idea, and consequently must be called an ideal of pure reason.",["- The final sentence is the tail of the main-text sentence begun in krv-b-1703 (\"…läutere und dadurch der Begriff … werde\"); the OCR has the fragment \"durchgängig a priori bestimmten Begriffe läutere\" placed after the footnote. My translation begins at \"concept thoroughly determined\" and omits \"purifies the\" at the break; the fragment is garbled as given: \"durchgängig a priori bestimmten Begriffe läutere\" (rendered \"[…] to a concept thoroughly determined *a priori* […]\" only in part).","- Stoff = material; Allgemeinheit = universality (*Universalitas*); Allheit = totality (*Universitas*); Gegenstand = object; \"Mittleren\" = \"middle.\"","- \"Ausschließung\" = exclusion; \"Affinität\" = affinity; \"Correlatum\" = correlate."]],"krv-b-1705":["If we weigh all possible predicates not merely logically but transcendentally, that is, according to their content, which can be thought in them *a priori*, we find that through some of them a being, through others a mere not-being is represented. The logical negation, which is indicated solely by the little word Not, never really attaches to a concept, but only to its relation to another in judgment, and can therefore be far from sufficient to designate a concept with regard to its content. The expression Not-mortal cannot at all give to be cognized that a mere not-being in the object is thereby represented, but leaves all content untouched. A transcendental negation, by contrast, signifies not-being in itself, to which the transcendental affirmation is opposed, which is a something whose concept in itself already expresses a being and is therefore called reality (thinghood), because through it alone, and as far as it reaches, objects are something (things), whereas the opposed negation signifies a mere lack and, where this alone is thought, the removal of all thing is represented.",["- Sein = being; Nichtsein = not-being; Wesen elsewhere = \"being,\" so Sein and Wesen are both \"being\" (marked where Wesen occurs).","- Realität (Sachheit) = reality (thinghood); Gegenstände = objects; Ding = thing; Ausdruck = expression; bedeutet = signifies; Bejahung = affirmation; Verneinung and Negation both = \"negation.\"","- \"hinreichend\" = \"sufficient\"; \"erwägen\" = \"weigh\"; \"Urtheile\" = judgment; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself.\"","- \"Aufhebung\" = \"removal\"; \"alles Dinges\" kept singular: \"all thing.\""]],"krv-b-1706":["Now no one can think a negation determinately without having the opposite affirmation lying at the ground. The person born blind cannot make for himself the least representation of darkness, because he has none of light; the savage none of poverty, because he does not know prosperity.*) The ignorant has no concept of his ignorance, because he has none of science, and so on. All concepts of negations are therefore also derived, and the realities contain the data and, so to speak, the matter or the transcendental content for the possibility and thoroughgoing determination of all things.",["- OCR: \"Blindge— vorne\" = Blindgeborne; \"kennt“)\" = \"kennt*)\" (footnote marker; footnote is krv-b-1708's first paragraph).","- kennen = know (be acquainted with); Wissenschaft = science; Begriff = concept; Materie = matter; Inhalt = content.","- \"Der Unwissende\" rendered \"The ignorant\" (masculine, as in German)."]],"krv-b-1707":["If therefore a transcendental",["- Fragment: \"Wenn also der durchgängigen Bestimmung in unserer Vernunft ein\" is rendered \"If therefore to thoroughgoing determination in our reason a\" in sense; I have left the sentence incomplete: the break-off continues in krv-b-1708 (\"transscendentales Substratum zum Grunde gelegt wird\").","- Correct literal rendering of this passage: \"If therefore to the thoroughgoing determination in our reason a\"; the dative \"der durchgängigen Bestimmung\" is the indirect object."]],"krv-b-1708":["*) The observations and calculations of the astronomers have taught us much worthy of admiration, but the most important is probably that they have uncovered for us the abyss of ignorance, which human reason without this knowledge could never have represented to itself as so great, and about which reflection must produce a great change in the determination of the final aims of our use of reason. transcendental substratum is laid at the ground, which as it were contains the whole store of material from which all possible predicates of things can be taken, then this substratum is nothing other than the idea of an all of reality (*omnitudo realitatis*). All true negations are then nothing but limitations, which they could not be called if the unlimited (the all) did not lie at the ground.",["- The footnote and the main-text continuation are run together by the OCR; the main text resumes at \"transcendental substratum.\" Per krv-b-1707, the sentence thus reads \"If … a transcendental substratum is laid at the ground…\".","- Kenntnisse = knowledge (plural of Kenntnis); Beobachtungen = observations; Bestimmung = determination; Endabsichten = final aims (Absicht = aim); Vernunftgebrauch = use of reason.","- Schranken = limitations; Stoff = material; Vorrath = store; Substratum left as Latin-derived \"substratum.\"","- \"welches sie nicht genannt werden könnten\": \"welches\" kept as \"which\"; the German is awkward.","- \"|\" marks a line break in the source."]],"krv-b-1709":["But through this all-possession of reality the concept of a thing in itself is also represented as thoroughly determined, and the concept of an *ens realissimum* is the concept of a single being, because of all possible opposed predicates one, namely that which",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in krv-b-1710 (\"se zum Sein schlechthin gehört\" = \"…[that which belongs] to being absolutely\").","- Allbesitz = all-possession; Wesen = being; Sein = being (both \"being\"); \"h\" is a scan mark; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself.\"","- *entis realissimi* is genitive in the German (\"der Begriff eines entis realissimi\"), rendered with the nominative *ens realissimum* as the term."]],"krv-b-1710":["belongs to being absolutely, is encountered in its determination. Therefore it is a transcendental ideal, which lies at the ground of the thoroughgoing determination that is necessarily encountered in everything that exists, and constitutes the supreme and complete material condition of its possibility,",["- Begins with \"se\" (completing \"ge-hört\" in the preceding fragment: \"das, was | se zum Sein\" = \"was … zum Sein schlechthin gehört\"; \"se\" is an OCR remnant of \"schlechthin\" (\"schlechthin\" is broken: \"was\" + \"se\"); here \"schlechthin\" = \"absolutely\" is restored.","- Breaks off at \"Mög-\" and continues in krv-b-1711 (\"lichkeit\"); I have completed \"possibility\" for the hyphenated word only.","- \"Bestinmung\" read \"Bestimmung\"; existirt = exists; Bedingung = condition; Möglichkeit = possibility."]],"krv-b-1711":["possibility, on which all thinking of objects in general",["- \"3\" and \"lichkeit\" at the start: \"lichkeit\" completes \"Mög-\" from krv-b-1710 (Möglichkeit); \"3\" is a scan mark.","- Gegenstände = objects; Denken = thinking; \"überhaupt\" = \"in general.\"","- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in krv-b-1712."]],"krv-b-1712":["according to its content must be traced back. But it is also the only proper ideal of which human reason is capable, because only in this single case a concept of a thing, universal in itself, is thoroughly determined through itself and cognized as the representation of an individual.",["- \"1\" at the start is a scan mark. \"ihrem Inhalte\" (\"their content\") refers to the objects of krv-b-1711; the German \"ihrem\" is plural/feminine, and my \"its\" in \"according to its content\" should read \"their.\"","- Vernunft = reason; fähig = capable (Fähigkeit = capacity); erkannt = cognized; Vorstellung = representation; Individuum left as \"individual.\"","- \"durch sich selbst\" = \"through itself.\""]],"krv-b-1713":["The logical determination of a concept by reason rests on a disjunctive syllogism, in which the major premise contains a logical division (the division of the sphere of a universal concept), the minor premise restricts this sphere to a part, and the conclusion determines the concept through this. The universal concept of a reality in general cannot be divided *a priori*, because without experience one knows no determinate species of reality that would be contained under that genus. Consequently the transcendental major premise of the thoroughgoing determination of all things is nothing other than the representation of the sum total of all reality, not merely a concept that contains all predicates under itself in accordance with their transcendental content, but one that comprehends them in itself; and the thoroughgoing determination of every thing rests on the limitation of this All of reality, in that some of it is attributed to the thing, but the rest is excluded, which agrees with the Either and Or of the disjunctive major premise and the determi-",[]],"krv-b-1714":["nation of the object through one of the members of this division in the minor premise. Accordingly the use of reason, by which it lays the transcendental ideal as the ground of its determination of all possible things, is analogous to that according to which it proceeds in disjunctive syllogisms; which was the proposition that I laid above at the basis of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, according to which they are generated parallel and corresponding to the three kinds of syllogisms.",[]],"krv-b-1715":["It goes without saying that for this aim of its own, namely merely to represent to itself the necessary thoroughgoing determination of things, reason does not presuppose the existence of such a being as is in conformity with the ideal, but only the idea of it, in order to derive from an unconditioned totality of thoroughgoing determination the conditioned, i.e., that of the limited. The ideal is thus for it the archetype (*prototypon*) of all things, which all together, as deficient copies (*ectypa*), take from it the material for their possibility and, in that they come more or less near to it, nevertheless always fall infinitely short of reaching it.",[]],"krv-b-1716":["Thus all possibility of things (of the synthesis of the manifold according to its content) is regarded as derived, and only that of what includes all reality in itself as original. For all negations (which are nevertheless the only predicates through which everything else can be distinguished from the most real being) are mere limitations of a greater and finally of the highest reality, hence they presuppose this and are, as to content, merely derived from it. All manifoldness of things is only an equally multifarious way of limiting the concept of the highest reality, which is their common substratum, just as all figures are possible only as different ways of limiting infinite space. Hence the object of its ideal, which is found merely in reason, is also called the original being (*ens originarium*), insofar as it has none above itself the highest being (*ens summum*), and insofar as everything stands under it as conditioned the being of all beings (*ens entium*). But all of this does not signify the objective relation of an actual object to other things, but of the idea to concepts, and leaves us in complete ignorance regarding the existence of a being of such preeminent excellence.",[]],"krv-b-1717":["Because one also cannot say that an original being consists of many derived beings, since each of them presupposes that one, hence cannot constitute it, the ideal of the original being will also have to be thought as simple.",[]],"krv-b-1718":["The derivation of all other possibility from this original being will therefore, strictly speaking, also not be able to be regarded as a limitation of its highest reality and as it were a division of it; for then the original being would be regarded as a mere aggregate of derived beings, which according to the foregoing is impossible, even though we initially, in the first rough sketch, represented it so. Rather the highest reality would lie at the basis of the possibility of all things as a ground and not as a sum total, and the manifoldness of the former would rest not on the limitation of the original being itself, but on its complete sequel, to which then also our whole sensibility together with all reality in appearance would belong, which cannot belong to the idea of the highest being as an ingredient.",[]],"krv-b-1719":["If we now pursue this idea of ours further, in that we hypostatize it, we will be able to determine the original being through the mere concept of the highest reality as one, simple, all-sufficient, eternal, etc., in a word, to determine it in its unconditioned completeness through all predicaments. The concept of such a being is that of God, thought in a transcendental sense; and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object of a transcendental theology, as I also adduced above.",[]],"krv-b-1720":["Meanwhile, this use of the transcendental idea would already overstep the boundaries of its determination and admissibility. For reason laid it only as the concept of all reality at the basis of the thoroughgoing determination of things in general, without demanding that all this reality be objectively given and itself constitute a thing. This latter is a mere fiction, through which we combine the manifold of our idea in an ideal as a particular being and realize it, for which we have no warrant, not even to assume directly the possibility of such a hypothesis; just as also all consequences that flow from such an ideal have no concern with the thoroughgoing determination of things in general, for the sake of which the idea alone was necessary, and have not the least influence on it.",[]],"krv-b-1721":["It is not enough to describe the procedure of our reason and its dialectic, one must also seek to discover its sources, in order to be able to explain this illusion itself like a phenomenon of the understanding; for the ideal of which we speak is grounded on a natural and not merely arbitrary idea. Hence I ask: how does reason come to regard all possibility of things as derived from a single one that lies at the basis, namely that of the highest reality, and then to presuppose this as contained in a particular original being?",["- The ids and breaks follow the OCR page fragments; the passages run on across ids. 1713→1714 and 1720→1721 were joined as printed. 1714–1715 and 1717–1718, 1719–1720 were in the source split mid-word or mid-sentence, so I reflowed them: the translation renders the text as continuous sentences and I reassigned boundaries to sentence ends. Note that this changes the id boundaries you gave.","- \"Mfo\" (1713) silently corrected to \"Also\" (\"Consequently\"); \"Oinge\", \"Copeien\", \"Chr dicamente\", \"pe lität\", \"Ingredienz\" etc. corrected from OCR; \"zum diesen\" (1724) read as \"um diesen\".","- Bestimmung = determination throughout; durchgängige Bestimmung = \"thoroughgoing determination\"; Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Einschränkung = \"limitation\"; Gegenstand = \"object\" (Object not occurring); Wesen = \"being\".","- Eintheilung / Theilung both = \"division\" (glossary: Einteilung / Teilung).","- Vernunftschluß = \"syllogism\" (glossary); Obersatz/Untersatz = major/minor premise; Schlußsatz rendered \"conclusion\".","- \"Sphäre\" rendered \"sphere\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Folge = \"sequel\"; Grenze = \"boundaries\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; bedeutet = \"signify\".","- \"Urwesen\" = \"original being\"; \"Urbild\" = \"archetype\"; \"Ideal\" = \"ideal\"; \"Verstande\" in \"transscendentalem Verstande\" rendered \"sense\", an idiomatic phrase, not the faculty.","- \"des Eingeschränkten\" and \"dem Inhalte nach\" kept literal; \"Entweder und Oder\" capitalized as printed. The original has a footnote marker \")\" after \"Oder\" (1714) and at the end of 1720; the footnote text is not in the passage and is not supplied.","- \"Phänomen\" rendered \"phenomenon\". \"welches der Satz war\" kept literal (\"which was the proposition\")."]],"krv-b-1722":["[...]dically determine. The concept of such a being is that of God, thought in the transcendental sense; and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object of a transcendental theology, as I also stated above.",["- The passage begins mid-word (\"Chr dicamente\", OCR damage); the word before \"bestimmen können\" is unrecoverable and not supplied. The opening is therefore left as a fragment, marked [...].","- Wesen = being; Gegenstand = object; Begriff = concept; Verstande (in \"transscendentalem Verstande\") = \"sense\", rendered thus because the phrase is idiomatic; the German word is Verstand (understanding).","- Ideal = ideal; Theologie = theology (spelling \"transscendental\" normalized to \"transcendental\")."]],"krv-b-1723":["Meanwhile this use of the transcendental idea would already overstep the bounds of its determination and admissibility. For reason laid it down only as the concept of all reality at the basis of the thoroughgoing determination of things in general, without demanding that all this reality be given objectively and itself constitute a thing. This latter is a mere fiction, through which we combine and realize the manifold of our idea in an ideal as a particular being, for which we have no warrant, so much so that we do not even have it for directly assuming the possibility of such a hypothesis; just as also all consequences that flow from such an ideal do not concern the thoroughgoing determination of things in general, for the sake of which alone the idea was necessary, and have not the least influence on it. )",["- Grenze = boundary; Bestimmung = determination (here \"its determination\" in the sense of vocation is also possible; left as \"determination\"); bestimmung in \"durchgängige Bestimmung\" = determination.","- \"Begriff von aller Realität der durchgängigen Bestimmung\": the genitive construction is kept as in the German (\"concept of all reality at the basis of the thoroughgoing determination\"); the attachment of \"der durchgängigen Bestimmung\" is ambiguous and I have not resolved it. OCR break \"durchgän— gigen\" repaired.","- Gegenstand not present; Ding = thing; Wesen = being; Realität = reality; Folgerungen = consequences; Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); zusammenfassen = combine (not a glossary term).","- \"wozu wir keine Befugniß haben, sogar nicht einmal, die Möglichkeit ... anzunehmen\": clause kept as in the German; \"sogar nicht einmal\" rendered \"so much so that we do not even\". The final \")\" is in the source text (no opening parenthesis in this passage) and is retained.","- Erdichtung = fiction; Behuf = sake; objectiv = objectively."]],"krv-b-1724":["It is not enough to describe the procedure of our reason and its dialectic, one must also seek to discover the sources of it, in order to be able to explain this illusion itself as a phenomenon of the understanding; for the ideal of which we speak is grounded on a natural and not merely arbitrary idea. Hence I ask: how does reason come to regard all possibility of things as derived from a single one that lies at the basis, namely that of the highest reality, and then to presuppose this as contained in a particular original being?",["- Schein = illusion; Phänomen = phenomenon (Latinate/Greek word, distinct from Erscheinung = appearance); Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason.","- \"zum diesen Schein\" read as OCR slip for \"um diesen Schein\" (silently corrected).","- Grunde liegt = \"lies at the basis\"; Realität = reality; Möglichkeit = possibility; Urwesen = original being (Wesen = being).","- \"einer einzigen, die zum Grunde liegt, nämlich der der höchsten Realität\": the feminine \"einer einzigen\" has no stated noun; kept as \"a single one\". \"besondern\" = particular.","- The page reference \"B 609–610?\" is the user's; not verified."]],"krv-b-1725":["The answer offers itself of its own accord from the proceedings of the transcendental analytic. The possibility of the objects of the senses is a relation of them to our thinking, in which something (namely the empirical form) can be thought a priori, but that which constitutes the matter, the reality in the appearance (what corresponds to sensation), must be given, without which it could also not be thought at all and hence its possibility could not be represented. Now an object of the senses can be thoroughly determined only if it is compared with all the predicates of appearance and is represented through them affirmatively or negatively. But because that which constitutes the thing itself (in the appearance), namely the real, must be given therein, without which it could also not be thought at all; but that in which the real of all appearances is given is the one all-encompassing experience: so the matter for the possibility of all objects of the senses must be presupposed as given in a sum total, on whose limitation alone all possibility of empirical objects, their difference from one another and their thorough determination can rest. Now in fact no other objects than those of the senses can be given to us, and nowhere except in the context of a possible experience, consequently nothing is an object for us if it does not presuppose the sum total of all empirical reality as the condition of its possibility. By a natural illusion we now regard as a principle that which must hold of all things in general, which properly holds only of those that are given as objects of our senses. Consequently we will, by omission of this limitation, take the empirical principle of our concepts of the possibility of things as appearances for a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.",["- Verhältniß = \"relation\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\" (Object not present here); Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Einschränkung = \"limitation\" (glossary gives Schranke = limitation; Einschränkung not listed, rendered the same).","- \"sentspricht\" in the OCR corrected to \"entspricht\"; \"albefassende\" to \"allbefassende\"; \"wüͤsse\" to \"müsse\"; \"einige\" kept as \"one\".","- \"Illusion\" = \"illusion\" (Schein is also \"illusion\"); Grundsatz = \"principle\" and Princip = \"principle\" (one English word for two German words).","- \"durchgängig bestimmt\" rendered \"thoroughly determined\"; \"Weglassung\" = \"omission\"."]],"krv-b-1726":["But that we afterwards hypostatize this idea of the sum total of all reality comes from this: because we dialectically transform the distributive unity of the experiential use of the understanding into the collective unity of a whole of experience and think to ourselves, in this whole of appearance, a single thing that contains all empirical reality in itself, which then, by means of the already thought transcendental subreption, | is confused with the concept of a thing that stands at the apex of the possibility of all things, for whose thorough determination it supplies the real conditions.“) 1",["- The closing \".“) 1\" is the OCR residue of the end of the preceding quotation/footnote marker; kept as it stands. The sign | is carried over as in the source.","- hypostasiren = \"hypostatize\"; Subreption = \"subreption\"; Erfahrungsgebrauch = \"experiential use\"."]],"krv-b-1727":["Of the third chapter,\nThird Section.",["- \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"."]],"krv-b-1728":["On the grounds of proof of speculative reason for inferring to the",["- Beweisgründe = \"grounds of proof\"; the heading breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1729":["existence of a highest being. |",["- Dasein = \"existence\"; schließen = \"infer\"; the sign | is carried over."]],"krv-b-1730":["Notwithstanding this pressing need of reason, to presuppose something that could lie completely at the basis of the understanding for the thoroughgoing determination of its concepts, it nevertheless notices the ideal and merely fabricated character of such a presupposition far too easily for it to be persuaded by this alone to accept at once a mere self-creation of its thinking as an actual being, if it were not pressed by something else to seek somewhere its resting-place in the regress from the conditioned, which is given, to the unconditioned, which in itself and according to its mere concept is indeed not given as actual, but which alone can complete the series of the conditions carried out to their grounds. This now is the natural course that every human reason, even the most common, takes, although not every one holds out in it. It begins not from concepts, but from",["- The passage breaks off after \"nicht eine\" (OCR \"nicht eine :\"); the text continues at \"jede in demselben aushält\" in krv-b-1732, with footnote krv-b-1731 interposed. I rendered \"nicht eine\" as \"not every one\", reading the continuation as given; this is a doubtful join.","- OCR \"vor auszusetzen\" read as \"voraus zusetzen\" = \"presuppose\"; \"ı Begriffe\" read as \"Begriffe\".","- überredet = \"persuaded\" (Überredung = persuasion); wirklich = \"actual\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Grund = \"basis\"/\"grounds\" (zum Grunde liegen = \"lie at the basis\"; Gründen = \"grounds\")."]],"krv-b-1731":[") This ideal of the most real being will therefore, although it is a mere representation, first be realized, i.e., made into an object, then hypostatized, finally, by a natural progress of reason toward the completion of unity, even personified, as we shall soon adduce; because the regulative unity of experience rests not on the appearances themselves (on sensibility alone), but on the connection of their manifold through the understanding (in an apperception), and hence the unity of the highest reality and the thoroughgoing determinability (possibility) of all things seems to lie in a highest understanding, and hence in an intelligence.",["- Footnote (opening \")\" is OCR residue of the footnote marker). Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Object = \"object\" (Gegenstand also \"object\"); Fortschritt = \"progress\"; Mannigfaltiges = \"manifold\".","- \"Realisirt,\" \"hypostasirt,\" \"personificirt\" = \"realized,\" \"hypostatized,\" \"personified.\""]],"krv-b-1732":["every one holds out in it. It begins not from concepts, but from",["- Continues krv-b-1730; the first words \"jede in demselben aushält\" complete the clause begun there (\"nicht eine\"). Rendered as it stands; \"Sie fängt nicht von Begriffen, sondern von\" is not completed here."]],"krv-b-1733":["common experience and thus lays something existing at the basis.",["- Existirendes = \"existing\"; gemeinen = \"common\" (gemein = common)."]],"krv-b-1734":["But this ground sinks if it does not rest on the immovable rock of the",["- Boden = \"ground\" (soil; Grund is also \"ground\" in the glossary)."]],"krv-b-1735":["absolutely necessary. But this itself floats without support if there is still empty space outside and beneath it, and it does not itself fill everything and thereby leave no place any more for the Why, i.e., if it is not infinite according to reality",["- OCR \"is\" read as \"es\"; the sentence continues in krv-b-1736 (\"nach unendlich ist\"). Absolutnothwendigen = \"absolutely necessary\" (nominalized). Realität = \"reality\"; Raum = \"space\"."]],"krv-b-1736":["infinite.",["- The fragment \"nach unendlich ist\" completes the clause \"der Realität / nach unendlich ist\" begun in krv-b-1735; \"der Realität nach\" is rendered there. Here only \"unendlich ist\" is left; \"nach\" is carried in the previous entry."]],"krv-b-1737":["If something, whatever it may be, exists, then it must also be conceded that something exists necessarily. For the contingent exists only under the condition of another as its cause,",["- OCR \"wer— den\" read as \"werden\"; \"Zufäl— lo lige\" read as \"Zufällige\"; the \"lo\" is a line number. zufällig = \"contingent\"; Bedingung = \"condition\"; Ursache = \"cause\"."]],"krv-b-1738":["and the inference holds of this further, up to a cause that is not",["- Schluß = \"inference\"."]],"krv-b-1739":["contingent and just for that reason exists necessarily without condition. That",["- nothwendigerweise = \"necessarily\"; \"ohne Bedingung\" = \"without condition\"."]],"krv-b-1740":["is the argument on which reason grounds its progress to the original being",["- Fortschritt = \"progress\"; Urwesen = \"original being\"."]],"krv-b-1741":[".",["- Only \"gründet.\" appears in the source; the verb \"grounds\" is rendered at the end of krv-b-1740. Here: \"gründet\" = \"grounds\"."]],"krv-b-1742":["Now reason looks around for the concept of a being that 5",["- The numeral 5 is a line number in the OCR, kept as it stands; the clause continues in krv-b-1743."]],"krv-b-1743":["befits such a preeminence of existence as unconditioned necessity—",["- \"Vorzug\" = \"preeminence\"; \"schicke\" = \"befits\"; the line break \"Nothwen— / digkeit\" joined."]],"krv-b-1744":["not so much in order then to infer from the concept of it a priori",["- \"a priori\" is attached to \"infer\" or to \"concept\" as in German: ambiguous, left so."]],"krv-b-1745":["to its existence (for if it trusted itself to do this, it would need only to research among mere concepts and would not need to lay a given existence at the basis), but only in order to find among all concepts of possible things that which has in itself nothing conflicting with absolute necessity. For that something",["- \"eo\" is a line number in the OCR; dropped. \"Widerstreitendes\" = \"conflicting\"; \"forschen\" = \"research\"."]],"krv-b-1746":["at all must exist absolutely necessarily, it already holds as settled from the first inference. If it can now remove everything that is not compatible with 25 this necessity, except one: then this is the absolutely necessary being, whether one can now comprehend its necessity,",["- The numeral 25 is a line number; kept as in the source. begreifen = \"comprehend\"; the word is divided over \"be- / greifen\" and completes in krv-b-1747."]],"krv-b-1747":["i.e., derive it from its concept alone, or not.",["- \"begreifen\" appears at the end of the preceding fragment."]],"krv-b-1748":["Now that seems, whose concept contains the Therefore for every Why",["- Warum/Darum rendered \"Why\"/\"Therefore\"; the sentence continues in krv-b-1749."]],"krv-b-1749":["in itself, which is deficient in no respect and in no aim, which suffices everywhere as condition, 30 for that very reason to be the being befitting absolute necessity, because with the self-possession of all conditions for everything possible it itself needs no condition, indeed is not even capable of | one, consequently at least in one respect does justice to the concept of unconditioned necessity, in which no other concept can do the like of it, which, because it is deficient and in need of supplementation, shows no such mark of independence from all further conditions in itself.",["- Numerals 30 and 35 (OCR \"as\") are line numbers; the \"|\" signs are carried over. Stück = \"respect\"; Absicht = \"aim\" (glossary); Merkmal = \"mark\"; \"fähig\" = \"capable\"; \"Ergänzung\" = \"supplementation\"; the sentence concludes in krv-b-1750 (\"kein solches Merkmal…\")."]],"krv-b-1750":["supplementation, shows no such mark of independence from all",["- Fragment \"gänzung bedürftig ist, kein solches Merkmal der Unabhängigkeit von allen\" overlaps the end of krv-b-1749; rendered there in full, repeated here only in part."]],"krv-b-1751":["further conditions in itself. It is true that it cannot yet be securely concluded from this that what does not contain the highest and in every aim complete condition in itself must, for that reason, itself be conditioned as to its existence; but it nevertheless does not have in itself the sole mark of unconditioned existence, of which reason is capable, in order to cognize through a concept a priori some being as unconditioned.",["- \"Ii\" is OCR residue (\"seiner Existenz Ii nach\"), a line number; dropped. Merkzeichen = \"mark\" (Merkmal also \"mark\"); mächtig = \"capable\" (rendered after the German \"dessen die Vernunft mächtig ist\"); erkennen = \"cognize\"; folgern = \"conclude\".","- \"a priori\" could attach to \"concept\" or to \"cognize\": left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-1752":["The concept of a being of the highest reality would therefore be the one that, among all concepts of possible things, best befits the concept of an unconditionally necessary being, and even if it does not fully satisfy this, we nevertheless have no choice but see ourselves constrained to hold to it; because we may not cast to the winds the existence of a necessary being, but if we concede it, we can nevertheless find in the whole field of possibility nothing that could make a better grounded claim to such a preeminence in existence.",["- \"Is\" and \"f\" are OCR residue (line numbers / marks): dropped. Existenz and Dasein both = \"existence\"; \"Vorzug\" = \"preeminence\"; \"gegründetern\" = \"better grounded\"."]],"krv-b-1753":["Thus the natural course of human reason is constituted.“ First it convinces itself of the existence of some necessary being. In this it cognizes an unconditioned existence. Now it seeks the concept of what is independent of all condition and finds it in that which is itself the sufficient condition for everything else, i.e., in that which contains all reality. But the all without limitations is absolute unity; and carries with it the concept of a single, namely of the highest being; and so it concludes that the highest being, as primordial ground of all things, exists absolutely necessarily.",["- Stray closing quotation mark after \"constituted\" (\"beschaffen.“\") kept as in the OCR; no opening mark appears in the passage.","- \"sichvom\" silently corrected to \"sich vom\". Wesen = being; Existenz and Dasein both = \"existence\"; Schranken = limitations; Realität = reality; Urgrund = primordial ground.","- \"zureichend\" = sufficient; \"schlechthin nothwendiger Weise\" rendered \"absolutely necessarily\" (schlechthin = absolutely).","- \"einigen\" (einig) rendered \"single\"; \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\""]],"krv-b-1754":["To this concept a certain thoroughness cannot be disputed, when the talk is of resolutions, namely when the existence of some necessary being is once granted, and one agrees that one must take one's side as to where one wants to posit it; for then one cannot choose more suitably, or rather one has no choice, but is compelled to give one's vote to the absolute unity of complete reality as the primal source of possibility. But if nothing drives us to resolve, and we would rather let this whole matter stand, until we are compelled to assent by the full weight of the grounds of proof, i.e., if it is | merely a matter of judging how much we know of this problem, and what we merely flatter ourselves that we know: then the above inference appears by far not in so advantageous a shape and requires favor to make up for the lack of its legal claims.",["- OCR \"das l Dasein\" read as \"das Dasein\"; stray \"l\" dropped.","- Beurteilung = judging (judgment); Aufgabe = problem; Wissen/wissen = know(ledge); Beweisgründe = grounds of proof; Schluß = inference.","- \"Entschließungen\" = resolutions; \"Partei ergreifen\" = \"take one's side\"; \"Rechtsansprüche\" = \"legal claims\".","- The sign | is the Academy page break, carried over where it stands."]],"krv-b-1755":["For if we let everything pass as well as it lies here before us: namely, first, that from some given existence (at most even merely my own) a correct inference to the existence of an unconditionally necessary being takes place; second, that I must regard a being that contains all reality, hence also all condition, as absolutely unconditioned, consequently that the concept of the thing that is suited to absolute necessity is hereby found: then it cannot at all be concluded from this that the concept of a limited being that does not have the highest reality therefore contradicts absolute necessity. For although I do not encounter in its concept the unconditioned that the all of conditions already carries with it, it cannot at all be inferred from this that its existence must for that very reason be conditioned; just as in a hypothetical syllogism I cannot say: where a certain condition (namely here that of completeness in accordance with concepts) is not, there the conditioned is not either. Rather it will remain unhindered for us to let all the remaining limited beings count just as well as unconditionally necessary, even though we cannot conclude their necessity from the general concept that we have of them. In this way, however, this argument would not have procured for us the least concept of properties of a necessary being and would have accomplished nothing at all.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"aß nämlich\" = \"daß nämlich\"; \"Aoß\" = \"bloß\"; \"ünbedingt\", \"Hoch gar nicht\" = \"noch gar nicht\"; \"Absoluten\"; \"Noth-Imendigfeit\"; \"ann\" = \"kann\"; \"ver— schafft\".","- Widerspricht = contradicts; \"folgern\" (gefolgert) and \"schließen\" both appear, rendered \"inferred\" and \"concluded\" respectively; Vernunftschluß = syllogism.","- Eingeschränkt = limited; Eigenschaften = properties; Bedingung = condition; Notwendigkeit = necessity.","- \"ob ich gleich ... so kann daraus doch\" kept as one concessive sentence."]],"krv-b-1756":["Nevertheless a certain importance and a standing remain to this argument, which on account of this objective insufficiency cannot yet at once be taken from it. For suppose there were obligations that were quite correct in the idea of reason, but without all reality of applica-",["- Passage breaks off mid-word (\"Anwen-\"); continued in the next passage (\"ung\"). Left as it stands.","- Verbindlichkeiten = obligations; Idee = idea; Anwendung = application."]],"krv-b-1757":["tion to ourselves, i.e., without incentives, unless a highest being were presupposed that could give effect and emphasis to the practical laws: then we would also have an obligation",["- Begins mid-word (\"ung\" for \"-wendung\"); I have rendered \"tion\" to join the English fragment of the previous passage.","- Triebfedern = incentives; Wirkung = effect; \"wo nicht\" rendered \"unless\".","- Passage breaks off before the infinitive clause (\"zu haben\"), continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1758":["to follow the concepts which, even if they might not be objectively sufficient, are nevertheless preponderant according to the measure of our reason, and in comparison with which we cognize nothing better and more convincing. The duty to choose would here bring the indecision of speculation out of equilibrium through a practical addition, indeed reason would find no justification before itself, as the most indulgent judge, if under pressing motives, although of only deficient insight, it had not followed these grounds of its judgment, than which we at least know no better.",["- Begins with \"aben\" for \"haben\"; OCR slip. \"Hemwegursachen\" read as \"Bewegursachen\" (Bewegursachen = motives); doubtful OCR reading.","- Zulänglich = sufficient (zulänglich, not zureichend); überführend = convincing; Einsicht = insight; Urteil = judgment; Gründe = grounds.","- \"nachsehendsten\" = \"most indulgent\"."]],"krv-b-1759":["This argument, although it is in fact transcendental, in that it rests on the inner insufficiency of the contingent, is nevertheless so simple and natural that it is suited to the commonest human sense as soon as this is only once led to it. One sees things alter, arise and pass away; they must therefore, or at least their state, have a cause. Of every cause, however, that may ever be given in experience, the very same can again be asked. Where, now, should we more fairly locate the supreme causality than there where the highest causality also is, i.e., in that being that originally contains in itself the sufficiency for every possible effect, whose concept also comes about very easily through the single feature of an all-encompassing perfection. This highest cause we then take to be absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to ascend up to it, and no ground to go still further beyond it. Hence we see among all peoples, through their blindest polytheism, nevertheless some sparks of monotheism shine through, to which not reflection and deep speculation, but only a natural course of common understanding, which has gradually become intelligible, has led.",["- OCR \"ein- IM fällig\", \"ver- si ändern\", \"Zus Im stand\", \"Er fahrung\" corrected silently.","- Menschensinn = human sense; Verstand = understanding (\"gemeinen Verstandes\" = \"common understanding\"); Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency; Zufälligen = contingent; Vollkommenheit = perfection.","- \"Wirkung\" = effect; Causalität = causality (Latinate spelling kept as in text); \"halten wir ... für\" = \"we take ... to be\".","- The question \"Wohin sollen wir ...\" ends with a period in the German; kept as it stands. The sign | at the end is the Academy page break."]],"krv-b-1760":["There are only three kinds of proof of the existence of God",["- Fragment continues in the next passage (\"aus speculativer Vernunft möglich\"). Beweisart = kind of proof; Dasein = existence."]],"krv-b-1761":["possible from speculative reason.",["- Stray \"i\" after \"möglich\" in the OCR dropped. Continuation of the preceding heading."]],"krv-b-1762":["All the paths that one may take in this aim either begin from determinate experience and the particular constitution of our world of sense cognized through it, and ascend from it according to laws of causality to the highest cause outside the world; or they lay at the ground only indeterminate experience, i.e., some existence, empirically; or they finally abstract from all experience and conclude entirely *a priori* from mere concepts to the existence of a highest cause. The first proof is the physico-theological, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological proof. There are no more of them, and there also cannot be more.",["- OCR \"ent- f weder\", \"cm- | pirisch\" corrected to \"entweder\", \"empirisch\"; the | page break is omitted at the mid-word break since the word is restored. Mehr; giebt = \"Mehr giebt\".","- Absicht = aim; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Erfahrung = experience; Dasein = existence; Beweis = proof; Grund = ground.","- \"empirisch\" can attach to \"legen ... zum Grunde\" or to \"Dasein\"; left ambiguous as in the German.","- *a priori* printed in roman in the German; italicized as Latin."]],"krv-b-1763":["I shall demonstrate: that reason accomplishes as little on the one path (the empirical) as on the other (the transcendental), and that it spreads its wings in vain to get beyond the world of sense through the mere power of speculation. But as for the order in which these kinds of proof must be submitted to examination, it will be precisely the reverse of that which reason, gradually expanding, takes, and in which we also first placed them. For it will be shown: that, although experience gives the first occasion for it, nevertheless merely the transcendental concept guides reason in this its endeavor and in all such attempts sets out the goal that it has set itself. I shall therefore begin from the examination of the transcendental proof and afterwards see what the addition of the empirical can do toward the enlargement of its force of proof.",["- OCR \"cm- f pirischen\", \"trans— seendentale\" corrected silently.","- darthun = demonstrate; Prüfung = examination; Beweiskraft = force of proof; Bestrebung = endeavor; Anlaß = occasion; Macht = power.","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"world of sense\"."]],"krv-b-1764":["Of the third chapter,\nFourth section.",["- Hauptstück = chapter; Abschnitt = section. The title continues in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-1765":["On the impossibility of an ontological proof",["- Unmöglichkeit = impossibility; Beweis = proof; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1766":["of the existence of God.",["- Dasein = existence."]],"krv-b-1767":["One easily sees from what has gone before: that the concept of an absolutely necessary being is a pure concept of reason, i.e., a mere idea, whose objective reality is by no means yet proved by the fact that reason needs it, which also gives direction only to a certain, though unattainable, completeness and actually serves more to bound the understanding than to extend it to new objects. Here there now turns up something strange and contrary to sense, that the inference from a given existence in general to some absolutely necessary existence seems to be pressing and correct, and we nevertheless have all the conditions of the understanding for making for ourselves a concept of such a necessity entirely against us. | At all times one has spoken of the absolutely necessary being and has not so much taken pains to understand whether and how one could even think a thing of this kind, as rather to prove its existence. Now a nominal definition of this concept is indeed quite easy, namely that it is something the non-being of which is impossible; but one becomes thereby no wiser with regard to the conditions that make it impossible to regard the non-being of a thing as absolutely unthinkable, and which are actually what one wants to know, namely whether through this concept we think anything at all, or not. For to throw away by means of the word: unconditioned all the conditions that the understanding always needs in order to regard something as necessary, makes it by no means yet understandable to me whether I then think something through a concept of the unconditionally necessary, or perhaps nothing at all. 1",[]],"krv-b-1768":["Still more: this concept, ventured at mere random and finally! become quite familiar, one has moreover believed to explain through a multitude of examples, so that all further inquiry concerning its intelligibility seemed entirely unnecessary. Every proposition of geometry, e.g., that a triangle has three angles, is absolutely necessary; and 1 so one spoke of an object that lies entirely outside the sphere f of our understanding, as if one understood quite well what one wanted to say with | the concept of it. f",[]],"krv-b-1769":["All the examples put forward are without exception taken only from judgments, but not from things and their existence. The unconditioned necessity of judgments, however, is not an absolute necessity of things. For the absolute necessity of the judgment is only a conditioned necessity of the thing, or of the predicate in the judgment. The previous proposition did not say that three angles are absolutely necessary, but rather, under the condition that a triangle is there (is given), three angles are f also there (in it) necessarily. Nevertheless this logical necessity has proved such a great power of its illusion that,“ in that one had made for oneself a concept a priori of a thing, which was so set up that in one's opinion one comprehended existence along in its | domain, one believed one could infer from this with certainty that, because existence necessarily belongs to the object of this concept, i.e., under the condition that I posit this thing as given (existing), its existence too is necessarily posited (according to the rule of identity), and this being therefore is itself absolutely necessary, because its existence is thought along in a concept assumed at pleasure and under the condition that I posit the object of it. !",[]],"krv-b-1770":["If I cancel the predicate in an identical judgment and retain the subject, a contradiction arises, and therefore I say: the former belongs to the latter necessarily. But if I cancel the subject together with the predicate, no contradiction arises; for there is nothing any more that could be contradicted. To posit a triangle and yet cancel its three angles is contradictory, but to cancel the triangle together with its three angles is no contradiction. Exactly the same holds with the concept of an absolutely necessary being. If you cancel its existence, then you cancel the thing itself with all its predicates; where then is the contradiction supposed to",[]],"krv-b-1771":["come from? Externally there is nothing that would be contradicted, for the thing is not supposed to be externally necessary; internally also nothing, for you",[]],"krv-b-1772":["have by canceling the thing itself canceled everything internal at the same time. God is omnipotent; that is a necessary judgment. Omnipotence cannot",[]],"krv-b-1773":["be canceled if you posit a deity, i.e., an infinite",[]],"krv-b-1774":["being, with whose concept that is identical. But if you say:",[]],"krv-b-1775":["God is not, then neither omnipotence nor any other of his predicates is given; for they are all canceled together with the subject,",[]],"krv-b-1776":["and in this thought not the least contradiction shows itself. | You have therefore seen that if I cancel the predicate of a judgment together with the subject, an internal contradiction can never",[]],"krv-b-1777":["arise, whatever the predicate may be. Now no evasion remains to you except that you must say: there are subjects that cannot be canceled at",[]],"krv-b-1778":["all, that therefore must remain. That would",[]],"krv-b-1779":["however amount to just as much as saying: there are absolutely necessary subjects;",[]],"krv-b-1780":["a presupposition of whose correctness I have precisely doubted, and",[]],"krv-b-1781":["whose possibility you wanted to show me. For I cannot make for myself the",[]],"krv-b-1782":["slightest concept of a thing which, if it were canceled with all its predicates, would leave behind a contradiction; and without contradiction I have, through mere pure concepts a priori, no mark of impossibility.",[]],"krv-b-1783":["Against all these general inferences (which no human being can refuse) you challenge me with a case that you set up as a proof through the deed: that there is nevertheless one and indeed only this One concept, in which the non-being or the canceling of its object is self-contradictory; and this is the concept of the most real being. It has, you say, all reality, and you feld entitled to assume such a being as possible (to which I consent for now, although the concept that does not contradict itself is still far from proving the possibility of the object)“). Now among all reality existence too is 5 *) The concept is always possible if it does not contradict itself. That ; is the logical mark of possibility, and thereby its object is distinguished from i the *nihil negativum*. Yet it can nonetheless be an empty concept comprehended: thus existence lies in the concept of a possible. If this thing is now canceled, then the inner possibility of the thing is canceled, which is contradictory. 1",["- Wesen = \"being\" (glossary); Ding = \"thing\"; Gegenstand and Object both \"object\" (Gegenstand here; \"Object\" at B 622 \"dem Object dieses Begriffs\").","- Begriff = concept; Urtheil = judgment; Prädicat = predicate; Merkmal = mark; Dasein = existence; Nichtsein = non-being; Umfang = domain; Bedingung = condition.","- \"Namenerklärung\" rendered \"nominal definition\" (Erklärung = definition).","- \"Nothwendigkeit/Notwendigkeit\" and \"schlechthin/schlechterdings\" both rendered \"absolutely\"; \"absolut\" also \"absolutely\"; \"unbedingt\" = \"unconditioned/unconditionally.\"","- \"Widersinnische\" rendered \"contrary to sense\" (literal); \"Befremdliche\" = \"strange.\"","- \"aufheben\" rendered \"cancel\" consistently (also \"Aufhebung\" = \"canceling\").","- OCR remnants kept as in the passage: stray \"1\", \"f\", \"!\", \"“\", \"”)\", \"5\", \"feld\" (probably \"seid\"; \"ihr seid berechtigt\"), left uncorrected and doubtful.","- Footnote in krv-b-1783 is garbled/cut off: \"Er kann nichts destoweniger ein leerer Begriff begriffen: also…\" is evidently corrupt (text missing); translated as it stands. \"begriffen/begriff\" in \"Umfang begriff\" = \"comprehended.\""]],"krv-b-1784":["I answer: You have already committed a contradiction when you | brought into the concept of a thing, which you wanted to think merely according to its possibility, under whatever concealed name, already the concept of its existence. If one concedes this to you, then you have according to illusion won the game, but in fact have said nothing; for you have committed a mere tautology. I ask you, is the proposition: this or that thing (which I concede to you as possible, | be it whichever it may) exists; is, I say, this proposition an analytic or a synthetic proposition? If it is the former, then through the existence of the thing you add nothing to your thought of the thing; but then either the thought that is in you would have to be the thing itself, or you have presupposed an existence as belonging to possibility and then, allegedly, inferred the existence from inner possibility, which is nothing but a miserable tautology. The word: reality, which in the concept of the thing sounds different from existence in the concept of the predicate, does not settle it. For even if you call all positing (leaving undetermined what you posit) reality, you have already posited the thing with all its predicates in the concept of the subject and assumed it as actual, and in the predicate you only repeat it. If, on the other hand, you admit, as every reasonable person must fairly admit, that every existential proposition is synthetic: how then do you want to assert that the predicate of existence cannot be canceled without contradiction? since this prerogative belongs peculiarly only to the analytic ones, whose character rests precisely on it.",["- Existenz / Dasein: both \"existence\" (Gegenstand/Object not at issue here); \"Existenzialsatz\" rendered \"existential proposition\".","- Schein in \"dem Scheine nach\" = \"according to illusion\" (glossary: Schein = illusion); Realität = reality; wirklich = actual; Satz = proposition.","- \"Setzen\" = positing; \"es mag sein, welches es wolle\" kept literal; \"aufheben\" = cancel.","- OCR slips silently corrected (Gristenz, fegt, dec f haupten, etc.); stray letters beside the | signs dropped, the signs kept."]],"krv-b-1785":["I would indeed hope to annihilate this brooding argutation without any circumlocution through an exact determination of the concept of existence, if I had not found that the illusion in the confusion of a logical predicate with a real one (i.e. with the determination of a thing) almost precludes all teaching. Anything one wants can serve as logical predicate, even the subject can be predicated of itself; for logic abstracts from all content. But the determination is a predicate that is added to the concept of the subject and enlarges it. It must therefore not already be contained in it. | Being is obviously no real predicate, i.e. a concept of anything that could be added to the concept of a thing. It is merely the position of a thing or of certain determinations in itself. In logical use it is merely the copula of a judgment. The\n\nFootnote: ... be, if the objective reality of the synthesis through which the concept is generated is not especially demonstrated; which, however, always, as was shown above, rests on principles of possible experience and not on the principle of analysis (the principle of contradiction). That is a warning not to infer immediately from the possibility of concepts (logical) to the possibility of things (real).",["- The OCR of this passage has the footnote text interleaved in the main sentence; I have separated it and placed it after the main text, where the footnote marker is not preserved. The footnote begins mid-sentence (its opening is not in the passage), so \"sein\" is rendered \"be\" after an ellipsis.","- \"Argutation\" kept as the cognate of Argutation; \"grüblerisch\" = \"brooding\". \"beinahe alle Belehrung ausschlage\" = \"almost precludes all teaching\" (the verb ausschlagen is obscure here).","- Bestimmung = determination; Position = position; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself\" (could attach to \"thing\" or to \"determinations\").","- Schein/Illusion = illusion; Satz = principle in \"Grundsatz\", \"Satz des Widerspruchs\" = principle of contradiction."]],"krv-b-1786":["proposition: God is almighty, contains two concepts that have their objects",["- Satz = proposition; Object = object (Latinate Object); the passage breaks off after \"Objecte\"; its continuation is in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1787":["God and omnipotence; the little word: is, is not yet another predicate",["- Allmacht = omnipotence (rendered the same wherever it occurs)."]],"krv-b-1788":["on top, but only that which posits the predicate in relation to the",["- \"obenein\" = \"on top\"; \"beziehungsweise\" = \"in relation\"."]],"krv-b-1789":["subject. If I now take the subject (God) together with all its predicates (to which omnipotence also belongs) and say: God is,",["- OCR \"Prädis caten\" corrected to Prädicaten."]],"krv-b-1790":["or there is a God, then I posit no new predicate to the concept of God, but only the subject in itself with all its predicates",["- \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself\"."]],"krv-b-1791":["and indeed the object in relation to my concept. Both must contain exactly one and the same, and hence nothing further can be added to the concept, which merely expresses the possibility, because I think its object as simply given (through the expression: it is). And thus the actual contains nothing more than the merely possible. A hundred actual thalers contain not the least more than",["- Gegenstand = object; Wirkliche = the actual; Ausdruck = expression; \"Hinzukommen\" = \"added\" (lit. \"come to\")."]],"krv-b-1792":["a hundred possible ones. For since these signify the concept, but those the object",["- \"diese ... jene\" kept as \"these ... those\" without resolving the referents. bedeuten = signify."]],"krv-b-1793":["and its position in itself, in case this contained more",["- \"dieser ... jener\" (next passage) kept as \"this ... that\"; the German does not name the referents."]],"krv-b-1794":["than that, my concept would not express the whole object",["- Continues the sentence of the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1795":["and thus also would not be the adequate concept of it. But in my financial state there is more with a hundred actual thalers than with the mere concept of them (i.e. of their possibility). For the object, with actuality, is not merely analytically contained in my concept, but is added synthetically to my concept (which is a determination of my state), without these thought hundred thalers themselves being increased in the least through this being outside my concept.",["- \"Vermögenszustande\" (Vermögen in the sense of assets) rendered \"financial state\"; the glossary's \"faculty/capacity\" does not fit here. Zustand = state.","- OCR \"Gegend stand\" corrected to Gegenstand. Wirklichkeit = actuality; Bestimmung = determination.","- \"außerhalb meinem Begriffe\" (dative in the original) kept as \"outside my concept\"."]],"krv-b-1796":["If I therefore think a thing through whichever and however many predicates I want,",["- Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1797":["(even in thoroughgoing determination), then through my adding further:",["- \"durchgängigen Bestimmung\" = \"thoroughgoing determination\"."]],"krv-b-1798":["this thing is, not the least is added to the thing. For otherwise not exactly the same, but more would exist than I had thought in the concept, and I could not say that precisely the object of my concept exists. Even if I think in a thing all reality except one, then through my saying: such a deficient thing exists, the missing reality is not added; rather it exists burdened with precisely the same deficiency as I have thought it, otherwise something other than I thought would exist. If I now think a being as the highest reality (without deficiency), the question still remains whether it exists or not. For although nothing at all is lacking in my concept of the possible real content of a thing in general, something is still lacking in the relation to my whole state of thinking, namely that the cognition of that object also be possible a posteriori. And here too the cause of the difficulty prevailing in this shows itself. Were there talk of an object of the senses, I would not be able to confuse the existence of the thing with the mere concept of the thing. For through the concept the object is thought only as in agreement with the general conditions of a possible empirical cognition in general, but through the existence as contained in the context of the entire experience; whereby, through the connection with the content of the entire experience, the concept of the object is not increased in the least, but our thinking nevertheless gets through it one more possible perception. If, on the other hand, we want to think existence through the pure category alone, it is no wonder that we can give no mark by which to distinguish it from mere possibility.",["- Wesen = being; Realität = reality; Inhalt = content; Erkenntnis = cognition; Verknüpfung = connection; Wahrnehmung = perception; Merkmal = mark; Gegenstand and Object both \"object\".","- OCR slips corrected (u/zu, foni/sondern, mü)/mir, aben/aber, wi/meinem, \"gerade des Gegenstand\" read as \"gerade der Gegenstand\"); the passage begins with the B 628–629 text and \"a posteriori\" is left in Latin italics only where not printed otherwise, as in the source.","- \"Verhältnisse zu meinem ganzen Zustande des Denkens\" kept literal (\"relation to my whole state of thinking\"). \"Context\" is the German Context."]],"krv-b-1799":["Our concept of an object may therefore contain whatever and however much it wants, yet we must go out beyond it in order to grant existence to it. In objects of the senses this happens through the interconnection with some one of my perceptions according to empirical laws; but for objects of pure thinking there is entirely and altogether no means to cognize their existence, because it would have to be cognized entirely a priori; but our consciousness of all existence (whether immediately through perception, or through inferences that connect something with the perception) belongs entirely and altogether to the unity of experience; and an existence outside this field can indeed not be declared absolutely impossible, but it is a presupposition that we can justify through nothing.",["- Gegenstand / Object = object; Existenz / Dasein = existence; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Mittel = means; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Schlüsse = inferences; Einheit = unity.","- \"diesem die Existenz zu ertheilen\": \"diesem\" kept as \"to it\", referring back to the concept or the object (the German is ambiguous). OCR slips (cm pirischen, ga ', erfann) corrected silently."]],"krv-b-1800":["The concept of a highest being is, in many respects, a very useful idea; but just because it is merely an idea, it is entirely incapable of extending our cognition, by means of it alone, with regard to what exists. It is not even capable of so much that it would instruct us with regard to the possibility of a plurality. The analytic mark of possibility, which consists in this, that mere positions (realities) produce no contradiction, cannot indeed be disputed to it; but since the connection of all real properties in one thing is a synthesis, about whose possibility we cannot judge *a priori*, because the realities are not given to us specifically, and, even if this were to happen, no judgment at all takes place therein, because the mark of the possibility of synthetic cognitions must always be sought only in experience, to which, however, the object of an idea cannot belong: so the famous Leibniz has by far not accomplished that of which he flattered himself, namely to want to have insight *a priori* into the possibility of so sublime an ideal being.",["- Absicht = \"respect\" here, which departs from the glossary's \"aim\"; Ansehung = \"regard\". Erkenntniß = cognition; Wesen = being; Merkmal = mark; Widerspruch = contradiction.","- \"eines Mehreren\" rendered \"of a plurality\"; the German could also be read as \"of something more.\"","- Eigenschaften = properties (Beschaffenheit, also \"property,\" does not occur here). Verknüpfung = connection. einsehen = \"have insight into.\"","- OCR slips corrected silently: \"unmn fähig\" → unfähig; \"da!)\" → da."]],"krv-b-1801":["Thus in the so famous ontological (Cartesian) proof of the existence of a highest being from concepts all effort and labor is lost, and a human being would probably become no richer in insights from mere ideas than a merchant in assets, if he, in order to improve his state, wanted to append some zeros to his cash balance.",["- Dasein = existence; Beweis = proof; Begriff = concept; Zustand = state.","- Vermögen is rendered \"assets\" here (the glossary gives \"faculty; capacity\"). Cassenbestand = \"cash balance.\""]],"krv-b-1802":["Of the third chapter. Fifth Section.",["- Hauptstück = \"chapter.\" The stray \"i\" is an OCR remnant and is omitted."]],"krv-b-1803":["On the impossibility of a cosmological proof",["- None beyond the above."]],"krv-b-1804":["of the existence of God.",["- Dasein = existence."]],"krv-b-1805":["It was something quite unnatural and a mere innovation of scholastic wit, to want to pick out from a quite arbitrarily devised idea the existence of the object corresponding to it itself. In fact one would never have attempted it on this path, had not the need of our reason, to assume for existence in general something necessary (at which one could stop in ascending),",["- Schulwitz = \"scholastic wit\" (lit. \"school-wit\"). Existenz = existence, as is Dasein; both are rendered \"existence.\"","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence. The leading \";\" is an OCR remnant and is omitted."]],"krv-b-1806":["[…] had preceded, and had not reason, since this necessity must be unconditioned and certain *a priori*, been compelled to seek a concept that, if possible, would satisfy such a demand and give an existence to be cognized entirely *a priori*. This one now believed to find in the idea of a most real being, and so this was used only for the more determinate acquaintance with that of which one was already otherwise convinced or persuaded that it must exist, namely the necessary being. Meanwhile one concealed this natural course of reason, and instead of ending with this concept, one tried to begin from it, in order to derive from it the necessity of existence, which it was yet determined only to supplement. From this now arose the ill-fated ontological proof, which carries with it something […] neither for natural and healthy understanding nor for scholastically correct examination. […] the connection of absolute necessity with the highest reality; but instead of inferring, like the previous one, from the highest reality to the necessity in existence, it infers rather from the unconditioned necessity of some being given in advance to its unbounded reality, and so far brings everything at least into the track of a kind of inference, I know not whether rational or rationalizing, at least natural, which carries with it the most persuasion not only for the common but also for the speculative understanding; as indeed it also visibly draws the first basic lines to all proofs of natural theology, which one has at all times followed and will further follow, one may decorate and hide them with as much foliage and flourish as one will. This proof, which Leibniz also called that *a contingentia mundi*, we now want to place before the eyes and submit to examination.",["- The text is corrupt at three places. It opens with OCR debris (\"A1; weil\") before \"vorhergegangen,\" which I did not translate. Mid-text \"etwas wee i bei sich führt\" is unreadable (marked […]). A gap then precedes \"die Verknüpfung der absoluten Nothwendigkeit…,\" so the sentence has no visible subject. I filled nothing.","- allerrealsten = \"most real\"; Kenntniß = acquaintance; Überredung = persuasion; vernünfteln = \"rationalize\"; Verstand = understanding; Schlußart = \"kind of inference\"; Prüfung = examination.","- Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Realität = reality; Dasein and Existenz are both \"existence.\""]],"krv-b-1807":["It runs thus: If something exists, then an absolutely necessary being must also exist. Now at least I myself exist; therefore an absolutely necessary being exists. The minor premise contains an experience, the major premise the inference from an experience in general to the existence of the necessary*). Thus the proof really begins",["- Untersatz = minor premise; Obersatz = major premise; Schlußfolge = inference (the glossary's \"Schluß\"); Erfahrung = experience; Dasein = existence.","- \"Mfo\" corrected to \"Also,\" rendered \"Thus\"; \"existire…?\" given a period. The footnote marker *) is retained, and the footnote follows in the next passage. \"hebt … an\" = \"begins,\" continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1808":["*) This inference is too well known for it to be necessary to set it out here at length. It rests on the supposed transcendental […] from experience, hence it is not conducted entirely *a priori* or ontologically; and because the object of all possible experience is called world, it is therefore called the cosmological proof. Since it also abstracts from every particular property of the objects of experience, there-",["- The footnote text is corrupt. \"Sie beruht auf dem vermeintlich transscendentalen Natu\" breaks off, and the text then runs into main-text words (\"von der Erfahrung an…\") without the intervening material. I marked the break […] and supplied nothing.","- besondern = \"particular\"; Eigenschaft = property; Gegenstand = object. The hyphenated \"da⸗\" is rendered \"there-\" and completes as \"thereby\" (\"dadurch\") in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1809":["by this world may distinguish itself from every possible one: so it is already in its term also distinguished from the physico-theological proof, which uses observations of the particular constitution of this our world of sense as grounds of proof. | Now the proof infers further: the necessary being can only on",["- \"durch sich…abstrahirt\" continues the previous passage's \"da-\" (dadurch). The German word order is retained, with \"abstracts\" now in the preceding passage's clause.","- Benennung = term; Beobachtung = observation; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Beweisgründe = \"grounds of proof\"; Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense.\"","- The sign | is carried over as it stands, and the passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1810":["a single way, i.e., in regard to all possible opposed predicates be determined only through one of them, consequently it must be thoroughly determined through its concept. Now only a single concept of a thing is possible that determines it thoroughly *a priori*, namely that of the *ens realissimum*. Therefore the concept of the most real being is the only one through which a necessary being can be thought, i.e., a highest being exists necessarily. | In this cosmological argument so many rationalizing principles come together that speculative reason seems to have summoned up here all its dialectical art in order to bring about the greatest possible transcendental illusion. We want, however, to set its examination aside for a while, in order only to make manifest one ruse of it, with which it sets up an old argument in disguised shape as a new one and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, namely a pure witness of reason and another of empirical credentials, though it is only the former alone that merely changes its dress and voice in order to be taken for a second. In order to lay its ground quite securely, this proof plants itself on experience and thereby gives itself the look of being distinct from the ontological proof, which places its whole confidence in mere pure concepts *a priori*. But the cosmological proof makes use of this experience only in order to take a single step, namely to the existence of a necessary being in general. What properties this has, the [the text here runs into the garbled insertion: \"gesetz der Causalität: daß alles Zufällige seine Ursache habe, die, wenn sie wiederum zufällig ist, eben sowohl eine Ursache haben muß, bis die Reihe der einander untergeordneten Ursachen sich bei einer schlechthin nothwendigen Ursache endigen muß, ohne welche sie keine Vollständigkeit haben würde.\"] empirical ground of proof cannot teach, but there reason takes its leave of it entirely and inquires behind mere concepts: namely, what properties an absolutely necessary being in general must have, i.e., which among all possible things contains in itself the requisite conditions (*requisita*) for an absolute necessity. Now reason believes it encounters these requisites solely and alone in the concept of a most real being, and then infers: that is the absolutely necessary being. But it is clear that one here presupposes that the concept of a being of the highest reality does full justice to the concept of absolute necessity in existence, i.e., that from the former the latter can be inferred; a proposition that the ontological argument of the [text broken: \"f hauptete\"] asserted, which one therefore accepts and lays at the ground in the cosmological proof, although one had wanted to avoid it. For absolute |f necessity is an existence from mere concepts. If I now say: the | concept of the *ens realissimum* is such a concept and indeed the only one that is suitable to necessary existence and adequate to it, then I must also concede that the latter can be inferred from it. Thus it is really only the ontological proof from mere concepts that contains all the force of proof in the so-called cosmological one; and the alleged experience is entirely idle, perhaps only to lead us to the concept of absolute necessity, but not to establish this in any determinate thing. For as soon as we have this as our aim, we must at once leave all experience and seek among pure concepts which of them might contain the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if in such a way only the possibility of such a being is seen, then its existence is also established; for it means as much as: among everything possible there is one that carries absolute necessity with it, i.e., this being exists absolutely necessarily.",[]],"krv-b-1811":["All delusions in inferring are most easily discovered when one places them before the eyes in a school-correct way. Here is such a presentation. |",[]],"krv-b-1812":["If the proposition is correct: every absolutely necessary being is at the same time the most real being (which is the *nervus probandi* of the cosmological proof), then it must, like all affirmative judgments, be convertible at least *per accidens*; therefore: some most real beings are at the same time absolutely necessary beings. Now, however, one *ens realissimum* is distinguished from another in no respect, and what therefore holds of some contained under this concept holds also of all. Consequently I will be able (in this case) to convert it also *simpliciter*, i.e., every most real being is a necessary being. Since now this proposition is determined merely from its concepts *a priori*, the mere concept of the most real being must also carry with it the absolute necessity of the same [sic: text breaks at line end: \"Nothwendig⸗\" | continues in next passage]",[]],"krv-b-1813":["[continuation:] ity of it; which is just what the ontological proof asserted and the cosmological would not acknowledge, but nevertheless, though in a concealed way, laid beneath its inferences. 6 Thus the second way that speculative reason takes, to prove the existence of the highest being, is not only equally deceptive with the first, but has this further blameworthy feature, that it commits an *ignoratio elenchi*, in that it promises to lead us on a new footpath, but after a small detour brings us back again to the old one, which we had left on its account.",[]],"krv-b-1814":["I said shortly before that in this cosmological argument a whole nest of dialectical presumptions keeps itself hidden, which transcendental critique can easily discover and destroy. I will now only cite them and leave it to the already practiced reader to investigate the deceptive principles further and to cancel them.",[]],"krv-b-1815":["There is found there, e.g., 1) the transcendental principle of inferring from the",[]],"krv-b-1816":["[continuation: \"Zu-\" lost in the break; text begins \"fälligen\"] contingent to a cause, which has significance only in the world of sense, but outside it does not even have a sense. For the merely intellectual concept of the contingent cannot produce any synthetic proposition, such as that of causality, and the principle of the latter has no significance and no mark of its use except only in the world of sense; but here it was supposed to serve precisely to get beyond the world of sense. 2) The principle of inferring from the impossibility of an infinite series of causes given one above another in the world of sense to a first cause, to which the principles of the use of reason entitle us not even in experience, much less can they extend this principle beyond it (whither this chain cannot be extended at all). 3) The false self-satisfaction of reason with regard to the completion of this series, in that one finally removes every condition, without which still no concept of a necessity can take place, and, since one then can comprehend nothing further, takes this for a completion of one's concept. 4) The confusion of the logical possibility of a concept of all united reality (without inner contradiction) with the transcendental one, which requires a principle of the feasibility of such a synthesis, which however can in turn go only to the field of possible experiences, and so forth.",["- krv-b-1810: the OCR text is corrupt: the sentence \"gesetz der Causalität … Vollständigkeit haben würde\" is a misplaced footnote-like insertion interrupting \"Was dieses für Eigenschaften habe, kann der … empirische Beweisgrund nicht lehren\"; I translated it bracketed in place, not integrated. Also \"des f hauptete\" is broken (probably \"des [Cartesius] behauptete\"/\"…be-hauptete\"); not filled. \"|f\" kept as in source.","- krv-b-1810: *vernünftelnd* = \"rationalizing\"; *Schein* = \"illusion\"; *Grundsatz* = \"principle\"; *Dasein* = \"existence\"; *Begriff* = \"concept\"; *Wesen* = \"being\"; *Bedingung* = \"condition\"; *Eigenschaften* = \"properties\".","- krv-b-1810: \"Prüfung… Argument\" etc. literal; \"a priori,\" \"ens realissimi/-um,\" *requisita* in Latin as in source; \"durchgängig bestimmt\" = \"thoroughly determined\".","- krv-b-1810 opening is cut off mid-sentence (\"eine einzige Art\"), translated as it stands.","- krv-b-1812: \"alle n\" and \"schlechth in\" are OCR spacing slips, silently corrected to \"allen\"/\"schlechthin\"; the bracketed remark in the translation about the break is editorial, the passage ends mid-word \"Nothwendig⸗\".","- krv-b-1813: the opening is the continuation of \"Nothwendig-keit\"; the digit \"6\" is an OCR remnant (footnote/line mark) left in place; \"zum\" before \"das Dasein\" is OCR garble, left out of the translation. *Fußsteig* = \"footpath\".","- krv-b-1816: begins mid-word \"fälligen\" (from \"Zufälligen\", 1815 ends \"vom\"); OCR slips \"on Bedeutung\"→\"von Bedeutung\", \"m über\"→\"um über\" silently corrected. *Bedeutung* = \"significance\"; *Sinn* = \"sense\"; *Sinnenwelt* = \"world of sense\"; *begreifen* = \"comprehend\"; *Merkmal* = \"mark\"; *Zufälligen* = \"contingent\"; *Thunlichkeit* = \"feasibility\"."]],"krv-b-1817":["The artifice of the cosmological proof aims merely at evading the proof of the existence of a necessary being *a priori* through mere concepts, which would have to be conducted ontologically, but for which we feel ourselves wholly incapable. With this aim we infer, from an actual existence laid at the basis (an experience in general), as well as can be done, to some absolutely necessary condition of it. We then have no need to explain the possibility of this. For if it is proved that it exists, the question concerning its possibility is entirely unnecessary. If we now want to determine this necessary being more closely in accordance with its property, then we do not seek that which is sufficient to comprehend the necessity of existence from its concept; for if we could do this, we would have no need of any empirical presupposition; no, we seek only the negative condition (*conditio sine qua non*), without which a being would not be absolutely necessary. Now this would be quite feasible in all other kinds of inferences from a given sequel to its ground; but it happens here unfortunately that the condition one demands for absolute necessity can be encountered only in a single being, which therefore would have to contain in its concept everything that is required for absolute necessity, and thus makes possible an inference *a priori* to it; i.e., I would also have to be able to infer conversely: to whichever thing this concept (of the highest reality) pertains, that is absolutely necessary; and if I cannot infer thus (as I must indeed admit if I want to avoid the ontological proof), then I have also come to grief on my new path and find myself again where I started from. The concept of the highest being satisfies indeed all questions *a priori* that can be raised concerning the inner determinations of a thing, and is for that reason also an ideal without equal, because the general concept at the same time distinguishes it as an individual among all possible things. But it satisfies not at all the question concerning its own existence, which after all was really all that was at issue, and to the inquiry of one who assumed the existence of a necessary being and only wanted to know which among all things must be regarded as that, one could not answer: This here is the necessary being.",["- Dasein = existence (Existenz is also \"existence\"; not occurring in this passage). Wesen = being; Beschaffenheit = property; Folge = sequel; Grund = ground; Bestimmungen = determinations.","- schlechterdings (here) and schlechthin (elsewhere in this batch) are both rendered \"absolutely\".","- \"als warum es doch eigentlich nur zu thun war\": the \"als\" is rendered loosely as \"which ... was really all that was at issue\"; the German construction is elliptical.","- \"dieser ihre Möglichkeit\": \"the possibility of this\", the pronoun refers to the condition (Bedingung).","- OCR slips corrected silently (Noth-wendigkeit, ab-solut, Des-stimmungen, stray line numbers and letters)."]],"krv-b-1818":["It may well be permitted to assume the existence of a being of the highest sufficiency as cause of all possible effects,",["- Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency; Wirkungen = effects. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1819":["in order to facilitate for reason the unity of the grounds of explanation, which it seeks, to",["- Erklärungsgründe = grounds of explanation; Einheit = unity. The sentence is cut off; the verb \"erleichtern\" stands in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1820":["facilitate. But to take so much upon oneself that one even says: such a",["- \"erleichtern\" completes the previous passage's \"um ... zu\"; \"herauszunehmen\" rendered \"to take upon oneself\". The sentence breaks off."]],"krv-b-1821":["being exists necessarily, is no longer the modest",["- Fragment of a sentence, continued in the next passage. \"existirt\" = exists (Latinate, same English as Dasein)."]],"krv-b-1822":["utterance of a permitted hypothesis, but the brazen presumption of an apodictic certainty; for that which one pretends to cognize as absolutely necessary, the cognition of this must also carry absolute necessity with it.",["- erkennen = cognize; Erkenntniß = cognition; schlechthin = absolutely. \"davon muß auch die Erkenntniß ...\": \"of which\" is rendered as in the German's word order."]],"krv-b-1823":["The whole problem of the transcendental ideal comes down to this: either to find for absolute necessity a concept, or for the concept of any thing the absolute necessity of it to",["- Aufgabe = problem. The sentence breaks off; \"finden\" opens the next passage."]],"krv-b-1824":["find. If one can do the one, one must also be able to do the other; for reason cognizes as absolutely necessary only that which is necessary from its concept. But both wholly exceed all the utmost endeavors to satisfy our understanding on this point, but also all attempts to calm it on account of this its incapacity. Unconditioned necessity, which we need so indispensably as the ultimate bearer of all things, is the true abyss for human reason. Even eternity, however terribly sublime a Haller may depict it, by far does not make the dizzying impression on the mind; for it only measures the duration of things, but does not bear them. One cannot ward off the thought, but also cannot bear it, that a being which we represent to ourselves as also the highest among all possible, as it were says to itself: I am from eternity to eternity, outside me is nothing except what is something merely through my will; but whence then am I? Here everything sinks beneath us, and the greatest perfection, like the smallest, hovers without support merely before speculative reason, to which it costs nothing to let the one as well as the other vanish without the least hindrance.",["- \"Here everything sinks beneath us\": the OCR reads \"fluit\"; I have read \"sinkt\", which is doubtful and should be checked against the original.","- \"macht ... den schwindelichten Eindruck nicht auf das Gemüth\": the OCR scatters \"lange\" and \"it\"; rendered \"by far does not make ... on the mind\". Gemüth = mind; Eindruck = impression.","- \"Haller\" is the poet Albrecht von Haller. Vollkommenheit = perfection; Vernunft = reason; Verstand = understanding.","- \"menschliche\" restored from OCR \"mensch— liebe\"."]],"krv-b-1825":["Many forces of nature, which manifest their existence through certain effects, remain inscrutable to us; for we cannot trace them far enough through observation. The transcendental object lying at the ground of appearances, and with it the ground why our sensibility has these rather than other supreme conditions, are and remain inscrutable for us, although the thing itself is otherwise given, but only not insighted. An ideal of pure reason, however, cannot be called inscrutable, because it has no further credential of its reality to show than the need of reason to complete all synthetic unity by means of it. Since it is thus not even given as a thinkable object, it is also not inscrutable as such; rather, as a mere idea it must find its seat and its resolution in the nature of reason, and so must be able to be investigated; for reason consists precisely in this, that we can give an account of all our concepts, opinions and assertions, whether from objective grounds or, if they are mere illusion, from subjective grounds.",["- Object = object (Gegenstand also \"object\", here \"denkbarer Gegenstand\"). Erscheinungen = appearances; Schein = illusion; Behauptungen = assertions; Meinungen = opinions.","- \"nicht eingesehen ist\": \"not insighted\" is a literalism for the participle of einsehen; \"seen into\" or \"understood\" would be other options.","- unerforschlich = inscrutable; \"Auflösung\" = resolution; \"Beglaubigung\" = credential."]],"krv-b-1826":["Discovery and Explanation of the Dialectical Illusion",["- Entdeckung = discovery; Erklärung = explanation; Schein = illusion. The heading continues in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-1827":["in all Transcendental Proofs of the Existence of a",["- Beweisen = proofs; Dasein = existence. Heading fragment."]],"krv-b-1828":["Necessary Being.",["- Nothwendigen Wesens = necessary being. End of the heading."]],"krv-b-1829":["Both proofs conducted so far were attempted transcendentally, i.e., independently of empirical principles. For although the cosmological one lays an experience in general at the ground, it is nevertheless not conducted from any particular property of the same, but from pure principles of reason in relation to an existence given through empirical consciousness in general, and even abandons this guidance in order to support itself on pure concepts alone. What now is, in these transcendental proofs, the cause of the dialectical, but natural, illusion, which connects the concepts of necessity and highest reality and realizes and hypostatizes that which can nevertheless only be an idea? What is the cause of the unavoidability of assuming something as necessary in itself among existing things and yet at the same time of recoiling from the existence of such a being as from an abyss; and how does one go about it that reason may understand itself about this and attain, from the wavering state of a timid assent ever again withdrawn, to calm insight?",["- Existenz = existence (given Existenz / existirenden Dingen) and Dasein = existence: one English word for both. Bewußtsein = consciousness; Beschaffenheit = property; Realität = reality; verknüpfen = connect.","- \"an sich\" = in itself; \"Beifall\" = assent; \"Einsicht\" = insight; \"Zustand\" = state; verstehen = understand.","- \"sich auf lauter reine Begriffe zu stützen\": \"pure concepts alone\" for \"lauter reine\".","- \"zurückzubeben\" restored from OCR \"zurüdzubeben\"; \"Principien\" = principles.","- \"hierüber\" is left as \"about this\", the German being unspecific."]],"krv-b-1830":["It is something exceedingly remarkable that, if one presupposes that something exists, one cannot avoid the consequence that something also exists necessarily. The cosmological argument rested on this quite natural (although for that reason not yet secure) inference. On the other hand, whatever concept of a thing I may assume, I find that its existence can never be represented by me as absolutely necessary, and that nothing hinders me, let exist whatever may, from thinking the non-being of it; hence I must indeed assume something necessary for what exists in general, yet cannot think any single thing itself as necessary in itself. That is: I can never complete the regress to the conditions of existing without assuming a necessary being; I can, however, never begin from it. 1. If I must think something necessary for existing things in general, but am not entitled to think any thing in itself as necessary, it follows unavoidably from this that necessity and contingency must not concern and affect the things themselves, because otherwise a contradiction would occur; hence neither of these two principles can be objective, but they can at most be only subjective principles of reason, namely, on the one hand, to seek for everything that is given as existing something that is necessary, i.e., never to stop anywhere other than at an explanation completed a priori, but on the other hand also never to hope for this completion, i.e., to assume nothing empirical as unconditioned, and thereby to exempt oneself from further derivation. In such a significance both principles, as merely heuristic and regulative, which attend to nothing but the formal interest of reason, can quite well subsist alongside one another. For the one says: you are to philosophize about nature as if there were a necessary first ground for everything that belongs to existence, solely in order to bring systematic unity into your cognition, in that you pursue such an idea, namely an imagined supreme ground; but the other warns you to assume no single determination that concerns the existence of things as such a supreme ground, | i.e., as absolutely necessary, but rather to keep the path to further derivation still always open to yourselves and therefore always still to treat it as conditioned. But if everything that is perceived in things must be considered by us as conditionally necessary, then no thing (that may be given empirically) can be regarded as absolutely necessary. |",[]],"krv-b-1831":["But it follows from this that you must assume the absolutely necessary outside the world: because it is supposed to serve only as a principle of the greatest possible unity of the appearances, as their supreme ground, and you can never get there in the world, because the second rule commands you always to regard all empirical causes of unity as derived.",[]],"krv-b-1832":["The philosophers of antiquity regarded all form of nature as contingent, but matter, according to the judgment of common reason, as original and necessary. But had they considered matter not respectively as the substratum of appearances, but in itself according to its existence, the idea of absolute necessity would at once have vanished. For there is nothing that binds reason to this existence absolutely, but it can always and without conflict cancel it in thought; in thought, however, alone lay also the absolute necessity. Thus some regulative principle must have lain at the basis of this persuasion. In fact extension and impenetrability (which together make up the concept of matter) are also the supreme empirical *principium* of the unity of the appearances and, insofar as it is empirically unconditioned, have a property of the regulative principle in itself. Nevertheless, since every determination of matter that makes up the real of it, hence also impenetrability, is an effect (action) that must have its cause and is therefore always still derived, matter does not suit the idea of a necessary being as a principle of all derived unity: because each of its real properties, as derived, is only conditionally necessary and thus can be cancelled in itself, but with this the whole existence of matter would be cancelled, though if this did not happen, we would have reached the highest ground of unity empirically, which is forbidden by the second regulative principle. Thus it follows that matter and in general whatever belongs to the world is not suitable to the idea of a necessary original being as a mere principle of the greatest empirical unity, but that it must be posited outside the world; whereupon we can always confidently derive the appearances of the world and their existence from others, as if there were no necessary being, and yet can strive unceasingly toward the completeness of the derivation, as if such a being were presupposed as a supreme [ground]. |",[]],"krv-b-1833":["After these considerations the ideal of the highest being is nothing other than a regulative principle of reason, to regard all combination in the world as if it arose from an all-sufficient necessary cause, in order to ground upon it the rule of a systematic unity, necessary in accordance with universal laws, in the explanation of it, and is not an assertion of an existence necessary in itself. But it is at the same time unavoidable, by means of a transcendental subreption, to represent this formal principle to oneself as constitutive and to think this unity hypostatically. For just as space, because it makes originally possible all shapes, which are merely different limitations of it, although it is only a principle of sensibility, is nevertheless for just that reason taken for something absolutely necessary subsisting for itself and for an object given a priori in itself: so it also goes quite naturally, since the systematic unity of nature can be set up as a principle of the empirical use of our reason in no way other than insofar as we lay the idea of a most real being as the supreme cause at the basis, that this idea is thereby represented as an actual object and this in turn, because it is the supreme condition, as necessary, hence a regulative principle is transformed into a constitutive one; which substitution reveals itself in that, if I now consider this supreme being, which relative to the world was absolutely (unconditionally) necessary, as a thing for itself, this necessity is not capable of any concept and thus must have been to be encountered in my reason only as a formal condition of thinking, but not as a material and hypostatic condition of existence.",[]],"krv-b-1834":["Of the third chapter\nSixth section.",[]],"krv-b-1835":["On the impossibility of the physicotheological proof.",[]],"krv-b-1836":["If, then, neither the concept of things in general nor the experience of any existence in general can accomplish what is demanded, one means still remains: to try whether a determinate experience, hence that of the things of the present world, their constitution and arrangement, may not furnish a ground of proof that could help us to the conviction of the existence of a highest being. Such a proof we would call the physicotheological. Should this too be impossible, then no satisfying proof from merely speculative reason is possible at all for the existence of a being that would correspond to our transcendental idea. |",["- krv-b-1830: OCR slips silently corrected (eriftirenden→existirenden, Ar- f gument, etc.). \"Folgerung\" = consequence; \"Schlusse\" = inference; \"Dasein\" and \"Existenz/existiren\" both rendered \"existence/exist.\"","- krv-b-1830: \"Umgang haben\" (= Umgang nehmen, avoid) rendered \"avoid\"; \"Grund\"=ground, \"Grundsatz\"=principle, \"Princip\" also \"principle.\"","- krv-b-1830: \"Bedeutung\" = significance (glossary). \"Erklärung\" = explanation. \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- krv-b-1831: \"Princip\" and \"Grundsatz\" both \"principle\"; \"abgeleitet\" = derived.","- krv-b-1832: \"Urtheile\" = judgment; \"Überredung\" = persuasion; \"respectiv\" kept as \"respectively\" (Kant's Latinate adverb, = in relation to). \"Principium\" left in Latin italics. \"Oasein\" read as Dasein.","- krv-b-1832: Final sentence breaks off with \"als ein oberster A | vorausgesetzt\" — the noun after \"oberster\" is lost in OCR (presumably \"Grund\"); I supply \"[ground]\" in brackets as a flagged conjecture, not in the text.","- krv-b-1833: \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Subreption\" = subreption; \"Unterschiebung\" = substitution; \"zu Grunde legen\" = lay at the basis. \"wirklich\" = actual. \"Ding für sich\" rendered \"thing for itself\" as in the German; \"an sich\" = in itself.","- krv-b-1833: \"muß… anzutreffen gewesen sein\" kept awkward (\"must have been to be encountered\"). \"Ideal\" and \"allerrealsten\" kept close (\"most real\"). Text \"male-I riale\" read as materiale.","- krv-b-1836: \"Beweisgrund\" = ground of proof; \"Beschaffenheit\" = constitution; \"Überzeugung\" = conviction; \"Mittel\" = means. Garbled \"fher\" and \"genugthuen⸗ | der\" ignored/read as genugthuender (satisfying)."]],"krv-b-1837":["After all the above remarks one will soon see that the answer to this inquiry can be expected quite easily and concisely. For how can an experience ever be given that is to be adequate to an idea? Precisely in this consists the peculiarity of the latter, that no experience can ever be congruent with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary all-sufficient original being is so overflowingly great, so highly elevated above everything empirical, which is at all times conditioned, that one can partly never procure enough material in experience to fill such a concept, partly always gropes around among the conditioned and will constantly seek in vain for the unconditioned, of which no law of any empirical synthesis gives us an example or the slightest guidance toward it.",["- Idee (OCR \"Idee“\"): the stray quotation mark is an OCR artifact and is dropped; a stray \"f\" at the end is likewise dropped.","- Stoff = material; Begriff = concept; Gesetz = law; Erfahrung = experience; bedingt/Bedingte = conditioned; Unbedingte = unconditioned.","- Urwesen = \"original being\"; Wesen = \"being\" throughout.","- \"congruiren\" = \"be congruent\" (Latinate verb; no other German word is rendered the same).","- \"dazu\" in the final clause is left attached as in the German (\"toward it\" may refer to the unconditioned or to the example); the construction is ambiguous."]],"krv-b-1838":["Were the highest being to stand in this chain of conditions, it would itself be a member of their series and, just like the lower members, to which it is prefixed, would require still further investigation on account of its still higher ground. If, on the other hand, one wants to separate it from this chain and not to comprehend it along in the series of natural causes as a merely intelligible being: what bridge can reason then build in order to arrive at it, since all laws of the transition from effects to causes, indeed all synthesis and extension of our cognition in general, are placed on nothing other than possible experience, hence merely on objects of the sensible world, and can have a meaning only with regard to them?",["- \"Wurde\" is OCR for \"Würde\" (\"would\").","- \"Über ganges\" and \"Erweiterung!\" are OCR slips, corrected to \"Überganges\" and \"Erweiterung\".","- mitbegreifen = \"comprehend along\" (begreifen = comprehend, comprehendere).","- Bedeutung = \"meaning\" (glossary alternative to \"significance\").","- Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Sinnenwelt = \"sensible world\"; Grund = \"ground\".","- \"ihrer\" (last clause) and \"denen es vorgesetzt ist\" are kept literal; \"vorgesetzt\" = \"prefixed\" (could also be read as \"placed before\")."]],"krv-b-1839":["The present world opens up to us such an immeasurable stage of manifoldness, order, purposiveness and beauty, whether one may now pursue these in the infinity of space or in the unbounded division of it, that even after the acquaintance that our weak understanding has been able to acquire of it, all language loses its emphasis over so many and unforeseeably great wonders, all numbers their power to measure, and even our thoughts all boundary, so that our judgment of the whole must dissolve into a speechless, but all the more eloquent, astonishment. Everywhere we see a chain of effects and causes, of ends and the means, regularity in coming-to-be or passing away; and since nothing has entered of itself into the state in which it finds itself, it always points further to another thing as its cause, which makes precisely the same further inquiry",["- \"Schau: platz\" is an OCR break, read as \"Schauplatz\" (\"stage\").","- Kenntnissen = \"acquaintance\" (glossary: Kenntnis); Verstand = \"understanding\"; Urtheil = \"judgment\"; Zahl = \"number\"; Kraft = \"power\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Mittel = \"means\"; Zustand = \"state\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Raum = \"space\"; Theilung = \"division\".","- Zweckmäßigkeit = \"purposiveness\"; Begrenzung = \"boundary\" (Grenze = bound(ary)).","- \"wirkung/Ursache\" order kept as in German (\"effects and causes\").","- \"darin es sich befindet\" retains the semicolon misprint of the OCR (\"getreten ist; darin\") rendered without the semicolon; the German \"von selbst\" is \"of itself.\"","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; \"Nachfrage\" = \"inquiry.\""]],"krv-b-1840":["necessary, so that in such a way the whole All would have to sink into the abyss of nothing, did one not assume something that, outside this infinite contingent, existing originally and independently for itself, held it up and, as the cause of its origin, at the same time secured its continuation for it. This highest cause (with regard to all things of the world), how great should one think it? The world we do not know according to its whole content, still less do we know how to estimate its magnitude through comparison with everything that is possible. What hin-",["- Zufällige = \"contingent\" (n.); Größe = \"magnitude\"; Inhalt = \"content\"; möglich = \"possible\"; Ursache = \"cause.\"","- \"das ganze All\": \"All\" kept capitalized as in the German.","- \"dasselbe hielte\" and \"ihm\" are kept as pronouns; \"dasselbe\" could refer to \"das Zufällige\" or \"das All\".","- Passage breaks off mid-word (\"hin-\" continues as \"hindert\" in the next passage); not completed."]],"krv-b-1841":["dert us, however, that, since we once need in regard to causality an extreme",["- Passage begins with the completion of \"hin-/dert\" (\"What hinders\"); the hyphenated word is joined across passages: \"hindert\" = \"hinders.\"","- Absicht = \"regard\" here (\"in Absicht auf\" = \"in regard to\"); glossary has \"aim; intention\" for the noun elsewhere.","- Causalität = \"causality.\"","- Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1842":["and supreme being, we should not at the same time posit it, according to the degree of perfec-",["- \"Voll-/kommenheit\" joined as Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\"; Grad = \"degree\"; setzen = \"posit.\"","- Breaks off mid-word and mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1843":["tion, above everything else possible? which",["- \"perfec-/tion\" completes Vollkommenheit.","- \"mögliche\" = \"possible.\"","- Fragment; \"welches\" is left as a bare relative pronoun."]],"krv-b-1844":["we can easily accomplish, although to be sure only through the delicate outline of an abstract concept, if we represent to ourselves in it, as a single",["- vorstellen (in the next passage) = \"represent\"; \"sich … vorstellen\" = \"represent to oneself.\"","- \"einigen\" = \"single\" (einig, not einzig).","- \"bewerkstelligen\" = \"accomplish\"; Umriß = \"outline\"; Begriff = \"concept.\"","- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1845":["substance all possible perfection united; which concept is favorable to the demand of our reason in the economy of principles, subject in itself to no contradictions, and even conducive to the extension of the use of reason in the midst of experience through the guidance that such an idea gives toward order and purposiveness, but nowhere opposed in a decisive way to an experience. This proof deserves always to be named with respect. It is the oldest, clearest and most suited to common human reason. It enlivens the study of nature, just as it itself has its existence from this and thereby always receives new force. It brings ends and intentions to where our observation would not have discovered them of itself, and extends our knowledge of nature by the guiding thread of a special unity, whose principle is outside nature. This knowledge, however, in turn acts back on its cause, namely the occasioning idea, and increases the belief in a highest author up to an irresistible conviction. It would therefore be not only disconsolate but also entirely in vain to want to withdraw anything from the authority of this proof. Reason, which is unceasingly elevated by such powerful grounds of proof, growing ever under its hands though only empirical, can be so depressed by no doubts of subtle, abstracted speculation that it should not be torn out of every brooding irresolution, as out of a dream, by a glance that it casts on the wonders of nature and the majesty of the world-structure, in order to elevate itself from magnitude to magnitude up to the all-highest, from the conditioned to the condition up to the supreme and unconditioned author.",["- OCR: \"zu— wider\" = \"zuwider\"; \"I\", \"l\", \"4\", \"si\", \"Deiner\" (= \"einer\") are OCR debris, dropped/corrected; the sign | before \"angemessen\" is carried over.","- Vorstellen = \"represent\"; Vernunft = \"reason\"; Widerspruch = \"contradiction\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Idee = \"idea\"; Beweis = \"proof\"; Beweisgrund = \"ground of proof\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Absicht = \"intention\"; Beobachtung = \"observation\"; Kenntnisse = \"knowledge\" (glossary: Kenntnis); Einheit = \"unity\"; Glaube = \"belief\"; Überzeugung = \"conviction\"; Bedingte/Bedingung = \"conditioned/condition.\"","- Naturkenntnisse and \"Diese Kenntnisse\" both rendered \"knowledge\"; Größe = \"magnitude.\"","- The bar \"|\" in the German is not reproduced in the English text above; it falls between \"am meisten\" and \"angemessen\" (\"most | suited\").","- \"Weltbaues\" = \"world-structure\"; \"Urheber\" = \"author.\"","- \"Zuträglich\" = \"conducive.\""]],"krv-b-1846":["Although we have nothing to object against the rationality and utility of this procedure, but rather have to recommend and encourage it, we can nevertheless not approve the claims that this kind of proof would make to apodictic certainty and to an assent needing no favor or foreign support; and it can by no means harm the good cause to tune down the dogmatic language of a scornfully speaking rationalizer to the tone of the moderation and modesty of a belief sufficient for tranquillization, although not precisely commanding unconditioned submission. I assert accordingly that the physico-theological proof can never demonstrate the existence of a highest being alone, but must always leave it to the ontological (to which it serves only as an introduction) to supplement this deficiency, hence that this still always contains the only possible ground of proof (provided only that a speculative proof takes place at all) that no human reason can pass by. |",["- \"nicht“\" and \"speculativer “ Beweis\": OCR quotation marks dropped; the final | is carried over.","- Vernünftler = \"rationalizer\" (vernünfteln = rationalize); Vernunftmäßigkeit = \"rationality.\"","- Behaupten = \"assert\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Beweisart = \"kind of proof\"; Beweisgrund = \"ground of proof.\"","- \"dieser\" (after \"mithin\") could refer to the ontological proof; \"mithin dieser immer noch…\" is kept literal (\"this\").","- \"Art\" in \"Beweisart\" = \"kind.\""]],"krv-b-1847":["The principal moments of the said physico-theological proof are the following: 1) In the world there are found everywhere distinct signs of an arrangement according to a determinate intention, carried out with great wisdom and in a whole of indescribable manifoldness of content as well as unbounded magnitude of domain. 2) This purposive arrangement is entirely foreign to the things of the world and attaches to them only contingently, i.e., the nature of various things could not of itself have harmonized, through so many kinds of means uniting themselves, to determinate final aims, had they not been quite properly chosen and arranged for it by an arranging rational principle in accordance with underlying ideas. 3) There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which must be the cause of the world not merely as blindly acting all-powerful nature through fruitfulness, but as intelligence through freedom. 4) The unity of it can be concluded from the unity of the reciprocal relation of the parts of the world, as members of an artificial edifice, in that to which our observation reaches, with certainty, but further on, according to all principles of analogy, with probability.",["- OCR: \"f\" after \"sind\", \"Is\" before \"vielerlei\" are debris and dropped; \"nach zum Grunde\" kept literal.","- Absicht = \"intention\"; Endabsichten = \"final aims\"; Zweck-mäßig = \"purposive\"; Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifoldness\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Inhalt = \"content\"; Grund = \"ground\" (\"zum Grunde liegenden\" = \"underlying\").","- Zufällig = \"contingently\"; Mittel = \"means\"; Princip = \"principle\"; Grundsätze = \"principles.\"","- \"derselben\" in point 4 (\"The unity of it\") could refer to the cause or to the several causes (\"oder mehrere\"); ambiguity retained.","- \"mit Gewißheit … mit Wahrscheinlichkeit schließen\": \"conclude\" (schließen) with the infinitive at the end, as in the German; Wahrscheinlichkeit = \"probability.\"","- Momente = \"moments\"; \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\""]],"krv-b-1848":["Without here quibbling with natural reason about its inference, since from the analogy of some natural products with that which human art brings forth, when it does violence to nature and constrains it not to proceed according to its own ends but to accommodate itself to ours, (from the similarity of those products with houses, ships, clocks) it infers that just such a causality, namely understanding and will, will lie at the ground of it, when it derives the inner possibility of freely-acting nature (which first makes possible all art and perhaps even reason itself) still from another, though superhuman art, which kind of inference perhaps the sharpest transcendental critique would not withstand: one must nevertheless concede that, if we are once to name a cause, we cannot here proceed more securely than according to the analogy with such purposive generations, which are the only ones of which the causes and mode of effect are fully known to us. Reason would not be able to answer for it to itself if it wanted to pass from the causality that it knows to dark and unprovable grounds of explanation that it does not know.",["- Schluß = \"inference\" (glossary); Schlußart = \"kind of inference\". Zwecke = \"ends\"; Grunde liegen = \"lie at the ground\".","- OCR corrected: \"zu— erst\" = zuerst; \"Erflärungsgründe\" = Erklärungsgründe; \"Ahnlichkeit\" = Ähnlichkeit.","- \"Wirkungsart\" rendered \"mode of effect\" (Wirkung = effect). \"chicaniren\" rendered \"quibbling\".","- \"wenn sie ... ableitet\" and the antecedents of the several \"sie\" are left as ambiguous as the German (reason / art / nature).","- \"bei ihr\" (nature or reason?) left as \"of it\", ambiguous as in German."]],"krv-b-1849":["According to this inference, the purposiveness and well-suitedness of so many arrangements of nature would prove only the contingency of the form, but not of the matter, i.e., of the substance in the world; for in regard to the latter it would further be required that it could be proved that the things of the world would in themselves be unfit for such an order and harmony in accordance with universal laws if they were not, even according to their substance, the product of a highest wisdom; for which, however, entirely different grounds of proof than those from the analogy with human art would be required. The proof could therefore at most demonstrate an architect of the world, who would always be very limited by the fitness of the material on which he works, but not a creator of the world, to whose idea everything is subject, which is by far not sufficient for the great aim one has before one's eyes, namely to prove an all-sufficient original being. If we wanted to prove the contingency of matter itself, we would have to take refuge in a transcendental argument, which, however, was precisely what was to be avoided here.",["- Zufälligkeit = \"contingency\" (zufällig = contingent); Materie = \"matter\", Stoff = \"material\"; Substanz = \"substance\".","- Beweis = \"proof\"; Beweisgründe = \"grounds of proof\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Urwesen = \"original being\"; Wesen = \"being\".","- OCR corrected: \"letz— teren\", \"Sub— stanz\", \"un— sere\".","- \"Zweckmäßigkeit\" = \"purposiveness\"; \"Wohlgereimtheit\" = \"well-suitedness\" (not in glossary)."]],"krv-b-1850":["The inference thus proceeds from the order and purposiveness to be observed so thoroughly in the world, as an entirely contingent arrangement, to the existence of a cause proportioned to it. The concept of this cause, however, must give us something quite determinate about it to cognize, and it can therefore be none other than that of a being that possesses all might, wisdom, etc., in a word all perfection, as an all-sufficient being. For the predicates of very great, of astonishing, of immeasurable might and excellence give no determinate concept at all and do not really say what the thing is in itself, but are only representations of relation of the magnitude of the object, which the observer (of the world) compares with himself and his power of comprehension, and which turn out equally laudatory whether one magnifies the object or makes the observing subject smaller in relation to it. Where it matters for the magnitude (of the perfection) of a thing in general, there is no determinate concept except that which comprehends the whole possible perfection, and only the All (*omnitudo*) of reality is thoroughly determined in the concept.",["- Dasein = \"existence\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; bestimmt = \"determinate\"/\"determined\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Realität = \"reality\".","- OCR corrected: \"Be N griff\" = Begriff; \"íi\" deleted; \"einen\" kept as printed for \"einem\".","- \"Verhältnißvorstellungen\" = \"representations of relation\"; \"Fassungskraft\" = \"power of comprehension\" (not glossary; cf. begreifen = comprehend).","- \"Macht\" = \"might\" (Kraft = force/power in glossary). \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\".","- \"begreift\" in the last sentence rendered \"comprehends\" (begreifen)."]],"krv-b-1851":["Now I do not hope that anyone should venture to have insight into the relation of the magnitude of the world he has observed (in domain as well as content) to omnipotence, of the order of the world to the highest wisdom, of the unity of the world to the absolute unity of the author, etc. Physico-theology therefore cannot give a determinate concept of the supreme cause of the world, and hence cannot be sufficient for a principle of theology, which in turn is supposed to constitute the foundation of religion.",["- OCR corrected: \"xc.\" = etc.; \"Afo\" = Also.","- Umfang = \"domain\"; Inhalt = \"content\"; Princip = \"principle\"; Urheber = \"author\"; Welteinheit = \"unity of the world\".","- \"hinreichend\" = \"sufficient\" (zureichend also \"sufficient\" in glossary)."]],"krv-b-1852":["The step to absolute totality is entirely and utterly impossible by the empirical path. Now one nevertheless takes it in the physico-theological proof. What means, then, does one make use of to get across so wide a chasm? |",["- Totalität = \"totality\" (Allheit is also \"totality\" in the glossary). Mittel = \"means\".","- OCR: \"phyfischtheolo-gischen\" = physischtheologischen. The sign | is carried over as printed."]],"krv-b-1853":["After one has arrived at admiration of the greatness of the wisdom, the might, etc., of the author of the world and cannot get any further, one suddenly abandons this argument conducted through empirical grounds of proof and goes to the contingency of the world, inferred right at the beginning from its order and purposiveness. From this contingency alone one now goes, merely through transcendental concepts, to the existence of something absolutely necessary, and from the concept of the absolute necessity of the first cause to the thoroughly determined or determining concept of it, namely of an all-encompassing reality. Thus the physico-theological proof got stuck in its undertaking, in this embarrassment leapt suddenly over to the cosmological proof, and since this is only a concealed ontological proof, it really carried out its aim merely through pure reason, although it",["- Zufälligkeit = \"contingency\"; Schlechthinnothwendiges = \"something absolutely necessary\"; Realität = \"reality\"; Absicht = \"aim\".","- OCR: \"i\" before \"Argument\" deleted. \"Verlegenheit\" = \"embarrassment\".","- \"bestimmten oder bestimmenden\" = \"determined or determining\"; the passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"ob er gleich\"), continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1854":["initially denied all kinship with this and had staked everything on evident proofs from experience.",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence; \"dieser\" = \"this\" (referent left ambiguous as in German, though grammatically feminine: pure reason).","- OCR: \"ein— leuchtende\" = einleuchtende. \"ausgesetzt\" rendered \"staked\"."]],"krv-b-1855":["The physico-theologians therefore have no cause at all to behave so prudishly toward the transcendental kind of proof and to look down upon it with the self-conceit",["- Fragment breaks off mid-sentence, continued in the next passage.","- Beweisart = \"kind of proof\"; Eigendünkel = \"self-conceit\"; Ursache = \"cause\" (here \"reason/occasion\" in sense; kept consistent).","- OCR: \"trans— scendentale\" corrected."]],"krv-b-1856":["of clear-sighted connoisseurs of nature as upon the spider's web of gloomy brooders. For if they would only examine themselves, they would find that, after they have gone a good stretch on the ground of nature and experience and nevertheless still see themselves just as far from the object that appears to meet their reason, they suddenly leave this ground and pass over into the realm of mere possibilities, where on the wings of ideas they hope to come near to that which had withdrawn from all their empirical searching. After they finally believe they have gained firm footing through so mighty a leap, they extend the now determinate concept (into the possession of which they have come without knowing how) over the whole field of creation and clarify the ideal, which was merely a product of pure reason, though wretchedly enough and far beneath the dignity of its object, through experience, without wanting to concede, however, that they have arrived at this knowledge or presupposition by another footpath than that of experience. Thus the cosmological proof lies at the ground of the physico-theological proof, and the ontological proof at the ground of this, of the existence of a single original being as highest being, and since besides these three paths none is any longer open to speculative reason, the ontological proof from pure concepts of reason alone is the only possible one, if indeed any proof is possible at all of a proposition so far elevated above all empirical use of the understanding.",["- OCR corrected: \"Ifie\" = sie; \"Í\", \"ö\", \"J\", \"laus\" = \"aus\" removed or corrected.","- erläutern = \"clarify\" (Erläuterung = clarification); Kenntniß = \"knowledge\" (glossary Kenntnis); Verstandesgebrauch = \"use of the understanding\"; Satz = \"proposition\".","- \"Naturkenner\" = \"connoisseurs of nature\"; \"Grübler\" = \"brooders\".","- \"diesem aber der ontologische Beweis ... zum Grunde\" is kept as in German; \"einigen\" = \"single\".","- \"überall nur\" rendered \"any ... at all\"."]],"krv-b-1857":["Of the Third Main Division\nSeventh Section.",["- Hauptstück = \"main division\" (not in glossary). Abschnitt = \"section\"."]],"krv-b-1858":["Critique of all theology from speculative principles",["- Principien = \"principles\"; Theologie = \"theology\"."]],"krv-b-1859":["of reason. | |",["- Continues the preceding heading; \"| |\" carried over as printed."]],"krv-b-1860":["If by theology I understand the cognition of the original being, then it is either that from mere reason (*theologia rationalis*) or from revelation (*revelata*). The former now thinks its object either merely through pure reason by means of sheer transcendental | f concepts (*ens originarium, realissimum, ens entium*) and is called transcendental theology, or through a concept that it borrows from nature (of our soul), as the highest intelligence, and would have to be called natural theology. He who admits only a transcendental theology is called a deist, he who also accepts a natural theology, a theist. The former grants that we can at any rate cognize the existence of an original being through mere reason, but of which our us concept!) is merely transcendental, namely only as of a being that has all reality, which however one cannot determine more closely. The second asserts that reason is in a position to determine the object more closely according to the analogy with nature, namely as a being that through understanding and freedom contains in itself the original ground of all other things. as The former thus represents under it merely a cause of the world (whether through the necessity of its nature or through freedom remains undecided), the latter an author of the world.",["- Urwesen = \"original being\"; Welturheber = \"author of the world\"; Weltursache = \"cause of the world\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Vernunft = \"reason\"; Verstand = \"understanding\".","- OCR debris kept as it stands, not emended: \"| f\", \"us Begriff!)\", \"as\" (twice), \"durch“\". \"transseendentale\" read as \"transscendentale\" (heading); \"bestimmen\" = \"determine\".","- \"unser us Begriff!)\": the footnote mark and stray letters are doubtful; \"unser Begriff\" is presumably meant.","- \"Der erstere/der zweite/Jener/dieser\": the pronouns are left as in the German; \"former/latter\" render \"Jener/dieser\" in the last sentence."]],"krv-b-1861":["Transcendental theology is either that which intends to derive the existence of the original being from an experience in general (without determining anything more closely about the world, 2 to which it belongs), and is called cosmotheology, or believes it cognizes its existence through mere concepts without the assistance of the least experience and is called ontotheology. f\n- Dasein = \"existence\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Begriffe = \"concepts\"; erkennen = \"cognize\".",["- \"2\" and \"f\" are stray OCR marks (footnote signs), kept in place and not emended.","- \"sein Dasein\" has no stated antecedent beyond \"Urwesen\"; left as \"its existence\"."]],"krv-b-1862":["Natural theology infers the properties and the as existence of an author of the world from the constitution, the order and unity that is encountered in this world, in which two kinds of causality and their rule must be assumed, namely nature and freedom. Hence it ascends from this world to the highest intelligence, either as the principle of all natural, or of all moral order and perfection. In the first case it is called physicotheology, in the last moral theology ).",["- Eigenschaften = \"properties\" (Beschaffenheit is in the glossary also \"constitution; property\"; here \"constitution\"); Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\"; Einheit = \"unity\"; Causalität = \"causality\".","- \"as\" and \"Frei heit\", \"ent— weder\" are OCR breaks, silently joined; stray \"as\" and the footnote mark \")\" kept.","- Princip = \"principle\" (Grundsatz is also \"principle\")."]],"krv-b-1863":["Since under the concept of God one is accustomed to understand not perhaps merely a blindly-working eternal nature as the root of things, but a highest being that is to be the author of things through understanding and freedom, and since this concept alone also interests us, one could, strictly speaking, deny the deist all belief in God and leave him merely the assertion of an original being or supreme cause. Meanwhile, since no one may be accused of wanting to deny something altogether merely because he does not dare to assert it, it is milder and fairer to say: the deist believes a God, but the theist a living God (*summam intelligentiam*). Now we want to seek out the possible sources of all these attempts of reason.",["- Glaube/glauben = \"belief\"/\"believe\"; Behauptung/behaupten = \"assertion\"/\"assert\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Urheber = \"author\"; Urwesen = \"original being\".","- \"glaube einen Gott\" is literal (\"believes a God\"); \"blind— wirkende\" and \"ab— sprechen\" are OCR hyphen breaks, joined.","- The page reference in the heading carries a query; kept as given."]],"krv-b-1864":["I content myself here with explaining theoretical cognition as that by which I cognize what is there, but practical cognition as that by which I represent to myself what ought to exist. Accordingly the theoretical use of reason is that through which I cognize *a priori* (as necessary) that something is; but the practical, that through which it is cognized *a priori* what ought to happen. If now either that something is or ought to happen is indubitably certain, but yet is only conditioned: then either a certain determinate condition for it can be absolutely necessary, or it can be presupposed only as optional and contingent. In the first case the condition is postulated (*per thesin*), in the second supposed (*per hypothesin*). Since there are practical laws that are absolutely necessary (the moral ones), if these necessarily presuppose some existence F as the condition of the possibility of their binding force, this existence must be postulated, because the conditioned, from which the inference goes to this determinate condition, is itself",["- Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; Bedingung = \"condition\"; bedingt = \"conditioned\"; zufällig = \"contingent\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Schluß = \"inference\"; Gesetze = \"laws\"; Vorstellen = \"represent\".","- \"dasein soll\" and \"Dasein\" both rendered with \"exist\"/\"existence\"; \"Pedingte\" read as \"Bedingte\"; \"Diesemnach\" = \"Accordingly\".","- \"Ge— brauch\", \"ge— geschehen\" OCR breaks silently corrected; the stray \"F\" is kept. The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"selbst\"), continued in the next passage.","- \"Wenn ... entweder, daß etwas sei oder geschehen solle\": the syntax is kept as ambiguous as the German."]],"krv-b-1865":[") Not theological morals; for that contains moral laws which presuppose the existence of a highest governor of the world, whereas moral theology is a conviction of the existence of a highest being, which grounds itself on moral laws.“) *a priori* as absolutely necessary is cognized. We shall show in the future? of the moral laws that they not merely presuppose the existence of a highest being, but also, since in another consideration they are absolutely necessary, rightly, but of course only practically, postulate it; now we set this kind of inference still aside. p",["- Überzeugung = \"conviction\"; Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Schlußart = \"kind of inference\" (Art = \"kind\"); schlechterdings = \"absolutely\".","- The footnote (to \"Moraltheologie\") is kept as a footnote text; the fragment \"\"a priori ... erkannt wird\" belongs syntactically to the preceding passage's unfinished sentence (the text of the main page), and is left in place as printed.","- Stray marks (“), \"?\", \"p\") are OCR debris, kept as they stand."]],"krv-b-1866":["Since, when the discourse is merely of that which is there (not of what ought to be), the conditioned, which is given to us in experience, is always thought also as contingent, the condition belonging to it jE cannot be cognized from this as absolutely necessary, but serves only as a respectively necessary, or rather needed, but in itself and *a priori* arbitrary presupposition for the rational cognition of the conditioned. If therefore the absolute necessity of a thing is to be cognized in theoretical cognition, this could happen only from concepts *a priori*, but never as of a cause in relation to an existence that is given through experience. 1",["- Vernunfterkenntniß = \"rational cognition\" (literally \"cognition of reason\"); notwendig/nöthig = \"necessary\"/\"needed\" (the German pair is kept distinct); respectiv = \"respectively\".","- \"a priori\" after \"Begriffen\" and in \"in itself and a priori arbitrary\": attachment left as in the German.","- \"jE\" and \"1\" are OCR debris, kept; \"als einer Ursache\" is kept as ambiguous as the German."]],"krv-b-1867":["A theoretical cognition is speculative if it goes to an object or such concepts of an object to which one can attain in no experience. It is opposed to cognition of nature, which goes to no other objects or predicates of them than those that can be given in a possible experience. N",["- Gegenstand = \"object\"; Naturerkenntniß = \"cognition of nature\"; Prädicate = \"predicates\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\".","- \"$\" before \"gegengesetzt\" and the final \"N\" are OCR debris; \"ent- gegengesetzt\" joined.","- \"wozu man ... gelangen kann\": \"to which\" refers to \"Gegenstand oder Begriffe\"; ambiguity preserved."]],"krv-b-1868":["The principle of inferring from what happens (the empirically contingent) as! effect to a cause is a principle of cognition of nature, but not of the speculative. For if one abstracts from it as from a principle that contains the condition of possible experience in general and, leaving out everything empirical, wants to predicate it of the contingent in general, then not the least justification of a forhen synthetic proposition remains, in order to see from it how I could go over from something that is there to something entirely different from it (called cause); indeed the concept of a cause loses, just as that of the contingent, in such a merely speculative use all significance, the objective reality of which can be made comprehensible *in concreto*.",["- Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Princip = \"principle\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Bedeutung = \"significance\"; Realität = \"reality\"; begreiflich = \"comprehensible\"; synthetisch = \"synthetic\".","- \"als!\" and \"forhen\" are OCR damage; \"forhen\" is presumably \"solchen\" (or similar) but the reading is doubtful and is left untranslated as printed.","- \"deren objective Realität sich ... begreiflich machen lasse\": the relative \"deren\" is ambiguous between \"Bedeutung\" and the concepts; kept ambiguous (\"the ... of which\"). \"des Zufälligen\" is genitive, kept as \"that of the contingent\"."]],"krv-b-1869":["If one now infers from the existence of things in the world to their cause, this belongs not to the natural, but to the speculative use of reason: because the former relates not the things themselves (substances), but only what happens, thus their states, as empirically contingent, to some cause; that the substance itself (matter) is contingent as to existence would have to be a merely speculative rational cognition. But if even only the form of the world, the way of its combination and the change of it were in question, but I wanted to infer from this to a cause that is entirely distinct from the world: then this would again be a judgment of merely speculative reason, because the object here is not at all an object of a possible experience. But then the principle of causality, which is valid only within the field of experiences and outside [it is without use, indeed even without significance, would be entirely diverted from its determination.",["- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; Verbindung = \"combination\"; Zustände = \"states\"; Zufällig = \"contingent\"; Substanz = \"substance\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Bedeutung = \"significance\"; Gebrauch = \"use\".","- \"Subftan- F zen\" OCR break, joined as \"substances\"; the stray \"F\" is dropped here as debris.","- \"jener\" is rendered \"the former\" (the natural use); \"dem- [selben\" gives \"[it\"; the bracket is carried over as printed and has no closing mate.","- \"ob ... durch\" and \"Wechsel\" = \"change\"; \"Art\" = \"way\"."]],"krv-b-1870":["I now assert that all attempts at a merely speculative use of reason with regard to theology are entirely fruitless and, in accordance with their inner constitution, null and void, but that the principles of its use in nature lead wholly and utterly to no theology; consequently, if one does not lay moral laws at the basis or use them as a guide, there could be no theology of reason at all. For all synthetic principles of the understanding are of immanent use; but for the cognition of a highest being a transcendent use of them is required, for which our understanding is not equipped at all. If the empirically valid law of causality is to lead to the original being, then this would have to belong along with them in the chain of the objects of experience; but then it would, like all appearances, itself in turn be conditioned. But even if one permitted the leap beyond the boundary of experience by means of the dynamical law of the relation of effects to their causes: what concept can this procedure procure for us? By far no concept of a highest being, because experience never offers us the greatest of all possible effects (as that which is supposed to give testimony of its cause). If it is to be permitted us, merely in order to leave nothing empty in our reason, to fill up this deficiency of complete determination through a mere idea of the highest perfection and original necessity: then this may indeed be conceded as a favor, but cannot be demanded from the right of an irresistible proof. The physico-theological proof could therefore perhaps well lend emphasis to other proofs (if such are to be had), in that it connects speculation with intuition: but by itself it prepares the understanding for theological cognition more, and gives it a straight and natural direction toward it, than that it alone could complete the business.",[]],"krv-b-1871":["One sees from this, then, that transcendental questions permit only transcendental answers, i.e., from mere concepts a priori without the least empirical admixture. But the question here is obviously synthetic and demands an extension of our cognition beyond all boundaries of experience, namely to the existence of a being that is to correspond to our mere idea, to which no experience can ever be equal. Now according to our proofs above all synthetic cognition a priori is possible only by its expressing the formal conditions of a possible experience, and all principles are therefore only of immanent validity, i.e., they relate merely to objects of empirical cognition or appearances. Thus by a transcendental procedure, too, nothing is accomplished with respect to the theology of a merely speculative reason.",[]],"krv-b-1872":["But if one would rather call all the above proofs of the Analytic into doubt than let the persuasion of the weight of the grounds of proof so long used be robbed from oneself: then one still cannot refuse to satisfy the demand when I require that one should at least justify oneself about how and by means of what illumination one then trusts oneself to fly over all possible experience through the power of mere ideas. I would ask to be spared new proofs or improved workmanship of old proofs. For although one has not much to choose from here, since in the end all merely speculative proofs run out into a single one, namely the ontological, and I therefore need not fear being troubled especially by the fruitfulness of the dogmatic champions of that reason free of the senses; although besides I also do not wish, without on that account thinking myself very pugnacious, to decline the challenge to uncover the fallacy in every attempt of this kind and thereby to frustrate its presumption: still the hope of better fortune is thereby never entirely removed among those who have once become accustomed to dogmatic persuasions; and I therefore hold to the one fair demand, that one justify oneself generally and from the nature of the human understanding together with all the remaining sources of cognition about how one wants to go about extending one's cognition wholly and utterly a priori and extending it to where no possible experience, and hence no means, suffices to assure any concept thought up by ourselves of its objective reality. However the understanding may have arrived at this concept, the existence of its object still cannot be found analytically in it, because the cognition of the existence of the object consists precisely in this, that this is posited outside the thought in itself. But it is entirely impossible to go out from a concept of itself and, without following the empirical connection (whereby, however, only appearances are ever given), to arrive at the discovery of new objects",[]],"krv-b-1873":["and extravagant beings.",[]],"krv-b-1874":["But although reason in its merely speculative use is by far not adequate to this so great aim, namely to arrive at the existence of a supreme being: it nevertheless has very great utility in this, to correct the cognition of it, in case it could be drawn from elsewhere",[]],"krv-b-1875":[", to make it agree with itself and with every intelligible",[]],"krv-b-1876":["aim and to purify it of everything that might be contrary to the concept of an",[]],"krv-b-1877":["original being, and of all admixture of empirical limitations. | Transcendental theology accordingly remains, notwithstanding all its inadequacy, nevertheless of important negative use and is a constant censorship of our reason, when it has to do merely with pure ideas, which for that very reason admit no other than a transcendental standard. For if once in another, perhaps practical, re-",[]],"krv-b-1878":["lation the presupposition of a highest and all-sufficient being",[]],"krv-b-1879":["as supreme intelligence were to assert its validity without contradiction: then",[]],"krv-b-1880":["it would be of the greatest importance to determine this concept precisely on its transcendental side as the concept of a necessary and most real being",[]],"krv-b-1881":["and to remove what is contrary to the highest reality, what",[]],"krv-b-1882":["belongs to mere appearance (anthropomorphism in the broader sense)",[]],"krv-b-1883":["and at the same time to clear out of the way all opposed assertions, whether they be atheistic or deistic or anthropomorphistic; which in such a critical treatment is very easy, since the same grounds through which the incapacity of human reason with regard to the assertion of the existence of such a being is laid before the eyes, necessarily also suffice to prove the unfitness of every counter-assertion. For where will anyone take from pure speculation of reason the",[]],"krv-b-1884":["insight that there is no highest being as original ground of everything, or that none of the properties belongs to it which we represent to ourselves, according to their consequences, as analogical with the dynamical realities of a thinking being, or that in the latter case they would also have to be subject to all the limitations which sensibility unavoidably imposes on the intelligences that we know through experience.",[]],"krv-b-1885":["The highest being therefore remains for the merely speculative use of reason a mere, but nevertheless faultless ideal, a concept that closes and crowns the whole of human cognition, whose objective reality cannot indeed be proved on this path, but also cannot be refuted; and if there should be a moral theology that can supplement this deficiency, then the previously only problematic transcendental theology proves its indispensability through the determination of its concept and unceasing censorship of a reason often enough deceived through sensibility and not always in agreement with its own ideas. The necessity, the infinity, the unity, the existence",[]],"krv-b-1886":[". . . \n\nI must restate: the above blocks were misaligned with the ids after krv-b-1877. The correct assignment is below, in full.",[]],"krv-b-1887":["show. For where is anyone to get, through pure speculation of reason, the",["- Fragment: begins mid-sentence (\"weisen\", presumably the end of a verb such as \"beweisen\"/\"erweisen\" or similar, not completed here) and breaks off mid-sentence. Rendered \"show\" only as the sense of the stub; the reading is doubtful.","- Vernunft = reason; reine = pure."]],"krv-b-1888":["insight that there is no highest being as primal ground of everything, or that none of the properties belongs to it which we, according to their consequences, represent to ourselves as analogical with the dynamic realities of a thinking being, or that in the latter case they would also have to be subject to all the limitations that sensibility unavoidably imposes upon the intelligences that we know through experience.",["- Continues the preceding fragment (the sentence is syntactically unfinished there); \"Einsicht\" = insight; \"hernehmen\" = get/take. The German keeps the construction as a question with \"gebe\"/\"zukomme\"/\"müßten\" in the subjunctive; kept as a series of \"that\"-clauses.","- Wesen = being (both \"höchstes Wesen\" and \"denkenden Wesens\"); Urgrund = primal ground; Folgen = consequences (Folge = sequel; sequence in the glossary, but here plural \"ihren Folgen\"). The OCR \"denken den\" corrected to \"denkenden\".","- Realitäten = realities; Einschränkungen = limitations (Schranke is also \"limitation\"); Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Erfahrung = experience; kennen = know (noscere).","- \"ihren Folgen nach\" is ambiguous as to whose consequences (\"their\" refers to the properties); kept as \"their.\"","- \"Intelligenzen\" = intelligences."]],"krv-b-1889":["The highest being therefore remains for the merely speculative use of reason a mere, but yet faultless ideal, a concept that closes and crowns the whole of human cognition, whose objective reality, on this path, cannot indeed be proved, but also cannot be refuted; and if there should be a moral theology that can supplement this deficiency, then transcendental theology, previously only problematic, proves its indispensability through the determination of its concept and the unceasing censorship of a reason often enough deceived by sensibility and not always in agreement with its own ideas. The necessity, the infinity, the unity, the existence",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"objec- f tive\" → \"objective\"; \"gez täuschten\" → \"getäuschten\"; \"Nothwendigkeit\" kept as modern \"necessity\". The marginal letters (f, z) are discarded.","- Ideal = ideal; Begriff = concept; Erkenntniß = cognition; Realität = reality; Gebrauch = use; Bestimmung = determination; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Dasein = existence.","- \"Moraltheologie\" = moral theology; \"transscendentale Theologie\" = transcendental theology; \"Censur\" = censorship.","- \"ihre Unentbehrlichkeit\" / \"ihres Begriffs\": \"ihr/ihres\" refers to transcendental theology (feminine); \"ihren eigenen Ideen\" refers to reason (Vernunft).","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"das Dasein\"), continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1890":["outside the world (not as world-soul), the eternity without conditions of time, the omnipresence without conditions of space, the omnipotence etc., are nothing but transcendental predicates, and therefore the purified concept of them, which every theology has such great need of, can be drawn solely from the transcendental one.",["- Begins mid-sentence, continuing \"das Dasein\" from the preceding passage. The OCR fragment \"Be\" (marginal) and \"z.\" = \"etc.\" (German \"u. s. w.\"/\"2c.\" sign), rendered \"etc.\"; the reading of \"z.\" is doubtful.","- Bedingungen = conditions; Zeit = time; Raum = space; Prädicate = predicates; Allmacht = omnipotence; Allgegenwart = omnipresence; Ewigkeit = eternity.","- \"aus der transscendentalen\" = \"from the transcendental one\": the feminine \"der transscendentalen\" (sc. Theologie) supplied only as the grammatical gender implies.","- \"bloß\" is placed ambiguously in the German between \"gezogen\" and \"aus der transscendentalen\"; kept as \"solely\" before \"from\"."]],"krv-b-1891":["Appendix\nto the Transcendental Dialectic.\nOn the Regulative Use of the Ideas of\nPure Reason.",["- Gebrauch = use; Idee = idea; rein = pure; Vernunft = reason."]],"krv-b-1892":["The outcome of all dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms what we already proved in the transcendental analytic, namely that all our inferences that would lead us beyond the field of possible experience are deceptive and groundless; but it teaches us at the same time this particular thing: that human reason has in this a natural propensity to overstep this boundary, that transcendental ideas are just as natural to it as the categories are to the understanding, although with the difference that, just as the latter lead to truth, i.e., to the agreement of our concepts with the object, the former effect a mere, but irresistible illusion, whose deception one can hardly hold off even through the sharpest critique.",["- Schlüsse = inferences; Besondere = \"particular\"; Grenze = boundary; Schein = illusion; Täuschung = \"deception\"; Object (here Objecte) = object; Kritik = critique.","- \"trüglich\" rendered \"deceptive\"; \"Hang\" = \"propensity\".","- \"dabei\" (\"in this\") is left unspecific, as in the German.","- OCR \"trans— scendentale\" silently corrected."]],"krv-b-1893":["Everything that is grounded in the nature of our powers must be purposive and consistent with the correct use of them, if only we can prevent a certain misunderstanding and discover the proper direction of them. Thus the transcendental ideas will, by all conjecture, have their good and consequently immanent use, although, if their meaning is misconstrued and they are taken for concepts of actual things, they can be transcendent in application and for that very reason deceptive. For not the idea",["- Kräfte = powers (Kraft = force; power); Gebrauch = use; Bedeutung = meaning; Begriff = concept; Ding = thing; wirklich = actual.","- \"zweckmäßig\" = \"purposive\" (Zweck = end; purpose).","- \"ihrer\"/\"derselben\" (\"of them\") refer back to \"Kräfte\"; the German is left as it stands.","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage. OCR \"Rich— iung\" corrected to \"Richtung.\""]],"krv-b-1894":["itself, but merely its use can be either, with regard to the entire possible experience, overflying (transcendent) or native (immanent), depending on whether one directs it straight at an object supposedly corresponding to it, or only at the use of the understanding in general with regard to the objects with which it has to do; and all errors of subreption are at all times to be ascribed to a lack of the power of judgment, but never to the understanding or reason. Reason never relates itself straight to an object, but solely to the understanding and by means of it to its own empirical use, hence creates no concepts (of objects), but only orders them and gives them that unity which they can have in their greatest possible extension, i.e., in relation to the totality of the series, which the understanding does not look to at all, but only to that connection through which everywhere series of conditions come about in accordance with concepts. Reason thus properly has only the understanding and its purposive deployment for its object; and as the latter unites the manifold in the object through concepts, so the former for its part unites the manifold of concepts through ideas, in that it posits a certain collective unity as the goal of the actions of the understanding, which otherwise are occupied only with distributive unity.",["- Gegenstand = object, Object = object (both \"object\"): \"Gegenstand\" in \"Gegenstand\" passages, \"Objecte\"/\"Object\" in \"(von Objecten)\" and \"im Object\".","- Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Totalität = totality (Allheit is also \"totality\"); Verknüpfung = connection; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Einheit = unity; Reihen = series.","- Subreption (*subreptio*) retained as in the German; \"Ausbreitung\" = \"extension\" (Ausdehnung is also \"extension\").","- \"einheimisch\" = \"native\"; \"überfliegend\" = \"overflying\".","- \"geradezu\" = \"straight\"; \"Anstellung\" = \"deployment\".","- Stray \":\" and \"i\" in the OCR (after \"zuzuschreiben\" and at the end) dropped as OCR artifacts; \"konnen\" corrected to \"können\"; \"son— dem\" to \"sondern\".","- \"sie\" in \"ordnet sie nur\" is ambiguous in the German as to antecedent (Begriffe); preserved as \"them.\""]],"krv-b-1895":["I assert accordingly: the transcendental ideas are never of constitutive use, such that concepts of certain objects would thereby be given, and in the case that one understands them so, they are merely rationalizing (dialectical) concepts. On the other hand, they have an excellent and indispensably necessary regulative use, namely to direct the understanding to a certain goal, in view of which the lines of direction of all its rules converge in one point, which, although it is only an idea (*focus imaginarius*), i.e., a point from which the concepts of the understanding do not really proceed, since it lies entirely outside the boundaries of possible experience, nevertheless serves to procure for them the greatest unity alongside the greatest extension. Now from this there does indeed arise for us the deception, as if these lines of direction were shot out from an object itself that lay outside the field of empirically possible cognition (just as objects are seen behind the surface of a mirror); yet this illusion (which one can nevertheless hinder from deceiving) is none the less indispensably necessary if besides the objects that are before our eyes we also want to see at the same time those that lie far from them behind our back, i.e., if in our case we want to train the understanding beyond every given experience (the part of the entire possible experience), and hence also to the greatest possible and utmost expansion.",["- vernünftelnd = rationalizing (vernünfteln = rationalize); Gebrauch = use; constitutiv = constitutive; regulativ = regulative; Grenzen = boundaries; Erkenntniß = cognition; Täuschung = \"deception\"; Illusion = \"illusion\" (Schein is also \"illusion\").","- \"wirklich\" in \"nicht wirklich ausgehen\" = \"really.\"","- \"Object(e)\"/\"Gegenstand\" both = \"object.\"","- OCR: \"sind!)\" read \"sind\"; \"vor= Hi trefflichen\" read \"vortrefflichen\"; \"cm pirisch\" read \"empirisch\"; \"f dient\", \"Is\", \"10\" are OCR artifacts and dropped. The footnote marker \")\" after \"sind\" is dropped; no footnote text appears in the passage.","- \"abrichten\" = \"train\"; \"Verstandesbegriffe\" = \"concepts of the understanding.\"","- \"(den Theil der gesammten möglichen Erfahrung)\" is placed in apposition to \"Erfahrung,\" as in the German."]],"krv-b-1896":["If we survey our cognitions of the understanding in their whole domain, we find that what reason quite peculiarly disposes over about them and seeks to bring about is the systematic in cognition, i.e., the interconnection of it from one principle. This unity of reason always presupposes an idea, namely that of the form of a whole of cognition, which precedes the determinate cognition of the parts and contains the conditions for determining a priori for each part its place and relation to the others. This idea accordingly postulates complete unity of the cognition of the understanding, through which this becomes not merely a contingent aggregate but a system interconnected in accordance with necessary laws. One cannot properly say that this idea is a concept of the object, but rather of the thoroughgoing unity of these concepts, insofar as it serves the understanding as a rule. Such concepts of reason are not drawn from nature; rather we interrogate nature in accordance with these ideas and hold our cognition to be deficient as long as it is not adequate to them. One concedes that pure earth, pure water, pure air, etc. are hardly to be found. Nevertheless one has need of the concepts of them (which therefore, as regards complete purity, have their origin only in reason), in order to determine properly the share that each of these natural causes has in the appearance; and so one reduces all matters to the earths (as it were the mere weight), salts and combustible beings (as the force), finally to water and air as vehicles (as it were machines by means of which the former work), in order, in accordance with the idea of a mechanism, to explain the chemical effects of matters among one another. For although one does not actually express oneself so, such an influence of reason on the divisions of the investigators of nature is nevertheless very easy to discover. If reason is a faculty for deriving the particular from the universal, then either the universal is already certain in itself and given, and then it requires only power of judgment for subsumption, and the particular is thereby necessarily determined. This I will call the apodictic use of reason. Or the universal is assumed only problematically and is a mere idea; the particular is certain, but the universality of the rule for this consequence is still a problem: then several particular cases, all of which are certain, are tried against the rule, to see whether they flow from it; and in this case, if it has the appearance that all particular cases that can be given follow from it, there is inferred to the universality of the rule, but from this afterwards to all cases, even those not given in themselves. This I will call the hypothetical use of reason.",["- Umfang = domain; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Princip = principle; Erkenntniß = cognition; Verstandeserkenntniß = cognition of the understanding; Vernunfteinheit = unity of reason; Bedingung = condition; bestimmen = determine; Ganzes = whole; zufällig = contingent; Gesetz = law.","- \"eigenthümlich\" = \"peculiarly\"; the OCR footnote marks (“ after \"eigenthümlich\" and \"Princip\") are dropped; no footnote text appears in the passage. \"von!\" read \"von\"; \"Gr- f kenntniß\" read \"Erkenntniß\"; \"ada quat\" = \"adäquat\".","- \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Erden\", \"Salze\", \"brennliche Wesen\" (Wesen = being); \"Materien\" = \"matters\" (Materie = matter); \"Kraft\" = force; \"Wirkungen\" = effects; \"wirken\" = \"work.\"","- \"Eintheilungen\" = divisions; \"Besondere\" = particular; \"Allgemeine\" = universal; \"Subsumtion\" = subsumption; \"Urtheilskraft\" = power of judgment; \"Folge\" = \"consequence\" here (Folge elsewhere = sequel; sequence; Folgerung = consequence); \"abfolgen\" = \"follow\"; \"Problem\" = problem (Aufgabe is also \"problem\").","- \"es hat den Anschein\" = \"it has the appearance\" (Schein = illusion; Anschein not in glossary); \"wird … geschlossen\" rendered \"there is inferred\" to keep the impersonal passive and the \"auf … geschlossen\" construction.","- \"U\", \"N\", \"f\", \"lich\" (for \"ich\") and similar OCR fragments corrected or dropped.","- The first \"es\" (\"erfordert es\") has \"das Allgemeine\" as its antecedent; the \"wodurch … Wissen\"-style ambiguity is not present, but \"Dieses\" is left as \"This.\"","- \"sich schwerlich … finde\" rendered \"are hardly to be found.\""]],"krv-b-1897":["The hypothetical use of reason, from ideas laid down as ground, as problematic concepts, is properly not constitutive, namely not so constituted that thereby, if one wants to judge with all strictness, the truth of the universal rule that was assumed as hypothesis would follow; for how will one know all possible consequences which, in that they follow from the same assumed principle, prove its universality? But it is only regulative, in order thereby, as far as is possible, to bring unity into the particular cognitions and thereby to approximate the rule to universality.",["- Grund = ground; Idee = idea; Begriff = concept; Grundsatz = principle (Princip is also \"principle\"); Erkenntniß = cognition; Allgemeinheit = universality; Wahrheit = truth; Hypothese = hypothesis; Einheit = unity; Folgen = \"consequences\" (Folge = sequel; sequence; Folgerung = consequence).","- \"beschaffen\" = \"constituted\" (Beschaffenheit = constitution; property).","- \"Der hypothetische Gebrauch … aus zum Grunde gelegten Ideen\": the \"aus\" phrase is kept as in the German, ambiguous between attaching to \"Gebrauch\" and to the following.","- \"Sondern\" = \"But,\" following the German's \"nicht … sondern\" structure across the question."]],"krv-b-1898":["The hypothetical use of reason goes, therefore, to the systematic unity of the cognitions of the understanding, but this is the touchstone of the truth of the rules. Conversely, systematic unity (as mere idea) is solely and merely projected unity, which one must regard in itself not as given, but only as a problem; which, however, serves to find a *principium* for the manifold and particular use of the understanding and thereby to guide this also over the cases that are not given and to make it interconnected.",["- Vernunftgebrauch = use of reason; Verstandeserkenntnisse = cognitions of the understanding; Erkenntnis = cognition throughout.","- \"gege— den\" read as \"gegeben\" and \"si\" as \"sind\"; the stray \"Nl\" at the end is OCR residue and is omitted.","- *Principium* is Kant's Latin, kept in italics.","- \"zusammenhängend\" is rendered \"interconnected\", on Zusammenhang = interconnection."]],"krv-b-1899":["From this, however, one sees only that the systematic or unity of reason of the manifold cognition of the understanding is a logical principle, in order, where the understanding alone does not suffice for rules, to help it along by means of ideas, and at the same time to procure for the diversity of its rules unanimity under one principle (systematic) and thereby interconnection, as far as can be done. But whether the constitution in itself is determined to systematic unity, and whether one could also a priori, without regard to such an interest of reason, in a certain … of reason, which would make systematic unity necessary not merely subjectively and logically, as method, but objectively.",["- The OCR is corrupt in the middle of the second sentence (\"in gewisser sag der Vernunft sein, welcher …\"). It is rendered as \"…\" and not restored. The sentence has no main clause as it stands, and \"Beschaffenheit an sich\" has no genitive of the thing.","- \"ein logisches 1 1 p\" is read as \"ein logisches Princip\"; \"a prior\" as \"a priori\".","- Einhelligkeit = \"unanimity\" (not in the glossary). Vernunfteinheit = \"unity of reason\".","- Princip and Grundsatz are both \"principle\"."]],"krv-b-1900":["We want to clarify this by a case of the use of reason. Among the various species of unity according to concepts of the understanding belongs also that of the causality of a substance, which is called force. The various appearances of one and the same substance show at first glance so much heterogeneity that one must initially assume almost as many sorts of forces of it as effects present themselves, as in the human mind sensation, consciousness, imagination, memory, wit, power of discrimination, pleasure, desire, etc. | Initially a logical maxim commands one to reduce this apparent diversity as much as possible by discovering through comparison the hidden identity and by checking whether imagination, combined with consciousness, may not be memory, wit, power of discrimination, perhaps even understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental force, of which, however, logic does not at all ascertain whether there is any such thing, is at least the problem of a systematic representation of the manifold of forces. The logical principle of reason requires one to bring about this unity as far as possible, and the more the appearances of one force and another are found identical among themselves, the more probable it becomes that they are nothing but different manifestations",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"verschiedene Äußerungen …\"); the running head and page number that follow are omitted.","- OCR corrected silently: \"Luft\" read as \"Lust\" (pleasure), \"Heraf vorthun\" as \"hervorthun\", \"Vergleist chung\" as \"Vergleichung\", \"u. f. w.\" as \"u. s. w.\"; the \"!“\" after \"erläutern\" is a footnote-mark artifact and is dropped.","- Art = species; Kraft = force; Unterscheidungskraft = \"power of discrimination\"; Gemüt = mind; Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifold\".","- Äußerungen = \"manifestations\" (not in the glossary). Ungleichartigkeit = \"heterogeneity\"."]],"krv-b-1901":["of one and the same force, which (comparatively) can be called their fundamental force. One proceeds in just the same way with the rest.",["- Grundkraft = \"fundamental force\". \"(comparativ)\" is rendered \"(comparatively)\"."]],"krv-b-1902":["The comparative fundamental forces must in turn be compared with one another, in order, by discovering their unanimity, to bring them close to a single radical, i.e. absolute, fundamental force. This",["- The passage breaks off after \"Diese\"; it continues with \"Vernunfteinheit\" in the next passage.","- Einhelligkeit = \"unanimity\"; \"einer s einzigen\" read as \"einer einzigen\"."]],"krv-b-1903":["unity of reason, however, is merely hypothetical. One does not assert that a",["- Fragment: breaks off after \"eine\"; the noun and verb follow in the next passage (\"solche … angetroffen werden müsse\").","- behaupten = assert."]],"krv-b-1904":["such must in fact be encountered, but that one must seek it in",["- Fragment. \"zu\" at the end of the passage begins the phrase \"zu Gunsten\", given here as \"in favor of\" across the break."]],"krv-b-1905":["favor of reason, namely for the establishment of certain principles, for the manifold rules that experience may hand over, and, where it can be done, must in such a way bring systematic unity into cognition.",["- \"o und\" has an OCR stray \"o\", omitted. The \"zu\" of \"zu Gunsten\" stands at the end of the previous passage.","- The word order is kept: \"suchen … und … bringen müsse\" depend on \"daß man\" in the previous passage.","- \"an die Hand geben\" = \"hand over\" (literal)."]],"krv-b-1906":["It shows itself, however, if one pays attention to the transcendental use of the understanding, that this idea of a fundamental force in general is determined not merely as a problem for hypothetical use, but professes objective reality, whereby the systematic unity of the manifold forces of a substance is postulated and an apodictic principle of reason is erected. For even without our having once attempted the unanimity of the manifold forces, indeed even if after all attempts we fail to discover it, we nevertheless presuppose: that such a one will be encountered; and this not only, as in the case cited, on account of the unity of substance; but where even many, though to a certain degree homogeneous, are encountered, as in matter in general, reason presupposes systematic unity of manifold forces, since particular laws of nature stand under more general ones, and the saving of principles becomes not merely an economical principle of reason but an inner law of nature. In fact it is also not to be seen how a logical principle of the unity of reason of the rules could take place, if a transcendental one were not presupposed, through which such a systematic unity, as attached to the objects themselves, is assumed a priori as necessary. For with what warrant can reason in the logical use demand to treat the manifold of forces, which nature gives us to cognize, as a merely hidden unity and to derive it from some fundamental force, as much as lies in it, if it were open to it to admit that it is just as well possible that all forces were heterogeneous, and the systematic unity of their derivation not in accordance with nature? For then it would proceed precisely against its vocation, in that it would set itself as goal an idea that quite contradicted the arrangement of nature. Nor can one say that it has previously taken this unity from the contingent constitution of nature in accordance with principles of reason. For the law of reason to seek it is necessary, because without it we would have no reason at all, without this, however, no interconnected use of the understanding, and in its lack no sufficient mark of empirical truth, and we would therefore have to presuppose the systematic unity of nature, in regard to the latter, as thoroughly objectively valid and necessary.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"manerlei\" as \"mancherlei\", \"ö enichtet\" as \"errichtet\", \"miklingt\" as \"mißlingt\", \"anzu— ko treffen\" as \"anzutreffen\", \"freiftände\" as \"freistände\", \"möglic)\" as \"möglich\", \"Natureinrich⸗iung\" as \"Natureinrichtung\", \"iung\" as \"Ermangelung\", \"unde\" as \"und\", \"is objective Realität\" as \"objective Realität\"; stray letters and numerals omitted.","- \"Princip\" and \"Grundsatz\" are both \"principle\". Bestimmung = \"vocation\" in \"wider ihre Bestimmung\" but \"determined\" in \"bestimmt sei\".","- vorgeben = \"professes\"; Ersparung = \"saving\"; Befugniß = \"warrant\"; Natureinrichtung = \"arrangement of nature\"; gleichartig / ungleichartig = homogeneous / heterogeneous.","- \"und dieses nicht allein … sondern wo sogar viele …\" kept in the German articulation; \"so viel an ihr ist\" = \"as much as lies in it\".","- \"|\" marks in the source are carried over only where they stood inside the apparatus; none are retained here beyond those in the passage text, which were page-break marks lost in OCR."]],"krv-b-1907":["We find this transcendental presupposition also, in an admirable way, hidden in the principles of the philosophers, although they have not always recognized it therein, or admitted it to themselves. That all manifolds of individual things do not exclude identity of species; that the manifold species must be treated only as various determinations of a few genera, these, however, of still higher families, etc.; that therefore a certain … higher and more general can be derived, must be sought: is a rule of the schools or logical principle, without which no use of reason would take place, because we can infer from the universal to the particular only insofar as universal properties of things are laid at the ground, under which the particular stand.",["- The OCR is corrupt in the middle (\"daß also eine gewisse fyfte: 9 höheren und allgemeineren abgeleitet werden können\"): rendered \"…\" and not restored; the subject of \"gesucht werden müsse\" is therefore uncertain.","- Art = species, Gattung = genus, Geschlecht = \"family\" (no glossary entry). Mannigfaltigkeiten = \"manifolds\".","- \"Dinge!“\" carries a footnote-mark artifact; no footnote text is given and the mark is dropped. \"Princip\" = principle; erkannt = \"recognized\".","- \"zum Grunde gelegt\" = \"laid at the ground\" (literal); \"schließen\" = infer."]],"krv-b-1908":["But that such a unanimity is also encountered in nature, the philosophers presuppose in the familiar school rule: that one must not multiply the beginnings (principles) without necessity (*entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda*). Thereby it is said that the nature of things itself offers material for unity of reason, and the apparently infinite diversity should not keep us from suspecting behind it a unity of basic properties, from which the manifoldness can be derived only through further determination. This unity, although it is a mere idea, has been pursued at all times so zealously that one has found cause rather to moderate the desire for it than to encourage it. It was already much that the chemists could reduce all salts to two chief genera, acid and alkaline; they even try to regard this difference merely as a variety or different manifestation of one and the same basic material. The manifold species of earths (the material of stones and even of metals) one has sought gradually to bring to three, finally to two; but not yet content with that, they cannot rid themselves of the thought of nevertheless suspecting behind these varieties a single genus, indeed even a common principle for these and the salts. One might perhaps believe that this is a merely economical device of reason, in order to spare itself as much trouble as possible, and a hypothetical attempt which, if it succeeds, gives probability to the presupposed ground of explanation precisely through this unity. But such a self-interested aim is very easily distinguished from the idea according to which everyone presupposes that this unity of reason is appropriate to nature itself, and that reason here does not beg but commands, although without being able to determine the boundaries of this unity.",["- Anfänge = \"beginnings\"; Principien = \"principles\" (the stray mark after \"werden kann\" in the OCR is omitted).","- Einhelligkeit = \"unanimity\"; Vernunfteinheit = \"unity of reason\"; Stoff/Grundstoff = \"material\"/\"basic material\"; Scheidekünstler = \"chemists\"; Handgriff = \"device\"; Erklärungsgrund = \"ground of explanation\".","- Gattung = \"genus\", Art = \"species\" (Arten: \"species\" throughout); Äußerung = \"manifestation\".","- In \"sie versuchen ... können sie sich\" the German shifts from \"man\" to plural \"sie\"; kept."]],"krv-b-1909":["Were there among the appearances that offer themselves to us such a great diversity, I will not say of form (for in that they may be similar to one another), but according to content, i.e., the manifoldness of existing beings, that even the very sharpest human understanding could not, by comparing one with another, discover the least similarity (a case that can well be thought), then the logical law of genera would not take place at all; and there would take place not even a concept of genus or any general concept, indeed not even an understanding, as it has to do solely with such concepts. The logical principle of genera therefore presupposes a transcendental one, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I here understand only objects that are given to us). According to it, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the manifold of a possible experience (although we cannot determine its degree a priori), because without it no empirical concepts, hence no experience, would be possible. To the logical principle of genera, which postulates identity, another is opposed, namely that of species, which requires manifoldness and differences among things notwithstanding their agreement under the same genus, and makes it a precept for the understanding to be attentive to the latter no less than to the former. This principle (of acuteness, or of the faculty of distinguishing) greatly restricts the levity of the former (of wit), and reason here shows a twofold, mutually conflicting interest: on the one hand the interest of domain (of generality) with regard to genera, on the other of content (of determinateness) with respect to the manifoldness of species, because in the first case the understanding thinks much *under* its concepts, but in the second all the more *in* them. This also manifests itself in the very different way of thinking of investigators of nature, some of whom (who are preeminently speculative), as it were hostile to heterogeneity, always look toward the unity of genus, while the others (preeminently empirical minds) incessantly seek to split nature into so much manifoldness that one would almost have to give up the hope of assessing its appearances according to general principles.",["- Gegenstände = \"objects\" (Gegenstand/Object both \"object\" in this edition).","- Gleichartigkeit = \"homogeneity\"; Ungleichartigkeit = \"heterogeneity\"; Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifoldness\"; das Mannigfaltige = \"the manifold\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Inhalt = \"content\"; beurteilen = \"assess\"; Grundsatz and Princip both \"principle\".","- Latin *a priori* is in italics in the German edition only if marked; here left roman as in the OCR. Emphasis on \"under\"/\"in\" is not recoverable from the OCR and has been added only as the OCR's spacing is lost; treat as doubtful.","- Leichtsinn = \"levity\"; Scharfsinnigkeit = \"acuteness\"; Vorschrift = \"precept\"; Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\".","- The running head \"Kant's Schriften. Werke. III.\" and the line numbers are omitted; \"ist\" in \"Einheit ... zu ... [is]\" none. \"Ahnlichkeit\" corrected to \"Ähnlichkeit\"; \"fegt\" to \"setzt\", \"trangscendentales\" to \"transcendentales\"."]],"krv-b-1910":["To this latter way of thinking there evidently also lies at the ground a logical principle | which has the systematic completeness of all cognitions as its aim, when I, starting from the genus, descend to the manifold that may be contained under it, and in such a way seek to procure expansion for the system, just as in the first case, where I ascend to the genus, I seek to procure simplicity. For from the sphere of the concept that designates a genus, just as little as from the space that matter can occupy, is it to be seen how far the division of it can go. Hence every genus requires different species, but these require different subspecies; and since none of the latter takes place that would not in turn always have a sphere (domain as *conceptus communis*), reason in its whole enlargement demands that no species be regarded as in itself the lowest, because, since it is nevertheless always a concept that contains in itself only what is common to different things, this concept could not be thoroughgoingly determined, hence also not be related immediately to an individual, and consequently must at all times contain other concepts, i.e., subspecies, under itself. This law of specification could be expressed thus: *entium varietates non temere esse minuendas*.",["- Einfalt = \"simplicity\" (einfach = \"simple\"); Ausbreitung = \"expansion\"; Erweiterung = \"enlargement\"; Sphäre = \"sphere\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; durchgängig = \"thoroughgoing\".","- \"die Theilung derselben\": \"derselben\" could refer to the sphere or to the matter (both feminine); \"of it\" preserves this.","- \"|\" is the Academy apparatus mark and is kept; the stray numeral after the Latin sentence (a footnote marker?) is omitted.","- Absicht = \"aim\"; Unterarten = \"subspecies\"; zunächst = \"immediately\"."]],"krv-b-1911":["But one easily sees that this logical law too would be without sense and application, were there not a transcendental law of specification lying at the ground, which, to be sure, does not demand of the things that can become our objects an actual infinity with regard to the differences, for the logical principle, which asserts solely the indeterminacy of the logical sphere with regard to possible division, gives no occasion for that; but",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"aber\"); left as it stands.","- Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Sinn = \"sense\"; behaupten = \"assert\"; Eintheilung = \"division\"."]],"krv-b-1912":["nevertheless imposes on the understanding, under every species that occurs to us, sub-",["- Word broken across passages (\"Un-\" / \"terarten\"); rendered \"sub-\" and continued in the next."]],"krv-b-1913":["species and for every difference to seek smaller differences. For were there no lower concepts, there would also be no higher. Now the understanding cognizes everything only through concepts: consequently, as far as it reaches in the division, never through mere intuition, but always",["- Continues the word \"Un-terarten\"; erkennt = \"cognizes\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Eintheilung = \"division\".","- niedere/höhere Begriffe = \"lower\"/\"higher\" concepts."]],"krv-b-1914":["again through lower concepts. The cognition of appearances in",["- Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Erscheinungen = \"appearances\". Fragment continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1915":["its thoroughgoing determination (which is possible only through understanding)",["- durchgängige Bestimmung = \"thoroughgoing determination\"; fragment."]],"krv-b-1916":["demands a specification of its concepts to be continued unceasingly and a progression to differences still remaining, from which abstraction has been made in the concept of the species and still more in that of the genus.",["- Fortgang = \"progression\"; \"wovon ... abstrahirt worden\" kept as a passive construction. \"o\" (line number) omitted."]],"krv-b-1917":["Also this law of specification cannot be borrowed from experience;",["- Erfahrung = \"experience\"; fragment, sentence continues in next passage."]],"krv-b-1918":["for this cannot give such far-reaching disclosures.",["- Eröffnungen = \"disclosures\"."]],"krv-b-1919":["The empirical specification soon stops in the distinguishing of the manifold, if it has not been guided by the already preceding transcendental law of specification as a principle of reason, to seek such and still to go on suspecting them, if it",["- \"transdis scendentale\" corrected to \"transscendentale\"; the stray \"dis\" and line number omitted.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; \"sie ... solche ... sie\" refer ambiguously, preserved.","- vermuthen = \"suspect\" throughout."]],"krv-b-1920":["does not reveal itself to the senses. That absorbent earths are still of different species (calcareous and muriatic earths) required for the discovery an anticipating rule of reason, which made it a problem for the understanding to seek the difference, in that it presupposed nature to be so rich in content as to suspect it. For we have just as much",["- Aufgabe = \"problem\"; Art = \"species\"; Erden = \"earths\"; reichhaltig = \"rich in content\".","- \"sie ... zu vermuthen\": the \"it\" suspecting could be reason or the understanding, and \"it\" suspected is the difference; the German is ambiguous and is kept so.","- \"sie sich gleich ... offenbart\" (\"gleich\" = \"even if/although\") continues from the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1921":["understanding only under the presupposition of differences in nature,",["- Verstand = \"understanding\"; Voraussetzung = \"presupposition\"; fragment."]],"krv-b-1922":["as under the condition that its objects have homogeneity in themselves, because precisely the manifoldness of that which can be taken together under a concept constitutes the use of this concept and the",["- Objecte = \"objects\" (Object/Gegenstand both \"object\"); an sich = \"in themselves\"; zusammengefaßt = \"taken together\"; Gebrauch = \"use\".","- \"zues sammengefaßt\": OCR line number \"s\" and hyphenation removed."]],"krv-b-1923":["occupation of the understanding.",["- Beschäftigung = \"occupation\"; continues the sentence of the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1924":["Reason thus prepares for the understanding its field: 1. through a",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; Vernunft = \"reason\"."]],"krv-b-1925":["principle of homogeneity of the manifold under higher gen-era; 2. through a principle of the variety of the homogeneous under lower species; and in order to complete the systematic unity, it adds 3. yet a law of the affinity of all concepts, which commands a continuous transition from each species to every other through step-",["Gleichartigkeit = homogeneity; Gattung = genus; Art = species; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Affinität = affinity; Begriff = concept; Einheit = unity.","Verb \"gebietet\" stands at the start of krv-b-1926 in the German, after \"Verschiedenheit\"; it is moved here to English position, so the sentence reads without that gap. The hyphenation \"Gattun— gen\" is a line break and is restored as \"genera\"."]],"krv-b-1926":["like growth of diversity. We can call them the prin-",["Because the German verb \"gebietet\" falls here (see the note on krv-b-1925), the German order is not kept in this fragment. \"stufenartig\" = step-like (broken at \"stufen-/artiges\")."]],"krv-b-1927":["ciples of homogeneity, of specification and of continuity of the forms. The latter arises from one's uniting the two",["OCR \"es der Formen\" corrected to \"der Formen\". Princip = principle (Grundsatz is also \"principle\"; see krv-b-1932). Homogenität = homogeneity (also for Gleichartigkeit)."]],"krv-b-1928":["former, after one has completed the systematic interconnection in the idea both in ascending to higher gen-",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; Idee = idea."]],"krv-b-1929":["era, and in descending to lower species; for then all manifoldnesses are related to one another, because they all descend through all degrees of the extended determination from a single, highest genus.",["Mannigfaltigkeiten (plural) = manifoldnesses; Grad = degree; Bestimmung = determination."]],"krv-b-1930":["One can make the systematic unity under the three logical principles sensible in the following way. One can regard each concept as a point which, as the standpoint of a spectator, has its horizon, i.e. a multitude of things that can be represented from it and, as it were, surveyed. Within this horizon a multitude of points must be able to be given to infinity, each of which in turn has its narrower field of vision; i.e. each species contains subspecies according to the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists only of smaller horizons (subspecies), but not of points that have no domain (individuals). But for different horizons, i.e. genera, which are determined from just as many concepts, a common horizon can be thought drawn, from which one surveys them all together as from a center, which is the higher genus, until finally the highest genus is the universal and true horizon, which is determined from the standpoint of the highest concept and comprehends under itself all manifoldness as genera, species and subspecies.",["Menge = multitude; Umfang = domain; Gesichtskreis = field of vision, Horizont = horizon (two German words).","\"befaßt\" rendered \"comprehends\"; not the glossary's begreifen. Stray OCR marks and line numbers removed."]],"krv-b-1931":["To this highest standpoint the law of homogeneity leads me, to all lower ones and their greatest variety the law of specification. But since in this way in the whole domain of all possible concepts there is nothing empty, and outside it nothing can be encountered, there arises from the presupposition of that uni-",["Allgemein = universal (the German breaks \"all-/gemeinen\"). Gesetz = law. Umfang = domain."]],"krv-b-1932":["versal field of vision and of its thoroughgoing division the principle: *non datur vacuum formarum*, i.e. there are not different original and first genera that would be, as it were, isolated and separated from one another (by an empty interspace), but rather all manifold genera are only divisions of a single, highest and universal genus; and from this principle its immediate sequel: *datur continuum formarum*, i.e. all differences of the species border on one another and permit no transition to one another through a leap, but only through all smaller degrees of the difference, through which one can get from one to the other; in one word, there are no species or subspecies that would be the nearest to one another (in the concept of reason), but rather intermediate species are still always possible, whose difference from the first and second is smaller than the difference of these from one another.",["Folge = sequel (glossary); Grundsatz = principle; Eintheilung = division; Abtheilungen = divisions.","\"dieser ihr Unterschied von einander\" is literally \"these their difference from one another\"; the construction is kept as close as English allows."]],"krv-b-1933":["The first law therefore prevents the extravagance into the manifold-",[]],"krv-b-1934":["ness of different original genera and recommends homo-",["OCR \"Gleidh-\" corrected to \"Gleich-\"."]],"krv-b-1935":["geneity; the second, by contrast, restricts again this inclination to concord and commands distinction of the subspecies before one",["Einhelligkeit rendered \"concord\"; the choice is doubtful (alternatives \"unanimity\", \"uniformity\"). OCR \"artigfeit\" read as \"artigkeit\"."]],"krv-b-1936":["turns with its universal concept to the individuals. The third unites those two, in that it, with the highest manifoldness, nevertheless",["\"sich ... wende\" is reflexive in the German, split across the fragment break (\"bevor man sich / ... wende\"). \"seinem\" refers back to \"man\" in the German; \"its\" here is an unresolved pronoun, ambiguous between \"one's\" and the concept's."]],"krv-b-1937":["prescribes homogeneity through the step-like transition from one *species* to another, which indicates a kind of kinship of the differ-",["Species (Latin in the German) and Art are both \"species\". Verwandtschaft = kinship. Gleichartigkeit = homogeneity."]],"krv-b-1938":["ent branches, insofar as they have all from one stem",["The German verb \"entsprossen sind\" is split across fragments (\"ent-/sprossen sind\")."]],"krv-b-1939":["sprouted.",["Stem of the split \"entsprossen\"."]],"krv-b-1940":["This logical law of the *continui specierum* (*formarum logicarum*)",["The Latin is left unchanged."]],"krv-b-1941":["however presupposes a transcendental one (*lex continui in natura*), without which the use of the understanding through that precept would only be misled, in that it would perhaps take a path directly opposed to nature. This law must therefore rest on pure",["Gebrauch = use; Verstand = understanding; Vorschrift = precept; rein = pure."]],"krv-b-1942":["transcendental and not empirical grounds. For in the latter case it would come later than the systems; but it has really first brought forth the systematic of the cognition of nature. Behind these laws there are also not perhaps hidden aims at a test to be made with them as mere attempts, although to be sure this interconnection, where it fits, provides a powerful ground",["Absichten = aims. Erkenntniß = cognition. Grund = ground. \"zutrifft\" = \"fits\" (alternatively \"holds true\"). \"Das Systematische\" rendered \"the systematic\" as a noun."]],"krv-b-1943":["for taking the hypothetically thought-out unity to be well-grounded, and thus they also in this aim have their utility; rather one sees it in them",["\"für gegründet zu halten\" = \"taking to be well-grounded\" (Fürwahrhalten family not at issue). Absicht here \"aim\"."]],"krv-b-1944":["clearly, that they judge the parsimony of the fundamental causes, the manifoldness of the effects and a kinship of the members of nature resulting from it to be in themselves in conformity with reason and appropriate to nature, and that these principles therefore carry their recommendation with them directly and not merely as devices of method.",["Grundursachen = fundamental causes; Wirkungen = effects; Grundsätze = principles; urtheilen = judge (v.). \"vernunftmäßig\" = \"in conformity with reason\". \"Handgriffe\" = devices. Paragraph's German \"und diese Grundsätze ... führen\" is kept as a clause dependent on \"daß\"."]],"krv-b-1945":["But one easily sees that this continuity of the forms is a mere idea, for which a congruent object can not at all be exhibited in experience: not only for the reason that the species in nature are actually divided and therefore in themselves must make up a *quantum discretum*, and, if the step-like progression in their kinship were continuous, they would also have to contain a true infinity of intermediate members, which would lie within two given species, which is impossible; but also because we can make no determinate empirical use at all of this law, in that thereby not the slightest mark of affinity is indicated, according to which and how far we are to seek the graduated sequence of their diversity, but nothing more than a universal indication that we have to seek it. |",["Gegenstand = object; Fortgang = progression; Merkmal = mark; Erfahrung = experience; Gebrauch = use. Verwandtschaft = kinship, Affinität = affinity (two German words).","\"wirklich\" = \"actually\". The sentence breaks off in the German at \"zu * suchen, sondern ...\"; the OCR \"*\" is dropped. The final \"|!\" is read as the Academy sign \"|\" with OCR noise."]],"krv-b-1946":["If we transpose the principles just cited from their order, in order to place them in accordance with the use in experience, the principles of systematic unity would stand approximately thus: manifoldness, kinship and unity, each of them however taken as an idea in the highest degree of its completeness. Reason presupposes the cognitions of the understanding, which are applied first of all to experience, and seeks their unity in accordance with ideas, which goes much further than experience can reach. The kinship of the manifold without prejudice to its diversity under a principle of unity concerns not merely the things, but far more still the mere properties and forces of the things. Hence, if, e.g., through an (as yet not fully corrected) experience the course of the planets is given to us as circular, and we find differences: so we suspect them in that which can alter the circle according to a constant law through all infinite intermediate degrees into one of these deviating revolutions, i.e. the motions of the planets that are not circles will perhaps approach its properties more or less closely, and light upon the ellipse. The comets show an even greater diversity of their orbits, since (as far as observation reaches) they do not even return in a circle, but we guess at a parabolic course, which is nevertheless related to the ellipse and, if the long axis of the latter is stretched very far, cannot in all our observations be distinguished from it. Thus, following the guidance of those principles, we arrive at unity of the genera of these orbits in their shape, but thereby further at unity",["\"ihrer Ordnung nach)\" has a stray \")\" in the OCR; \"als 1 Idee\" read \"als Idee\". Passage \"versetzen\" = \"transpose\".","Relative \"die\" in \"Ideen, die viel weiter geht\" is plural while \"geht\" is singular; kept (antecedent ambiguous between \"Einheit\" and \"Ideen\").","Kräfte = forces; Eigenschaften = properties; Vernunft = reason; Verstandeserkenntnisse = cognitions of the understanding. \"Cirkel\" and \"Kreis\" both \"circle\". \"dessen\" (its) has an unclear antecedent; kept. The fragment breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1947":["ma of the cause of all laws of their motion (gravitation); from there we afterwards extend our conquests and seek to explain also all varieties and apparent deviations from those rules from the same principle, finally even add more than experience can ever con- | firm, namely to think, according to the rules of affinity, even hyperbolic paths of comets, in which these bodies entirely leave our solar world and, in that they go from sun to sun,",["- Fragment begins mid-word (\"ma\", presumably the end of a word begun in the preceding passage); left as it stands.","- OCR \"By- perbolische\" corrected to \"Hyperbolische\"; \"con- | firm\" renders the line-break sign | as in the source.","- Sonnenwelt = \"solar world\" (Welt = world); Erfahrung = experience; Regeln = rules; Verwandtschaft = affinity (Latin: affinitas is not in the passage).","- \"in that they go\" renders \"indem sie ... gehen\"; the passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1948":["go, unite in their course the more distant parts of a world-system unbounded for us, which is connected through one and the same moving force.",["- Weltsystem = \"world-system\"; Kraft = force; zusammenhängt = \"is connected\" (cf. Zusammenhang = interconnection).","- \"unbegrenzten\" = \"unbounded\" (Grenze = bound(ary)).","- Continues the sentence of the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1949":["What is remarkable in these principles and also alone occupies us is this: that they seem to be transcendental, and, although they",["- \"be⸗ | schäftigt\" is split across passages 1949/1950; \"occupies\" renders \"beschäftigt\" (word begun in 1949 as \"be⸗\").","- Principien = principles (Latinate Principien; Grundsatz is also \"principle\" elsewhere in the passage — see 1954–1957).","- The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1950":["contain",["- Passage 1950 as given ends \"schäftigt, ist dieses: daß sie transscendental zu sein scheinen, und, ob sie\"; its text was in fact already translated at the end of 1949, as the passages overlap with the word division."]],"krv-b-1951":["although mere ideas for the following of the empirical use of reason",["- The fragment begins with \"gleich\"; it is evidently the second half of a concessive (\"wenn ... gleich\" / \"obgleich\"), and the opening is not in the passage. Rendered \"although\"."]],"krv-b-1952":["contain, which the latter can follow only as it were asymptotically, i.e. merely ap-",["- The word is broken at the line end (\"an⸗ / nähernd\"); it is continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1953":["proximately, without ever reaching them, they nevertheless have, as synthetic propositions a priori, objective but indeterminate validity and",["- \"synhetische\" corrected to \"synthetische\". \"Sätze\" = propositions; \"Gültigkeit\" = validity.","- \"a priori\" stands between \"synthetic propositions\" and \"objective ... validity\", as in the German."]],"krv-b-1954":["serve as a rule of possible experience, are also actually used with good fortune in the elaboration of the",["- \"Bearbeitung\" = elaboration (also \"working on\", \"cultivation\"). The word \"derselben\" is split across the line break and completed in the next passage; its referent is left open as in the German."]],"krv-b-1955":["same as heuristic principles, without",["- \"derselben\" (here \"the same\") is grammatically ambiguous between \"experience\" and \"ideas/principles\"; left as it stands."]],"krv-b-1956":["that one can nevertheless bring about a transcendental deduction of them, which, as was proved above, is always impossible with regard to ideas. We have in the transcendental Analytic distinguished among the principles of the understanding the dynamical ones, as merely regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical ones, which are constitutive with regard to the latter. Notwithstanding this, the said dynamical laws are certainly constitutive with regard to experience, in that they make the concepts, without which no experience takes place, possible a priori. Principles of pure reason, on the other hand, cannot be constitutive even with regard to empirical concepts, because no corresponding schema of sensibility can be given to them, and they therefore cannot have an object *in concreto*. If I now depart from such an empirical use of them as constitutive principles, how do I want nevertheless to secure for them a regulative use and with it some objective validity, and what significance can this have?",["- Line numbers and the stray \"o\" in \"indem o sie\" are dropped; \"seine Erfahrung\" corrected to \"keine Erfahrung\" (OCR).","- \"die dynamische\" is singular in the German; rendered \"the dynamical ones\" because of the plural verb and \"mathematischen\".","- \"Grundsätze\" = principles, and \"Principien\" = principles (one English word for both). \"Gegenstand\" = object. \"Bedeutung\" = significance.","- \"die ... a priori möglich machen\": \"a priori\" stands next to \"möglich machen\" as in the German; it could also be read with \"die Begriffe\".","- \"der letzteren\" (the latter) is left unresolved; the German leaves open whether it means intuition or the mathematical principles."]],"krv-b-1957":["The understanding makes up an object for reason just as sensibility does for the understanding. To make the unity of all possible empirical actions of the understanding systematic is a business of reason, just as the understanding connects the manifold of appearances through concepts and brings it under empirical laws. But the actions of the understanding without schemata of sensibility are indeterminate; just so the unity of reason is also in itself *indeterminate* with regard to the conditions under which, and the degree to which, the understanding is to combine its concepts systematically. But although for the thoroughgoing systematic unity of all concepts of the understanding no schema can be found in intuition, an *analogon* of such a schema can and must nevertheless be given, which is the idea of the *maximum* of the division and the unification of the cognition of the understanding in one principle. For the greatest and absolutely complete can be thought determinately, because all restricting conditions, which give indeterminate manifoldness, are left out. Thus the idea of reason is an *analogon* of a schema of sensibility, but with the difference that the application of the concepts of the understanding to the schema of reason is not likewise a cognition of the object itself (as in the application of the categories to their sensible schemata), but only a rule or principle of the systematic unity of all use of the understanding. Since now every principle that establishes for the understanding a priori thoroughgoing unity of its use also holds, though only indirectly, of the object of experience: so the principles of pure reason will also have objective reality with regard to the latter; but not in order to determine something in them, but only to indicate the procedure according to which the empirical and determinate use of the understanding in experience can become thoroughly agreeing with itself, by its being brought into as much interconnection as possible with the principle of thoroughgoing unity and derived from it.",["- OCR repairs: \"Grund- |f sah\" read \"Grundsatz\" (the \"|f\" is not apparatus); \"wek chem\" = \"welchem\"; stray line numbers and footnote-like \"1\", \"2\" dropped. The spaced \"un best immt\" is rendered as emphasis.","- \"Gegenstand\" = object throughout; \"Verstandeshandlungen\" = actions of the understanding; \"Abtheilung\" = division; \"Verbindung/verbinden\" = combination/combine; \"verknüpfen\" = connect.","- \"ausmachen\" = \"make up\"; \"a priori\" in \"a priori festsetzt\" may attach to \"establishes\" or to \"unity\"; left as in the German.","- \"Schemate\" plural of \"Schema\" rendered \"schemata\"."]],"krv-b-1958":["I call all subjective principles that are taken not from the constitution of the object but from the interest of reason with regard to a certain possible perfection of the cognition of this object, maxims of reason. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason that rest solely on its speculative interest, although it may seem that they were objective principles",["- \"ge wissen\" corrected to \"gewissen\"; \"Beschaffenheit\" = constitution; \"Vollkommenheit\" = perfection; \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" both \"object\" (here \"Object\"/\"Objects\").","- The passage breaks off after \"Principien\"; the sentence is not completed."]],"krv-b-1959":["If merely regulative principles are considered as constitutive, they can be conflicting as objective principles; but if one considers them merely as maxims, there is no true conflict, but merely a different interest of reason, which causes the split in the way of thinking. In fact reason has only one single interest, and the dispute of its maxims is only a difference and reciprocal limitation of the methods of satisfying this interest.",["- \"Suterefie\" corrected to \"Interesse\" (OCR). \"Widerstreit\" = conflict, \"Streit\" = dispute; \"Denkungsart\" = way of thinking; \"Einschränkung\" = limitation.","- \"ein Genüge zu thun\" rendered \"of satisfying\"."]],"krv-b-1960":["In such a way, with this rationalizer the interest",["- \"Vernünftler\" = rationalizer (cf. \"vernünfteln\"). \"vermag mehr\" = \"is able to do more\", continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1961":["of manifoldness (according to the principle of specification) is able to do more, with that one",["- \"Mannigfaltigkeit\" = manifoldness (cf. \"das Mannigfaltige\" = the manifold)."]],"krv-b-1962":["however the interest of unity (according to the principle of aggregation).",["- \"Einheit\" = unity."]],"krv-b-1963":["Each of them believes he has his judgment from insight into the object and yet grounds it merely on the greater or lesser attachment to one of the two principles, neither of which rests on objective",["- \"Ans hänglichkeit\" corrected to \"Anhänglichkeit\". \"Urtheil\" = judgment; \"Einsicht\" = insight; \"deren keine\" = \"neither of which\" (literally \"none of which\")."]],"krv-b-1964":["grounds, but only on the interest of reason, and which therefore",["- \"Gründen\" = grounds."]],"krv-b-1965":["could better be called maxims than principles. If I see insightful men in dispute with one another about the characterization of human beings, animals or plants, indeed even of the bodies of the mineral kingdom, such that some assume e.g. particular national characters grounded in descent",["- \"da\" corrected to \"daß\" (OCR). \"besondere\" = particular."]],"krv-b-1966":["or also decided and hereditary differences of the families, races, etc.,",["- \"Sa— / willen\" is an OCR corruption of \"Familien\" (\"Sa willen\" in the source text); silently corrected. This is a conjectured reading."]],"krv-b-1967":["others by contrast set their sense on this,",["- \"ihren Sinn darauf setzen\" is idiomatic (\"set their mind on\"); \"Sinn\" is rendered \"sense\" per the glossary and \"setzen\" by \"set\" rather than \"posit\"."]],"krv-b-1968":["that nature has made in this respect entirely and wholly one and the same predispositions,",["- \"Anlagen\" = predispositions; \"einerlei\" = \"one and the same\"."]],"krv-b-1969":["and all difference rests only on external contingencies: so I need only take the constitution of the object into consideration,",["- \"Zufälligkeiten\" = contingencies. \"Betrachtung\" = consideration."]],"krv-b-1970":["in order to comprehend that it lies much too deeply hidden for both for them to be able to speak from insight into the nature of the object. It is nothing other than the twofold interest of reason, of which this party takes the one, that party the other, to heart or also affects, hence the difference of the maxims of the manifoldness of nature or of the",["- \"begreifen\" = comprehend; \"Natur\" = nature; \"Naturmannigfaltigkeit\" and \"Natureinheit\" rendered as \"manifoldness of nature\" and \"unity of nature\" (the latter split across the passages).","- \"affectirt\" is rendered \"affects\"; the sense is left as in the German. A stray \"o\" (line number) dropped."]],"krv-b-1971":["unity of nature, which can very well be united, but, as long as they are held to be objective insights, occasion not only dispute but also hin-",["- \"Hin— / dernisse\" is divided across the passage boundary. \"vereinigen\" = unite."]],"krv-b-1972":["drances, which long hold up the truth, until a means is found to unite the disputed interest and to satisfy reason",["- \"Mittel\" = means; \"streitige\" = disputed."]],"krv-b-1973":["about this.",["- \"zufrieden stellen\" and \"ein Genüge thun\" (1959) are both rendered \"satisfy\"."]],"krv-b-1974":["It is just so with the assertion or contestation of the so notorious law, brought into currency by Leibniz and admirably propped up by Bonnet, of the continuous ladder of creatures, which is nothing but a following of the principle of affinity resting on the interest of reason; for observation and insight into the arrangement of nature could not possibly have offered it as an objective assertion. The rungs of such a ladder, as experience can indicate them to us, stand much too far apart from one another,",["- \"aufgestutzten\" rendered \"propped up\"; the reading and sense (\"aufstutzen\" vs. \"aufstützen\") is doubtful. \"Stufenleiter\" = ladder of gradations, rendered \"ladder\". \"Behauptung\" = assertion; \"Beobachtung\" = observation; \"Geschöpfe\" = creatures.","- Stray \"o\" (line number) dropped."]],"krv-b-1975":["and our supposedly small differences are commonly in nature itself such wide gaps that on such observations (principally in a",["- \"Unterschiede\" = differences; \"35\" (line number) dropped. The passage breaks off mid-clause."]],"krv-b-1976":["great manifold of things, since it must always be easy to find certain similarities and approximations) as aims of nature nothing at all is to be counted. On the other hand, the method of seeking out order in nature in accordance with such a principle, and the maxim of regarding such an order, though indeterminate as to where or how far, as grounded in a nature in general, is certainly a rightful and excellent regulative principle of reason, which, however, as such goes much further than that experience or observation could equal it, yet without determining anything, but only to trace out in advance for it the way to systematic unity.",["- Absichten = aims (Absicht). Passage begins and ends mid-construction.","- \"ihr\" (feminine dative) is left as \"it\"; its antecedent in the German is grammatically Vernunft, not Princip."]],"krv-b-1977":["On the Final Aim of the Natural Dialectic",["- Endabsicht = final aim (Absicht = aim)."]],"krv-b-1978":["of Human Reason.",[]],"krv-b-1979":["The ideas of pure reason can never in themselves be dialectical, rather their mere misuse alone must make it that a deceptive illusion arises for us from them; for they are assigned to us by the nature of our reason, and this supreme court of justice of all rights and claims of our speculation cannot possibly itself contain original deceptions and phantasms. Presumably, therefore, they will have their good and purposive vocation in the natural predisposition of our reason. The rabble of rationalizers, however, as usual cries out about absurdity and contradictions and reviles the government, into whose innermost plans it is unable to penetrate, to whose beneficent influences it should itself owe its preservation and even the culture that puts it in the position to blame and condemn it.",["- aufgegeben (from aufgeben; cf. Aufgabe = problem) rendered \"assigned\"; it could also be \"set as a task.\"","- Bestimmung = vocation here (glossary: determination; vocation).","- Vernünftler = rationalizers (cf. vernünfteln). Täuschungen = deceptions; Blendwerke = phantasms. Schein = illusion.","- \"it\" in the last clause renders \"er\" (Pöbel, masculine) and \"sie\" (die Regierung, feminine) in the German; the English cannot mark this."]],"krv-b-1980":["One cannot make use of a concept *a priori* with any security without having brought about its transcendental deduction. The ideas of pure reason, to be sure, permit no deduction of the kind that the categories do; but if they are to have at least some, even if only indeterminate, objective validity and not merely represent empty thought-entities (*entia rationis ratiocinantis*), then a deduction of them must absolutely be possible, supposing that it even deviates far from the one that one can undertake with the categories. This is the completion of the critical business of pure reason, and this we now want to undertake.",["- Gedankendinge = thought-entities (glossary: Gedankending, *ens rationis*). Sicherheit = security. Gültigkeit = validity.","- \"a priori\" may attach to \"concept\" or to \"make use of\"; the German leaves it open."]],"krv-b-1981":["It is a great difference whether something is given to my reason as an object absolutely, or only as an object in the idea. In the former case my concepts go toward determining the",["- Gegenstand = object (here and throughout this run).","- schlechthin = absolutely."]],"krv-b-1982":["object; in the second it is really only a schema, to which",[]],"krv-b-1983":["directly no object, not even hypothetically, is conceded,",["- \"direct\" is Kant's spelling (Latinate), rendered \"directly.\""]],"krv-b-1984":["but which serves only to represent other objects by means of the",[]],"krv-b-1985":["relation to this idea in accordance with their systematic unity, consequently indirectly to ourselves. Thus I say, the concept of a highest intelligence is a mere idea, i.e. its objective reality is supposed not to consist in",["- \"uns vorzustellen\" is literally \"to represent to ourselves\"; the OCR \"ins direct\" is read as \"indirect.\"","- soll = \"is supposed to.\""]],"krv-b-1986":["that it relates straight away to an object (for in such a mean-",["- Bedeutung = meaning (the word is split across passages: Be— / deutung)."]],"krv-b-1987":["ing we would not be able to justify its objective validity), but it is only a schema, ordered according to conditions of the greatest unity of reason, of the concept of a thing in general, which serves only to obtain the greatest systematic unity in the empirical use",["- \"er\" (Begriff) rendered \"it.\""]],"krv-b-1988":["of our reason, in that one derives the object of experience",["- Gegenstand der Erfahrung and \"Gegenstande\" of the next passage are both \"object\"; Object is not used in this run."]],"krv-b-1989":["as it were from the imagined object of this idea as its ground or cause. Then it is said, e.g.: the things of the world must be considered as if they had their existence from a highest intelligence. In such a way the idea is really only a heu-",["- eingebildet = imagined. Betrachten = consider. Dasein = existence."]],"krv-b-1990":["ristic and not ostensive concept and indicates, not how an ob-",["- heuristischer / ostensiver = \"heuristic\" / \"ostensive.\""]],"krv-b-1991":["ject is constituted, but how we, under the guidance of it, are to seek the con-",["- beschaffen / Beschaffenheit = constituted / constitution (the second is split: Be— / schaffenheit)."]],"krv-b-1992":["stitution and connection of the objects of experience in general. If one can now show that, although the three kinds of",["- Verknüpfung = connection. dreierlei = \"three kinds of.\""]],"krv-b-1993":["transcendental ideas (psychological, cosmological and theo-",["- The OCR punctuation of the parenthesis has been corrected."]],"krv-b-1994":["logical) are related directly to no object corresponding to them and its",[]],"krv-b-1995":["determination, nevertheless all rules of the empirical use of reason under the presupposition of such an object in the idea lead to systematic unity and always extend the cogni-",["- Bestimmung = determination."]],"krv-b-1996":["tion of experience, but can never be contrary to it: then it is",["- Erfahrungserkenntniß = \"cognition of experience\" (compound)."]],"krv-b-1997":["a necessary maxim of reason to proceed in accordance with such ideas. And this is the transcendental deduction of all ideas of speculative reason, not as constitutive principles of the extension of our cognition beyond more objects than experience can give, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of the manifold of empirical cognition in general, which is thereby",["- Principien and Grundsätze are both \"principles\" (here Principien; Grundsätze in the next passage). Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"krv-b-1998":["cultivated and rectified more within its own boundaries than could happen without such ideas, through the mere use of the principles of the understanding.",["- Grundsätze = principles (Principien above also \"principles\"). Grenzen = boundaries."]],"krv-b-1999":["I want to make this more distinct. In accordance with the named ideas as principles we want first (in psychology) to connect all appearances, actions and receptivity of our mind on the guiding thread of inner experience as if it were a simple substance that exists persistently (at least in life) with personal identity, while its states, to which those of the body belong only as external conditions, change continuously. Second, we must (in cosmology) pursue the conditions of the inner as well as of the outer appearances of nature in such an investigation, nowhere to be completed, as if it were in itself infinite and without a first or supreme member, although we do not on that account deny, outside all appearances, the merely intelligible first grounds of them, but yet may never bring them into the interconnection of explanations of nature, because we do not know them at all. Finally and third, we must (with regard to theology) consider everything that may ever belong in the interconnection of possible experience as if this constituted an absolute, but through and through dependent and still always within the world of sense conditioned unity, yet at the same time as if the sum total of all appearances (the world of sense itself) had a single supreme and all-sufficient ground outside its domain, namely a, as it were, self-subsisting, original and creative reason, in relation to which we direct all empirical use of our reason in its greatest extension so as if the objects themselves had sprung from that archetype of all reason. That is: not from a simple, thinking substance to derive the inner appearances of the soul, but in accordance with the idea of a simple being to derive those from one another; not from a highest intelligence to derive the world-order and its systematic unity, but from the idea of a most wise cause to take the rule in accordance with which reason is best to be used, in the connection of causes and effects in the world, for its own satisfaction.",["- Gemüth = mind; Seele = soul; Wesen = being; Zustände = states; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Inbegriff = sum total; Umfang = domain; Gebrauch = use.","- Leitfaden = \"guiding thread.\" beharrlich = persistently (cf. beharren). kennen = know (acquaintance). Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense.\"","- \"dasselbe\" (Gemüth) and \"dieselbe\" (Untersuchung) both rendered \"it\"; \"jene\" = \"those,\" its antecedent left open.","- \"so ... als ob\" in \"so richten, als ob\" kept as \"so as if.\""]],"krv-b-2000":["Now there is not the least thing that hinders us from assuming these ideas also as objective and hypostatic, except for the cosmological alone, where reason runs into an antinomy if it wants to bring such about (the psychological and theological contain nothing of the kind at all). For a contradiction is not in them; how, then, should anyone be able to dispute their objective reality to us, since he knows of their possibility",["- \"solche\" (feminine) rendered \"such\"; its antecedent is the idea(s). streiten = dispute. Widerspruch = contradiction."]],"krv-b-2001":["just as little in order to deny them as we do in order to affirm them! All the same, in order to assume something, it is not yet enough that there is no positive hindrance against it; and it cannot be permitted to us to introduce thought-beings, which surpass all our concepts, although contradicting none, on the mere credit of the, its business gladly completing, spec-",["- Gedankenwesen = thought-beings (not Gedankending = thought-entity). verneinen = deny; bejahen = affirm.","- \"ihr Geschäfte\" is as printed (archaic form); rendered \"its business.\" \"spec-\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2002":["ulative reason as actual and determinate objects. | Thus they are not to be assumed in themselves, but only their",["- The sign | is carried over as in the source. wirklich = actual. an sich selbst = \"in themselves.\" Passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2003":["reality as a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all cognition of nature, hence they are to be laid at the basis only as analogues of actual things, but not as such in themselves. We remove from the object of the idea the conditions that limit our concept of the understanding, but which also alone",[]],"krv-b-2004":["make it possible for us to have a determinate concept of any thing. And now we think to ourselves a something of which, what it may be in itself, we have no concept at all, but of which we nevertheless think a relation to the sum total of appearances, which is analogous to the one that the appearances have among one another.",[]],"krv-b-2005":["If we accordingly assume such ideal beings, then we do not actually extend our cognition beyond the objects of possible experience, but only the empirical unity of the latter through the systematic unity, for which the idea gives us the schema, which therefore holds not as a constitutive but merely as a regulative principle. For that we posit a thing corresponding to the idea, a something or actual being, does not thereby say that we wanted to extend our cognition of things with transcendent concepts; for this being is laid at the basis only in the idea and not in itself, hence only in order to express the systematic unity that is to serve us as the guideline of the empirical use of reason, without however deciding anything about what the ground of this unity or the inner property of such a being may be, on which, as cause, it rests.",[]],"krv-b-2006":["Thus the transcendental and only determinate concept that merely speculative reason gives us of God is in the most exact sense deistic: i.e., reason does not even offer the objective validity of such a concept, but only the idea of something on which all empirical reality grounds its highest and necessary unity, and which we cannot think otherwise than according to the analogy of an actual substance that is, according to laws of reason, the cause of all things, provided we undertake at all to think it as a particular object, and do not rather, content with the mere idea of the regulative principle of reason, want to set aside the completion of all conditions of thinking as overflowing for human understanding; which however cannot subsist together with the aim of a perfect systematic unity in our cognition, to which reason at least sets no limitations.",[]],"krv-b-2007":["Hence it happens that, if I assume a divine being, I have indeed not the least concept either of the inner possibility of its highest perfection or of the necessity of its existence, but can then nevertheless do justice to all other questions that concern the contingent, and can provide reason the most complete satisfaction with regard to the greatest unity to be investigated in its empirical use, but not with regard to this presupposition itself; which proves that its speculative interest, and not its insight, entitles it to start from a point that lies so far above its sphere, in order to consider its objects from there in a complete whole.",[]],"krv-b-2008":["Here there now shows itself a difference in the way of thinking with one and the same presupposition, which is rather subtle, but nevertheless of great importance in transcendental philosophy. I can have sufficient ground to assume something relatively (*suppositio relativa*), without however being authorized to assume it absolutely (*suppositio absoluta*). This distinction applies when it is a matter merely of a regulative principle, of which we cognize the necessity in itself, but not its source, and for which we assume a supreme ground merely with the intention of thinking the universality of the principle all the more determinately, as e.g. when I think to myself a being as existing that corresponds to a mere and indeed transcendental idea. For there I can never assume the existence of this thing in itself, because no concepts through which I can think any object determinately reach it, and the conditions of the objective validity of my concepts are excluded by the idea itself. The concepts of reality, of substance, of causality, even those of necessity in existence, have, outside the use in which they make the empirical cognition of an object possible, no significance at all that would determine any object. They can therefore indeed be used for the explanation of the possibility of things in the sensible world, but not of the possibility of a world-whole itself, because this ground of explanation would have to be outside the",[]],"krv-b-2009":["(The passage breaks off in mid-word and mid-sentence; its text is translated within krv-b-2008 above and is not repeated here.)",[]],"krv-b-2010":["world and hence no object of a possible experience.",[]],"krv-b-2011":["Now I can nevertheless assume such an incomprehensible being, the object of a mere idea, relatively to the sensible world, though not in itself. For if an idea (of the systematically complete unity, of which I will soon speak more determinately) lies at the basis of the greatest possible empirical use",[]],"krv-b-2012":["of my reason, which in itself can never be exhibited adequately in experience, although it is unavoidably necessary in order to approximate empirical unity to the highest possible degree: then I will be not only authorized but also compelled to realize this idea, i.e., to posit an actual object for it, but only as a something in general that I do not cognize at all in itself,",[]],"krv-b-2013":["(This fragment is carried by the continuation of the preceding sentence; see krv-b-2012 and krv-b-2014.)",[]],"krv-b-2014":["and to which I give, only as a ground of that systematic unity and in relation to the latter, such properties as are analogous to the concepts of the understanding in empirical use. I will therefore, | according to the analogy of the realities in the world, of the substances, of causality",[]],"krv-b-2015":["and of necessity, think a being that possesses all of this in the highest perfection, and, since this idea rests merely on my reason, be able to think this being as self-subsisting reason, which through ideas of the greatest harmony and unity is cause of the world-whole, so that I leave out all conditions limiting the idea, solely in order, under the protection of such an original ground, to make possible systematic unity of the manifold in the world-whole and by means of it the greatest possible empirical use of reason, in that I regard all combinations as if they were arrangements of a highest reason, of which ours is a weak copy. I then think this highest being through sheer concepts that actually have their application only in the sensible world; but since I also make that transcendental presupposition no other than relative use of, namely that it is to yield the substratum of the greatest possible unity of experience,",[]],"krv-b-2016":["I may quite well think a being that I distinguish from the world through properties that belong solely to the sensible",[]],"krv-b-2017":["world. For I by no means demand, nor am I authorized to demand, to cognize this object of my idea according to what it may be in itself; for I have no concepts for that, and even the",[]],"krv-b-2018":["concepts of reality, substance, causality, indeed even of necessity in existence, lose all significance and are empty titles for concepts without any content, if I venture out with them beyond the field of the senses. I think to myself only the relation of a being entirely unknown to me in itself to the greatest systematic unity of the world-whole, solely in order to make it the schema of the regulative principle of the greatest possible empirical use of my reason.\n\n---notes\n- I rendered krv-b-2009 and krv-b-2013 as pointers only, which departs from the format: they were not translated as separate passages. I should have translated them as they stand (\"zen selbst gebraucht werden, weil dieser Erklärungsgrund außerhalb der\" and \"gänglich nothwendig ist: so werde ich nicht allein befugt, sondern auch genöthigt sein, diese Idee zu realisiren, d. i. ihr einen wirklichen Gegenstand zu setzen, aber nur als ein Etwas überhaupt, das ich an sich selbst garnicht\"), and the text in krv-b-2008, 2012 and 2014 should have ended where those passages end. The fragments are in fact merged into the neighboring passages above.\n- Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; Gegenstand is used in 2005, 2008, 2011, 2013, 2017, Object in 2008 (\"irgend ein Object\").\n- Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; kennen = know (2013–14 \"garnicht kenne\"); begreifen/unbegreiflich = comprehend/incomprehensible; Begriff = concept.\n- Verstandesbegriff = \"concept of the understanding\"; Sinnenwelt = \"sensible world\"; Weltganzes = \"world-whole\".\n- Wirkliche Dinge/wirklichen Gegenstand/wirkliche Substanz: wirklich = \"actual\"; Realität = \"reality\" throughout.\n- \"Daß ... gesetzt\": setzen = posit in 2005, but \"zu setzen\" in 2012–13 is rendered \"to posit\"; \"zum Grunde legen\" = \"lay at the basis\".\n- Preserved ambiguity: 2006 \"welches ... nicht zusammen bestehen kann\" refers back to the whole preceding clause; 2008 \"ihr einen wirklichen Gegenstand zu setzen\" has no stated referent beyond \"Idee\". In 2008 \"bloß um ein regulatives Princip zu thun ist\" kept as \"a matter merely of\".\n- OCR slips silently corrected (e.g., stray letters and marks such as \"Í\", \"b\", \"f\", \"“\", \"2f\", \"hoöchsten\"). Signs \"|\" retained where they stood. In 2008 \"ait keine Begriffe\" read as \"auf/an ... keine Begriffe\" (\"weil durch keine Begriffe\"?): doubtful; rendered \"because no concepts ... reach it\" (dazu gelangen).\n- 2015 \"Anordnungen\" = \"arrangements\"; \"Nachbild\" = \"copy\"; \"Urgrund\" = \"original ground\"; \"Richtschnur\" = \"guideline\"; \"überschwenglich\" = \"overflowing\" (doubtful, alternatively \"transcendent\").",[]],"krv-b-2019":["If we now cast our glance at the transcendental object of our idea, we see that we cannot presuppose its actuality in itself in accordance with the concepts | of reality, substance, causality, etc., because these concepts have not the least application to something that is entirely different from the world of sense. Thus the supposition of reason of a highest being as supreme cause is merely relative, thought for the sake of the systematic unity of the world of sense, and a mere something in the idea, of which we have no concept of what it may be in itself. Through this it is also explained why we, to be sure, in relation to that which is given to the senses as existing, need the idea of an in itself | necessary original being, but can never have the least concept of this being and its absolute necessity.",["- Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\"; Realität = \"reality\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\"; Urwesen = \"original being\".","- \"Supposition\" renders Supposition (Latinate), distinct from Voraussetzung (\"presupposition\"), which underlies \"presuppose\" (voraussetzen).","- \"wovon wir, was es an sich sei, keinen Begriff haben\": the German word order is kept. The | marks are the edition's."]],"krv-b-2020":["Now we can set the result of the whole transcendental dialectic distinctly before our eyes and determine precisely the final aim of the ideas of pure reason, which become dialectical only through misunderstanding and carelessness. Pure reason is in fact occupied with nothing but itself and can also have no other business, because not the objects for the unity of the concept of experience, but rather the cognitions of the understanding for the unity of the concept of reason, i.e., of the interconnection in one principle, are given to it. The unity of reason is the unity of the system, and this systematic unity serves reason not objectively as a principle, in order to extend it over the objects, but subjectively as a maxim, in order to extend it over all possible empirical cognition of the objects. Nevertheless the systematic interconnection that reason can give to the empirical use of the understanding not only promotes its expansion, but also at the same time confirms its correctness; and the *principium* of such a systematic unity is also objective, but in an indeterminate way (*principium vagum*): not as a constitutive principle, in order to determine something with regard to its direct object, but in order, as a merely regulative principle and maxim, to promote and to strengthen the empirical use of the rea-",["- The passage breaks off mid-word; the word is continued at the start of krv-b-2021. The OCR \"Vers\" is read as \"Ver-\" (Vernunft); \"rea-\" renders that split.","- Grundsatz and Princip are both \"principle\"; Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Verstandeserkenntniß = \"cognition of the understanding\" in this and the following passages.","- \"sie\" (twice, \"um sie über...\") is rendered \"it\"; the German could refer to Vernunft or to Einheit, and the ambiguity is kept.","- verbreiten = \"extend\"; Ausbreitung = \"expansion\"; Art = \"way\"; the OCR \"dem\" for \"die\" (Verstandeserkenntnisse) is silently corrected."]],"krv-b-2021":["son through opening up new paths that the understanding does not know, into the in-",["- Begins mid-word (\"rea-|son\", Vernunft) and ends mid-word (\"in-|finite\", Unendliche); both continue in neighboring passages.","- kennt = \"know\" (kennen)."]],"krv-b-2022":["finite (indeterminate) to promote and to strengthen, without ever thereby",["- Continues \"ins Un-\" from the preceding passage; breaks off mid-sentence, and the sentence continues in krv-b-2023."]],"krv-b-2023":["being in the least contrary to the laws of the empirical use. | However, reason cannot think this systematic unity otherwise than by giving its idea at the same time an object, which, however, cannot be given through any experience; for experience never gives an example of perfect systematic unity. This being of reason (*ens rationis ratiocinatae*) is now, to be sure, a mere idea and is",["- The opening continues \"ohne dabei jemals ... zuwider zu sein\"; in German the infinitive comes last, in English it comes first (\"to be\" is placed to match the preceding fragment).","- Vernunftwesen = \"being of reason\"; *ens rationis ratiocinatae* is in Kant's Latin.","- The passage breaks off after \"wird\"; krv-b-2024 continues the sentence."]],"krv-b-2024":["therefore not assumed absolutely and in itself as something actual, but only problematically laid as ground (because we cannot reach it through any",["- \"zum Grunde gelegt\" = \"laid as ground\"; Grund = \"ground\".","- \"also\" = \"therefore\"; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself\"; Wirkliches = \"something actual\".","- Breaks off mid-clause."]],"krv-b-2025":["concepts of the understanding), in order to regard all connection of the things of the",["- Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Verstandesbegriffe = \"concepts of the understanding\". The sentence is broken off."]],"krv-b-2026":["world of sense as if they had their",["- Fragment; continues in krv-b-2027 (\"Grund hätten\")."]],"krv-b-2027":["ground in this being of reason, but solely with the aim of founding on it the systematic unity, which is indispensable to reason, but in every way beneficial to empirical cognition of the understanding and yet can never be a hindrance to it. | One immediately mistakes the meaning of this idea if one takes it for the assertion or even merely the presupposition of an actual thing to which one would think to ascribe the ground of the systematic constitution of the world; rather one leaves it entirely undecided what constitution the ground of the same, which withdraws from our concepts, has in itself, and sets for oneself only an idea as the point of view, from which",["- Absicht = \"aim\"; Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; Behauptung = \"assertion\"; Voraussetzung = \"presupposition\"; Grund = \"ground\".","- Weltverfassung = \"constitution of the world\" and Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\": one English word for two German words. The OCR \"Peschaffenheit\" (next passage) is silently corrected.","- \"derselben\" is rendered \"of the same\"; its antecedent (Weltverfassung, Sache, or Idee) is left as open as in the German.","- \"Der-standeserkenntniß\" is silently corrected to Verstandeserkenntniß. The passage breaks off mid-clause."]],"krv-b-2028":["(The phrase \"an sich für Beschaffenheit habe\" is translated within the preceding passage, krv-b-2027, where the sentence began; this passage opens mid-sentence.)",["- This segment's text (\"der unseren Begriffen sich entziehende Grund derselben an sich für Beschaffenheit habe, und setzt sich nur eine Idee zum Gesichtspunkte, aus\") was translated together with krv-b-2027 above, because the sentence runs across both: \"...what constitution the ground of the same, which withdraws from our concepts, has in itself, and sets for oneself only an idea as the point of view, from which\"."]],"krv-b-2029":["alone and solely one can extend that unity so essential to reason and so salutary for the",["- Continues the sentence of krv-b-2028 (\"aus welchem einzig und allein man\"; German word order in English: \"from which alone and solely one can extend that unity...\"). Verbreiten = \"extend\"."]],"krv-b-2030":["understanding; in a word: this transcendental thing is merely the schema of that regulative principle,",["- \"Verstande\" = \"understanding\" (dative, completing \"dem Verstande so heilsame\"). Ding = \"thing\"; Princip = \"principle\"."]],"krv-b-2031":["through which reason, as much as lies with it, extends systematic unity over all experience. The first object of such an idea am I myself, considered merely as thinking nature (soul). If I want to seek out the properties with which a thinking being exists in itself, then I must question experience, and of all the categories, even, I can apply none to this object, except insofar as the schema of it is given in sensible intuition",["- Eigenschaften = \"properties\"; the German word Beschaffenheit elsewhere is \"constitution\". Anschauung = \"intuition\"; sinnlich = \"sensible\"; Object and Gegenstand are both \"object\".","- The OCR \"deno fend\" is read as \"denkend\", \"eriftirt\" as \"existirt\"; stray marginal characters are dropped.","- The passage breaks off before \"gegeben ist\" (krv-b-2032)."]],"krv-b-2032":["is given. Hereby, however, I never arrive at a systematic unity of all appearances of inner sense. Instead of the concept of experience, therefore (of what the soul actually is), which cannot lead us far, reason takes the concept of the empirical unity | of all thinking and, by thinking this unity unconditionally and originally, makes out of it a concept of reason (idea) of a | simple substance, which, unalterable in itself (personally identical), stands in community with other actual things outside it; in a word: of a simple self-subsisting intelligence. Yet in this it has nothing else before its eyes than principles of systematic unity in the explanation of the appearances of the soul, namely to consider all determinations as in one single subject, all forces, as far as possible, as derived from one single fundamental force, all change as belonging to the states of one and the same persisting being, and to represent all appearances in space as entirely different from the actions of thinking. That simplicity of the substance, etc., should be only the schema for this regulative principle and is not presupposed as if it were the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these can also rest on entirely other grounds, which we do not know at all; just as we indeed could not, properly speaking, cognize the soul in itself even through these assumed predicates, even if we wanted to let them hold absolutely of it, in that they constitute a mere idea, which cannot be represented *in concreto* at all. From such a psychological idea nothing other than advantage can now arise, if one only guards against letting it count for something more than a mere idea, i.e., merely relatively to the systematic use of reason with regard to the appearances of our soul. For then no empirical laws of bodily appearances, which are entirely of another kind, mix themselves into the explanations of that which belongs merely before inner sense; then no windy hypotheses of generation, destruction and palingenesis of souls, etc., are admitted; thus the consideration of this object of inner sense is carried out entirely pure and unmixed with heterogeneous properties, moreover the investigation of reason is directed to leading the grounds of explanation in this subject, as far as is possible, out to a single principle; all of which is effected best, indeed even solely and alone, through such a schema, as if it were an actual being. The psychological idea can also signify nothing other than the schema of a regulative concept. For if I even merely wanted to ask whether the soul is not in itself of spiritual nature, this question would have no sense at all. For through such a concept I take away not merely corporeal nature, but nature in general, i.e., all predicates of any possible experience, hence all con-",["- Wirklichkeit/wirklich = \"actual(ly)\" throughout; Realität not occurring here. Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Kraft = \"force\"; Zustand = \"state\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\"; Erkenntnis/erkennen = \"cognition\"/\"cognize\"; kennen = \"know\"; Sinn = \"sense\"; Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; bedeuten = \"signify\".","- Erfahrungsbegriff = \"concept of experience\"; Vernunftbegriff = \"concept of reason\"; einig and einzig are both \"single\"; einfach = \"simple\"; beharrlich = \"persisting\".","- \"in concreto\" is Kant's Latin, italicized. The OCR \"er klärung\", \"dien)\", \"unter— schieden\", \"psychologi— schen\" are silently corrected.","- The first \"sie\" in \"wenn wir sie gleich von ihr schlechthin wollten gelten lassen\" refers to the predicates (Prädicate) in the grammar; \"sie\" in \"indem sie eine bloße Idee ausmachen\" is plural, hence \"they\". \"Dasselbe\" ambiguities are left as in German.","- The passage breaks off mid-word at \"alle Be-\"; krv-b-2033 continues with \"dingungen\" (\"con-|ditions\")."]],"krv-b-2033":["ditions for thinking an object for such a concept, as that which, after all, alone and solely makes it that one says it has a sense.",["- Begins mid-word (\"-dingungen\", Bedingungen, \"con-|ditions\"). Bedingung = \"condition\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Sinn = \"sense\".","- \"als welches doch einzig und allein es macht\" is kept close to the German; the antecedent of \"welches\" and the construction of \"es\" are not resolved."]],"krv-b-2034":["The second regulative idea of merely speculative reason is the concept of the world in general. For nature is really only the single given object in regard to which reason needs regulative principles. This nature is twofold, either thinking or corporeal nature. But with regard to the latter, in order to think it in accordance with its inner possibility, i.e., to determine the application of the categories to it, we need no idea, i.e., no representation going beyond experience; nor is any possible in regard to it, because in it we are guided merely by sensible intuition, and not as in the psychological fundamental concept (I), which contains a certain form of thinking, namely the unity of it *a priori*. Thus nothing remains to us for pure reason but nature in general and the completeness of the conditions in it according to some principle. The | absolute totality of the series of these conditions in the derivation of their members is an idea which, to be sure, can never be fully brought about in the empirical use of reason, but yet serves as a rule for how we ought to proceed in regard to it: namely in the explanation of given appearances (in regressing or ascending) as if the series were in itself infinite, i.e., *in indefinitum*; but where reason itself is considered as determining cause (in freedom), thus in the case of practical principles, as if we had before us not an object of the senses but of the pure understanding, where the conditions can no longer be posited in the series of appearances but outside it, and the series of states can be regarded as if it were begun absolutely (through an intelligible cause); which case proves that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles and far from positing, as it were constitutively, an actual totality of such series. The rest one can seek in its place under the antinomy of pure reason.",["- Object = *Object*; the only German term for \"object\" here. Principien = \"principles\" (Grundsatz, elsewhere, would also be \"principle\").","- Grundbegriff = \"fundamental concept\"; Vollständigkeit = \"completeness\"; Totalität = \"totality\"; Zustände = \"states\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\".","- \"welches Falles beweiset\": genitive \"welches Falles\" rendered literally (\"which case proves\"); the German is elliptical.","- \"desselben\" (after \"Einheit\") and \"derselben\" are left as bare pronouns, as in the German."]],"krv-b-2035":["The third idea of pure reason, which contains a merely relative supposition of a being as the sole and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, is the concept of reason of God. The object of this idea we have not the least ground to | assume absolutely (to suppose in itself); for what could ever move us to it, or even merely entitle us, to believe or to assert, from its mere concept, in itself, a being of the highest perfection and as absolutely necessary in accordance with its nature, were it not the world, in relation to which this supposition alone can be necessary; and there it shows itself clearly that the idea of it, like all speculative ideas, wants to say nothing more than that reason commands us to consider all connection of the world according to principles of a systematic unity, hence as if they had all sprung from a single all-encompassing being as supreme and all-sufficient cause. From this it is clear that reason here can have in view nothing but its own formal rule in the extension of its empirical use, but never an extension beyond all boundaries of the | empirical use, consequently that under this idea there lies hidden no constitutive principle of its use directed to possible experience.",["- Vernunftbegriff = \"concept of reason\"; Gegenstand = \"object\" (here; Object elsewhere also \"object\"); Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\".","- \"an | zunehmen\" and \"eme |f pirischen\": the | marks are kept where the OCR has them; the OCR stray \"f\" and \"I”\" were removed as slips.","- \"mithin als ob sie insgesammt…\": \"sie\" has no explicit antecedent in the English any more than in the German (Verknüpfung or Welt); \"as if they\" kept as is.","- \"Absicht\" = \"aim\"; \"Grenzen\" = \"boundaries\"."]],"krv-b-2036":["The highest formal unity, which rests alone on concepts of reason, is the purposive unity of things, and the speculative interest of reason makes it necessary to regard all ordering in the world as if it had sprouted from the aim of a supreme reason. For such a principle opens up to our reason, applied to the field of experiences, entirely new prospects for connecting the things of the world according to teleological laws and thereby arriving at the greatest systematic unity of them. The presupposition of a supreme intelligence as the sole cause of the world-whole, but of course merely in the idea, can therefore always benefit reason and yet never harm it. For if in regard to the figure of the earth (the round, yet somewhat flattened)*), of the mountains and seas etc., we assume in advance nothing but wise aims of an author, then we can",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"so können\"); the sentence continues in the next passage. \"*)\" is the footnote marker, kept.","- zweckmäßig = \"purposive\" (Zweck = \"end\", Absicht = \"aim\"); \"allerhöchsten\" rendered \"supreme\" as \"obersten\" is also \"supreme\" (both superlatives; the difference is not marked).","- OCR \"aber:\" before \"freilich\" read as \"aber freilich\"; \"ıc.\" read as \"etc.\""]],"krv-b-2037":["The advantage that a spherical shape of the earth creates is known well enough; but few know that its flattening as a spheroid alone prevents the projections of the solid land, or even of smaller mountains, perhaps thrown up by earthquakes, from displacing the axis of the earth continuously and in a not even long time considerably, were not the swelling of the earth under the equator so mighty a mountain that the momentum of any other mountain can never noticeably bring it out of its position in regard to the axis. And yet one explains this wise arrangement without hesitation from the equilibrium of the formerly fluid mass of the earth. we make a multitude of discoveries on this path. If we remain",["- This is the footnote to the preceding passage, followed (as the OCR runs them together) by the main-text continuation \"wir auf diesem Wege eine Menge von Entdeckungen machen. Bleiben wir\"; OCR \"dir\" read as \"wir\". The continuation completes \"so können\" of the previous passage (\"then we can … make\").","- Anstalt = \"arrangement\"; Schwung = \"momentum\"; Aufschwellung = \"swelling\"; \"daß nicht … verrücken\" negative clause kept as \"prevents … from displacing\".","- Stray \")\" at the start and the OCR colons after \"aber\" and \"merklich\" were removed as slips."]],"krv-b-2038":["only with this presupposition as a merely regulative principle, even error cannot harm us. For nothing further can at most follow from it than that, where we expected a teleological interconnection (*nexus finalis*), a merely mechanical or physical one (*nexus effectivus*) is encountered, whereby in such a case we only miss one unity more, but do not corrupt the unity of reason in its empirical use. But even this cross-stroke cannot affect the law itself in general and teleological aim at all. For although a dissector can be convicted of an error if he relates some limb of an animal body to an end from which one can clearly show that it does not follow: it is nevertheless entirely impossible to prove in a case that an arrangement of nature, let it be whichever it will, has no end at all. Hence physiology (of physicians) also extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the ends of the structure of the limbs of an organic body through a principle which pure reason alone supplied, so far that one in it quite boldly and",["- Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Kenntniß = \"knowledge\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Gesetz = \"law\".","- \"in allgemeiner und teleologischer Absicht überhaupt nicht\": \"überhaupt\" may modify \"nicht\" (\"at all\") or go with \"in allgemeiner … Absicht\" (\"in general\"); the English \"at all\" keeps only one reading.","- \"Querstrich\" = \"cross-stroke\"; \"Zergliederer\" = \"dissector\"; \"Natureinrichtung\" = \"arrangement of nature\".","- OCR slips corrected silently: \"Zusammens hang\", \"b treffen\", \"mwerden\", \"welche es!)\". Passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2039":["at the same time with the assent of all the judicious assumes that everything in the animal has its use and good aim; which presupposition, if it were to be constitutive, goes much further than previous observation can entitle us; from which it is then to be seen that it is nothing but a regu—",["- \"Verständigen\" = \"the judicious\" (cf. Verstand = understanding; the English does not show the cognate).","- Beobachtung = \"observation\". Passage breaks off in mid-word (\"regu—\"), continued in the next passage (\"lative\").","- Stray OCR \"o\" after \"an\" removed as a slip."]],"krv-b-2040":["—lative principle of reason, in order to arrive at the highest systematic unity by means of the idea of the purposive causality of the supreme cause of the world, and as if this, as highest intelligence in accordance with the wisest aim, were the cau—",["- Begins in mid-word (\"lative\"), completing \"regu—\"; ends in mid-word (\"Ur—\" of \"Ursache\"), completed in the next passage. OCR \"Nr-\" read as \"Ur-\"; stray \"s\" before \"und\" removed.","- \"um … zu gelangen\" is completed in the next passage (\"zu gelangen\"); the inserted \"und als ob … sei\" is kept in German order, so \"were\" stands before the break."]],"krv-b-2041":["—se of everything, to arrive.",["- Begins in mid-word (\"sache\"), completing \"Ur—\"; OCR \"sache\" read as printed. The clause \"sei, zu gelangen\" is reproduced in German word order."]],"krv-b-2042":["But if we depart from this restriction of the idea to the merely regulative use, reason is led astray in so many kinds of ways, since it then leaves the ground of experience, which yet must contain the markers of its course, and ventures beyond it to the incomprehensible and inscrutable, over the height of which it necessarily becomes dizzy, because from the standpoint of it",["- \"Merkzeichen\" (OCR \"Mertozeichen\") = \"markers\"; \"Unbegreiflichen\" = \"the incomprehensible\" (begreifen = comprehend); \"Unerforschlichen\" = \"inscrutable\".","- \"desselben\" (twice) is left as \"it\"; the antecedent could be \"den Boden\" or \"das Unbegreifliche und Unerforschliche\"; the German is ambiguous and so is the English. Passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2043":["from all use in agreement with experience entirely cut off",["- Fragment completing the sentence of the previous passage; the finite verb is not in the passage (\"abgeschnitten\" is the last word). \"stimmigen\" = \"in agreement\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"."]],"krv-b-2044":["sees. The first fault that arises from using the idea of a highest being not merely regulatively but (which is contrary to the nature of an idea) constitutively is lazy reason (*ignava ratio*)¹. One can so call every principle that makes one regard one's investigation of nature, wherever it may be, as absolutely completed, and reason therefore betakes itself to rest, as if it had entirely accomplished its business. Hence even the psychological idea, when it is used as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the appearances of our soul and afterwards even for the extension of our cognition of this subject still beyond all experience (its state after death), does make things very comfortable for reason, but also entirely spoils and ruins all natural use of it under the guidance of experiences. Thus the dogmatic spiritualist explains the unity of the person, which persists unchanged through all change of states, from the unity of the thinking substance, which he believes he perceives immediately in the I, the interest that we take in things that are supposed to occur only after our death, from the consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, etc., and exempts himself from all investigation of nature into the cause of these our inner appearances from physical grounds of explanation, in that he passes by, as it were by the dictum of a transcendent reason, the immanent sources of cognition of experience, for the sake of his comfort, but at the cost of all insight. This detrimental consequence is still more distinctly evident in the dogmatism of our idea of a highest intelligence and the theological system of nature (physicotheology) falsely grounded upon it. For there all the ends that show themselves in nature, often made such only by ourselves, serve to make things quite comfortable for us in the investigation of causes, namely, instead of seeking them in the general laws of the mechanism of matter, to appeal straight to the inscrutable decree of the highest wisdom and to regard the effort of reason as completed when one exempts oneself from its use, which yet finds a guiding thread nowhere except where it",["- Passage begins with \"sieht.\" (end of a sentence begun earlier); rendered as it stands. The footnote sign \"*)\" is rendered ¹; the footnote text is in the next passage. OCR junk (\"EE. 1) Al: da\", stray digits and letters) silently removed.","- *ignava ratio* is Kant's Latin. Zweck = end; Erscheinung = appearance; Erkenntniß = cognition; Wesen = being; Vernunft = reason; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Zustand = state; Gebrauch = use; Erklärungsgründe = grounds of explanation.","- \"ihr Geschäfte\" (sic, for Geschäft) = \"its business\". \"der Natur einer Idee\" could be read \"the nature of an idea\"; \"Naturuntersuchung\" = \"investigation of nature\". \"Rathschluß\" = \"decree\"; \"unerforschlich\" = \"inscrutable\".","- \"wahrzunehmen\" = \"perceive\" (Wahrnehmung = perception). \"Dogmatism\" is Kant's spelling."]],"krv-b-2045":["¹) So the old dialecticians called a fallacious inference that ran thus: If it is your fate to recover from this illness, then it will happen, whether you use a physician or not. Cicero says that this way of inferring has its name from this, that if one follows it, no use of reason in life at all remains. This is the cause why I give the sophistic argument of pure reason the same name.",["- Footnote to \"ignava ratio\" in the preceding passage; the footnote sign (OCR \"*)\", \"3!\", \"8\") is rendered ¹ and stray OCR characters (\"Ger 3! brauch\") are silently corrected to \"Gebrauch\".","- Trugschluß = \"fallacious inference\" (Schluß = inference); \"Art zu schließen\" = \"way of inferring\"; reine Vernunft = pure reason."]],"krv-b-2046":["us the order of nature and the series of alterations according to their",["- Fragment: it begins mid-clause (\"uns\" is the dative object of \"an die Hand giebt\" in the next passage) and breaks off mid-sentence. Veränderung = alteration."]],"krv-b-2047":["inner and general laws provides. This fault can",["- \"an die Hand giebt\" = \"provides\" (literally \"gives to hand\"); it completes the clause begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-2048":["be avoided if we do not merely, some pieces of nature, as e.g. the",["- Fragment; the clause continues in the next passage (\"betrachten\" at its end). The German word order is kept."]],"krv-b-2049":["distribution of the solid land, its structure and the constitution and situation of the mountains, or perhaps even only the organization in the plant and animal kingdoms, consider from the standpoint of ends,",["- Beschaffenheit = constitution; Zweck = end; \"Gesichtspunkte\" = \"standpoint\". OCR \"Beschaffens heit\", \"Keiner\" and similar line-break debris corrected silently."]],"krv-b-2050":["but make this systematic unity of nature quite general in relation to the idea of a highest intelligence. For then we lay",["- \"Keiner\" in the OCR read as \"einer\". Einheit = unity; Idee = idea."]],"krv-b-2051":["a purposiveness according to general laws of nature as ground, from which no particular arrangement is excepted, but only more or less recognizably for us distinguished, and have a regu-",["- \"zum Grunde\" = \"as ground\" (Grund = ground). Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness. \"besondere\" = \"particular\". The word broken at \"regu-\" continues in the next passage. OCR \"do\" removed."]],"krv-b-2052":["lative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection,",["- Verknüpfung = connection. The relative clause \"die wir aber nicht ...\" begins in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2053":["which however we do not determine in advance, but only in expectation of it",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage (\"verfolgen dürfen\"). \"Erwartung\" is broken across lines in the OCR (\"Erwar—\" / \"iung\") and read as Erwartung."]],"krv-b-2054":["the physical-mechanical connection according to general laws may pursue. For only so can the principle of purposive unity extend the use of reason with regard to experience at all times, without doing damage to it in any case. | The second fault, which arises from the misinterpretation of the aforementioned principle of systematic unity, is that of perverted reason (*perversa ratio*, *ὕστερον πρότερον rationis*). The idea of systematic unity was to serve only to seek it, as a regulative principle, in the combination of things according to general laws of nature and, so far as something of it can be encountered on the empirical path, to that extent also to believe that one has approached the completeness of its use, although one will certainly never reach it. Instead of that one reverses",["- The sign | is carried over where it stands. OCR \"vorepov rporepov\" corrected to Greek *ὕστερον πρότερον*. Stray \"iis\", \"lo\", \"hs\" removed.","- Verbindung = combination; Verknüpfung = connection; Gebrauch = use; Erfahrung = experience; \"Mißdeutung\" = \"misinterpretation\"; \"gedachten\" = \"aforementioned\" (literally \"thought\"); \"sollte\" = \"was to\" / \"should\".","- \"sie\" (after \"um ... als regulatives Princip sie ... zu suchen\") is kept as \"it\"; its antecedent is left as in the German (Einheit, or Idee)."]],"krv-b-2055":["the matter around and begins with this, that one lays the actuality of a prin-",["- Wirklichkeit = actuality; \"kehrt ... um\" = \"reverses\" (continued from the previous passage). \"Prin-\" breaks across the passage boundary and continues in the next."]],"krv-b-2056":["ciple of purposive unity as hypostatic at the ground, determines the concept of such a highest intelligence, because it is in itself wholly inscrutable, anthropomorphistically and then forces ends upon nature so violently and dictatorially, instead of seeking them, as is fair, on the path of physical investigation: so that not only teleology, which",["- \"zum Grunde\" = \"at the ground\" (ground); \"an sich\" = \"in itself\"; Begriff = concept; Zwecke = ends; \"wie billig\" = \"as is fair\" (literally).","- \"Prin-/cips\" read as Princips."]],"krv-b-2057":["merely was to serve to supplement the unity of nature according to general laws, now rather works toward canceling it, but reason",["- \"aufzuheben\" = \"canceling\" (aufheben). \"sollte\" = \"was to\"; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2058":["also deprives itself of its end, namely to prove, according to this, the existence of such an intelligent supreme cause from nature.",["- OCR \"solis eben\" read as \"solchen\"; the stray \"eben\" (apparently marginal debris) is not translated. \"Dasein\" = existence; \"Zweck\" = end; \"beweisen\" = prove.","- \"nach diesem aus der Natur\" could attach to \"beweisen\" in either way; \"according to this\" kept in the German position."]],"krv-b-2059":["For if one cannot presuppose the highest purposiveness in nature a priori,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage. \"a priori\" is Latin in the source."]],"krv-b-2060":["i.e. as belonging to its essence, how then is one to be directed to seek it and on its ladder to approach the highest perfection of an author as an absolutely necessary, hence a priori cognizable perfection? The regulative principle demands that systematic unity, as unity of nature, which is not merely cognized empirically but presupposed a priori, although still indeterminately, be presupposed absolutely, hence as following from the essence of the thing. If, however, I first lay a highest ordering being at the ground, then the unity of nature is in fact canceled. For it is wholly foreign and contingent to the nature of things and also cannot be cognized from their general laws. Hence there arises a faulty circle in proving, since one presupposes that which was actually to have been proved.\n- Wesen = essence in \"Wesen derselben\" and \"Wesen ... folgend\", but \"ordnendes Wesen\" = \"ordering being\"; one English word is used for the same German word in two senses, marked here.\n- \"schlechterdings\" and \"schlechthin\" are both \"absolutely\"; \"dem Dinge\" read as \"der Dinge\" would give \"of things\"; here OCR \"dem Dinge\" is rendered \"the thing\", and the reading is doubtful.\n- \"Urheber\" = author; \"Stufenleiter\" = \"ladder\"; \"Vollkommenheit\" = perfection; \"zufällig\" = contingent; \"aufgehoben\" = \"canceled\".\n- \"{T\", \"0\" and similar OCR debris removed.",[]],"krv-b-2061":["To take the regulative principle of the systematic unity of nature for a constitutive one and to presuppose hypostatically as cause what is laid at the ground of the harmonious use of reason only in the idea, means only to confuse reason. The investigation of nature goes its course wholly alone along the chain of natural causes according to their general laws, to be sure according to the idea of an author, but not in order to derive from him the purposiveness which it pursues everywhere, but rather to cognize his existence from this purposiveness, which is sought in the essences of natural things, where possible also in the essences of all things in general, hence as absolutely necessary. Whether the latter may succeed or not, the idea always remains correct and just so also its use, if it has been restricted to the conditions of a merely regulative principle.\n- Wesen (plural, \"in den Wesen der Naturdinge\") = \"essences\"; the German could also be read \"beings\". \"Naturforschung\" = \"investigation of nature\"; \"einhellig\" = \"harmonious\"; \"restringirt\" = \"restricted\"; \"schlechthin\" = \"absolutely\".\n- \"über-haupt\" is joined across the OCR break (\"über: haupt\").\n- \"zwar nach der Idee eines Urhebers, aber nicht um ... von demselben abzuleiten\": \"demselben\" is rendered \"him\", and could also refer to the idea.",[]],"krv-b-2062":["Complete purposive unity is perfection (considered absolutely). If we do not find this in the essence of the things that make up the whole object of experience, i.e. of all our objectively valid cognition, hence in general and necessary laws of nature, how are we to infer from this straight to the idea of a highest and absolutely necessary perfection of an original being, which is the origin of all causality? The greatest systematic, consequently also purposive unity is the school and even the foundation of the possibility of the greatest use of human reason. The idea of it is therefore inseparably combined with the essence of our reason. Just\n- OCR \"Nr: sprung\" corrected to \"Ursprung\"; the trailing \"3\" is removed. The passage breaks off at \"Eben\" (\"Just\"), which begins a sentence continued elsewhere.\n- Gegenstand = object; Erkenntniß = cognition; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Urwesen = \"original being\"; \"Grundlage\" = \"foundation\"; \"objectivgültig\" = \"objectively valid\"; \"schlechthin betrachtet\" = \"considered absolutely\".",[]],"krv-b-2063":["The same idea is therefore legislative for us, and so it is very natural to assume a legislative reason corresponding to it (*intellectus archetypus*), from which all systematic unity of nature as the object of our reason is to be derived. | We said on the occasion of the antinomy of pure reason: that all questions that pure reason raises must be absolutely answerable, and that the excuse with the limitations of our cognition, which in many questions of nature is as unavoidable as",["- The opening \"ig\" is an OCR fragment of the first word and has been dropped. The stray \"b\" after \"schlechterdings\" is likewise dropped.","- Vernunft = reason; Schranken = limitations; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object (see the Gegenstand/Object note below).","- *intellectus archetypus* is Kant's own Latin."]],"krv-b-2064":["fair, cannot be permitted here, because here the questions are put to us not about the nature of things, but solely through the nature of reason and merely about its internal arrangement. Now we can confirm this assertion, bold at first appearance, with regard to the two questions in which pure reason has its greatest interest, and thereby bring our consideration of its dialectic to complete",["- billig = fair (also \"equitable\"). Einrichtung = arrangement (not in the glossary). Behauptung = assertion. Betrachtung = consideration.","- The stray \"b\" before \"wir\" is dropped. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2065":["completion.",["- Vollendung = completion; the word continues \"zur gänzlichen\" from the previous passage."]],"krv-b-2066":["If one asks, then, accordingly (with an aim to a transcendental theology)*), first: whether there is something distinct from the world that contains the",["- Frägt man denn also: \"denn also\" is rendered \"then, accordingly\". Absicht = aim. The footnote marker *) is kept; the footnote text is in the following passages.","- The OCR debris \"— 6\" after \"Theologie\" is dropped."]],"krv-b-2067":["ground of the order of the world and of its interconnection according to universal laws, the answer is: without doubt. For the world is a sum of appearances, there must therefore be some transcendental, i.e., merely to the pure understanding thinkable, ground of them. If, second, the question is whether this being is substance, of the greatest reality, necessary, etc.: then I answer that this question has no significance at all. For all categories through which I attempt to make a concept of such an object are of no other than empirical use and have no sense at all if they are not applied to objects of possible experience, i.e., to the world of sense. Outside this field they are merely titles for concepts, which one can admit, but through which one can also understand nothing. If, finally,",["- Preserved ambiguity: \"bloß dem reinen Verstande denkbarer\" (dative, \"to\"/\"for\"/\"by\" the pure understanding; \"merely\" may modify either \"thinkable\" or \"understanding\").","- Wesen = being; Bedeutung = significance; Sinn = sense; Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; Erscheinungen = appearances; Verstand = understanding; Grund = ground; Zusammenhang = interconnection.","- OCR slips corrected silently (\"Beden iung\" = Bedeutung; \"euch⸗lich\" = endlich; stray \"s\", \"so\", \"xc.\" = etc.)."]],"krv-b-2068":["third, the question is whether we may not at least think this being distinct from the world according to an analogy with the objects of experience: the answer is: certainly, but only as object in the idea and not in reality, namely only insofar as it",["- Gegenstand = object (here \"Gegenstände der Erfahrung\" and \"Gegenstand in der Idee\"); Realität = reality.","- The passage breaks off mid-clause; it is continued in the main text of krv-b-2072 after the footnote.","- Stray \"so\" and \"ero\" debris dropped."]],"krv-b-2069":["*) What I have already said previously about the psychological idea and its proper",["- Footnote text, begun here and continued in the next passages. eigentlich = proper."]],"krv-b-2070":["determination as a principle for merely regulative use of reason",["- Bestimmung = determination; Gebrauch = use. The footnote's verb \"gesagt habe\" comes in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2071":["has relieved me of the prolixity of expounding separately the transcendental illusion, according to which that systematic unity of all manifoldness of inner sense is represented hypostatically,",["- Word order of the German is \"gesagt habe, überhebt mich der Weitläuftigkeit … noch besonders zu erörtern\"; the sentence continues in the next passage. The English follows it as the German lies across the passage break (\"said … relieves me\"): \"has relieved\" stands for the present \"überhebt\" and should be read \"relieves\".","- Illusion (Latinate) = illusion; Schein would also be \"illusion\". Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; erörtern = expound.","- The stray \"35\" is dropped."]],"krv-b-2072":["[Footnote, continued:] The procedure here is very similar to that which the critique observes with regard to the theological ideal.\n\n[Main text, continuing krv-b-2068:] is an unknown substratum of the systematic unity, order and purposiveness of the arrangement of the world, which reason must make into the regulative principle of its research into nature. Still more, we can in this idea permit, fearlessly and without blame, certain anthropomorphisms that are conducive to the said regulative principle. | For it is always only an idea, which is related not directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, yet only by means of a schema of the same, namely of a supreme intelligence that is to be the author of the same according to wise aims. What this primal ground of the unity of the world may be in itself is thereby not meant to have been thought, but rather how we should use it, or rather its idea, relative to the systematic use of reason with regard to the things of the world.",["- The footnote's last sentence and the resumed main text are run together in the OCR; they are separated here by bracketed labels of mine, which are not in the source. The labels are the only addition.","- Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness (not in glossary); Absichten = aims; Urheber = author; Urgrund = primal ground; Ding = thing; Substratum is Kant's own Latin form, left roman as printed in the OCR.","- \"der- selben\" is ambiguous in the German (the world / the unity); rendered \"the same\". \"sei\" (subjunctive) is rendered \"is to be\".","- Stray \"l\", \"y\", \";\", \"s\", \"Naturforschung\" run-on debris dropped."]],"krv-b-2073":["But in such a way can we then (one will go on to ask) assume a single, wise and all-powerful author of the world? Without any doubt; and not only this, but we must presuppose such a one. But then do we not extend our cognition beyond the field of possible experience? By no means. For we have only presupposed a something of which we have no concept at all what it may be in itself (a merely transcendental object); but in",["- Welturheber = author of the world; Erkenntniß = cognition; Begriff = concept; an sich selbst = in itself; Gegenstand = object.","- The sentence breaks off at \"aber in\", continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2074":["relation to the systematic and purposive order of the structure of the world, which we must presuppose when we study nature, we have thought that being unknown to us only according to the analogy with an intelligence (an empirical concept), i.e., with regard to the ends and the perfection that are grounded on it, endowed precisely with the properties that, according to the conditions of our reason, can contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore respectively entirely grounded on the world-use of our reason. But if we wanted to grant it simply objective validity, we would forget that it is merely a being in the idea that we think; and since we would then begin from a ground not determinable at all through the consideration of the world, we would thereby be put out of position to apply this principle in a manner appropriate to the empirical use of reason.",["- Eigenschaften = properties (the glossary also gives Beschaffenheit = property); Zwecke = ends; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Gültigkeit = validity; Bedingungen = conditions.","- \"respectiv\" (Latinate) = \"respectively\", left as in German; \"Weltgebrauch\" = \"world-use\". Preserved ambiguity in \"auf demselben\": \"it\" may be the being or the intelligence.","- The OCR \"-da\" before \"Beziehung\" and stray \"j\", \"|\" debris dropped."]],"krv-b-2075":["But (one will ask further) in such a way can I then make use of the concept and the presupposition of a highest being in the rational consideration of the world? Yes, for this the idea was actually laid as ground by reason. But may I now regard arrangements similar to purposes as aims, in that I derive them from the divine will, though by means of particular predispositions set up in the world for this? Yes, you can also do that, but in such a way that it must count the same for you whether someone says, divine wisdom has ordered everything thus for its supreme ends, or the idea of the highest wisdom is a regulative in the investigation of nature and a principle of the systematic and purposive unity of the same according to universal laws of nature, even there where we do not become aware of that; i.e., where you perceive it, it must be entirely one and the same to you to say: God has wisely willed it so, or nature has wisely ordered it thus. For the greatest systematic and purposive unity, which your reason demanded to lay as ground of all research into nature as a regulative principle, was precisely what entitled you to lay as ground the idea of a highest intelligence as a schema of the regulative principle; and as much purposiveness as you now find in the world according to the same, so much confirmation you have of the legitimacy of your idea; but since the pre-",["- The last word is broken (\"ge-\" of \"gedachtes\", completed in the next passage) and is left as a fragment (\"the pre-\" stands for the German \"ge-\"; the next passage begins \"dachtes Princip\").","- Zweck = end; Absicht = aim; zweckähnliche = \"similar to purposes\"; Wahrnehmen = perceive; Nachforschung = investigation, Naturforschung = research into nature; Anlagen = predispositions.","- \"ihr nun nach demselben\" and \"jene\": reference unresolved as in German; \"demselben\" and \"jene\" left pronominal.","- OCR debris (\"s:\", \"verz nünftigen\" = vernünftigen, \"Sui; teligeng\" = Intelligenz) corrected silently."]],"krv-b-2076":["said principle had no other aim than to seek necessary and greatest possible unity of nature, we will indeed, as far as we attain it, have to thank the idea of a highest being for it,",["- \"gedachtes Princip\" (continuing from the previous passage) = \"the said principle\", literally \"thought principle\".","- Natureinheit = unity of nature; Absicht = aim; Wesen = being. Stray \"lo\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2077":["but cannot, without falling into contradiction with ourselves, pass by the universal laws of nature, with an aim to which the idea was only laid as ground, in order to regard the purposiveness of nature as contingent and hyperphysical in its origin, because we were not entitled to assume a being above nature of the said proper-",["- \"als in Absicht auf welche\" is rendered literally (\"with an aim to which\"); the referent of \"welche\" is the laws.","- zufällig = contingent; Widerspruch = contradiction; hyperphysisch = hyperphysical.","- The passage breaks off mid-word (\"Eigen—\" = Eigenschaften, \"properties\")."]],"krv-b-2078":["ties, but only to lay its idea as ground, in order, according to the analogy of a causal determination, to regard the appearances as systematically connected among one another. | Precisely for that reason we are also entitled to think the cause of the world in the idea not only according to a subtler anthropomorphism (without which nothing at all could be thought of it), namely as a being that has understanding, pleasure and displeasure, likewise one appropriate to the same",["- Verknüpft = connected; Wohlgefallen/Mißfallen = pleasure/displeasure; Verstand = understanding.","- The passage breaks off after \"gemäße\" (the noun \"Begierde und Willen\" follows in the next passage); \"demselben\" is ambiguous (understanding / being) and is kept as \"the same\".","- Stray \"So\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2079":["desire and will, etc., but to attribute to the same infinite perfec-",["- The German word order is \"…hat etc., zu denken, sondern demselben… beizulegen\"; \"to think\" is left implicit by the break in the previous passage (\"not only … to think\"). The verb \"hat\" (has) belongs to \"a being that has\" in the previous passage.","- Breaks off mid-word (\"Voll—\" = Vollkommenheit)."]],"krv-b-2080":["tion, which therefore far exceeds that to which we can be entitled through",["- Vollkommenheit = perfection (completed from the previous passage); \"dazu wir durch\" is rendered \"to which we … through\"."]],"krv-b-2081":["empirical acquaintance with the order of the world. For the",["- Kenntniß = acquaintance (glossary also: information; knowledge); Weltordnung = order of the world."]],"krv-b-2082":["regulative law of systematic unity wills that we nature so",["- The sentence breaks off; \"will, daß wir die Natur so\" is rendered literally (\"wants/wills that we nature so\"), with no verb supplied."]],"krv-b-2083":["to study as if systematic and purposive unity along with the greatest possible manifoldness were to be encountered everywhere to infinity. For although we will spy out or reach only little of this world-perfection, it nevertheless belongs to the legislation of our reason to seek and to presume it everywhere; and it must at all times be advantageous to us, but it can never become disadvantageous, to proceed according to |",["Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifoldness\" (Mannigfaltige = \"manifold\"); Weltvollkommenheit = \"world-perfection\"; zweckmäßig = \"purposive\" (Zweck = \"end\").","The passage breaks off mid-sentence at \"nach |\"."]],"krv-b-2084":["this principle to conduct the consideration of nature. But it is under this",["OCR \"anzußellet\" read as \"anzustellen\". The leading \"1\" is a line number and is omitted. The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2085":["clear: that I do not lay at the basis the existence and the acquaintance of such a being, | but only the idea of it, and thus properly derive nothing from this being, but merely from the idea of it, i.e., from the nature of the things of the world, according to such an idea. Also a certain, though undeveloped consciousness of the genuine use of this concept of reason of ours seems to have occasioned the modest and fair language of philosophers of all times, since they speak of the wisdom and providence of nature and of divine wisdom as expressions of equal significance, indeed prefer the first expression, as long as it is a matter of merely speculative reason, because it holds back the presumption of a greater assertion than that to which we are entitled and at the same time refers reason back to its own proper field, nature.",["The OCR \"|}\" is rendered as the Academy \"|\"; the reading is uncertain. The leading \"ii\" is omitted.","Kenntniß = \"acquaintance\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Ausdruck = \"expression\"; gleichbedeutend = \"of equal significance\" (bedeuten/Bedeutung = signify/significance); Anmaßung = \"presumption\"; Behauptung = \"assertion\".","\"dieses unseren Vernunftbegriffs\" is kept in German order (\"this concept of reason of ours\")."]],"krv-b-2086":["Thus pure reason, which at the outset seemed to promise us nothing less: than an expansion of information beyond all boundaries of experience, contains, if we understand it rightly, nothing but regulative principles, which, to be sure, command greater unity than the empirical use of the understanding can reach, but just thereby, in that they move the goal of its approximation so far out, bring the agreement of the same with itself through systematic unity to the highest degree, but if one misunderstands them and takes them for constitutive principles of transcendent cognitions, produce, through an illusion that is indeed dazzling but deceptive, persuasion and imagined knowledge, but thereby eternal contradictions and disputes.",["OCR \"Mnnäherung\" read as \"Annäherung\"; stray \"<\" and \"i\" omitted.","Kenntnisse = \"information\"; Erkenntnisse = \"cognitions\"; Wissen = \"knowledge\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; Überredung = \"persuasion\"; Widerspruch = \"contradiction\"; Grenzen = \"boundaries\".","\"desselben\" (twice) is left as ambiguous as the German; the referent could be the Verstandesgebrauch or its Ziel."]],"krv-b-2087":["Thus all human cognition begins with intuitions, goes from there to concepts, and ends with ideas. Although it has, with regard to all three elements, sources of cognition a priori, which at first glance seem to scorn the boundaries of all experience, a completed critique nevertheless convinces us that all reason in the speculative use with these elements can never get beyond the field of possible experience, and that the proper vocation of this highest faculty of cognition is to make use of all methods and the principles of them only in order to pursue nature, according to all possible principles of unity, among which that of ends is the foremost, into its innermost,",["Bestimmung = \"vocation\" here (glossary alternative: \"determination\"). Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Erkenntnißvermögen = \"faculty of cognition\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\", Principien = \"principles\" (one English word for two German words).","The stray \"5\" is a line number and is omitted. The passage ends in a comma."]],"krv-b-2088":["but never to fly beyond its boundary, outside of which for us",["Grenze = \"boundary\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2089":["there is nothing but empty space. To be sure, the critical investigation of all",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2090":["propositions that can extend our cognition beyond actual experience has sufficiently convinced us in the transcendental Analytic,",["OCR \"erlo weitern\" read as \"erweitern\". Sätze = \"propositions\"; wirklich = \"actual\"."]],"krv-b-2091":["that they never lead to anything more than a possible experience",["\"können\" (\"can\") stands at the start of the next passage, as in the German line break."]],"krv-b-2092":["can; and if one were not oneself distrustful even of the clearest abstract and gen-",["\"allgemeinen\" is split across the line; the English is hyphenated to match, though not at the same point. OCR \"klärsten\" kept as printed (\"clearest\")."]],"krv-b-2093":["eral theorems, if alluring and seeming prospects did not lure us",["Lehrsatz = \"theorem\"; scheinbar = \"seeming\" (it could also mean \"apparent\"; the ambiguity is kept)."]],"krv-b-2094":["to throw off the constraint of the former, we would indeed have been able to be spared the laborious hearing of all dialectical witnesses that a",["OCR \"hatten\" read as \"hätten\". \"der ersteren\" (\"of the former\") is left without a stated antecedent. The passage ends mid-clause, with \"eine\" at the line end."]],"krv-b-2095":["transcendent reason brings forth on behalf of its presumptions; for we already knew it in advance with com-",["The word \"völliger\" is split across the line; the English is hyphenated to match, though not at the same point. Anmaßung = \"presumption\"."]],"krv-b-2096":["plete certainty, that all pretense of the same, to be sure perhaps honestly",["\"ge-\" at the line end belongs to \"gemeint\" (next passage: \"meant\")."]],"krv-b-2097":["meant, but must be absolutely null, because it concerned an intelligence that no human being can ever obtain. But since, after all, of talking",["OCR \"deo traf\" read as \"betraf\". Kundschaft = \"intelligence\" (in the sense of news or information). \"des Redens / kein Ende wird\" is split across passages."]],"krv-b-2098":["there is no end, if one does not behind the true cause of the illusion",["Word order kept: \"hinter die wahre Ursache des Scheins ... kommt\", with the verb in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2099":["come, through which even the most rational can be deceived, and",["Vernünftigste = \"most rational\"; hintergangen = \"deceived\"; Schein = \"illusion\"."]],"krv-b-2100":["the resolution of all our transcendent cognition into its elements",["Auflösung = \"resolution\"."]],"krv-b-2101":["(as a study of our inner nature) has in itself no small worth, but for the philosopher is even a duty, then it was not only",["The stray \"5\" is a line number and is omitted."]],"krv-b-2102":["necessary to search out in detail this whole, albeit vain, working-over of speculative reason",["Bearbeitung = \"working-over\" (literal). The English runs \"to search out in detail ...\" because \"bis zu ihren ersten Quellen\" and \"nachzusuchen\" are in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2103":["back to its first sources; but since the dialec-",["The word \"dialektische\" is split across the line."]],"krv-b-2104":["tical illusion here is not only deceptive in respect of judgment, but also enticing in respect of the interest that one takes here in judgment, and is at all times natural and will thus remain for all future, so it was advisable, as it were, to draw up in detail the records of this trial and to deposit them in the archive of human reason for the prevention of future errors of similar kind.",["The stray \"p\" and \"jedero\" are OCR slips (\"jederzeit\"). Urtheil = \"judgment\"; täuschend = \"deceptive\" (trüglich in 2086 is also \"deceptive\"); Irrungen = \"errors\"; Acten = \"records\"."]],"krv-b-2105":["Su si\nii",["- The passage is OCR noise, not recognizable German; no translation is possible, and the characters are reproduced as they stand without supplying a reading."]],"krv-b-2106":["as\n2 > wer",["- The passage is illegible OCR residue; \"wer\" (\"who\") is the only possible German word, but no sentence can be recovered. Nothing has been supplied."]],"krv-b-2107":["TUIR Al err",["- Illegible OCR residue; no German text can be identified. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2108":["x Abl",["- Illegible OCR residue; \"Abl\" may be a fragment of a word (e.g., \"Ableitung\"), but this is conjecture and nothing has been supplied."]],"krv-b-2109":["t- 2K pG d Si + In 4 Fan u riy S 3 20 ; Ei y 11 r i ra s",["- Illegible OCR residue; no German text can be recovered. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2110":["moin en RIRI F ee 2 J are",["- Illegible OCR residue; no German text can be recovered. \"are\" and \"en\" are not usable as words here. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2111":["o ceu PER P nal Er = Rumi Heit a Bei BEIDEN ET — | Luhn e ne else",["- Illegible OCR residue. \"Heit\" (perhaps a suffix, as in \"-heit\") and \"Bei\"/\"BEIDEN\" (\"beiden\", \"both\") appear, but no sentence can be recovered. The sign | is carried over as it stands, though it may be OCR noise rather than the Academy Edition's line-break sign."]],"krv-b-2112":["pspriasg i Rd muH A ($ e Ab hi ii al 1 y",["- Illegible OCR residue; no German text can be recovered. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2113":["las IN i i Be ennio ige! Ih B de age 2",["- Illegible OCR residue; \"las\", \"Be\", \"de\" are not interpretable as words in context. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2114":["rim ch s lfte 8 r une su $",["- Illegible OCR residue; no German text can be recovered. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2115":["Aid g über l Kay dii Emine 8 9 :",["- Illegible OCR residue; \"über\" (\"over; about; on\") is the only recognizable word. Not translated further."]],"krv-b-2116":["r ch Un a den — a ra",["- Illegible OCR residue; \"den\" is perhaps the German article, but no sentence can be recovered. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2117":["„ ich 8 Hr",["- Illegible OCR residue; \"ich\" (\"I\") appears after an opening quotation mark, but no further text can be recovered. Not translated."]],"krv-b-2118":["See In mm SERER ih an u SA Tai sA dun wegen Wingate Do",["- Illegible OCR residue; \"wegen\" (\"because of; on account of\") is the only recognizable word. Not translated further."]],"krv-b-2119":["| Transcendental Doctrine of Method.",["The leading \"IT\" is OCR noise and is omitted; \"|\" is kept from the source. The line is the running title of the Methodenlehre."]],"krv-b-2120":["If I look at the sum total of all cognition of pure and speculative reason like a building, of which we at least have the idea in us, then I can say: in the transcendental Doctrine of Elements we have estimated the building material and determined for which building, of what height and strength, it suffices. To be sure it turned out that, although we had in mind a tower that was to reach to the heavens, the supply of materials sufficed only for a dwelling-house, which was just spacious and high enough for our business on the plane of experience to survey it; but that that bold undertaking had to fail from lack of material, without even reckoning on the confusion of tongues, which had inevitably to divide the workers over the plan and scatter them throughout the world, in order to build up separately, each according to his own design. Now it is a matter for us not so much of the materials as rather of the plan and, since we are warned not to venture it on an arbitrary blind design, which might perhaps exceed our whole faculty, yet nevertheless cannot well desist from the erection of a firm dwelling place, to make the estimate for a building in relation to the supply that is given to us and at the same time appropriate to our need. Thus by the transcendental Doctrine of Method I understand the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. With this aim we shall have to do with a discipline, a canon, an architectonic, finally a history of pure reason, and accomplish with a transcendental aim that which, under the name of a practical logic with regard to the use of the understanding in general, is sought in the schools but poorly accomplished, because, since general logic is restricted to no particular kind of cognition of the understanding (e.g., not to the pure), nor to certain objects, it, without borrowing information from other sciences, can do nothing more than set forth titles for possible methods and technical expressions, which one uses with regard to the systematic in all sorts of sciences, which make the apprentice acquainted in advance with names whose significance and use he is first of all to get to know in the future.",["Silent corrections: \"Art de\" to \"Art der\"; \"gew Gegenstände\" to \"gewisse Gegenstände\"; \"Bitten N schaften\" to \"anderen Wissenschaften\"; \"Me j thoden\" to \"Methoden\"; \"neu matischen\" to \"systematischen\". The running head \"466 Methodenlehre. 1. Hauptstück.\" is omitted, as are the stray \"i\" and \":\". Gebäude = \"building\"; Stoff = \"material\" and Materialien = \"materials\" (one English word for two German words); Vorrath = \"supply\".","\"sie zu übersehen\": \"sie\" could refer to Geschäfte or to Ebene (or Erfahrung); \"it\" is a choice, and the German is ambiguous.","\"im Sinne hatten\" = \"had in mind\" (Sinn). Absicht = \"aim\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Kenntnisse = \"information\"; Bedeutung = \"significance\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Ausdrücke = \"expressions\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Wissenschaften = \"sciences\".","\"der uns gegeben ... angemessen ist\": the relative \"der\" is taken as Vorrath; \"angemessen\" is left as in German."]],"krv-b-2121":["Of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nFirst Chapter.\nThe Discipline of Pure Reason.",["Hauptstück = \"Chapter\" (the glossary does not cover it)."]],"krv-b-2122":["Negative judgments, which are such not merely in logical form but also in content, are held in no particular esteem by the human thirst for knowledge; one even regards them as envious enemies of our drive for cognition, which strives incessantly toward extension, and it almost requires an apology to procure for them merely toleration, and still more to procure for them favor and high esteem. p",["- \"Wißbegierde\" = thirst for knowledge (literally \"desire to know\"); \"Erkenntnißtrieb\" = drive for cognition. \"Erweiterung\" = extension (Guyer-Wood list Ausdehnung = extension; here \"Erweiterung\" is also rendered \"extension\"; the two German words are not distinguished).","- \"Urtheile\" = judgments; \"Inhalte\" = content.","- OCR: \"inn\" corrected to \"in\"; \"Feind!\" corrected to \"Feinde\"; the trailing \"p\" is a stray OCR mark, left as it stands."]],"krv-b-2123":["One can, to be sure, express logically all propositions as one wishes negatively; but with regard to the content of our cognition in general, whether it is extended or restricted by a judgment, the negative ones have the peculiar business solely of keeping off error. Hence also negative propositions, which are supposed to keep off a false cognition, where yet an error is never possible, are indeed very true, but yet empty, i.e., not at all appropriate to their purpose, and for just that reason often laughable; like the proposition of that school orator, that Alexander could not have conquered any lands without an army. f",["- \"Sätze\" = propositions; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Zwecke\" = purpose; \"verneinenden\" rendered \"negative ones\" (same as \"negativ\").","- OCR: \"we\" read as \"wo\"; \"ausdrücken!\" read as a semicolon; \"leer!\" read as a comma. The trailing \"f\" is a stray footnote mark or OCR mark, left as it stands.","- \"beschränkt\" = restricted; \"Schranken\" elsewhere = limitations."]],"krv-b-2124":["But where the limitations of our possible cognition are very narrow, the stimulus to judging great, the illusion that presents itself very deceptive, and the disadvantage from error considerable, there the negative of instruction, which serves merely to guard us against errors, has still more importance than many a positive teaching through which our cognition could receive an increase. One calls the constraint through which the constant propensity to deviate from certain rules is restricted and finally eradicated, discipline. It is distinguished from culture, which is supposed to procure merely a skill, without annulling another, already present one in doing so. For the formation of a talent, which already of itself has an impulse toward expression, discipline will therefore make a negative contribution, but culture and doctrine a positive one.",["- \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Belehrung\" = teaching; \"Unterweisung\" = instruction (two German words, two English words); \"Doctrin\" = doctrine (Latinate, as printed).","- \"Schranken\" = limitations; \"Disciplin\" and \"Cultur\" kept as discipline and culture.","- OCR: \"negativen“)\" read as a negative with footnote mark; the footnote is printed below as krv-b-2127.","- \"Zwang\" = constraint; \"Hang\" = propensity."]],"krv-b-2125":["That temperament, likewise that talents that gladly allow themselves a free and unrestricted movement (such as imagination and wit) need a discipline in many respects, everyone will readily grant. But that reason, which properly has the duty of prescribing its discipline to all other strivings, should itself still need such a thing, may indeed seem strange; and in fact it has also escaped such a humiliation until now precisely because, given the solemnity and the thorough decorum with which it appears, no one could easily fall under the suspicion of a frivolous game with fancies instead of concepts and with words instead of things.",["- \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination; \"Einbildungen\" = fancies (a different word, plural); \"Begriffen\" = concepts; \"Absicht\" = respect here (\"in mancher Absicht\"), not \"aim.\"","- \"Sachen\" = things (as \"Dinge\" would also be); two German words, one English word."]],"krv-b-2126":["No critique of reason is needed in the empirical use, because its principles are subjected to a continual testing on the touchstone of experience; likewise also not in mathematics, where its concepts must immediately be exhibited *in concreto* in pure intuition, and everything groundless and arbitrary thereby becomes at once manifest. But where neither empirical nor pure intuition holds reason in a visible track, namely in its transcendental use according to mere concepts, there it needs so much a discipline that would tame its propensity toward extension beyond the narrow boundaries of possible experience and keep it from extravagance and error, that even the whole philosophy of pure reason has to do merely with this negative utility. Individual aberrations can through censure",["- \"Grundsätze\" = principles; \"Grenzen\" = boundaries; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Philosophie\" = philosophy (not Weltweisheit).","- \"darstellen\" = exhibit. OCR: \"so sehr!)\" read as \"so sehr\" with footnote mark 1 (footnote follows as krv-b-2127); hyphenation breaks repaired (\"Ver— nunft\", \"mög— lieber\" = möglicher).","- The sentence breaks off; it continues in krv-b-2127 after the footnote."]],"krv-b-2127":[") I know well that in the language of the schools one is accustomed to use the name of discipline as equivalent to that of instruction. But there are, on the other hand, so many other cases in which the former expression, as discipline, is carefully distinguished from the second, as teaching, and the nature of things also itself demands that the only fitting expressions be preserved for this distinction, that I wish one would never permit that word to be used in any but a negative meaning. and the causes of them be remedied through critique. But where, as in pure reason, a whole system of deceptions and illusory shows is encountered, which are well connected among themselves and united under common principles, there a quite peculiar and indeed negative legislation seems to be required, which under the name of a discipline, from the nature of reason and of the objects of its [lacuna in the text] erect, before which no false rationalizing illusion can stand, but must betray itself at once notwithstanding all the grounds of its palliation.",["- The text mixes the footnote (to \"Disciplin\") with the main-text continuation of the sentence from krv-b-2126 (\"und den Ursachen derselben ... abgeholfen werden\"); both kept in the order given.","- \"Zucht\" = discipline; \"Unterweisung\" = instruction; \"Belehrung\" = teaching; \"Ausdrücke\" = expressions; \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"vernünftelnder Schein\" = rationalizing illusion.","- The passage as given breaks off after \"Gegenstände ihres\" and lacks the verb of the relative clause; I have marked this and not supplied the missing words (\"Ausübung\"/\"Erkenntniß\" or the like). \"errichte\" appears without its auxiliary or subject, so the clause is corrupt or cut.","- OCR: \"wi\" = wie; \"Blend werken\" = Blendwerken."]],"krv-b-2128":["Is",["- Stray OCR fragment (\"Is\"); no translatable text."]],"krv-b-2129":["It is, however, well to note: that in this second main part of the transcendental critique I direct the discipline of pure reason not at the content but merely at the method of cognition from pure reason. The former has already happened in the doctrine of elements. But the use of reason has so much that is similar, upon whatever object",["- \"Elementarlehre\" = doctrine of elements; \"Vernunftgebrauch\" = use of reason; \"Gegenstand\" = object.","- The sentence breaks off here and continues in krv-b-2130."]],"krv-b-2130":["it may be applied, and is yet, insofar as it is to be transcendental, at the same time so essentially distinguished from everything else, that without the warning negative doctrine of a discipline specially set up for it the errors cannot be prevented that must necessarily arise from an unsuitable following",["- Initial \"1\" is a stray OCR mark. \"Negativlehre\" = negative doctrine; \"Disciplin\" = discipline.","- The sentence breaks off and continues in krv-b-2131."]],"krv-b-2131":["of such methods, which otherwise suit reason, but only not here¹), necessarily arise.",["- \"si\" is a stray OCR mark, omitted. The footnote mark after \"anpassen\" is retained as \"¹)\"; the footnote itself is not in the passage.","- \"entspringen müssen\" renders the verb of the preceding sentence; \"arise\" appears again here as in the German."]],"krv-b-2132":["Of the first chapter,\nFirst Section.",["- \"Hauptstück\" = chapter."]],"krv-b-2133":["The discipline of pure reason in the dogmatic",["- Title breaks off; continues in krv-b-2134 (\"Gebrauche\" = use)."]],"krv-b-2134":["use.",[]],"krv-b-2135":["Mathematics gives the most brilliant example of a pure reason that happily extends itself of its own accord without the assistance of experience. Examples are contagious, above all for the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself to have the very same luck in other cases | that has fallen to its share in one case. Hence pure reason in the transcendental use hopes to be able to extend itself just as happily and thoroughly as it has succeeded in doing in the mathematical, if | it applies above all the same method there that has been of such evident benefit here. It is therefore very much to our interest to know:",["- \"Vermögen\" = faculty; \"Gebrauche\" = use; \"Erweiterung\" = extension; \"Methode\" = method.","- The | signs are the Academy Edition's line/page breaks, kept in place. OCR: \"reine :\" and \"augeni scheinlichem\" repaired.","- The sentence ends with a colon and continues in krv-b-2136."]],"krv-b-2136":["whether the method of arriving at apodictic certainty, which in the latter science one calls mathematical, is one and the same with that with which one seeks the very same certainty in philosophy, and which there | would have to be called dogmatic.",["- \"Wissenschaft\" = science; \"Gewißheit\" = certainty; \"Philosophie\" = philosophy.","- The | marks the Academy Edition's break, kept in place."]],"krv-b-2137":["Philosophical cognition is cognition of reason from concepts, mathematical cognition from the construction of concepts. But to construct a concept means: to exhibit *a priori* the intuition corresponding to it. For the construction of a concept a non-empirical intuition is therefore required, which consequently, as intuition, is a single object, but nonetheless, as the construction of a concept (of a universal representation), must express in the representation universal validity for all possible intuitions that belong under the same concept. Thus I construct a triangle by exhibiting the object corresponding to this concept either through mere imagination in pure, or, following that, also on paper in empirical intuition, but in both cases completely *a priori*, without having borrowed the pattern for it from any experience. The single figure drawn is empirical and yet serves to express the concept without damage to its universality, because in this empirical intuition one looks always only at the action of the construction of the concept, to which many determinations, e.g., of the magnitude, the sides and the angles, are quite indifferent, and thus abstracts from these differences, which do not alter the concept of the triangle.",["- \"Vernunfterkenntniß\" = cognition of reason; \"Construction\" = construction (Latinate); \"darstellen\" = exhibit; \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" both = object (\"ein einzelnes Object\" and \"den ... Gegenstand\").","- \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations; \"Größe\" = magnitude; \"Einbildung\" = imagination (here the faculty is named by \"Einbildung,\" not \"Einbildungskraft\").","- \"a priori\" attaches to \"darstellen\"/\"construiren\" in the German as it does in the English; \"Allgemeingültigkeit\" = universal validity. Stray OCR quotation mark before \"mögliche\" omitted."]],"krv-b-2138":["Philosophical cognition therefore considers the particular only in the universal, mathematical cognition the universal in the particular, indeed even in the single, yet nevertheless *a priori* and by means of reason, so that, as this single is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, so too the object of the concept, to which this single corresponds only as its schema, must be thought as universally determined.",["- \"das Besondere\" = the particular; \"das Einzelne\" = the single; \"Allgemeine\" = the universal; \"Bedingungen\" = conditions; \"bestimmt\" = determined; \"Gegenstand\" = object.","- \"gleichwohl doch a priori und vermittelst der Vernunft\": \"a priori\" could attach to the preceding \"considers\" (as here) or to the whole clause; kept as ambiguous as the German."]],"krv-b-2139":["In this form, then, consists the essential difference of these two kinds of rational cognition, and it does not rest on the difference of their matter or objects. Those who thought to distinguish philosophy from mathematics by saying of the former that it | has merely quality, but the latter only quantity, as object, have taken the effect for the cause. The form of mathematical cognition is the cause that this can go solely to *quanta*. For only the concept of magnitudes can be constructed, that is, presented *a priori* in intuition, but qualities cannot be exhibited in any |",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"in keiner\" followed by a stray OCR \"u\" and \"|\"); left unfinished. The continuation follows in the next passage.","Vernunfterkenntniß = \"rational cognition\"; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstände / Object = \"objects\" (both).","Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; darlegen = \"present\"; darstellen = \"exhibit\"; Materie = matter.","The running head and the stray OCR letters (N, í) are omitted; the \"|\" is the page break."]],"krv-b-2140":["than empirical intuition. Hence a rational cognit-",["Begins mid-sentence (\"[…] als empirischen Anschauung darstellen\"); the preceding \"in keiner [anderen]\" is in the previous passage and is not supplied. \"Ie\" is OCR debris.","Breaks off in the middle of the word Vernunfterkennt-(niß); the English is split accordingly, and the next passage opens with \"-ion\"."]],"krv-b-2141":["-ion of these be possible only through concepts. Thus no one can take an intuition corresponding to the concept of reality from anywhere other than experience, but never become a partaker of it *a priori* from himself and before empirical consciousness of it. The conical shape one will be able to make intuitive without any empirical assistance, merely according to the concept, but the color of this cone will have to be given beforehand in one experience or another. The concept of a cause in general I can in no way exhibit in intuition other than in an example that experience puts in my hand, and so forth. Moreover, philosophy deals just as much with magnitudes as mathematics does, e.g., with totality, infinity, and so forth. Mathematics also occupies itself with the difference of lines and surfaces as spaces of different quality, with the continuity of extension as a quality of them. But although in such cases they have a common object, the way of treating it through reason is nevertheless entirely different in philosophical and in mathematical consideration. The former holds merely to universal concepts, the latter can accomplish nothing with the mere concept but hastens at once to intuition, in which it considers the concept *in concreto*, but not empirically, rather merely in one that it exhibits *a priori*, that is, has constructed, and in which that which follows from the universal conditions | of construction must also hold universally of the object of the constructed concept.",["Begins mid-word (\"niß\" = end of Vernunfterkenntniß); \"I nich\" is OCR debris, and \"kann eine\" stands in the previous passage.","anschauend machen: \"make intuitive\" renders the participle anschauend, which differs from anschaulich (\"intuitive\").","allgemein = \"universal\" throughout (also Allgemeinheit); überhaupt = \"in general\"; Gegenstand and Object both = \"object\".","Ausdehnung = extension; Betrachtung = consideration; Art = \"way\"; Beihülfe = \"assistance\"; Qualität = quality.","\"theilhaftig werden\" = \"become a partaker\"; the \"|\" in \"Bedingun-|gen\" is the page break."]],"krv-b-2142":["Let one give a philosopher the concept of a triangle and let him find out in his way how the sum of its angles may stand to the right one. He now has nothing but the concept of a figure that is enclosed in three straight lines, and in it the concept of just as many angles. Now he may reflect on this concept as long as he wants, he will produce nothing new. He can dissect the concept of the straight line or of an angle or of the number three and make it distinct, but not arrive at other properties that do not lie in these concepts at all. But let the geometer take up this question. He begins at once by constructing a triangle. Because he knows that two right angles together amount to exactly as much as all adjacent angles that can be drawn from one point on a straight line, together, he extends one side of his triangle and gets two adjacent angles that together are equal to two right ones. Now he divides the exterior one of these angles by drawing a line parallel to the opposite side of the triangle, and sees that here an exterior adjacent angle arises that is equal to an interior one, and so forth. In such a way he arrives, through a chain of inferences, always guided by intuition, at the fully evident and at the same time universal solution of the question.",["zum rechten: \"to the right one\" leaves open the noun (Winkel) as the German does.","Eigenschaften = \"properties\" (Beschaffenheit, not used here, is also \"property\" in the glossary); zergliedern = \"dissect\"; deutlich = distinct.","berührende Winkel = \"adjacent angles\" (literally \"touching\"); Schlüsse = \"inferences\"; Auflösung = \"solution\".","Nachdenken = \"reflect\" (Überlegung = \"reflection\" is not the word here)."]],"krv-b-2143":["But mathematics constructs not merely magnitudes (*quanta*), as in geometry, but also mere magnitude (*quantitatem*), as in algebra, whereby it entirely abstracts from the constitution of the object that is to be thought in accordance with such a concept of magnitude. It then chooses for itself a certain designation of all constructions of magnitudes in general (numbers), such as addition, subtraction, and so forth, extraction of roots; and after it has also designated the universal concept of magnitudes according to their different relations, it exhibits all treatment that is generated and altered through magnitude, according to certain universal rules, in intuition; where one magnitude is to be divided by another, it puts together the characters of both according to the designating form of division, and so forth, and thus by means of a symbolic construction, just as well as geometry by means of an ostensive or geometrical one (of the objects themselves), arrives at that to which discursive cognition by means of mere concepts could never arrive. What may be the cause of this so different situation in which two artists of reason find themselves, of whom one takes his way according to concepts, the other according to intuitions that he exhibits *a priori* in accordance with the concepts? According to the transcendental principles [Grundlehren] set forth above, this cause is clear. What matters here is not analytic propositions, which can be generated through mere dissection of concepts (in this the philosopher would without doubt have the advantage over his rival), but synthetic ones, and indeed such as are to be cognized *a priori*. For I am not to look to that which I actually think in my concept of the triangle (this is nothing further than the mere definition); rather I am to go beyond it to properties that do not lie in this concept but nevertheless belong to it. Now this is not possible otherwise than by my determining my object according to the conditions either of empirical intuition or of pure intuition. The former would yield only an empirical proposition (through measuring its angles), which would contain no universality, still less neces- | sity, and of such there is no question at all. But the second procedure is the mathematical and indeed here the geometrical construction, by means of which I in a pure intuition",["Breaks off mid-sentence (\"in einer reinen Anschauung\"); the continuation is in the next passage. OCR debris (\"i t\", \"pi\", \"fol\") silently corrected to \"dieses ist nichts weiter als\", \"soll ich\", \"nach den Bedingungen\".","Grundlehren: \"principles [Grundlehren]\" is marked because Grundsatz = principle in the glossary; here the word is Grundlehren (doctrine = Lehre).","Größe = magnitude (quanta / quantitatem retained in Latin); Beschaffenheit = constitution; Sätze = propositions; Bezeichnung = designation.","Vernunftkünstler = \"artists of reason\"; Ursache = cause; Lage = \"situation\"; Verfahren = \"procedure\"; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Allgemeinheit = universality.","\"Nebenbuhler\" = \"rival\"; \"Behandlung\" = \"treatment\"; \"ostensiven\" kept as \"ostensive.\""]],"krv-b-2144":["just as in the empirical one the manifold that belongs to the schema of a triangle in general, hence to its concept, add, whereby universal synthetic propositions must indeed be constructed.",["Begins mid-sentence; the subject (\"ich\") of hinzusetze is in the previous passage and is not supplied. \"in der empirischen\" lacks its noun, as in the German.","hinzusetze is subjunctive; \"add\" is left bare. Mannigfaltige = manifold; allerdings = \"indeed\"; Schema = schema; überhaupt = \"in general\".","OCR debris (1, F, !, I) omitted."]],"krv-b-2145":["I would therefore philosophize in vain about the triangle, that is, | reflect discursively, without getting any further thereby than to the mere definition, with which, however, I would properly have to begin. There is, to be sure, a transcendental synthesis from mere concepts, which again succeeds only for the philosopher, but which never concerns more than a thing in general, under what conditions its perception could belong to | possible experience. But in mathematical problems there is no question of this and of existence in general at all, but of the properties of objects in themselves, solely insofar as these are combined with the concept of them.",["Der Ort of the \"|\" is the page break; the words discursiv and möglichen are split in the source, and the \"|\" has been placed at the break without splitting the English words.","Eigenschaften = \"properties\"; Aufgaben = \"problems\"; Existenz = existence; an sich selbst = \"in themselves\" (an sich); Wahrnehmung = perception; Synthesis = synthesis.","Gegenstände = objects; verbunden = \"combined\" (Verbindung = combination).","\"philosophiren\" = \"philosophize\"; \"nachdenken\" = \"reflect.\""]],"krv-b-2146":["In the example cited we have sought only to make distinct what great difference is to be encountered between the discursive use of reason according to concepts and the intuitive use through the construction of concepts. Now it is naturally asked what the cause is that makes such a twofold use of reason necessary, and by what conditions one can cognize whether only the first or also the second takes place.",["frägts sich = \"it is asked\" (literally \"it asks itself\"); \"zwiefachen\" = \"twofold.\"","Vernunftgebrauch = \"use of reason\"; erkennen = cognize; stattfinde = \"takes place\"; Ursache = cause; nothwendig = necessary; deutlich = distinct.","OCR debris (\"3\", \"?\") omitted."]],"krv-b-2147":["All our cognition nevertheless relates in the end to possible intuitions; for through these alone is an object given. Now a concept *a priori* (a non-empirical concept) contains either already a pure intuition within itself, and then it can be constructed;",["Breaks off after a semicolon; a stray \"a\" at the end is OCR debris and is dropped. The continuation is in the next passage.","Anschauung = intuition; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object; rein = pure; Begriff = concept."]],"krv-b-2148":["or nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions, which are not given *a priori*",["- Preserved the German's ambiguity in \"*a priori* nicht gegeben sind\": the adverb is placed as in the German."]],"krv-b-2149":["given, and then one may well judge synthetically and",[]],"krv-b-2150":["*a priori* by means of it, but only discursively, according to concepts, and never) in-",["- The OCR reads \"niemals!)\"; I took the \"!\" as a misreading, since the closing parenthesis answers one opened in the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-2151":["tuitively, through the construction of the concept. Now of all intuition none is given *a priori* except the mere form of appearances, space and time; and a concept of these",["- Stray \"is\" removed as an OCR slip. The sentence is joined to the previous one across the passage boundary (\"in-/tuitiv\")."]],"krv-b-2152":["as *quantis* can",["- Passage 2151 as given ends \"...und ein Begriff von diesen\"; I have moved the clause break accordingly. I read the final clause \"als quantis\" as continuing in 2153."]],"krv-b-2153":["Correction: see note.",["- Check: 2152 and 2153 above are misassigned. The German of 2152 is \"und ein Begriff von diesen\" and of 2153 is \"als quantis läßt sich entweder zugleich mit der Qualität derselben (ihre\". See the corrected renderings in the next entries."]],"krv-b-2154":["figure), or also merely their quantity (the mere synthesis of the homo-",["- Breaks off mid-word (gleich-/artig); \"homo-\" renders the hyphenation. Gestalt = figure (not in glossary)."]],"krv-b-2155":["geneous manifold) *a priori* in intuition through number, i.e., construct. But the matter of appearances, through which to us",["- Stray OCR \"do\" before \"d. i.\" dropped. darstellen = exhibit (Guyer-Wood usage; not in glossary); construiren = construct. Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2156":["things in space and time are given, can only in per-",["- Word order of the German retained; \"wodurch uns\" (end of previous passage) and \"Dinge ... gegeben werden\" are joined only by the passage break."]],"krv-b-2157":["ception, hence *a posteriori*, be represented. The only concept,",["- Wahrnehmung = perception (hyphenated across the passage break)."]],"krv-b-2158":["that represents *a priori* this empirical content of appearances, is the concept of a thing in general, and the synthetic cognition of the same *a priori* can deliver nothing further than the mere rule of the synthesis of that which perception may give *a posteriori*, but never the intuition of the real object *a priori*, because this must necessarily be empirical. Synthetic propositions that go to things in general, whose intuition cannot be given *a priori* at all, are transcendental. Accordingly transcendental propositions can never be given through construction of concepts, but only according to concepts *a priori*. They contain merely the rule according to which a certain synthetic unity of that which cannot *a priori*",["- Gehalt = content (Inhalt is also \"content\" in the glossary). Gegenstand = object. \"von demselben\" rendered \"of the same.\"","- \"auf Dinge überhaupt ... gehen\" rendered \"go to\"; \"a priori\" in \"durch Construction ... sondern nur nach Begriffen a priori\" could attach to \"given\" or to \"concepts\", and is left as ambiguous as the German.","- Line numbers and a stray punctuation mark in the OCR (\"; \") silently dropped; \"laassen\" corrected to \"lassen\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2159":["be represented intuitively (of perceptions), is to be sought empirically. But they cannot exhibit a single one of their concepts *a priori*",["- anschaulich = intuitive(ly). OCR \"geks sucht ... fol\" read as \"gesucht ... soll\"; \"keinen einzigen\" = \"a single one.\""]],"krv-b-2160":["in any case, but do this only *a posteriori*,",["- None beyond the above."]],"krv-b-2161":["by means of experience, which according to those synthetic principles first of",["- Grundsätze = principles. \"allererst\" is split across the passage break (aller-/erst): \"first of / all.\""]],"krv-b-2162":["all becomes possible.",["- See the previous note on allererst."]],"krv-b-2163":["If one is to judge synthetically of a concept, then one must go out from this concept and indeed to the intuition in which it is given. For if one remained with what is con-",["- \"von einem Begriffe ... urtheilen\" rendered \"judge ... of a concept,\" which in English could also be read \"about\"; the German preposition-less genitive/dative construction is kept as plain as possible. Line number \"30\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2164":["tained in the concept, then the judgment would be merely analytic and an explanation of the",["- Erklärung = explanation (also \"definition\" in the glossary)."]],"krv-b-2165":["thought according to that which is actually contained in it. I can",["- wirklich = actually (Wirklichkeit = actuality in the glossary)."]],"krv-b-2166":["but go from the concept to the pure or empirical intuition corresponding to it, in order to consider it in the same *in concreto* and to cognize, what pertains to the object of it, *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The former is the rational and mathematical cognition through the construction of the concept, the second the merely empirical (mechanical) cognition, which can never give necessary and apodictic propositions. Thus I could dissect my empirical concept of gold without thereby gaining anything further than to be able to enumerate everything that I actually think with this word, whereby a logical improvement does indeed go on in my cognition, but no increase or addition is acquired. But I take the matter that occurs under this name, and make perceptions with it, which will hand me various synthetic, but empirical propositions. The mathematical concept of a triangle I would construct, i.e., give *a priori* in intuition, and in this way get a synthetic, but rational cognition. But if the transcendental concept of a reality, substance, force etc. is given to me, then it designates neither an empirical nor a pure intuition, but solely the synthesis of empirical intuitions (which thus cannot be given *a priori*); and from it, since the synthesis cannot go out *a priori* to the intuition that corresponds to it, no determining synthetic proposition, but only a principle of the synthesis*) of possible empirical intuitions can arise. Thus a transcendental proposition is a synthetic cognition of reason according to mere concepts and consequently discursive, since through it all synthetic unity of empirical cognition first becomes possible, but no intuition is thereby given *a priori*.",["- OCR readings corrected silently: \"empiri- [den\" = empirischen; \"ie)\" = ich; \"Mfo\" = Also; line number \"35\" dropped. The footnote marker *) is kept; the footnote text appears in the following passage.","- Beginning \"aber\" follows \"Ich kann\" of the previous passage; the passage's first line is a continuation of that sentence. Erwägen = consider; zergliedern = dissect; Vernunfterkenntniß = cognition of reason (rationale = rational); bestimmend = determining; Realität = reality; Kraft = force.","- \"Wahrnehmungen anstellen\" rendered \"make perceptions.\" \"Anschauung ... bezeichnet\" = designates. The passage's page citation (B 749–750?) is uncertain in the source."]],"krv-b-2167":["Thus there is a twofold use of reason, which in spite of the universality of cognition and its generation *a priori*, which they have in common, is nevertheless very different in progression, and indeed for this reason, because in the appearance, through which all objects are giv-",["- Fortgang = progression; Gegenstände = objects. Breaks off mid-word (gege-/ben); the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2168":["en to us, there are two pieces: the form of intuition (space and time), which can be cognized and determined completely *a priori*, and the matter (the physical) or the content, which signifies a something that is encountered in space and time, hence contains an existence and to the\n\n*) By means of the concept of cause I actually go out from the empirical concept of an occurrence (where something happens), but not to the intuition that exhibits the concept of cause *in concreto*, but to the time-conditions in general, which might be found in experience in accordance with the concept of cause. I proceed therefore merely according to concepts and cannot proceed through construction of concepts, because the concept is a rule of the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions and thus cannot be given *a priori*.",["- The OCR placed the footnote before the main text; I have restored the main text first and given the footnote after it. \"Beitbedingungen\" corrected to Zeitbedingungen. Begebenheit = occurrence (Ereignis = event).","- Stücke = pieces; Gehalt = content; Dasein = existence (Existenz also = existence). \"hence contains an existence and to the\" breaks off before \"sensation corresponds\" in the next passage (und der / Empfindung correspondirt)."]],"krv-b-2169":["sensation corresponds. In regard to the latter, which can never be given in a determinate way otherwise than empirically, we can have nothing *a priori* except indeterminate concepts of the synthesis of possible sensations, insofar as they belong to the unity of apperception (in a possible experience). In regard to the former we can determine our concepts in intuition *a priori*, in that we create the objects themselves in space and time through uniform synthesis, in that we consider them merely as *quanta*. That is called the use of reason according to concepts, in which we can do nothing further than to bring appearances, as to real content, under concepts, which thereupon can be determined in no other way than empirically, i.e., *a posteriori* (but in accordance with those concepts as rules of an empirical synthesis); this is the use of reason through construction of concepts, in which these, since they already go to an intuition *a priori*, also for that very reason",["- OCR \"unio fere\" read as \"unsere\"; stray \"w\" before \"darauf\" dropped.","- \"Jener ... dieser\" rendered \"That ... this,\" leaving open which of the two members (letzteren/erstern) each refers to. Gleichförmig = uniform (gleichartig = homogeneous earlier). Empfindung = sensation; Vernunftgebrauch = use of reason.","- The passage ends before the text's \"a priori\" is completed; the next passage begins with \"priori\"."]],"krv-b-2170":["*priori* and without any empirical *data* can be determined or given in pure intuition. To consider everything that is there (a thing in space or time), whether and how far it is a *quantum* or not, that an existence in it or a lack must be represented, how far this something (which fills space or time) is a first *substratum* or mere determination, has a relation of its existence to something else as cause or effect and finally stands isolated or in reciprocal dependence with others with regard to existence, to consider the possibility of this existence, the actuality and necessity, or the opposites of them: all this belongs to the cognition of reason from concepts, which is called philosophical. But to determine an intuition *a priori* in space (figure), to divide time (duration), or merely to cognize the universal of the synthesis of one and the same in time and space and the magnitude of an intuition in general arising from it (number), that is a business of reason through construction of concepts and is called mathematical.\n- The first word begins with \"priori,\" a continuation of \"a priori\" from the previous passage. OCR \"o\" in \"bestimmt o gegeben\" read as \"oder\"; \"set\" read as \"sei\"; \"ma-/ii sch\" read as \"mathematisch\"; line number \"25\" dropped.\n- Philosophisch = philosophical (Weltweisheit = philosophy). Dasein = existence; Möglichkeit/Wirklichkeit/Nothwendigkeit = possibility/actuality/necessity; Größe = magnitude; Zahl = number; Dauer = duration; Vernunftgeschäfte = business of reason.",[]],"krv-b-2171":["The great good fortune that reason makes by means of mathematics, brings about quite naturally the presumption that it,",["- Breaks off mid-sentence. \"Glück machen\" = \"makes ... good fortune\"; Vermuthung = presumption; \"zuwege bringen\" = bring about."]],"krv-b-2172":["not itself, yet its method will succeed also outside the field of magnitudes, in that it brings all its concepts to intuitions, which it can give *a priori*, and whereby it becomes, so to speak, master over nature; whereas pure philosophy with discursive concepts *a priori* bungles about in nature, without being able to make the reality of the same *a priori* intuitive and precisely thereby authenticated. Also it seems that the masters in this art are not at all lacking in this confidence in themselves, and the commonwealth in great expectations of their skill, should they once concern themselves with this. For since they | have hardly ever philosophized about their mathematics (a difficult business), the specific difference of the one use of reason from the other does not come at all into their sense and thoughts. Current rules, used empirically, which they borrow from common reason, then count for them in place of axioms. Whence the concepts of space and time, with which they occupy themselves (as the only original *quanta*)¹, may come, is of no concern to them at all; and just so it seems to them to be useless to investigate the origin of pure concepts of the understanding and herewith also the domain of their validity, but only to make use of them. In all this they do quite right, if only they do not overstep their assigned boundary, namely that of nature. But thus they unnoticed stray from the field of sensibility onto the uncertain ground of pure and even transcendental concepts, where the ground (*instabilis tellus, innabilis unda*) permits them neither to stand nor to swim, and only fleeting steps let themselves be taken, of which time preserves not the slightest trace, whereas their² course in mathematics makes a highway, which even the latest posterity can tread with confidence.",["- \"ihr selbst … ihrer Methode\": datives with gelingen; \"not itself, yet its method\" keeps the elliptical construction. \"a priori anschauend\" rendered \"*a priori* intuitive\"; the adverbial attachment is left open.","- \"das gemeine Wesen\" = \"the commonwealth\" (idiom; Wesen elsewhere \"being\"). \"in Sinn und Gedanken\": Sinn rendered \"sense\" per glossary.","- \"Geschäftel\" in the OCR silently corrected to \"Geschäfte\"; \"|!\" corrected to \"|\"; stray line numbers and OCR debris omitted.","- Footnote markers ¹ and ² are as in the passage; the footnote texts are not part of the selection. Verbs: Gebrauch = use; Grenze = boundary; Quantis = *quanta*."]],"krv-b-2173":["Since we have made it our duty to determine the boundaries of pure reason in transcendental use precisely and with certainty, but this kind of endeavor has the peculiarity about it, notwithstanding the most emphatic and clearest warnings, of still always letting itself be put off by hope, before one entirely gives up the plan of getting, beyond boundaries of experiences, into the charming regions of the intellectual: so it is necessary to take away, as it were, still the last anchor of a fanciful hope and to show that the following of the mathematical method in this kind of cognition cannot create the slightest advantage, unless it were this, to uncover its own bare spots all the more distinctly: that geometry and philosophy are two entirely different things, although they offer each other the hand in natural science, hence that the procedure of the one can never be imitated by the other. The thoroughness of mathematics rests on definitions, axioms, demonstrations. I will content myself with showing: that none of these pieces in the sense in which the mathematician takes them, can be accomplished by the",["- Meßkunst = \"geometry\" (Meßkünstler, in the next passage, = \"geometer\"). Mathematik = mathematics.","- Art stands for both \"kind of endeavor\" (Art der Bestrebung) and \"kind of cognition\" (Art Erkenntniß). Erkenntniß = cognition.","- \"das Besondere an sich hat\": \"has the peculiarity about it\". \"Blößen ihrer selbst\": \"its own bare spots\"; the antecedent of \"ihrer\" (the method or the cognition) is left open as in the German.","- \"Anschlag\" = plan; \"Stücke\" = pieces; \"Gründlichkeit\" = thoroughness; \"Erfahrungen\" is plural in the German. The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"von der\" at the end)."]],"krv-b-2174":["philosophy can be accomplished, nor imitated; that the geometer according to his method brings about in philosophy nothing but houses of cards, the philosopher according to his own in the share of mathe-",["- The sentence begins in the preceding passage (\"von der\"); the English opens at \"Philosophie\" as given. \"noch\" is rendered \"nor\", reflecting the German; the passage breaks off mid-word (\"Ma-\").","- Meßkünstler = \"geometer\"; Kartengebäude = \"houses of cards\"; Antheil = \"share\"."]],"krv-b-2175":["matics can only arouse a chatter, although philosophy consists precisely in this, to know his boundaries, and even the mathematician, if his talent is not perhaps already bounded by nature and restricted to his own subject, cannot reject the warnings of philosophy, nor set himself above them. | 1. On definitions. To define is supposed, as the expression itself gives it, properly to signify only as much as to exhibit originally the exhaustive concept of a thing within its boundaries†. According to such a demand an empirical concept cannot be defined at all, but only *explicated*. For since we have in it only some",["- \"seine Grenzen zu kennen\": \"seine\" cannot agree with feminine Philosophie; rendered \"his\", left open (philosopher or \"one\").","- \"ausführlich\" = \"exhaustive\" (Ausführlichkeit = \"exhaustiveness\"); \"darstellen\" = \"exhibit\"; \"explicirt\" = \"*explicated*\"; Ausdruck = expression; bedeuten = signify; kennen = know.","- Footnote marker \"*)\" in the OCR rendered \"†\"; the footnote text is in the next passage. Fach = \"subject\".","- The passage begins mid-word (\"-thematik\") and breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2176":["marks of a certain kind of objects of the senses, it is never certain whether under the word that designates the same object one does not think at one time more, at another time fewer marks of it. Thus one person in the concept of gold may think, besides the weight, the color, the tenacity, also the property that it does not rust,",["- Merkmale = marks; Eigenschaft = \"property\" (also used for Beschaffenheit, \"constitution; property\" in the glossary); Art = kind.","- \"Gegenstände der Sinne\": the German Sinne is plural, \"senses\".","- The \"$\" and \"—\" in the OCR (\"Ge— $ wichte\") are debris, silently corrected."]],"krv-b-2177":["think, the other perhaps know nothing of it. One makes use of certain marks only as long as they are sufficient for distinguishing;",["- \"wissen\" rendered \"know\" (not kennen, which is \"know; be acquainted with\")."]],"krv-b-2178":["new observations, on the other hand, remove some and add some,",["- \"Bemerkungen\" is rendered \"observations\", the same word as for Beobachtung; both German words lie behind this one English word."]],"krv-b-2179":["the concept thus never stands between secure boundaries. And what should it serve, also, to define such a concept, since, if e.g. water and its properties are in question, one will not at that stop- |\n\n† Exhaustiveness signifies the clarity and sufficiency of the marks; boundaries the precision that there are no more of them than belong to the exhaustive concept; originally, however, that this determination of boundary not be derived from somewhere and thus still require a proof, which would make the putative definition incapable of standing at the head of all judgments about an ob-",["- The main text breaks mid-word (\"auf-\"; the completion \"halten wird\" is in the next passage, after the end of the footnote). The footnote also breaks mid-word (\"Gegen-\"). \"will not … stop-\" translates \"nicht auf-\" with the auxiliary \"wird\" falling in the next passage.","- Ausführlichkeit = exhaustiveness; Grenzen = boundaries; Erklärung = \"definition\" here (glossary: explanation; definition; declaration); Beweis = proof; Urtheile = judgments.","- Footnote marker given as †."]],"krv-b-2180":["ject, to stand.\n\nping will, what one thinks with the word water, but proceeds to experiments, and the word with the few marks that adhere to it is supposed to constitute only a designation and not a concept of the thing, hence the so-called definition is nothing other than determination of a word. Second, also, to speak precisely, no concept given *a priori* can be defined, e.g. substance, cause, right, equity etc. For I can never be certain that the distinct representation of a (still confusedly) given concept has been developed exhaustively, except when I know that the same is adequate to the object. But since the concept of it, as it is given, can contain many obscure representations, which we pass over in the analysis, although we always need them in the application: so the exhaustiveness of the analysis of my concept is always doubtful and can be made presumable only through many fitting examples, but never apodictically certain. Instead of the expression definition I would rather use that of exposition, which always still remains cautious, and with which the critic can let it count to a certain degree and yet still have misgivings because of the exhaustiveness. Since, then, neither concepts given empirically nor *a priori* can be defined, there remain none other than arbitrarily thought ones, on which one can try this artifice. My concept I can in such a case always define; for I must surely know what I have wanted to think, since I myself have deliberately made it, and it has been given to me neither through the nature of the understanding nor through experience, but I cannot say that I have thereby defined a true object. For if the concept rests on empirical conditions, e.g. a ship's clock, then the object and its possibility are not yet given through this arbitrary concept; from it I do not even know whether it has an object at all, and my definition can better be called a declaration (of my project) than a definition of an object. Thus there remain no other concepts that are fit for defining than those that contain an arbitrary synthesis, which can be constructed *a priori*, hence only mathematics has definitions. For the object that it thinks it also exhibits *a priori* in intuition, and this can certainly contain neither more nor less than the concept, because",["- The opening \"ject, to stand.\" completes the footnote of the previous passage (\"Gegen-\" / \"stand zu stehen\"); the main text then resumes mid-word (\"halten wird\", the second half of \"auf-halten\"). The English \"-ping will\" is a makeshift for this split.","- Erklärung = \"definition\" except where set against \"declaration\" (Declaration) in the same clause; Definition and Erklärung are thus both \"definition\"; Exposition = \"exposition\". Ausführlichkeit = exhaustiveness; Zergliederung = \"analysis\"; Anwendung = application.","- \"gelten lassen\" = \"let it count\" (\"sie\" = Exposition); Bedenken tragen = \"have misgivings\"; vorsetzlich = \"deliberately\"; Kunststück = \"artifice\"; \"überall\" = \"at all\".","- \"weder empirisch, noch a priori gegebene Begriffe\" kept ambiguous: \"neither concepts given empirically nor *a priori*\". \"als wenn\" = \"except when\".","- OCR \"Mfo\" silently corrected to \"Also\"; \"et c.\" to \"etc.\""]],"krv-b-2181":["through the definition the concept of the object was given originally, i.e. without deriving the definition from anything. The deut",["- The passage breaks off mid-word (\"deut\", presumably \"deutlich\"); left as it stands, not completed. OCR \"en\" at the start is a stray fragment and omitted.","- Erklärung = \"definition\" (see previous note); \"ursprünglich\" = \"originally\"."]],"krv-b-2182":["...sche language has for the expressions *Exposition*, *Explication*, *Declaration* and *Definition* nothing more than the one word expla—",["Fragment: it begins in mid-word (\"...sche Sprache\") and breaks off at \"Erklä—\", the first part of *Erklärung*. I have rendered \"expla—\" so that the next passage's \"rung\" can be rendered \"nation\".","The four Latinate words are mentioned expressions. I italicized them as foreign words; the OCR does not show the type.","*Ausdrücke* = expressions. *Erklärung* is glossed \"explanation; definition; declaration\".","The running head and page number are omitted."]],"krv-b-2183":["nation; and therefore we must already from the strictness of the demand, since",["\"rung\" completes *Erklärung* from the previous passage: \"explanation\".","*Forderung* = demand.","The main clause (\"müssen wir ... von der Strenge der Forderung ... etwas ablassen\") is split by the *da*-clause and runs into the next passage. I kept the German order."]],"krv-b-2184":["we namely refused to philosophical explanations the honorific name of definition, desist somewhat and want to restrict this whole remark",["*Erklärungen* = explanations; *Definition* = definition. The same German word *Erklärung* is glossed for both \"explanation\" and \"definition\", so the distinction is lost in English.","*ablassen* (with *von*, in the preceding passage) = desist. *Anmerkung* = remark.","The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2185":["to this, that philosophical definitions can be brought about only as expositions of given, mathematical ones however as constructions of originally made",["The verb \"brought about\" (*zu Stande gebracht werden*) stands in the next passage and governs this clause. I supplied it here at the point where the German construction requires it.","*Expositionen* and *Constructionen* are Latinate words that I did not italicize, since they are not mentioned.","*Begriffe* (concepts) is in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2186":["concepts, the former only analytically through dissection (whose completeness is not apodictically certain), the latter synthetically, and thus make the concept itself, whereas the former only ex-",["*Zergliederung* = \"dissection\", kept distinct from *analytisch* = \"analytically\".","\"The former\" translates both *jene* (first occurrence) and *die ersteren* (second). I kept the German's shared reference.","\"ex-\" is the first part of *erklären*, \"explain\", completed in the next passage.","*zu Stande gebracht werden* is rendered with \"brought about\" in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-2187":["plain. From this it follows:",["\"plain\" completes *erklären* (to explain) from the previous passage."]],"krv-b-2188":["a) that in philosophy one must not so imitate mathematics,",["*es der Mathematik nachthun* = \"imitate mathematics\". *es* is not rendered separately.","The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2189":["in sending definitions ahead, except perhaps for a mere at-",["\"at-\" begins *Versuche* (rendered \"attempt\"), completed in the next passage.","*voranschicken* = send ahead."]],"krv-b-2190":["For since they are dissections of given concepts, these concepts, though only still confusedly, come first, and the incomplete exposition goes before the complete, so that from some marks that we have drawn from a still unfinished dissection we can infer much beforehand, before we have arrived at the complete exposition, i.e. at the¹) definition; in a word, that in philosophy the definition, as measured distinctness, must conclude the work rather than begin it*). On the other hand, in mathematics we have no concept at all before the definition, as that through which the concept is first given; it must therefore and can also always begin with it.",["The text begins with \"suche\", the continuation of *Versuche*: \"attempt\".","¹) is a footnote marker after *zur* (\"zur¹) Definition\"). The footnote text is not in the passage, so I translated none.","*schließen* appears twice: \"infer\" (*vorher schließen*) and \"conclude\" (*das Werk eher schließen als anfangen*), following the glossary's *Schluß* = inference; conclusion.","*Zergliederung* = dissection; *Merkmal* = mark; *Deutlichkeit* = distinctness; *verworren* = confused; *abgemessen* = \"measured\".","\"as that through which\" renders *als durch welche*, kept literal.","*Exposition* is the same Latinate word as in the glossary's *Erörterung* (exposition).","*) is the footnote marker for the next passage."]],"krv-b-2191":["*) Philosophy teems with faulty definitions, especially those that indeed contain elements for the definition, but not yet completely. Were one now to be unable to begin anything at all with a concept before one had defined it, things would stand very badly with all philosophizing. But since, as far as the elements (of the dissection) reach, a good and secure use can always be made of them, deficient definitions too, i.e. propositions that are actually not yet definitions, but otherwise true and therefore approximations to them, can be used very usefully. In mathematics the definition belongs *ad esse*, in philosophy *ad melius esse*. It is fine, but often very difficult, to arrive at it. The jurists are still seeking a definition for their concept of right¹).",["This is a footnote, from the marker \"*)\" in the preceding text.","¹) is a footnote marker after *vom* (\"vom¹) Recht\"). The footnote text is not in the passage.","*Recht* = \"right\" (it could also be \"law\"); I kept it literal.","*Zergliederung* = dissection; *Gebrauch* = use; *Satz* = proposition.","*ad esse*, *ad melius esse* are Kant's Latin and are left in Latin."]],"krv-b-2192":["b) Mathematical definitions can never err. For since only that which the definition wants to have thought through it. But although¹) as to content nothing incorrect can occur in it, still occasionally, though only rarely, there may be a failing in the form (the clothing), namely with regard to precision. Thus the common explanation of the circular line, that it is a curved line all of whose points are equally distant from a single one (the center), has the fault that the determination curved has crept in unnecessarily. For there must be a particular theorem, which is concluded from the definition and can easily be proved: that every line all of whose points are equally distant from a single one is curved (no part of it straight). Analytic definitions, on the other hand, can err in manifold ways, either by bringing in marks that really did not lie in the concept, or by lacking the thoroughness that constitutes what is essential to a definition, because one cannot be so fully certain of the completeness of one's dissection. On account of this the method of mathematics in defining cannot be imitated in philosophy. |",["The second sentence (\"Denn weil nur das, was die Definition durch ihn gedacht haben will\") is incomplete in the text as given and looks corrupt or cut off. I translated what stands and did not fill the gap.","¹) after *obgleich* is a doubtful mark (OCR “). I treated it as a footnote marker, but no footnote text is given.","*einigen* (\"von einem einigen\") = \"single\". *Einkleidung* = \"clothing\". *gefehlt werden* is impersonal (\"there may be a failing\").","*Erklärung* = explanation (not \"definition\", though the glossary gives both). *Bestimmung* = determination; *Lehrsatz* = theorem; *besonder* = particular; *folgern* = conclude; *Merkmal* = mark; *Zergliederung* = dissection; *Vollständigkeit* = completeness.","The final | is the Academy Edition's page/line break and is carried over."]],"krv-b-2193":["2. On the axioms. These are synthetic principles *a priori*, insofar as they are immediately certain. Now one concept cannot be combined with the other synthetically and yet immediately, because, in order that we can go beyond a concept, a third, mediating cognition is necessary. Since now philosophy is merely rational cognition through concepts, no principle will be encountered in it that would deserve the name of an axiom. Mathematics, on the other hand, is capable of axioms, because | it can, by means of the construction of concepts in the intuition of the object, connect its predicates *a priori* and immediately, e.g. that three points always lie in one plane. On the other hand, a synthetic principle merely from concepts can never be immediately certain, e.g. the proposition: everything that happens has its cause; since I must look around for a third, namely the condition of the time-|determination in an experience, and could not cognize such a principle directly, immediately from the con|cepts alone. Discursive prin- | ciples are therefore something quite different from intuitive ones, i.e. axioms. The former always require a deduction besides, which the latter can do without entirely; and since these for the sake of the same ground¹) are evi-",["*Grundsatz* = principle, *Satz* = proposition (the glossary also gives \"principle\" for *Satz*). Both are English \"principle\" only for *Grundsatz*; *Satz* stays \"proposition\" here.","*Vernunfterkenntniß* = \"rational cognition\", with *nach Begriffen* = \"through concepts\" (literally \"according to concepts\").","*Vermittelnd* = mediating; *Anschauung* = intuition; *intuitive* (German) and *anschaulich* are both \"intuitive\" in English, here and in later passages.","*konnte* (\"erkennen konnte\") is kept as it stands in the German.","The | signs (including those inside \"time-|determination\", \"con|cepts\" and \"prin- | ciples\") are the Academy apparatus and are carried over where they stand.","¹) is a footnote marker; the footnote text is at the start of the next passage.","\"evi-\" begins *evident*, completed in the next passage. A stray \"!\" after *Erkenntniß* is treated as OCR noise."]],"krv-b-2194":["dent, which the philosophical principles with all their certainty can yet never claim, there is infinitely much lacking for any synthetic proposition of pure and transcendental reason to be as obvious (as one is wont to express oneself defiantly) as the proposition: that twice two give four. I have indeed in the Analytic, in the table of the principles of the pure understanding, also mentioned certain axioms of intuition; but the principle cited there was itself no axiom, but served only to indicate the *principium* of the possibility of axioms in general, and is itself only a principle from concepts. For even the possibility of mathematics must be shown in transcendental philosophy. Philosophy therefore has no axioms and may never command its principles *a priori* so absolutely,\n\n¹) A¹: wegen dent",["The footnote at the end of the passage reads \"Al: wegen dent\" in the source, which is corrupt or an OCR error (perhaps \"wegen dessen\", \"on account of this\"). I left the German as it stands and translated nothing.","\"dent\" completes *evident*, begun in the previous passage.","*augenscheinlich* = \"obvious\", kept distinct from *evident* (the Latinate word).","*Grundsatz* = principle, *Principium* = *principium* (left in Latin), *Satz* = proposition.","*vorgeben* = \"claim\". *gebieten* = command. *Verstand* = understanding; *Möglichkeit* = possibility.","The passage breaks off in mid-sentence (\"...gebieten,\"); the next passage continues it."]],"krv-b-2195":["but must accommodate itself to justifying its warrant concerning them through thorough deduction. 3. On the demonstrations. Only an apodictic proof, insofar as it is intuitive, can be called demonstration. Experience teaches us indeed what there is, but not that it could not be otherwise at all. Hence | empirical grounds of proof cannot provide an apodictic proof. From concepts *a priori* (in discursive cognition) intuitive certainty, i.e. evidence, can never arise, however much the judgment may otherwise be apodictically certain. Only mathematics therefore contains demonstrations, because it derives its cognition not from concepts, but the construction of them, i.e. the intuition that can be given corresponding to the concepts *a priori*. Even the procedure of algebra with its equations, from which it brings forth by reduction the truth together with the proof, is indeed no geometrical, but still a characteristic construction, in which one sets out by the signs the concepts, especially of the relation of magnitudes, in intuition and, without even looking to the heuristic, secures all inferences against errors by this, that each of them is placed before the eyes. Since, on the other hand, philosophical cognition must do without this advantage, in that it must always consider the universal *in abstracto* (through concepts), while mathematics can weigh the universal *in concreto* (in the single intuition) and yet through pure representation *a priori*, whereby every misstep becomes visible. I would therefore prefer to call the former acroamatic (discursive) proofs, because they can be conducted only through mere words (the object in thought), than demonstrations, which, as the expression already indicates, proceed in the intuition of the object.",["*sondern der Construction derselben* is elliptical (\"not from concepts, but the construction of them\"); I kept the ellipsis. In \"corresponding to the concepts *a priori*\", *a priori* could attach to \"given\" or to \"corresponding\"; the German is equally ambiguous and so is the English.","*anschauende Gewißheit* = \"intuitive certainty\", the same English word as for *anschaulich* and *intuitiv*.","*Beweis* = proof, *Beweisgründe* = grounds of proof; *Demonstration* = demonstration; *Evidenz* = evidence.","\"Da hingegen ... wird.\" is a subordinate clause with no main clause in the German. I kept it as it stands.","*akroamatisch* is transliterated \"acroamatic\". *erwägen* = \"weigh\"; *betrachten* = consider; *Schluß* = inference; *Ausdruck* = expression.","The stray \"i\" before \"3. Von den Demonstrationen\" is dropped as an OCR slip. The | is carried over as in the German."]],"krv-b-2196":["From all this it now follows that it is not at all fitting for the nature of philosophy, especially in the field of pure reason, to strut about with a dogmatic gait and to adorn itself with the titles and ribbons of mathematics, in whose order it nonetheless does not belong, although it has every reason to hope for a sisterly union with it. Those are vain presumptions, which can never succeed, and must rather make their aim go backwards, which is to discover the delusions of a reason that misjudges its boundaries and, by means of sufficient clarification of our concepts, to lead the self-conceit of speculation back to modest but thorough self-cognition. Reason will thus not be able, in its transcendental attempts, to look ahead so confidently, just as if the path that it has traveled led so entirely straight to the goal, and will not be able to count so boldly on its premises laid down as a ground, that it would not be necessary to look back often and to take heed whether faults might not be discovered in the progress of the inferences, which were overlooked in the principles and make it necessary either to determine them more, or to alter them entirely.",["- Blendwerke = delusions; Aufklärung = clarification (not Erläuterung); Eigendünkel = self-conceit; Selbsterkenntniß = self-cognition; Fortgang = progress; Schlüsse = inferences; Prämissen = premises; Principien = principles (Grundsatz is also \"principle\").","- Fehler = faults (Irrthum is rendered \"errors\" in krv-b-2206). \"Ihre Absicht\" (their aim) is kept as in the German; the antecedent is not resolved.","- OCR corrected silently: ftrogen → strotzen; stray letters and quotation marks removed."]],"krv-b-2197":["I divide all apodictic propositions (whether they may now be demonstrable or also immediately certain) into *Dogmata* and *Mathemata*. A directly synthetic proposition from concepts is a *dogma*; on the other hand¹) such a proposition through construction of concepts is a *mathema*. Analytic judgments actually teach us nothing more about the object than what the concept that we have of it already contains in itself, because they do not extend the cognition beyond the concept of the subject, but only clarify it. They therefore cannot properly be called dogmas (which word one could perhaps translate by doctrinal sayings). But among the aforementioned two species of synthetic propositions a priori, according to common usage of speech only those belonging to philosophical cognition can bear this name, and one would hardly call the propositions of the art of calculation or of geometry *dogmata*. Thus this use confirms the definition that we gave, that only judgments from concepts and not those from the construction of concepts can be called dogmatic.",["- \"Doctrinal sayings\" translates *Lehrsprüche*; Lehrsatz (theorem) is a different word. Erläutern = clarify; Erklärung = definition here (glossary also: explanation); Rechenkunst = the art of calculation; Art = species.","- Satz = proposition; Urtheil = judgment; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gebrauch = use (Redegebrauch = usage of speech).","- The footnote marker ¹) is attached to \"hingegen\" in the source; the footnote is krv-b-2199."]],"krv-b-2198":["Now the whole of pure reason in its merely speculative",["- Breaks off; the sentence continues in the footnote passage and krv-b-2200 (\"Gebrauche nicht ein einziges …\")."]],"krv-b-2199":["¹) A: dagegen use not a single directly synthetic judgment from concepts.",["- \"A: dagegen\" is the variant reading of the first edition for \"hingegen\" (both \"on the other hand\"). \"Gebrauche\" begins the continuation of krv-b-2198, hence \"use\".","- Footnote text and the continuation of the main sentence are run together in the OCR; kept as they stand."]],"krv-b-2200":["For through ideas it is, as we have shown, not capable at all of any synthetic judgments that would have objective validity; through concepts of the understanding",["- Artheile → Urtheile and \"synthetischer\" (for synthetischen) corrected/left as OCR; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; Gültigkeit = validity."]],"krv-b-2201":["however, it does establish secure principles, but not at all directly from concepts, but always only indirectly through relation of these concepts to something entirely contingent, namely possible experience; whereupon they are, when this (something as an object of possible experiences) is presupposed, to be sure apodictically certain, but in themselves (directly) a priori",["- Grundsätze = principles; zufällig = contingent; Gegenstand = object; errichten = establish.","- \"In themselves (directly) a priori\" may be read with \"cannot be cognized\" in the next passage; \"a priori\" is left unattached as in the German."]],"krv-b-2202":["not even to be cognized at all. Thus no one can have thorough insight into the proposition: everything that happens has its cause, from these given concepts alone. Hence it is no dogma, although in an",["- erkannt = cognized; einsehen = have insight into; gründlich = thoroughly; geschieht = happens."]],"krv-b-2203":["other point of view, namely the sole field of its possible us-",["- The word Gebrauchs is split across krv-b-2203 (\"Ge—\") and krv-b-2204 (\"brauchs\"); rendered \"us-\" / \"e\"."]],"krv-b-2204":["e, that is, of experience, can very well and apodictically be proved. But it is called a principle and not a theorem, although it",["- Grundsatz = principle; Lehrsatz = theorem; \"d. i.\" = that is."]],"krv-b-2205":["must be proved, because it has the particular property that it itself first makes its ground of proof, namely experience, possible and must always be presupposed in the case of this.",["- Eigenschaft = property; Beweisgrund = ground of proof; \"bei dieser\" = in the case of this (referring to Erfahrung, grammatically feminine)."]],"krv-b-2206":["If there are now in the speculative use of pure reason, even as to content, no dogmata at all, then all dogmatic method, whether it may be borrowed from the mathematician or is to become a peculiar manner, is in itself unfitting. For it only conceals the faults and errors and deceives philosophy, whose proper aim is to let all steps of reason be seen in their clearest light. Nonetheless the method can always be systematic. For our reason (subjectively) is itself a system, but in its pure use,",["- Fehler = faults; Irrthümer = errors; Absicht = aim; Manier = manner; eigenthümlich = peculiar; \"Absieht\" in the OCR corrected to Absicht.","- \"für sich\" rendered \"in itself\"; it is not \"an sich\"."]],"krv-b-2207":["by means of mere concepts, only a system of inquiry after principles of unity, for which experience alone can provide the material. But about the peculiar method of a transcendental philosophy nothing can be said here, since we have to do only with a critique of our financial circumstances, whether we can build at all, and how high we might raise our edifice from the material that we have (the pure concepts a priori).",["- Vermögensumstände (OCR \"Bermöͤgensumstände\") = financial circumstances; Vermögen here is not \"faculty\". Stoff = material; Einheit = unity; Grundsätze = principles."]],"krv-b-2208":["Of the First Chapter,\nSecond Section.",["- Hauptstück = chapter."]],"krv-b-2209":["The Discipline of Pure Reason with regard to its",["- Breaks off; continues in krv-b-2210."]],"krv-b-2210":["Polemical Use.",["- Gebrauch = use."]],"krv-b-2211":["Reason must subject itself in all its undertakings to critique and cannot infringe upon the freedom of the same by any prohibition without harming itself and drawing upon itself a disadvantageous suspicion. There is now nothing so important with regard to utility, nothing so holy, that it might withdraw from this examining and reviewing scrutiny, which knows no regard of the person. On this freedom even the existence of reason rests, which has no dictatorial authority, but whose pronouncement is at all times nothing but the agreement of free citizens, each of whom must be able to express his misgivings, indeed even his veto, without holding back.",["- \"Of the same\" (derselben) could refer to reason or to critique; left ambiguous. Existenz = existence; Ansehen = authority/regard (rendered \"regard\" in \"Ansehen der Person\", \"authority\" elsewhere).","- Bedenklichkeiten = misgivings; Ausspruch = pronouncement; stray OCR marks removed."]],"krv-b-2212":["But although reason can never refuse itself to critique, it nevertheless does not always have cause to fear it. But pure reason in its dogmatic (not mathematical) use is not so much conscious of the most precise observance of its supreme laws that it would not have to appear with timidity, indeed with complete abandonment of all presumed dogmatic authority, before the critical eye of a higher and judicial reason.",["- Beobachtung = observance (glossary: observation); Blödigkeit = timidity; Gesetze = laws; angemaßt = presumed."]],"krv-b-2213":["It is quite otherwise with it when it has to do not with the censure of the judge but with the claims of its fellow citizen and is merely to defend itself against them. For since these want to be dogmatic just as much, though in denying, as those in affirming: there takes place a justification κατ' ἄνθρωπον, which secures against all infringement and procures a titled possession that need not fear any foreign presumptions, although it itself cannot be sufficiently proved κατ' ἀλήθειαν.",["- OCR \"xat avdpwrov\" and \"xat' Anderav\" restored as κατ' ἄνθρωπον and κατ' ἀλήθειαν; \"fol\" read as soll. Bejahen = affirm; Anmaßungen = presumptions; Mitbürger = fellow citizen.","- \"Those\" (jene) is left unresolved as in the German."]],"krv-b-2214":["By the polemical use of pure reason I now understand the defense of its propositions against the dogmatic denials of the same. Here it does not matter whether its assertions might not perhaps also be false, but only that no one could ever assert the opposite with apodictic certainty (indeed even only with greater illusion). For we are then still not by way of request",["- Behauptungen = assertions; Schein = illusion (glossary); bittweise = by way of request; Verneinungen = denials."]],"krv-b-2215":["in our possession, if we have an, although not sufficient, title of the",["- \"Der—selben\" is split across krv-b-2215 and krv-b-2216; \"of the same\" is left without resolved antecedent."]],"krv-b-2216":["same before us, and it is completely certain that no one could ever prove the illegi-",["- Unrechtmäßigkeit is split across krv-b-2216 and krv-b-2217 (\"Unrecht—\" / \"mäßigkeit\")."]],"krv-b-2217":["timacy of this possession.",["- Besitz = possession."]],"krv-b-2218":["It is something distressing and depressing that there should be an antithetic of pure reason at all, and that this, which after all represents the highest court of justice over all disputes, should in dispute with itself ge-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"ge-\"); not completed. \"Befümmerndes\" corrected to Bekümmerndes. Antithetik = antithetic; Streit = dispute."]],"krv-b-2219":["advise. To be sure, we had above such an apparent antithetic of the same before us; but it showed that it rested on a misunderstanding, in that, namely, in accordance with the common prejudice, one took appearances for things in themselves and then demanded an absolute completeness of their synthesis in one way or another (which, however, was equally impossible in both ways), which, however, cannot be expected at all from appearances. There was thus at that time no actual contradiction of reason with itself in the propositions: the series of appearances given in themselves has an absolutely first beginning, and: this series is absolutely and in itself without any beginning; for both propositions subsist quite well together, because appearances in accordance with their existence (as appearances) are in themselves nothing at all, i.e., something contradictory, and thus the presupposition of them must naturally draw contradictory consequences after it.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"rathen soll\"); translated as it stands, with \"advise\" for *rathen* (the preceding words are missing).","- *Erscheinungen* = appearances; *Sachen an sich selbst* = things in themselves (*Sache*, not *Ding*); *Dasein* = existence; *Folgerungen* = consequences; *Sätze* = propositions; *Mißverstand* = misunderstanding.","- \"an sich gegebener Erscheinungen\": \"in themselves\" could attach to \"given\" or to \"appearances\"; the ambiguity is kept.","- \"welches aber von Erscheinungen gar nicht erwartet werden kann\": \"which\" refers ambiguously to the demand or to the completeness; kept ambiguous.","- \"auf eine oder andere Art ... auf beiderlei Art\": *Art* = \"way\" in both places."]],"krv-b-2220":["But such a misunderstanding cannot be pretended and the dispute of reason thereby settled, if, say, it were asserted theistically: there is a highest being, and against this atheistically: there is no highest being; or in psychology: everything that thinks is of absolute persisting unity and is thus distinct from all transitory material unity, to which another opposed: the soul is not an immaterial unity and cannot be exempted from transitoriness. For the object of the question is here free of everything foreign that contradicts its nature, and the understanding has to do only with things in themselves and not with appearances. A true conflict would therefore certainly be encountered here, if only pure reason had something to say on the negating side that came close to the ground of an assertion; for as far as the critique of the grounds of proof of the one who affirms dogmatically is concerned, one can very well concede it to him, without therefore giving up these propositions, which after all have at least the interest of reason on their side, to which the opponent cannot appeal at all.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"was!)\" read \"was\"; \"male— riellen\" = *materiellen*; \"gar“\" = *gar*; stray \"1\", \"ii;\" omitted.","- *Wesen* = being; *Gegenstand* = object; *Sachen an sich selbst* = things in themselves; *Beweisgründe* = grounds of proof; *Beharrlichkeit* is rendered \"persisting\" (*beharrlich*).","- *Behauptung/behaupten* = assertion/assert; *bejahend* = affirms; *verneinend* = negating; *Widerstreit* = \"conflict\" (distinct from *Widerspruch* = contradiction).","- \"vorgewandt\" is the original's spelling of *vorgewandt* (*vorwenden*); rendered \"pretended.\"","- \"welchem ein anderer entgegensetzte\": the verb is subjunctive in the German; the main verb of the sentence is \"würde\"; the grammar is kept loose as in the original."]],"krv-b-2221":["I am indeed not of the opinion that excellent and reflective men (e.g., Sulzer) have so often expressed, when they felt the weakness of the previous proofs: that one could hope that one will some day yet discover evident demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of our pure reason: there is a God, there is a future life. Rather I am certain that this will never happen. For where is reason to get the ground for such synthetic assertions, which do not | relate to objects of experience and their inner possibility? But it is also apodictically certain that no human being will ever come forward who could assert the opposite with the least illusion, let alone dogmatically. For since he could demonstrate this only through pure reason, he would have to undertake to prove: that a highest being, that the thinking subject in us as pure intelligence is impossible. But where will he get the information that would entitle him to judge synthetically in this way about things beyond all possible experience? We can therefore be entirely unconcerned about whether someone will some day prove the opposite to us, so that we do not need on that account to think up school-proofs, but can in any case accept those propositions that hang together quite well with the speculative interest of our reason in its empirical use and are moreover the only means for uniting it with the practical interest. For the opponent (who must here not be considered merely as a critic) we have our *non liquet* in readiness, which must unfailingly confuse him, while we do not refuse the retorsion of it upon us, in that we constantly have in reserve the subjective maxim of reason, which the opponent necessarily lacks, and under whose protection we can look upon all his blows in the air with calm and indifference.",["- The sign | is carried over as in the passage; the first \"der- | einst\" (*dereinst*) is joined and the sign dropped there only because it breaks a word, so it is kept in the German position: \"some day\" stands for *dereinst*; the second | is kept in the text.","- OCR slips corrected: \"reinen“\", \"Vielmehr“\", \"gar“\", stray \"1.\", \"F\", \"2\", \"3\" omitted; \"Kenntnisse\" = information (glossary); *Scheine* = illusion (*Schein*).","- \"Gegenstände\" = objects; *Dingen* = things; *Mittel* = means; *Retorsion* kept as in the German.","- \"als reine Intelligenz\": \"as\" could attach to \"subject\" or to \"impossible\"; kept ambiguous.","- \"schulgerechte Beweise\" = \"school-proofs\" is a literal compound for \"proofs conforming to the schools\"; \"auf ... sinnen\" = \"think up.\"","- Footnote markers 2 and 3 in the OCR are not footnote text and are omitted."]],"krv-b-2222":["In such a way there is actually no antithetic of pure reason at all. For the only battlefield for it would have to be sought in the field of pure theology and psychology; but this ground bears no fighter in his full armor and with weapons that would be to be feared. He can appear only with mockery or boasting, which can be laughed at as a children's game. That is a consoling remark, which gives reason courage again; for on what else would it rely, if it, which alone is called to do away with all errors, were deranged in itself, without being able to hope for peace and quiet possession?",["- OCR slips corrected: \"fürde ten\" = *fürchten*; \"wei 2i ches\" = *welches*; \"fidh\" = *sich*; \"den reinen\" read *der reinen*.","- *Antithetik* kept as \"antithetic\"; *Kampfplatz* = battlefield; *Rüstung* = armor.","- \"das ... Waffen, die zu fürchten wären\": \"to be feared\" is literal for *zu fürchten wären*.","- \"welches\" (after *Großsprecherei*) could refer to either \"mockery\" or \"boasting,\" or the whole; kept ambiguous."]],"krv-b-2223":["Everything that nature itself arranges is good for some aim. Even poisons serve to overpower other poisons, which are generated in our own juices, and therefore may not be missing from a complete collection of remedies (dispensary). The objections against the persuasions and the conceit of our merely speculative reason are themselves given as a task by the nature of this reason and must therefore have their good vocation and aim, which one must not throw to the wind. Why has providence placed many objects, although they are connected with our highest interest, so high that it is almost only granted to us to encounter them in an indistinct perception doubted by ourselves, whereby spying glances are more aroused than satisfied? Whether it is useful to venture bold determinations with regard to such prospects is at least doubtful, perhaps even harmful. But always and without any doubt it is useful to put the researching as well as the examining reason into complete freedom, so that it can attend to its own interest unhindered, which is promoted just as much by its setting limits to its insights as by its extending them, and which always suffers when foreign hands intervene in order to steer it against its natural course according to forced aims.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"vollständi— gen\", \"miissen\" = *müssen*, \"wird!)\" read \"wird\".","- *Absicht* = aim; *Bestimmung* = vocation in \"gute Bestimmung\" but \"determinations\" for *Bestimmungen*; *Überredung* = persuasion; *Aufgabe* = task (glossary \"problem\"; *aufgegeben* rendered \"given as a task\"); *Schranken* = limits (*Schranke* = limitation); *Wahrnehmung* = perception; *undeutlich* = indistinct; *Zweifel* = doubt.","- *Einsichten* = insights; *Officin* is in parentheses in the German as glossed by *Heilmittel*; *Zweck* is not used here.","- \"ob sie gleich ... zusammenhängen\": \"they\" refers to the objects; \"mehr gereizt als befriedigt\" is kept in the original order.","- \"welches ... befördert wird\": the antecedent \"which\" is *Interesse*; ambiguous between \"interest\" and the whole clause is not preserved in English."]],"krv-b-2224":["Let your opponent therefore only show reason, and combat him merely with weapons of reason. For the rest, be without worry on account of the good cause (of the practical interest), for it never enters into the game in the merely speculative dispute. The dispute then discovers nothing but a certain antinomy of reason, which, since it rests on its nature, must necessarily be listened to and examined. It cultivates the same through consideration of its object on two sides and rectifies its judgment by restricting it. What becomes disputed here is not the matter but the tone. For there still remains enough for you to speak the language of a firm belief, justified before the sharpest reason, even if you have had to give up that of knowledge.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"feld\" read *seid*; \"außer Sorgen\" kept; \"specu— lativen\", \"be— ruht\"; \"Interesse\" for *Interesses*.","- *Glaube* = belief; *Wissen* = knowledge; *Betrachtung* = consideration; *Urteil* = judgment; *Antinomie* = antinomy; *Sache* = \"matter\" (*die Sache* in \"nicht die Sache, sondern der Ton\"), but \"cause\" in \"die gute Sache.\"","- \"Lasset ... zeigen\" translated as imperative in the plural \"Let your opponent...\"; *Vernunft* = reason.","- \"dieselbe\" (after *cultivirt*) = \"the same,\" referring to the antinomy or reason; ambiguity preserved.","- \"angehört\" = \"listened to\" (*anhören*)."]],"krv-b-2225":["If one were to ask the cold-blooded David Hume, who was actually created for the equilibrium of judgment: what moved you to undermine, through laboriously brooded-over scruples, the persuasion, so comforting and useful for human beings, that their insight of reason suffices for the assertion and for a determinate concept of a highest being?, he would answer: nothing but the aim of bringing reason further in its self-cognition, and at the same time a certain indignation at the constraint that one wants to do to reason, in that one makes a great show with it and at the same time hinders it from making a candid confession of its weaknesses, which become manifest to it in the examination of itself. If, on the other hand, you ask Priestley, who is solely | devoted to the principles of the empirical use of reason and averse to all transcendent speculation, what motives he had for tearing down the freedom and immortality of our soul (the hope of the future life is with him only the expectation of a miracle of resurrection), two such pillars of all religion, he who is himself a pious and zealous teacher of religion: he could answer nothing other than: the interest of reason, which loses thereby, that one wants to withdraw | certain objects from the laws of material nature, the only ones that we know precisely and can determine. It would seem unfair to decry the latter, who knows how to unite his paradoxical assertion with the aim of religion, and to hurt a well-thinking man, because he cannot find his way as soon as he had lost himself from the field of the doctrine of nature. But this favor must benefit no less the no less well-disposed Hume, who is blameless in his moral character, who cannot leave his abstracted speculation, because he rightly holds that its object lies entirely outside the bounds of natural science, in the field of pure ideas.",["- Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\"; Naturwissenschaft = \"natural science\" (two different German words, kept distinct).","- Gegenstand = \"object\"; Bewegungsgründe = \"motives\"; Absicht = \"aim\" (twice, including Religionsabsicht); Grenzen = \"bounds\"; Überredung = \"persuasion\".","- \"alle | ein\" (OCR) read as \"allein\", with the | kept after \"solely\"; \"ger | wisse\" read as \"gewisse\", with the | placed before \"certain\". Stray OCR letters dropped.","- Vernunfteinsicht = \"insight of reason\"; Selbsterkenntniß = \"self-cognition\" (Erkenntniß = cognition).","- \"den den Grundsätzen … ergebenen\" is syntactically awkward in the German; kept as it stands."]],"krv-b-2226":["What is now to be done here, primarily with regard to the danger that seems thereby to threaten the common good? Nothing is more natural, nothing fairer than the resolution that you have to take on that account. Just let these people go about their work; if they show talent, if they show deep and new investigation, in a word, if they only show reason, reason always gains. If you seize upon other means than those of a reason free from constraint, if you cry out about high treason, summon the commonwealth, which does not understand itself at all about such subtle treatments,",["- das gemeine Wesen = \"the commonwealth\" (Wesen elsewhere \"being\"); das gemeine Beste = \"the common good\".","- Mittel = \"means\"; Bearbeitungen = \"treatments\". The passage breaks off after \"versteht,\" and is left uncompleted."]],"krv-b-2227":["as it were as for putting out a fire, then you make yourselves ridiculous. For the talk is not at all of what to the common good",["- Fragment ends mid-clause; continued in the next passage (\"hierunter … sei\")."]],"krv-b-2228":["in this regard is advantageous or disadvantageous, but only how far the rea-",["- \"Ver—\" at the end is the first half of \"Vernunft\", which the next passage completes; the hyphen is kept."]],"krv-b-2229":["-son can get in its speculation abstracting from all interest, and whether one can count on this at all, or must rather give it up altogether in favor of the practical. Instead, then, of striking in with the sword, look on rather, from the secure seat of critique, calmly at this dispute, which must turn out laborious for the combatants, entertaining for you, and, with an assuredly bloodless outcome, profitable for your insights. For it is very much something absurd to expect enlightenment from reason and yet to prescribe to it beforehand to which side it must necessarily turn out. Besides, reason is already of itself so well tamed and kept within limits by reason that you have no need at all to call out patrols, in order to set civil resistance against that party whose worrisome superior power seems dangerous to you. In this dialectic there is no victory about which you would have cause to be concerned.",["- The opening \"-son\" completes \"Ver—nunft\" from the preceding passage. Schranken = \"limits\" (glossary: limitation).","- Einsichten = \"insights\"; Ungereimtes = \"absurd\"; Scharwachen = \"patrols\" (literally watch-detachments); Obermacht = \"superior power\".","- \"es … bringen könne\" is idiomatic (\"how far it can get\"); \"es\" is left as in the German."]],"krv-b-2230":["Reason also very much needs such a dispute, and it were to be wished that it had been conducted sooner and with unrestricted public permission. For so much the sooner would a mature critique have come about, at whose appearance all these quarrels must fall away of themselves, in that the disputants learn to see their delusion and prejudices, which have set them at odds.",["- Streithändel = \"quarrels\"; Streit = \"dispute\"; Verblendung = \"delusion\"."]],"krv-b-2231":["There is a certain impurity in human nature, which in the end, like everything that comes from nature, must nevertheless contain a predisposition to good",["- Anlage = \"predisposition\". The passage breaks off before \"Zwecken\" is combined with \"zu guten\" (continued in the next)."]],"krv-b-2232":["ends, namely an inclination to conceal one's true dispositions",["- Gesinnungen = \"dispositions\". German has \"seine wahre Gesinnungen\" (plural noun with singular possessive), rendered \"one's true dispositions\"."]],"krv-b-2233":["and to put on display certain assumed ones, which one holds to be good and laudable. Quite certainly human beings, through this propensity both to conceal themselves and to assume an illusion advantageous to them, have not merely civilized themselves but gradually, to a certain measure, moralized themselves, because no one could penetrate through the rouge of decency,",["- Schein = \"illusion\" (glossary); \"semblance\" would also be possible. \"Maße\" = \"measure\".","- \"angenommene\" has no noun expressed (\"assumed ones\")."]],"krv-b-2234":["honorableness and modesty, and so in supposedly genu-",["- \"äch—\" is the beginning of \"ächten\", completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2235":["ine examples of the good, which he saw around him, found a school of improvement for himself. Yet this predisposition to present oneself as better than one is, and to express dispositions that one does not have, serves only, as it were, provi-",["- \"sach\" read as \"sah\"; line number dropped. \"pro-\" in German ends the next passage's first word; \"provi-\" here follows the break at \"visorisch\".","- Gesinnungen = \"dispositions\"; Anlage = \"predisposition\"."]],"krv-b-2236":["sionally to bring the human being out of crudeness and to",["- \"visorisch\" completes \"provisorisch\" from the previous passage. Rohigkeit = \"crudeness\"."]],"krv-b-2237":["let him at first at least adopt the manner of the good that he knows; for afterwards, when the genuine principles have once been developed and have passed over into the way of thinking, that falseness must gradually be vigorously combated, because otherwise it corrupts the heart and does not let good dispositions come up under the weed of fine illusion.",["- Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\"; kennt = \"knows\" (kennen); Scheins = \"illusion\".","- Wucherkraut = \"weed\" (literally a rank, proliferating plant)."]],"krv-b-2238":["It pains me to perceive the very same impurity, dissimulation and hypocrisy even in the expressions of the speculative way of thinking, in which human beings, however, to disclose the confession of their thoughts fairly, openly and unconcealed, have far fewer hindrances and no advantage at all. For what can be more detrimental to insights than to communicate even mere thoughts to one another falsified, to conceal doubts that we feel against our own assertions, or to give a varnish of evidence to grounds of proof that do not satisfy even ourselves? So long, however, as merely private vanity instigates these secret intrigues (which in speculative judgments, which have no particular interest and are not easily capable of apodictic certainty, is commonly the case), the vanity of others resists, with public approval, and things come finally to the point to which the purest disposition and sincerity, though far earlier, would have brought them. But where the commonwealth holds that hair-splitting rationalizers are occupied with nothing less than making the foundation of the public welfare shaky, there it seems not only in accordance with prudence but also permitted and indeed even laudable to come to the aid of the good cause rather through illusory grounds than to leave to its supposed opponents even the advantage of lowering our tone to the moderation of a merely practical conviction and of compelling us to confess the lack of speculative and apodictic certainty. Meanwhile I should think that, with the aim of asserting a good cause, nothing worse can be united in the world than guile, dissimulation and deception. That in the weighing of the grounds of reason of a mere speculation everything must proceed honestly is surely the least that can be demanded. But if one could count with certainty even on this little, the dispute of speculative reason about the important questions of God, of immortality (of the soul) and of freedom would either long since have been decided, or",["- \"Hen | chelei\" = Heuchelei, with the | kept as in the passage (\"Hypocrisy\" appears without it in English since the break falls mid-word; the sign is therefore not shown). OCR \"dinge-bracht\" read as \"hingebracht\"; stray \"*\" and line numbers dropped.","- \"billigermaßen\" rendered \"fairly\"; \"Hindernisse und gar keinen Vortheil\" has no complement stated for \"Vortheil\" and is left so.","- Vernünftler = \"rationalizers\" (vernünfteln); Scheingründe = \"illusory grounds\"; Beweisgründen = \"grounds of proof\"; Vernunftgründe = \"grounds of reason\"; Überzeugung = \"conviction\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Gesinnung = \"disposition\"; das gemeine Wesen = \"the commonwealth\".","- Welt = \"world\"; Behauptung = \"assertion\"; Einsichten = \"insights\"."]],"krv-b-2239":["would very soon be brought to an end. Thus the purity of disposition often stands in inverse relation to the good nature of the matter itself, and this perhaps has more sincere and honest opponents than defenders.",["- Line number \"35\" dropped. Gutartigkeit = \"good nature\"; Sache = \"matter\"; Verhältniß = \"relation\"; Lauterkeit = \"purity\" (cf. Unlauterkeit \"impurity\")."]],"krv-b-2240":["I therefore presuppose readers who do not want to see a just cause defended with injustice. With regard to them it is now decided that, according to our principles of the critique, if one looks not at what happens but at what ought fairly to happen, there properly must be no polemic of pure reason at all. For how can two persons conduct a dispute over a matter whose reality neither of the two can exhibit in an actual, or even only possible experience, over whose idea he alone broods, in order to bring out of it something more than idea, namely the actuality of the object itself? By what means do they want to get out of the dispute, since neither of the two can make his cause straightaway comprehensible and certain, but can only attack and refute that of his opponent? For this is the fate of all assertions of pure reason: that, since they go beyond the conditions of all possible experience, outside of which no document of truth is encountered anywhere, but nevertheless must make use of the laws of the understanding, which are determined merely for empirical use, but without which no step can be taken in synthetic thinking, they can at any time give their opponent openings and can reciprocally turn to their advantage the opening of their opponent.",["- Realität = reality; Wirklichkeit = actuality (glossary gives \"reality\" as an alternative for Wirklichkeit; \"actuality\" is used here to keep the two apart). Gegenstand = object. Mittel = means. Behauptungen = assertions. Verstandesgesetze = laws of the understanding.","- \"Blößen\" (twice) = \"openings\"; literally \"bare places, exposed spots\".","- \"sich gegenseitig\" is rendered \"reciprocally\"; the syntax of the final clause is kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-2241":["One can regard the critique of pure reason as the true court of justice for all disputes of the same; for it is not involved in the latter, which go immediately to objects, but is set up to determine and to judge the rights of reason in general according to the principles of its first institution.",["- OCR \"Gerichts— hot\" read as \"Gerichtshof\"; \"Grundfäßen\" as \"Grundsätzen\" = principles. Objecte = objects.","- \"derselben\" is feminine singular or plural genitive; \"of the same\" keeps it ambiguous between \"reason\" and \"pure reason\".","- \"Rechtsame\" = rights; \"beurtheilen\" = judge, as glossary."]],"krv-b-2242":["Without it reason is, as it were, in the state of nature and cannot make its assertions and claims valid or secure other than through war. The critique, on the contrary, which takes all decisions from the fundamental rules of its own institution, whose authority no one can doubt, procures for us the tranquility of a lawful state, in which we are to conduct our dispute in no other way than by process. What ends the quarrels in the first state is a victory, of which both parties boast, which is mostly followed by only an insecure peace, which the authorities establish, who intervene, but in the second the sentence, which, since it here strikes the source of the disputes themselves, must guarantee an eternal peace. The endless disputes of a merely dogmatic reason also compel us finally to seek tranquility in some critique of this reason itself and in a legislation that is grounded on it; just as Hobbes asserts: the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, and one must necessarily leave it in order to submit to the lawful constraint which alone so restricts our freedom that it can coexist with every other's freedom and just thereby with the common good.",["- OCR \"Genteng\" read as \"Sentenz\" (sentence); \"Obrigkeit\" = authorities; \"Proceß\" = process (legal proceeding); \"Stand/Zustand\" both appear: Stand der Natur = state of nature, Zustand = state (glossary).","- \"Streit/Streitigkeit/Händel\" = dispute/dispute/quarrels. \"gemeinen Besten\" = common good (gemein = common).","- \"dahin einschränkt, daß\" is rendered \"so restricts ... that\" (\"to the point that\" would be an alternative).","- \"welche sich ins Mittel legt\" is rendered \"who intervene\"; the relative-clause reference is ambiguous as in the German (peace or authorities)."]],"krv-b-2243":["To this freedom there belongs then also that of putting up publicly for judgment one's thoughts, one's doubts that one cannot resolve oneself, without being decried on that account as a restless and dangerous citizen. This already lies in the original right of human reason, which recognizes no other judge than itself, once again | universal human reason, in which everyone has his voice; and since all improvement of which our state is capable must come from this, such a right is sacred and may not be curtailed. It is also very unwise to cry out as dangerous certain daring assertions or presumptuous attacks on those that already have on their side the assent of the greatest and best part of the commonwealth; for that means giving them an importance they should not have at all. When I hear that a person of no common mind is supposed to have demonstrated away the freedom of the human will, the hope of a future life and the existence of God, I am eager to read the book, for I expect of his talent that he will bring my insights further. This I already know beforehand quite certainly, that he will have accomplished nothing of all this; not because I believe myself already to be in possession of invincible proofs of these important propositions, but because the transcendental critique, which uncovered for me the whole stock of our pure reason, has fully convinced me that, just as it is wholly insufficient for affirmative assertions in this field, so little, and even less, will it know in order to be able to assert any- | thing negatively about these questions. For where does the alleged freethinker want to get his knowledge that there is, e.g., no highest being? This proposition lies outside the field of possible experience and therefore also outside the bounds of all human insight. The dogmatic defender of the good cause against this enemy I would not read at all,",["- Stray OCR letters (f, si, a, etc.) removed. The \"|\" signs are kept where they stand in the OCR; the first sits in a garbled place (\"selbst | si wiederum\"), read as \"selbst wiederum die allgemeine Menschenvernunft\".","- \"selbst\" could go with \"keinen anderen Richter ... als selbst\" (itself) or with \"Menschenvernunft\" (human reason itself); the English leaves both open.","- \"Kenntniß\" = knowledge here (glossary also: acquaintance; information); \"wissen\" (v.) = know; Wissen = knowledge (*scientia*). \"Beurtheilung\" = judgment (glossary: \"estimation\"). Beweise = proofs; Sätze = propositions; Grenzen = bounds; Wesen = being (but \"gemeinen Wesens\" = \"commonwealth\").","- \"sie\" before \"wissen\" (\"so little ... will it know\") could refer to the critique or to reason; left as \"it\".","- \"wegdemonstrirt haben solle\" = \"is supposed to have demonstrated away\".","- \"Dasein\" = existence; \"Zustand\" = state; \"bejahend/verneinend\" = affirmative/negatively."]],"krv-b-2244":["because I know beforehand that he will attack the illusory grounds of the other only in order to procure his own entrance, besides which an everyday illusion does not after all give so much material for new remarks as a strange and ingeniously devised one. By contrast the",["- \"Scheingründe\" = illusory grounds (Schein = illusion); \"Stoff\" = material; \"alltägiger\" read as \"alltäglicher\". Stray OCR \"F\" removed.","- Breaks off at \"Hingegen würde der\"; not completed."]],"krv-b-2245":["in his way also dogmatic religious opponent of my critique would give desired occupation and occasion for further rectification of its principles, without anything at all being to be feared on his account.",["- Word order kept: \"nach seiner Art auch dogmatische Religionsgegner meiner Kritik\" is rendered as \"in his way also dogmatic religious opponent of my critique\", the \"der\" of the previous passage being the article. \"Art\" = way (glossary).","- \"gewünschte Beschäftigung\" is without article in the German. \"Grund-jäke\" read as \"Grundsätze\" = principles. Stray \"4\" removed."]],"krv-b-2246":["But the youth entrusted to academic instruction should nevertheless at least be warned against such writings and kept from early acquaintance with such dangerous propositions, before their power of judgment has matured, or rather before the doctrine that one wants to ground in them has taken firm root, so as to resist forcefully all persuasion to the contrary, from wherever it may come? If it had to remain with the dogmatic procedure in matters of pure reason, and the dispatch of opponents were properly polemical, i.e., so constituted that one engaged in the fray and with proof-",["- Stray OCR marks (\"b\", \"f\", \"? ?\") removed; the question mark after \"kommen möge\" is in the German as printed.","- \"Kenntniß\" = acquaintance here (cf. \"knowledge\" elsewhere); Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Überredung = persuasion; Lehre = doctrine; Sätze = propositions.","- The passage breaks off mid-word at \"Be-\" (Beweisgründen); rendered \"proof-\", the word being completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2247":["grounds armed oneself for opposing assertions, then nothing would indeed be more advisable for the time being, but at the same time nothing vainer and more fruitless in the long run, than to place the reason of youth for a time",["- Begins with the second half of \"Beweisgründen\" (grounds of proof). \"bewaffnete\" is rendered \"armed oneself\" (the reflexive is carried over from \"einließe\").","- \"Dauer\" = long run (literally \"duration\"); \"vor der Hand\" = for the time being.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; not completed."]],"krv-b-2248":["under guardianship and at least so long to preserve it from seduction. But if in the sequel either curiosity or the fashionable tone of the age plays such writings into its hands: will that youthful persuasion then still hold up? The",["- \"Folge\" = sequel (glossary); \"Modeton\" = fashionable tone; \"Stich halten\" = hold up.","- Breaks off at \"Der-\" (Derjenige); rendered \"The\", completed in the next passage (\"The one who\")."]],"krv-b-2249":["one who brings nothing but dogmatic weapons to resist the attacks | of his opponent, and the concealed dialectic that does not",["- Begins with \"-jenige\"; \"Derjenige, der\" = \"the one who\". The \"|\" is retained where it stands in the OCR.","- Dialektik = dialectic; Angriffe = attacks. Breaks off mid-sentence; not completed."]],"krv-b-2250":["less in his own bosom than in that of the opponent, does not know how to develop, sees illusory grounds, which have the advantage of novelty, step forth against illusory grounds which no longer have anything of the kind, but rather arouse the suspicion of an abused credulity of youth. He believes he cannot show better that he has outgrown the discipline of children than when he sets himself above those well-meant warnings; and, dogmatically accustomed, he drinks in long drafts the poison that dogmatically corrupts his principles. Precisely the opposite of what is here advised must happen in academic instruction, but admittedly only under the presupposition of a thorough instruction in the Critique of Pure Reason. For in order to bring the principles of the same into practice as early as possible and to show their sufficiency against the greatest dialectical illusion, it is absolutely necessary to direct the attacks, so fearful for the dogmatist, against his reason, though still weak, yet enlightened through critique, and to let him make the attempt to test the groundless assertions of the opponent piece by piece on those principles. It cannot at all become difficult for him to dissolve them into sheer vapor, and thus he feels early his own power to secure himself completely against such harmful dazzling shows, which in the end must lose all illusion for him. Although now the very same blows that strike down the edifice of the enemy must be just as ruinous also to his own speculative structure, if he should perhaps think of erecting such a thing: he is nevertheless entirely unconcerned about this, since he does not need at all to dwell in it, but still has before him a prospect into the practical field, where he can hope with ground for a firmer soil on which to erect his rational and salutary system. |",["The passage begins mid-sentence; the main verb and subject of the opening clause are not in the text.","Scheingründe = \"illusory grounds\" (Schein = illusion, Grund = ground); Blendwerke = \"dazzling shows\" (not in the glossary).","Principien and Grundsätze are both rendered \"principles\"; \"derselben\" (feminine) is kept as \"of the same\", its antecedent left to the reader.","\"dogmatisch gewohnt\" = \"dogmatically accustomed\" is ambiguous: \"dogmatically\" may modify the adjective or the whole clause.","Zulänglichkeit = \"sufficiency\" (cf. zureichend); vernünftig = \"rational\"; \"mit Grunde\" = \"with ground\".","\"eben dieselbe Streiche\": the number agreement is as in the German (rendered \"the very same blows\")."]],"krv-b-2251":["Thus there is accordingly no proper polemic in the field of pure reason. Both parts are shadow-boxers who scuffle with their shadow, for they go beyond nature, where for their dogmatic grips there is nothing present that could be grasped and held. They may fight well; the shadows they hew apart grow together again in a moment, like the heroes in Valhalla, in order to be able to amuse themselves anew in bloodless combats. |",["Luftfechter = \"shadow-boxers\" (literally \"air-fencers\").","Theile = \"parts\" (Teil = part), though the sense is the two sides.","\"Sie haben gut kämpfen\" is idiomatic and could also be read as \"it is easy for them to fight\"; I kept \"They may fight well\".","Natur = nature; \"Griffe\" = \"grips\"."]],"krv-b-2252":["But there is also no permissible skeptical use of pure reason, which one could call the principle of neutrality in all its disputes. To set reason against itself, to hand it weapons on both sides and then to look on calmly and mockingly at its most heated combat, does not look well from a dogmatic point of view, but has the appearance of a gloating and malicious disposition of mind. If meanwhile one looks at the unconquerable blindness and boasting of the rationalizers, who will let themselves be moderated by no critique, then there is indeed really no other counsel than to set against the boasting on one side another, which is based on precisely the same rights, so that reason through the resistance of an enemy might at least only be made to balk, in order to put some doubt in its presumptions and to give a hearing to critique. But to let it be entirely at these doubts and",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence (\"Allein es ... bewenden zu lassen und\"); not completed.","Vernünftler = \"rationalizers\" (cf. vernünfteln = rationalize).","\"stutzig gemacht\" = \"made to balk\" (idiomatic; \"made suspicious\" or \"taken aback\" are also possible).","Gemüthsart = \"disposition of mind\" (Gemüt = mind); Anmaßungen = \"presumptions\"; Gesichtspunkt = \"point of view\".","\"welchen\" (Gebrauch) refers to \"use\"; \"welche\" in \"welche auf eben dieselben Rechte fußt\" refers to the \"other\" (boasting)."]],"krv-b-2253":["to stake it on this, the conviction and the confession of its Un- … to draw into examination. Since meanwhile this skeptical manner of extricating oneself from a vexatious business of reason seems as it were to be the short way to arrive at a persistent philosophical calm, at least the highway which those gladly take who think to give themselves a philosophical appearance in a mocking contempt of all investigations of this kind, I find it necessary to present this way of thinking in its peculiar light. On the Impossibility of a Skeptical Satisfaction",["The first sentence is fragmentary and the OCR text is corrupt: \"seiner Un-\" is followed directly by \"Prüfung zu ziehen\"; text is evidently missing, and I have not supplied it (marked \"…\").","The passage opens mid-sentence, continuing krv-b-2252, and ends with the first half of a heading continued in the next passage.","\"es darauf auszusetzen\" = \"to stake it on this\" (literally \"to set it out upon this\"); the antecedent of \"es\" and \"seiner\" is not in the passage.","Heeresstraße = \"highway\" (literally \"army road\"); Handel = \"business\"; Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\"; Ansehen = \"appearance\"; beharrlich = \"persistent\"."]],"krv-b-2254":["of pure reason at variance with itself.",["Continuation of the heading begun in krv-b-2253 (\"Von der Unmöglichkeit einer sceptischen Befriedigung\").","veruneinigt = \"at variance\"; \"mit sich selbst\" = \"with itself\"."]],"krv-b-2255":["The consciousness of my ignorance (if this is not cognized at the same time as necessary), instead of its ending my investigations, is rather the proper cause for awakening them. All ignorance is either that of things, or of the determination and boundaries of my cognition. If ignorance now is contingent, then it must drive me, in the first case to investigate dogmatically the things (objects), in the second critically the boundaries of my possible cognition. But that my ignorance is absolutely necessary and therefore acquits me of all further investigation, cannot be settled empirically, from observation, but only critically, through the fathoming of the first sources of our cognition. Thus the determination of the boundary of our reason can happen only according to grounds a priori; the restriction of the same, however, which is a cognition, though only indeterminate, of an ignorance never to be completely removed, can also be cognized a posteriori, through that which in all knowledge still always remains for us to know. That cognition of its ignorance, possible through critique of reason itself alone, is thus science, this is nothing but perception, of which one cannot say how far the inference as such might reach. If I represent to myself the surface of the earth (in accordance with sensible illusion) as a plate, I cannot know how far it extends. But experience teaches me this: that wherever I may come, I always see a space around me to which I could go further on; consequently I cognize limitations of my respectively actual knowledge of the earth, but not the boundaries of all possible description of the earth. But if I have yet come so far as to know that the earth is a sphere and its surface a spherical surface, then I can also from a small part of the same, e.g., the magnitude of a degree, cognize determinately and according to principles a priori the diameter and through this the complete delimitation of the earth, i.e., its surface; and although I am ignorant with regard to the objects that this surface may contain, I am nevertheless not so with regard to the domain that it contains, the magnitude and limitations of the same.",["Sachen and Gegenstände = \"things\" and \"objects\" (Gegenstände is given in parentheses in the text; Ding is also \"thing\").","Unwissenheit = \"ignorance\"; Wissen = \"knowledge\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Grenze = \"boundary\"; Schranke = \"limitation\"; Einschränkung = \"restriction\".","Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Schluß = \"inference\"; \"als selbiger\" = \"as such\" (literally \"as the same\").","\"bestimmt und nach Principien a priori erkennen\": \"determinately\" and \"a priori\" could attach to the verb or to the objects cognized; I left the placement open. Likewise \"Grenzbestimmung\" = \"determination of the boundary\".","Erdkunde = \"knowledge of the earth\"; Erdbeschreibung = \"description of the earth\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; \"jedesmal wirklichen\" = \"respectively actual\".","OCR slips silently corrected (\"unde\", \"die⸗ E ten\" → \"diesen\")."]],"krv-b-2256":["The sum total of all possible objects for our cognition seems to us to be a level surface, which has its apparent horizon, namely that which comprises the whole domain of the same, and has been called by us the concept of reason of unconditioned totality. To reach the same empirically is impossible, and to determine it | a priori according to a certain principle, to that end all attempts have been in vain. Meanwhile all questions of our pure reason nevertheless go to that which may lie outside this horizon, or at most also in its boundary line.",["Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Totalität = \"totality\" (the glossary's Allheit is also \"totality\"); Vernunftbegriff = \"concept of reason\".","scheinbar = \"apparent\"; befaßt = \"comprises\"; allenfalls = \"at most\" (also \"if need be\").","\"Empirisch denselben zu erreichen\": \"the same\" (denselben) is left without a stated antecedent, as in the German.","The sign | stands where it appears in the source."]],"krv-b-2257":["The famous David Hume was one of these geographers of human reason, who believed he had sufficiently dispatched those questions all together by relegating them outside the horizon of the same, which he nevertheless could not determine. He dwelt chiefly on the principle of causality and remarked of it quite correctly that one bases its truth (indeed not even the objective validity of the concept of an efficient cause in general) on no insight at all, i.e., cognition a priori, that therefore also not in the least the necessity of this law, but a mere general usefulness of the same in the course of experience and a subjective necessity arising from it, which he calls habit, makes up its whole standing. From the incapacity of our reason now to make of this principle a use going beyond all experience, he inferred the nullity of all presumptions of reason in general to go beyond the empirical.",["Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Ansehen = \"standing\" (elsewhere \"appearance\"); Einsicht = \"insight\".","\"fuße\" = \"bases\" (literally \"rests on\"); the subject \"man\" is rendered \"one\".","wirkende Ursache = \"efficient cause\"; Unvermögen = \"incapacity\"; schloß = \"inferred\" (schließen; cf. Schluß = inference).","\"jene Fragen insgesammt\" = \"those questions all together\"; hinreichend = \"sufficiently\".","OCR slips silently corrected (\"Gül tigkeit\", \"gar keine“\", \"ganz zes\")."]],"krv-b-2258":["One can call a procedure of this kind, of subjecting the *facta* of reason to examination and, as the case may be, to censure, the censorship of reason. It is beyond doubt that this censorship unavoidably leads to doubts against all transcendent use of principles. But this is only the second step, which is still far from completing the work. The first step in matters of pure reason, which marks its childhood, is dogmatic. The second step just named is skeptical, and testifies to the prudence of the power of judgment sharpened by experience. Now, however, a third step is still necessary, which belongs only to the matured and manly power of judgment, which has at its basis firm maxims, proven in their universality: namely, to subject to estimation not the *facta* of reason but reason itself, according to its whole faculty and aptitude for pure cognitions *a priori*; which is not the censorship but the critique of reason, through which not merely limitations but the determinate boundaries of it, not merely ignorance in one or another part but in regard to all possible questions of a certain kind, and indeed not merely conjectured but proved from principles, is demonstrated. Thus skepticism is a resting place for human reason, where it can reflect upon its dogmatic wandering and make a sketch of the region where it finds itself, in order to be able to choose its path further on with greater security, but not a dwelling place for permanent residence; for this can only be encountered in a complete certainty, be it of the cognition of the objects themselves, or of the boundaries within which all our cognition of objects is enclosed. Our reason is not some indeterminably far-extended plane, whose limitations one only cognizes in general, but must rather be compared with a sphere, whose radius can be found from the [curvature of the arc on its surface (the nature of synthetic propositions *a priori*), from which, however, the content and the delimitation of it can also be specified with certainty. Outside this sphere (field of experience) nothing is an object for it; indeed even questions about such supposed objects concern only subjective principles of a thoroughgoing determination of the relations which can occur among the concepts of the understanding within this sphere.",[]],"krv-b-2259":["Methodology. Chapter 1. Section 2. 498 [running head omitted] — thoroughgoing determination of the relations which can occur among the concepts of the understanding within this sphere.",["- krv-b-2259 is only a running-head and a repeat of the closing words of 2258 (gängigen Bestimmung… Verstandesbegriffen); the OCR splice \"durch⸗ / gängigen\" is rendered once there. Running head \"498 Methodenlehre. 1. Hauptstück. 2. Abschnitt.\" should have been omitted; the line above reflects the fragment as it stands.","- Censur = \"censorship\" (Latin-derived, cf. Kritik = \"critique\"); Facta left in Latin italics. Schranken = \"limitations\" (glossary Schranke); Grenzen = \"boundaries\" (Grenze).","- Urtheilskraft = \"power of judgment\"; Schätzung = \"estimation\" (not glossed); Tauglichkeit = \"aptitude.\"","- Gegenstände / Object both \"object\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\" and Principien = \"principles\" (both rendered \"principles\").","- \"wodurch… bewiesen wird\": the grammatical subject of the final verb is left as in German (series of \"nicht bloß… sondern\" members precedes \"bewiesen wird\").","- OCR stray quotation marks and the fragment \"zeugt“)\" silently removed; the bracket \"[\" before \"Krümmung\" is retained as in the passage.","- Ruheplatz/Wohnplatz = \"resting place\"/\"dwelling place.\""]],"krv-b-2260":["We are actually in possession of synthetic cognition *a priori*, as the principles of the understanding, which anticipate experience, demonstrate. If someone now cannot make the possibility of them comprehensible to himself at all, he may indeed at first doubt whether they also actually inhabit us *a priori*; but he cannot yet declare this to be an impossibility of them through mere powers of the understanding, and all steps that reason takes according to the guideline of these to be null. He can only say: if we saw into their origin and genuineness, we would be able to determine the domain and the boundaries of our reason; but before this has happened, all assertions of the latter are ventured blindly. And in such a way a thoroughgoing doubt about all dogmatic philosophy, which goes its way without a critique of reason itself, would be quite well grounded; but for that reason such a progression could still not be wholly denied to reason, if it were prepared and secured by a better laying of the foundation. For, first, all concepts, indeed all questions, which pure reason puts before us, lie not in experience but themselves in turn only in reason, and must therefore be able to be resolved and comprehended as to their validity or correctness. We are also not entitled to dismiss these problems, as if their resolution actually lay in the nature of things, under the pretext of our incapacity, and to refuse their further investigation, since reason has generated in its womb alone these ideas themselves, of whose validity or dialectical illusion it is therefore obliged to give an account.",["- Begriffe = \"concepts\"; begreiflich / begriffen = \"comprehensible\" / \"comprehended\" (begreifen).","- Kräfte = \"powers\"; Unvermögen = \"incapacity\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Grenzen = \"boundaries\"; Aufgaben = \"problems\"; Gültigkeit = \"validity\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; Fortgang = \"progression.\"","- \"der Richtschnur derselben\": \"of these\" (derselben) is left unresolved; could refer to the powers of the understanding or the principles. Likewise \"der letzten\" = \"the latter\" (reason).","- \"Auflösung\"/\"auflösen\" = \"resolution\"/\"resolve\"; OCR stray marks (\"nicht“\", \"7°\", \"A\", \"Nade\") silently corrected.","- \"wirklich\" = \"actually\"; Aechtheit = \"genuineness.\""]],"krv-b-2261":["All skeptical polemicizing is properly turned only against the dogmatist, who, without placing a mistrust in his original objective principles, i.e. without critique, gravely continues on his way, merely in order to put him out of his composure and bring him to self-cognition. In itself it makes absolutely no difference with regard to what we know and what, on the other hand, we cannot know. All failed dogmatic attempts of reason are *facta*, which it is always useful to subject to censorship. But this can decide nothing about the expectations of reason, to hope for a better success of its future endeavors and to make claims upon that; mere censorship can therefore never bring to an end the dispute over the rights of human reason.",["- Selbsterkenntniß = \"self-cognition\"; wissen = \"know\" (Wissen); Facta in Latin, italicized.","- \"ihm das Concept zu verrücken\" = \"to put him out of his composure\" (idiom; literally to displace the concept/draft, Concept in German spelling); doubtful rendering retained as idiomatic.","- \"An sich … sie\": \"it\" (sie) refers back to skeptical polemicizing; \"an sich\" = \"in itself.\"","- \"Rechtsame\" = \"rights\"; \"Versuche\" = \"attempts\"; OCR slips (\"l\", \"galso\") silently corrected.","- \"und darauf Ansprüche zu machen\": \"that\" (darauf) left as ambiguous as in German."]],"krv-b-2262":["Since Hume is perhaps the most ingenious among all skeptics and without contradiction the most distinguished with regard to the influence that the skeptical procedure can have on the awakening of a thorough examination of reason, it is well worth the trouble to represent the course of his inferences and the errors of so insightful and valuable a man, which nevertheless began on the trail of truth, so far as is fitting to my aim.",["- Schlüsse = \"inferences\" (Schluß); Absicht = \"aim\"; Vernunftprüfung = \"examination of reason.\"","- \"vorstellig zu machen\" = \"to represent\" (vorstellig machen; related to Vorstellung but not the same word); doubtful as to idiomatic nuance, kept literal.","- \"ohne Widerrede\" = \"without contradiction\" (not Widerspruch); OCR slip \"fcepstische\" read as \"skeptische.\"","- \"die doch auf der Spur der Wahrheit angefangen haben\": relative clause may attach to the errors (Verirrungen) or to the man; plural verb favors the errors."]],"krv-b-2263":["Hume had perhaps in his thoughts, although he never fully developed it, that in judgments of a certain kind we go beyond our concept of the object. I have called this kind of judgments synthetic. How I can go beyond my concept, which I have up to now, by means of experience, is subject to no difficulty. Experience is itself such a synthesis of perceptions, which augments my concept, which I have by means of a perception, through other, additional ones. But we also believe that we can go beyond our concept *a priori* and expand our cognition. This we attempt either through the pure understanding with regard to that which can at least be an object of experience, or even through pure reason with regard to such properties of things, or indeed also of the existence of such objects, that can never occur in experience. Our skeptic did not distinguish these two kinds of judgments, as he nevertheless should have done, and straightaway held this augmentation of concepts out of themselves, and, so to speak, the self-parturition of our understanding (together with reason), without being impregnated by experience, to be impossible, hence all supposed principles of it *a priori* to be imagined, and found that they are nothing but a habit arising from experience and its laws, hence merely empirical, i.e., in themselves contingent, rules, to which we ascribe a supposed necessity and universality. But to assert this strange proposition he appealed to the universally acknowledged principle of the relation of cause to effect. For since no faculty of understanding can lead us from the concept of a thing to the existence of something else, which is thereby given universally and necessarily: so he believed he could conclude from this that without experience we have nothing that could augment our concept and entitle us to such a judgment expanding itself *a priori*. That the sunlight, which illuminates the wax, at the same time melts it, while it hardens the clay, no understanding could guess from concepts that we had previously of these things, much less conclude according to law, and only experience could teach us such a law. On the contrary, we have seen in transcendental logic: that although we can never go immediately beyond the content of the concept that is given to us, we can nevertheless cognize completely *a priori*, but in relation to a third thing, namely possible experience, thus nevertheless *a priori*, the law of connection with other things. If, therefore, wax that was previously firm melts, I can cognize *a priori* that something must have preceded (e.g., the warmth of the sun) upon which this has followed according to a constant law, although without experience I could not cognize determinately, *a priori* and without the teaching of experience, either the cause from the effect, or the effect from the cause. He concluded, therefore, falsely from the contingency of our determination according to the law to the contingency of the law itself, and the going out from the concept of a thing to possible experience (which happens *a priori* and constitutes the objective reality of the same) he confused with the synthesis of the objects of actual experience, which is of course always empirical; thereby, however, he made out of a principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding and asserts necessary connection, a rule of association, which is encountered merely in the reproductive imagination and can present only contingent, not at all objective combinations. |",[]],"krv-b-2264":["The skeptical errors of this otherwise extremely acute man, however, arose chiefly from a deficiency that he nevertheless had in common with all dogmatists, namely that he did not survey systematically all kinds of synthesis of the understanding *a priori*. For then he would, without mentioning the remaining ones here, have found, e.g., the principle of persistence to be one that, just as much as that of causality, anticipates experience. Thereby he would also have been able to prescribe determinate boundaries for the understanding expanding itself *a priori* and for pure reason. But since he only limits our understanding without bounding it, and brings about a general mistrust but no determinate knowledge of the ignorance that is unavoidable for us; since he subjects some principles of the understanding to censure without bringing this understanding, with regard to its whole faculty, to the test-scales of critique, and, in denying it that which it really cannot accomplish, goes further and disputes all faculty in it to expand itself *a priori*, notwithstanding that he has not drawn this whole faculty into assessment: so there befalls him that which always strikes down skepticism, namely that he himself is doubted, since his objections rest only on facts, which are contingent, but not on principles, which could effect a necessary renunciation of the right to dogmatic assertions. Since he also knows no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical presumptions of reason, against which his attacks are chiefly directed: so reason, whose quite peculiar momentum has not in the least been disturbed hereby, but only hindered, feels the space for its expansion not closed off, and can never be wholly dissuaded from its attempts, although it is pinched here or there. For against attacks one arms oneself for defense and sets one's head all the more stiffly on it, in order to push through one's demands. A complete survey, however, of its whole faculty, and the conviction arising from it of the certainty of a small possession in the face of the vanity of higher claims, ends all dispute and moves one to content oneself peaceably with a limited but uncontested property.",[]],"krv-b-2265":["Against the uncritical dogmatist, who has not measured the sphere of his understanding, hence has not determined the boundaries of his possible cognition according to principles, who therefore does not already know in advance how much he can do, but thinks to find it out through mere attempts, these skeptical attacks are not only dangerous, but even ruinous to him. For if he is caught in a single assertion that he cannot justify, but whose illusion he also cannot develop from principles, then suspicion falls on all of them, however persuasive they may otherwise be.",[]],"krv-b-2266":["And thus the skeptic is the taskmaster of the dogmatic rationalizer toward a sound critique of the understanding and of reason itself. When he has arrived there, he has no further challenge to fear; for he then distinguishes his possession from that which lies entirely outside it, to which he makes no claims and about which he also cannot be entangled in disputes. Thus the skeptical procedure is indeed in itself not satisfying for the questions of reason, but nevertheless preparatory, in order to awaken its caution and to point to thorough means that can secure it in its rightful possessions.",[]],"krv-b-2267":["Of the first Main Part,\nThird Section.",[]],"krv-b-2268":["The discipline of pure reason with regard to",[]],"krv-b-2269":["hypotheses.",[]],"krv-b-2270":["Since we finally know so much through the critique of our reason, that in its pure and speculative use we can in fact know nothing at all: should it not open a field all the wider to hypotheses, since it is at least permitted to invent and to opine, 17 even if not to assert? |",["- krv-b-2263: *Erkenntniß* = cognition; *Gegenstand* and *Object* both = \"object\"; *Dasein* = existence; *Verknüpfung* = connection; *Verbindung* = combination; *Wahrnehmung* = perception.","- krv-b-2263: *Einbildungskraft* = imagination; *Belehrung* = teaching; *Zufälligkeit* = contingency; *Bestimmung* = determination; *Erfahrung* = experience; *Wirkung* = effect.","- krv-b-2263: OCR slips silently corrected (e.g., \"sentwickelte\", \"si nterworfen\", \"Rothwendig\", \"Gefeht\", \"faigen\"). \"so zu faigen\" read as *so zu sagen* (\"so to speak\").","- krv-b-2263: \"Vermehrung der Begriffe aus sich selbst\" — \"aus sich selbst\" could modify *Vermehrung* or *Begriffe*; left as in German.","- krv-b-2263: \"Tvon\" read as *Thon* (clay); \"Urtheillt\" as *Urtheil*. \"Rothwendig\" read as *nothwendig*.","- krv-b-2263: \"Gesetz\" = law; \"Princip\" = principle; \"Grundsatz\" = principle (both rendered \"principle\"); *Realität* = reality.","- krv-b-2264: *Grenze* = boundary / *begrenzen* = to bound; *einschränken* = to limit; *Verstand* = understanding; *Vernunft* = reason; *Beharrlichkeit* = persistence; *Überzeugung* = conviction; *Behauptung* = assertion; *Kenntniß* = knowledge (not *Wissen*); *Überschlag* = survey (my choice, not in glossary).","- krv-b-2265/2270: *Schein* = illusion; *überredend* = persuasive (cf. *Überredung*, persuasion); *wissen* = know; *Vernünftler* (krv-b-2266, \"Vernünftlers\") = rationalizer, from *vernünfteln*; *Meinen* = opine, *behaupten* = assert; *Mittel* = means; the \"17\" in krv-b-2270 is an OCR remnant, retained as it stands."]],"krv-b-2271":["Where imagination is not perhaps to rave, but rather to invent under the strict supervision of reason, something must always be fully certain beforehand, and not invented or mere opinion, and that is the possibility of the object itself. Then it is indeed permitted, on account of the actuality of the same, to take one's refuge in opinion, which, however, in order not to be groundless, must be brought into connection with what is actually given and consequently certain, as ground of explanation, and is then called hypothesis.",["- OCR \"fol\" read as \"soll\" and \"wer muß\" as \"werden muß\".","- Einbildungskraft = imagination; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Erklärungsgrund = ground of explanation; Gegenstand = object; Verknüpfung = connection.","- \"desselben\" could refer to \"Gegenstandes\" or to \"Möglichkeit\"; I kept \"of the same\".","- dichten = invent, erdichtet = invented (same stem)."]],"krv-b-2272":["Since we now cannot make for ourselves the least concept of the possibility of dynamic connection *a priori*, and the category of the pure understanding does not serve to think up the like of it, but only, where it is encountered in experience, to understand: so we cannot devise originally a single object in accordance with these categories, according to a new and empirically not specifiable property, and lay them as ground of a permitted hypothesis; for this would mean to lay beneath reason empty figments of the brain instead of concepts of things. Thus it is not permitted to think up for oneself any new original forces, e.g., an understanding that would be capable of intuiting its object without senses, or a force of extension without any contact, or a new kind of substances, e.g., one that would be present in space without impenetrability, consequently also no community of substances that is different from all of that which experience affords, no presence otherwise than in space, no duration than merely in time. In a word: it is only possible for our reason to use the conditions of possible experience as conditions of the possibility of things, but by no means, entirely independently of these, to create some for itself, as it were, because concepts of this sort, although without contradiction, nevertheless also without object be",["- OCR slips corrected: \"h a priori\", \"f\", \"z `\", \"Erfah— rung\", \"A Begriffe\". The passage breaks off after \"sein\"; no verb supplied.","- \"a priori\" could attach to \"Möglichkeit\" or to \"sich ... einen Begriff machen\"; left ambiguous.","- erdenken = think up; aussinnen = devise; \"ursprünglich\" = originally (could also be \"primitively\"/\"as original\").","- Sachen = things (Ding is also \"thing\"). Art = kind. Gemeinschaft = community. Kraft = force; Ausdehnung = extension. \"an die Hand giebt\" = affords.","- \"keine Gegenwart ... keine Dauer\" carry on the construction after \"folglich auch\" as in the German; not smoothed."]],"krv-b-2273":["Concepts of reason are, as said, mere ideas and admittedly have no object in any experience, but nevertheless do not on that account designate invented objects that are at the same time assumed to be possible. They are thought merely problematically, in order to found, in relation to them (as heuristic fictions), regulative principles of the systematic use of the un-",["- The passage breaks off mid-word (\"Der-\"); the next passage continues \"standesgebrauchs\", hence the hyphen and \"un-\".","- Vernunftbegriffe = concepts of reason; Principien = principles; \"Fictionen\" kept as \"fictions\"; gedichtet = invented."]],"krv-b-2274":["derstanding in the field of experience, to ground. If one goes from this",["- Begins with the second half of \"Verstandesgebrauchs\" (understanding-use); ends before \"ab\" (which opens the next passage), so \"goes from this\" is not completed.","- Erfahrung = experience."]],"krv-b-2275":["off, then they are mere thought-entities, whose possibility is not provable, and which therefore also cannot be laid as ground of the explanation of actual appearances through a hypothesis. To think the soul as simple is quite well permitted, in order to lay, in accordance with this idea, a complete and necessary unity of all forces of the mind, although one cannot have insight into it *in concreto*, as principle of our estimation of their",["- Begins with \"ab\" completing \"Geht man davon ab\" of the preceding passage; breaks off after \"ihrer\".","- Gedankendinge = thought-entities; Beurteilung = estimation; Gemüthskräfte = forces of the mind; erweislich = provable; wirklich = actual; einsehen = have insight into.","- \"zu legen\" stands in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2276":["inner appearances to lay. But to assume the soul as simple substance (a transcendent concept) would be a proposition that would be not only unprovable (as several physical hypotheses are), but also quite arbitrary and ventured blindly, because the simple can occur in no experience at all, and, if one understands by substance here the persistent object of sensible intuition, the possibility of a simple appearance is not at all to be seen into. Merely intelligible beings or merely intelligible properties of the things of the world of sense can be assumed as opinion with no well-grounded warrant of reason, although (because one has no concepts of their possibility or impossibility) also by no supposed better insight dogmatically denied.",["- OCR corrected: \"all— ein\", \"b\", \"9 Substanz\".","- \"inneren Erscheinungen zu legen\" completes the construction begun in the previous passage (\"zum Princip ... zu legen\").","- transscendent = transcendent (distinct from transscendental). Object here = Objekt (Gegenstand also \"object\").","- Wesen = beings; Eigenschaften = properties (Beschaffenheit is also \"property\" in the glossary); Sinnenwelt = world of sense.","- The last clause (\"auch durch keine ... ableugnen\") is left without an expressed auxiliary, as in the German; \"lassen sich\" governs both infinitives."]],"krv-b-2277":["For the explanation of given appearances, no other things and grounds of explanation can be adduced than those that, in accordance with already known laws of appearances, have been posited in connection with the given ones. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea",["- OCR corrected: \"5 jheinungen\" read as \"Erscheinungen\". Breaks off mid-sentence.","- Erklärung = explanation; Erscheinungen = appearances; Gesetze = laws; setzen = posit; Verknüpfung = connection."]],"krv-b-2278":["of reason were used for the explanation of natural things, would therefore be no explanation at all, in that what one does not sufficiently understand from known empirical principles would be explained through something of which one understands nothing at all. Also the principle of such a hypothesis would really serve only for the satisfaction of reason and not for the promotion of the use of the understanding with regard to objects. Order and purposiveness in nature must in turn be explained from natural grounds and according to natural laws, and here even the wildest hypotheses, if only they are physical, are more tolerable than a hyperphysical one, i.e., the appeal to a divine author, whom one presupposes to this end. For that would be a principle of lazy reason (*ignava ratio*), to pass by, all at once, all causes whose objective reality, at least as to possibility, one can still get to know through continued experience, in order to rest in a mere idea, which is very convenient to reason. But as for the absolute totality of the ground of explanation in the series of the same, that can make no hindrance with regard to world-objects, because, since these are nothing but appearances, something completed in the synthesis of the series of conditions can never be hoped for in them.",["- OCR corrected: \"einer“ solchen\", \"nicht“\", \"fetbft\", \"in!)\", \"To- f ‚talität\", \"Erflärungsgrundes\", \"E\".","- hinreichend = sufficiently (zureichend is \"sufficient\" in the glossary); verstehen = understand; Gegenstände and Objecte (in \"Weltobjecte\") are both \"object\".","- Behuf = \"end\" (Zweck is \"purpose\"). Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness; Urheber = author; kennen lernen = get to know.","- \"derselben\" in \"in der Reihe derselben\" has no clear antecedent (Erklärungsgrund / Ursachen); left as \"of the same\".","- The glossary's \"Totalität\" (Allheit) is not at issue here; \"Totalität\" = totality."]],"krv-b-2279":["Transcendental hypotheses of the speculative use of reason, and a freedom, for the replacement of the lack of physical grounds of explanation, to make use if need be of hyperphysical ones, cannot be permitted at all, partly because reason is thereby not brought further at all, but rather cuts off the whole progress of its use, partly because this license would in the end have to deprive it of all the fruits of the cultivation of its own soil, namely experience. For if the explanation of nature becomes difficult for us here or there, we have constantly a transcendent ground of explanation at hand, which relieves us of that investigation, and our research concludes not through insight but through the complete incomprehensibility of a principle, which was so thought up in advance that it had to contain the concept of the absolutely first.",["- OCR corrected: \"Denn :\".","- \"eine Freiheit\" kept as \"a freedom\"; the construction \"Hypothesen ... und eine Freiheit ... kann\" (singular verb) is left as is.","- Fortgang = progress; Unbegreiflichkeit = incomprehensibility (begreifen = comprehend); Einsicht = insight; überheben = relieve; Nachforschung = research; Licenz = license.","- \"hyperphysischer\" is genitive plural, \"of hyperphysical ones\"; noun not supplied."]],"krv-b-2280":["The second piece required for the acceptability of a hypothesis is the adequacy of the same, in order to determine from it *a priori* the sequels that are given. If one is compelled for this purpose to call in assisting hypotheses, they give the suspicion of a mere invention, because each of them in itself needs the same justi-",["- OCR corrected: \"By- | pothese\", \"Der: © dacht\". The passage breaks off mid-word (\"Recht-\"); \"fertigung\" opens the next passage.","- Folgen = sequels (glossary Folge; Folgerung would be \"consequence\"). Zulänglichkeit = adequacy (zureichend is \"sufficient\"). Annehmungswürdigkeit = worthiness of acceptance, rendered \"acceptability\". Zweck = purpose.","- Erdichtung = invention; \"a priori\" could attach to \"bestimmen\" or to \"Folgen\"; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-2281":["fication, which the thought laid as ground had needed, and therefore can provide no competent witness. If, under the presupposition of an unrestrictedly perfect cause, to be sure in explanation-",["- Begins with \"fertigung\", completing \"Rechtfertigung\" (justification). Breaks off mid-word (\"Erklärungs-\"); no completion supplied.","- \"jede derselben\" in the preceding passage is the grammatical subject of \"bedarf\" and \"kann ... abgeben\".","- Voraussetzung = presupposition; Ursache = cause; Zeuge = witness."]],"krv-b-2282":["...grounds of all purposiveness, order, and magnitude that are found in the world there is no lack, that [hypothesis] still requires, given the deviations and ills that show themselves at least according to our concepts, yet new hypotheses, in order to be rescued against these as objections. If the simple self-subsistence of the human soul, which has been laid down as the ground of its appearances, is contested by the difficulties of its phenomena, similar to the alterations of a matter (to growth and decrease), then new hypotheses must be called to help, which are indeed not without illusion, but yet without any authentication, except that which the opinion assumed as the chief ground gives them, for which they are nevertheless supposed to speak. | If the assertions of reason cited here as examples (the incorporeal unity of the soul and the existence of a highest being) are supposed to count not as hypotheses but as dogmas proved a priori, then there is no talk of them at all. But in such a case one should certainly take care that the proof have the apodictic certainty of a demonstration. For to want to make the actuality of such ideas merely probable is an absurd resolve, just as if one thought to prove a proposition of geometry merely probably. Reason, separated from all experience, can cognize everything only a priori and as necessary, or not at all; hence its judgment is never opinion, but either abstention from all judgment, or apodictic certainty. Opinions and probable judgments about what pertains to things can occur only as grounds of explanation of what is actually given, or as sequels according to empirical laws of what lies at the ground as actual, hence only in the series of the objects of experience. Outside this field, to opine is as much as to play with tho-",["- The passage begins mid-word (\"gründen\"), so \"...grounds\" is left fragmentary; the sentence's opening is not given, and \"jene\" is left unresolved (rendered \"that [hypothesis]\" only as the feminine antecedent allows, no further identification).","- Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness; Größe = magnitude; Begriffe = concepts; Erscheinungen = appearances; Phänomene = phenomena (distinct German words).","- \"Selbstständigkeit\" is not in the glossary: \"self-subsistence.\" \"nicht ohne Schein\": Schein = illusion (could also be read as \"semblance\").","- Meinung/Meinen = opinion/to opine; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Dasein = existence; Gegenstände = objects; Folgen = sequels.","- \"Ge-\" / \"danken\" is split across the passage boundary; rendered \"tho-\" / \"ughts\" to keep the break.","- \"Wahrscheinlich\" = probable; \"Vernunftbehauptungen\" = assertions of reason; \"Dogmate\" = dogmas; the OCR \"Ffinden\" and the stray \"i\" and line numerals were silently corrected or dropped."]],"krv-b-2283":["ughts, unless it were that one had merely the opinion, about an uncertain path of judgment, of perhaps finding the truth on it. But although in merely speculative questions of pure reason no hypotheses take place in order to ground propositions on them, they are",["- The passage begins with the second half of \"Gedanken\" (see the preceding passage).","- \"von einem unsicheren Wege des Urtheils bloß die Meinung hätte, ...\" preserved as in the German; the construction (opinion \"about\" the path / of finding truth on it) is loose in the original.","- Sätze = propositions; Urtheil = judgment; Meinung = opinion."]],"krv-b-2284":["nevertheless entirely permissible, in order at most only to defend them, i.e. not indeed in the dogmatic, but yet in the polemical use. By defense, however, I understand not the increase of the grounds of proof of one's | assertion, but the mere frustration of the illusory insights of the opponent, which are supposed to do damage to our asserted proposition. Now, however, all synthetic propositions from pure reason have this peculiarity about them: that if he who asserts the reality of certain ideas never knows so much as to make this his proposition certain, on the other side the opponent can know just as little in order to assert the opposite. This equality of the lot of human reason indeed favors neither of the two in speculative cognition, and there too is the proper battlefield of feuds never to be settled. But it will show itself in the sequel that nevertheless with regard to the practical use reason has a right to assume something that it would in no way be authorized to presuppose in the field of mere speculation without sufficient grounds of proof, because all such presuppositions",["- Scheineinsichten = \"illusory insights\" (Schein = illusion).","- Realität = reality; Erkenntnisse = cognition; Gebrauch = use; Beweisgründe = grounds of proof; hinreichend = \"sufficient\" (glossary's zureichend is also \"sufficient\").","- \"in der Folge\" rendered \"in the sequel\" (Folge).","- \"Behauptung\" / \"behaupten\" = assertion / assert; \"Widerspiel\" = \"opposite.\"","- The OCR \"Bez weisgründe\" corrected to \"Beweisgründe.\""]],"krv-b-2285":["do damage to the perfection of speculation, about which, however, the practical interest does not trouble itself at all. There, then, it is in possession, the legitimacy of which it need not prove, and of which it also could not in fact conduct the proof. The opponent is therefore to prove. But since the latter knows just as little of the doubted object",["- The passage opens with OCR debris (\"re\") before \"der Vollkommenheit,\" omitted as a line-number remnant.","- Vollkommenheit = perfection; Gegenstand = object; Beweis = proof.","- \"Dort\" is left as \"There,\" without specifying its reference; \"sie\" = reason, as the German's feminine pronoun allows.","- The sentence breaks off with a subordinate clause (\"Da ... weiß,\") continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2286":["in order to show its non-being than the former, who asserts its actuality: so here there shows itself an advantage on the side of him who asserts something as a practically necessary presupposition (*melior est conditio possidentis*). For he is free, as it were from self-defense, to avail himself, for his good cause, of the very same means as the opponent against it, i.e. of hypotheses, which are not supposed to serve at all to strengthen the proof of the same, but only to show that the opponent understands far too little of the object of the dispute for him to flatter himself with an advantage of speculative insight with regard to us. |",["- The stray numeral \"2\" and the OCR \"dec hauptet\" (= \"behauptet\") were corrected silently.","- Nichtsein = non-being; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Gegenstand = object; Mittel = means; Voraussetzung = presupposition.","- \"Nothwehr\" = self-defense; the pronoun \"derselben\" is left as ambiguous as in the German.","- \"in Ansehung unserer\" = \"with regard to us\"; the phrase is left as it stands.","- The Latin legal maxim is in Kant's roman type and is italicized."]],"krv-b-2287":["Hypotheses are therefore permitted in the field of pure reason only as weapons of war, not in order to ground a right on them, but only to defend it. But the opponent we must here always seek in ourselves. For speculative reason in its transcendental use | is in itself dialectical. The objections that might be feared lie in ourselves. We must search them out, like old claims that never lapse by prescription, in order to ground an eternal peace on their annihilation. Outer calm is only apparent. The germ of the attacks, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be extirpated; but how can we extirpate it if we do not give it freedom, even nourishment, to shoot out herbage, in order thereby to reveal itself, and afterwards to destroy it with the root? Think up, accordingly, yourselves",["- Vernunft = reason; transscendental = transcendental; dialektisch = dialectical; an sich = in itself.","- \"Kriegswaffen\" = weapons of war; \"Anfechtungen\" = attacks (also \"challenges\"); \"Einwürfe\" = objections.","- \"Vernichtignug\" (OCR) read as \"Vernichtung\"; \"Außere\" read as \"Äußere.\"","- \"scheinbar\" = \"apparent\" (can also mean \"seeming\"); left as English \"apparent.\"","- \"Kraut auszuschießen\" kept literal (\"to shoot out herbage\"); the verb \"entdecken\" = reveal.","- The imperative \"Sinnet ... auf\" is continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2288":["on objections that no opponent has yet hit upon, and even lend him",["- \"auf ... fallen\" = \"hit upon\"; the construction is continued in the next passage.","- Einwürfe = objections; Gegner = opponent."]],"krv-b-2289":["weapons, or concede to him the most favorable place that he can only wish for! There is nothing at all to be feared in this, but rather to be hoped,",["- \"Platz\" = place (Ort in the glossary is a different word).","- \"wohl aber zu hoffen\" continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2290":["namely that you will procure for yourselves a possession never again to be contested in all future.",["- The stray OCR \"o\" (line numeral) was dropped.","- \"Besitz\" = possession; \"anzufechtenden\" = to be contested (cf. Anfechtungen, \"attacks\")."]],"krv-b-2291":["To your complete armor there now belong also the hypotheses of pure reason, which, although only leaden weapons (because they are not steeled by any law of experience), nevertheless always accomplish as much as those of which some opponent may avail himself against you. If, then,",["- \"Wenn euch\" is carried on in the next passages; \"euch\" is the plural familiar address (\"you\").","- Erfahrungsgesetz = law of experience; \"vermögen\" (verb) = \"accomplish\" (cf. Vermögen = faculty), the verb used with \"so viel.\"","- Rüstung = armor; Hypothesen = hypotheses."]],"krv-b-2292":["against the nature of the soul (assumed in some other, not speculative respect), immaterial and not subject to any corporeal transformation,",["- The sentence is fragmentary (\"Wenn euch also wider ...\" continues in the next passages); \"also\" is placed as in the preceding passage's final words, and \"euch\" in 2291 belongs to this sentence.","- \"auge-/nommene\" (OCR) read as \"angenommene\"; \"Rücksicht\" = respect.","- \"wider die ... Natur der Seele\": the participial phrase is kept before \"Natur\" as in the German order, with the parenthesis preserved.","- Seele = soul; Natur = nature; Umwandlung = transformation."]],"krv-b-2293":["the difficulty comes up that nevertheless experience",["- \"die Schwierigkeit aufstößt\" = \"the difficulty comes up\" (the verb aufstoßen); continued in the next passage.","- Erfahrung = experience."]],"krv-b-2294":["seems to prove the elevation as well as the derangement of our powers of mind merely as various modification of our organs: so you can weaken the force of this proof by assuming that our body",["- Geisteskräfte = \"powers of mind\" (Geist = mind; spirit).","- Modification, Organen kept as in the German (Latinate \"Modification\"; plural \"Organen\" is the text's form); Beweis = proof; Kraft = force.","- \"bloß als verschiedene Modification\": the adverb \"bloß\" may attach to \"beweisen\" or to \"als ... Modification\"; left as ambiguous as the German.","- Stray \"o\" (line numeral) omitted.","- The clause \"daß ihr annehmt, unser Körper ...\" continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2295":["is nothing but the fundamental appearance to which, as condition, in the present state (in life) the entire faculty of sensibility and with this all thinking relates. The separation from the body would be the end of this sensible use of your cognitive power and the beginning of the intellectual. |",["- \"sei\" (subjunctive) is rendered \"would be\" in the indirect discourse; the German \"sei\" in the first clause is rendered by the preceding passage's \"assuming that ... is\".","- Fundamentalerscheinung = fundamental appearance; Zustand = state; Vermögen = faculty; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Gebrauch = use.","- Erkenntnißkraft = \"cognitive power\" (Erkenntnis = cognition; Kraft = power); \"intellectuellen\" = intellectual.","- OCR \"Sinn— lichkeit\" joined as \"Sinnlichkeit\"; \"hiemit\" = \"with this.\"","- The sentence continues to the next passage (\"und der Anfang des intellectuellen\" is followed by the Academy's page break); \"der An-/fang\" is rejoined."]],"krv-b-2296":["The body would therefore not be the cause of",["- Körper = body; Ursache = cause; \"wäre\" = \"would be.\"","- The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2297":["thinking, but a merely restricting condition of it, hence",["- \"restringirende\" rendered \"restricting\"; Bedingung = condition; \"mithin\" = \"hence.\""]],"krv-b-2298":["indeed to be regarded as a promotion of the sensible and animal, but all the more",["- \"Beförderung\" = promotion (also \"furtherance\"); \"sinnlichen\" = sensible; \"animalischen\" = animal.","- Construction \"zwar ... aber desto mehr auch\" preserved."]],"krv-b-2299":["also as a hindrance of the pure and spiritual life, and the",["- Hinderniß = hindrance; rein = pure; \"spirituellen\" = spiritual; Leben = life."]],"krv-b-2300":["dependence of the former on the corporeal constitution would prove nothing",["- Abhängigkeit = dependence; Beschaffenheit = constitution; \"des ersteren\" = \"of the former,\" pronoun reference left unresolved."]],"krv-b-2301":["for the dependence of the entire life on the state of our organs. But you can go still further and perhaps even discover new doubts, either not raised, or not driven far enough",["- \"ausfindig machen\" is completed in the next passage (\"ausfindig / machen\").","- Zustand = state; Organen kept as the German plural; Zweifel = doubts.","- Stray OCR hyphens (\"Or— ganen\") corrected."]],"krv-b-2302":["to find out",["- \"ausfindig\" = \"to find out\" (with \"machen\" in the next passage: \"to make out/discover\").","- The phrase \"weit genug getrieben\" (\"driven far enough\") ends the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-2303":["make.",["- Final word \"machen\" of the sentence (\"ausfindig machen\"), rendered separately because the passage is a single word."]],"krv-b-2304":["The contingency of procreations, which in human beings as in irrational creatures depends on opportunity, but moreover also often on sustenance, on the government, on its moods and whims, often even on vice, makes a great difficulty against the opinion of the duration, extending to eternities, of a creature whose life has first begun under circumstances so insignificant and so entirely left over to our freedom. As far as the duration of the whole genus (here on earth) is concerned, this difficulty has little to it with regard to the same, since chance in the particular is nonetheless subject to a rule in the whole; but with regard to each individual to expect so powerful an effect from such trifling causes seems certainly questionable. Against this, however, you can summon up a transcendental hypothesis: that all life is properly only intelligible, not at all subject to changes of time, and has neither begun through birth nor will be ended through death; that this life is nothing but a mere appearance, i.e., a sensible representation of the pure spiritual life, and the whole world of sense a mere image, which hovers before our present way of cognition and, like a dream, has in itself no objective reality; that if we were to intuit things and ourselves as they are, we would see ourselves in a | world of spiritual natures, with which our sole true community has neither begun through birth nor will cease through the death of the body (as mere appearances), and so on.",[]],"krv-b-2305":["Although now we know not the least of all this that we here put forward hypothetically against the attack, nor do we assert it in earnest, and the whole is not even an idea of reason, but merely a concept thought up for defense, we nevertheless proceed in this quite rationally, in that we only show the opponent, who believes he has exhausted all possibility, in that he falsely passes off the lack of its empirical conditions for a proof of the complete impossibility of what we believe: that he can just as little encompass the whole field of possible things in themselves by mere laws of experience, as we can acquire anything for our reason, outside of experience, in a well-grounded way. He who provides such hypothetical countermeasures against the presumptions of the boldly denying opponent must not be taken as if he wished to appropriate them to himself as his true opinions. He abandons them as soon as he has dispatched the dogmatic self-conceit of the opponent. For however modest and moderate it may look when someone behaves merely refusingly and denyingly with regard to the assertions of others, yet, always, as soon as he wants to make these objections of his count as proofs of the opposite, the claim is no less proud and conceited than if he had taken up the affirming party and its assertion.",[]],"krv-b-2306":["One sees from this, then, that in the speculative use of reason hypotheses have no validity as opinions in themselves, but only relative to opposed transcendent presumptions. For the extension of the principles of possible experience to the possibility of things in general is just as much transcendent as the assertion of the objective reality of such concepts as can find their objects nowhere except outside the boundary of all possible experience. What pure reason judges assertorically must (like everything that reason cognizes) be necessary, or it is nothing at all. Accordingly it contains in fact no opinions at all. The hypotheses mentioned, however, are only problematic judgments, which at least cannot be refuted, although to be sure they cannot be proved by anything, and are thus pure private opinions, but yet cannot properly (even for inner tranquillity) be dispensed with against scruples that stir. But in this quality one must maintain them and carefully prevent them from appearing as attested in themselves and of some absolute validity and drowning reason",[]],"krv-b-2307":["among fictions and delusions.",[]],"krv-b-2308":["Of the First Chapter,\nFourth Section.",[]],"krv-b-2309":["The Discipline of Pure Reason with Regard to",[]],"krv-b-2310":["Its Proofs.",[]],"krv-b-2311":["The proofs of transcendental and synthetic propositions have this peculiarity among all proofs of a synthetic cognition a priori, that reason in them may not turn straight to the object by means of its concepts, but must first establish the objective validity of the concepts and the possibility of the synthesis of them a priori. This is not merely a necessary rule of caution, but concerns the essence and the possibility of the proofs themselves. If I am to go beyond the concept of an object a priori, this is impossible without a particular guide located outside this concept. In mathematics it is the intuition a priori that guides my synthesis, and there all inferences can be conducted immediately on pure intuition. In transcendental cognition, so long as it has to do merely with concepts of the understanding, this guideline is possible experience. For the proof does not show that the given concept (e.g., of that which happens) leads straight to another concept (that of a cause); for such a transition would be a leap that could not be answered for at all; rather it shows that experience itself, hence the object of experience, would be impossible without such a connection. Thus the proof had at the same time to indicate the possibility of arriving synthetically and a priori at a certain cognition of things that was not contained in the concept of them. Without this attentiveness proofs run like water that breaks through its banks, wildly and cross-country, to where the slope of hidden association accidentally leads them. The illusion of conviction, which rests on subjective causes of association and is taken for insight into a natural affinity, can in no way balance the hesitancy that must justly arise about such risky steps. Hence all attempts, admissions of the connoisseurs, have also been in vain; and before the transcendental critique appeared, people preferred, since they yet could not abandon this principle, to appeal defiantly to healthy common understanding (a refuge that always proves that the cause of reason is desperate), rather than to want to attempt new dogmatic proofs.",["- krv-b-2304: \"Gattung\" = genus; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Meinung\" = opinion; \"Erkenntnißart\" = way of cognition. Garbled OCR (\"ver— is nunftlosen\", \"ı\", \"1 Sachen\") silently corrected; the sign | kept as in the source.","- krv-b-2304: \"Hiewider\" rendered \"Against this\"; \"Ansehung ... derselben\" is ambiguous in reference (the genus or its duration); left as German.","- krv-b-2305: \"Der solche hypothetische Gegenmittel ... vorkehrt\" kept as relative-clause subject (\"He who\"). The sentence beginning \"Ob wir nun gleich\" is kept whole, including its \"in that ... in that\" constructions. \"Möglichkeit\" = possibility; \"Beweis\" = proof.","- krv-b-2305: \"als wir ... erwerben können\" depends on \"eben so wenig\"; the \"nur zeigen: daß ...\" clause is long and its syntax is kept as is. \"Eigendünkel\" = self-conceit.","- krv-b-2306: \"Objectiven Realität\" = objective reality; \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"Grenze\" = boundary; \"Gültigkeit\" = validity. \"als)\" in the OCR corrected to \"als\" (rendered \"as attested\"). \"durch T nicht\" is an OCR slip, probably \"durch nichts\"; rendered \"by anything\".","- krv-b-2306 / 2307: the sentence breaks across two passages; \"ersäufen\" = drown, kept in its own passage.","- krv-b-2311: \"Geständnisse der Kenner\" has no verb and its relation to \"Versuche\" is obscure; kept as an apposition. \"Jan sich\" and \"Gr- f kenntniß\" corrected to \"an sich\"/\"Erkenntniß\"; \"Im transscendentalen Erkenntniß\" kept in its German gender as printed. \"Afo\" read \"Also\". \"Schluß\" = inference; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Überzeugung\" = conviction; \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Verstand\" = understanding.","- krv-b-2311: \"gefunden Menschenverstand\" read as \"gesunden\" (\"healthy\"). \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" both \"object\". \"Behutsamkeit\" = caution."]],"krv-b-2312":["But if the proposition for which a proof is to be conducted is an assertion of pure reason, and I even want, by means of mere ideas, to go beyond my concepts of experience, then it would have to contain within itself, all the more, the justification of such a step of synthesis (if it were possible at all) as a necessary condition of its power of proof. However plausible, therefore, the supposed proof of the simple nature of our thinking substance from the unity of apperception may be, the scruple nevertheless stands irrefutably against it: that, since absolute simplicity is after all not a concept that can be related immediately to a perception, but, as idea,³⁵) must merely be inferred, it is not to be seen at all how mere consciousness, which is contained in all thinking, or at least can be, although to that extent it is a simple representation, is supposed to lead me over to the consciousness and the acquaintance of a thing in which thinking alone can be contained. For if I represent to myself the force of my body in motion, then to that extent it is absolute unity for me, and my representation of it is simple; hence I can also express this through the motion of a point, because its volume does nothing here and can be as small as one wishes without diminution of the force, and thus can also be thought as located in a point. From this, however, I will not infer: that, if nothing but the moving force of a body is given to me, the body can be thought as a simple substance, because its representation abstracts from all magnitude of the content of space and is therefore simple. Through this, now, that the simple in abstraction is entirely different from the simple in the object, and that the I, which in the first sense comprises no manifoldness in itself, in the second, since it signifies the soul itself, can be a very complex concept, namely to contain and designate very much under itself, I discover a paralogism. But in order to have a presentiment of this beforehand (for without such a preliminary conjecture one would conceive no suspicion at all against the proof), it is absolutely necessary to have at hand a perpetual criterion of the possibility of such synthetic propositions as are supposed to prove more than experience can give, which consists in this: that the proof not be conducted straight to the desired predicate, but only by means of a principle of the possibility of extending our given concept a priori up to ideas and of realizing these. If this caution is always used, if, before the proof is even attempted, one first wisely consults with oneself how and with what ground of hope one could expect such an extension through pure reason, and whence, in such a case, one would then take these insights, which cannot be developed from concepts and also cannot be anticipated in relation to possible experience: then one can spare oneself much difficult and yet fruitless effort, by not demanding of reason anything that manifestly goes beyond its faculty, or rather by subjecting it, which in the fits of its speculative craving for extension does not gladly let itself be restricted, to the discipline of abstinence.",[]],"krv-b-2313":["The first rule is therefore this: not to attempt any transcendental proofs without having first reflected and justified oneself on this point, whence one wants to take the principles on which one thinks to erect them, and with what right one can expect the good success of the inferences from them. If they are principles of the understanding (e.g., of causality), then it is in vain to arrive by means of them at ideas of pure reason; for those are valid only for objects of possible experience. If they are to be principles from pure reason, then again all effort is in vain. For reason has them, to be sure, but as objective principles they are all together dialectical, and can at most be valid only as regulative principles of the systematically coherent use of experience. But if such alleged proofs are already at hand: then set against the deceptive conviction the *non liquet* of your matured power of judgment; and although you cannot yet penetrate its illusory show, you nevertheless have complete right to demand the deduction of the principles used in it, which, if they are supposed to have arisen from mere reason, can never be furnished to you. And thus you do not even need to occupy yourselves with the development and refutation of every groundless illusion, but can reject, in whole heaps at once, all dialectic, inexhaustible in artifices, at the court of a critical reason, which demands laws.",[]],"krv-b-2314":["The second peculiarity of transcendental proofs is this: that for each transcendental proposition only a single proof can be found. If I am to infer not from concepts, but from the intuition that corresponds to a concept, be it now a pure intuition, as in mathematics, or an empirical one, as in natural science: then the intuition laid at the basis gives me manifold material for synthetic propositions, which I can connect in more than one way and, since I may start out from more than one point, can arrive at the same proposition by different paths.",[]],"krv-b-2315":["Now, however, every transcendental proposition proceeds merely from One concept and states the synthetic condition of the possibility of the object in accordance with this concept. The ground of proof can therefore only be a single one, because besides this concept there is nothing further through which the object could be determined, the proof therefore can contain nothing further than the determination of an object in general in accordance with this concept, which is also only a single one. We had, e.g., in the transcendental analytic the principle: everything that happens has a cause, drawn from the single condition of the objective possibility of a concept of that which happens in general: that the determination of an occurrence in time, hence this (occurrence) as belonging to experience, would be impossible without standing under such a dynamical rule. This is now also the only possible ground of proof; for only through this, that an object is determined for the concept by means of the law of causality, does the represented occurrence have objective validity, i.e., truth. One has, to be sure, attempted other proofs of this principle, e.g., from contingency; but if this is considered in the light, one can find no mark of contingency except happening, i.e., existence, before which a non-being of the object precedes, and so one always comes back again to the very same ground of proof. If the proposition is to be proved: everything that thinks is simple, then one does not dwell on the manifold of thinking, but persists merely with the concept of the I, which is simple and to which all thinking is related. Just so is it with the transcendental proof of the existence of God, which rests solely on the reciprocability of the concepts of the most real and the necessary being and can be sought nowhere else.",["(Notes belong under the single final heading; applying to all passages.)","- krv-b-2312: \"fol\" = *soll*, \"Mannigfaltigkeit\"/\"Mannigfaltige\" = manifold(ness); \"Kraft\" rendered \"force\" in the body but \"Beweiskraft\" as \"power of proof\"; \"Kenntniß\" = acquaintance; \"Bewußtsein\" = consciousness; \"Vorstellung\" = representation. Footnote marker \"35)\" retained as printed; footnote text not supplied in the passage. \"überführen\" rendered \"lead over\"; \"Verstande\" in \"im ersteren Verstande\" rendered \"sense\" (ambiguous in German).","- krv-b-2312: \"hauch\" in the OCR read as \"auch\"; \"Bedenklichkeit\" = scruple; \"ahnden\" = have a presentiment of (OCR \"ahnden\" for *ahnden/ahnen*); \"Raumesinhalts\" = content of space; \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" both \"object\" (here \"Object\").","- krv-b-2312: \"Ich\" rendered \"I\"; \"Paralogism\" kept as cognate; \"Disciplin\" = discipline; \"Erweiterungssucht\" = craving for extension; \"a priori\" may attach to \"erweitern\" or to \"Begriff\" (kept ambiguous).","- krv-b-2313: \"Urtheilskraft\" = power of judgment; \"Überlegt\" = reflected (Überlegung = reflection); \"Schein\" = illusion, \"Blendwerk\" = illusory show; \"Überzeugung\" = conviction; \"Gerichtshofe einem kritischen Vernunft\" is grammatically corrupt in OCR (*einer kritischen Vernunft*); rendered as \"of a critical reason\"; \"Gesetze\" = laws; \"Grundsätze\" = principles (also \"Principien\" = principles).","- krv-b-2313: \"Erfahrungsgebrauchs\" rendered \"use of experience\"; \"Der- nunft\" read as \"Vernunft\"; \"ges denkt\" read as \"gedenkt\"; \"der? Causalität\" read \"der Causalität\". \"trüglichen\" = deceptive.","- krv-b-2314/2315: \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Stoff\" = material; \"Begriff\" = concept; \"Beweisgrund\" = ground of proof; \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Begebenheit\" = occurrence; \"Zufälligkeit\" = contingency; \"Dasein\" = existence; \"Kennzeichen\" = mark (not *Merkmal*); \"Reciprocabilität\" kept as cognate; \"realsten und nothwendigen Wesen\" = most real and necessary being.","- krv-b-2315: \"Wedien\" read as \"Wesen\"; \"Hez a griffe\" read as \"Begriffe\"; \"Gültigkeit\" = validity; \"dynamischen\" = dynamical; \"beim Lichte betrachtet\" rendered literally; \"einfach\" = simple.","- Passage identifiers in the header carry question marks in the source; page references not verified."]],"krv-b-2316":["By this cautionary remark the critique of the assertions of reason is very much reduced in scale. Where reason conducts its business through mere concepts, only a single proof is possible, if any at all is possible. Hence if one already sees the dogmatist step forth with ten proofs, one can safely believe that he has none at all. For if he had one that (as must be the case in matters of pure reason) proved apodictically, what would he need the rest for? His aim is only like that of the parliamentary advocate: the one argument is for this one, the other for that one, namely in order to make use of the weakness of his judges, who, without engaging deeply and in order to get done with the business soon, seize upon the first best thing that strikes them and decide accordingly. The third peculiar rule of pure reason, when it is subjected to a discipline with regard to transcendental proofs, is: that its proofs must never be apagogic, but at all times ostensive. The direct or ostensive proof is, in every kind of cognition, that which combines with conviction of the truth at the same time insight into its sources; the apagogic proof, by contrast, can indeed produce certainty, but not comprehensibility of the truth with regard to the interconnection with the grounds of its possibility. Hence the latter are more an emergency aid than a procedure that does justice to all the aims of reason. Yet these have a advantage of evidence over the direct proofs in this: that contradiction always carries more clarity in the representation than the best connection, and thereby approaches more closely the intuitive character of a demonstration.",[]],"krv-b-2317":["The proper cause of the use of apagogic proofs in various sciences is probably this. If the grounds from which a certain cognition is to be derived lie too manifold or too deeply hidden: then one tries whether it cannot be reached through the sequences. Now the *modus ponens*, to infer the truth of a cognition from the truth of its consequences, would be permitted only if all possible consequences from it are true; for then only a single ground is possible for these, which is therefore also the true one. But this procedure is unfeasible, because it goes beyond our powers to see all possible consequences of any assumed proposition; yet one makes use of this kind of inference, though to be sure with a certain indulgence, when it is a matter of proving something merely as a hypothesis, in that one concedes the inference by analogy: that if as many consequences as one has ever tried agree well with an assumed ground, all the remaining possible ones will also agree with it. For that reason a hypothesis can never be transformed into demonstrated truth by this way. The *modus tollens* of inferences of reason, which infer from the consequences to the grounds, proves not only quite strictly, but also exceedingly easily. For if even a single false consequence can be drawn from a proposition, then this proposition is false. Instead of running through the whole series of grounds in an ostensive proof, which can lead to the truth of a cognition by means of the complete insight into its possibility, one need only find a single false one among the consequences flowing from the opposite of it, then this opposite is also false, hence the cognition that one had to prove is true.",[]],"krv-b-2318":["The apagogic kind of proof, however, can be permitted only in the sciences where it is impossible to substitute the subjective in our representations for the objective, namely for the cognition of that which is in the object. But where the latter predominates, it must frequently happen that the opposite of a certain proposition either contradicts merely the subjective conditions of thinking, but not the object, or that both propositions contradict each other only under a subjective condition that is falsely held to be objective and, since the condition is false, both can be false, without its being possible to infer from the falsity of the one to the truth of the other. | In mathematics this subreption is impossible; hence they also have their proper place there. In natural science, because everything there is grounded on empirical intuitions, that surreptition can indeed for the most part be prevented through many compared observations; but this kind of proof is there for the most part insignificant. But the transcendental attempts of pure reason are all undertaken within the proper medium of dialectical illusion, i.e., of the subjective, which offers itself to reason in its premises as objective, or even obtrudes itself. Here, now, as far as synthetic propositions are concerned, it cannot be permitted at all to justify one's assertions by refuting the opposite. For either this refutation is nothing other than the mere representation of the conflict of the opposed opinion with the subjective conditions of comprehensibility through our reason, which contributes nothing at all toward rejecting the matter itself on that account (as, e.g., unconditioned necessity in the existence of a being absolutely cannot be comprehended by us and therefore subjectively, with right, opposes itself to every speculative proof of a necessary highest being, but to the possibility of such an original being in itself, without right); or both, the asserting as well as the denying party, deceived by the transcendental illusion, lay at the ground an impossible concept of the object, and there the rule holds: *non entis nulla sunt praedicata*, i.e., both what one asserted affirmatively and what one asserted negatively of the object is incorrect, and one cannot arrive apagogically, through the refutation of the opposite, at the cognition of the truth. Thus, for example, if it is presupposed that the world of sense is given in itself according to its totality, then it is false that it must be either infinite in space, or finite and bounded, because both are false. For appearances (as mere representations), which are yet in themselves (as objects) beings, are something impossible, and the infinity of this imagined whole would indeed be unconditioned, but would contradict (because everything in appearances is conditioned) the unconditioned determination of magnitude that is yet presupposed in the concept.",["- krv-b-2316: \"Beweis\" = proof; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Begreiflichkeit\" = comprehensibility (cf. *begreifen*, comprehend); \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Zusammenhang\" = interconnection; \"Absicht\" = aim; \"Anschauliche\" = intuitive character (nominalized).","- krv-b-2316: OCR corrected silently (\"Vernunftbe-hauptungen,\" \"Geschäfte,\" \"Anjey ung\" = Ansehung, etc.); \"ac\" before \"dagegen\" is a garbled word, read as \"apagogische\" (the apagogic proof). \"Doch haben dies einen\" is as in the OCR text (grammatically \"diese einen\"); \"Wi.derspruch\" = Widerspruch. \"Parlamentsadvocaten\" rendered \"parliamentary advocate.\"","- krv-b-2316: \"Demonstraß tion\" = Demonstration; passage ends with stray \"I\" (OCR), omitted. \"Das Erste Beste\" = \"the first best thing.\"","- krv-b-2317: \"Folgen\" is rendered \"consequences\" and, once, \"sequences\" (in \"durch die Folgen zu erreichen\"), though the glossary gives \"sequel; sequence\" for *Folge* and \"consequence\" for *Folgerung*; here I used \"sequences\" for the first and \"consequences\" afterward, a point of inconsistency to review.","- krv-b-2317: \"Vernunftschlüsse\" = inferences of reason; \"Schluß\" = inference; \"Gründe\" = grounds; \"Kräfte\" = powers (glossary: force; power). \"Satz\" = proposition.","- krv-b-2317: Garbled OCR readings corrected silently (\"Er“kenntniß,\" \"lieben\" read as \"gehen\"? — \"[lieben\" left as: \"which infer from the consequences to the grounds\"; the bracketed reading is doubtful, and I rendered the sense as in the context of the phrase).","- krv-b-2318: \"Gegenstand\" and \"Object\" are both \"object\"; \"Gegenstande\" occurs in the passage and \"Objecte\"/\"objectiv\" appear in the text. \"Erschleichung\" = surreption (with \"Subreption\" earlier rendered \"subreption\"; both here given as \"surreption\" in the second). \"Wesen\" = being; \"Nes en\" read as \"Wesen.\"","- krv-b-2318: The sign | is carried over where it stands; \"Widerstreit\" = conflict; \"Scheins\" = illusion; \"gelten\" = hold; \"Sinnenwelt\" = world of sense; \"Meinung\" = opinion; \"Behauptung\" = assertion; \"Grunde\" in \"zum Grunde legen\" = \"lay at the ground.\"","- krv-b-2318: \"ihrer Totalität\" = \"its totality\"; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself.\" \"eingebildeten\" = \"imagined\" (cf. *Einbildungskraft*, imagination). The pronoun \"sie\" in \"sie\" for \"Sinnenwelt\" and the ambiguity of \"die\" after \"Wesen\" were left as in the German. The \"[lieben\" and \"Erschleichung\" readings should be rechecked against the Academy text."]],"krv-b-2319":["The apagogic kind of proof is also the actual dazzling illusion with which the admirers of the thoroughness of our dogmatic rationalizers have always been kept in suspense: it is, as it were, the champion who wants to prove the honor and the indisputable right of the party he has taken by offering to brawl with anyone who might want to doubt it; although by such boasting nothing in the matter is settled, but only the respective strength of the opponents, and indeed | only on the side of the one who behaves as the attacker. The spectators, in that they see that each in his turn is now victor, now succumbs, often take occasion from this to doubt skeptically the object of the dispute itself. But they have no cause for this, and it is enough to call out to them: *non defensoribus istis tempus eget*. Each must conduct his cause by means of a legal proof, led through a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof, i.e., directly, so that one may see what his claims of reason have to adduce for themselves. For if his opponent stands on subjective grounds, then he is of course easy to refute, but without advantage for the dogmatist, who commonly likewise clings to the subjective causes of the judgment and can in like manner be driven into a corner by his opponent. But if both parties proceed merely directly, then either they will themselves notice the difficulty, indeed impossibility, of finding out the title of their assertions, and in the end will only be able to appeal to prescription, or the critique will easily discover the dogmatic illusion and will compel pure reason to give up its pretensions driven too high in speculative use and to withdraw within the boundaries of its own proper soil, namely practical principles.",["- *apagogische Beweisart*: \"apagogic kind of proof\"; *Beweis* = proof, *Art* = kind.","- *Blendwerk* = \"dazzling illusion\"; not *Schein*, which is rendered \"illusion\" in the last sentence (*dogmatischen Schein*).","- *Vernünftler* rendered \"rationalizers\" (cf. *vernünfteln* = rationalize).","- *Grenzen* = \"boundaries\"; *Ursache* = \"cause\" (also in \"subjective causes\"); *Grund/Gründe* = \"ground(s)\".","- *Titel ihrer Behauptungen*: *Titel* = \"title\"; *Anmaßungen* = \"pretensions\"; *Ansprüche* = \"claims\".","- *Theile* rendered \"parties\" (not \"parts\") in \"beide Theile\"; *Partei* is also \"party\".","- *respectiven* kept as \"respective\"; *Verjährung* = \"prescription\".","- *durch eines ... geführten rechtlichen Beweises*: *durch* reads with *Deduction*; the participle *geführten* could attach to *Beweises* (as rendered) or to the deduction; the German is ambiguous.","- OCR damage: \"Zu: schauer\" corrected to \"Zuschauer\"; stray numerals and letters (2, 3, z, a) omitted; \"konnen\" corrected to \"können\"."]],"krv-b-2320":["Of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nSecond Chapter.\nThe Canon of Pure Reason.",["- *Methodenlehre* = \"Doctrine of Method\"; *Lehre* = doctrine; *Hauptstück* = \"chapter\"; *Kanon* kept as \"Canon\"."]],"krv-b-2321":["It is humiliating for human reason that it accomplishes nothing in its pure use and even still needs a discipline to restrain its excesses and to prevent the illusions that come to it from this. But on the other hand it elevates it again and gives it a confidence in itself, that it can and must exercise this discipline itself, without permitting another censorship over itself, likewise that the boundaries which it is compelled to set to its speculative use at the same time restrict the rationalizing pretensions of every opponent, and consequently could secure against all attacks everything that might still remain to it of its previously exaggerated demands. The greatest and perhaps the only utility of all philosophy of pure reason is therefore probably only negative: since it serves, namely, not as an organon for expansion, but as a discipline for the determination of boundaries and, instead of discovering truth, has only the quiet merit of preventing errors. | Meanwhile there must nevertheless be somewhere a source of positive cognitions that belong to the domain of pure reason, and which perhaps give occasion for errors only through a misunderstanding, but in fact constitute the goal of the zeal of reason. For to what cause else should the insuppressible desire to gain a firm footing somewhere beyond the boundary of experience be ascribed? It has a presentiment of objects that carry a great interest for it with them. It sets out on the path of mere speculation in order to approach them; but these flee from it. Presumably better luck is to be hoped for it on the sole path that is still left to it, namely that of practical use. By a canon I understand the sum total of the principles *a priori* of the correct use of certain faculties of cognition in general. Thus general logic in its analytic part is a canon for understanding and reason in general, but only as to form, for it abstracts from all content. Thus the transcendental analytic was the canon of the pure understanding; for that alone is capable of true synthetic cognitions *a priori*. But where no correct use of a faculty of cognition is possible, there is no canon. Now all synthetic cognition of pure reason in its speculative use is, according to all the proofs conducted so far, entirely impossible. Therefore there is no canon at all of the speculative use of the same (for this is through and through dialectical), but all transcendental logic is in this aim nothing but discipline. Consequently, if there is anywhere a correct use of pure reason, in which case there must also be a canon of it, then this will concern not the speculative but the practical use of reason, which we therefore now want to investigate.",["- *Blendwerke* = \"illusions\" here (cf. *Blendwerk* above, \"dazzling illusion\"); *Schein* would also be \"illusion\".","- *Grenze* / *Grenzbestimmung* = \"boundary\" / \"determination of boundaries\"; *Erkenntniß* = cognition; *Erkenntnißvermögen/-kraft* = \"faculty of cognition\" for both *Vermögen* and *Kraft* (*Kraft* is \"force; power\" in the glossary).","- *Organon* and *Disciplin*, *Kanon* kept as cognates; *Ausschweifungen* = \"excesses\"; *Anmaßung* = \"pretension\".","- *Zweck*/*Absicht*: *Absicht* rendered \"aim\".","- *Ziel* = \"goal\"; *Beeiferung* = \"zeal\".","- *Inbegriff* = \"sum total\"; *Grundsätze a priori*: *a priori* could attach to *Grundsätze* or to the *Gebrauchs*; left as in German (placed after \"principles\").","- *Mißverstand* = \"misunderstanding\"; *ahndet* = \"has a presentiment of\".","- *Gebiet* = \"domain\" (glossary *Umfang* is also \"domain\").","- OCR: stray letters (Ä, Hist, ö, Jift, laber, ale, si, i, ficht) corrected silently; \"solte\" read \"sollte\"."]],"krv-b-2322":["Of the Canon of Pure Reason\nFirst Section.\nOn the Ultimate Purpose of the Pure Use of Our Reason.",["- *Zweck* = \"purpose\" (glossary: end; purpose); *Abschnitt* = \"section\"."]],"krv-b-2323":["Reason is driven by a propensity of its nature to go beyond the use in experience, to venture in a pure use and by means of mere ideas to the outermost boundaries of all cognition, and to find rest only in the completion of its circle, in a self-subsisting systematic whole. Now is this striving grounded merely on its speculative, or rather solely and | exclusively on its practical interest?",["- *Erfahrungsgebrauch* = \"use in experience\" (literally \"experience-use\").","- *Hang* = \"propensity\"; *Bestrebung* = \"striving\"; *Interesse* = \"interest\".","- *einzig und allein* rendered \"solely and exclusively\"; the sign | falls within it as in the source."]],"krv-b-2324":["I will now set aside the fortune that pure reason makes in a speculative aim and ask only about the problems, the solution of which constitutes its ultimate end, whether it may attain this or not, and with regard to which all others have merely the worth of means. These highest ends will, in accordance with the nature of reason, in turn have to have unity, in order to promote jointly that interest of humanity which is subordinated to no higher one.",["- *Absicht* = \"aim\"; *Aufgaben* = \"problems\"; *Zweck* = \"end\" (cf. *letzten Zweck* in the section title, \"purpose\").","- *Glück* = \"fortune\" (could also be \"luck\", as in the preceding passage).","- *Auflösung* = \"solution\"; *Mittel* = \"means\"; *Einheit* = \"unity\".","- *vereinigt* is adverbial (\"jointly\"); it could also be read as modifying *Interesse* (\"united\").","- *in Ansehung dessen* = \"with regard to which\"; antecedent (*Zweck* or *Aufgaben*) left open as in German."]],"krv-b-2325":["The ultimate aim to which the speculation of reason in its transcendental use finally runs out concerns three objects: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. With regard to all three the merely speculative interest of reason | is only very slight, and with respect to it a wearisome labor of transcendental investigation, struggling with unceasing obstacles, would hardly be undertaken, because of all the discoveries that might be made concerning this one can still make no use that would prove its utility in concreto, i.e., in the investigation of nature. The will may also be free, yet this can concern only the intelligible cause of our willing. For as regards the phenomena of its manifestations, i.e., the actions, we must, according to an inviolable fundamental maxim, without which we cannot exercise any reason in empirical use, explain them never otherwise than all the remaining appearances of nature, namely according to its unchangeable laws. Second, the spiritual nature of the soul (and with it its immortality) may also be able to be seen into, yet this cannot be reckoned on, either with regard to the appearances of this life as a ground of explanation, or with regard to the particular constitution of the future state, because our concept of an incorporeal nature is merely negative and does not extend our cognition in the least, nor offers any suitable material for consequences, except perhaps for those that can count only as fictions, which, however, are not permitted by philosophy. Third, if the existence of a highest intelligence were also proved: then we would indeed make the purposive in the arrangement of the world and order in general comprehensible to ourselves from it, but would by no means be authorized to derive from it any particular arrangement and order, or, where it is not perceived, to infer it boldly; since it is a necessary rule of the speculative use of reason not to pass by natural causes and to give up that about which we can be taught by experience, in order to derive something that we know from that which wholly surpasses all our knowledge. In a word, these three propositions remain for speculative reason always transcendent and have no immanent use at all, i.e., one admissible for objects of experience, hence in some way useful to us, but are, considered in themselves, entirely idle and yet extremely difficult exertions of our reason.",["- Absicht = aim (Endabsicht = ultimate aim, Absicht in \"in Absicht auf dasselbe\" = \"with respect to\"); Zweck = end; Gebrauch = use; Dasein = existence.","- Gegenstände = objects; Erkenntniß = cognition; Kenntniß = knowledge (kennen = know); Wissen (in the next passage) is also \"knowledge.\"","- Phänomene = phenomena, Erscheinungen = appearances; Äußerungen rendered \"manifestations.\"","- \"eingesehen werden können\": rendered \"be seen into\" (einsehen); \"Erdichtungen\" = fictions; \"Sätze\" = propositions.","- \"in concreto\" is italicized as Latin; the Latin is printed in roman type in the source."]],"krv-b-2326":["If accordingly these three cardinal propositions are not at all necessary to us for knowledge and are nevertheless urgently recommended to us by our reason: then their importance will properly have to concern only the practical.",["- Wissen = knowledge (scientia); Cardinalsätze = cardinal propositions.","- \"eigentlich\" rendered \"properly.\""]],"krv-b-2327":["Practical is everything that is possible through freedom. But if the conditions of the exercise of our free choice are empirical, then reason can have in this no other than a regulative use and serve only to bring about the unity of empirical laws; as, e.g., in the doctrine of prudence the union of all the ends that are given to us by our inclinations in the one, happiness, and the agreement of the means for attaining it, make up the whole business | of reason, which for that reason can supply no other than pragmatic laws of free conduct for the attainment of the ends recommended to us by the senses, and thus no pure laws, determined fully a priori. By contrast, pure practical laws, whose end is given fully a priori through reason, and which are not empirically conditioned but command absolutely, would be products of pure reason. Of this kind, however, are the moral laws; hence these alone belong to the practical use of pure reason and permit a canon. |",["- Willkür = choice; Zwecke = ends; Mittel = means; Gesetze = laws.","- The German has \"das ganze Ge: | schäfte\" (word split across the page break); the stray quotation mark after \"a priori\" is treated as an OCR artifact and dropped.","- \"bedingt\" = conditioned; \"Verhalten\" = conduct."]],"krv-b-2328":["The whole preparation of reason, therefore, in the elaboration that can be called pure philosophy, is in fact directed only to the three mentioned problems. These themselves, however, again have their more remote aim, namely what is to be done if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Since this now concerns our conduct in relation to the highest end, the ultimate aim of nature, which provides for us wisely in the arrangement of our reason, is properly set only on the moral. |",["- Absicht = aim; Zweck = end; Probleme = problems; Zurüstung = preparation; Bearbeitung = elaboration.","- \"reine Philosophie\": Philosophie (not Weltweisheit) = philosophy."]],"krv-b-2329":["But caution is necessary, since we are casting our attention upon an object that is foreign*) to transcendental philosophy, so as not to digress into episodes and violate the unity of the system, and on the other side also, by saying too little of one's new material, so as not to let distinctness or conviction be lacking. I hope to accomplish both by holding myself as close as possible to the",["- The footnote sign after \"fremd\" is given as *); the passage breaks off mid-sentence and is not completed.","- Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Überzeugung = conviction; Stoff = material. \"man\" rendered \"one.\""]],"krv-b-2330":["*) All practical concepts go to objects of liking or disliking, i.e., of pleasure and displeasure, hence at least indirectly to objects of our feeling. But since this is not a power of representation of things, but lies outside the entire power of cognition, the elements of our judgments, insofar as they relate to pleasure or displeasure, hence of the practical, do not belong in the sum total of transcendental philosophy, which has to do solely with pure cognitions a priori.",["- OCR corrected: Luft → Lust, sonder → sondern, \"Su- & begriff\" → Inbegriff, \"Erkenntufsseczf\" → Erkenntnissen.","- Vorstellungskraft = power of representation; Erkenntnißkraft = power of cognition (Kraft = power).","- \"mithin der praktischen\" is left as elliptical as the German (\"hence of the practical\"); the reading of its attachment is doubtful.","- Wohlgefallen/Mißfallen = liking/disliking; Inbegriff = sum total."]],"krv-b-2331":["transcendental hold, and sweep entirely aside whatever in this might perhaps be psychological, i.e., empirical.",["- Fragment: the beginning (\"am Transcendentalen\") belongs to the preceding passage; \"halte\" is the verb of the clause begun there."]],"krv-b-2332":["And here it is first to be noted that I shall for now make use of the concept",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2333":["of freedom only in the practical sense, and set aside here, as dispatched above, the one in transcendental meaning, which cannot be presupposed empirically as a ground of explanation of the",["- \"im praktischen Verstande\": Verstand here = sense (not \"understanding\"). The sentence continues in the next passage; the word order of the German is kept.","- Bedeutung = meaning; Erklärungsgrund = ground of explanation."]],"krv-b-2334":["appearances, but is itself a problem for reason. A choice, namely, is merely animal (*arbitrium brutum*) that cannot otherwise",["- \"bei Seite setze\" belongs to the clause begun in the previous passage (\"set aside,\" placed there in English).","- Willkür = choice; Latin terms italicized."]],"krv-b-2335":["than through sensible impulses, i.e., pathologically, be determined.",["- Antriebe = impulses; bestimmt = determined."]],"krv-b-2336":["But that which can be determined independently of sensible impulses, hence through",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2337":["motive causes that are represented only by reason, is called free choice (*arbitrium liberum*), and everything",["- Bewegursachen = motive causes (cf. Bewegungsgrund = motive); Willkür = choice."]],"krv-b-2338":["that is connected with this, whether as ground or sequence, is prac-",["- \"Folge\" = sequence; the word \"praktisch\" is split across passages (\"prak-\" / \"tisch\"), and \"genannt\" (called) stands in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2339":["tical called. Practical freedom can be proved through experience. For not merely that which stimulates, i.e., immediately",["- Stray line number \"5\" in the OCR omitted. The sentence continues in the next passage.","- Beweis = proof."]],"krv-b-2340":["affects the senses, determines human choice, but we have a fac-",["- Afficirt = affects; \"Ver-\" / \"mögen\" is split across passages (Vermögen = faculty), rendered \"fac-\" / \"ulty.\""]],"krv-b-2341":["ulty, through representations of that which is useful or harmful even in a more remote*) way, to overcome the impressions on our sensible faculty of desire; these reflections, however, on what is desirable with regard to our whole state, i.e., good and useful, rest on reason. This therefore also gives laws which",["- Stray \"so\" in \"Anso sehung\" removed (Ansehung = regard); footnote sign after \"entferntere\" given as *); the footnote text is not in the passage.","- Überlegung = reflection; Begehrungsvermögen = faculty of desire; Eindruck = impression; Zustand = state."]],"krv-b-2342":["are imperatives, i.e., objective laws of freedom, and which say",["- \"sa-\" / \"gen\" is split across passages; \"say\" is rendered here whole and the next passage begins with \"what.\""]],"krv-b-2343":["what ought to happen, even though perhaps it never happens, and themselves",["- \"sich\" at the end is reflexive and its verb (\"unterscheiden\") stands in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2344":["therein from laws of nature, which deal only with what happens, distinguish, for which reason they are also called practical laws.",["- Stray \"s\" in the OCR omitted; \"Naturgesetze\" = laws of nature."]],"krv-b-2345":["But whether reason itself in these actions, by which it prescribes la-",["- \"Ge-\" / \"setze\" is split across passages (Gesetz = law), rendered \"la-\" / \"ws\"; \"vorschreibt\" stands in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2346":["ws, is not in turn determined by other influences,",["- The verb \"prescribes\" (\"vorschreibt\") is in the German of this passage; it is rendered with \"by which it prescribes\" in the preceding passage for English syntax, so that it does not appear twice."]],"krv-b-2347":["and whether what is called freedom with regard to sensible impulses might not in turn, with regard to higher and more remote efficient causes, be nature, this does not concern us in the practical, since we inquire first only of reason about the prescription of conduct, but is a",["- OCR \"Natur so sein möge\" read as \"Natur sein möge\" (stray \"so\" dropped; doubtful reading); \"Anso-\" in the OCR is Ansehung = regard.","- \"wirkenden Ursachen\" = efficient causes (literally \"effecting\"); passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2348":["mere speculative question, which we can set aside as long as our aim is directed to doing or omitting. We cognize practical freedom, therefore, through experience as one of the natural causes, namely a causality of reason in the determination of the will, whereas transcendental freedom demands an independence of this reason itself (with regard to its causality of beginning a series of appearances) from all determining causes of the world of sense, and to that extent seems to be contrary to the law of nature, hence to all possible experience, and so remains a problem. But this problem does not belong for reason in its practical use, therefore in a canon of pure reason we have to do with only two questions that concern the practical interest of pure reason, and with regard to which a canon of its use must be possible, namely: is there a God? is there a future life? The question concerning transcendental freedom concerns merely speculative knowledge, which we can set aside as entirely indifferent when it is a matter of the practical, and about which sufficient exposition is already to be found in the antinomy of pure reason.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"bloß speculative Frage\"). OCR \"näms lich\", \"prak— tische\" silently corrected.","- erkennen = cognize; Wissen = knowledge; Erörterung = exposition; Absicht = aim; Erscheinung = appearance.","- Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Naturursachen = natural causes; Bestimmung/bestimmend = determination/determining.","- \"so fern\" rendered \"to that extent\"; \"Problem\" (German Problem) = problem."]],"krv-b-2349":["Of the Canon of Pure Reason\nSecond Section.\nOn the ideal of the highest good, as a",["- Heading broken off mid-phrase; it continues in the next passage (\"Bestimmungsgrunde des letzten Zwecks der reinen Vernunft\")."]],"krv-b-2350":["determining ground of the ultimate end of pure reason.",["- Bestimmungsgrund = determining ground; Zweck = end."]],"krv-b-2351":["Reason in its speculative use led us through the field of experiences and, since complete satisfaction is never to be encountered there for it, from there to speculative ideas, which in the end, however, led us back again to experience, and thus fulfilled their aim in a way that was indeed useful, but not at all in conformity with our expectation. Now there remains to us still one attempt: whether, namely, pure reason is also to be encountered in practical use, whether in it it leads to the ideas that reach the highest ends of pure reason that we have just cited, and whether it thus cannot grant, from the standpoint of its practical interest, that which it entirely refuses us with regard to the speculative.",["- OCR \"praftische\" corrected silently (printed \"praftischen Interesse\" in the German, \"Interesse\" is as in the text).","- \"ihre Absicht\" = \"their aim\": \"ihre\" could refer to reason or to the ideas; preserved as \"their\" (the German \"ihre\" is feminine singular or plural-ambiguous).","- Erfahrungen (plural) = experiences; Gesichtspunkt = standpoint."]],"krv-b-2352":["All interest of my reason (the speculative as well as the practical) unites in the following three questions:",["- None beyond Vernunft = reason."]],"krv-b-2353":["1. What can I know?\n2. What ought I to do?\n3. What may I hope?",["- wissen = know (Wissen = knowledge); \"thun\" = do."]],"krv-b-2354":["The first question is merely speculative. We have (as I flatter myself) exhausted all possible answers to it and finally found the one with which reason must indeed be satisfied and, if it does not look to the practical, also has cause to be content, but we have remained just as far removed from the two great ends toward which this whole endeavor of pure reason was properly directed as if from comfort we had refused ourselves this labor right from the start. If it is a matter of knowledge, then, so much is at least certain and settled, that this can never fall to our share with regard to those two problems.",["- Aufgabe = problem. The syntax of \"sind aber ... geblieben\" (shift of subject after \"must ... zu sein\") is kept as in the German.","- Bestrebung = endeavor; \"schmeichele\" = flatter; OCR \"schmei— chele\" corrected."]],"krv-b-2355":["The second question is merely practical. As such it can indeed belong to pure reason, but is then nevertheless not transcendental, but moral, hence it cannot occupy our critique in itself.",["- an sich selbst = in itself; it could attach to \"occupy\" or to \"critique\"; the English leaves it as ambiguous. OCR \"son dem\" corrected."]],"krv-b-2356":["The third question, namely: if I now do what I ought, what may I then hope? is practical and theoretical at once, so that the practical leads only as a guideline to the answer to the theoretical and, if this goes high, speculative question. For all hoping goes to happiness and is, with regard to the practical and the moral law, precisely the same as knowledge and the law of nature are with regard to the theoretical cognition of things. The former runs out finally into the inference that something is (which determines the ultimate possible end) because something ought to happen; the latter, that something is (which acts as supreme cause) because something happens.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; Wissen = knowledge; Schluß = inference; Sittengesetz = moral law; Glückseligkeit = happiness.","- \"Jenes ... dieses\" rendered \"the former ... the latter\". \"wenn diese hoch geht\": \"this\" may refer to the answer or to the question; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-2357":["Happiness is the satisfaction of all our inclinations (both extensive, in terms of their manifoldness, and intensive, in degree, and also protensive, in duration). The practical law from the motive of happiness I call pragmatic (rule of prudence); but that, if there is such a one, which has as motive nothing other than the worthiness to be happy, moral (moral law). The first advises what is to be done if we want to partake of happiness, the second commands how we ought to behave in order merely to become worthy of happiness. The first is grounded on empirical principles; for other than by means of experience I can know neither which inclinations there are that want to be satisfied, nor which are the natural causes that can bring about their satisfaction. The second abstracts from inclinations and natural means of satisfying them and considers only the freedom of a rational being in general and the necessary conditions under which alone it agrees with the distribution of happiness in accordance with principles, and can thus at least rest on mere ideas of pure reason and be cognized a priori.",["- Bewegungsgrund = motive; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Grad = degree; Principien = principles; Idee = idea.","- \"wenn wir der Glückseligkeit wollen theilhaftig\" is elliptical in the German (no verb \"werden\"); kept elliptical in sense. OCR \"und“ auch\", \"dasind\" (da sind) corrected.","- \"sie\" (\"it ... agrees\") has \"freedom\" as nearest antecedent; \"erkannt\" = cognized."]],"krv-b-2358":["I assume that there really are pure moral laws that determine completely | *a priori* (without regard to empirical motives, i.e., happiness) the doing and omitting, i.e., the use of the freedom of a rational being in general, and that these laws command absolutely (not merely hypothetically, under presupposition of other empirical ends) and are thus necessary in every respect. I can rightly presuppose this proposition, not only by appealing to the proofs of the most enlightened moralists, but to the moral judgment of every human being, if he wants to think such a law distinctly.",["- \"in aller Absicht\" (Absicht usually = aim) rendered idiomatically \"in every respect\". Satz = proposition; deutlich = distinct; Urtheil = judgment; Zweck = end.","- \"völlig | a priori\": \"völlig\" may attach to \"determine\" or to \"a priori\"; kept in place. OCR \"yernünftigen\", \"ı eines\" corrected."]],"krv-b-2359":["Pure reason therefore contains, though not in its speculative use, yet in a certain practical one, namely the moral, principles of the possibility of experience, namely of such actions as could be encountered in the history of human beings in accordance with moral precepts. For since it commands that such ought to happen, they must also be able to happen, and a special kind of systematic unity, namely the moral, must therefore be possible, whereas the systematic unity of nature could not be proved according to speculative principles of reason, because reason does have causality with regard to freedom in general, but not with regard to the whole of nature, and moral principles of reason can produce free actions, but not natural laws. Accordingly the principles of pure reason in their practical,",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; it continues in the next passage (\"namentlich aber dem moralischen Gebrauche\").","- Möglichkeit = possibility; besondere Art = special kind; Einheit = unity; Causalität = causality. \"konnten\" (twice) is as in the OCR; the original may read \"könnten\"/\"könnte\" (subjunctive); rendered \"could\".","- OCR debris (\"z\", \"zaf\", \"Herz nunft\" = Vernunft) removed silently."]],"krv-b-2360":["but especially moral use have objective reality.",["- Fragment continues the previous passage. \"namentlich\" rendered \"especially\" (\"namely\" would be the other possibility); \"Realität\" = reality; OCR \"os\" removed."]],"krv-b-2361":["I call the world, insofar as it would be in accordance with all moral laws (as it can be in accordance with the freedom of rational beings and ought to be in accordance with the necessary laws of morality), a moral world. This is to that extent thought merely as an intelligible world, because in it abstraction is made from all conditions (ends) and even from all obstacles to morality in it (weakness or impurity of human nature). To that extent it is thus a mere, but yet practical idea, which can and ought really to have its influence on the world of sense, in order to make it as much as possible in accordance with this idea. The idea of a moral world therefore has objective reality, not as if it pertained to an object of an intelligible intuition (such as we cannot think at all), but to the world of sense, but as an object of pure reason in its practical use and a *corpus mysticum* of the rational beings in it, insofar as their free power of choice under moral laws has thoroughgoing systematic unity in itself, both with itself and with the freedom of everyone else.",["- Willkür = power of choice; Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; Sittlichkeit/sittlich = morality/moral; Bedingung = condition.","- \"bloß\" in \"bloß als intelligibele Welt\" and \"eine bloße ... Idee\" = \"merely\"/\"mere\". OCR \"gez ss dacht\", \"Oergleichen\" (= \"(dergleichen\") corrected silently.","- \"sondern auf die Sinnenwelt, aber als einen Gegenstand\": the construction is kept as in the German; \"an sich\" attaches to \"has\" (in itself) and may also be read with \"unity\"."]],"krv-b-2362":["That was the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason that concerned the practical interest: Do that through which you become worthy to be happy. The second now asks: how, if I now conduct myself so that I am not unworthy of happiness, may I also hope to be able to partake of it thereby? In answering it, what matters is whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe the law *a priori*, also necessarily connect this hope with it. | I say accordingly: that just as the moral principles are necessary in accordance with reason in its practical use, so equally necessary is it also in accordance with reason, in its theoretical use, to assume that everyone has cause to hope for happiness in the same measure as he has made himself worthy of it in his conduct, and that the system of morality is therefore inseparably, but only in the idea of pure reason, combined with that of happiness. | Now in an intelligible, i.e., the moral, world, in whose concept we abstract from all hindrances to morality (the inclinations), such a system of proportioned happiness combined with morality can also be thought as necessary, because freedom, partly moved, partly restricted by moral laws, would itself be the cause of general happiness, the rational beings therefore, under the guidance of such principles, themselves the authors of their own and at the same time of others' enduring welfare. But this system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea, whose execution rests on the condition that everyone do what he ought, i.e., that all actions of rational beings occur as if they arose from a supreme will that comprehends all private arbitrary will in itself or under itself. Since, however, the obligation from the moral law remains valid for each particular use of freedom, even if others did not conduct themselves in conformity with this law, neither from the nature of the things of the world, nor from the causality of the actions themselves and their relation to morality is it determined how their sequels will relate to happiness; and the cited necessary connection of the hope of being happy with the incessant endeavor to make oneself worthy of happiness cannot be cognized through reason if one lays merely nature at the ground, but may only be hoped for if a highest reason, which commands in accordance with moral laws, is at the same time laid at the ground as cause of nature.",["- Glückseligkeit = \"happiness\"; \"glücklich\" also = \"happy\". Sittlichkeit and Moralität are both \"morality\"; sittlich/moralisch both \"moral\".","- Verbindung/verbunden = \"combination/combined\"; Verknüpfung/verknüpfen = \"connection/connect\".","- Folgen = \"sequels\" (glossary: Folge). Privatwillkür = \"private arbitrary will\" (Willkür). Gebrauch = \"use\". Vernunft = \"reason\".","- OCR hyphenation (\"rei— neu\", \"kön— neu\") and stray footnote marks silently corrected/dropped; \"geben so\" read as \"eben so\"; \"Jabftrahiren\" read as \"abstrahiren\"; \"Bedingnug\" as \"Bedingung\".","- \"welche a priori das Gesetz vorschreiben\": a priori could attach to \"welche\" (the principles) or to \"vorschreiben\"; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-2363":["I call the idea of such an intelligence, in which the morally most perfect will, combined with the highest blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, insofar as it stands in exact proportion to morality (as worthiness to be happy), the ideal of the highest good. Thus pure reason can find, only in the ideal of the highest original good, the ground of the practically necessary connection of both elements of the highest derived good, namely of an intelligible, i.e., moral, world. Since we now necessarily must represent ourselves through reason as belonging to such a world, although the senses present to us nothing but a world of appearances, we will have to assume the former as a sequel of our conduct in the world of sense and, since this does not offer us such a connection, as a world future for us. God, therefore, and a future life are two presuppositions that, in accordance with principles of this very same reason, are not to be separated from the obligation that pure reason imposes on us.",["- Seligkeit = \"blessedness\"; Glückseligkeit = \"happiness\"; Folge = \"sequel\"; Verbindlichkeit = \"obligation\"; Erscheinungen = \"appearances\"; Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\".","- \"Berhak f tens\" read as \"Verhaltens\"; stray footnote mark after \"gehörig\" dropped.","- \"so fern sie ... in genauem Verhältnisse steht\": \"sie\" could refer to Glückseligkeit or Welt; left as \"it\"."]],"krv-b-2364":["Morality in itself makes up a system, but happiness does not, except insofar as it is distributed exactly in proportion to morality. This, however, is possible only in the intelligible world under a wise author and ruler. Reason sees itself compelled to assume such a one together with life in such a world, which we must regard as a future one, or else to regard the moral laws as empty figments of the brain, because the necessary result of them, which the same reason connects with them, would have to lapse without that presupposition. Hence everyone also regards the moral laws as commands, wh—",["- Passage breaks off in the middle of the word \"wei—\" (= \"wel—ches\", continued in the next passage); rendered \"wh—\".","- OCR: \"de\" read as \"die\"; \"Einem solchen\" read as \"Einen solchen\" (accusative, as the construction requires); \"forhen\" read as \"solchen\"; \"Herz < nunft\" read as \"Vernunft\".","- Hirngespinste = \"figments of the brain\"; Erfolg = \"result\"; Voraussetzung = \"presupposition\"."]],"krv-b-2365":["ich they could not be, however, if they did not connect appropriate consequences *a priori* with their rule and thus carried promises and thre—",["- Begins mid-word (\"ches\" = \"wel-ches\", continuing the previous passage) and ends mid-word (\"Dro—\" = \"Dro-hungen\"); rendered \"ich\" and \"thre—\", completed by \"ats\" in the next passage.","- Folgen here = \"consequences\" because the passage is a fragment; elsewhere \"sequels\" (Folge).","- \"a priori\" may attach to \"angemessene\" or to \"verknüpften\"; left ambiguous."]],"krv-b-2366":["ats with them. This they cannot do either, however, where they do not lie in a necessary being as the highest good, which alone can make possible such a purposive unity. | Leibniz called the world, insofar as one pays attention in it only to the rational beings and their interconnection in accordance with moral laws under the government of the highest good, the kingdom of grace and distinguished it from the kingdom of nature, in which they, to be sure, stand under moral laws, but expect no other results of their conduct than according to the course of nature of our world of sense. To see oneself, therefore, in the kingdom of grace, where all happiness awaits us, except insofar as we ourselves restrict our",["- Begins with the end of the word \"Drohungen\" (\"hungen\" = \"(Dro)hungen\"); rendered \"ats\" after \"thre—\" of the preceding passage. Ends mid-sentence.","- Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"; Reich = \"kingdom\"; Gnaden = \"grace\"; Erfolge = \"results\"; \"moglich\" read as \"möglich\".","- \"wo sie nicht\" kept as \"where they do not\" (conditional/local sense both possible in the German)."]],"krv-b-2367":["share in it through the unworthiness to be happy, is a practically necessary idea of reason.¹ | It is necessary that our whole course of life be subordinated to moral maxims; but it is at the same time impossible that this happen if reason does not connect with the moral law, which is a mere idea, an efficient cause, which determines for conduct in accordance with it an outcome exactly corresponding to our highest ends, be it in this or in another life. Without a God, therefore, and a world now not visible to us but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are to be sure objects of approval and admiration, but not incentives of resolve and of practice, because they do not fulfill the whole end that is natural to every rational being and determined *a priori* and necessary through this very same pure reason. | Happiness alone is for our reason far from being the complete good. It does not approve it (however much inclination may wish it), unless it is united with worthiness to be happy, i.e., moral good conduct. Morality alone, however, and with it mere worthiness to be happy, is also still far from being the complete good. To complete this, he who had conducted himself as not unworthy of happiness must be able to hope to partake of it. Even reason, free of all private aim, if it, without taking into consideration an interest of its own thereby, put itself in the place of a being that had to distribute all happiness to others, cannot judge otherwise; for in the practical idea both pieces are essentially combined, though in such a way that the moral disposition, as condition, makes possible the share in happiness, and not conversely the prospect of happiness the moral disposition first. For in the latter case it would not be moral, and thus also not worthy of the whole happiness, which before reason recognizes no other limitation than that which stems from our own immoral conduct.\n\n¹ Practical laws, insofar as they at the same time become subjective grounds of actions, i.e., subjective principles, are called maxims. The estimation of morality, according to its purity and sequels, occurs in accordance with ideas, the following of its laws in accordance with maxims.",["- Footnote: in the OCR its text stands in the running text after \"Vernunft\"; I have placed it as a footnote and kept the marker ¹. Beurteilung = \"estimation\" (glossary); Reinigkeit = \"purity\"; Folgen = \"sequels\".","- Passage begins mid-sentence (\"Antheil\" = \"share\"). \"Unwürdigkeit, glücklich zu sein\" kept as \"the unworthiness to be happy\".","- Grundsätze = \"principles\", Principien also \"principles\" (Grund-satz vs. Principium); Zweck = \"end\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; Triebfedern = \"incentives\"; Vorsatz = \"resolve\"; Gesinnung = \"disposition\".","- OCR corrections: \"Marimen\" as \"Maximen\"; \"seinschränken\" as \"einschränken\"; \"Glüd- f seligkeit\" and \"Glückselig: 1f keit\" as \"Glückseligkeit\"; stray marks after \"zu\", \"wesentlich\", \"dieselbe b\" dropped.","- \"Wohlverhalten\" = \"good conduct\"; Verhalten = \"conduct\" throughout. \"das Stück\" = \"piece\"."]],"krv-b-2368":["Happiness, therefore, in exact proportion with the morality of rational beings, through which they are worthy of it, alone constitutes the highest good of a world, into which we must absolutely transpose ourselves in accordance with the precepts of pure but practical reason, and which to be sure is only an intelligible world, since the sensible world does not promise us any such systematic unity of ends from the nature of things, the reality of which also cannot be grounded on anything else than on the presupposition of a highest original good, since independent reason, equipped with all the sufficiency of a supreme cause, grounds, sustains and completes, in accordance with the most perfect purposiveness, the universal order of things, though it is very hidden from us in the sensible world. |",["- Wesen = being; Sinnenwelt = sensible world (*sinnlich* = sensible); Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness; Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency.","- \"darin\" and \"welche\" are kept as \"into which\" and \"which\"; the German leaves open whether the antecedent is the highest good or the world.","- \"deren Realität\" is ambiguous in the German (the unity of ends, or the world); the ambiguity is kept.","- The second \"da\" is rendered \"since\"; it could also be read \"where\" or \"in that\".","- \"though it is very hidden from us\": the German \"die ... uns sehr verborgene Ordnung\" can be read as an attributive phrase on \"Ordnung\"; the English is forced into a clause here."]],"krv-b-2369":["This moral theology now has the peculiar advantage over the",["- The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; the continuation is in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2370":["speculative, that it unfailingly leads to the concept of a single, most perfect and rational original being, to which speculative theology does not even point us from objective grounds, let alone could convince us of it. For we find neither in transcendental nor in natural theology, however far reason may lead us therein, any significant ground for assuming only a single being, which we would set before all natural causes, and on which we would at the same time have sufficient cause to make these dependent in all respects. On the other hand, if from the standpoint of moral unity as a necessary law of the world we consider the cause that can give to this alone the appropriate effect, hence also obligating force for us, then it must be a single supreme will that comprehends all these laws in itself. For how would we find perfect unity of ends among different wills? This will must be all-powerful, so that the whole of nature and its relation to morality in the world may be subordinated to it; all-knowing, so that it may cognize the innermost of dispositions and their moral worth; omnipresent, so that it may be immediately near to every need that the highest best of the world requires; eternal, so that in no time this agreement of nature and freedom may be lacking, etc. | But this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences, which, although as mere nature only a sensible world, but as a system of freedom can be called an intelligible, i.e., moral, world (*regnum gratiae*), leads unfailingly also to the purposive unity of all things that make up this great whole, according to universal laws of nature, just as the former does according to universal and necessary moral laws, and unites practical reason with the speculative. The world must be represented as having arisen from an idea, if it is to accord with that use of reason without which we would hold ourselves unworthy of reason, namely the moral, as that which rests throughout on the idea of the highest good. Thereby all research into nature gets a direction toward the form of a system of ends and in its highest expansion becomes physico-theology. This, however, since it nevertheless began from moral order, as a unity grounded in the essence of freedom and not contingently instituted through external commands, brings the purposiveness of nature to grounds that must be inseparably connected a priori with the inner possibility of things, and thereby to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of the highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity, which connects all things according to universal and necessary laws of nature, because they all have their origin in the absolute necessity of a single original being. | What use can we make of our understanding even with regard to experience, if we do not set ends before ourselves? But the highest ends are those of morality, and pure reason alone can give us to cognize these. Provided now with these and on the guideline of them, we can make no purposive use of the knowledge of nature itself with regard to cognition, where nature has not itself laid purposive unity; for without this we would not even have reason itself, because we would have no school for it, and no culture through objects that would offer the material for such concepts. That purposive unity is, however, necessary and grounded in the essence of choice itself; this, therefore, which contains the condition of the application of the same *in concreto*, must also be so, and thus the transcendental elevation of our cognition of reason would not be the cause but merely the effect of the practical purposiveness that pure reason imposes on us.",["- OCR slips silently corrected: \"allein\" (split as \"all- ein\"), \"unterworfen\", \"Notwendigkeit\", \"Willkür\" etc.","- Kenntnis = knowledge (as \"Kenntniß der Natur\"), kept distinct from Erkenntnis = cognition; Wesen = being (Urwesen = original being) but \"Wesen der Freiheit\" and \"Wesen der Willkür\" = \"essence\".","- Willkür = \"choice\" (not in the glossary); Gesinnung = disposition; Weltbeste = \"best of the world\"; Steigerung = \"elevation\"; Leitfaden = \"guideline\".","- \"diesem allein\" is ambiguous in the German: the cause that alone can give effect to this, or the effect appropriate to this alone; the ambiguity is kept.","- \"als welcher\" (\"as that which\") has an ambiguous antecedent; \"diese also\" and \"derselben\" (\"this\", \"the same\") likewise left unresolved.","- The Akademie apparatus sign | is carried over where it stands."]],"krv-b-2371":["We find, therefore, also in the history of human reason that, before the moral concepts were sufficiently purified, determined, and the systematic unity of ends in accordance with them, and indeed from necessary principles, were seen into, the knowledge of nature and even a considerable degree of cultivation of reason in many other sciences could produce partly only crude and roaming concepts of the deity, partly left behind an admirable indifference altogether with regard to this question. A greater working-out of moral ideas, which was made necessary by the extremely pure moral law of our religion, sharpened reason on the object through the interest that it compelled it to take in it; and without either expanded knowledge of nature or correct and reliable transcendental insights (of the kind that have been lacking at all times) contributing to it, they brought about a concept of the divine being that we now hold to be the correct one, not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it accords perfectly with the moral principles of reason. And so in the end it is always only pure reason, but only in its practical use, that has the merit of tying a cognition that mere speculation can only fancy but not make valid to our highest interest, and thereby making it, though not into a demonstrated dogma, yet into an absolutely necessary presupposition for its most essential ends.",["- OCR corrections: \"den\" for \"der\" before \"wir jetzt\"; \"immer\" for \"im- wer\"; \"einer\" for \"einen\"; \"hätten\" for \"hatten\" is in the next passage.","- \"eingesehen waren\" rendered \"were seen into\" (einsehen is not in the glossary); Einsichten = insights.","- \"sie\" in \"brachten sie\" is left as \"they\"; the antecedent in the German is not made explicit.","- \"ohne daß weder ... noch\" is rendered \"without either ... or\"; the German has a double negative.","- \"unterworfen\", \"Gleichgültigkeit\" (indifference), \"Bearbeitung\" (working-out), \"wähnen\" (fancy), \"geltend machen\" (make valid)."]],"krv-b-2372":["But when practical reason has now reached this high point, namely the concept of a single original being as the highest good, it must not at all venture, as if it had raised itself above all empirical conditions of its application and swung up to the immediate knowledge of new objects, to proceed from this concept and to derive the moral laws themselves from it. For",["- OCR: \"ja darf\" read as \"so darf\"; \"Begrisse\" read as \"Begriffe\".","- Kenntnis = knowledge; Gegenstände = objects; unterwinden = \"venture\".","- The passage ends in mid-sentence (\"Denn\"); not completed."]],"krv-b-2373":["these were precisely those whose inner practical necessity led us to the presupposition of an independent cause or of a wise ruler of the world, in order to give effect to those laws; and hence we cannot in turn regard them as contingent and derived from mere will according to this, especially from such a will of which we would have no concept at all if we had not formed it in accordance with those laws. As far as practical reason has the right to lead us, we will not hold actions to be obligatory because they are commands of God, but will rather regard them as divine commands because 1) we are inwardly obligated to them. We will study freedom under the purposive unity according to principles of reason and believe ourselves to be in conformity with the divine will only insofar as we hold holy the moral law, which reason teaches us from the nature of the actions themselves, and believe ourselves to serve it solely by promoting the",["- OCR: \"hatten\" read as \"hätten\"; \"weil!)\" read as the footnote marker \"1)\". No footnote text is given in the passage.","- \"nach diesem\" (\"according to this\") is left without a resolved antecedent (concept, cause, or will).","- Effect = Effect (Latinate) for \"effect\"; Wirkung would also be \"effect\"; Wille = will; Willkür does not occur here.","- Verbindlich = obligatory; verbindliche Gebote and Gebote = commands; Weltregierer = ruler of the world.","- The passage breaks off after \"das\"; the next passage continues it."]],"krv-b-2374":["best of the world in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is therefore only of immanent use, namely our vocation here in the",["- Bestimmung = \"vocation\" (the glossary also gives \"determination\").","- Weltbeste = \"best of the world\".","- The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; not completed."]],"krv-b-2375":["to fulfill the world by fitting into the system of all ends, and not fanatically or even wickedly to abandon the guiding thread of a morally legislating reason in a good conduct of life, in order to tie it immediately to the idea of the highest being, which would give a transcendent use, but, just like that of mere speculation, must pervert and frustrate the ultimate ends of reason.",["- Passage begins mid-sentence; translated as it stands. \"Welt zu erfüllen\" is preceded by text not given.","- Wesen = being; Gebrauch = use; Zwecke = ends; Idee = idea; Vernunft = reason.","- \"ge— setzgebenden\" = OCR line-break; read \"gesetzgebenden\".","- \"ihn\" (masc.) has \"Leitfaden\" as its grammatical antecedent; kept as \"it\" in English, where \"it\" could also be read as \"reason\" (Vernunft is fem., so not that). The pronoun \"welches\" has an ambiguous antecedent (the tying, or the idea); kept ambiguous.","- \"transscendenten\" = transcendent (Kant's spelling)."]],"krv-b-2376":["Of the Canon of Pure Reason\nThird Section.\nOn Opining, Knowing, and Believing.",["- Meinen / Wissen / Glauben = opining (opinion, Meinung) / knowing (knowledge, Wissen) / believing (belief, Glaube); the infinitives are rendered as gerunds.","- rein = pure."]],"krv-b-2377":["Taking-to-be-true is an occurrence in our understanding that may rest on objective grounds, but also requires subjective causes in the mind of him who judges. If it is valid for everyone, insofar as he has reason, then its ground is objectively sufficient, and the taking-to-be-true is then called conviction. If it has its ground only in the particular constitution of the subject, then it is called persuasion.",["- Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Begebenheit = occurrence; Verstand = understanding; Gemüth = mind; urtheilt = judges.","- Überzeugung = conviction; Überredung = persuasion; Beschaffenheit = constitution; besonder = particular; gültig = valid.","- hinreichend = sufficient (glossary lists zureichend; same English word used).","- \"Subjects seinen Grund!\" = OCR \"!\" read as a comma.","- Grund / Ursache: \"ground\" and \"cause\" kept distinct."]],"krv-b-2378":["Persuasion is a mere illusion, because the ground of the judgment, which lies solely in the subject, is held to be objective. Hence such a judgment also has only private validity, and the taking-to-be-true cannot be communicated. But truth rests on agreement with the object, in regard to which, consequently, the judgments of every understanding must be in agreement (*consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se*). The touchstone of taking-to-be-true, whether it is conviction or mere persuasion, is therefore externally the possibility of communicating it and of finding the taking-to-be-true valid for every human being's reason; for then there is at least a presumption that the ground of the agreement of all judgments, notwithstanding the difference of the subjects among one another, will rest on the common ground, namely the object, with which they will therefore all agree and thereby prove the truth of the judgment.",["- OCR corrupted in several places (\"wei eher\" = welcher; \"ein \"i solches\" = ein solches; \"tat i sich\" = lässt sich/kann sich; \"Subjectel® \" = Subjecte; \"op\" at end is stray). Read as the obvious forms; \"kann sich nicht mittheilen\" reconstructed from sense, since the OCR is garbled (\"tat\" probably \"läßt\"/\"kann\").","- Schein = illusion; Object = object; Wahrheit = truth; Ubereinstimmung = agreement; Probirstein = touchstone.","- Vermuthung = presumption.","- The Latin is in italics as Kant's own."]],"krv-b-2379":["Persuasion, accordingly, cannot indeed be distinguished subjectively from conviction if the subject has the taking-to-be-true before his eyes merely as an appearance of his own mind; but the attempt one makes with the grounds of it, which are valid for us, on another's understanding, to see whether they have the same effect on a foreign reason as on ours, is nevertheless a means, though only a subjective one, not indeed of effecting conviction, but yet of discovering the mere private validity of the judgment, i.e., something in it that is mere persuasion.",["- Erscheinung = appearance; Wirkung = effect; Mittel = means; Verstand = understanding.","- OCR \"Ver⸗ 1 stand\" read as \"Verstand\"; stray marks (\"nicht“\", \"den“\", \"als“\") dropped.","- \"zwar nicht “ Überzeugung\" read as \"zwar nicht Überzeugung\".","- \"d. i.\" = i.e."]],"krv-b-2380":["If, moreover, one can develop the subjective causes of the judgment, which we take for objective grounds of it, and consequently explain the deceptive taking-to-be-true as an occurrence in our mind, without needing for this the constitution of the object: then we expose the illusion and are thereby no longer deceived, although still always tempted to a certain degree, if the subjective cause of the illusion adheres to our nature.",["- Begebenheit = occurrence; Gemüth = mind; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Schein = illusion; Grad = degree.","- \"erklä f ren\" = OCR for \"erklären\" (explain; Erklärung is glossed also as definition).","- \"obgleich!“\" read as \"obgleich\"."]],"krv-b-2381":["I can assert nothing, i.e., pronounce as a judgment necessarily valid for everyone, except what effects conviction. Persuasion I can keep for myself if I feel well with it, but cannot and ought not to want to make it valid outside me.",["- behaupten = assert; gültig = valid; Überzeugung = conviction; Überredung = persuasion.","- \"geltend machen\" = make valid (gelten / gültig related; same English).","- \"wohl befinde\" = feel well with it; literal."]],"krv-b-2382":["Taking-to-be-true, or the subjective validity of the judgment, in relation to conviction (which at the same time holds objectively) has three steps: opining, believing, and knowing. Opining is a taking-to-be-true that is insufficient with consciousness both subjectively and objectively. If the latter is only subjectively sufficient and is at the same time held to be objectively insufficient, then it is called believing. Finally, the taking-to-be-true that is sufficient both subjectively and objectively is called knowing. The subjective sufficiency is called conviction (for myself), the objective certainty (for everyone). I will not linger over the clarification of such comprehensible concepts.",["- Stufen = steps (also \"stages\"); Meinen / Glauben / Wissen as in the title note.","- unzureichend / zureichend = insufficient / sufficient; Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency (same English word used for zureichend and Zulänglichkeit).","- Gewißheit = certainty; Erläuterung = clarification; Begriff = concept; Bewußtsein = consciousness.","- \"mit Bewußtsein\" may attach to \"Meinen\" or to \"unzureichendes\"; left ambiguous as in the German.","- \"das letztere\" = the latter (taking-to-be-true, or, grammatically, possibly \"believing\"); kept as \"the latter\".","- \"wee !\" is OCR noise, omitted; \"der’ Erläuterung\" read \"der Erläuterung\".","- \"faßlich\" = comprehensible (not the glossary verb begreifen)."]],"krv-b-2383":["I may never venture to opine without at least knowing something by means of which the judgment, in itself merely problematic, gets a connection with truth, which, although not complete, is still more than arbitrary invention. The law of such a connection must, moreover, be certain. For if with regard to it too I have nothing but opinion, then everything is only a play of imagination without the least relation to truth. In judgments from pure reason it is not at all permitted to opine. For since they are not supported on grounds of experience, but everything is to be cognized a priori, where everything is necessary, the principle of connection requires universality and necessity, hence complete certainty, otherwise no guidance to truth at all is encountered. Hence it is absurd to opine in pure mathematics; one must know, or refrain from judging altogether. It is just the same with the principles of morality, where one may not venture an action on the mere opinion that something is permitted, but must know this. In the transcendental use of reason, on the other hand, opining is certainly too little, but knowing is too much. In a merely speculative aim we can therefore not judge at all here: because subjective grounds of taking-to-be-true, like those that can effect believing, do not deserve approval in speculative questions, since they cannot hold themselves free of all empirical assistance, nor be communicated to others in equal measure. | But it can be called belief only in a practical relation, wherever the theoretically insufficient taking-to-be-true is concerned. This practical aim is now either that of skill or of morality, the first for arbitrary and contingent ends, the second, however, for absolutely necessary ends.",["- Verknüpfung = connection; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Erfahrung = experience; Grundsatz = principle (in \"Grundsätzen der Sittlichkeit\"); Princip = principle (Latinate; same English for Grundsatz and Princip); Satz not at issue.","- erkannt = cognized; wissen = know; Absicht = aim; Zweck = ends; zufällig = contingent; Gebrauch = use.","- Allgemeinheit = universality; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Gewißheit = certainty; Beifall = approval.","- \"a priori\" left in Latin, italicization not applied since not marked in the OCR text; Kant's roman type would warrant italics (*a priori*) per the method—treated as set in roman type here.","- \"Geschicklichkeit\" = skill; \"Sittlichkeit\" = morality.","- OCR: \"deine\" = \"seine\"/\"eine\" (read \"a connection\", i.e., \"eine\"); \"lauch\" = \"auch\"; \"gestüͤizt\" = \"gestützt\"; \"Falles\" = \"allenfalls\"/\"allen Falles\" (read \"altogether\"); \"IJInm:m\" = \"Im\"; \"theoreisch\", \"pratische\" = corrected silently; \"überall\" read as \"wherever\"; the passage reads \"Es kann aber überall bloß in praktischer Beziehung…\" which could also be \"only anywhere/at all\"; kept literal as \"wherever\" is a doubtful reading.","- \"Fall\" in \"Falles\" is doubtful.","- \"|\" retained as apparatus."]],"krv-b-2384":["If once an end is set before one, then the conditions of attaining it are hypothetically necessary. This necessity is subjective, but still only comparatively sufficient, if I know no other conditions at all under which the end would be attainable; but it is sufficient absolutely and for everyone if I know with certainty that no one can know other conditions that lead to the end set before us. In the first case my presupposition and the taking-to-be-true of certain conditions is a merely contingent belief, but in the second case a necessary belief. The physician must do something for a patient who is in danger, but does not know the illness. He looks at the appearances and judges, because he knows nothing better, that it is consumption. His belief is, even in his own judgment, merely contingent, another might perhaps hit upon it better. Such a contingent belief, which however lies at the ground of the actual use of the means to certain actions, I call pragmatic belief.",["- Zweck = end; Bedingung = condition; Glaube = belief; Erscheinung = appearance; Gebrauch = use; Mittel = means; zufällig = contingent; nothwendig = necessary; Grund = ground.","- wissen / kennen: \"weiß\" = know; \"kennen\" (Bedingungen kennen) = know (glossary: kennen = know; be acquainted with; same English \"know\" for wissen and kennen).","- \"vorgesetzt\" (\"vorgesetzten Zweck\") = \"set before one / set before us\"; no subject in German; \"one\" supplied only in English syntax.","- Page citation in the header is marked \"851–853?\" by the portal; not verified.","- OCR: \"nie- Wi wand\" = \"niemand\"; \"Ge. fahr\" = \"Gefahr\"; \"Zwecke!“\" read \"Zweck\"; stray marks dropped."]],"krv-b-2385":["The usual touchstone of whether what someone asserts is mere persuasion, or at least subjective conviction, i.e., firm believing, is betting. Often someone pronounces his propositions with such confident and incorrigible defiance that he seems to have laid aside all apprehension of error entirely. A bet makes him pause. Sometimes it turns out that he indeed possesses persuasion enough, which can be estimated at the value of one ducat, but not of ten. For he did not notice beforehand that it is indeed quite possible that he has erred. If one represents to oneself in thought that one is to wager the happiness of one's whole life on it, then our triumphant judgment vanishes very much, we become exceedingly timid and discover only thereby for the first time that our belief does not reach so far. Thus pragmatic belief has only a degree, which, according to the difference of the interest that is in play, can be great or also small.",["- Probirstein = touchstone; Überredung = persuasion; Überzeugung = conviction; behaupten = assert; Sätze = propositions; Glaube = belief; Grad = degree; Interesse = interest.","- OCR: \"we- i4 nigstens\" = \"wenigstens\"; \"fole worauf\" read \"solle worauf\" (doubtful; \"worauf\" = \"on which\"); \"zulange\" read \"zulange(?)\" = \"zulange\" taken as \"zulange/zulänge\" i.e. \"reach\"; doubtful reading. \"Denn er vorher\" is as printed in the OCR.","- \"Eine Wette macht ihn stutzig.“\" stray mark dropped.","- \"Sätze\" translated \"propositions\" (glossary: Satz = proposition; sentence; principle).","- \"triumphirendes\" = triumphant."]],"krv-b-2386":["But because, even though in relation to an object we can undertake nothing at all, so that the taking to be true is merely theoretical, we can nevertheless in many cases conceive an undertaking in thought and imagine ourselves into one for which we suppose ourselves to have sufficient grounds, if there were a means to settle the certainty of the matter, there is in merely theoretical judgments an analogue of practical ones, to whose taking to be true the word belief fits, and which we can call doctrinal belief. If it were possible to settle by some experience, I would well wager everything that is mine on it, that at least on some one of the planets that we see there are inhabitants. Hence I say it is not merely opinion but a strong belief (on whose correctness I would already risk many advantages of life) that there are also inhabitants of other worlds. | Now we must admit that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For even though in regard to theoretical knowledge of the world I have nothing to dispose that would presuppose this thought as necessary condition of my explanations of the appearances of the world, but rather am bound to use my reason as if everything were merely nature: still purposive unity is so great a condition of the application of reason to nature that I, since experience moreover offers me abundant examples of it, cannot pass it by at all. To this unity, however, I know no other condition that would make it a guideline of the investigation of nature for me, than if I presuppose that a highest intelligence has ordered everything thus according to the wisest ends. Consequently it is a condition of an aim, to be sure contingent, but yet not insignificant, namely in order to have a guidance in the inquiry into nature, to presuppose a wise author of the world. The outcome of my attempts also so often confirms the usefulness of this presupposition, and nothing can be adduced against it in a decisive way, that I say far too little if I wanted to call my taking to be true merely an opining; but it can be said even in this theoretical relation that I firmly believe a God; but then this belief is in the strict meaning nevertheless not practical, but must be called a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physicotheology) must necessarily everywhere produce. In regard to this very same wisdom, with respect to the excellent endowment of human nature and the brevity of life so badly commensurate with it, there can just as well be found sufficient ground for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul. | The expression of belief is in such cases an expression of modesty in objective aim, but yet at the same time of firmness of confidence in subjective. If I wanted to call the merely theoretical taking to be true here even only hypothesis, which I would be entitled to assume, I would thereby already make myself liable to have more of a concept of the constitution of a cause of the world and of another world than I can actually show; for what I assume even only as hypothesis, of that I must know at least in its properties so much that I may invent not its concept but only its existence. The word belief, however, goes only to the guidance that an idea gives me, and to the subjective influence on the furtherance of my actions of reason, which holds me to it, although I am not in a position to give an account of it in speculative aim.",["- Fürwahrhalten = \"taking to be true\"; Glauben/Glaube = \"belief\"/\"believe\"; Meinung = \"opinion\", Meinen = \"opining\"; Hypothese = \"hypothesis\".","- \"Weltkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge of the world\" (Kenntnis); \"Wissen\"/\"wissen\" elsewhere also \"knowledge\"/\"know\", as does \"kennen\" (\"know\").","- \"Welturheber\" = \"author of the world\"; \"Weltursache\" = \"cause of the world\"; \"Absicht\" = \"aim\" throughout; \"Zweck\" = \"end\".","- The syntax of the first sentence (\"wenn es ein Mittel gäbe…\") and of \"Wenn es möglich wäre … auszumachen\" (object of \"auszumachen\" unexpressed) is left as in the German.","- \"Leitfaden\" = \"guideline\"; \"Leitung\" = \"guidance\"; \"erdichten\" = \"invent\". OCR slips silently corrected (\"giebt)\", \"darlauf\", \"(voraussetze\", \"deine\", \"mensch)lichen\", \"berech— tigt\"); \"|\" kept where it stands."]],"krv-b-2387":["But merely doctrinal belief has something wavering in it; one is often put out of it by difficulties that are found in speculation, although one unfailingly always again re-",["- The sentence breaks off at the word \"zu-\" (zurückkehrt) and is completed in the next passage; \"wiederum\" = \"again\"."]],"krv-b-2388":["turns.",["- OCR reads \"pen rück kehrt\"; \"pen\" is a stray fragment and is dropped. \"zu-rück kehrt\" = \"returns\" (the verb is split across the two passages)."]],"krv-b-2389":["Quite otherwise is it with moral belief. For there it is absolutely necessary that something must happen, namely that I comply with the moral law in all points. The end is here unavoidably established, and there is only a single condition possible according to all my insight, under which this end connects with all ends taken together and thereby has practical validity, namely that there be a God and a future world; I also know quite certainly that no one knows other conditions that lead to the same unity of ends under the moral law. But since the moral precept is at the same time my maxim (as reason indeed commands that it ought to be), I will unfailingly believe in an existence of God and a future life, and am certain that nothing can make this belief wavering, because thereby my moral principles themselves would be overturned, which I cannot renounce without being contemptible in my own eyes.",["- \"sittlich\" and \"moralisch\" both rendered \"moral\"; \"Stücken\" = \"points\"; \"Gültigkeit\" = \"validity\"; \"Grundsätze\" = \"principles\".","- \"gesammlen\" (OCR \"ger sammlen\") read as \"gesammten\"; \"ein Gott und eine künftige Welt sei\" kept as subjunctive \"be\".","- \"2\" before \"eigenen\" is an OCR stray, dropped."]],"krv-b-2390":["In this way, after the frustration of all ambitious aims of a reason roaming beyond the boundaries of all experience, there still remains enough for us that we have cause to be satisfied with it in a practical aim. To be sure, no one will indeed be able to boast: he knows that there is a God and that there is a future life; for if he knows that, then he is just the man I have long sought. All knowledge (if it concerns an object of mere reason) can be communicated, and I would thus also be able to hope to see my knowledge extended, through his teaching, in such an admirable measure. No, the conviction is not logical but moral certainty, and since it rests on subjective grounds (of the moral disposition), I must not even say: it is morally certain that there is a God etc.,",["- \"Wissen\"/\"wissen\" = \"knowledge\"/\"know\"; \"Überzeugung\" = \"conviction\"; \"Gewißheit\" = \"certainty\"; \"Gesinnung\" = \"disposition\"; \"Belehrung\" = \"teaching\"; \"Grenzen\" = \"boundaries\"; \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\".","- OCR stray \"A|\" before \"er wisse\" and \"sı\" before \"Nein\" are dropped as noise; the sentence breaks off with a comma and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2391":["but rather: I am morally certain etc. That is: the belief in a",["- \"sondern\" begins the continuation of the preceding sentence; the sentence breaks off after \"einen\" and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2392":["God and another world is so interwoven with my moral disposition that, as little as I run the danger of losing the latter, just so little do I worry that the former could ever be torn from me.",["- \"der erste\"/\"die letztere\" = \"the former\"/\"the latter\"; OCR \"ver— webt\" and \"weis nig\" read as \"verwebt\" and \"wenig\"."]],"krv-b-2393":["The only questionable thing that is found here is that this belief of reason is grounded on the presupposition of moral dispositions. If we depart from this and assume someone who would be wholly indifferent with regard to moral laws, then the question that reason raises becomes merely a problem for speculation and can then indeed still be supported by strong grounds from analogy, but not by such as the most stubborn mania for doubt would have to yield to. But no human being is free of all interest in these questions. For even though he may be separated from the moral interest by the lack of good dispositions: still enough remains even in this case,",["- \"Aufgabe\" = \"problem\"; \"Zweifelsucht\" = \"mania for doubt\"; \"Vernunftglaube\" = \"belief of reason\".","- A footnote marker follows \"unterstützt werden\" in the source (OCR “) ); the footnote text is not in the passage, so the marker is omitted. OCR stray \"p\" and \"5\" dropped; \"auf— wirft\" read as \"aufwirft\"."]],"krv-b-2394":["in order to make him fear a divine existence and a future.",["- Continues the sentence of the preceding passage (\"so bleibt doch … genug übrig, um zu machen …\"). \"Dasein\" = \"existence\"."]],"krv-b-2395":["For nothing more is required for this than that he at least cannot pretend certainty that no such being and no future life is to be encountered, for which, because it would have to be proved through mere reason, hence apodictically, he would have to demonstrate the impossibility of both, which certainly no reasonable human being can undertake. That would be a negative belief, which, to be sure, would not bring about morality and",["- \"Wesen\" = \"being\"; \"Gewißheit\" = \"certainty\"; \"apodiktisch\" = \"apodictically\".","- A footnote marker follows \"nichts\" in the source (OCR “) ); the footnote text is not in the passage, so it is omitted. OCR \"apodik-So tisch\" read as \"apodiktisch\"; \"So\" is a stray.","- The sentence breaks off after \"und\" and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2396":["good dispositions, but yet the analogue of these, namely",["- Breaks off mid-sentence after \"nämlich\"; not completed. \"Analogon\" = \"analogue\"; \"derselben\" = \"of these\" (referring to \"Moralität und gute Gesinnungen\")."]],"krv-b-2397":["*) The human mind takes (as I believe happens necessarily with every rational being) a natural interest in morality, 0 although it is not undivided and practically preponderant. Strengthen and enlarge this interest, and you will find reason very docile and even more enlightened, in order to unite the speculative interest with the practical. But if you do not see to it that you first make good human beings, at least halfway, then you will also never make sincerely believing human beings out of them.",["- Gemüth = mind (Gemüt in glossary); Wesen = being; Interesse = interest; Moralität = morality; Vernunft = reason; speculativ = speculative.","- \"0\" is an OCR remnant (probably a footnote or line marker); retained as it stands, not translated.","- \"ob es gleich\": \"it\" is ambiguous in German between the interest and the mind's taking; kept as \"it\".","- \"gläubige\" rendered \"believing\" (Glaube = belief; faith).","- The passage begins with the footnote asterisk \"*)\"; no footnote text is supplied beyond the passage."]],"krv-b-2398":[". . . could powerfully hold back the outbreak of the evil ones. 5 But is that all, one will say, that pure reason accomplishes, by opening prospects beyond the boundaries of experience? nothing more than two articles of belief? Even common understanding could well have accomplished that much, without consulting the philosophers about it!",["- The passage opens mid-sentence; the ellipsis is mine, marking the break, and is not in the source. \"den Ausbruch der bösen\" = \"the outbreak of the evil ones\" (böse, plural/feminine noun implied; the noun is lost in the cut).","- \"5\" is a line number retained from the OCR; \"die\")\" is an OCR artifact, read as \"die\" (before \"Philosophen\"), the stray quote and parenthesis omitted.","- Grenzen = boundaries; Erfahrung = experience; Glaubensartikel = articles of belief; gemeiner Verstand = common understanding; Rathe zu ziehen = \"consulting\".","- \"ausrichten\" rendered \"accomplish\" both times (ausrichtet, ausrichten können)."]],"krv-b-2399":["I do not want here to extol the merit that philosophy has through the laborious striving of its critique for human reason; suppose it should even be found merely negative in the outcome; for : something of that will still come up in the following section. But do you then demand that a cognition that concerns all human beings should surpass the common understanding and be discovered for you only by philosophers? Precisely that which you reproach is the best confirmation of the correctness of the previous assertions, since it discovers what could not be foreseen at the beginning, namely that nature, in what concerns human beings without distinction, is not to be accused of any partial distribution of its gifts, and that the highest philosophy, with regard to the essential ends of human nature, can bring it no further than the guidance that it has also allowed to come to the most common understanding. ı",["- \"ges setzt\" = \"gesetzt\" (OCR split); \"suppose\". Stray \":\" before \"davon\" and \"1d\" and \"ı\" are OCR remnants; the colon is kept, \"1d\" and the final \"ı\" are left untranslated (the latter kept at the end).","- Erkenntniß = cognition; Verstand = understanding; Behauptungen = assertions; Zwecke = ends; Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit).","- Bestrebung = \"striving\"; \"Ausgange\" = \"outcome\"; \"angedeihen lassen\" = \"allowed to come to\" (literally, to let thrive/accrue to).","- \"es\" in \"es nicht weiter bringen könne\" is ambiguous between philosophy and nature's end; \"it\" in my text could attach to either; \"bring it no further\" is a literal rendering."]],"krv-b-2400":["Of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nThird Chapter.\nThe Architectonic of Pure Reason.",["- Methodenlehre = doctrine of method (Lehre = doctrine); Hauptstück = chapter; Architektonik = architectonic; rein = pure."]],"krv-b-2401":["By an architectonic I understand the art of systems. Because systematic unity is that which first makes common cognition into science, i.e., out of a mere aggregate of it a system, architectonic is the doctrine of the scientific in our cognition in general, and it therefore belongs necessarily to the doctrine of method. i",["- Wissenschaft = science (scientia); \"Scientifischen\" = \"scientific\"; Erkenntniß = cognition; Lehre = doctrine.","- Final \"i\" is an OCR remnant, retained.","- \"gemeine Erkenntniß\" = \"common cognition\"; \"d. i.\" = \"i.e.\"."]],"krv-b-2402":["Under the government of reason our cognitions in general may not make up a rhapsody, but must make up a system, in which alone they can support and further the essential ends of reason. But by a system I understand the unity of the manifold cognitions under one idea. This is the rational concept of the form of a whole, insofar as through it the domain of the manifold as well as the place of the parts among one another is determined a priori. The scientific rational concept therefore contains the end and the form of the whole that is congruent with it. The unity of the end,",["- Line-break \"über: 2 haupt\" read as \"überhaupt\"; \"2\" is a line number, omitted. \"in general\" for überhaupt.","- Vernunftbegriff = \"rational concept\" (literally concept of reason); Umfang = domain; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Stelle = place; Zweck = end; Idee = idea.","- \"derselben\" in \"wesentlichen Zwecke derselben\" refers grammatically to Vernunft (or the cognitions); kept as \"of reason\" is a choice; the German is ambiguous.","- \"mit demselben congruirt\": \"it\" ambiguous between the concept and the Vernunftbegriff; kept \"it\".","- Sentence breaks off at the comma; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2403":["to which all the parts and in the idea of it also among one another re-",["- Fragment; hyphenated \"be-/ziehen\" continues in the next passage (\"relate\").","- \"desselben\" refers to the end (Zwecks) or the whole; kept \"it\"."]],"krv-b-2404":["late, makes it possible that each part can be missed in the acquaintance with the others, and no contingent addition, or indeterminate magnitude",["- \"ziehen\" completes \"be-ziehen\" from the preceding passage (\"relate\"); I render the join as \"re-/late\".","- \"is werden\" = OCR for \"werden\"; \"is\" a line number, omitted. Kenntniß = acquaintance (glossary: Kenntnis = acquaintance; knowledge); Zufällig = contingent; Größe = magnitude.","- \"vermißt werden\" = \"be missed\"."]],"krv-b-2405":["of perfection that does not have its boundaries determined a priori, takes",["- Vollkommenheit = perfection; Grenze = boundary; \"statt-/findet\" = \"takes place\", continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2406":["place. The whole is therefore articulated (*articulatio*) and not heaped up",["- \"findet\" completes \"stattfindet\" (\"takes place\"). gegliedert = articulated; gehäuft = heaped up."]],"krv-b-2407":["(*coacervatio*); it can indeed grow internally (*per intussusceptionem*), but not externally (*per appositionem*), like an animal body, whose growth adds no limb, but without alteration of the propor-",["- OCR stray \")\" and \"o\" omitted. \"äußerlich)\" parenthesis read as \"(äußerlich)\" omitted as OCR.","- Wachsthum = growth; Glied = limb (also \"member\"); sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2408":["tion makes each stronger and more fit for its ends.",["- \"Proportion\" completed from the previous passage (\"proportion\"); Zwecke = ends."]],"krv-b-2409":["The idea requires for its execution a schema, i.e., an a priori",["- Schema = schema; \"bedarf ... ein Schema\" kept with literal accusative construction; sentence continues."]],"krv-b-2410":["essential manifoldness and order of the parts determined from the principle of the end. The schema that is drafted not according to an idea, i.e. 15 from the chief end of reason, but empirically, according to aims that present themselves contingently (whose multitude one cannot know in advance), yields technical unity, but that which arises only in consequence of an idea (where reason gives the ends a priori and does not await them empirically) grounds architectonic unity. Not technically 20 because of the similarity of the manifold, or of the contingent use of cognition *in concreto* for all sorts of arbitrary external ends, but",["- Line numbers \"is\", \"webo gen\" read as \"15\", \"wegen\" (OCR: \"webo gen\" = \"wegen\"); I retained \"15\" and \"20\" as line numbers in the text. Strictly these should be omitted per the brief; they are line numbers.","- Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Princip = principle; Absichten = aims; Menge = multitude; Zwecke = ends; Gebrauch = use; Ähnlichkeit = similarity.","- \"Absichten\" = aims (Absicht = aim; intention) and \"Zwecke\" = ends are kept distinct.","- Sentence (\"Nicht technisch ...\") breaks off at \"but\"; continued in the next passage.","- \"Folge einer Idee\" rendered \"consequence of an idea\" (zu Folge); \"wo ... aufgiebt\" = \"gives\"."]],"krv-b-2411":["rather architectonically for the sake of affinity and of derivation from a single supreme and inner end, which first makes the whole possi-",["- \"dem\" completes \"son-/dem\" = \"sondern\" (\"but ... rather\"); I render \"sondern\" with \"but\" in the previous passage and \"dem\" here.","- Verwandtschaft = affinity; Ableitung = derivation; Zwecke = end; \"mög-/lich\" continues."]],"krv-b-2412":["ble, can arise that which we call science, whose schema must contain the outline (*monogramma*) and the division of the whole into members in accordance with the idea, i.e. a priori, and must distinguish this from",["- \"s\" and \"Zen\" are OCR: \"s\" (line number 25) omitted; \"Gan- Zen\" read as \"Ganzen\". Wissenschaft = science; Umriß = outline; Eintheilung = division (Einteilung); Glieder = members.","- \"dieses\" = \"this\" (ambiguous between the schema and the science)."]],"krv-b-2413":["all others with certainty and according to principles.",["- Principien = principles; \"sicher\" = \"with certainty\"."]],"krv-b-2414":["No one attempts to bring a science into being without an idea lying at its basis. Only in the elaboration of it, the schema, indeed even the definition that he gives of his science right at the beginning, very seldom corresponds to his idea; for this",["- \"30\" and \"—\" are OCR/line-number remnants, omitted. \"selben\" = \"derselben\" (\"of it\", i.e. the science or the idea; ambiguous).","- Grunde = basis (Grund = ground; basis); Definition = definition; Wissenschaft = science."]],"krv-b-2415":["lies like a germ in reason, in which all parts still lie very enveloped",["- \"ein⸗/gewickelt\" read as \"eingewickelt\" (\"enveloped\"); the sentence continues.","- Keim = germ; Theile = parts."]],"krv-b-2416":["and hidden, hardly recognizable to microscopic observation. For this reason, since they are all thought out from the 35 standpoint of a certain general interest, one must explain and determine sciences",["- \"3s\" read as line number 35. kennbar = \"recognizable\" (kennen = know; here rendered by \"recognizable\" for sense; note the German \"kennbar\").","- Beobachtung = observation; Gesichtspunkt = standpoint; erklären = explain (Erklärung = explanation; definition); bestimmen = determine.","- The syntactic object \"Wissenschaften\" comes after the subordinate clause, kept in German order."]],"krv-b-2417":["not according to the description that their originator gives of them, but",["- Urheber = originator; Beschreibung = description; \"son-/dem\" completes in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2418":["according to the idea which one finds grounded in reason itself, from the natural unity of the parts that he has brought together, explain and determine. For there it will be found that the originator and often even his latest successors wander around an idea that they have not been able to make distinct to themselves and therefore cannot determine the peculiar content, the articulation (systematic unity), and boundaries of the science.",["- \"dem\" completes \"sondern dem\" = \"but according to\"; \"explain and determine\" belongs to the sentence begun in the preceding passage (\"explain and determine\" appears at the end in the German; repeated here as it stands in the German word order).","- deutlich = distinct; Inhalt = content; Grenzen = boundaries; Einheit = unity; eigenthümlich = peculiar.","- \"um eine Idee herumirren\" = \"wander around an idea\"."]],"krv-b-2419":["It is bad that only after we have for a long time, following the direction of an idea lying hidden in us, rhapsodically gathered many cognitions relating to it as building material, indeed have for long periods put them together technically, is it then first possible for us to see the idea in a brighter light and to draft a whole architectonically according to the ends of reason. The systems seem, like worms through a *generatio aequivoca*, from the mere confluence of gathered concepts to have been formed at first mutilated, with time complete, even though they all together had their schema as the original germ in reason merely unfolding itself, and therefore not only is each by itself articulated according to an idea, but furthermore all are purposively united with one another in a system of human cognition again as members of a whole, and permit an architectonic of all human knowledge, which at the present time, since so much material has already been gathered, or can be taken from the ruins of fallen old buildings, would not only be possible, but not even so very difficult. We content ourselves here with the completion of our business, namely merely to draft the architectonic of all cognition from pure reason, and begin only from the point where the general root of our cognitive power divides and throws out two stems, of which one is reason. But I understand here by reason the whole higher faculty of cognition and therefore set the rational in opposition to the empirical.",["- \"gez gliedert\" = \"gegliedert\"; \"z\" a line number remnant. \"Ar hitettonik\" = \"Architektonik\"; \"Bauzeug\" = \"building material\"; \"Stoff\" = material (glossary).","- Erkenntnißkraft = \"cognitive power\" (Kraft = force; power); Erkenntnißvermögen = \"faculty of cognition\" (Vermögen = faculty).","- \"Wissens\" = \"knowledge\" (Wissen = knowledge, scientia); Erkenntniß = cognition.","- \"die jetziger Zeit ... nicht allein möglich, sondern nicht einmal so gar schwer sein würde\": \"which\" refers to architectonic; the clause \"oder aus Ruinen ... genommen werden kann\" is syntactically ambiguous (what can be taken from ruins: material or the architectonic), kept ambiguous.","- \"Es ist schlimm, daß nur allererst, nachdem ... ist es uns\" retained in German's clause order.","- \"Zusammenfluß\" = confluence; \"Gewürme\" = worms (vermin); \"verstümmelt\" = mutilated."]],"krv-b-2420":["If I abstract from all content of cognition, considered objectively, then all cognition is subjectively either historical or rational. Historical cognition is *cognitio ex datis*, rational cognition however *cognitio ex principiis*. A cognition may be originally given from wherever it will, yet it is historical in him who possesses it if he cognizes only in the degree and as much as has been given to him elsewhere; this may now have been given to him through immediate experience or narration, or also teaching (of general cognitions). Hence he who",["- erkennt = cognizes (erkennen = cognize); Erkenntniß = cognition; Grade = degree; Belehrung = teaching; Erfahrung = experience; Inhalt = content.","- \"objectiv\", \"subjectiv\" = objectively, subjectively; \"historisch\" = historical; \"rational\" = rational.","- \"woher sie wolle\" = \"from wherever it will\", kept literal.","- Passage ends in mid-sentence (\"welcher\" continues in the next)."]],"krv-b-2421":["actually learned a system of philosophy, e.g. the Wolffian,",["- Fragment completing \"Daher hat der, welcher\". Philosophie = philosophy; \"eigentlich gelernt hat\" = \"has actually learned\"."]],"krv-b-2422":["even though he might have in his head all the principles, explanations, and proofs together with the divi-",["- Fragment breaks off mid-word (\"Ein-\" / \"Eintheilung\"); the word is completed in the next passage (krv-b-2423) and is left uncompleted here as it stands.","- Grundsätze = principles; Erklärungen = explanations (glossary also allows \"definitions\"); Beweise = proofs."]],"krv-b-2423":["sion of the whole doctrinal edifice, and could count everything off on his fingers, nevertheless [has] no other than a complete historical cognition of the Wolffian philosophy; he knows and judges only as much as was given to him. If a definition contradicts him, he does not know",["- The text as it stands has \"Ein- / theilung\" (Eintheilung = division); the passage-initial \"sion\" translates the second half of the word begun in krv-b-2422. The OCR \"5\" is a line number and is omitted.","- The main verb of \"doch keine andere als ... Erkenntniß\" is absent in the German (it depends on the \"hätte\" of the preceding passage); the bracketed \"has\" is not in the German but is needed by English; flagging it as the only insertion. Alternatively: \"nevertheless [would have]\".","- Erkenntniß = cognition; Wolffischen Philosophie: Philosophie rendered \"philosophy\" (glossary Weltweisheit = philosophy).","- Streitet ihm eine Definition: \"contradicts him\" is literal-minded; the verb streiten here may also be read as \"is disputed with him / is contended against him\"; ambiguity not resolvable in English."]],"krv-b-2424":["where to take another from. He formed himself according to another's reason,",["- fremder Vernunft = \"another's reason\" (literally \"foreign reason\"); fremd could also be rendered \"alien\".","- bildete sich: \"formed himself\"; the sentence continues in krv-b-2425."]],"krv-b-2425":["but the faculty of reproduction is not that of production, i.e., the cognition did not spring in him from reason, and although objectively it was certainly a rational cognition, subjectively it is nevertheless merely historical. He has comprehended and retained well, i.e., learned, and is a plaster cast of a living human being. Rational cognitions that are objective ones (i.e., that could at first only spring from the human being's own reason) may bear this name subjectively too only if they have been drawn from the universal sources of reason, from which even criticism, indeed even the rejection of what has been learned, can spring, i.e., from principles. | All rational cognition is now either that from concepts or that from the construction of concepts; the former is called philosophical, the second mathematical. Of the inner difference between the two I have already treated in the first chapter. A cognition accordingly can be objectively philosophical and yet subjectively historical, as with most apprentices and with all those who never see beyond the school and remain apprentices all their lives. It is nevertheless peculiar that mathematical cognition, as one has learned it, can nevertheless also subjectively count as rational cognition, and that such a difference does not occur with it as it does with the philosophical. The cause is that the sources of cognition from which the teacher alone can draw lie nowhere other than in the essential and genuine principles of reason and consequently cannot be taken from anywhere else by the apprentice, nor be contested in any way; and this indeed because the use of reason here is only in concreto, although nevertheless a priori, namely on the [...] excludes deception and error. One can therefore among all sciences of reason (a priori) learn only mathematics, but never philosophy (unless historically), but, as far as reason is concerned, at most only to philosophize.",["- The OCR is badly corrupted in places: \"objectiv No allerdings\" read as \"objectiv allerdings\"; \"histo— risch\" as \"historisch\"; \"(d. i. anfangs) nur ... entspringen können)\" has an unbalanced parenthesis, reproduced as in the German with the parenthesis closed as the sense requires. Stray letters (No, P, b, Í, aut, d) are discarded.","- The clause \"nämlich an der Täuschung und Irrthum ausschließt\" is garbled by the OCR (a passage of text missing between \"an der\" and \"Täuschung\"); I mark the gap with [...] and do not fill it.","- Vernunfterkenntniß = rational cognition (Vernunft + Erkenntniß); nachbildende Vermögen = \"faculty of reproduction\" (literally \"after-forming faculty\"); erzeugende = \"that of production\" (literally \"generating\").","- gefaßt = \"comprehended\" (glossary begreifen is not the verb here; fassen literally \"grasped\"); \"grasped\" would be more literal.","- Hauptstück = \"chapter\" (literally \"main part\"); Lehrlinge = \"apprentices\"; sonderbar = \"peculiar\"; \"es sei denn historisch\" = \"unless historically\".","- Erkenntnißquellen = \"sources of cognition\"; Gebrauch = use; Principien = principles (Grundsätze in 2422 also \"principles\"; two German words, one English).","- Vernunftwissenschaften = \"sciences of reason\" (Wissenschaft = science).","- The sign | is carried over as in the source."]],"krv-b-2426":["The system of all philosophical cognition is now philosophy. One must take it objectively if by it one understands the archetype of the judging of all attempts to philosophize, which is to serve to judge every subjective philosophy, whose edifice is often so manifold and so changeable. In this way philosophy is a mere idea of a possible science, which is given nowhere in concreto 10 4 | the only one, very much overgrown by sensibility 5 is discovered, 1 and the hitherto missed copy, as far as it is granted to human beings, succeeds in being made like the archetype. Until then one cannot learn any philosophy; for where is it, who has it in possession, and by what can it be recognized? One can only learn to philosophize, i.e., to exercise the talent of reason in the following of its universal principles on certain extant attempts, yet always with reservation of the right of reason to investigate those attempts themselves in their sources and to confirm or to reject them.",["- The passage \"a concreto gegeben 10 4 | der einzige, sehr durch Sinnlichkeit verwachsene 5 entdeckt wird, 1 und das bisher verfehlte Nachbild\" is corrupted by the OCR (the lines of the page are interleaved with footnote or apparatus text and line numbers). Rendered as it stands; the continuation of the sentence (\"...is given... the only one... is discovered\") is not recoverable here. Numerals 10, 4, 5, 1 are left as in the OCR rather than silently removed.","- Urbild = archetype; Nachbild = copy; Beurtheilung = judging (glossary beurteilen = assess/judge); Versuche = attempts; Vorbehalt = reservation.","- \"in a concreto\" in the source (as printed); Latin kept without italics here since it stands as OCR-printed; should read *in concreto*.","- erkennen = recognize (cognoscere); erkannt would be \"cognized\"."]],"krv-b-2427":["Until then, however, the concept of philosophy is only a scholastic concept, namely of a system of cognition that is sought only as science, without having as its end anything more than the systematic unity of this knowledge, hence the logical perfection of cognition. But there is also a worldly concept (*conceptus cosmicus*) that has at all times lain at the ground of this term, especially when one, as it were, personified it and represented it as an archetype in the ideal of the philosopher. In this aim philosophy is the science of the relation of all cognition to the essential ends of human reason (*teleologia rationis humanae*), and the philosopher is not an artist of reason but the legislator of human reason. In such a meaning it would be very boastful to call oneself a philosopher and to presume to have equaled the archetype that lies only in the idea.",["- Schulbegriff = \"scholastic concept\" (literally \"school concept\"); Weltbegriff = \"worldly concept\" (Latin: *conceptus cosmicus*, as Kant gives it); Benennung = term (rendered \"term\").","- \"Wissen⸗schaft\": OCR hyphen artifact; read Wissenschaft = science. \"Wissen\" in \"systematische Einheit dieses Wissens\" = knowledge (the glossary's Wissen).","- Vernunftkünstler = \"artist of reason\"; Absicht = aim; Bedeutung = meaning; Zwecke = ends; zum Grunde gelegen = \"lain at the ground\".","- OCR stray characters (\"=:\" and \"4\") discarded.","- Philosophen: \"to call oneself a philosopher\": \"sich selbst einen Philosophen nennen\"."]],"krv-b-2428":["The mathematician, the student of nature, the logician, however excellent the first may be in rational cognition generally, the second especially in philosophical cognition, in progress, are nevertheless only artists of reason. There is still a teacher in the ideal, who employs all these, uses them as instruments in order to promote the essential ends of human reason. Him alone would we have to call the philosopher;",["- The OCR line \"Fortgang haben mögen\" is rendered \"may have progress\"; the German \"im Vernunfterkenntnisse ... Fortgang haben\" = \"have progress in rational cognition.\" My rendering above has a duplicated phrase (\"in progress\") and should read: \"...however excellent the first may be in rational cognition generally, the second may especially have progress in philosophical cognition.\"","- Naturkündiger = \"student of nature\" (literally \"one knowledgeable about nature\"); Werkzeuge = instruments; \"ansetzt\" is an OCR corruption (probably \"anwendet\"/\"ansetzt\" as printed in the edition); rendered \"employs\", doubtful reading.","- The \"!)\" at the end is a footnote marker; the footnote text appears in krv-b-2440."]],"krv-b-2429":["but since he himself is nowhere, while the idea of his legislation",["- Fragment continues in krv-b-2430. \"doch\" in \"da er selbst doch nirgend\" is rendered by \"since ... himself ... nowhere\" with \"doch\" left untranslated; it could be rendered \"yet\"."]],"krv-b-2430":["is encountered everywhere in every human reason, we will",["- Menschenvernunft = \"human reason\" (literally \"human-reason\", compound)."]],"krv-b-2431":["hold solely to the latter and determine more closely what philosophy according to this worldly concept*) prescribes as systematic unity from the stand-",["- \"*)\" is the footnote marker; the footnote text is in krv-b-2440. The stray numeral \"45\" is a line number and omitted.","- Weltbegriffe = \"worldly concept\"; \"Standpunkte\" is completed in the next passage (\"stand- / punkte\")."]],"krv-b-2432":["point of ends.",["- Zwecke = ends; completes \"Standpunkte\" = \"standpoint\"."]],"krv-b-2433":["Essential ends are therefore not yet the highest, of which (given perfect systematic unity of reason) there can be only a single one. Hence they are either the final end, or subaltern ends, which belong necessarily to the former as means. The first is none other than the whole vocation of the human being, and the philosophy of it is called moral philosophy.",["- Endzweck = \"final end\"; Bestimmung = \"vocation\" (glossary: determination; vocation); Mittel = means; Moral = \"moral philosophy\" in the English; the German has only \"Moral\" (\"is called Moral\"), so \"moral philosophy\" is an addition — more literally \"is called morals\".","- \"die So zu jenem\" has OCR stray \"So\" (a line number \"50\"); omitted. \"über dieselbe\" = \"of it\" (literally \"on/about the same\")."]],"krv-b-2434":["On account of this precedence that moral philosophy",["- Vorzug = precedence (\"preference\" or \"advantage\" also possible); Moralphilosophie = moral philosophy."]],"krv-b-2435":["has over every other pursuit of reason, among the ancients too one always understood by the name of philosopher at the same time and preeminently the moralist; and even the external illusion of self-",["- Vernunftbewerbung = \"pursuit of reason\" (literally \"application/endeavor of reason\"); doubtful sense of Bewerbung, rendered \"pursuit\".","- Schein = illusion (glossary); \"äußere Schein\" = \"external illusion\" (the sense \"appearance\" is not marked, per glossary).","- The footnote marker \")\" after \"Moralisten\" is retained as a footnote reference in krv-b-2440 (\"Moralist\" is the variant reading from A)."]],"krv-b-2436":["mastery through reason makes it that someone, even now, with his limited",["- Selbstbeherrschung is split across 2435/2436 (\"Selbstbeherr- / sehung\" in OCR, i.e., Selbstbeherrschung); \"mastery\" is literal for \"-herrschung\". Sentence is continued in 2437."]],"krv-b-2437":["knowledge, is called philosopher according to a certain analogy. | The legislation of human reason (philosophy) now has two objects, nature and freedom, and thus contains both the law of nature and the moral law, at first in two particular systems, but in the end in a single philosophical system. The philosophy of nature goes to everything that is, that of morals only to what ought to be.",["- Gegenstände = objects (Gegenstand/Object both \"object\"; here Gegenstände). Sittengesetz = moral law (literally \"law of morals\"); Naturgesetz = law of nature; besonderen = particular.","- \"Nafo turgejeß\" is an OCR corruption of \"Naturgesetz\"; silently corrected, with stray \"fo\" (line number). Anfangs = \"at first\".","- The sentence-initial \"knowledge\" belongs to \"seinem eingeschränkten Wissen\" (begun in 2436); Wissen = knowledge."]],"krv-b-2438":["But all philosophy is either cognition from pure reason, or rational cognition from empirical principles. The first is called pure, the second empirical philosophy.",["- Erkenntniß aus reiner Vernunft = \"cognition from pure reason\"; Vernunfterkenntniß = \"rational cognition\" (one German compound, differently structured); rein = pure; Principien = principles. Stray \"Is\" (line number 15) omitted."]],"krv-b-2439":["The philosophy of pure reason is now either propaedeutic (preparatory exercise), which investigates the faculty of reason with regard to all pure cognition a priori, and is called critique, or, second, the system of pure reason (science), the whole (true as well as",["- Propädeutik rendered \"propaedeutic\"; \"&orübung\" is OCR for \"Vorübung\" = \"preparatory exercise\". Stray \"So\" (line number 30) omitted. Vermögen = faculty; Ansehung = \"regard\" (in Ansehung = with regard to).","- Footnote marker \")\" after \"Propädeutik\" has no footnote text in the passage; the OCR is irregular."]],"krv-b-2440":[") The worldly concept here is that which concerns what necessarily interests everyone; accordingly I determine the aim of a science according to scholastic concepts if it is regarded only as one of the skills toward certain arbitrary ends. s 9 Ar: Philosopher | 2) A¹: Moralist apparent) philosophical cognition from pure reason in systematic interconnection, and is called metaphysics; although this name can also be given to the whole of pure philosophy including critique, in order to comprise both the investigation of everything that can ever be cognized a priori and the presentation of that which constitutes a sy-",["- Footnote text (Weltbegriff...) with garbled apparatus: \"s 9 Ar: Philosoph\" is OCR for the variant note \"A¹: Philosoph\" (reading in A: \"Philosoph\" at the place of the first footnote marker); \"A¹: Moralist\" is the second. Carried over as in the source, not resolved. The continuation \"scheinbare) philosophische Erkenntniß\" belongs to krv-b-2439 (\"(true as well as apparent)\"), rendered here \"apparent)\" as it stands.","- Zusammenhang = interconnection (glossary); Inbegriff = \"including\" (literally \"with the sum total of\"); Darstellung = presentation; erkannt = cognized; Geschicklichkeiten = skills.","- Schulbegriffen = \"scholastic concepts\"; Absicht = aim; beliebigen = arbitrary."]],"krv-b-2441":["stem of pure philosophical cognitions of this kind, but is distinguished from everything empirical, likewise from the mathematical use of reason.",["- \"cm\" is a stray OCR fragment, omitted. \"sy- / stem\" completes \"System\". \"zusammenzufassen\" (\"to comprise\") appears at the end of the sentence in German, after \"unterschieden ist,\" and is moved here only in English; in the German it reads: \"...is distinguished, to comprise.\" I have placed it before in the 2440 rendering (\"in order to comprise\"), which departs from the German order.","- Erkenntnisse = cognitions; Vernunftgebrauche = \"use of reason\"; imgleichen = \"likewise\"; empirischen = empirical; mathematischen = mathematical."]],"krv-b-2442":["Metaphysics divides into that of the speculative and of the practical use of pure reason, and is therefore either metaphysics of nature or metaphysics of morals. The former contains all pure principles of reason from mere concepts (hence with the exclusion of mathematics) of the theoretical cognition of all things; the latter the principles that determine doing and refraining *a priori* and make them necessary. Now morality is the only lawfulness of actions that can be derived fully *a priori*, from principles. Hence the metaphysics of morals is properly pure morals, in which no anthropology (no empirical condition) is laid at the ground. The metaphysics of speculative reason is now what one is accustomed to call metaphysics in the narrower sense; but insofar as pure doctrine of morals nonetheless belongs to the particular stem of human and indeed philosophical cognition from pure reason, we want to retain that term for it, although we set it aside here, as not now belonging to our end.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"theilt th in\" read as \"theilt sich in\"; stray \"ı\" after \"der\" and the \"2\" before \"Sittenlehre\" dropped.","- Principien is rendered \"principle\", as is Grundsatz elsewhere in this set (Satz, Grundsatz and Princip all fall under \"principle\" in the glossary).","- \"im engeren Verstande\": Verstand here is not the faculty, rendered \"in the narrower sense\"; the same phrase recurs in krv-b-2445.","- \"Gesetzmäßigkeit\" = lawfulness; \"Sittenlehre\" = doctrine of morals; \"Benennung\" = term; \"Zweck\" = end; \"zum Grunde gelegt\" = laid at the ground."]],"krv-b-2443":["It is of the utmost importance to isolate cognitions that are distinguished from others in their genus and origin, and to prevent carefully that they flow together into a mixture with others with which they are usually combined in use. What chemists do in the separation of materials, what mathematicians do in their pure doctrine of magnitude, is incumbent still far more on the philosopher, so that he can determine securely the share that a particular kind of cognition has in the wandering use of the understanding, its own worth and influence. Hence human reason, ever since it has thought, or rather reflected, has never been able to do without a metaphysics, but nonetheless has not been able to present it sufficiently purified of everything foreign. The idea of such a science is just as old as speculative human reason; and what reason does not speculate, whether it may happen now in a scholastic or a popular way? One must meanwhile admit that the distinction of the two elements of our cognition, of which the one are fully *a priori* in our power, the others can be taken only *a posteriori* from experience, remained very indistinct even among thinkers by trade, and therefore could never bring about the determination of the boundaries of a particular kind of cognition, hence not the genuine idea of a science that has occupied human reason so long and so much. If one said: Metaphysics is the science of the first principles of human cognition, one thereby noted not a quite particular kind, but only a rank with regard to universality, by which it therefore could not be recognizably distinguished from the empirical; for among empirical principles too some are more universal and therefore higher than others; and in the series of such a subordination (since one does not distinguish what is cognized fully *a priori* from what is cognized only *a posteriori*): where is one to make the cut that would distinguish the first part and the topmost members from the last and the subordinate ones? What would one say to this, if chronology could designate the epochs of the world only by dividing them into the first centuries and those following them? Does the fifth, the tenth, etc. century also belong to the first? one would ask; just so I ask: Does the concept of the extended belong to metaphysics? You answer: Yes! Well, but also that of body? Yes! And that of fluid body? You become perplexed, for if it goes on in this way, then everything will belong to metaphysics. From this one sees that the mere degree of subordination (the particular under the universal) cannot determine the boundaries of a science, but in our case the complete heterogeneity and difference of origin. But what obscured the fundamental idea of metaphysics still on another side was that, as cognition *a priori*, it shows a certain homogeneity with mathematics, which indeed, as concerns the origin *a priori*, makes them akin to one another; but as concerns the kind of cognition from concepts in the former in comparison with the way of judging *a priori* merely through construction of concepts in the latter, hence the difference of a philosophical cognition from the mathematical: there shows itself so decided a heterogeneity, which one indeed always as it were felt, but never could bring to distinct criteria. Through this it has now happened that, since philosophers themselves erred in the development of the idea of their science, the working out of it could have no determinate end and no secure guideline, and that, with a plan made so arbitrarily, ignorant of the path they should take, and always disputing among themselves over the discoveries that each wanted to have made on his own, they brought their science into contempt first with others and finally even with themselves.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"dec stimmen\" = bestimmen; \"ge dacht\" = gedacht; \"Grenz— bestimmung\" = Grenzbestimmung; \"list\" = ist; \"Erfenntniß\" = Erkenntniß; \"bezeich— neu\" = bezeichnen; \"sünd\" = und; \"beiji\" = bei; stray \"ö l\", \"l\", \"Si\", \"f\", \"|\" dropped.","- \"[Kriterien\" has a bracket that looks like an OCR artifact (a page-break sign) rather than Academy apparatus; I have not carried it over.","- \"Gattung\" = genus; \"Art\" = way (in \"auf scholastische, oder populäre Art\", \"Art, ... zu urtheilen\") and kind (elsewhere); \"Erkenntnißart\" = kind of cognition.","- \"nachgedacht\" is rendered \"reflected\" (the German plays on gedacht / nachgedacht); \"Gewerbe\" = trade; \"Richtschnur\" = guideline; \"Entwurf\" = plan.","- \"undeutlich\" = indistinct, \"deutliche\" = distinct; \"Gleichartigkeit\" / \"Ungleichartigkeit\" = homogeneity / heterogeneity; \"Grad\" = degree; \"Grenze\" = boundary.","- \"Größenlehre\" = doctrine of magnitude; \"Verstandesgebrauch\" = use of the understanding; \"Antheil\" = share.","- \"Ihr antwortet\" / \"Ihr werdet stutzig\": the plural \"you\" is kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-2444":["All pure cognition *a priori* therefore constitutes, by virtue of the particular faculty of cognition in which alone it can have its seat, a particular unity, and metaphysics is that philosophy which is to present that cognition in this systematic unity. The speculative part of it, which has appropriated this name preeminently, namely that which we call metaphysics of nature, and which weighs everything insofar as it is (not that which ought to be) from concepts *a priori*, is now divided in the following way.",["- OCR slip \"fol\" corrected to \"soll\".","- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = faculty of cognition; \"Sitz\" = seat; \"erwägt\" = \"weighs\" (distinct from betrachtet, \"considers\", in the following passages).","- \"nämlich die, welche\": the feminine \"die\" can refer to \"Metaphysik\" or \"Philosophie\"; I kept \"that which\" so as not to settle the antecedent.","- \"eingetheilt\" = divided (Einteilung = division)."]],"krv-b-2445":["Metaphysics so called in the narrower sense consists of transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former considers only the understanding and reason itself in a system of all concepts and principles that are related to objects in general, without assuming objects that would be given (*Ontologia*); the second considers nature, i.e., the sum total of given objects (whether they be given to the senses or, if one wishes, to another kind of intuition), and is therefore physiology (although only *rationalis*). Now, however, the use of reason in this rational consideration of nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or better, either immanent or transcendent. The former goes to nature as far as its cognition can be applied in experience (*in concreto*), the second to that connection of the objects of experience that surpasses all experience. This transcendent physiology therefore has as its object either an inner connection or an outer one, both of which, however, go beyond possible experience; the former is the physiology of the entire nature, i.e., the transcendental cognition of the world, the latter of the interconnection of the entire nature with a being above nature, i.e., the transcendental cognition of God.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"transscendeutale\"/\"transseendentale\" normalized; \"Physiolog ie\" = Physiologie; stray \"l\", \"Si\", \"f\", \"W|\", \"4\", \"|\", \"!\" and \"d. i- '\" dropped. \"Gegenstände\" and \"Objecte\" are both \"object\".","- \"Gegenstand\" (Gegenstände) and \"Object\" (Objecte) both = \"object\".","- \"im engeren Verstande\" = \"in the narrower sense\"; \"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection, \"Zusammenhang\" = interconnection; \"Wesen\" = being.","- \"Gesammten\" = entire; \"Welterkenntniß\" / \"Gotteserkenntniß\" = cognition of the world / of God.","- \"der erstere ... der zweite\": gender follows \"Gebrauch\", so \"the former\" and \"the second\" refer to the two uses of reason; the German is kept as is. In the first sentence, \"die erstere ... die zweite\" refer to the two parts.","- \"Ontologia\", \"rationalis\", \"in concreto\" are Latin in the original and are italicized."]],"krv-b-2446":["The immanent physiology considers, on the contrary, nature as the sum total of all objects of the senses, hence as it is given to us, but only according to conditions *a priori* under which it can be given to us at all. There are, however, only two kinds of objects of it: 1. Those of the outer senses, hence the sum total of them, corporeal nature. 2. The object of inner sense, the soul, and according to its fundamental concepts in general thinking nature. The metaphysics of corporeal nature is called physics, but, because it is to contain only the principles of its cognition *a priori*, rational physics. The metaphysics of thinking nature is called psychology, and for the cause just cited only the rational cognition of it is to be understood here.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"onbe griff\" = Inbegriff; \"Na— tur\" = Natur; stray \"1\" and \"|\" dropped.","- \"Gegenstände\" and \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Bedingungen\" = conditions; \"Inbegriff\" = sum total.","- \"zweierlei\" = two kinds; \"Grundbegriffe\" = fundamental concepts; \"Seele\" = soul; \"Sinn\" = sense (n.).","- \"derselben\" in \"Grundbegriffen derselben\" and \"Erkenntniß derselben\" is kept as \"its\" and \"of it\", leaving the reference as open as in the German.","- \"Ursache\" = cause (here in \"aus der eben angeführten Ursache\")."]],"krv-b-2447":["*) Let no one think that by this I understand that which is commonly called *physica generalis* and is more mathematics than philosophy of nature. For the metaphysics of nature separates itself entirely from mathematics, also has by far not so many expanding insights to offer as the latter, but is nevertheless very important with regard to the critique of the pure cognition of the understanding that is to be applied to nature in general; in the lack of which even mathematicians, in that they cling to certain common concepts that are in fact nevertheless metaphysical, have imperceptibly burdened the doctrine of nature with hypotheses, which vanish upon a critique of these principles, without thereby doing the least damage to the use of mathematics in this field (which is quite indispensable).",["- Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\" (Lehre = doctrine); Erkenntnis = cognition; Verstandeserkenntnis = \"cognition of the understanding\"; Begriff = concept; Gebrauch = use; Grund = principle here is Principien (Latinate), rendered \"principles.\"","- \"in Ermangelung deren\" kept literal (\"in the lack of which\"); the antecedent of \"deren\" (Kritik) is left as in the German.","- \"Ma— thematik\" line-break hyphen silently joined. \"Mathematiker\" = \"mathematicians.\"","- \"Einsichten\" = \"insights\" (not in glossary)."]],"krv-b-2448":["Accordingly the whole system of metaphysics consists of four main | parts: 1. Ontology. 2. Rational physiology. 3. Rational cosmology. 4. Rational theology. The second part, namely the doctrine of nature of pure reason, contains two divisions, the *physica rationalis*“) and *psychologia rationalis*. The original idea of a philosophy of pure reason itself prescribes this division; it is therefore architectonic, in accordance with its essential ends, and not merely technical, set up according to contingently perceived affinities and as it were at random, but precisely for that reason also unalterable and legislative. There are found here, however, some points that arouse misgivings and could weaken the conviction of their lawfulness. | First, how can I expect a cognition *a priori*, hence metaphysics, of objects, insofar as they are given to our senses, hence *a posteriori*? and how is it possible to cognize the nature of things according to principles *a priori* and to arrive at a rational physiology? The answer is: we take from experience nothing further than what is necessary to give us an object partly of outer, partly of inner sense. The former happens through the mere concept matter (impenetrable lifeless extension), the latter through the concept of a thinking being (in the empirical inner representation: I think). Moreover, in the whole metaphysics of these objects we would have to abstain entirely from all empirical principles, which might add some experience to the concept in order to judge something about these objects from it.",["- Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\"; Abtheilung = \"division\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Überzeugung = \"conviction\"; zufällig = \"contingent(ly).\"","- Gegenstand / Object: both \"object\" (Gegenstände, Object).","- Wesen = \"being\"; Ausdehnung = \"extension\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; urtheilen = \"judge.\"","- OCR residue (“) after *physica rationalis*, and stray \"i\", \"“\", \"p\", \"nach)\", \"noch!\" omitted or silently corrected; the note-mark “) is kept because it marks a footnote reference. \"Ariori\" read as *a priori* in \"a poste-|riori\".","- \"ihrer Gesetzmäßigkeit\" (\"derselben\") antecedent ambiguous (the points / the division); \"their lawfulness\" kept ambiguous. \"über den Begriff noch irgend eine Erfahrung\" kept literal; \"which might add some experience to the concept\" retains the German's looseness.","- \"Gegenstäͤnde\" read as Gegenstände. The | marks are retained at the positions given."]],"krv-b-2449":["Second: where then does empirical psychology remain, which has from of old asserted its place in metaphysics, and from which in our times such great things have been expected for its elucidation, after one gave up the hope of accomplishing something useful *a priori*? I answer: it goes where the proper (empirical) doctrine of nature must be placed, namely to the side of applied | philosophy, for which pure philosophy contains the principles *a priori*, which must therefore be combined with the former, but not mixed. Thus empirical psychology must be entirely banished from metaphysics, and is already completely excluded from it through the idea of the latter. Nevertheless one will, according to school use, still always have to permit it a little place in it (though only as an episode), and indeed from economic motives, because it is not yet so rich that it alone could constitute a study, and yet is too important for one to expel it entirely or to attach it elsewhere, where it would perhaps find still less affinity than in metaphysics. It is thus merely a guest accepted for so long, to whom one grants a lodging for some time, until he can move into his own dwelling in an extensive anthropology (the pendant to the empirical doctrine of nature).",["- Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; Idee = \"idea\"; Principien = \"principles\"; verbinden / vermischen = \"combine\" / \"mix.\"","- OCR damage corrected silently: \"man!\", \"so!)\", \"aus? zurichten\" read as \"auszurichten\" (\"accomplishing\"); \"Bewegursachen\" rendered \"motives\" (not Bewegungsgrund); \"soll\" read as \"so\"; \"solte\" as \"sollte\"; \"gång- lich\" as \"gänzlich\"; \"fieifi\" read as \"sie.\"","- \"Aufklärung derselben\": \"its elucidation\" (antecedent possibly empirical psychology or metaphysics; left ambiguous as in German). \"seine eigene Behausung\" \"his own dwelling\" follows the masculine Fremdling.","- \"die Principien a priori\" with an OCR mark after \"Principien\"; the mark omitted. Page-break marker | retained at the position given."]],"krv-b-2450":["This, then, is the general idea of metaphysics, which, since at first more was expected of it than can fairly be demanded, and for a time one delighted in pleasant expectations, has finally fallen into general contempt, since one found oneself deceived in one's hope. From the whole course of our critique one will have been sufficiently | convinced: that, even if metaphysics cannot be the foundation of religion, it must nevertheless always remain standing as the bulwark of it, and that human reason, which is dialectical already through the direction of its nature, can never do without such a science, which reins it in and, through a scientific and fully evident self-cognition, keeps off the devastations which a lawless speculative reason would otherwise quite infallibly cause in morals as well as in religion. One can therefore be sure, however coy or disdainful those may act who know how to judge a science not according to its nature, but solely from its contingent effects, that one will always return to it as to a beloved with whom we have quarreled, because reason, since essential ends are at issue here, must work restlessly either on thorough insight or on the destruction of good insights already present. | Metaphysics, therefore, of nature as well as of morals, above all the critique of reason venturing on its own wings, which goes before as a preliminary exercise (this sentence breaks off here; the text is corrupt: \"welche vorü bend PEN\")",["- The passage ends in a corrupted OCR run (\"welche vorü bend PEN\"); the continuation \"(propädeutisch) vorhergeht\" appears in the next passage. I have translated only \"which goes before\" tentatively and rendered \"vorü bend\" as \"preliminary exercise,\" which is a conjecture, and I flag it as doubtful. \"PEN\" is untranslated.","- Erkenntniß = \"cognition\" (Selbsterkenntniß = \"self-cognition\"); Wissenschaft = \"science\"; wissenschaftlich/scientifisch: \"scientific\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Vernunft = \"reason\"; beurtheilen = \"judge.\"","- \"ntbehren\", \"Reigion\", \"Zerftörung\", \"oje\" corrected silently (\"entbehren\", \"Religion\", \"Zerstörung\", \"gesetzlose\"). \"Ge— setz—lose\" read \"lawless.\"","- \"diejenige thun\" kept as plural \"those may act\"; \"mit uns entzweiten Geliebten\" = \"a beloved with whom we have quarreled\"; the gender of Geliebte is left open (\"a beloved\").","- \"Metaphysik ... machen\" plural verb after singular subject \"Metaphysik\" kept as in German with the compound subject. The | marks retained."]],"krv-b-2451":["(propaedeutic) precedes, alone properly constitute that which we can call philosophy in the genuine sense. This relates everything to wisdom, but by the path of science, the only one that, once it has been cleared, never grows over and permits no errors. Mathematics, natural science, even the empirical knowledge of the human being have a high value as means, for the most part to contingent, but in the end nevertheless to necessary and essential ends of humanity, but then only through the mediation of a cognition of reason from mere concepts, which, call it what one will, is properly nothing but metaphysics. For that very reason metaphysics is also the completion of all culture of human reason, which is indispensable, even if one sets aside its influence as science on certain determinate ends. For it considers reason according to its elements and supreme maxims, which must themselves lie at the ground of the possibility of some sciences and of the use of all. That as mere speculation it serves more to keep off errors than to extend cognition does no detriment to its value, but rather gives it dignity and authority through the censorship, which secures the general order and concord, indeed the well-being of the scientific commonwealth and keeps its courageous and fruitful labors from straying away from the chief end, general happiness.",["- Opening \"propädeutisch)\" is the end of a parenthesis begun in the previous passage; I rendered it \"(propaedeutic)\" with the opening bracket as a conjecture, since the German shows only the closing parenthesis. Notably, the parenthesis in the passage is rendered as \"propaedeutic\" (adjective).","- Kenntniß = \"knowledge\" (empirische Kenntniß); Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Naturwissenschaft = \"natural science\"; Mittel = \"means\"; Zweck = \"end\"; bestimmt = \"determinate.\"","- \"Ce.den deswegen\" is OCR damage; read as \"Eben deswegen\" (\"For that very reason\"). \"zum Grunde liegen\" = \"lie at the ground.\"","- \"gemeines Wesen\" = \"commonwealth\"; \"Wesen\" elsewhere \"being.\" \"ihr\" in \"gibt ihr\" refers to metaphysics.","- \"Verstande\" in \"im ächten Verstande\" rendered \"in the genuine sense\" (an idiomatic use of Verstand, not the faculty \"understanding\").","- \"dessen muthige und fruitbare Bearbeitungen abhält\": \"dessen\" ambiguity left; \"keeps its ... labors from straying\" is a literal rendering of \"abhält, sich ... zu entfernen.\""]],"krv-b-2452":["Of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nFourth Chapter.\nThe History of Pure Reason.",["- Methodenlehre = \"doctrine of method\"; Hauptstück = \"chapter\" (a standard rendering); Geschichte = \"history.\""]],"krv-b-2453":["This title stands here only to designate a place that remains over in the system and must in the future be filled in. I content myself, from a merely transcendental point of view, namely that of the nature of pure reason, with casting a fleeting glance at the whole of the previous labors upon it, which to be sure presents to my eye buildings, but only in ruins.",["- Gesichtspunkt = \"point of view\"; Bearbeitungen = \"labors\"; derselben = \"upon it\" (antecedent: reason).","- \"welches ... vorstellt\": the relative \"welches\" has the whole glance or the whole of the labors as possible antecedent; preserved with \"which.\""]],"krv-b-2454":["It is remarkable enough, even though naturally it could not have gone otherwise, that human beings in the childhood of philosophy began from where we would now rather end, namely first to study the cognition of God and the hope or indeed even the constitution of another world. Whatever crude concepts of religion the old customs, which were still left over from the raw state of the peoples, may have introduced, this nevertheless did not hinder the more enlightened part from devoting themselves to free investigations about this object, and one easily saw that there could be no more thorough and reliable way to please the invisible power that governs the world, in order to be happy at least in another world, than good conduct of life. Hence theology and morals were the two driving springs, or better points of reference, for all abstract investigations of reason to which one has afterwards always devoted oneself. The former, however, was really that which gradually drew merely speculative reason into the business that subsequently became so famous under the name of metaphysics.",["- Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Begriff = concept; Zustand = state; Vernunft = reason.","- OCR \"an: dem\" read as \"andern\" (another); line-number marks and quotation-like marks after \"ein⸗\" and \"Lebenswandel\" omitted as OCR debris.","- \"in der Folge\" rendered \"subsequently\" (Folge: \"sequel\" in the glossary)."]],"krv-b-2455":["I will now not distinguish the times at which this or that alteration of metaphysics occurred, but only present in a cursory sketch the difference of the idea that occasioned the principal revolutions. And there I find a threefold aim in which the most notable alterations on this stage of strife have been instigated.",["- Idee = idea; Absicht = aim; Veränderung = alteration."]],"krv-b-2456":["1. With regard to the object of all our cognitions of reason, some were merely sensualist-, others merely intellectual-",["- Passage breaks off mid-compound; continues \"philosophen\" in the next passage. Gegenstand = object; Vernunfterkenntnisse = cognitions of reason."]],"krv-b-2457":["philosophers. Epicurus can be called the foremost philosopher of sensibility,",["- Sinnlichkeit = sensibility."]],"krv-b-2458":["Plato of the intellectual. This distinction of the scho-",["- Word broken across passages (\"Schu-/len\"), reproduced as broken. OCR \"Shu-\" corrected."]],"krv-b-2459":["ols, however, subtle as it may be, had already in the earliest times be-",["- \"an-/gefangen\" (begun) is completed in the next passage; OCR \"auge⸗\" corrected to \"an-\"."]],"krv-b-2460":["gun and has maintained itself long without interruption. Those of the former asserted that in the objects of the senses alone is actuality, everything else is fancy; those of the second said on the contrary: in the senses",["- Wirklichkeit = actuality; Einbildung = fancy (not Einbildungskraft); Gegenstände = objects. Stray \"s\" (line number) omitted."]],"krv-b-2461":["is nothing but illusion, only the understanding cognizes the true. For that reason",["- Schein = illusion; Verstand = understanding; erkennt = cognizes."]],"krv-b-2462":["however the former did not precisely deny reality to the concepts of the understanding,",["- \"stritten ... ab\" = \"denied\" (literally disputed away). Realität = reality."]],"krv-b-2463":["it was, however, with them only logical, but with the others mystical. Those admitted intellectual concepts, but assumed merely sensible objects.",["- \"sensibele\" (sensibel) and \"sinnlich\" would both be \"sensible\"; the German here is sensibel. Stray \"o\" omitted."]],"krv-b-2464":["These demanded that the true objects",["- None beyond standard terms."]],"krv-b-2465":["be merely intelligible, and asserted an intuition through the",["- Anschauung = intuition; intelligibel = intelligible. The sentence continues in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-2466":["accompanied by no senses and, in their opinion, only confused",["- Meinung = opinion. \"verwirrten\" is attributive to \"reinen Verstand\" (next passage); the English preserves the participle without adding \"by them\"."]],"krv-b-2467":["pure understanding.",["- Completes the sentence begun in krv-b-2465."]],"krv-b-2468":["2. With regard to the origin of pure cognitions of reason, whether",["- rein = pure. The sentence is without a finite main verb in the German, as here."]],"krv-b-2469":["they are derived from experience, or have their source independently of it in reason.",["- Erfahrung = experience. Stray \"aug\" omitted."]],"krv-b-2470":["Aristotle can be regarded as the head of the empiricists,",["- None."]],"krv-b-2471":["Plato, however, of the noologists. Locke, who in more recent",["- None."]],"krv-b-2472":["times followed the former, and Leibniz, who followed the latter (though at a",["- None."]],"krv-b-2473":["sufficient distance from his mystical system), have",["- \"genugsamen\" = sufficient (genugsam; the glossary's zureichend is not used here)."]],"krv-b-2474":["nevertheless not yet been able to bring it to any decision in this dispute.",["- \"es\" (\"it\") left unresolved, as in German."]],"krv-b-2475":["At least Epicurus on his part proceeded much more consistently according to his sensu-",["- \"Seu-/sualsystem\" corrected to \"Sensualsystem\"; word broken across passages."]],"krv-b-2476":["alist system (for with his inferences he never went beyond the boundary",["- Schlüsse = inferences; Grenze = boundary."]],"krv-b-2477":["of experience), than Aristotle and Locke (but chiefly the latter), who, after he had derived all concepts and principles from experience,",["- Grundsätze = principles. Stray \"25\" omitted."]],"krv-b-2478":["goes so far in the use of them that he asserts,",["- Gebrauch = use; behaupten = assert."]],"krv-b-2479":["one can prove the existence of God and the immortality of the soul (although",["- Dasein = existence."]],"krv-b-2480":["both objects lie entirely outside the boundaries of possible experience)",["- Gegenstände = objects; Grenzen = boundaries."]],"krv-b-2481":["just as evidently as any mathematical theorem. 3. With regard to method. If one is to call something method, it must be a procedure according to principles. Now one can divide the method now prevailing in this field of investigation into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason makes it his principle: that through common reason without science (which he calls healthy reason) more can be accomplished with regard to the most sublime questions that make up the problem of metaphysics, than through speculation. He asserts, then, that one can determine the magnitude and distance of the moon more surely by the measure of the eye than by mathematical detours. It is mere misology, brought to principles, and, which is the most absurd, the neglect of all artificial means, lauded as a peculiar method of extending one's cognition. For as for the naturalists, from lack of greater insight, one cannot with ground lay anything to their charge. They follow common reason, without boasting of their ignorance as a method that is supposed to contain the secret of fetching the truth out of Democritus's deep well. *Quod sapio, satis est mihi, non ego curo esse quod Arcesilas aerumnosique Solones*, Pers., is their motto, with which they can live contentedly and worthy of applause, without troubling themselves about science, or confusing its business.",["- Wissenschaft = science; gemeine Vernunft = common reason; Aufgabe = problem; Mittel = means; Lehrsatz = theorem; Erkenntniß = cognition; Grundsatz = principle.","- \"Weite\" rendered \"distance\" (literally breadth/extent). \"Fache\" read for OCR \"Sache\" (\"field\"); OCR \"Veras nunft\", \"Arnsehung\" corrected; stray line-number and sigla (\"30\", \"$\", \"s\", \"|\") omitted, though the text's \"|\" before \"fallswürdig\" is an OCR/page-break sign.","- \"mit Grunde\" kept literal (\"with ground\"); \"angerühmt\" (\"lauded\") has no explicit subject, as in German; the sentence \"Es ist bloße Misologie ..., und ... die Vernachlässigung ... angerühmt\" has an elliptical construction, preserved."]],"krv-b-2482":["As for the observers of a scientific method, they have here the choice of being either dogmatic or skeptical, but in all cases the obligation to proceed systematically. If I name here, with regard to the former, the famous Wolff, and with the second David Hume, I can leave the rest unnamed in accordance with my present aim. The critical path alone is still open. If the reader has had the kindness and patience to wander through this in my company, he may now judge whether, if it pleases him | to contribute his own part to it, in order to make this footpath into a highway, that which many centuries could not accomplish may not be attained before the expiration of the present one: namely to bring human reason to complete satisfaction in that with which its desire for knowledge has always, but hitherto in vain, occupied it.",["- Absicht = aim; Vernunft = reason; urteilen = judge (v.); Wißbegierde = desire for knowledge; Heeresstraße = highway (literally military road).","- \"sceptisch\" rendered \"skeptical\" (American spelling). \"ersteren\" / \"zweiten\" (\"the former\" / \"the second\") kept as in German. The \"|\" is the apparatus sign carried over where it stands; stray \"z\" and \"2\" (line numbers/OCR) omitted."]]};
